inl note_of_ps1_to_spiral_4 () = Note ("PS1-TO-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night inventory of own PowerShell (caches, vendor, deps, samples excluded): about 13,300 lines in 282 files: spiral 6,897 / 72, polyglot 2,520 / 131, eoie 1,832 / 19 (compiler-contracts test scripts are the largest), dice 1,056 / 26, alphabet 492 / 16, x 293 / 8, i574n.github 210 / 10.")
inl note_of_ps1_to_spiral_5 () = Note ("PS1-TO-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-19T12:37Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:37 helper-lane lap 1: recount 10-19 (git ls-files *.ps1, samples excluded, non-blank lines): spiral 50 files / 4,439 lines (35 / 3,360 on 10-14), eoie 20 / 1,806, polyglot 27 / 678, dice 13 / 431, x 9 / 358, alphabet 8 / 212, i574n.github 5 / 82, blog 5 / 64, dir-tree-html 4 / 53. Growth is concentrated in spiral's harness: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts 20 files, scripts 11, formatter 5. Gates unchanged: EOIE-ONE-CALL Planned 0%, PIPELINES-GREEN Active 35%. Since the item cannot start, the useful prep is choosing the first port by size and churn: the tracked tree's smallest self-contained scripts are the blog and dir-tree-html wrappers (4-5 files each, under 65 lines), which only chain init/build/publish.")
inl note_of_ps1_to_spiral_6 () = Note ("PS1-TO-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-19T13:23Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:23 helper-lane lap 2: sized the first port candidate against lib: dir-tree-html's scripts are build.ps1 19 lines, init.ps1 17, workflow.ps1 10, publish.ps1 7 (53 total). build.ps1 needs: run a process with cwd and env and a timeout (BuildSpiral, cargo build, the self-test exe) - lib runtime has execution_options + execute_with_options (Rust and F# arms); read cargo metadata JSON (lib json decode, now growing in CONVO-IN-SPIRAL); create a directory (file_system.create_dir_all), delete a file (file_delete), write text (write_all_text). Missing in lib file_system: copy a file, remove a directory tree, and file_exists as a plain path check (only directory_info_exists and path_buf_exists exist). So the port needs 2-3 small file_system primitives first (copy, remove_dir_all, exists over a string path); those are also the most common verbs across the 124 .ps1 files (Copy-Item, Remove-Item, Test-Path).")
inl note_of_ps1_to_spiral_7 () = Note ("PS1-TO-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:54Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:54 helper-lane lap 3: ranked the primitives over the tracked corpus (git ls-files *.ps1 in 9 repos: 142 files, raw occurrence counts): Join-Path 791, Invoke-Block 221, Test-Path 201, Write-Host 145, Write-Output 132, Set-Location 119, New-Item 100, Split-Path 93, Remove-Item 82, Get-ChildItem 72, Resolve-Path 59, Copy-Item 49, Get-Content 46, Get-Date 44, ConvertFrom-Json 33, Set-Content 23, Push/Pop-Location 18 each, Select-String 16, ConvertTo-Json 11, Start-Process 8, Move-Item 5. Mapped onto lib/spiral file_system.spi (47 functions, 18 tests): already there - path join, path_buf_exists/is_dir/is_file, create_dir_all, path_buf_parent/file_name, walk_dir, read_all_text/write_all_text, rename, file_delete; console covers Write-*, runtime the process side of Invoke-Block, json the Json pair. Missing, in corpus order: set/get current directory (Set-Location 119 + Push/Pop 36), remove_dir_all (most of Remove-Item 82 is -Recurse -Force), a one-level read_dir (Get-ChildItem 72 is mostly non-recursive), canonicalize (Resolve-Path 59), copy_file (Copy-Item 49). Those five are the first lib slice (Rust std::env::set_current_dir, fs::remove_dir_all, fs::read_dir, fs::canonicalize, fs::copy), each with a Rust test block.")
inl note_of_ps1_to_spiral_8 () = Note ("PS1-TO-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:36Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:36 helper-lane lap 4: drafted the five missing primitives in file_system.spi's own style (the !\\\\ emit operator with  and the !\\ expression form, resultm.result' over stream.io_error) as lab/PS1-TO-SPIRAL/file_system-addition.spi: remove_dir_all (std::fs::remove_dir_all), copy_file to path (std::fs::copy, returns bytes), canonicalize (std::fs::canonicalize to path_buf), set_current_dir and get_current_dir (std::env), and read_dir_names (one level, std::fs::read_dir mapped to file names as a vec of std strings). One rust test block covers four of them on a temp tree: create, write, copy (1 byte), list (2 names), canonicalize ok, remove_dir_all, directory_exists false. To append to lib file_system.spi after the core-switch freeze and run through its Cells gate; Rust arms only for now, other backends keep failing at their compilers as the existing rename/create_dir_all do.")
inl note_of_ps1_to_spiral_9 () = Note ("PS1-TO-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-19T22:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:22 helper-lane lap 6: the five file_system primitives are in lib. lib/spiral/file_system.spi gains remove_dir_all, copy_file, canonicalize, set_current_dir/get_current_dir and read_dir_names (uncommitted, additive; staged as lab/PS1-TO-SPIRAL/file_system-addition.spi). The gate (lab/PS1-TO-SPIRAL/file_system-run.ps1, file_system-20261019-1817.log) passes 18 of 18 tests with failed_targets empty, including the new rust test block on a temp tree, and the change was kept. With these, the ps1 helpers that only move, copy, list and delete files can be ported. Next: pick the first ps1 to port (one that uses only these plus run).")
inl note_of_ps1_to_spiral_10 () = Note ("PS1-TO-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-22T05:37Z", "main", "01:36 main: lab run outputs moved to the run cache, per the lab rule that run outputs never sit in lab: 2 file(s) (logs, notebooks, diffs, metrics/gate/timing TSVs) are now under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\PS1-TO-SPIRAL\\ at the same relative paths, sha256 checked. Scripts and probes stay in lab/PS1-TO-SPIRAL until the item lands.")
inl note_of_ps1_to_spiral_11 () = Note ("PS1-TO-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-22T06:47Z", "main", "02:47 main: lab drained. file_system-addition.spi is in lib/spiral/file_system.spi (remove_dir_all, copy_file, canonicalize, set_current_dir/get_current_dir, read_dir_names; its test block is in lib); file_system-run.ps1 drove that gate (18/18, log in the run cache). Folder deleted. Next as before: port the first ps1 that uses only these primitives plus run.")
inl note_of_ps1_to_spiral_12 () = Note ("PS1-TO-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): file_system-addition ready; landing needs a Cells gate (jobs held) -> next: gate then land | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: 5 primitives in lib -> next: port the first ps1 helper")
inl task_HOPAC_WARM_SERVER () = Task ("HOPAC-WARM-SERVER", "Make the hopac core stable as a long-lived warm server (fix 57: per-request join point state keyed per request) so harness and Kino stop paying ~10 s warm-up per process; prerequisite for Kino SOTA and for HOPAC-SPEED numbers; scoping 2026-10-08 (FRONTIER.md fix 57): warm --batch already compiles a suite 12x faster (186 s vs 2,295 s) with 1,054/1,089 rows unchanged; the ~35-100 diverging rows come from JP work items whose hard-deadline watchdog (left by a build the host abandoned) sets the process-wide terminal-failure latch inside a later build; point resets (latch, invalidation count, sequential flag, per-request watchdog ordinal) are done, tagging work items per request did not help; the remaining design is one per-BuildFile session object owning JP work items, cells, caches, deadlines and the latch, created per request and dropped after it; single-flight is already warm in the Kino daemon; 2026-10-08 12:3x DONE for the harness: host defers a failure from a process that already served a build (withholds the result, exit 4; the runner resumes that job first in a fresh process); -WarmRecycle is hopac default; same DLL -Suite all: 782 s vs 1,834 s fresh (2.35x), 2,014/2,014 rows identical (status, residual hash, verdict); 605 deferrals, 106 warm-only failures caught; remaining for Kino/fully warm (~12x): the per-BuildFile session object; 16:3x deployed hopac (CodegenC fixes + readable names) on the default warm-recycle path, -Suite all -Parallel 2: 2,024 jobs in 851 s, every oracle row in the parity class (no missing/REGRESSED), 53 parity-residual-differs = the 37 known + 14 rows of the three lib/spiral samples (variable numbering inside lib backend_switch code differs between the cores, not names); 2026-10-13 22:47 naming: the core this item calls hopac is now gpt5parallel (user decision; SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL, -Mode gpt5parallel, lanes/gpt5parallel); the id keeps HOPAC until eoie can rename ids (EOIE-AGILE-RENAME-ID); the planned rewrite is HOPACSOTA; closed 2026-10-13 23:34 as superseded by hopacsota (user decision): goal inherited by HOPACSOTA-P2 (local scheduler per compile, no per-build state leaking)", Epic, P1, D7, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,hopac,kino", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_hopac_warm_server () = Planning ("HOPAC-WARM-SERVER", Minutes 900u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl task_KINO_SOTA () = Task ("KINO-SOTA", "Kino to state of the art: warm compiler server, concurrent cells over a shared prefix with correct memoization (KINO-PARALLEL-CACHE), fast incremental re-run, rich outputs; acceptance test: SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM scenarios pass their invariants at scale; (history audit 2026-10-12: user 06:39 asked for Kino speed and memory benchmarks: \"i'm sure erlang is not that slow if we push that kino codebase to the limit, using gleam 1.19 with subjects or something\"); 2026-10-14 04:32 lane 1 step (uncommitted): incremental reuse when one definition of a shared prefix changes; measured first (private memo dir, private daemon, scratch copy of lib/spiral, Invoke-Heavy): with today's step memo, stream.spi cold 101 s, warm 16 s (16/16 hits), convert.spi warm 20 s, but appending one unused definition to stream.spi drops every stream test (0/16 hits, 132 s) and every convert test (0/2, 87 s) because the memo key carries the whole lib package print; added a second memo level keyed by the generated code (Memo.code_result: kino identity, explicit tool paths, builder, backend, generated text, show_value, print_code, build_only, test_cell, root; only {:ok, chunk} results kept) wrapped around the target build+run in Spiral.Kino run_builder (kino.ex run_compiled), so a source edit that leaves a test's generated code unchanged skips rustc/target build and run, while compile still runs; numbers with it: stream after the unused definition 16/16 code hits, rustc+run 0 ms (cold summed 218739 ms), wall 86 s vs 155 s cold on the same loaded machine; convert after the stream edit 2/2 code hits; a real change (take_while body starts with failwith) recompiled and ran exactly sum_2,sum_3,sum_4 (the 3 tests the definition impact analysis selects) and failed exactly those, 13 code hits; two tests whose generated code coincides (zip, zip_with) share one run even cold; remaining cost after such an edit is the per-test compile (about 91 s summed for 16 tests, 4 cells in parallel); tests: memo_test adds a code_result case (hit on equal code, miss on changed code, errors never kept, off when memo is off); in a private copy run from a sibling path memo_test + kino_test + notebook_test + notebook_report_test + document_test 99/99, then landed; next step: skip the compile too for tests whose reached definitions (ZED-DEFINITION-IMPACT analysis, or compiler-provided dependencies) did not change; 2026-10-14 05:59 lane 1 step 2 (uncommitted, landed after private-copy runs): the step memo key is now reach-based: Memo.reached_program keeps only the program blocks reachable from the entry (non-definition statements, main, instances) through the identifier/operator graph, and the package part of the key is the set of package definitions reachable from that program (definition-level index of every module: blocks with names, tokens, operators; instances and non-definition statements always count; identities are module + names + occurrence, so inserting code does not shift them) instead of whole-module prints; the last step of a run is keyed on the whole source and whole package (whole_source_key), so an edit that only breaks unreached code still compiles once and fails there; numbers (memo fresh per variant, private daemons, Invoke-Heavy, machine loaded by the accept and other lanes): stream.spi unused definition appended: code memo 50 s (0/16 step hits, 16 compiles) vs reach memo 13 s and 61 s on two runs (16/16 hits, 0 compiles); take_while changed: code memo 48 s (16 compiles) vs reach 36 s and 87 s (13/16 hits, compiled exactly sum_2,sum_3,sum_4 = the 3 that fail); am'.spi unused definition: code memo 91 s (10 compiles) vs reach 6 s and 51 s (10/10 hits); slice changed: code memo 61 s vs reach 57 s and 162 s, both recompile all 10 (the global name match over-approximates: slice is a common name across lib modules), failure only in slice; broken unreached definition inserted before the last test: stream 15/16 hits, only unzip compiled and failed with the parse error; am' 9/10, only to_list compiled and failed; soundness: in every mutation the recompiled set contains every failing test (never fewer); tests: memo_test 14 (reached vs unreached definitions, instances and // inside strings, package definitions, inserted code keeps ids, whole-source key), memo + kino + notebook + notebook_report + document 104/104 from a sibling path; precision left: name matching is global across the package (am' slice/init over-approximate); next: scope unqualified names to the program, opened (-) modules and modules opened with open, and qualified m.x to module m", Epic, P1, D8, 35u32, Paused, "KINO-PARALLEL-CACHE,QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER,KINO-RESULT-MEMO", "kino,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kino_sota () = Planning ("KINO-SOTA", Minutes 3600u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl note_of_kino_sota_1 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 1u32, "2026-10-14T13:03Z", "lane-b", "precision step located: memo.ex reached_package_print seeds by_name over every module of the package (roots = all identifiers in the program) and package_index drops the opened flag (for {file, _opened}); fix = key by_name per module, seed unqualified names only from opened (- / *) modules and 'open m' lines, and m.x tokens only from module m")
inl note_of_kino_sota_2 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 2u32, "2026-10-14T14:01Z", "lane-b", "scoping implemented in a private copy (memo.ex, not landed): package blocks carry their module scope; an unqualified name seeds only opened (- / *) modules plus modules named by 'open' in the code (a package block also sees its own module and its file's opens); m.x seeds only module m; memo_test 15/15 incl. a new case (unopened other.a edit keeps the key; open other, other.a and user.through via its open change it); real am'.spi mutation run waits for the gate")
inl note_of_kino_sota_3 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 3u32, "2026-10-14T18:48Z", "lane-b", "scoping landed (uncommitted, apps/kino memo.ex + memo_test.exs) after a real mutation run on a lib copy (am'.spi, 10 tests): cold 214 s; warm 10/10 hits (34 s); parsing.slice edited (a same-named def in a module am' does not open): 10/10 hits, 48 s (lane 1 measured 0/10 before); am'.slice broken: all 10 recompiled and failed with the package type error (no false hit); memo+notebook tests 54/54")
inl note_of_kino_sota_4 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 4u32, "2026-10-14T20:17Z", "lane-b", "soundness bug found and fixed (uncommitted memo.ex + memo_test): a test body without a trailing assertion is wrapped as 'inl _ = spiral_kino_test ()', which the reach memo read as a definition named _, so the body never entered the key and two such tests with the same prefix shared one result (seen: polyglot Tasks.spi 'lines' recalled 'cols' result, same 34,353 ms); 'inl _'/'inl ()' are now statements (roots); new test red before, 55/55 after")
inl note_of_kino_sota_5 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 5u32, "2026-10-15T06:36Z", "lane-b", "02:36 lane B: reach memo now takes block boundaries from the compiler (SourceBlocks, one batched --source-blocks call per run, pack-1g); re-ran the lane-1 mutation set on a private lib copy (am'.spi, 10 tests, installed 1g DLL, fresh memo): cold 148 s (214 s on 10-14 with heuristics), warm 10/10 hits 23 s (34 s), parsing.slice edited in a module am' does not open 10/10 hits 37 s (48 s), am' slice broken: 0 hits, all 10 recompiled and failed (no false hit); soundness and precision unchanged by the switch")
inl note_of_kino_sota_6 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 6u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: warm-compiler part is in (Kino runs --server-tcp compilers only; relay removed; reach memo takes boundaries from --source-blocks). Remaining big pieces: concurrent cells over a shared prefix (KINO-PARALLEL-CACHE) and QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER, now P1.")
inl note_of_kino_sota_7 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 7u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: user 21:05: Kino must be battle-tested to the fullest run: granular Spiral like lib but better, and generated target code split per unit so it compiles in parallel. History: the gpt5parallel-era F# splitter went unused (weeks of dead code) and the gear method did not make dotnet compile in parallel; eoie's crates are the working example. Rewriting the compiler through Kino under X's 280-char limit will be hard.")
inl note_of_kino_sota_8 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 8u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: memo soundness fix: editing only a test fails text used to replay a stale pass from the memo; the expectation is now part of the key (TEST-FAILS-SPECIFIC).")
inl note_of_kino_sota_9 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 9u32, "2026-10-17T11:29Z", "main-thread", "07:29 main-thread day: memo cache stability (apps/kino/lib/spiral/kino/memo.ex, tree). (1) Soundness: compiler identity was {path,size,mtime} of the 278 KB host SpiralCompiler.dll only; a core-only rebuild (11.5 MB SpiralCompilerCore.dll) would have replayed stale results. Now sha256 content of every SpiralCompiler*.dll beside the configured one, path-free. (2) Stability: source-block bounds (one dotnet --source-blocks per run, 1.3-1.7 s, a failure flipped the run to the whole-source key family) are stored in the memo dir keyed by (binaries, text). memo_test +3, 23/23, mutants killed. lib/spiral/sm'.spi memo on: cold 509 s, then 15 s and 13 s with 37/37 hits. Builds are not deterministic across cache folders, so a rebuild elsewhere re-keys (sound, costs one cold run).")
inl note_of_kino_sota_10 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 10u32, "2026-10-17T11:48Z", "main-thread", "07:48 main-thread day: per-target cost from sm' ipynb metadata (cost-report -Notebooks): gleam 27 targets 8.6 min (median 19 s), fsharp 32 / 8.1 min (15 s), cuda 27 / 5.0 min. Gleam: every cell built a fresh package (gleam_stdlib + 5 deps recompiled, 7.2 s); now up to 4 warm packages per manifest under <cache>/kino-gleam-warm with only src replaced (FileLock try-lock, all busy -> cold package): cell 9.7 s cold -> 2.9-3.1 s warm (targets_test +1). Also fixed a latent Path.wildcard-on-backslash bug in run_gleam_program. F#: cold 'dotnet fsi --exec' floor is 4.6-5.5 s of the 15 s median (rest is type-checking generated F#); a warm fsi would leak state across cells and runs against the no-interactive direction, so not built - the lever is moving F# targets to a compiled path or dropping them from default target sets.")
inl note_of_kino_sota_11 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 11u32, "2026-10-17T13:38Z", "main-thread", "09:38 main-thread day: memo cache stable end to end on the rc.2 compiler (lib/spiral/sm'.spi, 38 tests). A new compiler gives a cold run with 0 hits: 225 s, against 509 s this morning before the warm Gleam packages (Gleam median 19.2 s -> 5.6 s). The warm rerun hits 38/38 in 9 s. With SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL pointing at a byte-identical build from another cache folder it still hits 38/38 in 7 s, because the key follows compiler content now that builds are reproducible (MATRIX-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL).")
inl note_of_kino_sota_12 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 12u32, "2026-10-17T16:33Z", "main-thread", "12:33 main-thread day: the first --slowest reading of the Kino suite (167 s, 245 tests) put one test at a quarter of it: RunnerTest 'timeout kills the whole process tree' at 40.7 s, because it always waited for a fixed 40 s timeout. It now escalates the budget per attempt (8 s, then 20 s, then 40 s) and keeps its retry on a timeout that fires before the grandchild runs: 8.7 s here, passing on the first attempt, and the same 40 s ceiling under load. The next slowest are inherent (FsiSession budget kill 9.8 s is mostly dotnet fsi startup; the gleam lock and warm-package tests 8.2 and 6.5 s run real gleam builds).")
inl note_of_kino_sota_13 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 13u32, "2026-10-17T16:44Z", "main-thread", "12:44 main-thread: the Kino cache is already two-level, as the harness now is. Cell level: key = compiler binaries + reached program + bounds, so a compiler change misses. Code level: Memo.code_result, key = Kino code identity + tool options + generated code, with no compiler identity, so a compiler change that leaves a cell's generated code unchanged still replays its target run. Measured on lib/spiral/sm'.spi (38 tests): fully cold 509 s; Kino code changed (both levels miss, plus the warm Gleam packages) 225 s; compiler changed with Kino unchanged (cell misses, code hits) 83 s; nothing changed 7-9 s (38/38 cell hits); a byte-identical compiler built in another folder 7 s (38/38). Run 4's 0 code hits came from my own Kino edits that morning, not from the compiler change.")
inl note_of_kino_sota_14 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 14u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 from the bundles (samples alpha705, brzozowski alpha54): a long-lived warm compiler session went stale and made byte-identical megas compile in 8-24 s instead of 2-4 s until idle sessions were cleaned up (--cleanup-idle --idle-ms 1); a 13-min-old session produced a false CIC 'cliff'; a stale fixed socket (/tmp/...verifier.sock) attached a new gate to an older daemon. For the warm Kino server: give each session an identity bound to the compiler/runtime fingerprint, reap stale sessions, avoid fixed global sockets, record session age in each result, and treat rc5 or timing anomalies after a long-lived session as suspect until a fresh session reproduces them (a GADT probe showed fake rc5 type errors that disappeared after shutdown).")
inl note_of_kino_sota_15 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 15u32, "2026-10-17T20:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-17 16:28 suite time is the main drag for an app with many simulation tests: one 27-test file takes 6-7 minutes memo-off, and every model change needs a full memo-off gate because the memo was unsafe across modules for a while; parallel cells and a warm server matter most.")
inl note_of_kino_sota_16 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 16u32, "2026-10-19T12:20Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:20 lap 1 grain: verified the 10-14 next step is in: memo.ex resolves unqualified names by opened scope and qualified m.x by module (by_scope/visible/scope_named?, memo.ex ~370-387), plus reached_program drops unreached definitions; remaining epic deps: KINO-PARALLEL-CACHE (70%), QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER (0%), KINO-RESULT-MEMO Next: measure the tibia lane's 27-test file memo-on vs memo-off now that scoping is in (their 6-7 min complaint)")
inl note_of_kino_sota_17 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 17u32, "2026-10-19T14:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:22 lap 2 grain: today's relevant measurements: a cold lib-backed compile costs 5.7-6.0 s of which about 4.3 s is lib parse/typecheck (QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER), and the Cells sm' gate (146 compiles) took 537 s cold; HEAVY QUEUE 2 runs the same gate on the warm compiler daemon to size the win for gates, the epic's warm-server leg Next: read the warm-gate A/B and, if the results match, make the daemon the gate default (CELLS-GATE)")
inl note_of_kino_sota_18 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 18u32, "2026-10-19T18:15Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:15 lap 4 grain: warm-server leg verified in code: Cells already defaults to the warm compiler daemon, cells.ex:747 and notebook.ex:729 and mix spiral.notebook (spiral.notebook.ex:21) take the daemon unless SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process or SPIRAL_CELLS_WARM=0 (probe-lib only sets the env explicitly); the measured win is 217 s warm vs 537 s cold on the sm gate with identical results, so that leg is done; left: KINO-PARALLEL-CACHE, QUERY-CACHE-COMPILER (the 4.3 s lib parse floor per cold compile), KINO-RESULT-MEMO Next: pick the cheapest remaining dep: read KINO-RESULT-MEMO state and its gate")
inl note_of_kino_sota_19 () = Note ("KINO-SOTA", 19u32, "2026-10-24T02:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main grooming: Paused by rule (Active, no note since 2026-10-19T18:15Z, no child item noted in 3 days). Resume when picked; progress kept at 35%.")
inl task_KINO_SPIRAL_GLEAM () = Task ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", "Experiment: Kino logic written in Spiral compiled to Gleam (BEAM), called from a thin Elixir/Livebook shim; stage two replaces Livebook runtime pieces with our own runner to drop Elixir; style reference: polyglot examples/elixir exercism multi-clause functions; user decision 2026-10-12 19:09: after the warm runner: land KINO-WARM-RUNNER in Elixir first, then port module by module to Spiral/Gleam with the existing tests as the oracle", Feature, P1, D8, 10u32, Planned, "KINO-WARM-RUNNER,KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR", "kino,gleam,elixir,main,lab-held", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kino_spiral_gleam () = Planning ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", Minutes 2400u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kino_spiral_gleam_1 () = Note ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:58Z", "lane-b", "baseline 2026-10-14: Kino domain already Spiral->Gleam (spi/*.spi 1,458 lines, 15 exports via domain.ex, generated domain.gleam 1.1 MB) vs 7,565 lines of Elixir (notebook 973, kino 928, compiler_daemon 884, targets 597); today's panic-id fix went into cell.spi (domain) not Elixir; next port candidate: builders.ex (396, pure argv/flag tables)")
inl note_of_kino_spiral_gleam_2 () = Note ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", 2u32, "2026-10-14T18:08Z", "lane-b", "first port step (private copy, not landed): new domain module spi/targets.spi (typed target union, backend and extension tables) exported to Elixir; Builders.backend/ext now read it (Elixir -39 lines); builders_test parity case over all 13 targets; full Kino suite 226/227, the one failure (a memo recall test) passes alone 4/4, rerun after the install freeze before landing")
inl note_of_kino_spiral_gleam_3 () = Note ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", 3u32, "2026-10-14T18:34Z", "lane-b", "landed (uncommitted): spi/targets.spi + Builders.backend/ext via the domain; suite 227/227 (the earlier single memo-test failure did not recur)")
inl note_of_kino_spiral_gleam_4 () = Note ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:49Z", "main-thread", "06:49 groom: step 1 (spi/targets.spi + Builders via the domain) is committed (tracked, clean since 570be6a6). Next grain: move the next Elixir table (toolchain resolution) into targets.spi.")
inl note_of_kino_spiral_gleam_5 () = Note ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", 5u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night data point: the Spiral port of toolchain.ex resolution (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR) runs its model tests on gleam (BEAM) and rust, and the real PATH probe on rust and fsharp; file_system.file_exists now has a Gleam arm (erlang filelib is_regular, tested), so the real PATH probe also runs on gleam and resolves the machine python.exe and rustc.exe on BEAM (4/4). The (</>) default arm, F# interpolation text that was invalid in Gleam and Zig, is now a plain Spiral join (tested).")
inl note_of_kino_spiral_gleam_6 () = Note ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:38 helper-lane lap 1: measured the split today: Cells' Spiral domain (apps/cells/spi) is 1,674 non-blank lines in 7 files (document 573, cell 374, text 313, notebook 250, directives 82, targets 62, main 20), compiled to Gleam for BEAM, while the Elixir side is 8,247 lines in 37 .ex files, led by notebook.ex 1,041, cells.ex 939, compiler_daemon.ex 898, targets.ex 696, compiler_client.ex 549, memo.ex 463. So about 17 percent of Cells logic is Spiral. The toolchain-resolution port named as the next grain lives in lab/WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB/toolchain.spi (main's WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB, queued Cells gate), not yet in apps/cells/spi; targets.ex (696 lines) is the natural next table once it lands. Deps unchanged: KINO-WARM-RUNNER Done, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR Active 10%.")
inl note_of_kino_spiral_gleam_7 () = Note ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", 7u32, "2026-10-19T13:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:24 helper-lane lap 2: listed targets.ex's pure, side-effect-free parts as the next Spiral move (apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/targets.ex, 696 lines): data tables @gleam_deps (line 341), @c_flags (774), @wgsl_no_adapter (487), @gleam_warm_slots (363), @spiral_rust_wait_ms (133), @cupy_missing_cuda_path_warning (716); pure functions gleam_manifest (manifest text from a dep list), c_args (flags + shim + output), zig_run_args, exe_name, host_extension, host_key, format_ms, strip_gleam_warnings / gleam_warning_line, without_cupy_cuda_path_warning, normalize, shown_output, decode_result / result_map_line. Everything else (run_*, gleam_build, invoke, tool_env, tmp_dir) does IO and stays in Elixir until KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR moves the process layer. These pure pieces are text-in/text-out, so they fit the existing spi/targets.spi domain and its Gleam compile path directly.")
inl note_of_kino_spiral_gleam_8 () = Note ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", 8u32, "2026-10-19T14:55Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:55 helper-lane lap 3: first pure port written and checked against the Elixir text: lab/KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM/manifest.spi carries targets.ex @gleam_deps (line 341, the 6 pinned deps) as a deps union table and gleam_manifest (line 350) as Spiral: name main or main_real, target line, a blank line, [dependencies], the table in order, then the extra deps the table does not already list. Probe via try.ps1 C (bounded, 5 s): both expectations hold (exit 11 = plain manifest byte-equal to the Elixir output for no extras, and the main_real manifest with a duplicate gary dropped and exception appended). Two pieces stay in Elixir until KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR: choosing main_real from the path's basename (the port takes a bool) and cleaning extra versions (backslash removal and quote trimming happen where the cell flags are parsed). Not yet in apps/cells/spi/targets.spi (helper lane 2 edits Cells; lands with a gleam test block in one Cells gate).")
inl note_of_kino_spiral_gleam_9 () = Note ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:57 helper-lane lap 5: staged the move for lane 2: lab/KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM/targets-addition.spi is the gleam_manifest port as an appendix to apps/cells/spi/targets.spi. A Spiral test block does not fit there - Cells' spi package (packages |core- only; text, cell, directives, document, notebook, targets, main) has no testing module, and its logic is tested from ExUnit through the Gleam-compiled module - so the check is a differential ExUnit case instead (lab/KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM/differential_test.exs): for a plain and a main_real path with extras, :spiral_cells@targets.gleam_manifest must equal Spiral.Cells.Targets.gleam_manifest, which also keeps the Elixir original as the oracle until it is deleted. One API change needed when moving: extras as list (string * string) instead of the lab's deps union, so Elixir can pass [{name, version}] tuples straight into the Gleam function.")
inl note_of_kino_spiral_gleam_10 () = Note ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", 10u32, "2026-10-22T07:59Z", "main", "03:59 main: review of the staged port (not run; no Cells job tonight). The lab file already types extras as list (string * string), but that does NOT make Elixir's [{name, version}] usable. In Cells' generated src/spiral_cells/domain.gleam, Spiral recursive unions are Gleam custom types (Uh0NoLine / Uh0Line(String, Uh0), ...), so a Spiral 'list' arrives as a Nil/Cons custom type, not a native Gleam List. The differential test would hit a badmatch at the first call. The boundary must take a native list, as sm'.split does: 'listm'.list' (string * string)' (a Gleam List, convertible with listm'.to_array'), turned into the Spiral list inside. Spiral pairs are Gleam tuples (#(a, b)), which Elixir {a, b} already is. Also: the test calls :spiral_cells@domain.gleam_manifest, which matches the generated module name (domain.gleam builds from the spi package), not targets. With those two fixes it can join a Cells pack; the Elixir Targets.gleam_manifest (targets.ex:351) stays the oracle until the port passes.")
inl note_of_kino_spiral_gleam_11 () = Note ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", 11u32, "2026-10-22T08:00Z", "main", "04:00 main: addendum. lib has the conversion (listm'.unbox : list' t -> list t, listm'.spi:639), but Cells' spi package is core-only (packages |core-), so it cannot use it. The boundary needs either a core-level native-list fold or a 3-line Gleam-side wrapper in the exported module, which takes List(#(String, String)) and folds it into the Spiral list. Pick one when the port moves.")
inl note_of_kino_spiral_gleam_12 () = Note ("KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM", 12u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:36 (subagent): blocked: moving manifest.spi into spi/targets.spi needs lane 2 coordination and a Cells gate (bounded jobs frozen) -> next: after the all-clear: stage the targets.spi addition + gleam test block, queue the gate | lap 6 18:26 (subagent): re-read: targets-addition.spi + differential_test.exs staged; both touch apps/cells -> next: lane 2 applies, then mix test the differential case | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: targets-addition + differential test staged; Cells is helper lane 1's -> next: Cells window")
inl task_GLEAM_UPGRADE () = Task ("GLEAM-UPGRADE", "Gleam 1.14.0 -> 1.19.1 (emits Erlang abstract forms instead of .erl source): local done via scoop, Gleam probe rerun, then the CI gleam-version pins in spiral/polyglot/dice/i574n.github (already 1.19.1 in each gh-pages.yml: spiral 59d77c99, polyglot 525c5f44, dice bd4f5c2, i574n.github 2d04303, committed 2026-10-08); done 2026-10-11: local toolchain verified (gleam 1.19.1, OTP 29), all four gh-pages.yml pins still read 1.19.1 (spiral:28, polyglot:28, dice:31, i574n.github:31), and the probe rerun is the Gleam examples ladder single-flight-20261011-174150 / hopac-20261011-174720 / restore single-flight-20261011-175313: 127 Gleam rows, 126 agree plus the one Known, DISAGREE 0, hopac missing 0, script exit 0; the GitHub CI run itself lands with the next push", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "gleam,toolchain", "current", "current")
inl fsx_free () = Task ("FSX-FREE", "Iterate until spiral lib/spiral/*.fsx (common, sm, crypto, date_time, async_, threading, networking, platform, runtime, file_system, trace, lib) are no longer needed (user 2026-10-08): dice lib.ps1 GetFsxModulePaths/PublishFsharp, polyglot lib/fsharp/Notebooks.livemd and apps/parser #!import; restored byte-exact after the 98492a63 deletion broke dice+polyglot CI; 2026-10-08 08:2x: replaced by hand-written lib/spiral/spiral_runtime.fs (Lib facades used by consumers + new_disposable/retry_fn), trace output byte-identical to the generated stack; dice lib publishes dice.fsx alone, dice lib/fsharp + polyglot Notebooks.livemd use the runtime; verified dice both F# paths, polyglot apps/parser and lib/fsharp 6/6 with the 13 .fsx deleted; confirm on the next CI push; 18:4x re-verified locally: lib/spiral holds no .fsx at all (0 files) and spiral_runtime.fs is 8,512 bytes, the only .fsx left under lib being 113 generated bench/parsing/target files with none outside target; the CI confirmation lands with the next push", Feature, P0, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "fsharp,ci,spiral,polyglot,dice", "current", "current")
inl ci_spiral_wasm_native () = Task ("CI-SPIRAL-WASM-NATIVE", "spiral Windows CI 07:03: apps/wasm built through bash/WSL (cargo: command not found); now native on every platform like apps/spiral: near-workspaces default-features=false (its build.rs install panicked Unsupported platform), sandbox fetched at runtime; RUST-OK apps/wasm locally on Windows; confirm on next push; local preflight of the steps after wasm on Windows: apps/plot RUST-OK (native), scripts/outdated.ps1 exit 0; polyglot CI path (spiral app, parser, lib/fsharp 6/6, wasm, lib/math has no F# cells) checked; lib/spiral/build.ps1 step: the two notebooks that failed yesterday (file_system, parsing) now exit 0 when run alone (outputs to tmp), the other 36 passed yesterday; 20:0x CI on the 18:34 push: dice windows-2025 still red here: contract build runs process_rust wasm through SPIRAL_WASM_COMMAND with -Linux (bash on the Windows runner) from D:\\a\\dice\\spiral, `workspace/target/release/spiral_wasm: No such file` (exit 127), while dice scripts/init.ps1 builds and ships spiral_wasm.exe under polyglot/deps/spiral/workspace; needs the native (non-Linux) invocation with the .exe from the same checkout; 21:5x decided: dice windows contract run stays on the WSL path (spiral_wasm deploys into near_workspaces.sandbox_worker, Linux-only), so it is WSL work and waits for the WSL-last phase; no Windows skip switch; 03:55 local no-WSL repro: workspace/target/release/spiral_wasm is an ELF leftover (2026-10-07) beside spiral_wasm.exe (PE, 2026-10-08). Native exe --help exits 0. exe -w a file -t debug retries 15 times with Could not initialize sandbox node (near-workspaces 0.11.1 InitFailure) then exits 0, because spiral_wasm.run turns an exhausted Error into Ok and main returns 0. near-sandbox-utils platform() is None on Windows through 0.15.1 (linux x86 and darwin arm only). The GitHub windows runner has no ELF, so bash -Linux reports the unsuffixed path missing. Pointing that job at the exe would not run the sandbox; 2026-10-13 23:57 sweep lane: spiral Windows CI builds apps/wasm natively: run 38327352498 windows job 115069009880 printed RUST-OK apps/wasm, shipped spiral_wasm.exe to workspace/target/release, then RUST-OK apps/plot (the job was later cancelled only because ubuntu failed first under fail-fast); run 38389311114 windows reached the native cargo build of spiral_wasm before the same cancel; dice windows contract stays WSL-last (WORKFLOW-END-TO-END); open gap for that phase: spiral_wasm exits 0 when the NEAR sandbox never initializes (run turns an exhausted Error into Ok), a silent false pass; 2026-10-14 00:22 closing on run 38327352498 (windows job 115069009880: RUST-OK apps/wasm natively, spiral_wasm.exe shipped, RUST-OK apps/plot); the newest run 38508233036 (d94dc380) never reached it on windows (cancelled by fail-fast after ubuntu failed in the apps/wasm Kino step, see CI-AFTER-SPI-MIGRATION); the spiral_wasm exit-0-on-sandbox-failure false pass stays open for the WSL-last dice contract work (WORKFLOW-END-TO-END)", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "ci,spiral,wasm,windows", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ci_spiral_wasm_native () = Planning ("CI-SPIRAL-WASM-NATIVE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Pair)
inl wasm_backend () = Task ("WASM-BACKEND", "WebAssembly as a full Spiral backend: codegen in spiral_compiler.fs, a harness backend list, a native tier with a run check, C oracle parity on the examples suite (wasm module run by a local runtime); 22:4x codegenWasm (section CodegenWasm, --backend Wasm / .wat): WAT text run by wasmtime (WASI fd_write/proc_exit, -W max-wasm-stack=1 GB like the other natives); scalars on the four value types with sub-word narrowing, unsigned ops, conversions incl. trunc_sat; join points as funcs, self tail calls as loop/br, while as block/loop; unions/heap/mutable layouts/arrays as i32 pointers into linear memory (bump allocator, tag + 8-byte slots), closures as [table index][captures] called through call_indirect over a funcref table; strings [len][utf-8] with concat/eq/slice (exit 3 on bad bounds or a cut UTF-8 sequence); portable Printf %s/%d; failwith -> stderr + exit 1; lib backend.spi/console/math gain Wasm arms; harness: Wasm list, native tier, wasmtime tool; exploration over the 144 Zig samples: 135 agree with C (from 62 in v1), 3 excluded (C-syntax macros), Log/Pow pending; 23:43 blessed: full review + -Bless + repro single-flight-20261008-231513 vs -232952, 2179 rows, 0 differences, DISAGREE 0; 143 Wasm rows in the oracle; the 29 residual changes elsewhere were proven renumbering only (BackendSwitch now evaluates the Wasm arms too); next: convert Wasm arms ($parse_i64 strtol-like, $integer_text_ok strict), then Log/Pow/atan2 (no WASM instruction: software or WASI-free math); 00:0x convert arms (strtol-like $parse_i64, strict $integer_text_ok) and software math ($math_exp/log/sin/cos/tanh/pow/atan2: range reduction + series, worst relative error 3.5e-16 vs .NET libm, identities exact) make portable_convert_parse, portable_math_rounding, native_float_math_family and native_float_pow_pi agree; the prelude is tree-shaken by call $name reachability (average .wat 7 KB); lib math floor/ceil/round use typed macros (`t.floor); Wasm list 147, all agree with C (zig_lib_console with its Zig row, lua_while_union_pair agree-fail); full bless cycle running; 00:53 blessed into the main oracle (an earlier cycle had silently blessed the dev cache: host-build-dev*.ps1 leaked SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR into the chain): single-flight-20261009-002903 vs -004217, 2183 rows, 0 differences, DISAGREE 0; the 161 changed rows were 143 Wasm (tree-shaken prelude) + 18 renumbered rows of portable_convert_parse/portable_math_rounding (their lib modules gained Wasm arms), natives agree; 147 Wasm rows in the oracle; 00:5x coverage sweep over the 56 other samples with a C native row: none portable as written (29 have their own BackendSwitch records without a Wasm key, 21 call C-only helpers, 3 C-syntax macros, 3 not run), so the Wasm list stays 147 = every sample whose code is portable; temporary entries and 24 stray .wat reverted; 07:48 closed (main, autonomous): the Wasm list covers every sample whose code is portable (147); the remaining samples need Wasm arms in their own BackendSwitch records or drop C-only helpers, tracked by PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES / BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS; 2026-10-14 02:57 lane 3: lua_while_union_pair Wasm exit 1 (oracle agree-fail) is not a codegen error: the module computes 235 and wasmtime 39 proc_exit rejects statuses of 126 and above ('exit with invalid exit status outside of [0..126)'); keep fixture results below 126 or let the Wasm runner print the value; the agree-fail rule that hid it is SAMPLE-READMES H1", Feature, P1, D6, 100u32, Done, "", "backend,compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_wasm_backend () = Planning ("WASM-BACKEND", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl wgsl_backend () = Task ("WGSL-BACKEND", "WGSL as a full Spiral backend: codegen in spiral_compiler.fs, a matrix backend list, a native tier with a run check, C oracle parity on the examples suite (compute shaders run on a local WebGPU runtime; lowering for no recursion and no function pointers); 2026-10-10: codegenWgsl (--backend Wgsl / .wgsl) and spiral-wgsl (wgpu 24, tests/native-shims/wgsl-run). The shader writes main's i32 into storage SpiralIo.code; the runner exits with that code and prints nothing. No adapter is exit 3 (no-toolchain); validation is exit 2 (build-fail). Scalars i32/u32/f32/bool, char as i32, flattened tuple structs, if/while, methods, self-tail as loop/continue, shift counts forced to u32, macros emitted as text (Wgsl arm of set' is !a = !b). Matrix 20 samples. single-flight-20261010-132430: 20 Wgsl rows agree with C, DISAGREE 0. hopac-20261010-133655: all 20 Wgsl rows, including tuples, agree with C, DISAGREE 0. Out of v1: f64, i64/u64, sub-word integers, unions, arrays, strings, closures, heap refs, globals, failwith, non-self-tail recursion, BackendSwitch records without a Wgsl key", Feature, P0, D7, 85u32, Active, "WGSL-NULLARY-UNIONS,WGSL-PAYLOAD-UNIONS,WGSL-STORAGE-ARRAYS", "backend,compiler,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_wgsl_backend () = Planning ("WGSL-BACKEND", Minutes 1200u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
