inl openqasm3_backend () = Task ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", "OpenQASM 3 as a full Spiral backend: codegen in spiral_compiler.fs, a matrix backend list, a native tier with a run check, C oracle parity on the examples suite (classical subset first, run on a local simulator); 2026-10-10: codegenOpenQasm (--backend OpenQasm / .qasm). OPENQASM 3.0 classical subset, output int[32] spiral_io, empty stdout. Simulator is tests/native-shims/openqasm-run/run.py (32-bit wrap, toward-zero div and mod, arithmetic signed shift, logical unsigned shift, float[32] rounded per op). Scalars int[32]/uint[32]/float[32]/bool, char as int[32]. Tuples are a deliberate extension beyond standard OpenQASM 3 (no tuple or multi-value type) and the local simulator accepts them: a tuple domain flattens to one parameter per element and a tuple return flattens to `-> (T1, T2, ...)` with `v0, v1 = f(...)` at the call (a nested tuple flattens recursively; a tuple-returning self-tail's dead return is `return 0, 0, ...;`). Matrix 21 samples (the 16 non-tuple names plus tuple_pair, ebpf_nest3, ebpf_tacc, ebpf_t2, ebpf_t3). single-flight-20261010-145423: 16 rows agree with C, DISAGREE 0 (backend_smoke exits 13, native_bitwise_scalar exits 42). hopac-20261010-150227: the same 16 rows agree with C, DISAGREE 0. single-flight-20261010-150322 (rerun after hopac rewrote the samples): agree 16, DISAGREE 0. 2026-10-11 tuples: single-flight-20261011-125450, hopac-20261011-130419, and restore single-flight-20261011-130704 each ran -Backend C,OpenQasm -Suite examples -Native: 504 jobs, 10 build-fail, 21 OpenQasm rows agree with C, DISAGREE 0, hopac missing 0, broken 0, exit 0; the restore run reproduces single-flight-20261011-125450 row for row (0 of 504 rows differ). Out of v1: f64, i64/u64, NaN/infinity, strings, unions, arrays, closures, heap refs, globals, failwith, non-self-tail recursion.; 2026-10-14 00:09 samples/native_tail_recursion's OpenQasm native run takes 41.3 s in the Python simulator on a quiet machine (single-flight-20261013-225859) and timed out at 60 s under suite load (gpt5parallel-20261013-231516, NATIVE-DIFF with the identical residual 1e6714c8f9c3ca7f): the run-qasm interpreter is too slow for deep tail recursion; a faster simulator (compiled, or loop-aware) or a per-sample timeout is needed so the row is not load-dependent; 2026-10-14 01:55 run.py now compiles the AST once into Python closures with static kinds (types checked at compile time, constants folded, i32/u32 add/sub wrap inlined): self-test million 45.7 s -> 9.4 s and native_tail_recursion 67.7 s -> 14.7 s under load; tuple pass-through returns fixed (ebpf_t3/ebpf_tacc); the 21 OpenQasm rows agree with C in rung 1; 2026-10-14 05:09 run.py differential fixes applied (lane 3: 6 divergence classes vs HEAD, 2 silent wrong values; fixed copy matches HEAD on 21 samples and 8,000 generated programs), self-test 8.97 s; accepted in single-flight-20261014-043715 (OpenQasm rows identical to rung 1)", Feature, P1, D8, 65u32, Active, "", "backend,compiler,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_openqasm3_backend () = Planning ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", Minutes 900u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_1 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:40Z", "main-thread", "08:40 single-flight-20261014-073517: OpenQasm 21 rows, all compile, run and agree with C (run.py closure-compiled, ~5x faster since last night). Coverage is the gap (21 vs 137+ on Lua/Lean); the 16-sample scalar core plus 5 tuple samples have held since 10-11. Next slice: the remaining Ebpf v1 scalar samples (51 agree on Ebpf, same scalar subset), added to the OpenQasm list through -Probe.")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_2 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 2u32, "2026-10-14T13:06Z", "main-thread", "09:06 int[64]/uint[64] added. run.py: per-width wrap (as_i64/as_u64), 64-bit shifts mask the count with 63 and accept a narrower count, mixing widths is an error; checked on all 21 current .qasm outputs, which give the same exits as the morning run, and on a hand-written 64-bit program that matches gcc -fwrapv on 8 checks (INT64 overflow, u64 wrap, arithmetic and logical shifts, toward-zero div/rem, a 2^32 multiply, narrowing). codegenOpenQasm (source, next pack): int[64]/uint[64], 64-bit literals, and one integral conversion rule emitting the old text for 32-bit.")
