inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_8 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 8u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: the 06:48 next-grain note is stale (f64 shipped in pack-1k); review 232243: 31 of 31 OpenQasm rows agree with C. Next gaps: NaN and infinity, strings, unions, arrays.")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_9 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 9u32, "2026-10-19T12:34Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:34 lap 1 grain: today's pack 5 review: OpenQasm 31 rows agree with C, 0 disagree (same 31 as 10-17); next gaps unchanged: NaN and infinity, strings, unions, arrays Next: pick NaN/infinity first: the simulator is Python, so float('nan') and inf exist; count C-list samples that only need them")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_10 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 10u32, "2026-10-19T13:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:29 lap 2 grain, re-ranks the next gap: static scan of the C-native single-file samples not on the OpenQasm list (31 entries): NaN/infinity gates only 3 samples, and only 1 of them alone (native_float_infinity; native_float_nan_is and ts_float_nan also need BackendSwitch arms), so NaN/inf is worth +1 row. Far larger and already inside the documented subset: 21 ebpf_* samples are absent from the OpenQasm list (ebpf_arith, bacc, bits, bool, cast, char, conv, div, facc, fdiv, float, flt, ineq, neq, sh31, shift, snot, tworec, uacc, uadd, umul; the OpenQasm list holds only ebpf_nest3, tacc, t2, t3), all int32/uint32/float/bool with self-tail loops like the Ebpf v1 set, plus frontier_hello, zig_exit_wide, ts_int_wrap, native_float_math_family (sqrt/exp/log/sin/cos/pow, supported since 10-15), record_value, wgsl_record_loop, typecheck_long_let_body. HEAVY QUEUE (main, matrix list edit): add those 28 to the OpenQasm list and run test.ps1 -Mode single-flight -Backend C,OpenQasm -Suite examples -Native (no accept); can share one run with the CALYX-BACKEND row (-Backend C,Calyx,OpenQasm). Next: after the run, fix the first failing construct (likely char equality or shift edge cases), then NaN/inf for the +1")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_11 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 11u32, "2026-10-19T14:06Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:06 lap 3 grain: codegenOpenQasm's refusals (spiral_compiler.fs 171800-172042) are NaN, infinity, non-primitive ops, closures, while in value position, unsupported conversions, failwith, macro type literals, globals and NanIs; there is no width or tuple refusal (tuples flatten, 64-bit maps to int[64]). None of the 28 queued candidates uses any of those (the static scan excluded macros, strings, unions, arrays and closures), so all 28 should compile; the behaviour risks are in the simulator: float[32] rounding after every op for ebpf_float/fdiv/flt/facc, shift counts keeping 5 bits for ebpf_sh31/shift, and char as int[32] equality for ebpf_char. NaN/inf (+1 row, native_float_infinity) needs a simulator literal plus lifting the two LitFloat refusals at 171830-171837. Next: read the queued run; for any DISAGREE compare against those three simulator rules first")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_12 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 12u32, "2026-10-19T14:54Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:54 lap 5 grain: NaN and infinity prepared end to end. OpenQASM 3 has no inf/nan literals, so lab/OPENQASM3-BACKEND/patch.py (2 anchored edits to codegenOpenQasm, unique 8-line and 3-line anchors because the eBPF section repeats the same refusal lines) emits them as constant expressions the simulator evaluates in Python floats: +inf = (1e308 * 10.0), -inf = (-(1e308 * 10.0)), NaN = ((1e308 * 10.0) - (1e308 * 10.0)) for f32 and f64 literals, and NanIs a = (a != a). Simulator check with a hand-written program in the emitted shape (float[32]/float[64] inf and -inf, a NaN, finite != finite, nan != nan, the same comparisons as samples/native_float_infinity): exit 0, i.e. the float[32] rounding keeps inf and != reports NaN. The patch stacks with the EBPF-C-PROFILE and GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS patches on one copy (no overlap). HEAVY QUEUE: ride pack 7 with the other codegen patches, and add samples/native_float_infinity (plus native_float_nan_is and ts_float_nan if their BackendSwitch arms allow) to the OpenQasm list between runs; expected +1..3 rows agreeing with C, no other residual change (no current OpenQasm row has a non-finite literal)")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_13 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 13u32, "2026-10-19T15:20Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:20 lap 6 grain: the coverage row is now a -Probe OpenQasm run (test.ps1 probe mode: no matrix input edit, outputs in the run scratch, never accepted) filtered to the ebpf_* samples plus the 7 named ones; queued after the switch window, replacing the matrix-edit row.")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_14 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 14u32, "2026-10-19T15:53Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:53 lap 7: the NaN/inf patch's own check moved into the probe, so it needs no matrix edit either: the -Probe OpenQasm row's filter now includes native_float_infinity, and the probe run after pack 7 shows whether inf/nan as constant overflow expressions agree with C (expect exit 0 like C). The 'add native_float_infinity to the OpenQasm list' step is needed only when it should become a permanent matrix row.")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_15 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 15u32, "2026-10-19T16:35Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:35 lap 8 grain: checked the simulator half of pack 7's NaN/infinity patch without a build: run.py's f32 rounding (struct pack <f) raises OverflowError for a finite 1e308, but literals stay real (f64) until they meet a typed value, so the patch's (1e308 * 10.0) folds to inf before any f32 rounding and inf packs fine. A hand-written program in the patch's exact forms (float[32] inf, float[32] nan as inf - inf with v != v true, float[64] -inf, ordered compares) exits 0 in run.py. So the probe row tests codegen only. Next: the -Probe OpenQasm row after main's pack 7 build")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_16 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 16u32, "2026-10-19T17:08Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:08 lap 9 grain: pack 7 inf/nan literals (spiral_compiler.fs 171894-171902) and NanIs (172107) are in the tree; native_float_infinity reaches them through Infinity -> LitFloat (161021), but native_float_nan_is and ts_float_nan build NaN via BackendSwitch with no OpenQasm arm. Staged lab/OPENQASM3-BACKEND/nan-arms.py (adds OpenQasm arms with the same overflow expression after the Zig arms; dry run 2 anchors per sample, stacks with lean-arms.py). Queue row supersedes the probe row with the 2 samples added. Next: main applies nan-arms between runs, then the probe row (expect +3 rows exit 0 like C)")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_17 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 17u32, "2026-10-19T17:35Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:35 lap 10 grain: the doc half of pack 7's NaN/infinity patch was missing: apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md still lists NaN and infinity under the OpenQASM backend's Not supported yet. Staged lab/OPENQASM3-BACKEND/agents-note.py (one anchored sentence, dry run 1 match): states the three constant expressions, why they work in the simulator (literals stay double until typed), and NanIs as (a != a), and keeps the rest of the unsupported list. Next: main applies it with the accept bookkeeping (doc lands with the reviewed code), then nan-arms.py + the probe row")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_18 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 18u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:43 its pack 7 patch is in the accepted baseline (pack 7 ACCEPTED single-flight-20261019-125623 (7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451, C rows unchanged)); the item stays open for its remaining scope.")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_19 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 19u32, "2026-10-19T17:53Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:53 lap 11 grain: main applied the two pack-7 doc notes (AGENTS.md now carries the OpenQASM NaN/infinity sentence and the eBPF two-profile sentence; both anchors gone, new text present once). Fresh static re-rank of the 408 C-row samples outside the OpenQasm list (excluding mega/abi/rust/python/ebpf): the largest blocker groups are strings with macros (61), strings+unions+closures (53), strings alone (43) and unions+match (39), so strings are the next gap by count; 7 scalar-looking names not in the probe yet (method_i8, method_depth, method_return, package_default_int_i64, native_multimodule, frontier_static_list_eq, mutable_alias_method_branch) joined a superseding -Probe OpenQasm queue row. Next: the probe row after nan-arms.py; then strings as the next feature (simulator first)")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_20 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 20u32, "2026-10-19T22:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 18:42 lap 5 grain: probe rung (single-flight-20261019-181659, -Probe OpenQasm): 64 new rows agree with C, 0 disagree, 12 build-fails, 424 compile errors (documented gaps); staged OpenQasm-next.txt for pack 11 Next: pack 11 review and accept")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_21 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 21u32, "2026-10-19T23:06Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:06 lap 20 grain: census of main's 18:42 probe (single-flight-20261019-181659, 424 OpenQasm compile errors) by cause: strings 156, no OpenQasm key in a backend switch 39, int arrays 37, heap 12, closures 12, string arrays 9, sub-word ints 11 (Int8T 8, UInt8T 3), options/unions about 15. Strings stay the largest gap; sub-word ints are the cheapest: OpenQASM 3 has int[n]/uint[n] natively, but the simulator accepts only 32 and 64 (run.py:223-231), so it needs a simulator change (native shim, main's) plus the width map in codegenOpenQasm. Next: decide strings vs sub-word first with main (shim edits are main's)")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_22 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 22u32, "2026-10-19T23:12Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:12 drive grain, sub-word integers staged as two lab patches (dry run by default, write only after every anchor matched, refuse an already-patched file): subword_sim.py (11 edits to tests/native-shims/openqasm-run/run.py, main's file: int[8]/uint[8]/int[16]/uint[16] kinds wrap at their width; a sub-word shift by a count of another kind goes the wide path's way, count masked to 5 bits as C's promotion to int does; mixed-width messages) and subword_codegen.py (4 edits to codegenOpenQasm: the four types, their zeros, literals with the MinValue cases parenthesized like i32, integer conversions between any widths through the assignment's wrap; its anchors are unique against the eBPF codegen and it stacks after CALYX int_widths.py). Checked: the patched simulator passes its own --self-test (7.4 s for the million loop), and four hand-written programs in the backend's emitted style run as C does: lab subword_ops_probe.qasm (small_int_ops' 15 checks) exit 0, subword_scalar_probe.qasm (small_int_scalar's 8) exit 0, subword_method_probe.qasm (method_i8) exit 0, subword_wrap_probe.qasm exit -128000, equal to gcc -O2 -fwrapv on the same C; the tree simulator rejects all four (only int[32] and int[64]). Expected new rows: method_i8, small_int_ops, small_int_scalar, ts_int_wrap. Next: main applies both with the next pack (run.py change reruns only OpenQasm native rows under the per-backend memo keys), then -Probe OpenQasm")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_23 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 23u32, "2026-10-20T00:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 20:21 main: pack 11 ACCEPTED 20:21 (single-flight-20261019-191017: 7,276 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release candidate review 190039); OpenQasm gained 64 rows.")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_24 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 24u32, "2026-10-22T06:54Z", "main", "02:54 main: lab drained; every staged patch has landed, checked by running each in its own check mode: subword_codegen.py ('already has OpenQASM sub-word types'), subword_sim.py on tests/native-shims/openqasm-run/run.py ('already has the sub-word kinds'), nan-arms.py ('already has an OpenQasm arm'), agents-note.py (anchor consumed; AGENTS.md has the pack-7 NaN/infinity paragraph), patch.py (run on a COPY because it writes without an apply flag; its pre-pack-7 NaN anchor is gone). The 5 hand-written .qasm probes are covered by pack 13's 4 OpenQasm matrix rows (method_i8, small_int_ops, small_int_scalar, ts_int_wrap). Folder deleted; the tree core is unchanged (9E336ED57C8ACFE5).")
inl note_of_openqasm3_backend_25 () = Note ("OPENQASM3-BACKEND", 25u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 10:32 (subagent2): waiting: queued C,OpenQasm run with 28 candidates -> next: read the run | lap 12 14:22 (subagent2): nan-arms.py is applied (dry run: native_float_nan_is already has an OpenQasm arm); pack 7 accept holds 31 OpenQasm rows, all agree -> next: the queued -Probe OpenQasm row (12 samples) is now unblocked; then strings, simulator first | lap 13 14:40 (subagent2): probe readiness: all 48 samples matched by the queued probe filter have a C row ok + agree in the pack 7 accept (31 ebpf_ + 17 scalar, incl. zig_exit_wide 305, typecheck_long_let_body 236, package_default_int_i64 7), so every OpenQasm probe row gets a C oracle -> next: the probe row (main's queue) | lap 14 15:02 (subagent2): strings slice scoped: the OpenQasm native tier is our own interpreter (tests/native-shims/openqasm-run/run.py, 45 KB: lexer, parser, i32/u32/i64/u64/f32/f64 helpers, no string type), and OpenQASM 3 itself has no string type, so strings need a run.py extension plus a codegen encoding; native shims are off limits to helper lanes -> next: main decides the string encoding (shim + codegen); helper lanes can stage the codegen half after that | lap 16 16:06 (subagent2): waiting: probe row in main's queue; strings need main's shim decision -> next: main | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): unchanged -> next: main | lap 19 18:56 (subagent2): main's 18:42 probe closed my 4 queue rows (done row appended); OpenQasm-next.txt rides pack 11 -> next: pack 11 accept, then strings in run.py first | lap 20-drive 19:12 (subagent2): sub-word ints staged: subword_sim.py (run.py, 11 edits; self-test ok) + subword_codegen.py (4 edits, stacks after Calyx int_widths.py); 4 hand-written probes run as C does (incl. gcc cross-check -128000) -> next: main: next pack + -Probe OpenQasm | lap 21-drive 19:32 (subagent2): subword_ts_int_wrap_probe.qasm (all 12 nested checks of samples/ts_int_wrap: u8/u32/u64 wraps, i8 overflow, toward-zero div/mod, shifts) exits 0 on the patched simulator -> next: main's next pack")
inl ebpf_c_profile () = Task ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", "eBPF as a profile of the C backend (verifier-friendly bounded loops, no recursion, BPF maps), started once the WASM, WGSL, Calyx, Kotlin, Swift and OpenQASM 3 backends are complete; 2026-10-10: codegenEbpf (--backend Ebpf / .ebpf). Windows gcc -std=c11 -O2 -w -x c runs it. No kernel load: the installed clang has no BPF target. Loops are for-loops capped at 16777216. Maps are a 64-slot array (spiral_map_lookup, spiral_map_update). Matrix is the Kotlin 20 plus samples/ebpf_map, ebpf_map_full, ebpf_arith, ebpf_nested, ebpf_float, ebpf_conv, ebpf_unit, ebpf_shift, ebpf_bool, ebpf_cast, ebpf_div, ebpf_bits, ebpf_key, ebpf_umul, ebpf_neq, ebpf_facc, ebpf_uacc, ebpf_bacc, ebpf_char, ebpf_sh31, ebpf_flt, ebpf_minkey, ebpf_nest3, ebpf_uadd, ebpf_fdiv, ebpf_tacc, ebpf_t2, ebpf_ineq, ebpf_tworec, ebpf_t3, and ebpf_snot. single-flight-20261010-154804: 20 rows agree with C, DISAGREE 0 (backend_smoke exits 13, native_bitwise_scalar exits 42). hopac-20261010-155533: those 20 agree, DISAGREE 0. single-flight-20261010-155601 (rerun after hopac): agree 20, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_map stores 42 at key 7, overwrites it with 9, and looks up a missing key: single-flight-20261010-155757, hopac-20261010-155818, and single-flight-20261010-155823 agree with C, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_map_full fills 64 slots (including key -1 and a stored 0), rejects key 63, and overwrites a full map; samples/ebpf_arith checks toward-zero signed division and remainder, arithmetic right shift, and unsigned wrap. hopac-20261010-161118 and single-flight-20261010-161123 agree with C on those two, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_nested (self-tail with an inner while) and samples/ebpf_float: hopac-20261010-161541 and single-flight-20261010-161809 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_conv (INT32_MIN, casts, unsigned division of the high bit, logical shift, left at runtime) and samples/ebpf_unit (map update from a join point): single-flight-20261010-162410, hopac-20261010-162431, and single-flight-20261010-162437 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_shift (runtime shift counts: arithmetic right shift of a negative, left shift of a positive, unsigned left and right): single-flight-20261010-162550, hopac-20261010-162607, and single-flight-20261010-162612 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_bool (runtime bool arguments, &&, ||, inequality): single-flight-20261010-162736, hopac-20261010-162802, and single-flight-20261010-162807 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_cast (runtime i32, negative i32, and u32 to f32, and f32 to u32): single-flight-20261010-162924, hopac-20261010-162942, and single-flight-20261010-162948 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_div (negative signed divisor, and a runtime u32 divisor): single-flight-20261010-163012, hopac-20261010-163033, and single-flight-20261010-163039 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_bits (runtime u32 and, or, xor, and complement): single-flight-20261010-163054, hopac-20261010-163123, and single-flight-20261010-163128 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_key (a shifted i32 is the map key) and samples/ebpf_umul (unsigned multiply wrap): single-flight-20261010-163156, hopac-20261010-163216, and single-flight-20261010-163222 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_neq (runtime f32 negation, and <> as equality plus bool !=): single-flight-20261010-163335, hopac-20261010-163411, and single-flight-20261010-163417 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_facc (self-tail f32 accumulator, dead return 0.0f) and samples/ebpf_uacc (self-tail u32 accumulator, unsigned add wrap, dead return 0u): single-flight-20261010-163445, hopac-20261010-163459, and single-flight-20261010-163506 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. Combined reprove of those 17 Ebpf samples: hopac-20261010-163533 and single-flight-20261010-163544 agree 17, DISAGREE 0, every native exit 0. samples/ebpf_bacc (self-tail bool accumulator, dead return false) and samples/ebpf_char (runtime char equality, 'A' is 65; char fails the number constraint so < is rejected): single-flight-20261010-163634 for bacc, single-flight-20261010-163652 for char, then hopac-20261010-163736 and single-flight-20261010-163743 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_sh31 (u32 shift by 31 and by 0) and samples/ebpf_flt (negative f32 ordering): single-flight-20261010-163819, then the 21-sample hopac-20261010-163835 and single-flight-20261010-163848 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. That pair of runs also reproves the earlier 19 profile samples, agree 21, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_minkey (map key INT32_MIN) and samples/ebpf_nest3 (nested i32, u32, f32 flattened to one struct): single-flight-20261010-163933, hopac-20261010-163950, and single-flight-20261010-163957 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_uadd (u32 add across 2^31): single-flight-20261010-164046, hopac-20261010-164105, and single-flight-20261010-164111 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_fdiv (f32 division by a negative divisor): single-flight-20261010-164142, hopac-20261010-164203, and single-flight-20261010-164209 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_tacc (self-tail returning a pair, dead return (SpiralTuple0){0}): single-flight-20261010-164304, hopac-20261010-164320, and single-flight-20261010-164326 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_t2 (pair and triple in one program): single-flight-20261010-164419, hopac-20261010-164436, and single-flight-20261010-164442 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. Full matrix reprove of all 47 Ebpf samples: hopac-20261010-164510 and single-flight-20261010-164532 agree 47, DISAGREE 0 (backend_smoke exits 13, native_bitwise_scalar exits 42). On-disk outputs are the single-flight-20261010-164532 generation. samples/ebpf_ineq (runtime NEQ on i32, u32, and f32, emitted as !=): single-flight-20261010-165107, hopac-20261010-165121, and single-flight-20261010-165152 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_tworec (two self-tail methods, +3 and -2, each with continue and dead return 0): single-flight-20261010-165227, hopac-20261010-165252, and single-flight-20261010-165321 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_t3 (self-tail returning a triple, live three fields, dead return (SpiralTuple0){0}): single-flight-20261010-165358, hopac-20261010-165414, and single-flight-20261010-165429 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. samples/ebpf_snot (complement of runtime -1i32 and -8i32): single-flight-20261010-165553, hopac-20261010-165558, and single-flight-20261010-165604 agree, exit 0, DISAGREE 0. Out of v1: f64, i64/u64, NaN/infinity, strings, unions, arrays, closures, heap refs, globals, failwith, non-self-tail recursion, a kernel verifier.", Feature, P0, D6, 85u32, Active, "WASM-BACKEND,WGSL-BACKEND,CALYX-BACKEND,KOTLIN-BACKEND,SWIFT-BACKEND,OPENQASM3-BACKEND", "backend,compiler,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ebpf_c_profile () = Planning ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", Minutes 900u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_1 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:32Z", "main-thread", "08:32 single-flight-20261014-073517 (after the runbook): Ebpf 51 rows, 51 compile, 51 ran, 51 agree with C, 0 build-fail. Next v2 slice from the out-of-v1 list: i64/u64 (BPF registers are 64-bit, cheapest win), then failwith as exit code.")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_2 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 2u32, "2026-10-14T12:59Z", "main-thread", "08:59 i64/u64 implemented in codegenEbpf source: int64_t/uint64_t, ll/ull literals, INT64_MIN, and a single integral-cast rule whose 32-bit cases emit the old text byte for byte. The Ebpf build already passes -fwrapv, so signed wrap matches the C oracle. Lands with the next pack; then probe existing i64/u64 scalar samples onto the Ebpf list (no new samples).")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_3 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 3u32, "2026-10-14T14:48Z", "main-thread", "10:48 pack-1b candidate: samples/i64_scalar compiles on this backend and exits 0, the same as C (gcc -fwrapv). The next step after install is to add i64_scalar to this backend's harness list.")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_4 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 4u32, "2026-10-14T18:25Z", "main-thread", "14:24 i64/u64 installed with pack-1d; i64_scalar is on the Ebpf list and agrees with C in single-flight-20261014-140125.")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_5 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 5u32, "2026-10-15T10:48Z", "main-thread", "06:48 groom: Ebpf 52/52 agree (pack-1h oracle). Next grain: f64 or a kernel-verifier smoke run.")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_6 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 6u32, "2026-10-15T11:04Z", "main-thread", "07:04 decision (user 'we need all of them sota'): raised to P2; every backend stays on the active rotation.")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_7 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 7u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: the no-BPF-target blocker is stale: scoop llvm 23.1.3 (not on PATH) has bpf targets, and all 52 committed main.ebpf outputs compile with clang -target bpf -O2 to elf64-bpf objects (up to 412 instructions). A verifier load needs Linux: next is a Linux CI job loading them with bpftool or libbpf (WSL stays off for now).")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_8 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T12:34Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:34 lap 1 grain: verifier-load prerequisite found: the 52 committed main.ebpf outputs end in a plain int main(void) with no SEC() program section and no license section (ebpf_arith/main.ebpf:103), so bpftool or libbpf has nothing to load even on Linux; a kernel-load check first needs a codegen mode that wraps main as SEC(xdp) or SEC(socket) returning the exit code through a map slot, plus char LICENSE[] SEC(license); clang -target bpf from scoop llvm 23.1.3 is present locally for the compile half Next: draft the SEC wrapper as a text transform over one main.ebpf in lab/EBPF-C-PROFILE and compile it with clang -target bpf -O2 -c (light), before touching codegenEbpf")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_9 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:30Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:30 lap 2 grain: the SEC wrapper works as a text transform. lab/EBPF-C-PROFILE/sec-wrap.ps1 replaces the host int main(void) { return spiral_main(); } with int32_t spiral_exit_code; SEC(socket) int spiral_socket(void *context) { spiral_exit_code = spiral_main(); return 0; } plus char LICENSE[] SEC(license) = GPL (SEC defined inline, no libbpf headers), and compiles with scoop clang 23.1.3 -target bpf -O2 -g -c into spiral-bin/runs/ebpf-sec-wrap/<sample>. Results (heavy gate, seconds): ebpf_arith, ebpf_tacc, ebpf_tworec each give a 6-instruction socket section (clang folds the whole program, since every input is a literal), 4-byte .bss (the exit code: user space reads it through libbpf's global-data map), license section, BTF + BTF.ext emitted; ebpf_minkey keeps its map code: 75-instruction socket section, .bss 772 bytes (the 3 map arrays of 64 int32 + the exit code). So codegenEbpf needs no restructuring for the compile half: the wrapper is 8 lines at the end. Open for the verifier half: loops capped at SPIRAL_LOOP_CAP 16777216 exceed the verifier's 1M-instruction budget whenever a loop survives -O2, so the cap must drop (e.g. 8192) under the kernel profile; load proof needs Linux (bpftool prog load, then read .bss), which waits for the WSL-last rule. Next: emit the wrapper from codegenEbpf behind a kernel-profile switch (env SPIRAL_EBPF_PROFILE=kernel) as a compiler patch in lab, with the smaller loop cap")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_10 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T14:08Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:08 lap 3 grain: one output for both profiles, no switch. Instead of an env-var kernel mode, lab/EBPF-C-PROFILE/patch.py (2 anchored edits to codegenEbpf's program assembly, spiral_compiler.fs ~172389 and ~172415, anchor-checked) wraps the target-specific parts in #ifdef __bpf__ (predefined by clang -target bpf, never by the host gcc): SPIRAL_LOOP_CAP is 8192 under __bpf__ (inside the verifier's 1M-instruction budget) and 16777216 on the host as today; the entry is the SEC(socket) spiral_socket that stores main's i32 in the .bss spiral_exit_code (0 for a unit main) plus the license section under __bpf__, and the unchanged int main(void) otherwise. Simulated on 4 committed outputs with the exact emitted text (lab/EBPF-C-PROFILE/dual-profile.ps1): host gcc exits 0 for ebpf_arith, minkey, tacc, tworec, matching the accepted oracle exits, and clang -target bpf builds each with socket (0x30, or 0x258 for minkey), .bss and license sections. Matrix impact: 52 Ebpf residual-only changes, native results unchanged (host path identical). HEAVY QUEUE: apply patch.py with the next compiler pack. Left for Done: a real kernel load (bpftool prog load + read .bss) on Linux, which waits for the WSL-last rule. Progress 75")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_11 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T15:22Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:22 lap 5 grain: the real kernel load does not have to wait for WSL. GitHub's Ubuntu runners have passwordless sudo and a BPF-capable kernel, and spiral's compiler job already runs there. Wrote lab/EBPF-C-PROFILE/kernel-load.ps1 (Linux only, parse-checked, not run): for every samples/**/main.ebpf it builds the host binary with gcc (exit = oracle), builds clang -O2 -g -target bpf, loads it with sudo bpftool prog load ... type socket pinmaps, runs it once with bpftool prog run over a 64-byte packet, reads spiral_exit_code from the pinned .bss map (raw value bytes from bpftool -j), and compares it with the host exit mod 256; verdicts ok, host-build-fail, bpf-build-fail, verifier-reject, run-fail, no-bss, differs. It needs the dual-profile patch.py outputs (pack 7). Proposal for the user: one gh-pages.yml compiler-job step (apt install clang bpftool linux-tools, then pwsh lab script promoted to apps/compiler/tmp/scripts) after the matrix; agents do not trigger runs. Unverified details to check on the first run: the pinned .bss map file name under pinmaps, and that a socket program returning 0 is accepted by test-run.")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_12 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T15:39Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:39 lap 6 grain, a correctness gap in the dual profile, fixed in the lab patch: under __bpf__ the loop cap is 8192, so a longer self-tail loop (native_tail_recursion counts down 1,000,000) falls out of its for loop into the dead return, and the kernel result looked like a real answer (0 here, by luck the right one). patch.py gets a 3rd edit: every self-tail loop is followed by SPIRAL_LOOP_CAPPED(), which under __bpf__ sets spiral_loop_capped and on the host is ((void)0), and the socket entry stores -2147483647 in spiral_exit_code when a loop was capped. Host behavior is unchanged, and the residual gains one line per tail function (still Ebpf residual-only). Checked: the 4 patches still stack on a scratch copy, and dual-profile.ps1 (updated to the new text) builds ebpf_arith, minkey, tacc, tworec and native_tail_recursion on host gcc (exits 0 = oracle) and clang -target bpf (socket, license, .bss 8 bytes; minkey 0x308 with its maps). kernel-load.ps1 now reads spiral_exit_code by name from bpftool's BTF-formatted dump (a raw offset breaks once .bss holds the maps or the capped flag; checked on a mock dump) and reports loop-capped rows as their own verdict. !!!!While loops cannot tell break from cap and stay undetected (documented).")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_13 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 13u32, "2026-10-19T15:59Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:59 lap 7 correction to lap 6's cap flag: SPIRAL_LOOP_CAPPED() now goes only after the loops of value-returning tail functions (inside if result <> ''). Their base cases leave the loop through return (checked on native_tail_recursion/main.ebpf: return 0 inside the loop, continue otherwise), so control falls out only when the cap is hit. A unit tail function has no such guarantee, and no committed Ebpf output has one (0 static void functions in 52 outputs), so it gets no flag rather than a possible false one. Re-applied on a scratch copy: 3 edits, the flag sits before the dead return. Caveat on lap 6's evidence: dual-profile.ps1's text replace also inserts the flag after the loops in spiral_map_lookup and spiral_map_update, so it does not simulate patch.py's flag. It proves only that host exits match and that the bpf builds work; the flag itself gets its proof from the pack 7 outputs plus kernel-load on Linux.")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_14 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 14u32, "2026-10-19T16:36Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:36 lap 8 grain: fixed the caveat from lap 7: dual-profile.ps1 no longer puts SPIRAL_LOOP_CAPPED after the loops of spiral_map_lookup and spiral_map_update; it applies the insertion only after the map prelude, which matches patch.py (value-returning tail functions only), and when an output already carries the emitted dual profile (#ifdef __bpf__, i.e. pack 7's regenerated samples) it uses that text unchanged. Rerun (4 tiny compiles): ebpf_arith, ebpf_minkey, ebpf_tacc, ebpf_tworec host exit 0, each builds for -target bpf with socket, license and .bss sections, and ebpf_tacc's simulated output has exactly 1 flag (3 before). Next: rerun on pack 7's emitted outputs, then kernel-load")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_15 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 15u32, "2026-10-19T17:09Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:10 lap 9 grain: pack 7's accept wrote the emitted dual profile into all 52 committed main.ebpf (each has #ifdef __bpf__); dual-profile.ps1 on the emitted text as-is: ebpf_arith, ebpf_minkey, ebpf_tacc, ebpf_tworec and native_tail_recursion host exit 0 (oracle 0), all 5 build for -target bpf with socket, license and .bss sections; ebpf_tacc has exactly 1 SPIRAL_LOOP_CAPPED. Next: kernel-load.ps1 (Linux kernel verifier + run) once WSL work is allowed again")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_16 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 16u32, "2026-10-19T17:36Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:36 lap 10 grain: AGENTS.md's eBPF paragraph is stale after pack 7 (it says the loops are capped at 16777216 and describes no bpf profile). Staged lab/EBPF-C-PROFILE/agents-note.py (2 anchored edits, dry run both match once): the two profiles (host cap 16777216 + int main; __bpf__ cap 8192, SPIRAL_LOOP_CAPPED after value-returning tail loops, SEC socket entry storing spiral_exit_code or -2147483647 when capped, GPL LICENSE), text checked against the emitted ebpf_tacc/main.ebpf lines 4-10, 61, 106-117; the native tier sentence now says it does not load the kernel profile. Next: main applies it with the accept bookkeeping; kernel-load.ps1 once WSL is allowed")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_17 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 17u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:43 its pack 7 patch is in the accepted baseline (pack 7 ACCEPTED single-flight-20261019-125623 (7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451, C rows unchanged)); the item stays open for its remaining scope.")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_18 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 18u32, "2026-10-19T17:54Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:54 lap 11 grain: main applied agents-note.py (AGENTS.md now describes both profiles; anchors gone, new sentence present once). For the last step, a WSL-free route: kernel-load.ps1 already refuses anything but Linux and needs only pwsh, clang -target bpf and sudo bpftool, all available on the GitHub ubuntu runner (bpftool through the linux-tools package), and the spiral workflow already checks out the committed main.ebpf outputs; so a CI step running it after the compiler job would close the item without WSL. Workflow edits are the user's (and need gh actions-lock if a new uses: is added; this needs none, only a run: step). Next: the user decides CI step vs WSL run")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_19 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 19u32, "2026-10-19T18:23Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:23 lap 12: two bugs fixed in lab kernel-load.ps1 before any Linux run: (1) /sys/fs/bpf is root-only on Ubuntu (mode 1700), so listing the pinned maps without sudo would have reported no-bss for every program; it now lists them through sudo ls; (2) the host program's stdout was emitted into the result rows, which breaks the verdict grouping and the exit code; it is now discarded. Parses with 0 errors")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_20 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 20u32, "2026-10-19T19:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 15:00 precision on the 14:23 note: the root-only /sys/fs/bpf mode is the usual systemd default on Ubuntu, not something checked on the GitHub runner itself; the sudo ls listing works either way. kernel-load.ps1 now also prints the kernel, clang, bpftool and gcc versions first, so the first Linux run records the toolchain behind each verdict")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_21 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 21u32, "2026-10-19T23:16Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:16 lap 20 grain: (1) doc drift found: AGENTS.md still lists i64/u64 as unsupported for eBPF and its scalars as int32_t/uint32_t/float/bool, while codegenEbpf emits int64_t/uint64_t (spiral_compiler.fs:172283-172301) and samples/i64_scalar's Ebpf row agrees with C in the pack 9 accept. (2) Sub-word ints staged: lab/EBPF-C-PROFILE/subword.py (4 edits: int8_t/uint8_t/int16_t/uint16_t, zeros, literals, integer casts between all widths; the emitted C is plain C like the C oracle's, so store-time wrapping matches), dry run 4 anchors once, refuses re-apply, and it stacks with CALYX int_widths.py and OPENQASM3 subword_codegen.py in either order to the same bytes. agents-widths.py fixes both doc sentences (2 anchors, stacks with CALYX agents-note.py). Candidate rows: method_i8, small_int_ops, small_int_scalar (Ebpf list). Next: main applies with the next pack and probes -Probe Ebpf")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_22 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 22u32, "2026-10-21T23:26Z", "main", "19:26 the 'main applies with the next pack' row is closed by facts: subword.py reports the tree core already has eBPF sub-word types (pack 13, accepted 10-20 00:27, BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS), and method_i8, small_int_ops and small_int_scalar are on the Ebpf list. The doc half was still open: agents-widths.py applied now to apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md, so the eBPF paragraph lists int64_t/uint64_t and the sub-word ints instead of saying i64/u64 are unsupported (user to commit). What remains is the kernel side (kernel-load.ps1: clang -target bpf plus a real verifier load), which needs a Linux kernel or WSL and stays last per the no-WSL-until-the-workflow-passes rule.")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_23 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 23u32, "2026-10-22T04:19Z", "main", "00:19 incident and open question from main's lab drain: lab/EBPF-C-PROFILE/patch.py has no dry-run mode and writes its target unconditionally; run as a check at 00:19 it applied its 3 edits to the tree spiral_compiler.fs. Restored at 00:21 from the git index (A9A71772B249FB8B, the user's staged version); nothing read the tree core in between (the night install had composed at 00:11, other jobs use copies). The open question: its anchors (the single program.AppendLine of SPIRAL_LOOP_CAP 16777216) still matched the tree, so the __bpf__ dual profile (cap 8192, SPIRAL_LOOP_CAPPED) that tmp/AGENTS.md describes since pack 7 may not be in this code path; check which codegen sections carry the dual profile before this item closes. Folder kept.")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_24 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 24u32, "2026-10-22T05:59Z", "main", "01:59 main: open question answered and lab drained. The dual profile IS in this code path: core 173146-173152 emits '#ifdef __bpf__' with SPIRAL_LOOP_CAP 8192, spiral_loop_capped and SPIRAL_LOOP_CAPPED(), '#else' 16777216 and a no-op; 173179-173189 is the SEC(\"socket\") entry with spiral_exit_code (-2147483647 when capped) and the license. patch.py's anchor matched only because the #else branch carries the single 16777216 line, so patch.py was a stale pre-dual-profile script (it would have stacked a second profile). Every staged edit has landed: dual profile and SEC wrap in core, subword types (pack 13), and both AGENTS.md notes (8192 present). Promoted checks: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/ebpf-dual-profile.ps1 (clang -target bpf compile of emitted samples; supersedes sec-wrap.ps1) and ebpf-kernel-load.ps1 (verifier load on Linux; samples path fixed to ../samples). Deleted: patch.py, subword.py, agents-note.py, agents-widths.py, sec-wrap.ps1, and the folder. Left for the item: the kernel-side run on Linux, last per no-WSL-until-the-workflow-passes.")
inl note_of_ebpf_c_profile_25 () = Note ("EBPF-C-PROFILE", 25u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 10:32 (subagent2): waiting: dual-profile patch rides next pack; kernel load needs Linux -> next: read pack review (52 Ebpf residual-only) | lap 13 14:40 (subagent2): kernel-load.ps1 prints its toolchain identity first (kernel uname -r, clang, bpftool, gcc first lines), so a CI or WSL log names the verifier and compilers behind each verdict (MATRIX-TOOLCHAIN-IDENTITY style); parse 0 -> next: user: CI step vs WSL run | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): unchanged -> next: user | lap 19 18:55 (subagent2): unchanged -> next: user (first Linux run of kernel-load.ps1) | lap 20-drive 19:16 (subagent2): doc drift (AGENTS.md says i64 unsupported; codegen + i64_scalar row say otherwise) + subword.py staged (4 edits, stacks with the Calyx/OpenQasm patches order-independently) + agents-widths.py -> next: main: next pack + -Probe Ebpf")
inl c_user_globals_dropped () = Task ("C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED", "C backend drops every user global: CodegenC seeds its dedupe set from env.globals (global x = if has_added.Add x then globals.Add x) but prints only its private globals buffer, never env.globals, so !!!!Global / core global \"...\" text (e.g. #include) never reaches C output; the same in upstream The Spiral Language 2/CodegenC.fs (lines 175-176, 898); every other codegen prints env.globals; CodegenC maps to the upstream file, so the fix goes upstream (COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION) instead of a local edit; repro: tmp/cglobal (global \"#include <tgmath.h>\" absent from main.c); lib/spiral math C arms use __builtin_* with _Generic meanwhile; 13:4x FIXED here (correction: the upstream-section exception was only for comment stripping; functional fixes in CodegenC are the normal workflow, exported to the fork with scripts/upstream.py export --since): the C program assembly now writes env.globals before its own includes (Seq.append env.globals globals), as every other codegen does; repro tmp/cglobal now emits #include <tgmath.h>; upstream PR candidate (The Spiral Language 2/CodegenC.fs line 898); 18:2x re-verified live with the committed compiler: a scratch repro (an !!!!Global call carrying the text #include <tgmath.h>) compiles to C and main.c carries #include <tgmath.h> on line 6, after the backend's own stdbool/stdint/stdio/stdlib/string includes (Seq.append globals env.globals in CodegenC, HEAD a602d846); the repro directory was deleted afterwards; the exported patch material at %LOCALAPPDATA%/spiral-bin/upstream-pr (2026-10-08) already contains the fix; opening the upstream PR is the user's call", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,upstream,c", "current", "current")
inl ci_kino_shared_tmp_delete () = Task ("CI-KINO-SHARED-TMP-DELETE", "spiral ubuntu CI 18:34: 17 lib notebooks failed because spiral gleam/lua on CI delete their package dir = grandparent of the code file, which for Kino cells (/tmp/spiral_kino/<id>/main.gleam) is the shared /tmp/spiral_kino (other cells: could not write main.spi, remove_dir_all DirectoryNotEmpty); fix: package_owns_code (code under <package>/src) guards the CI deletion in process_gleam and process_lua; 21:1x verified locally with CI=1 and the Kino layout <root>/<id>/main.gleam plus a sibling <root>/other: unfixed CLI deleted the sibling and the root, fixed CLI keeps both (gleam.toml lands in the cell dir); apps/spiral rebuilt (run check: 43 notebook exports, rust/cuda ok) and shipped; first build failed because parsed_integer conv ran at peval for u = ref str via every BackendSwitch arm, now gated by a static integral check; 21:39 local CI=1 sweep with the fixed CLI: all 16 notebooks CI reported (sm, testing, guid, runtime, trace, resultm, base, date_time, math, optionm, listm, reflection, lustre, env, convert, console) exit 0 and %TEMP%/spiral_kino survives; parsing and file_system are the two known failures; awaits the next push; 01:2x spiral CI after the 21:49/22:56 pushes: only 2 notebooks left (from 17), and the new cause lines named them: date_time (Lua): ticks subtracted a hardcoded 14400 s (the authors UTC-4 offset) to cancel unix_epochs local-time os.time{1970..}, so it held only in UTC-4 (CI UTC: actual 6.21355824e17 vs 6.21355968e17, exactly 4 h); now subtracts the same os.time{1970..} value at runtime; file_system (Rust): asserted the cwd name was polyglot/spiral/fsharp, but programs run inside the spiral workspace inherit apps/kino (get_workspace_root_external is Error there by design); the test now asserts get_full_path \".\" names the same directory as get_current_directory (); verifying under CI=true and TZ=UTC0; 01:1x verified locally under CI=true: date_time passes with TZ=UTC0 (proven effective: os.time{1970} is 14400 locally, 0 under TZ=UTC0) and in local time, file_system passes; awaits push; 02:1x CI on the 00:31 push: polyglot green; dice ubuntu green, windows red only on the known WSL contract path (CI-SPIRAL-WASM-NATIVE); spiral red only on date_time + file_system, both fixed locally after that push (await the next push); 06:2x dice CI on the 05:40 push red on ubuntu: ui/rust_derive.ps1 stripped only numeric case names (U[SH]n_m), so readable US0_Some counted as an unknown type and US enums + the Heap structs holding them lost serde/borsh derives (5 E0277). Fixed the pattern to U[SH]n_<name>; regenerated dice ui passes cargo check locally. spiral + polyglot CI still running; 18:1x local re-verification under CI=true TZ=UTC0 with the shipped CLI: date_time exit 0 (228.9 s) and file_system exit 0 (123.6 s), and %TEMP%/spiral_kino survived the pair with 223 cell dirs before and after (the shared root is what process_gleam/process_lua used to delete); the notebook exports went to a temp dir so the tree is untouched; the CI run of those two notebooks lands with the next push", TaskKind, P0, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "ci,kino,spiral", "current", "current")
inl matrix_native_lanes () = Task ("MATRIX-NATIVE-LANES", "spiral harness native tier ran ~1260 build-and-run jobs one after another (46 min of job time; 70 min suites under load): now 6 lanes (ForEach-Object -Parallel, SPIRAL_NATIVE_PARALLEL overrides), each with its own gleam project and zig cache, per-row native_ms in results.tsv; full suite single-flight-20261008-221030: 12.8 min wall, all 2036 rows parity, DISAGREE 0 (Gleam 1149 s and Lean 852 s of job time dominate)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "harness,performance", "current", "current")
inl dir_tree_html_repo () = Task ("DIR-TREE-HTML-REPO", "Move spiral/apps/dir-tree-html into its own repo i574n/dir-tree-html (C:\\home\\git\\dir-tree-html): spiral standards (build.ps1, staged native build with a run check, LF, no comments), reuse spiral (deps/spiral for core lib, BuildSpiral, scripts/core.ps1, CI shape), its own gh-pages CI, adapt every reference in spiral/polyglot/dice/i574n.github (workflow.ps1, spiral scripts/build.ps1, cargo workspace member); 23:2x moved: C:\\home\\git\\dir-tree-html holds build.ps1 (BuildSpiral -> dir_tree_html.rs -> cargo +nightly-2025-11-01 --release -> dist/DirTreeHtml + --self-test run check), Cargo.toml standalone with spiral release profile, package.spiproj core- from deps/spiral, scripts/init.ps1 (links deps/spiral to the sibling spiral checkout, cloning it when missing, then spiral scripts/init-app-build.ps1), scripts/workflow.ps1 + publish.ps1 (renders dist/index.html with itself), .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml (ubuntu+windows build, gh-pages push), LICENSE, LF; spiral: apps/dir-tree-html removed, workspace member + Cargo.lock entry dropped, scripts/init-app-build.ps1 extracted from init.ps1 (fork + app toolchain, reused by app repos), scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1 helper (repo next to the resolved spiral checkout, clone+build when the binary is missing, prints its path); consumers rewired: spiral workflow/publish, polyglot publish/publish-bin/build.livemd, dice init/publish/publish-bin, i574n.github init (clone + init) and build; verified locally: init + build + self-test ok, all 5 consumer call forms resolve to the new binary, render ok, 17 scripts parse, LF; awaits commit + push for its CI; 00:3x binary renamed DirTreeHtml -> dir-tree-html (the package name, Cargo default): [[bin]] keeps path = dir_tree_html.rs at the repo root, which makes cargo require name, so name = package name; build.ps1, publish.ps1, README and spiral scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1 use dist/dir-tree-html(.exe); rebuilt + self-test + publish index ok, helper resolves it from dice; 01:0x the published page (https://i574n.github.io/dir-tree-html/) showed only the linux binary: publish rendered dist/ instead of the repo tree; fix by reuse: spiral scripts/publish-tree.ps1 (extracted from spiral publish.ps1: rsync the repo with the standard filters into dist, _-rename dotfiles, render index; -Root, -Tool, copies the tool aside because dist is wiped), spiral publish.ps1 and dir-tree-html publish.ps1 both call it; rsync install for Windows moved from spiral init.ps1 into init-app-build.ps1; local Windows runs exposed a latent bug in the shared filter: choco rsync is cygwin and globbed the unquoted */ and * filters into extra sources (target/release leaked into dist), fixed with CYGWIN=noglob; verified: dir-tree-html dist = exactly its repo files + index, spiral dist top-level unchanged; awaits push. polyglot/dice/i574n.github publish.ps1 keep their own rsync copies (same latent glob bug on Windows) - move them to publish-tree next; 01:1x publish-tree.ps1 now serves all five repos (spiral, dir-tree-html, polyglot with -Include *.png, dice, i574n.github): one filter list built as an argument array, CYGWIN=noglob; each publish verified locally: exit 0, index rendered, no target/release leaks; -Include verified (*.lock adds Cargo.lock); 18:4x re-verified: the repo is clean at 43c3558 (4 commits) with dist/dir-tree-html(.exe) built, and publish-tree.ps1 serves all five consumers, spiral, dir-tree-html, polyglot, dice and i574n.github each calling it from their own scripts/publish.ps1; the commit and the push are the user's call", Feature, P0, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "repo,spiral,ci", "current", "current")
inl alphabet_ci_xelatex () = Task ("ALPHABET-CI-XELATEX", "alphabet CI red since 2025-11: apps/documents notebook asserts every crowbook output Ok, but on ubuntu-24.04 xelatex fails for test.md.pdf and test.hangul.md.pdf (\"xelatex did not return successfully\"); html/epub pass. Capture the xelatex .log in CI (missing TeX package or Nanum font with the cached texlive-xetex), fix the apt package set or the crowbook template. 2026-10-09: alphabet synced with dice (publish-tree.ps1, scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1, gleam 1.19.1, setup-bun); 2026-10-12: cause found: crowbook 0.17.1 tex.template needs hyphenat/breakurl/lettrine/stdpage/spverbatim/newunicodechar (texlive-latex-extra, confirmed hyphenat.sty via packages.ubuntu.com noble) and ulem (texlive-plain-generic); CI only installed texlive-xetex. Added both to gh-pages.yml apt step and scripts/init.ps1; plain + hangul PDFs build locally (MiKTeX); awaits CI after push; 2026-10-12 root cause from the new xelatex diagnostics step (run 38280261902): every package crowbook's template loads is present (english.ldf, lmodern, fontspec, xunicode ...), but hyperref under XeTeX (PU encoding) inputs puenc-greek.def, which Debian/Ubuntu ship in texlive-lang-greek (\"! LaTeX Error: File puenc-greek.def not found\", l.612, Emergency stop; MiKTeX installs it on demand locally, hence no local repro); fix (uncommitted): texlive-lang-greek added to gh-pages.yml apt packages and scripts/init.ps1; diagnostics step kept (runs only on failure); 18:00 run 38295913402 (commit 82538e8 with texlive-lang-greek): ubuntu build PASSES (20m18s), so the Linux xelatex fix is confirmed; windows-2025 fails in apps/documents: crowbook \"failed to run command xelatex\": no TeX at all on the Windows runner (earlier runs hid it because ubuntu failed first and cancelled windows; locally xelatex is scoop latex = MiKTeX); fix (uncommitted): gh-pages.yml windows-only miktex step (choco install miktex, PATH, [MPM]AutoInstall=1, update package db) and the xelatex diagnostics step now runs on any OS on failure; untested in CI (CLI flags from MiKTeX docs), the Hangul PDF may additionally need a Korean font on Windows; superseded by DOCUMENTS-TECTONIC (user decision 2026-10-12 18:53); the Linux fix (texlive-lang-greek) is verified green in run 38312799493", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "ci,alphabet,dice-parity", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_alphabet_ci_xelatex () = Planning ("ALPHABET-CI-XELATEX", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl backend_switch_hermetic () = Task ("BACKEND-SWITCH-HERMETIC", "BackendSwitch evaluation, state of the art for both cores: every non-current arm is evaluated in key order on every backend for its type only (compile-time state identical across backends, a type mismatch is an error everywhere, like Kotlin expect/actual), hermetically (throwaway seq, its own variable counter from 1<<<30 so no tag reaches the residual: single-flight had numbered around the discarded arms, the cause of the BackendSwitch cross-core residual class), once per (backend, arm closure) per build (type cache; hopac gains one, cleared with the JP dicts). Hopac validates again (backend_switch_validate_all on; it skipped other arms since 2026-10-02 for speed, unsound). 2026-10-12: edits in spiral_compiler.fs, unbuilt; proof ladder single-flight -> hopac -> restore pending; proven and blessed 2026-10-12 05:07: cross-core residual differences 83 -> 0, behaviour differences 0, hopac missing 0, restore 0 of 3,768; hopac rung 76 min vs 54.5 (validation cost ~40% under full load: next, evaluate type-check-only join points inline); 2026-10-12 closed: blessed twice (dev40 05:07, dev41 08:36), cross-core 0 differences; the ~40% hopac validation cost moves to HOPAC-SPEED", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,hopac,parity", "current", "current")
inl c_format_any () = Task ("C-FORMAT-ANY", "C arm for lib sm_real format_debug_real (format of any value): frontier_format_any_union / frontier_join_format_any C rows build-fail on backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral, so Rust has no oracle there, and sm format test has no C expectation. Structure can reuse the portable serialize_union_rust/record_fold/tuple arms (as Zig/Lean do); the C-specific gap is primitive-to-string: lib globals precede the C prelude that defines StringLit, so a lib helper cannot build a String; needs a compiler-emitted helper or a portable Spiral digit loop; 2026-10-12 done in lib (sm.livemd cells 46/58/87, exported sm_real.spir): append_real C via StaticStringConcat, format_debug_real C arm (snprintf into a static buffer, String built at the call site with StringLit; shortest round-trip floats, quoted chars; unions/tuples/records/nominals/arrays via the portable arms), format_real C arms (empty, prim p with %+.6f/NaN/Infinity, append). frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any C now build and exit 0 (were build-fail); a probe of union/record/tuple/f32 formatting prints byte-identical text in C and Rust; 2026-10-12 compiler lane: C rows frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any build, exit 0, Rust agrees; Delphi still fails (backend_switch_has_no_Delphi_arm_in_lib_spiral): needs a Delphi arm in lib/spiral BackendSwitch; 07:47 Delphi done (lib/spiral/sm'_real.spir, uncommitted): Delphi arms for append_real (s + x), format_debug_real (bool BoolToStr true/false, char, floats FloatToStr, tuples/records/unions/nominals through the portable arms, ints IntToStr) and format_real's three switches (empty, per-value: floats signed fixed 6 decimals, chars, strings; append); frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any Delphi now build with fpc and exit 0 (were build-fail); oracle rows flip at the next pack's bless", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,c,format", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_c_format_any () = Planning ("C-FORMAT-ANY", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl self_checking_samples () = Task ("SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES", "Samples and tests as self-checking source (user design session 2026-10-12, nothing fixed yet; dogfood before committing): no prose, code only; expectations live inline in the source of truth, expect-test style (ppx_expect, Rust expect-test/insta, Zig test blocks): a promote run rewrites the expected blocks in place, git diff is the review, a clean run leaves the file untouched, dev mode can persist cell results and clear them after a successful run; the oracle backend is configurable per file or cell (C default) and other backends are checked against it, replacing hand-written per-backend copies and the uncommittable cache EXPECTED.tsv; end state: .spi itself is the notebook (compiler-understood test blocks with inline expectations, Zig/Rust #[test]-like), .livemd becomes a view, so edits are plain file edits; negative samples stay as test blocks whose expectation is a diagnostic (rustc UI tests, clang expected-error); multi-file packages must work (package-manager litmus): measure the dice symlink cost on Windows, then compare compiler-resolved path dependencies (Cargo path/workspaces) with junctions. Phases: 1 benchmark the same sample family through the matrix and through Kino (warm workers, parallel cells: KINO-PARALLEL-CACHE); 2 measure dice symlink package resolution on Windows; 3 dogfood one family (ebpf_* 27 samples) as an inline-expectation file with promote mode, keeping matrix rows byte-identical; 4 decide; phase 2 first look 2026-10-12: dice reaches lib through two chained symlinks (dice/deps/polyglot -> polyglot, polyglot/deps/spiral -> spiral); raw I/O over the 315 lib .spi/.spir files through the chain is 1.4-2.5x slower warm (hundreds of ms at worst, not the main cost); the compiler normalizes package paths with Path.GetFullPath, which collapses .. but does not resolve links, so a package identity is the path spelling: the same lib reached as dice\\deps\\polyglot\\deps\\spiral\\lib and as spiral\\lib is two packages (parsed, cached and watched twice), the likelier cost and a blocker for one canonical identity per package in a package manager; next: time a dice compile via the link path vs the direct path, then try canonicalizing package roots (FinalPath/ResolveLinkTarget) in the compiler; test block prototype 2026-10-12: survey of the 39 lib notebooks: 1,904 cells, 364 test cells (44 static, 12 in .spir real code, 11 print_code), targets fsharp 169 / cuda 126 / rust 107 / gleam 93 / typescript 93 / lua 34 / c 1, per-target options (rust -d crates, gleam -t javascript -d), all timeouts default; test cells hold top-level-only statements (open, nominal, #r), so a test is a scoped group of top-level statements, not a function: syntax `///> target opts` lines then `test name [static] [print_code] =` with an indented body; semantics = file prefix up to the block + the body (Kino notebook-prefix semantics, Spiral is top-down so position preserves scope); compiler: blockParsingParse returns Error [] for a block starting with `test` (both cores; the same no-statement result as comment-only blocks), unbuilt; converter livemd -> spi/spir with test blocks reproduces the current exports byte for byte in 38 of 40 files (benchmark and lustre carry stale doc text from cell outputs); spi -> livemd adapter round-trips all 37 notebooks with every test source preserved (order of .spir tests and one directive line moved); next: run original vs regenerated notebooks through Kino and compare per-test results, then the build with the parser change; dice package identity fix (2026-10-12, unbuilt): canonical_directory in the shared SpiProj schema resolves every path component with Directory.ResolveLinkTarget (symlinks and junctions), used for packageDir/moduleDir combines and the default package dir, so a package reached through deps/polyglot/deps/spiral and through spiral/ is one package; no matrix path crosses a link (checked), so sample outputs must stay byte-identical; to measure: a dice compile through the link path before and after, and dice/alphabet packageDir pointing straight at the sibling ../spiral/lib (CI and local init already create that layout); dice measured 2026-10-12 (cold compile of lib/dice.spi to Rust, one process each): old compiler through the symlinks 19.3 s, new compiler (canonical package dirs + tonight batch) through the symlinks 17.4 s, new compiler with packageDir ../spiral/lib (no links) 18.0 s; the new compiler output is byte-identical through links and without them (one package identity), so dice can drop the links; a single cold compile shows no real symlink cost, so the slowdown the user saw likely sits elsewhere (cargo/rustc over symlinked dirs, Kino watching, repeated warm compiles): measure those next. Test blocks: with the new parser all 364 test blocks embedded in 35 lib files compile to byte-identical C (pristine-copy check; an earlier failure came from stale files in a reused copy, not from test blocks); 2026-10-12 lib/spiral migrated (unstaged): the 39 lib notebooks are now their .spi (370 test blocks; sm' keeps its real-mode code and tests in sm'_real.spir); the 39 .livemd are deleted; Kino document domain (apps/kino/spi/document.spi, Spiral) gained to_spi_tested/to_spir_tested (test cells -> test blocks named by heading) and from_spi/parse_spi (blocks -> test cells), byte-identical to the validated prototype on all 37 notebooks (644 cells); run_notebook.ps1 --path x.spi runs a source file as a notebook and never writes over it; lib/spiral/build.ps1 and near/wallet/build.ps1 run .spi and write <nb>.spi.ipynb (README links -> .spi.html); evidence: per-test A/B original livemd vs round trip 353 tests in 31 notebooks same outcome, .spi run directly math/seq/sm' 79 tests same, the 7 lib-dependent matrix samples 53 rows identical to the accepted repro, Kino mix test 140/0; open: the full lib build from .spi (running 09:30), then folder samples -> test blocks; 10:10 user committed the lib migration (dc7b955c 09:51); all 39 lib notebooks run from .spi with exit 0 (Kino run_notebook, 2 at a time), 268 tests in 29 notebooks have the same outcome as the original livemd runs (physics/parsing/rust outputs vanished before comparison, both matched in the A/B); .spi -> .livemd kept for a cell UI: from_spi now restores /// doc runs as markdown cells and adds a #### name (test) heading only when the name would not re-derive; run_notebook --livemd-path writes the Livebook view, --with-tests exports back; 39/39 lib notebooks .spi -> .livemd -> .spi byte-identical, 26/39 livemd byte-identical to the deleted originals, 3,503 of 3,531 old cells verbatim (sm' real cells move ahead of the rest); Kino mix test covers both properties; UI direction: Zed (LSP + runnables, ZED-TEST-RUNNABLES) as the editor, Livebook via --livemd-path for rich outputs; split 2026-10-12 into SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS, TEST-EXPECT-PERSIST, CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE, KINO-RUN-ONE-TEST, NOTEBOOKS-TO-SPI-SIBLINGS, SPIR-INTERLEAVED-LIVEMD, ZED-TEST-RUNNABLES; children also TEST-RUN-REPORT, TEST-IDENTITY-I18N, SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY (from the 2026-10-12 external review of dc7b955c)", Epic, P1, D7, 55u32, Active, "", "samples,kino,compiler,dogfood,packages,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_self_checking_samples () = Planning ("SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned User)
inl note_of_self_checking_samples_1 () = Note ("SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:39 touch: phase-3 family measured: ebpf_* is 31 samples, 407 source lines, 166 oracle rows (C, Ebpf 31 each; Bend, Delphi, F#, Rust 25; OpenQasm 4), every C exit 0: one ebpf test-block file with inline expectation exit 0 replaces 31 folders")
