inl note_of_fsharp_utf8_strings_5 () = Note ("FSHARP-UTF8-STRINGS", 5u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: F# rows can now be run natively (FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER prototype): of 329 F# rows with a C row, 327 agree in exit and stdout (string_utf8 included) and 2 fail to compile in fsi (FSHARP-MACRO-MUTATION); none differ.")
inl note_of_fsharp_utf8_strings_6 () = Note ("FSHARP-UTF8-STRINGS", 6u32, "2026-10-18T16:10Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:10 main-thread, re-checked against today's native F# tier. Review 5 (single-flight-20261018-084525) ran all 976 F# rows natively, and every one of the 330 that has a C row matches C on exit and stdout, including samples/string_utf8 (exit 0; it exited 1 under fsi on 10-12) and string_eq. The managed UTF-8 samples have no F# row yet, so I probed them by hand with the tier's fsi exit wrap: managed_string_utf8_boundaries F# exit 0 (C 0) and managed_string_invalid_utf8_slice F# exit 3 (C 3). So F# has C's byte semantics for length, index and slice, and the item's other request (an F# native tier) landed today (FSHARP-NATIVE-TIER). Left, after today's accept (adding rows before it would break the row-for-row reproduction): add both samples to the Fsharp list in tests/matrix.psd1, then close.")
