inl note_of_package_registry_design_4 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:55Z", "main-thread", "06:55 groom correction: the +5 since 10-14 comes from files committed at 10-14 21:31, not from tonight's additions. The new sample and eoie_dev_spiral_tests are untracked and not in the 1,528.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_5 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 5u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user direction 2026-10-17: the Rust/cargo ecosystem (rayon, many small crates under the 1k-line gate, compiled in parallel) is the end-product target to the end, not Zig and its package manager, and no dynamic languages for end products. So the registry design builds on cargo: Spiral packages map to crates, locks pin crate versions and Spiral package digests together, and the 1k-line crate split is a design input the registry must make cheap (many tiny crates, one lock).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_6 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 6u32, "2026-10-19T12:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:40 helper-lane lap 1: LAB-IN-REPO fix: the 10-14 typed model lived only in job tmp (C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\registry\\model.spi, 285 lines, comment-free); copied into spiral/lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi (original left). Drift found against note 5 (user 10-17: build on cargo, Spiral packages map to crates): the model has 0 mentions of cargo or crate; its versioning is exact_version + exact_requirement over sha256 package_id/blob_id with its own index_checkpoint and blob_origin (R2 bucket/object_key) and social mirrors. Under the cargo direction, release_manifest needs a crate mapping (crate name, crate version, features, Cargo.lock pin) and the own index/blob store becomes optional (crates.io or a cargo alternative registry index).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_7 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 7u32, "2026-10-19T13:27Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:27 helper-lane lap 2: brought the lab model in line with the cargo direction (note 5): lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi gains nominal crate_name and cargo_feature, type cargo_crate {crate; crate_version : exact_version; features}, union lock_pin = SpiralSource : exact_requirement | CargoLock : cargo_crate * sha256_hex (the Cargo.lock checksum), and release_manifest gets rust_crates : list cargo_crate and pins : list lock_pin next to its Spiral requires. Spiral sources keep sha256 path identity (exact_requirement with package_id); Rust dependencies are pinned the way Cargo.lock pins them. Both the 10-14 model and this revision type-check on today's compiler (try.ps1 C with a trivial probe main, exit 0).")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_8 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:00Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:00 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the index decision into the model (lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi, type-checks via try.ps1 C, exit 0): union index_backend = OwnContentAddressedIndex url | OwnIndexWithStaticMirror url * url | CargoSparseRegistry url, union rust_resolution = CratesIoWithLock | AlternativeRegistry url, a sparse_index_path union mirroring cargo's 1/2/3/prefixed name layout, and registry_choice. Decision recorded in chosen_registry: Spiral packages keep the own sha256 content-addressed index served as static files with a mirror (a GitHub Pages copy plus a raw-git copy of the same tree), because their identity is the source hash (exact_requirement, package_id) and cargo's sparse index keys by name+semver, which cannot express that; Rust crates resolve the normal cargo way (crates.io plus the Cargo.lock checksum pins already in lock_pin), so no alternative registry is needed. CargoSparseRegistry stays a case only for the day Spiral packages ship as Rust crates by name.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_9 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:40 helper-lane lap 4: added read_index's path function to the model, index_path name (lab/PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN/model.spi): the own index uses cargo's sparse layout so the same static-file tooling and caching rules apply - 1/name, 2/name, 3/first-char/name, otherwise first-two/next-two/name - over sm.slice (inclusive end) and sm.length from the core. Probe path_probe.spi checks a, ab, abc and spiral (expected exit 15: 1/a, 2/ab, 3/a/abc, sp/ir/spiral); unrun until main's freeze all-clear. Cargo lowercases names for the path; Spiral package names are already lowercase identifiers, so no case folding is modelled.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_10 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 10u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: path_probe.spi exits 15 as predicted - index_path gives 1/a, 2/ab, 3/a/abc and sp/ir/spiral.")
inl note_of_package_registry_design_11 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 11u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): path_probe.spi unrun (jobs held) -> next: run after main's go | lap 6 13:42 (subagent): path_probe PASS (15) -> next: index entry line format | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: path_probe 15 -> next: index format")
inl social_transport_sim () = Task ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", "X-like simulator as Kino's battle test (user 2026-10-12: deferring it was a bad call; it truly battle-tests Kino): a deterministic, seeded event simulator of many humans/agents publishing Spiral cells as posts (post, reply = downstream cell, quote/link = upstream dependency, edit = new revision, delete, like = demand/interest, unlike = withdrawn demand), with delayed, duplicate, reordered and stale deliveries and cancelled consumers, driving Kino's real compile/run path (warm server, concurrent cells over shared prefixes); invariants checked on every schedule: each visible result equals the sequential re-run of its exact revision, stale revisions never surface, shared prefixes compile once (cache hits counted), withdrawn demand cancels work and frees memory, no cross-request contamination (hopac fix 57 class), equivalent reorderings commute; phase 1: event log format + replay runner + 5 invariants + a 100-cell scenario from lib notebooks; phase 2: scale (10^4 cells), memory ceilings, agent personas; later: transport frames (280-char, twitter-text v3 weighting, NFC) and optional real X/Bluesky/Mastodon mirrors; 2026-10-12 phase 1 built (apps/xsim, untracked, Elixir, path dep on apps/kino): Scenario (cells of any .spi via Kino parse_spi -> posts: definitions as a reply chain, tests replying to the latest definition; seeded likes/unlikes/edits; deliveries shuffled in a window with duplicates), World (idempotent per event index, causal per post+user, replies/edits/likes before their post buffered), Runner (demand-driven: wanted posts -> prefix chain key -> one task per key, cancel on withdrawn demand or edit, results in completion order), Invariants (sequential equivalence vs a fresh baseline, no stale results, one compile per prefix, withdrawn demand hidden, commute across schedules), fault knobs (naive_visibility, cancel: false, rerun_completed, leaking executor) each proven to trip its invariant; mix test 8/8 x5 seeds; mix xsim.run on lib math/date_time/sm' with the fake executor: up to 195 posts, 1,068 deliveries, 104 duplicates, 80 buffered, all invariants ok, commute ok; a leaking executor fails sequential equivalence and commute; next: --executor kino on Kino's new in-memory Notebook.evaluate (agent B), then scale and memory ceilings; 11:55 first runs on real Kino (Notebook.evaluate): two genuine findings, KINO-SOURCE-DIAGNOSTICS (temp paths make outputs nondeterministic) and TEST-PRELUDE-SEMANTICS (tests depend on an earlier static test's open); xsim now models Kino's linear prefixes (tests on the trunk, --branch tests optional) and normalizes temp paths; 12:15 simulator made incremental (prefix key = H(parent key, source), edits re-key only their subtree, the runner only touches posts each delivery changed, refcounted prefix keys, lazy prefix sources): full lib corpus (36 notebooks with tests as interleaved author threads, 1,651 posts, 5,916 deliveries, 529 duplicates, 4 edits + 2 breaking edits per thread) 626.5 s -> 17.5 s simulator time with the fake executor, ~linear (228/535/1,651 posts: 2.3/5.1/17.5 s), every invariant and commute ok; tests 10/10 (new: root edit re-keys the subtree); multi-notebook corpus (--spi a,b,...) and --breaking edits added; 12:25 transport frames: Xsim.Frames splits a cell into numbered frames within 280 weighted characters (twitter-text v3 ranges: Latin weight 1, CJK/Hangul 2; NFC; split at grapheme boundaries; header \"<post> k/n\"), World buffers frames per post and applies the rebuilt post at its earliest frame index once complete (parent missing -> pending), --frames in mix xsim.run; tests 12/12 (Hangul cell -> several frames, each <= 280, shuffled rebuild exact; framed corpus with 30% duplicates and a 20-wide shuffle window keeps every invariant and commutes with the unframed run); 12:20 real-Kino finding KINO-DAEMON-START-RACE; 12:40 --churn N (N edits cycling the root of each thread, woven through the stream: agent-rewrite storms re-key every prefix and cancel in-flight work) exposed an over-strict invariant, not a runner bug: cancelled-then-restarted prefixes counted as double compiles (14 cancels, 5 keys restarted); one_compile_per_prefix is now starts - cancellations <= 1 per key (the rerun_completed fault still trips it); --save/--replay delivery logs (JSON lines) for exact bug reproduction; full lib corpus with frames: 1,651 posts -> 6,643 events, ~8,000 deliveries incl. ~1,330 duplicates, all invariants + commute ok in 20-24 s per schedule; tests 14/14; 12:50 phase-2 scale reached with the fake executor: lib corpus x6 as 216 author threads (--spi-file, since mix.bat caps the command line at 8,191 chars), 9,906 posts -> 40,585 events -> 46,604 deliveries incl. 6,019 duplicates, all framed, with edits, breaking edits and root churn: every invariant + commute ok, 118.9 s simulator time, peak BEAM 110 MB, 300 s wall incl. the sequential baseline; next: real-Kino scenarios at growing size, memory ceilings of the daemon side, agent personas; 13:20 in CI (uncommitted): apps/xsim/build.ps1 (mix test; the framed lib corpus with edits, breaking edits and churn over 2 schedules + commute; unless -fast a cold 2-concurrent real-Kino scenario that guards KINO-DAEMON-START-RACE, then the daemon is stopped) runs from spiral scripts/build.ps1 after the lib notebooks; local -fast run 190 s wall incl. deps and compile; 14:20 semantic oracle added after the KINO-C-FALSE-PASS report: breaking edits are known-wrong revisions, so on the real Kino path any visible ok result for a post whose current revision is a breaking edit is a violation (breaking_edits_fail); a lenient executor that passes everything is caught, an honest one is not (test); the concurrent-vs-sequential invariants alone cannot see a false pass because both sides are wrong the same way; tests 15/15; 18:53: CI run 38312827285 (spiral 17:47 commit) failed in apps/xsim mix test: the churn test asserted map_size(results) > 6, which depends on timing (a fast runner cancels every stale revision, leaving exactly 6); replaced with a deterministic check (the root ends at the last churn edit); 15/15 pass on seeds 0, 1, 7, 42, 1234; uncommitted; 2026-10-14 01:14 agent personas (uncommitted): Xsim.Personas, mix xsim.run --personas lurker=N,fickle=N,editor=N,breaker=N; lurkers like about half the posts, fickle users toggle like/unlike 5 times on one post (withdrawn demand, cancel and restart), editors rewrite one definition 3 times and then revert to the original source (ABA: the prefix key returns to an earlier value), breakers push a breaking test edit and then fix it back (the broken source joins scenario.broken); persona streams interleave round-robin and weave with churn; fixed an off-by-one: --edits 0 produced one edit; tests 25/25 on seeds 0, 1, 7, 42 (new: 4 schedules with 30% duplicates and a 10-wide window, every invariant, breaking_edits_fail with an honest executor, every post ends at its original source, the schedules commute; an unknown persona raises); full lib corpus (36 notebooks) with personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2, frames, edits, breaking edits and churn: 1,662 posts, 8,467 events, about 9,730 deliveries, 5,690-5,745 prefixes, 47-54 cancels, every invariant and commute ok, 17.5-21.6 s per schedule, peak BEAM 70 MB; next: the real-Kino size ladder with daemon memory ceilings (script ready at the job scratch xsim/ladder.ps1: isolated port 13977, peak working set of the daemon process tree, retire over TCP), waiting for the heavy gate; 2026-10-14 02:04 real-Kino size ladder (job scratch xsim/ladder.ps1: its own daemon on port 13977 with SPIRAL_KINO_COMPILER_IDLE_MIN=3, peak working set of the daemon process tree, retire over TCP, users 2, 2 schedules, concurrency 2, through the heavy gate): math.spi 3 cells: 3 prefixes, 52 s cold + 10 s, daemon tree peak 1,464 MB; 6 cells: 5 prefixes, 69-101 s + 22-34 s, 1,735-1,858 MB; 12 cells: 10 prefixes, 201 s + 103 s, 2,353 MB (one pool compiler reached 1,116 MB); 8 cells with --memo and personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1: 8 prefixes, 43.8 s then 0.8 s (memo hits 15 then 43 cumulative, misses 13), 2,331 MB, every invariant and commute ok; daemon tree = erl 58 MB + 3 pool compilers (dotnet 374-1,116 MB) + 3 pwsh compiler_bridge relays at 101-114 MB each (about 320 MB, filed as KINO-BRIDGE-OVERHEAD); genuine finding: the 6-cell rung failed sequential equivalence and commute only because a failing Rust cell prints the OS thread id of its panic (thread '<unnamed>' (52388) panicked), so two runs of one revision differ; xsim's Invariants.comparable now normalizes the id as it did temp paths (test added, 26/26), rerun passes; Kino-side normalization filed as KINO-PANIC-THREAD-ID; apps/xsim/build.ps1's CI run now passes --personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2 (that exact command: pass, 102 s wall); 2026-10-14 02:17 phase-2 scale with personas (fake executor): lib corpus x6 (216 author threads) with frames, edits, breaking edits, churn and personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2 per thread: 9,972 posts -> 50,647 events -> about 58,160 deliveries incl. about 7,510 duplicates, 22,638-22,888 prefixes, 63-64 cancels, every invariant and commute ok, 89-93 s per schedule, peak BEAM 147 MB, 467 s wall; xsim tests 26/26 on seeds 0, 1, 5, 7, 13, 42, 99, 1234, 2026; daemon-side ceiling observed on the real path: each pool compiler recycles past about 1.2 GB (logged at 1,228-1,318 MB, KINO-DAEMON-MEMORY policy), so with 3 compilers plus 3 pwsh relays and erl the daemon tree is bounded near 4.3 GB; 2026-10-14 02:21 full math.spi on real Kino with --memo, personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3: 22 posts, 80 events, about 92 deliveries, 21 prefixes, 2-3 cancels, schedule 1 291 s then schedule 2 5.2 s (memo hits 179 then 390 cumulative, misses 32), daemon tree peak 3,115 MB (compilers 788/803/1,087 MB, 3 recycles at 1,228-1,318 MB), 32.7 min wall including the sequential baselines; sequential equivalence, no stale results, one compile per prefix, withdrawn demand and commute all ok, but breaking_edits_fail flagged f0p4: an xsim oracle bug, not a Kino false pass: the breaking edit bumped the last digit run, turning 7.3890560989306495 into 7.3890560989306496 (the same f64, and inside _assert_approx_eq's tolerance), so the edit was not breaking; Scenario.break_source now bumps the whole literal's integer part (7.389... -> 8.389..., 9 -> 10), shared by --breaking and the breaker persona, with a test (27/27 on seeds 0, 1, 42); 2026-10-14 02:48 fix verified on real Kino: math.spi 5 cells with --breaking 1 (the edit now turns the f0p4 test's 7.3890560989306495 into 8.3890560989306495) on its own daemon: 5 prefixes, 53 s + 18 s, every invariant ok including breaking_edits_fail (the cell now fails honestly), commute ok, daemon tree 1,796 MB; xsim tests 27/27 on 69 seeds (0, 1, 5, 7, 13, 42, 99, 1234, 2026 and 100-159); 2026-10-14 03:00 the --edits 0 fix changes build.ps1's cold real-Kino race guard (math.spi 3 cells, users 2, edits 0, 1 schedule, concurrency 2) from 8 to 7 events; that exact scenario on a cold isolated daemon still passes with peak_inflight=2 (3 prefixes, 32.7 s, every invariant ok, daemon tree 1,226 MB, 47.8 s wall); churn-heavy persona stress over math+date_time+sm' (319 posts, 2,000+ events, 40-50% duplicates, churn 6-12, fickle 6-8, breaker 4-6) passes on seeds 1-16 at concurrency 1, 3 and 8; 2026-10-14 03:26 simulator-side memory ceiling: Runner evict: true / mix xsim.run --evict frees a prefix's result when its last demand goes (withdrawn like, edit, re-key), recomputes on renewed demand, counts evictions in one_compile_per_prefix (starts - cancels - evictions <= 1) and adds the invariant unwanted_results_freed (every held result belongs to a wanted post); summary gains peak_results and evictions; full lib corpus with personas, frames, edits, breaking edits and churn: results held 1,608 instead of about 5,700 (-72%), about 4,000 evictions per schedule, starts 5,657-5,689 vs 5,744-5,792 kept (no extra compiles at this scale), every invariant and commute ok; on math+date_time+sm' with churn 6: 316 held instead of 1,548-1,599 at +1.4-4% starts; tests 28/28 on seeds 0-29 (new: eviction commutes with keeping, never holds more results, evictions happen); the default stays off so earlier numbers compare; 2026-10-14 03:37 second notebook on real Kino: date_time.spi 20 cells with --memo, personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3: 20 posts, 70 events, about 80 deliveries, 20 prefixes, schedule 1 94.5 s then 3.1 s (memo hits 131 then 306 cumulative, misses 44), every invariant incl. breaking_edits_fail and commute ok, daemon tree peak 2,181 MB (one compiler at 1,190 MB, 5 recycles in the daemon log), 11 min wall; my daemon port 13977 is free again; next: multi-notebook real-Kino corpora (math+date_time interleaved threads) and --evict against Kino's memo; 2026-10-14 03:39 eviction at phase-2 scale (lib corpus x6, same options as the 9,972-post run): results held 8,232 instead of about 22,700 (-64%), but 24,250-24,798 starts instead of 22,701-22,952 (+7-8% recomputes from renewed demand after churn and fickle toggles), 148-188 s per schedule instead of 89-93 s, peak BEAM 133 vs 147 MB; every invariant incl. unwanted_results_freed and commute ok; so dropping results on withdrawn demand trades about 8% more compiles for a bounded result store, which is the policy question for Kino's memo (keep, evict, or evict with a grace window); 2026-10-14 03:44 correction: the 4.3 GB daemon-tree bound above is an estimate from the recycle threshold (3 compilers at about 1.3 GB, relays, erl), not a measurement; the highest measured peak is 3,115 MB (full math.spi); 2026-10-14 04:09 first multi-notebook real-Kino run (math.spi + date_time.spi, 10 cells each, as two interleaved author threads via --spi-file; a comma list through mix.bat had silently kept only math): 20 posts, 91 events, about 105 deliveries, 16 prefixes, with --memo, --evict and personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3: schedule 1 224 s then 21 s (memo hits 52 then 121 cumulative, misses 18-19), 0 evictions needed, every invariant incl. unwanted_results_freed and breaking_edits_fail ok, commute ok; daemon tree peak 3,398 MB (compilers 877/981/1,104 MB), the highest measured so far; 16.6 min wall; evict stress on 3 notebooks passes on seeds 20-35 at concurrency 1 and 6; xsim tests 28/28 on seeds 0-29 and 200-259; next: a daemon-memory sampler in xsim itself (today the ladder script reads the tree after the run), larger multi-notebook corpora on real Kino when the gate is quiet, and Kino's memo policy under eviction; 2026-10-14 04:14 final code checked as CI runs it: pwsh apps/xsim/build.ps1 -fast 1 (mix test, then the personas corpus run) exit 0 in 117 s: 1,662 posts, about 9,730 deliveries, every invariant and commute ok; 2026-10-14 04:26 review fixes: --breaking and the breaker persona now only pick test cells whose source Scenario.break_source actually changes (a tail like pair.1 was picked, left unchanged and then flagged as a false breaking_edits_fail violation), and the fickle persona returns no events on an empty thread instead of crashing; test added, 29/29 on seeds 0-19", Epic, P0, D7, 100u32, Done, "", "social,simulator,cells,stress,x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_social_transport_sim () = Planning ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", Minutes 480u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_1 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 1u32, "2026-10-14T13:36Z", "lane-b", "daemon-memory sampler written in a private copy (apps/xsim lib/xsim/daemon_memory.ex + mix xsim.run --daemon-memory): finds the daemon pid from Kino's hello banner, samples its process tree's working set every 1 s from one pwsh sampler, prints 'daemon pid= peak_mb= procs= samples=' per schedule; test queued behind the heavy gate")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_2 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 2u32, "2026-10-14T20:17Z", "lane-b", "sampler proven on real Kino (private copy, apps/xsim daemon_memory.ex + xsim.run --daemon-memory; the pwsh port needs :hide on Windows or it exits silently): math.spi 4 cells, 2 schedules, every invariant + commute ok, daemon tree peak 1,150 MB over 7 processes, 36 samples; xsim tests 30/30")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_3 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 3u32, "2026-10-15T09:07Z", "lane-b", "05:07 lane B: scale step (fake executor, whole lib/spiral: 76 .spi, 200 users, 5 edits, 20% duplicates, concurrency 16, xsim on the tree Kino with tonight's landed changes): 1,960 posts, 217,004 events, ~260k deliveries (43k duplicates), ~6,000 starts over ~5,900 prefixes, 120-133 cancels per schedule; every invariant ok on both schedules and commute ok; 158 s and 200 s per schedule, peak BEAM 171/258 MB; xsim tests 30/30; 10^4 cells not reached yet (the lib yields ~2k posts; --churn or a second corpus would scale it)")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_4 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: scale run: whole lib/spiral, 1,960 posts, 217k events, invariants ok, peak BEAM 258 MB (lane B, 10-15). Next grain: run it against the pack-1h compiler with concurrency raised.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_5 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 5u32, "2026-10-16T03:11Z", "claude", "(2026-10-15 23:11 -04:00, Claude b7595ac3) apps/xsim moved to the i574n/x repo as apps/sim (user request: everything X-related in x). It depends on the sibling spiral/apps/kino by path, so it still battle-tests Kino from downstream: from x, mix test 30/30 and the lib-corpus run (2 schedules, every invariant ok, commute ok). spiral's scripts/build.ps1 and CI no longer run it; its kino-executor run now uses a private daemon port (13950) and stops only that daemon.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_6 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 6u32, "2026-10-18T07:25Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 03:25 new mix xsim.world: writes the X state a scenario leaves (framed posts as reply chains, edits as new versions with edit_history_post_ids, like and reply counts, optional lost frames) as an e2e stub world plus the expected program; x client e2e uses it, apps/sim/build.ps1 regenerates and compares the fixtures. Elixir until SIM-IN-SPIRAL")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_7 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 7u32, "2026-10-18T08:34Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 04:34 found while adding the xsim.world drift guard: (1) on Windows mix.bat drops everything after a comma in a bare argument, so build.ps1's corpus run '--personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2' ran lurker=2 only; Personas.parse now also accepts '+' and the scripts use it (test added); (2) JSON.encode! writes map keys in atom-creation order (OTP 26+), which differs across builds; xsim.world writes key-sorted JSON (Xsim.XWorld.json). Guard now passes across MIX_BUILD_PATHs")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_8 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 8u32, "2026-10-18T12:14Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 08:14 x/apps/sim follow-ups to KINO-RENAME: --executor kino is now --executor cells (Xsim.Executors.cells; DaemonMemory takes daemon_port), verified on real Cells (math.spi 3 cells, every invariant incl. breaking_edits_fail, commute ok, daemon tree 934 MB via --daemon-memory, which landed from lane B's private copy); --spi lists accept '+' like --personas (mix.bat comma bug). With the persona fix the CI corpus run grew from 1,662 to 1,714 posts (10,002 events, ~11,500 deliveries), every invariant and commute ok. Left in this epic: real-Cells corpora beyond 2 notebooks, Cells' memo policy under eviction, 10^4 cells on the real path")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_9 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 9u32, "2026-10-18T20:46Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 16:46 edits travel as frames too: Frames.explode_posts turns {:edit, id, source} into {:edit_frame, id, rev, k, n, body} (rev counts a post's edits, so frames of different edits never mix; an empty source still gets one frame), the World gathers each revision's frames and applies the edit at its first frame's index, waiting for the post if needed; summary gains framed_edits. Test: 3 edits + 1 breaking edit framed at 20 weight, 30% duplicates, 12-wide shuffle: every invariant ok, framed_edits = edits, commutes with the unframed run; sim tests 37/37")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_10 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 10u32, "2026-10-18T21:18Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 17:18 grace-window eviction built: Runner evict: {:grace, n} (mix xsim.run --evict-grace N) marks a result for eviction n deliveries after its last demand goes, drops the mark when demand returns (counted as rescues when the result was held), flushes all marks at the end so unwanted_results_freed still holds; test: fickle+editor+churn scenario keeps every invariant, commutes with keep, ends with no marks (timing-dependent counts left out of the test). First measurement, lib corpus 1,714 posts, frames, churn, personas, 1 schedule each: keep starts 9,900 peak 9,809; evict 10,100 (+2.0%) peak 1,665; grace25 10,344 peak 1,705; grace100 10,769 peak 1,853 rescues 7; grace400 10,032 (+1.3%) peak 2,138 rescues 116; all invariants ok. Starts vary run to run with cancel timing, so repeats decide (preliminary)")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_11 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 11u32, "2026-10-18T22:27Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 18:27 decision on the result-store policy (memo under eviction), measured: total starts are dominated by cancel/restart timing (keep 11,092 vs evict 9,822 mean over 3 runs under machine contention), so the runner now counts recomputes (a key queued again after its result was evicted). Lib corpus, 1,714 posts, 2-3 runs each: evict 261 recomputes (2.6% of ~10k compiles), peak 1,664 results (-85% vs keep ~11,000); grace100 220 (-16%), peak 1,790; grace400 152 (-42%), peak 2,202 (-80% vs keep), 104 rescues; every invariant ok in every run. Chosen for Cells' memo: evict with a grace window (~400 deliveries): on the real path a recompute is a compile of seconds while a held result is a small text, so -42% recomputes for +32% held results (still -80% vs keeping all) is the better trade; immediate eviction only when memory is the binding constraint. Most evictions come from re-keys (edits, churn), which no grace window can save")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_12 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 12u32, "2026-10-19T00:48Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 20:48 correction to note 11, measured on the real path: the fake executor has no persistent memo, real Cells does. Unpaced real runs never evict (deliveries arrive in ms, compiles take seconds, so lapses and re-keys cancel in-flight work instead), so the runner gained --pace-ms N (waits up to N ms for finished tasks between deliveries). math.spi 6 cells, fickle=6+editor=1, 3 users, paced 3 s, fresh SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO_DIR per run: keep 25 real compiles (memo misses), peak 21 results; evict 26 misses, peak 6, 17 evictions, 2 recomputes all served by the memo (hits 37); grace10 26 misses, peak 16, 0 rescues. Decision revised: with Cells' disk memo behind the result store, evict immediately (same real compiles, smallest store); a grace window only pays without a persistent memo. CI corpus run now uses --evict; the grace path stays covered by its unit test")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_13 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 13u32, "2026-10-19T00:59Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 20:59 first three-notebook real-Cells run: math + date_time + sm' (8 cells each, interleaved author threads via --spi-file), --memo --evict, personas fickle=2+editor=1+breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3, fresh memo dir, own daemon port: 24 posts, 121 events, 137-139 deliveries, 21 prefixes, every invariant incl. breaking_edits_fail on both schedules, commute ok; schedule 1 122 s cold, schedule 2 32 s (memo hits 33 then 99, misses 34-35); daemon tree peak 3,445 MB over 8 processes (--daemon-memory), the highest measured so far (previous 3,398 MB with two notebooks); not scaled further on a shared 16 GB machine")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_14 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 14u32, "2026-10-19T12:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:25 lap 1 grain: located: xsim lives in the x repo (x/apps/sim, x lane's tree), so main records only; the 10-18 notes show the next steps already moved: --pace-ms for real Cells, the three-notebook real run (math + date_time + sm', all invariants, daemon peak 3,445 MB via --daemon-memory), so the sampler grain is done Next: ask nothing; next lap read x lane's SIM-IN-SPIRAL progress and decide whether this epic's remaining 18% is scale runs on a quiet machine")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_15 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 15u32, "2026-10-19T13:08Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:08 lap 2 grain: x lane state (read only): X-CELLS-SPLIT at 35 percent, its prelude, glyphs, protocol, splitter and events modules pass Cells on rust + gleam (+ zig) after today's byte-unit lib flip; those are the modules xsim's frames reuse (SIM-IN-SPIRAL note 3), so the sim's Spiral port has its shared base; this epic's remaining 18 percent stays scale runs of the real-Cells three-notebook scenario on a quiet machine Next: when a quiet window opens (evening), rerun the three-notebook real-Cells scenario at concurrency 3 with --daemon-memory to confirm the 3,445 MB peak holds after today's lib changes")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_16 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 16u32, "2026-10-19T18:22Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:22 lap 4 grain: core v1 holds for the sim: the x lane rebuilt x on the fixed core at 13:0x (view 1005 -> 987 lines, 16 crates under 1000, no _Lt order enum) and its e2e passes, 31 tutorial steps + 5 extra cases with all 4 sim threads, post-thread 34 posts read back, delete-thread 34; helper lane 2 proposes splitting SIM-IN-SPIRAL into a bulk story owned by the x lane with no KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dep Next: decide the SIM-IN-SPIRAL split with the x lane (main proposes yes: the Spiral port does not need Kino without Elixir)")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_17 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 17u32, "2026-10-20T20:43Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 16:43 three-notebook real-Cells rerun on today's Spiral-backed sim (scenario + invariants through the Gleam build): math + date_time + sm', --cells 8, --executor cells --memo --evict, personas fickle=2+editor=1+breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3, fresh memo dir, own daemon port 13953, --daemon-memory. 24 posts, 178 events, 199-202 deliveries, 22 prefixes; daemon tree peak 3,049 MB over 8 processes (below the 3,445 MB of 10-18). FINDING: schedule 1 sequential_equivalence=FAIL[f2p1] and commute=FAIL[f2p1]: the concurrent evaluation of f2p1 (sm' thread, 2 cells) returned {:error, 'errors were found at the given arguments: 1st argument: the table identifier does not refer to an existing ETS table'} while the sequential baseline of the same sources returned ok; schedule 2 kept every invariant. An ETS table used after its owner exited, under concurrency 3, in Cells' evaluation path (not the sim: the executor is Spiral.Cells.Notebook.evaluate). Reported to main (Cells owner). Script: x lane scratch three_notebooks.ps1 (eoie job job-1792527165391-x-three-notebooks)")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_18 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 18u32, "2026-10-21T00:04Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 20:04 Cells ETS race (found by x-lane on job-1792527165391) fixed by main as CELLS-ETS-OWNER (named tables owned by a VM-lifetime process) and proven end to end by rerunning the same three-notebook repro (x-ets-proof: math, date_time, sm' via the cells executor, max-concurrency 3, 2 schedules, memo + evict + daemon-memory): clean, commute ok, daemon peak 3,427 MB")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_19 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 19u32, "2026-10-21T01:46Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 21:46 x-lane backlog refactor: estimate 2400m -> 480m, now meaning remaining minutes (82% done). Left: scale runs on real Cells only (lib corpus beyond 3 notebooks on the cells executor at higher concurrency with --daemon-memory and --pace-ms, which the runner already has since 10-18 note 12; 10^4 cells on the real path), on a quiet machine. The three-notebook real run is done (x-ets-proof 10-20). Corrected 21:53: the first version of this note listed --pace-ms as still to do")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_20 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 20u32, "2026-10-21T11:44Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-21 07:44 ladder attempt 1 (x-ladder 07:04-07:43) stopped by the x lane during rung 1 (6 notebooks, cells 8, concurrency 3): the rung's Cells daemon tree reached 2,979 MB within minutes while free memory fell to 1.5 GB beside four other lanes' heavy jobs, and Claude Code's memory reaper had started killing background shells. Stopping it brought free memory back to 7.3 GB. A scale measurement under that contention would mix load with the sim's own cost (last night's 300 s Cells timeouts were load), so the ladder reruns after Tibia's PR-17 live block at a quieter hour, rung by rung, stopping before free memory goes under ~2 GB")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_21 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 21u32, "2026-10-21T13:17Z", "x", "2026-10-21 09:17 x: the ladder rerun waits for tibia's PR-17 and PR-18 live blocks, which run back to back after x-postid-round releases (PR-18 ends about 13:15-13:30). It gets queued only after tibia says PR-18 has ended, and stops before free memory drops below about 2 GB.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_22 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 22u32, "2026-10-21T19:40Z", "x", "2026-10-21 15:40 x: rule set before measuring. The ladder reruns after main's compiler install (night pack with the Gleam slice boundary check, after tibia's 15:45-17:20 block), so no compiler swap lands mid-run, through scratchpad x_ladder_guarded.ps1: one rung per process (6:8:3, 10:8:4, 14:6:4), each tree killed if machine free memory falls under 2,000 MB (the 07:43 run sank to 1.5 GB). A rung passes on exit 0 with verdict=exit; each rung records wall_s, the daemon peak_mb and lowest_free_mb. Close the item when all three rungs pass. If a rung hits the floor, record its peak and the rung before it as the ladder's measured ceiling on this 16 GB machine; that counts as 'scale ladder recorded' (X-DONE D3) and closes the item with the ceiling as the finding. Then delete lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_23 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 23u32, "2026-10-21T20:30Z", "x", "2026-10-21 16:30 x: main's compiler install moved to ~18:50-19:20 (after tibia's live block ~17:40 and eoie r30-r33). Ladder slot: after the install ping, until 20:50, when the run ends with this session's 21:00 deadline. Added rule: a rung still running at 20:50 is stopped and recorded as 'not finished (time)', not as a failure; the item then stays open with the finished rungs recorded and the rest for the next session.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_24 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 24u32, "2026-10-21T21:08Z", "x", "2026-10-21 17:08 x: tibia's live block ended early (17:07), so the ladder started now instead of after main's install: job-1792616902882-x-ladder-guarded, x_ladder_guarded.ps1 with -StopAt 18:45 so it can't overlap main's ~18:50 compiler install (it uses the single-flight DLL). Rules per notes 22-23, except the time cutoff is 18:45 instead of 20:50. Main was told.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_25 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 25u32, "2026-10-21T21:37Z", "x", "2026-10-21 17:37 x: main's install moved to ~20:00-20:30 (eoie release until ~19:40), so the 18:45 hard stop of the running job no longer protects anything; its -StopAt can't be changed in flight. Rule added before results: if the 18:45 stop cuts a rung, rerun the unfinished rungs (same guard, -StopAt 19:50) right after, and record them as normal results; 'not finished (time)' applies only to a rung still running at 19:50.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_26 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 26u32, "2026-10-21T21:44Z", "x", "2026-10-21 17:44 x: ladder rung 1 (6 notebooks : 8 cells : concurrency 3) PASS, job-1792616902882-x-ladder-guarded. Real-Cells executor over 48 posts, 375 events: schedule 1 ms=929708 (cold compiles), schedule 2 ms=70625 (memo); commute=ok; sequential_equivalence, no_stale_results and one_compile all ok on both schedules; peak_inflight 3, peak_beam_mb 175/126. Daemon (pid 100764) peak_mb=3418, last 2058, 8 procs. Guard: verdict=exit, exit 0, wall 1963 s, peak tree private 2295 MB, lowest machine free 2344 MB (floor 2000; other lanes held ~9 GB). Rung 2 (10:8:4) started ~17:42.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_27 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 27u32, "2026-10-21T22:15Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:15 x: rule change before rung 2 reports. Drop note 25's rerun: eoie's r30-r33 release and main's night-pack install (the Gleam slice fix every lane gets) queue behind this job's heavy slot, and a rerun until 19:50 would push the install toward 21:00. So the ladder releases at 18:45 at the latest. A rung cut then is recorded as 'not finished (time)', and unfinished rungs stay for the next session on a quieter machine. The item stays open in that case.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_28 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 28u32, "2026-10-21T22:19Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:19 x: resume tooling now lives in the repo lab (it was in the session scratchpad): lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/ladder-guarded.ps1 wraps ladder.ps1 one rung per process. It kills the rung's tree when machine free memory drops under -FloorMb (2000) or at -StopAt, and it now refuses to start a rung unless free memory is at least -AdmitMb (6000; rung 1 needed ~3.4 GB of daemon plus headroom while other lanes held ~9 GB). It continues past a failed rung and exits with the count of failed or skipped rungs. Next session: eoie job start --heavy x-ladder 6000000 pwsh -NoProfile -File lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/ladder-guarded.ps1 -Rungs <remaining>, at a quiet hour, with a background job wait.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_29 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 29u32, "2026-10-21T22:46Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:46 x: ladder rung 2 (10 notebooks : 8 cells : concurrency 4) NOT FINISHED (time), per note 27. Stopped by the 18:45 -StopAt after 3,830 s with no XSIM line, so its first schedule (cold compiles) didn't complete, where rung 1's took 930 s. Peak tree private 1,710 MB, lowest machine free 2,636 MB; the memory floor never tripped. Rung 3 (14:6:4) not started. The time, not memory, was the limit on a machine where tibia-build-pace and eoie notebook checks held the other heavy slot and compile windows throughout. The slot went to eoie-release-r30to33 at 18:46; no leftover processes, daemon port 13954 closed. Item stays open with rung 1 recorded (note 26). Next session: lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/ladder-guarded.ps1 -Rungs 10:8:4,14:6:4 alone on a quiet machine, budget ~2 h per rung at today's pace (note 28).")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_30 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 30u32, "2026-10-21T22:48Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:48 x: why rung 2 ran over 2.5x slower than rung 1's pace (80 posts would be ~1,550 s cold at rung 1's 19 s/post; not done at 3,830 s). Machine contention: tibia-build-pace held the other heavy slot and eoie notebook checks held compile windows the whole time, and the machine sat at 12.3 of 17 GB committed. The os_mon/memsup crash loop in the rung's log (get_mem_info timing out at 60 s, os_mon exiting 18:39) is a symptom of that load: it is os_mon's background polling in the xsim VM, which pulls in os_mon as a Cells extra_application, and it isn't on the runner's path. One latent Cells issue found on the way: CompilerDaemon.memory_pressed?/0 calls :memsup.get_system_memory_data() inside the daemon's GenServer (handle_info :idle_check, only after 60 s idle), so when the Windows sysinfo port hangs, a compile request arriving during that check waits up to the memsup timeout. A Task with a short timeout, or a cached reading, would remove the stall. Main/Cells lane to judge.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_31 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 31u32, "2026-10-22T00:15Z", "x", "2026-10-21 20:15 x: rung 10:8:4 diagnosed from its memo dir (lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/memo_timeline.ps1, decodes each .etf entry's kind and write time; no compile). Rung 1 (6:8:3, 17:10-17:41) wrote 202 entries: 54 cell results + 17 code results + 131 source_blocks. Rung 2 (17:42-18:42, killed by StopAt) wrote 149: 134 source_blocks (all also in rung 1's memo, content-addressed) but only 13 cell results + 2 code results, i.e. about 15 results/hour against rung 1's ~140/hour. Not scale: eoie job ids show 57 jobs overlapping rung 2 (eoie-release-r30to33, main-night-prereview, tibia-build-pace, ~25 eoie nbcheck/preview/check Cells runs, ~20 tibia hunt runs) against 6 for rung 1, and gaps of 100-520 s between result writes. So rung 2 has not failed; it never got the machine. Memo keys hold across runs of one scenario (morning and evening rung 1: 163 of 202 entries equal, the rest from today's lib edits), so a resumed rung keeps its 15 results, while rung 1's results do not carry over (each rung's scenario edits differ). Guard fixes made: memo hit/miss lines now reach the report (the filter dropped '  memo'), a killed rung prints its last 5 output lines, the leftover ladder-PID-N.txt list is removed, -Rungs accepts '6:8:3,10:8:4' through pwsh -File (it used to arrive as one bogus string), and -ResumeMemoDir (one rung only) continues a rung's memo. Rule written before the next run: rung 2 runs alone (heavy job admitted at free >= 6000 MB, and no other lane's heavy job or Cells-suite job in the window, checked with eoie job list at start), resumed from target/build/sim/ladder-memo-113232-10 unless lib/spiral changed since 10-21 (then fresh); pass = exit 0 with sequential_equivalence=ok, no_stale_results=ok and commute=ok on both schedules; budget 90 min, else verdict not-finished-time and record; rung 3 (14:6:4) only after rung 2 passes. The ladder counts as recorded for X-DONE D3 once rung 2 has a pass or a real failure on a quiet machine; a contention kill is neither.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_32 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 32u32, "2026-10-22T01:40Z", "x", "2026-10-21 21:40 x: lab rule (LAB-SESSION-SWEEP): ladder.ps1 and ladder-guarded.ps1 are carried once, for rung 10:8:4. The session that runs it promotes the ladder to typed Spiral in x or prunes it; memo_timeline.ps1 is pruned (method in note 31).")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_33 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 33u32, "2026-10-22T01:46Z", "x", "2026-10-21 21:46 x: ladder scripts moved to x/target/lab-attic/2026-10-21/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/. Before rung 10:8:4, move both back to x/lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM/ (they resolve target/build/sim relative to their own folder).")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_34 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 34u32, "2026-10-22T01:52Z", "x", "2026-10-21 21:52 x: correction to note 33: the ladder scripts are back in x/lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM (the target attic is withdrawn; target/ gets deleted for disk). Run rung 10:8:4 from there as written in note 31.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_35 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 35u32, "2026-10-22T03:08Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:08 x: rung 10:8:4 was refused at 23:08 (guard: free 3,881 MB < admit 4,500). The quiet machine sits at about 4.0 GB free and 4.0 GB available (the counters agree). Admission lowered once more, by rule: 3,800 = the 2,000 floor + this rung's observed tree peak (1,710 MB on 10-21). The floor kill stays the safety net. A floor trip is recorded as 'rung 10:8:4 exceeds this machine's quiet headroom', a real result, not a contention kill.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_36 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 36u32, "2026-10-22T03:19Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:19 x: rung 10:8:4 verdict on a quiet machine (no other job or window, no lane armed): free-floor after 600 s. Peak tree private 2,009 MB, lowest free 1,996 MB (floor 2,000); the BEAM raised system_memory_high_watermark at 23:10:51 and 23:12:54 before the guard stopped it. Started at about 3.73 GB free with 149 warm memo entries; wrote 93 more (242 now), so a later resume keeps them. By note 31's rule, written before the run, this is a real result, not a contention kill: 10 notebooks x 8 cells at concurrency 4 need more than this 16 GB machine's quiet headroom, which is about 2 GB above the floor because the other Claude sessions, Fork and RustDesk hold the rest. Ladder record for X-DONE D3: 6:8:3 PASS (1,963 s, tree peak 2,295 MB, daemon peak 3.4 GB); 10:8:4 EXCEEDS HEADROOM (>2.0 GB tree with about 1.7 GB to spare); 14:6:4 not attempted (larger than 10:8:4 on both counts). The scale result: real Cells on this box holds about 6 notebooks x 8 cells at concurrency 3; beyond that it needs a bigger machine or fewer resident VMs. What remains on the item is landing the ladder (apps/sim/build.ps1 -Ladder, written tonight, verification next) and then deleting x/lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM.")
inl note_of_social_transport_sim_37 () = Note ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", 37u32, "2026-10-22T03:38Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:38 x: done. The ladder landed as apps/sim/build.ps1 -Ladder <notebooks:cells:concurrency,...> [-LadderMemoDir <dir>], run after the Gleam build and deps, returning before the fixture steps; README row added. Smoke 23:36 through the real build in a compile window: -Ladder 1:2:1 passed both schedules (sequential_equivalence, no_stale_results and the rest ok, commute ok; schedule 2 got its memo hit), rung wall 80 s, build 135 s; the daemon on port 13954 was stopped, no ladder list file was left. The memory guard of the old lab script belongs to eoie (EOIE-JOB-MEMORY-CAP, job start --max-private-mb, drafted for the r30-r33 release that is running now); until it ships, a ladder runs as eoie job start --heavy (admission checks free memory). Scale record (note 36): 6:8:3 passes, 10:8:4 exceeds this machine's quiet headroom. x/lab/SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM is deleted, so x/lab no longer exists.")
inl language_name () = Task ("LANGUAGE-NAME", "Pick the edition-3 language name once SPIRAL3-EDITIONS parses real programs (votes 2026-10-12: Espira 835, Spile 790, Geandra 610 - Geandra as a release codename); prefer a name that keeps .spi/package.spiproj; run a real clearance search before announcing; user decision 2026-10-12 18:53: decide later; keep 'Spiral 3' / 'edition 3' until it parses real programs and a clearance search is done", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", "naming,edition", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_language_name () = Planning ("LANGUAGE-NAME", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned User)
inl note_of_language_name_1 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:46Z", "lane-a", "08:46 lane-a: first-pass web search (not legal clearance): no language named Espira, Spile or Geandra; near names Spira (Haskell DSL), SPIL (1973 Liskov), Spice. Spile is closest to taken names. Next: USPTO/EUIPO/WIPO + package registries. measure\\language-name-search.txt")
inl note_of_language_name_2 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: unchanged; needs the user's call. P3.")
inl note_of_language_name_3 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:55Z", "main-thread", "06:55 groom: no new candidates tonight; needs a user decision session, not agent time. Kept P3.")
inl note_of_language_name_4 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:41Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:41 helper-lane lap 1: package-registry part of the clearance (read-only lookups, 2026-10-19): espira, spile and geandra are all free on crates.io, npm, hex.pm and PyPI (404 on each). On GitHub all three logins are taken by personal user accounts with 0 public repos (Espira, spile, Geandra), so a GitHub org would need a variant (e.g. espira-lang). Trademark offices (USPTO, EUIPO, WIPO) are still unchecked; they need a manual search session. Decision still the user's (votes Espira 835, Spile 790, Geandra 610), gated on SPIRAL3-EDITIONS.")
inl note_of_language_name_5 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:27Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:27 helper-lane lap 2: first trademark pass for the leading vote, Espira (web search, not a register query): no software mark named Espira surfaced; the nearest live marks are Inflectra's Spira / SpiraTest / SpiraTeam / SpiraApps (QA, test management, ALM - software, so the closest conflict risk), a separate SPIRA mark for field-ticketing software, and ISPIRER (database migration). Sources: trademarks.justia.com SPIRA 77700186, ISPIRER 88728652, inflectra.com SpiraApps. The authoritative step is still a direct search of the USPTO Trademark Search system and EUIPO eSearch for Espira in class 9/42, which needs a browser session; the user decides the name once SPIRAL3-EDITIONS parses real programs.")
inl note_of_language_name_6 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:01Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:01 helper-lane lap 3: same web pass (search engine, not a register query) for the other two candidates, so the table has three rows. Spile: no software mark surfaced; the name is taken on GitHub by zorbyte/Spile (a TypeScript Minecraft Java Edition server library, MIT; goodfirstissue.org lists it), and PLDB lists SPIL, a 1973 Liskov/Smith language one letter away; spile is also an ordinary English noun (a tap or peg), which hurts search. Geandra: no product, app or mark found at all (only unrelated GEOS, GEOMATRIX, Gehna ERP), so it is the most distinctive and searchable of the three. Table: Espira - nearest conflict Inflectra Spira/SpiraTest (QA software, class 9/42 risk); Spile - an existing open-source project name plus SPIL; Geandra - clear in this pass. All three still need a direct USPTO Trademark Search and EUIPO eSearch in class 9/42 (browser session). The name stays the user's decision.")
inl note_of_language_name_7 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 7u32, "2026-10-20T02:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 22:33 user decision 2026-10-19 21:4x: no new language name; it stays edition 3, a modified fork (the way languages like Rust name editions), so the votes are closed. Docs, posts and the compiler say Spiral edition 3.")
inl note_of_language_name_8 () = Note ("LANGUAGE-NAME", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:39 (subagent): blocked: three-name table complete; the choice is the user's -> next: the user's decision; then a register search for the chosen name | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): user decision -> next: the user's pick | lap 6 18:22 (subagent): re-read: still the user's call (decide later, 10-12); three-candidate table complete since lap 3 -> next: user decision | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: the user's call -> next: user decision")
inl eoie_agile_typed_fields () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-TYPED-FIELDS", "eoie agile typed planning fields instead of key:value tags (user 2026-10-12): estimate_minutes, load (light|medium|heavy: memory/CPU class on the 16 GB box), assignee kind (agent|pair|user), and a show/list/edit surface for them, modeled as typed Spiral in the agile domain (agile.spi records + verbs), with a one-time migration of the est:/load:/agent: tags written on 2026-10-12; list filters (--load heavy, --agent yes) and a planned-minutes rollup per status/epic so the backlog answers what can run in parallel tonight; 17:25 eoie lane: typed planning fields shipped in the live eoie.exe (sha256 37A8F7A4..., old binary kept at tmp/eoie-lane/eoie-baseline.exe): unions task_load/task_assignee/planned_estimate/planned_load/planned_assignee/task_planning plus one planning_of_<id> row per task (11-field Task tuple unchanged, old binaries read migrated files); verbs: edit estimate|load|assignee (360, 360m, 6h, none; rejects bad values untouched), show planning line, list EST/LOAD/WHO + --load/--assignee filters, plan rollups (status/load/assignee/epic+deps), migrate-tags (dry run default, idempotent); crates agile_planning_* (8 owners + model package), topology ok; tests 197/0, golden compat diff clean; 17:40 main applied migrate-tags to the live backlog: 106 tasks, 318 tags moved, 0 rejected, second apply unchanged, agile check ok (build_attested=1); open: strict evidence renewal (two chains passed every test but exceeded the 900 s cargo budget; -TestTimeoutMs added, untried in a full chain); 18:19 eoie lane: strict evidence renewed (attempt 4, -TestTimeoutMs 2700000): build.ps1 -Test -CompilerContracts 197/0, cold renewal 133 owners in 67 shards, products a == b, test-strict-preflight -RequireReady ready=True diagnostics=0; live eoie.exe sha256 8E92F11E... (golden compat diff clean again); rollbacks tmp/eoie-lane/eoie-baseline.exe and eoie-live-37A8.exe", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,agile,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_typed_fields () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-TYPED-FIELDS", Minutes 360u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl tree_sitter_spiral () = Task ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", "A real tree-sitter grammar for Spiral (both editions) to replace the borrowed tree-sitter-rust in apps/zed: top-level inl/let/type/union/nominal/prototype/instance/open, test blocks with ///> targets, doc runs, macros ($\"...\" $'...' !!!!), so highlights/outline/indents are exact and runnables.scm can tag each `test name =` for gutter run buttons; generate edition-3 symbol tokens from the same table as the compiler tokenizer (SPIRAL3-EDITIONS); 2026-10-14 01:25 lane 1 built the cheapest unblocking grammar (uncommitted, apps/zed/tree-sitter-spiral: grammar.js, generated src/ parser.c ABI 14 with tree-sitter-cli 0.25.10, queries/highlights.scm, runnables.scm, outline.scm, test/corpus/test_headers.txt): every token is a whole line; kinds test_header (prec 6: test_keyword /test[ \\t]+/, test_name, test_signature as immediate tokens), target_directive (///>), doc_comment (///), line_comment (//), definition_line (inl/let/type/union/nominal/prototype/instance/open/and at column 0), indented_line, other_line; tree-sitter test 3/3; gate on the 583-file corpus (spiral lib+apps, eoie src, dice, alphabet, polyglot lib): 375 real test blocks, 375 headers found, 375 correct by row and name, recall 100%, precision 100%, files with parse errors 0 of 583 (the borrowed Rust grammar scored 71%/82%); highlighting lost vs the Rust-borrowed queries (corpus captures): keyword 18144, number 65914, operator 61920, punctuation 145808, string 8214 nodes covering 1.33 MB of the 3.35 MB corpus (40%: Spiral primes open Rust char literals, so most of it was wrong), string.escape 32452, type 11466, type.builtin 4701, boolean 465, variable.special 1128; the new queries capture test_keyword 375, test_name 375, target_directive 660, doc_comment 2042; the spiral-zed language server's semantic tokens (legend: variable, symbol, string, number, operator, unary_operator, comment, keyword, parenthesis, type_variable, escaped_char, unescaped_char, number_suffix, escaped_var, type; a Spiral-aware scanner with macros, (* *) comments, primes) cover keyword, type, string, escapes, number, operator, brackets, comment; not covered: builtin types (i32 etc. come back as variable), booleans (variable), delimiters as their own class; wiring left for after a commit: extension.toml [grammars.spiral] repository https://github.com/i574n/spiral, rev = the commit with apps/zed/tree-sitter-spiral, path = apps/zed/tree-sitter-spiral; languages/spiral/config.toml grammar = spiral; replace languages/spiral/{highlights,outline}.scm with the grammar's queries, add runnables.scm, drop brackets.scm and indents.scm (they name Rust nodes); set languages.Spiral.semantic_tokens to combined; then the manual Zed check in apps/zed/README", Feature, P0, D6, 95u32, Blocked, "", "zed,grammar,editor,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tree_sitter_spiral () = Planning ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", Minutes 900u32, Load Light, Assigned User)
inl note_of_tree_sitter_spiral_1 () = Note ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:54Z", "lane-b", "wired (uncommitted): extension.toml [grammars.spiral] rev 3c76d3ed path apps/zed/tree-sitter-spiral, grammar = spiral, grammar queries + runnables.scm, brackets/indents.scm emptied (old brackets query fails to compile on this grammar; user: git rm), .zed/settings.json semantic_tokens combined; generate reproduces src byte-identical, test 3/3, all 5 queries compile; left: Zed manual check")
inl note_of_tree_sitter_spiral_2 () = Note ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-14T19:25Z", "lane-b", "checked against the compiler's own test-block authority (installed pack-1d --test-blocks): on 582 .spi/.spir files, Zed runnables.scm tags 420 rows, the compiler reports 420 test blocks, 420 equal, 0 false, 0 missed")
inl note_of_tree_sitter_spiral_3 () = Note ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:48Z", "main-thread", "06:48 groom: 10-15: zed test-at-row now takes spans from the compiler (--source-blocks, pack-1g), so the grammar only drives highlighting/outline/runnables. Left: the manual gutter check in Zed (user).")
inl note_of_tree_sitter_spiral_4 () = Note ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:35 lap 1 grain: unchanged since 10-15: grammar in apps/zed/tree-sitter-spiral, wiring waits for a commit rev (extension.toml [grammars.spiral] rev) and the user's Zed check; nothing CPU-light left for agents beyond keeping the corpus test current Next: re-run the grammar's corpus parse over today's lib (401 test headers) to catch drift from new syntax (one light tree-sitter parse)")
inl note_of_tree_sitter_spiral_5 () = Note ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:32Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:32 lap 2 grain, corrects lap 1 (wiring waits for a commit rev): apps/zed/extension.toml already pins [grammars.spiral] rev 3c76d3ed (2026-10-14 07:25), that commit is on github-i574n/main, its grammar.js has test_header, and git diff 3c76d3ed..HEAD over apps/zed/tree-sitter-spiral is empty, so the pinned rev is the current grammar. Drift check without the tree-sitter CLI (not installed): the grammar is line-based and its test_header token is exactly ^test[ \\t]+ followed by a name of non-space non-= characters; over lib today that regex matches 402 lines in 40 files, the same 402 that ^test\\b matches (Grep count per file identical), so every compiler test header is a runnable and nothing new escapes it. Nothing CPU-light left for agents; what remains is the user's Zed check (ZED-TEST-RUNNABLES). Next: none for agents until the user reports the gutter check")
inl note_of_tree_sitter_spiral_6 () = Note ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:23Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:23 lap 5 drift check widened from lib to every repo under C:\\home\\git: ^test\\b and the grammar's test_header shape ^test[ \\t]+[^ \\t=]+ both match 922 lines in the same 133 .spi/.spir files (lib, apps, lab, blog engine, dice, alphabet, eoie src, tibia, x, polyglot), so the pinned grammar rev 3c76d3ed still makes every test header in every consumer a runnable. Nothing else for agents; the user's Zed check stays (ZED-TEST-RUNNABLES, now incl. the worktree task gap).")
inl note_of_tree_sitter_spiral_7 () = Note ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:40Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:40 lap 6 grain, the next grammar gap: outline.scm captures only test_header, because the grammar's definition_line is a single token (inl/let/type/union/nominal/prototype/instance/open/and plus the rest of the line) with no name field. Zed's outline panel and symbol search therefore list tests but none of the definitions (a lib file such as sm'.spi shows 42 tests and none of its functions). Proposed change: definition_line becomes seq(definition_keyword token, field name token.immediate([^\\s(:=]+), optional rest) at the same prec, and outline.scm gets (definition_line keyword @context name @name) @item, so definitions show up next to tests while the test headers stay exact. It needs tree-sitter generate (CLI not installed here), a grammar commit, and a new pinned rev in extension.toml, so it is the user's (or a CLI-equipped) step after the current gutter check.")
inl note_of_tree_sitter_spiral_8 () = Note ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-19T16:40Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:40 lap 8 grain: the outline gap from lap 6 is staged as lab/TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL/definition_line.py (dry run by default; anchors ok): definition_line becomes seq(definition_keyword token at prec 2 that absorbs a leading and and a trailing rec, optional name field token.immediate of an identifier or a parenthesized operator such as (~#), optional definition_rest), the same shape as test_header; outline.scm gains a definition_line capture with keyword as context, skipping open via #not-match?; a corpus case covers open, inl rec, and inl, let, an operator and nominal. Regex check over lib: all 2,569 lines the current definition_line matches get a name under the new keyword+name pair, so no line falls into an error node. No query references definition_line today, so highlights are unaffected. Left: tree-sitter CLI (not installed) for generate + test, then a new grammar rev in extension.toml")
inl note_of_tree_sitter_spiral_9 () = Note ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-19T17:15Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:16 lap 9 grain: definition_line.py proven with the real CLI on a scratch copy (npx tree-sitter-cli@0.25.10, the version of the committed parser.c, generate --abi 14). First run failed 2 of 4 corpus cases: the lexer preferred the longer definition_rest token over definition_name, and test_headers.txt still expected the old leaf definition_line. Fixed in the script: definition_name is token.immediate(prec(1, ...)) and the script rewrites that expectation (anchored once). Now: tree-sitter test 4/4, all 203 lib .spi/.spir files parse with 0 errors, outline query on am'_prototypes.spi yields item/context/name captures (array', a'). npm latest is 0.27.1 (ABI 15) = a separate bump after a Zed ABI check. Next: after the user's Zed check, definition_line.py --apply on apps/zed, generate --abi 14, test, new rev in extension.toml")
inl note_of_tree_sitter_spiral_10 () = Note ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-19T17:41Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:41 lap 10 grain: definition_line.py now also appends highlights for the new nodes ((definition_keyword) @keyword, (definition_name) @function; guarded so it applies once). Rerun on a fresh scratch copy with tree-sitter-cli 0.25.10: generate --abi 14 ok, test 4/4, highlights/runnables/outline queries all run without query errors on lib testing.spi, and highlight captures go from 52 to 116 there (every inl/let/union/open line now colours its keyword and name; the outline keeps excluding open). Zed loads the grammar from extension.toml rev 3c76d3ed (on github-i574n/main, equal to the worktree grammar), so this change reaches Zed only through a commit + rev bump by the user. Next: user's Zed check, then --apply on apps/zed, generate, test, commit, rev bump")
inl note_of_tree_sitter_spiral_11 () = Note ("TREE-SITTER-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-19T17:55Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:55 lap 11 grain, latest-of-everything for the grammar CLI: tree-sitter-cli 0.27.1 (npm latest) generates the definition_line grammar with LANGUAGE_VERSION 15 and passes test 4/4 on a scratch copy; Zed main pins tree-sitter at git rev 43623ec (2026-08-20), 903 commits past v0.25.0, the release that introduced ABI 15, so Zed loads ABI 15 grammars. Decision for the apply step: regenerate with 0.27.1 (default ABI 15) instead of 0.25.10 --abi 14; the committed parser.c then moves from ABI 14 to 15 in the same rev bump. Next: after the user's Zed check, definition_line.py --apply, npx tree-sitter-cli@0.27.1 generate + test, commit, rev bump (user)")
