inl note_of_eoie_agile_body_15 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-BODY", 15u32, "2026-10-14T23:35Z", "main-thread", "19:35 (main, from lane B's handback) renewal attempt 3 at 18:58 failed the cold budget under load: eoie_archive_contracts 22.5 s, eoie_patch_grammar 21.9 s, eoie_patch_mutation 19.2 s, eoie_agile_state 18.6 s against the 15 s limit; median 4.3 s; bundle_retention_domain 4.3 s in that run (14.9 s and 16.0 s earlier). Owner time follows machine load, so the budget is load-sensitive. Tomorrow: one renewal on a truly quiet machine first, then split whichever owners still exceed 15 s; raising the limit is not the plan. state/ is at HEAD.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_body_16 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-BODY", 16u32, "2026-10-15T01:15Z", "main-thread", "21:15 NEXT SESSION #2: the strict-evidence renewal on a truly quiet machine FIRST, before any lane starts heavy work (90 min, 1 chain). If owners still exceed 15 s, split them (granular Spiral); do not raise the limit.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_body_17 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-BODY", 17u32, "2026-10-15T01:34Z", "main-thread", "21:34 (user asked why a full eoie run takes so long; drift ruled out: the second regeneration this afternoon changed 0 of 145 owners). Time breakdown, loaded machine: build.ps1 -Test -CompilerContracts ~520 s (267 test binaries); cold rebuild a ~676 s and cold rebuild b ~676 s at 1 chain; checks a few min. The dominant cost appears to be the cold-rebuild matrix (eoie proxy cold-rebuild-matrix) starting a fresh compiler process per owner: .NET start plus core-library re-parse is ~5-7 s per AGENTS.md, so the 4.3 s median per owner is mostly fixed cost, paid ~145 x 2. This also makes the 15 s per-owner budget load-sensitive. Options: (1) one warm compiler per chain (--server-tcp or batch) with outputs still from scratch, estimated 11 -> 2-3 min per pass; (2) budget on compile time rather than process wall time. Plan: measure option 1 on a copy first; it changes what 'cold' proves, so no renewal change until measured.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_body_18 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-BODY", 18u32, "2026-10-15T02:00Z", "main-thread", "22:00 strict-evidence renewal PASSED on a quiet machine (main, 21:33-22:00): build.ps1 -Test -CompilerContracts 21:33:50-21:39:18 (5.5 min), renew-cold-rebuild -Apply -ParallelChains 3 21:39:19-21:58:29 (19 min), agile check, release closeout, strict preflight -RequireReady: ready=True, diagnostics=0. Total 26.5 min, cold budget passed. Today's failures were machine load. Renewed state/*.spi (10 files) uncommitted for the user. The warm-compiler measurement (one compiler per chain) is still worth doing to make the budget load-proof.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_body_19 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-BODY", 19u32, "2026-10-15T08:58Z", "main-thread", "04:58 strict-evidence renewal re-done after lane B's eoie src changes (Spiral test blocks, eoie_dev split into eoie_dev_spiral_tests). The 04:00 attempt stopped in preflight: crate eoie_dev 1,016 lines > 1,000 gate; fixed by the split (crate max 995). Run 04:40-04:57 (18 min, quiet machine, -Test -ParallelChains 3): 269 test suites, 216 tests passed, 0 failed; cold-rebuild matrix 146 pairs; final strict preflight ready=True diagnostics=0. state/ renewed (10 files, uncommitted).")
inl spiral_formatter () = Task ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", "Spiral formatter (user request 2026-10-13 23:48, plan only, later): probably inside the compiler to reuse its tokenizer/parser (a trivia-preserving pass over the same tokens, both editions, test blocks and ///> target lines kept), exposed as a compiler subcommand plus an eoie verb and a Zed format command; the style is the user's personal style in lib/spiral and the CLIs, mined from those files as measured rules (indentation, line breaks, operator and lambda spacing, match layout, blank lines), not invented; gates: formatting the user-authored corpus is a no-op (or only diffs the user approves), idempotent (format twice = once), and every reformatted file compiles to the same residual hash; open question for planning: which CLIs are user-authored vs agent-generated; 2026-10-13 23:48 user: the goal is to harden the 120-character line limit: every formatted line fits in 120 columns (a hard gate, with a deterministic break strategy for long applications, pipelines, records and match arms), checked by a lint over lib, CLIs and samples; 2026-10-14 01:32 style mined (read-only) from the user's own code, polyglot@bb856d13 (2025-01-12, 86 .spi/.spir, 14,683 non-blank lines; git authors cannot separate user from agents, so the split is by date, confirmed on pre-2025-09 blame lines of spiral lib/spiral and apps/spiral+apps/wasm): operators spaced 9,832/0, annotation colon spaced 4,265/0, +4 indent steps 3,871/7, multi-line body at +4 1,739/0, pipeline stages on their own lines aligned with the head 1,027/1, match arms at the match column 141/0, bare lambdas 1,168 vs 117 parenthesized, then-first if (if c / then a / else b) 107 vs 31, multi-line records with { ending the line, fields at +4 without ; 224/0, long definition heads as name / params at +4 / : type / = alone (44 vs 16 single-line heads over 120), one blank line between top-level definitions 1,923 of 2,105; 334 user lines exceed 120 but 272 of them hold a literal that cannot fit even alone, so a hard 120 gate needs a literal continuation or an allowlist (open question); rules, per-construct break strategy and open questions in C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\formatter\\style-rules.md (scratch); 2026-10-14 01:40 refinement: of the 272 unbreakable-literal lines, 230 are plain strings that !!!!StaticStringConcat folds at compile time (split possible with the residual unchanged, given an always-in-scope concat operator), 42 are macros that need a tokenizer continuation or an allowlist; a spacing-only pass would change 29 of 14,689 user lines (22 trailing whitespace); 2026-10-14 02:23 plan written (not started): spiral/apps/compiler/tmp/lanes/formatter/PLAN.md in the hopacsota PLAN.md shape: facts with counts, decisions F1-F10 (inside the compiler on the Tokenize/BlockParsing tokens, measured style only, indentation is syntax, token-level spacing rules, deterministic outermost-first break order from the user's templates, semantics checked by token sequence + residual hash, untouched comments/literals/headers, both editions, pure function, content-keyed allowlist), a banned list, phases 0-4 with gates G1 idempotence, G2 user corpus formats to itself except the 29 listed lines, G3 residual hashes identical, G4 0 lines over 120 outside allowlist.tsv, G5 determinism, G6 banned lint; Q1-Q7 are decisions awaiting the user with a recommendation each (Q1: a compile-time core concat operator for plain strings plus a macro continuation, allowlist meanwhile; Q2: keep the author's breaks)", Feature, P0, D6, 90u32, Active, "-", "compiler,tooling,formatter,main", "current", "current")
