inl spiral_dispatch_unify () = Task ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", "One backend dispatch pattern in lib/spiral and apps (user 2026-10-15 23:00): run_target and its family date from the Fable era, is_zig/is_rust guards are drift; unify on the compiler-native BackendSwitch, measured", Feature, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,spiral,backends", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:15Z", "main-thread", "23:15 main: review. History: run_target (polyglot e8e47636, 2023-10) emitted both arms into one F# file under #if FABLE_COMPILER_RUST / #else, so its API is a union target plus a function of it; when the compiler gained BackendSwitch it was re-implemented on lib backend_switch but kept that API and a 7-case union (Gleam Lua Fsharp Cuda=Python Cpp Rust TypeScript). Consequence: a run_target '| _ =>' arm never reaches Zig, C, Lean, Wasm, Bend or Delphi (they hit backend_switch's per-backend no-arm placeholder), which is why 33 is_zig () guards appeared (14 lib files) next to 45 is_rust (); usage 10-15: run_target family ~150 uses in 21 files, backend_switch records 217 in 30, direct BackendSwitch 4. A Zig case in the union would break every exhaustive run_target match, and GADTs or prototypes do not help because the backend is a compile-time constant, not a value to refine.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 2u32, "2026-10-16T03:15Z", "main-thread", "23:15 main: benchmark (job tmp/dispatchbench: generate.ps1 + run-batch.ps1, 400 generated functions per style, one warm --batch process, first-compile ms, medians over 4 rotated style orders; Rust column = parse + typecheck + peval, Zig/C = peval + codegen): run_target 1,955 / 1,135 / 851; is_zig guard 2,899 / 618 / 1,294; record listing all 14 keys 3,263 / 1,734 / 1,437; record with only the needed keys + a default (backend_switch_or) 1,920 / 941 / 838. Decision: backend_switch_or (fallback) { needed keys } everywhere: cost-neutral or better than run_target, its default really covers every backend, every arm still validated, no union, no guards; listing every key is the slowest form and is avoided. Grain 1 done: backend_switch_or added to lib/spiral/backend.spi, platform.is_windows migrated (is_zig guard + run_target -> one record with Rust/Fsharp/Gleam/Zig + failwith default): Rust/F#/Zig/Gleam outputs byte-identical, C changes from the uncompilable backend_switch_has_no_C_arm placeholder to a runtime failwith, platform.spi Kino test 1/1. Next: the 33 is_zig guard sites file by file with the lib sweep (lane-b lib-sweep.ps1) before/after, then the standalone run_target sites; run_target and its union are deleted when the last caller is gone.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 3u32, "2026-10-16T04:20Z", "main-thread", "00:20 main: grain 2 done in the tree: all 32 is_zig () guard chains in lib/spiral (am', base, common, console x9, crypto, env, file_system x5, guid x2, listm', sm' x4, testing, trace x3) rewritten by tmp/dispatchbench/guards-to-records.ps1 into inl otherwise () = <old else branch, unchanged> + backend_switch_or otherwise { Rust/Zig keys from the old branches }; is_zig () now has 0 callers outside backend.spi. Needed by hand: type annotations on 4 sites (record arms are separate fields, so a bare macro arm is a value-restriction error; a typed_arm helper does not work because Spiral generalizes the local otherwise) and Kino's cell console.spi gained CppHost/C/Delphi/Wasm/Bend keys (lib records validate all 14 keys, and inside a Kino cell console.write_line is Kino's 9-key switch). Proof: probe programs for 15 migrated functions x 8 backends (tmp/dispatchbench/probes.ps1): 118 of 120 outputs byte-identical, same 91 compile; the 2 differences are substring on C (old: is_rust () evaluated to the backend_switch_has_no_C_arm placeholder as a C bool; new: the real default) and file_delete on Zig (extra unused trace globals, BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK). Lib sweep of the 13 touched notebooks 126/126 before and after; harness rows of the 7 lib-using samples 53/53 parity with the oracle, DISAGREE 0; Kino mix test 239 (1 known env-only failure); Tibia combat.spi 7/7. Semantic lesson: a record arm is evaluated (for its type) under every backend's context, an if-guard branch never was, so code moved into a default must be valid under every key. Not done: apps/spiral regeneration; the standalone run_target sites (~100) and the 45 is_rust () uses (step 3).")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 4u32, "2026-10-16T04:48Z", "main-thread", "00:48 main: design revised with evidence. Converting the ~100 run_target matches to records would need a type annotation on every arm (records make each arm a separate field; matches unify their arms), so the scalable unification is the other way round: complete union backend (+ Zig, C, Lean, Delphi, Wasm, Bend; run_target_args' maps every BackendSwitch key), so every | _ => really reaches every backend and Zig gets | Zig => arms; the 24 step-2 records whose default is a run_target fold back into it (tmp/dispatchbench/fold-zig-arms.ps1). Proof in a lib copy: probes 120/120 identical to the unfolded union version, 113/120 identical to the current tree, the 7 differences all C rows moving from backend_switch_has_no_C_arm placeholders to the _ default; only base.spi's test-only exhaustive match needed a | _ arm. Cost (400 generated functions, 4 rotations, machine loaded by a concurrent review): the complete-union run_target is +28% (Rust), +16% (Zig), +15% (C) over today's 8-key run_target, and +28/+24/+38% over a record with a default. Decision: complete union for value-level dispatch (correctness on the Zig default and every backend outweighs the cost); records stay for type-level switches and keyed defaults; is_rust/is_zig go. Applying to the tree after the pack-1p review frees the lib, with a sweep of every run_target notebook.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 5u32, "2026-10-16T04:49Z", "main-thread", "00:49 main: is_rust () decision: the ~30 standalone uses in lib (async, resultm, threading, date_time, am', parsing, runtime, convert, wasm, testing, file_system) stay. They are plain two-way if splits, not guards in front of a run_target; a static if evaluates only the taken branch, so it compiles cheaper and avoids the every-backend evaluation trap that the Kino console hit. Rule: a guard in front of a dispatch is drift and becomes an arm (done for all is_zig sites); a standalone binary predicate is allowed.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 6u32, "2026-10-16T07:18Z", "main-thread", "03:18 main: final design applied to the tree lib (14 files from the proven copy tmp/dispatchbench/libunion; backups tmp/rotation/lib-before-union): union backend has every key, run_target_args' maps each, lib dispatch is run_target matches, backend_switch_or removed (no code references left). AGENTS.md lib dispatch paragraph rewritten (guard chain in front of a dispatch = drift; standalone is_rust () predicate = fine). Lib sweep on the installed pack-1p DLL: 14 of 38 notebooks pass (am', crypto, guid, parsing, physics, runtime, sm', testing, trace, rust/testing, ...) before a memory reap; the remaining 24 run after the pack-1q probes.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 7u32, "2026-10-16T08:58Z", "main-thread", "04:58 main: lib sweep of the tree lib (complete-union design) on the installed compiler: 38/38 notebooks pass, 329 tests, 0 failed (02:41-03:08 first 19 before a memory reap, 177 tests; 04:47-04:58 the other 19 on pack-1q, 152 tests: date_time 32, seq 26, listm' 22, stream 16, file_system 15, math 14, ...). Harness: the pack-1q review shows 0 residual changes on every non-Kotlin/Swift backend, so the rewrite is output-neutral on all harness samples. Left: a Kino mix test on the tree lib, and the kino console.spi switch (records with every key) is fine as is.")
inl note_of_spiral_dispatch_unify_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DISPATCH-UNIFY", 8u32, "2026-10-16T10:07Z", "main-thread", "06:07 main: Kino mix test on the tree (union-design lib, installed pack-1r compiler): 239 tests, 0 failures, 1 excluded (06:04-06:07). All proofs in: probes 120/120 vs the fold, harness 0 residual changes on non-Kotlin/Swift backends (pack-1q review), lib sweep 38/38 notebooks 329 tests, Kino 239/0. Done; the 14 lib files + backend.spi + AGENTS.md paragraph are ready to commit.")
inl bend2_in_spiral () = Task ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", "Rewrite the Bend 2 checker and compiler (TypeScript today) in Spiral with GADTs/existentials/HKTs/prototypes/reflection from day one, as a benchmark against the TS version and Bend 2 self-hosted later (user 2026-10-15 23:00)", Epic, P1, D8, 0u32, Planned, "LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS", "bend,spiral,kernel,benchmark,main,lab-held", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_bend2_in_spiral () = Planning ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:15Z", "main-thread", "23:15 main: assessment of the local clone (spiral-bin toolchains/bend, bendlang/bend d3cacb7 2026-10-08): core ~12.8k TS lines (bend2/comp.ts 6,385, bend.ts 3,882, safe.ts 1,567, main.ts 924) plus 1,693 .bend test files (tests/check 265, flatten 245, io 157, parse 153, compile 130, reg 111, run 96, proof 94); the repo ships its own flake.nix. Worth it: the test corpus is a ready differential oracle (TS implementation as reference, the Tibia pattern), a native Spiral checker gives the speed benchmark against bun/TS and later Bend-in-Bend, and its term/substitution/normalization/conversion core is the same layer as LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS, so both kernels can share bottom modules. Cost: fast upstream churn (#1337 on 10-08). Start after the Lean kernel's first layers: parser + checker against the 418 parse/check tests, measured against bun bend2.")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:13 helper-lane lap 1: churn measured (git fetch into the toolchain clone only, worktree still d3cacb7): upstream bendlang/bend moved 105 commits in 11 days, d3cacb7 (10-08, #1337) -> 8f86f73 (10-19 09:05 -0300, #1653), and bend2/ alone changed 21 files, +2,292/-1,526 lines. Today's local bend2 sizes (non-blank lines): comp.ts 5,756, bend.ts 3,674, safe.ts 1,457, main.ts 853. So a Spiral port chasing HEAD would rewrite ~30% of the checker per fortnight: the port must target one pinned commit and use the 1,693 .bend tests of that commit as the oracle, rebasing deliberately. Still gated on LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS.")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:05Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:05 helper-lane lap 2: listed the kernel layer a shared bottom module must cover, from the local clone (d3cacb7) bend2/bend.ts (156 top-level functions) and safe.ts (78): evaluation term_wnf, term_snf, term_force, term_apply/term_unapply; conversion term_compare, term_descend, term_key; binding ctx_bind, tele_bind, tele_unbind, term_higher/term_lower (de Bruijn level shifts); checking Infer, Check, term_infer, term_check, term_check_kind, tele_check, def_check; and safe.ts's subst, scope_bind, kernel_check, safe_check, inferable. That is the same layer as LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS' whnf/defeq/infer, so the shared Spiral module boundary is: terms with levels + shift (higher/lower) + subst, then wnf/snf, then compare, then infer/check; the parser (parse_term* , ~11 functions) stays per-language.")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_4 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:22 helper-lane lap 3: answered the term-representation question by reading bend2/bend.ts (d3cacb7): Bend 2 keeps TWO forms, LTerm = TermOf<[LTerm]> whose Var.i is a de Bruijn LEVEL (term_lower line 782 binds Var(k, d + j) at depth d) and HTerm = TermOf<HBody> where every binder body is a host closure (x: HTerm) => HTerm (HOAS); term_higher (693) evaluates LTerm into HTerm by indexing an env list by level, i.e. normalization by evaluation with levels for quoting. Lean 4's kernel is locally nameless instead (bvar indices + fvar ids, instantiate/abstract). Decision for the shared bottom module: levels plus NbE values whose binders are DEFUNCTIONALIZED closures (env list, body term), not host lambdas: levels need no shifting under binders (Bend's choice), Lean's fvars map onto levels at check time, and defunctionalized closures stay comparable and printable on every Spiral backend (C/Zig/Lua have no cheap first-class closures; HOAS bodies cannot be hashed for term_key). Lean's instantiate becomes eval in an extended env. Still gated on LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS (Active 5%).")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_5 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:30Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:30 helper-lane lap 4: wrote the lap-3 representation decision as code, lab/BEND2-IN-SPIRAL/nbe.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): terms with de Bruijn LEVELS (Var level, Lam body, App), values with defunctionalized closures (VLam of env list * body, plus neutral VVar level and VApp), eval extends a closure's env by one value per binder (the binder's level is the env length), apply beta-reduces or builds a neutral, and quote depth reads a VLam back by evaluating its body against VVar depth, so no index shifting appears anywhere (the property that made Bend choose levels). Check: the normal form of Church two applied to two is lambda lambda with 4 applications, and (lambda x. x) (lambda y. y) quotes to Lam (Var 0); expected exit 142. This is the bottom layer the Lean kernel notebooks and a Bend 2 checker would share.")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_6 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-19T16:00Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 12:00 helper-lane lap 5: extended nbe.spi with the next kernel layer (unrun until the freeze all-clear): Pi of domain * codomain (codomain under one binder) and Typ in terms, VPi carrying the domain value plus a defunctionalized codomain closure and VTyp in values, eval/quote for both, and conversion as equality of normal forms (conv a b = quote 0 a = quote 0 b). New checks: (lambda A. A) Typ converts to Typ, (lambda A. Pi A A) Typ converts to Pi Typ Typ (the codomain's level-0 reference resolves through the closure env), and Typ does not convert to Pi Typ Typ; expected exit 111142 together with the earlier 142.")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_7 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: nbe.spi failed to parse at a one-line nested pattern (match identity with | Lam (Var 0i32) => ...); replaced by an is_identity helper with nested multi-line matches, rerun pending.")
inl note_of_bend2_in_spiral_8 () = Note ("BEND2-IN-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 13:42 (subagent): parse error on one-line nested pattern; fixed with helper -> next: rerun (expect 111142) | lap 6 13:49 (subagent): nbe probe PASS (111142): levels NbE + Pi/Typ + conversion -> next: infer/check | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: nbe.spi 111142 -> next: next reduction rule")
inl crate_gate_harden () = Task ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", "Harden the 1,000-line generated-crate gate (user 2026-10-15 23:00: nice seeing the 1k loc paying off): it already counts .rs + .spi per crate dir in eoie source-topology; make it a gate in every repo that emits Rust crates and decide whether .spi stays inside the count", TaskKind, P1, D3, 45u32, Active, "EOIE-CRATE-GATE", "eoie,gate,rust,lab-held", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_crate_gate_harden () = Planning ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", Minutes 75u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_1 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:15Z", "main-thread", "23:15 main: evidence 10-15: the gate stopped the strict renewal when agile_todo_scan reached 1,184 (922 .rs + 167 .spi + 95 notebook .spi) and forced a clean split into sources/scan crates; the largest crate after the split is command_spec_domain at 998, so it must be split before the next agile verb.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_2 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 2u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night inventory: gated: eoie (crate max 949-998), x (report-only, view.rs 1,885 over); ungated monoliths: apps/spiral spiral.rs 24,281 lines, dice ui main.rs 16,878, dice lib dice.rs 6,639, apps/wasm spiral_wasm.rs 3,464.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_3 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 3u32, "2026-10-18T05:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 01:53 x: all 12 generated crates under 1,000 after the view split (view 999, digest 989, jsonl 929, args 889, xc 862); the margin on view is 1 line, so the gate stays report-only until RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION or a further split gives room")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_4 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:45Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:45 helper-lane lap 1: re-inventory 10-19 (lines of the generated .rs): ungated monoliths apps/spiral spiral.rs 23,418 (24,281 on 10-17), dice ui src/main.rs 16,878 (same), dice lib dice.rs 7,777 (6,639 on 10-17, grew 17 percent), apps/wasm spiral_wasm.rs 3,283, dice contract dice_contract.rs 3,396 and polyglot chat_contract.rs 1,374 (NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES); x's largest crates are now digest 991, view 991, args 960, so view gained 8 lines of margin since 10-18 but the report-only mode should stay until there are ~5 percent of headroom. The gate (eoie source-topology) still exists only in eoie; dice lib growing past 7.7k is the strongest case for adding report-only counts to dice's build first (it mirrors to alphabet).")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_5 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:31 helper-lane lap 2: wrote the report-only step as lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/crate-report.ps1 (a generated crate = an .rs with a sibling .spi; prints those over the limit, never fails) and ran it on dice, alphabet, spiral/apps and polyglot/apps: 448 generated crates, 12 over 1,000 lines - spiral.rs 23,418, alphabet apps/documents documents.rs 15,574 (missing from the 10-17 inventory), dice lib dice.rs 7,777, dice contract 3,396, spiral_wasm.rs 3,283, chat_contract 1,374, and 6 compiler samples' main.rs (mega roots and typecheck_long_let_body, which are matrix fixtures and should be excluded from any gate). dice/ui/src/main.rs (16,878) is not caught because its source .spi is not beside it, so the rule needs the [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output convention (x's) to find every generated crate. Call it in-process (& script -Root a, b): pwsh -File collapses the -Root array.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_6 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:13 helper-lane lap 3: switched the report to the planned detection (lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/crate-report.ps1, still report-only): generated crates = outputs declared by a Cargo.toml [package.metadata.spiral] output = path, plus the sibling rule (an .rs beside its .spi); apps/compiler/tmp/samples (matrix fixtures) and target/deps/dist are excluded. Run over dice, alphabet, spiral/apps, polyglot/apps, x and blog: 26 generated crates (17 declared by metadata, all of them x's, every one under 1,000 lines), 6 over the limit, all sibling-rule: spiral.rs 23,418, alphabet documents.rs 15,574, dice lib dice.rs 7,777, dice contract 3,396, spiral_wasm.rs 3,283, chat_contract 1,374. dice/ui/src/main.rs (16,878) is still invisible because neither rule reaches it; giving dice ui's Cargo.toml the x-style [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output is the one-line fix that makes it count. Next: propose the step as non-failing in dice and alphabet build.ps1 (mirror rule) after their CI is green.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_7 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:38 helper-lane lap 4: staged the one-line fix that makes dice ui visible to the gate: lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/dice_ui_metadata.py adds [package.metadata.spiral] entry = src/dice_ui.spi, output = src/main.rs to dice/ui/Cargo.toml (anchor --check OK; values read from ui/build.ps1, which compiles src/dice_ui.spi to src/main.rs). Cargo ignores package.metadata, so builds are unaffected; with it the report finds main.rs (16,878 lines) as the seventh over-limit crate. The report step for dice and alphabet build.ps1 waits for their CI to be green (mirror rule; alphabet has no ui crate, its counterpart is apps/documents).")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_8 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 8u32, "2026-10-19T21:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:40 helper-lane lap 6: lab/CRATE-GATE-HARDEN/dice_ui_metadata.py dry run matches dice/ui/Cargo.toml today. Per the dice/alphabet mirror rule it gets applied to both repos at once.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_9 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 9u32, "2026-10-21T22:50Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:50 x: reference implementation for other repos: x/scripts/gate.ps1 reads [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output from every apps/**/Cargo.toml, counts the generated output's lines and, with -Enforce, throws on any crate over -Limit (1000) and on finding no crate at all (vacuity). x's apps/client/build.ps1 now runs it with -Enforce right after compiling the crates (until today it only reported). For dice/alphabet: once dice_ui_metadata.py adds the metadata, the same script with -Root works unchanged. On .spi in the count: x counts only generated .rs, which is what the 1k limit protects.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_10 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 10u32, "2026-10-21T23:16Z", "eoie-lane", "19:16 eoie team, evidence 10-21 (generated .rs lines from previews on trees rebuilt from live): the gate fired four times on tonight's drafts, each before any release: analysis.rs 1108 (r34 where filters inlined through the eoie_table_query package), watch.rs 1208 (r35 job-state code inlined through eoie_jobs), read_query.rs 1007 (r35 grep root + tail), and gate.rs reached exactly 1000 (r35 window holder). Fixes were crate boundaries, never trimming: a row-mask export (eoie_table_rows_kept, analysis.rs 965, table_test.rs 736), a job-state export (eoie_job_state_word, watch.rs 954), a crate split (eoie_grep_query; read_query.rs 993 -> 632), and computing range bounds once. Pattern: a Spiral package import inlines the used functions into every consumer, so a shared helper growing in one package pushes all its importers toward the gate at once; an export at the crate boundary is the structural fix. Margins after r34/r35/r36: gate.rs 1000 (0), do_program.rs 988, do_edit.rs 976, analysis.rs 965, command_spec.rs 962, watch.rs 954, experiments.rs 949. Proposal for the gate: report the 10 crates nearest 1000 in every preview so a slice sees its margin before it lands.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_11 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 11u32, "2026-10-23T10:58Z", "main", "2026-10-23 06:58 main: dice and alphabet now carry the crate metadata x's gate reads (unstaged). dice ui/Cargo.toml gets [package.metadata.spiral] entry src/dice_ui.spi, output src/main.rs (lab dice_ui_metadata.py, applied). alphabet apps/documents/Cargo.toml gets the mirror, entry documents.spi, output documents.rs. Measured: both are far over the limit today. dice ui main.rs is 16,281 generated lines (dist copy 16,878), dice contract is the 178 KB dice_contract.rs (NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES), alphabet documents.rs is 15,315. So the gate cannot be enforced in these repos until their output is split into module crates (GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES, NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES), and until then it runs report-only. Two gaps in reusing x/scripts/gate.ps1 as is: it scans only apps/**/Cargo.toml (dice keeps its crates in ui/ and contract/, so it would find none and fail its vacuity check), and the same counting already lives in eoie source-topology. Per eoie-first, the cross-repo gate should be one eoie verb ('eoie crate gate <root> [--enforce] [--limit 1000]' over every Cargo.toml with [package.metadata.spiral]), not a fourth copy of the script; filing that request for the eoie team.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_12 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 12u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): dice ui metadata patch staged; dice CI red -> next: after green: apply + report step | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: dice_ui_metadata.py dry run clean -> next: apply with the alphabet mirror")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_13 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 13u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 75m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 40%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_14 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 14u32, "2026-10-25T00:59Z", "eoie", "20:59 From x's build research (finding 7): generated functions grow superlinearly in LLVM and MIR dataflow. The crate gate already counts lines per crate, so add a per-crate report of the largest function (name, lines) to show where to cut. Done when eoie crate gate prints largest_fn=<name>:<lines> per crate and a notebook case checks it on a fixture crate.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_15 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 15u32, "2026-10-25T11:23Z", "eoie", "07:23 eoie: the largest_fn milestone (note 2) is built: each crate gate row ends with largest_fn=<name>:<lines> (the first on a tie; generics end the name). model.spi has largest_function_of (a pure line scan for fn and pub fn) plus largest_text, the counters are folded into one verdict_count, and every line is now at or under 120 (the legacy long lines were migrated in the same change). The gate.spi walker reads each output once for both counts. Spec case 43's expected rows carry the new column. The model notebook passes, including the_largest_function_names_where_to_cut; regen plus cargo check of eoie_crate_gate on 5k is running. Motivation from today: the eoie_cli (direct_verb_usage 136 lines) and agile_task_listing (list_request_from 143) cuts were both found by hand profiling that this column now prints. Left: the per-repo gate in every repo that emits Rust crates, and the decision on .spi in the count.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_16 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 16u32, "2026-10-25T11:27Z", "eoie", "07:27 eoie: correction to note 15. The model notebook passed and cargo check was green, but on 5k the regen put eoie_crate_gate at 1092 lines (926 published): function_name 73 + largest_from 50 + largest_function_of 23 + helpers. That breaks the gate the feature exists to serve, and the package is HostFree, so a sibling-crate call is not allowed either. Reverted under the 950 rule (gate.spi and model.spi match the before-s-stages snapshot by hash; spec case 43 rows restored). Draft kept at scratch clean\\gate-largest\\ (model with largest_function_of, largest_text, verdict_count and the migrated long lines; gate.spi reading each output once). Next step, ordered: split eoie_crate_gate by hand first (manifest scan and walk in one crate, measure and verdict rows in another, or a Spiral-only model package shared by two crates), then re-add largest_fn into the half with room, and measure. A cheaper scan (split on newline-fn instead of per-line substring and index_of) is the alternative to try first if the split costs more.")
inl note_of_crate_gate_harden_17 () = Note ("CRATE-GATE-HARDEN", 17u32, "2026-10-25T11:40Z", "eoie", "07:40 eoie: cheaper-scan arm measured (one split on newline-fn plus a chunk loop using the existing line_count): eoie_crate_gate 1011 on 5k (versus 1092 for the per-line scan, 926 published before 5j). The model notebook passes and cargo check is green, but the crate is over 950, so it is reverted under the same rule (both files match the snapshot by hash; draft kept as scratch clean\\gate-largest\\model-cheap-scan.spi). Conclusion: largest_fn does not fit inside eoie_crate_gate at its current size, so the split comes first, and the full 5l' measure will show the gate's own baseline on the new compiler. Split plan: a Spiral-only eoie_crate_gate_model package (manifest parse, verdicts, rows, largest_fn), plus the walker crate and a measure crate exporting row text per output file, so the walker never holds the row code.")
inl x_repo_template () = Task ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", "i574n/x as the from-scratch Spiral repo template (create-react-app style): find the shape every new Spiral repo should start from; record each decision with its reason and measured cost", Story, P2, D3, 82u32, Blocked, "", "template,spiral,x,ci", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_repo_template () = Planning ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", Minutes 180u32, LoadUnset, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_repo_template_1 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:27Z", "claude", "(2026-10-15 23:27 -04:00, Claude b7595ac3) Decisions so far: (1) spiral cloned beside the repo (eoie model), no symlinks (dice's deps/polyglot link is the older, slower model) and no SPIRAL_ROOT override, because package.spiproj fixes packageDir ../../../spiral/lib; scripts/common.ps1 resolves XRoot/SpiralRoot and fails with the init hint. (2) scripts/init.ps1 clones spiral from the same owner and runs spiral's init-app-build; scripts/build.ps1 builds every app; scripts/workflow.ps1 = init + build; one CI file .github/workflows/ci.yml (ubuntu + windows) runs workflow.ps1, nothing else standardized (yml standardization stays with the main lane). (3) Root Cargo workspace, stable Rust (generated Rust builds on cargo 1.99.0, no nightly pin), release profile lto+cg1. (4) Kino test blocks run from the sibling Kino with SPIRAL_KINO_COMPILER=process (no daemon left), outputs under target/. (5) .gitattributes LF + generated client.rs marked. Measured locally (spiral already beside, 16 GB box, heavy gate): full scripts/build.ps1 517 s (client: cargo cold 130 s, e2e ~60 s, 4 zig test files ~180 s; sim: mix test 30/30, corpus run ~60 s, kino executor run ~30 s); client-only rebuild 345 s. Not measured yet: init from scratch, Linux CI.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_2 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 2u32, "2026-10-16T03:31Z", "claude", "(2026-10-15 23:31 -04:00, Claude b7595ac3) Open decision: the sibling clone tracks spiral's branch tip with no pin. Everything in x was proven against spiral's uncommitted working tree (tonight's compiler packs, other lanes' Kino edits), so CI (init.ps1 clones spiral HEAD) can differ until spiral is committed and pushed. Options: a ref in init.ps1, or a recorded validated spiral commit per x commit. Also measured: stable Rust via rust-toolchain.toml; the generated client builds on cargo 1.99.0.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_3 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 3u32, "2026-10-16T05:40Z", "claude", "2026-10-16 01:40 template shape now includes a Cargo workspace of apps/<app>/<crate>/ entries, each Cargo.toml carrying [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output, a metadata-driven build loop, and scripts/gate.ps1 (report-only, -Enforce throws) for the 1000-line generated-file rule")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_4 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night inventory of x/scripts (4.5 KB): generic as-is init.ps1 (clone spiral beside from the remote owner and domain, spiral init-app-build), gate.ps1 (1,000-line generated Rust gate over package.metadata.spiral crates) and workflow.ps1; common.ps1 is generic except the XRoot name; only build.ps1 lists the apps. The blog repo is the second consumer to prove the template on.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_5 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 5u32, "2026-10-18T05:20Z", "claude", "(2026-10-18 01:20 -04:00, Claude fc1568a6) User decision, supersedes the open pin question in note 2: no spiral pin. Consumer repos keep cloning spiral main for dogfooding; a red downstream run after a spiral bug is the intended signal (fix spiral, rerun). Stop suggesting a pin unless a concrete new friction outweighs dogfooding.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_6 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 6u32, "2026-10-18T06:41Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 02:41 closed, not open: the template clones spiral main with no pin (user 2026-10-18, memory slide-with-spiral-main). A spiral regression turning x red is the dogfooding signal: fix spiral and rerun x CI. The 2026-10-15 23:31 'open decision' note is superseded")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_7 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 7u32, "2026-10-18T12:14Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 08:14 measured since note 1: Linux CI runs (ci.yml: xc-windows 1.4-2.1 min, build ubuntu-24.04 7.8 min, build windows-2025 11.4 min on the green run 39075003305); CI pins OTP 27 / Elixir 1.18 while the box runs OTP 28. Still unmeasured: init.ps1 from an empty folder")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_8 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 8u32, "2026-10-18T14:47Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 10:47 CI toolchain bumped to the latest releases in x, spiral, eoie, dice, alphabet, blog, polyglot, i574n.github, tapas2, vault: OTP 27 -> 29 (29.1.2, 2026-10-14), Elixir 1.18 -> 1.20 (1.20.4 supports OTP 27-29), Gleam -> 1.19.2, rebar3 3.24.0 -> 3.27.3, setup-beam@v1 -> v1.24.1 where floating. Checked locally on official Elixir 1.20.4/OTP 29: x/apps/sim compiles with --warnings-as-errors, 30/30; Cells had 4 new 1.20 type-checker warnings (3 unpinned size() variables in domain.ex, 1 unreachable warm/1 clause), fixed; Cells still builds on 1.19 and the sim drift guard plus a real-Cells run pass. fc1943s backups, mechahaze and tibia untouched")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_9 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 9u32, "2026-10-18T18:17Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 14:17 template gotcha: .github/workflows/actions.lock (gh actions-lock) is enforced by GitHub; a commit that adds an action to a workflow without regenerating the lock fails at startup in 0 s with no log (x run 39140732842: the gh-pages publish job's download-artifact and actions-gh-pages were missing from the committed lock). The template should regenerate the lock in the same commit as any workflow change")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_10 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 10u32, "2026-10-19T00:17Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 20:17 init from an empty folder, measured on clean CI runners instead of locally (run 39137498346, the last green ci run): ubuntu-24.04 job 8.4 min, of which scripts/workflow.ps1 (init.ps1 clones spiral beside + init-app-build, then the whole build: client crates, cargo, e2e, sim) 7.6 min; windows-2025 job 19.1 min: moonrepo/setup-rust 6.5 min (toolchain install dominates), setup-beam 0.6, workflow.ps1 11.5; xc-windows (xc only) 1.4 min. Locally the same takes 30-60 min on this shared machine, so CI is the reference for template cost. Left: fold the gh-pages publish job and the actions.lock rule into the template, and the yml standardization the main lane owns")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_11 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 11u32, "2026-10-20T03:51Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-19 23:51 CI red on x 07b119c (both build jobs): apps/sim/build.ps1 fixture guard, sim-small.world.json stale since the prelude grew (cut, delimited, digit helpers; p2 6 -> 9 posts). Fixed uncommitted: build.ps1 -Accept regenerated the 4 sim worlds/expected, prelude.events.jsonl and prelude.final.json (sim-stress and thread-oauth1 unchanged), e2e -Accept rewrote the one README walkthrough output (xc thread 3000001); sim build green incl. xsim.run invariants and thread_run, client e2e 31+5. Template lesson: any edit to a client .spi that a sim world is built from needs apps/sim/build.ps1 before the commit")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_12 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 12u32, "2026-10-20T06:34Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 02:34 actions.lock rule is now a check in the template, not just a note: scripts/actions-lock.ps1 compares every .github/workflows/*.yml uses: (skipping ./ and docker://) with the lock's per-workflow list and throws with missing/stale entries and the fix (gh actions-lock, commit with the workflow change); scripts/build.ps1 runs it first, so a local build catches the 0 s startup failure before a push. Proven both ways: real repo passes (8 actions), a bumped setup-python fails naming missing v6.4.0 and stale v6.3.0. Also e2e.ps1 steps now set ProgressPreference SilentlyContinue: windows-2025 serialized a progress record as #< CLIXML into a step's stderr (run 39367603402, xc refresh step)")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_13 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 13u32, "2026-10-20T23:06Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 19:06 template lesson: apps/sim/build.ps1 ran the byte-exact ExUnit suite before its fixture guard, so a client source edit that a sim world is built from (posts.spi -> sim-stress) could not be accepted through build.ps1 -Accept (ExUnit failed first and the accept step never ran). mix test now runs after the guard: without -Accept the guard fails first with 'rerun apps/sim/build.ps1 -Accept and review the diff', with -Accept the fixtures refresh and ExUnit then checks them byte for byte (x-chain23 green both ways)")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_14 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 14u32, "2026-10-21T03:15Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 23:15 pre-commit proof (eoie job x-precommit, 22:01-23:14): CI's own scripts/build.ps1 (no -fast) over the uncommitted tree exits 0 locally: actions-lock 8 actions locked, crate gate 16 crates 0 over 1000, x-compat 0 in 36 files, postable 1617 posts with 0 cells over one post, e2e tutorial and extra cases incl. delete-thread 34, Cells 35 of 35 notebook files with 0 failures, sim ExUnit 43/43 after the fixture guard, corpus commute on both schedules, real-Cells executor run, thread_run accepts the thread and rejects a tampered one. CI is red on HEAD 61b8fff only from the windows-2025 CLIXML e2e failure (run 39367603402) that the uncommitted e2e.ps1 ProgressPreference fix addresses, so the user's next commit of this tree is expected to turn ci green; D1 needs two consecutive green runs on main")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_15 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 15u32, "2026-10-21T21:22Z", "x", "2026-10-21 17:22 x: grooming. Status Active -> Blocked so the stop gate's ready list stops offering it: x's part is verified (CI's own scripts/build.ps1 green locally on the uncommitted tree; 10-21 x-split-fix3 green after the post_id slice and the lib Gleam fixes). The remainder is the user's commit and push plus two consecutive green ci runs on main (X-DONE D1), which no agent can do. Unblock when the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_16 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 16u32, "2026-10-22T03:48Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:48 x: template pieces added tonight (all in x, uncommitted). scripts/gate.ps1 -Compiler <dll> regenerates every crate with a candidate compiler and reports identical/DIFFERS before an install. The gate also measures every non-Spiral file against 1,000 lines with a named data allowlist (report-only until the generated Gleam is split). apps/sim/build.ps1 -Ladder runs scale ladders. If tonight's sim check passes, scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 splits generated Gleam into <=1,000-line modules. Consumers of the template inherit all of them; the publish step should move to git checkout-index per TREE-PAGES-V2 note 1.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_17 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 17u32, "2026-10-22T12:22Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:22 x: the template's shape now lives in x itself, not only in these notes: README section 'Starting another Spiral repo from x' (4 bullets, checked against the files). (1) scripts/ as is, only build.ps1 names the apps; init.ps1 clones spiral beside from the remote's owner, unpinned (note 5). (2) ci.yml + actions.lock, with gh actions-lock in the same commit (notes 9, 12). (3) Root Cargo.toml workspace, rust-toolchain.toml, .cargo/config.toml, .gitattributes, .gitignore. (4) package.spiproj packageDir ../../../spiral/lib, and per-crate Cargo.toml [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output read by apps/client/build.ps1's loop. Correction to note 1: the toolchain is pinned to nightly-2026-10-18 now, not stable. The blog repo (note 4) remains the second consumer that proves it.")
inl note_of_x_repo_template_18 () = Note ("X-REPO-TEMPLATE", 18u32, "2026-10-23T00:26Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:26 x: grooming check before the commit. Every file the README's 'Starting another Spiral repo from x' section names exists in the tree (scripts/init.ps1 and build.ps1, ci.yml + actions.lock, Cargo.toml, rust-toolchain.toml, .cargo/config.toml, .gitattributes, .gitignore, apps/client/package.spiproj with packageDir ../../../spiral/lib). The Rust pin agrees in all three places, so a repo made from the template inherits one nightly: rust-toolchain.toml channel, ci.yml's two setup-rust channels, and Cells' own +nightly-2026-10-18. Note 23:48's publish point is already met: spiral's publish-tree.ps1 copies with git checkout-index. Status stays Blocked on the user's commit and two green CI runs (X-DONE D1).")
