inl backend_switch_globals_leak () = Task ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", "BackendSwitch validation is not hermetic for globals (found 23:58 2026-10-15 while migrating lib is_zig guards): evaluating a non-selected arm for its type runs global calls it reaches, so their text lands in the selected backend's output", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,backend-switch,both-cores", "current", "current")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 1u32, "2026-10-16T03:58Z", "main-thread", "23:58 main: repro: file_system.file_delete as a backend_switch_or record; on Zig the validated default arm calls trace Critical (Rust path), and trace's own Zig arm registers spiralHold/GetLocalTime/spiralClock globals, which then appear in the Zig output although nothing uses them (probe tmp/dispatchbench probes-before/after file_delete main.zig). AGENTS.md says validation pushes into a throwaway statement list and its own variable counter; globals are not covered. Fix direction (shared section, both cores): snapshot the globals table before validating each non-current arm and restore it afterwards, like the statement list; expect residual-only shrinkage on rows whose records reach global-emitting code. Harmless today (unused definitions), but it makes records emit more than if-guards did.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 2u32, "2026-10-16T05:16Z", "main-thread", "01:16 main: fixed in pack-1p (tree core, both cores): backend_switch_validation_floor names the 1 <<< 30 validation counter base, and the 4 Global sites (single-flight term Global; gpt5parallel term Global, nested term Global, worklist replay, raw unit annotation) record a global only when s.i.contents < floor, i.e. outside any validation, nested evaluations included. Review: 0 harness rows changed by it. Probes on the guard-migrated lib: 17 outputs lose only unreferenced leaked globals (SpiralUse in Rust, std_* type aliases in F#, unused Gleam imports, spiralClock/spiralHold/GetLocalTime in Zig, spiral_node_fs/spiral_trace_state in TypeScript); file_delete Zig is byte-identical to its pre-migration output again. Install + bless pending.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_3 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 3u32, "2026-10-16T05:51Z", "main-thread", "01:51 main: fix withdrawn from pack-1p. The corpus check on the candidate failed lib file_system 12/15 (get_source_directory, get_full_path, trace_file: dotnet fsi failed): the suppressed globals included F# type aliases (type std_path_PathBuf = string, std_string_String, Ref) that F# code uses. They come from backend.rust_type -> fsharp_global, called inside Rust arms; in an F# build those arms are only validated, and the nested fsharp_global switch then runs its Fsharp arm with s.backend = Fsharp = env.backend. The compiler comment at the Global site documents this as intended (F#-only declarations run in every build). So the 'leak' is load-bearing for F#. Real fix: make rust_type's F# alias come from the type use (the nominal's F# side) or from a declaration evaluated outside arms, then suppress globals in validation; until then the unused extra globals (Zig spiralClock etc.) stay. Status back to Planned.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_4 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 4u32, "2026-10-16T06:35Z", "main-thread", "02:35 main: correction: the withdrawn change did alter one harness row, portable_convert_parse Gleam residual (review 003349 80ff3f0a vs blessed d09c5594, exit and verdict equal). Lib file_system F# is 15/15 on the installed pack-1p without it.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_5 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 5u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night design conclusion: validation must keep emitting nested Fsharp globals (backend.rust_type F# aliases are needed in F# builds), so the fix is dead-global pruning after codegen, not suppressing globals during validation. Measured: 0 leaked helpers in the 457 sample Zig outputs (only prelude and union constructors are unreferenced) and 0 of 1,551 Rust fns; the leak only shows through lib code (trace), so the check must be a lib Kino probe.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_6 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 6u32, "2026-10-18T12:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:19 main-thread: implemented in the tree (not built yet), the dead-global pruning from the 10-17 design, on the final text so it is core-independent: UnusedGlobals.prune (shared CodegenUtils section) runs in both cores' build helper on every backend's output. A global is dropped only if it starts with a keyword declaration (fn/func/fun/function/def/const/let/var/type/struct/class/enum/interface/module, optional pub/export/local/static/inline/async), its declared name occurs nowhere else in the output, and it does not head a multi-part global; removal repeats to a fixpoint. Imports, includes, attributes, comments and C static definitions are always kept, so F# aliases that F# code uses stay. Expected effect: residual-only shrinkage on lib rows (Zig spiralClock/spiralHold/GetLocalTime, unused F# std_* aliases, unused Rust helpers); 0 behavior change. Verification: build + full review vs bless, after the current bless.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_7 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 7u32, "2026-10-18T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:30 main-thread: rule tightened before review (git-0e found that a rust_global blob starting with a private fn whose callers sit in the same blob next to pub fn API would have been dropped whole): a candidate must also be a single item (only the declaration and closing braces at column 0) with no pub/export token anywhere; extern \"lib\" fn declarations count. Probe on the installed first build: file_system.file_delete on Zig loses spiralClock, spiralHold and GetLocalTime (144 lines), zig build-exe -fno-emit-bin ok; 6 lib-backed Zig samples byte-identical in size. git-0e will run the eoie regeneration with -CargoCheck after the install as the cross-crate check.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_globals_leak_8 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-GLOBALS-LEAK", 8u32, "2026-10-18T14:04Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:04 main-thread: Done. Installed in pack 2 (all 3 modes 08:30-08:44) and reviewed: single-flight-20261018-084525 vs bless 061211 has DISAGREE 0 and no residual change from pruning on any harness row (the 13 residual-only rows are git-0e's extended multiline_triple_string); the lib leak probe (file_system.file_delete on Zig) drops spiralClock, spiralHold and GetLocalTime and still passes zig's semantic check; git-0e's eoie regeneration on this compiler with -CargoCheck: 139/139 owners byte-identical, every pub fn and multi-item rust_global blob kept.")
inl rust_union_impls () = Task ("RUST-UNION-IMPLS", "Rust backend emits an enum plus 'impl tag' and 'impl Default' (about 18 lines) for every union instantiation, and one array-collect loop per element type: x/apps/client/view/view.rs (1786 lines) spends 302 on 17 enum impls and 182 on 8 from_* loops; repro: any crate using list post, list link, list medium, option x -- emit Default/tag only when used, or generically", Story, P3, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,rust,code-shape", "current", "current")
inl note_of_rust_union_impls_1 () = Note ("RUST-UNION-IMPLS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: tag half done in staging: pack-c/rust-lazy-tag.ps1 stores each union tag impl and emits it only when a UnionTag op uses it. All 427 Rust rows: 0 behavior cells, 127 residuals; tag impls 194 -> 4 (the 4 are stale outputs, SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS), main.rs -30,353 bytes. Default half: 478 impl Default in 210 files, of which only 57 are reachable from a ::default() use (array creation roots, closure over nested defaults incl. RefCell-wrapped ones; no untyped or derived uses exist), so reachability-based emission drops 421 (88 percent): staged as pack-c/rust-lazy-default.ps1 verified on all Rust rows: 0 behavior cells, 201 residuals, exactly 57 impls left, -52 KB; not yet in a full review. In x/apps/client all 55 Default impls and all tag impls are unused: view.rs 1,885 -> 1,663 lines without them (still over the gate, the rest is RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION), args.rs 995 -> 853. Pack: night pack 10-17 (scripts %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\packs\\night-1017, each with -DryRun; final review single-flight-20261017-053315 on the exact pack: 6,906 rows, DISAGREE 0, residual-only Zig 457 and Rust 415, 3 intended Rust exit cells (shown as 2 NATIVE-DIFF rows); builds in single-flight, hopacsota and gpt5parallel; hopacsota inline and random:23 equal single-flight on every compile and residual cell; corpus 144 of 144; Zig at C speed on the bench).")
inl note_of_rust_union_impls_2 () = Note ("RUST-UNION-IMPLS", 2u32, "2026-10-18T05:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 01:53 x/apps/client regenerated with the installed compiler (lazy tag/Default): every crate shrank 2-19% (view 1884 -> 1662, args 994 -> 852, write 562 -> 454) with no source change")
inl note_of_rust_union_impls_3 () = Note ("RUST-UNION-IMPLS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:46Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:46 helper-lane lap 1: DONE with evidence: the title's fix (emit Default/tag only when used) is in the committed, accepted compiler: across today's 441 samples/*/main.rs there are exactly 57 impl Default for (the reachable set predicted on 10-17, was 478) and 4 fn tag( (the 4 stale outputs of SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS, was 194); x/apps/client's 16 crates have 0 of either, and view.rs went 1,786 -> 991 lines. The other half of the title's measurement, the per-element-type array loops, is not a union-impl or backend issue: today's from_N functions in x (digest 8, refs 4, fetch 3, thread 3, locate 3 ...) are monomorphized copies of x's own recursive JSON-array-to-list helper (from_7, from_10 in digest.rs each walk serde_json as_array for one element type), which every backend specializes the same way; shrinking them is a source-level change in x (decode the array once, then map), which belongs to the x lane.")
inl note_of_rust_union_impls_4 () = Note ("RUST-UNION-IMPLS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 17:42 (subagent): Done (status re-read; pack 9/10 installs touched none of its evidence) -> next: - | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): Done (status re-read) -> next: -")
inl rust_match_duplication () = Task ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", "Pattern-match lowering duplicates arm bodies on every backend (core peval, not codegenRust): or-patterns and wildcard fallbacks reach one shared continuation through the prepass EPatternMemo node, which peval evaluates once per path, so the body is emitted per alternative (or as a nested-match staircase); plus aliasing specialization (SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION) and Rust-only flattened-record moves through recursion (x posts.merge_newest 107 lines); measured in x/apps/client args (1243 before workarounds) and view", Story, P1, D4, 20u32, Active, "", "compiler,rust,code-shape,main,lab-held", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_match_duplication () = Planning ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_1 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night measurement (6-line repeated windows, variable numbers normalized): x view.rs 26 percent of 1,884 lines, top window x16 = runs of Rc str clone moves (flattened record fields moved one by one), fetch.rs 18, args.rs 11. The text metric also counts program shape (a mega sample scores 98 from unrolled straight-line code), so the next measure is by AST: clone-move runs and duplicated arm bodies.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_2 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:47Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:47 helper-lane lap 1: first AST-level measurement, and it moves the item: lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_pattern.spi (union of 3 payload cases + Dot, one or-pattern arm | Circle x | Square x | Triangle x => x * x + 7i32 * x - 3i32, inside a let function) compiled with the installed compiler (Invoke-Heavy, try.ps1) emits the arm body 3 times on every backend checked: C (v3/v8/v13 = 7l * v...), Zig (@as(i32, 7) *% ...) and Rust (7i32.wrapping_mul ...), all exit 41. So the or-pattern copy is made by partial evaluation, shared by all backends, not by codegenRust; the fix belongs in the core's or-pattern lowering (bind the matched payload to one variable and emit the body once, or wrap it in a join point), and its gain shows on C/Zig too. The other shapes in the title (_ staircases, aliasing specialization, flattened-record moves) still need their own probes.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_3 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:32Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:32 helper-lane lap 2: second shape probed (lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/wildcard_pair.spi: match a, b with | Red, Red | Green, Green | _ => 7 * 3 + 1, inside a let function; C and Rust exit 224): the wildcard arm becomes a staircase of nested matches with the fallback body written 3 times (once under each first-scrutinee case plus the outer _), on both C and Rust - here the body folded to 22, but a non-constant body is copied the same way, so like the or-pattern this is partial evaluation's multi-scrutinee lowering, not codegenRust. Same probe also shows the aliasing duplication from SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION: pair_score a b and pair_score b b produce two functions, pair_score_0(v0, v1) and pair_score_1(v0), because the second call passes one variable twice. So 3 of the title's 4 shapes are core (peval) behaviours shared by every backend; only the flattened-record moves are Rust-specific.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_4 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:14Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:14 helper-lane lap 3: retitled to the core cause and located it. In the pattern prepass (spiral_compiler.fs, pattern compile around 53005-53040) PatOr(a, b) builds cp id a on_succ (cp id b on_succ on_fail) with on_succ wrapped as EPatternMemo, and every clause's failure path chains into the next, ending in EPatternMiss; EPatternMemo is only a memo of the prepassed term (Utils.memoize at 52386/52525/52706) and is gone before partial evaluation (157548/159834 fail if one survives), so peval sees the same E reached from several control paths and inlines it on each - hence one copy of the arm per or-alternative, and the wildcard staircase where the fallback is reached from under each first-scrutinee case. Fix direction (the standard ML or-pattern lowering): when a memoized continuation is reachable from more than one path and is not trivially small, lower it to a local join point whose parameters are the pattern's bound variables, and have each path call it; that turns N copies into one body plus N calls on every backend. Related: SPIRAL-LITERAL-ALIAS-SPECIALIZATION covers the aliasing shape.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_5 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 5u32, "2026-10-19T15:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:44 helper-lane lap 4: pinned the elimination point and the tool for the fix. The shared continuation dies in the prepass rewrite at spiral_compiler.fs 52706 (EPatternMemo x -> Utils.memoize dict f x: every reference gets the same rewritten E object, so peval later walks it once per path), with the free-variable pass doing the same at 52386. The prepass already has the node the fix needs: EJoinPoint of Range * E * TPrepass option * backend option * name option (51957), whose body becomes one method that every call site invokes with its free variables (the pattern-bound variables included) as arguments. Sketch: at 52706, when the memo is hit for the second time and the continuation is not trivially small (a literal, a variable, or a short op), emit it once as a local EJoinPoint bound by an ELet and replace each reference by an application of that binding; the first visit cannot know yet, so the rewrite needs a reference count of EPatternMemo nodes first (one cheap prior walk over the clause tree). Cost is one call per path instead of one body copy per path, on every backend. Next: a lab patch with the count + join-point lowering, probed on the or-pattern and wildcard samples via a Debug candidate.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_6 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:48Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:48 helper-lane: correction to the lap-4 sketch and a concrete first patch. Lowering at 52706 cannot add join points: lower (52580) turns each EJoinPoint into EJoinPoint' using scope.[x], a free-variable table computed earlier per node, so a node created during lowering has no scope entry. The join point must exist before that pass, i.e. at pattern compilation. Staged lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_join_patch.py (anchor --check OK, shared prepass so both cores): PatOr now memoizes EJoinPoint(range, on_succ, None, None, None) instead of the bare continuation, unless the continuation is a literal, unit, variable or symbol; both alternatives then call one join-point method whose arguments are the pattern-bound variables. Scope: or-patterns only (the wildcard/next-clause chain stays as is for now, it touches every multi-clause match). run.ps1 compiles or_pattern.spi before and after on C and Rust and counts the arm body's copies (expect exit 41, 3 copies -> 1). HEAVY QUEUE row added.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_7 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 7u32, "2026-10-19T18:27Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 14:27 helper-lane: pack 8 bisect (lab/BACKLOG-LAPS/pack8-bisect): or_join_patch.py alone breaks samples/frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any on every backend (TracedError: Expected a symbol as the index into the layout type. Got: %d, i32), while the positional-rename and Assign candidates compile both. lib's format code uses type-annotated or-patterns in real code (sm'_real.spir 660-689: | (_ : f32) | (_ : f64) => ...), and the prepass does not distinguish real code (RawReal(r,a) -> f a, 53272), so the join-point wrapping also hit compile-time, type-directed continuations. Dropped from pack 8. Redesign options: skip the wrapping when an alternative carries a type annotation or type test (the continuation is type-specialized anyway), or lower only in runtime code by threading a real flag through the prepass; whichever is chosen must pass the format_any samples and a lib Cells sweep before it rides a pack.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_8 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 8u32, "2026-10-22T07:35Z", "main", "03:35 main: v2 of the or-pattern join point drafted (lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_join_patch_v2.py; dry run by default; refuses if already applied). It applies the 10-19 14:27 redesign: wrap the shared continuation in EJoinPoint only when NEITHER alternative is type-directed. typeDirected walks the pattern and is true for PatAnnot, PatExists, PatFilledDefaultValue, PatDefaultValue and PatNominal (all compile-time type tests), recursing through Dyn/Unbox/When/Pair/Or/And/record members/arrays. So sm'_real.spir's '| (_ : f32) | (_ : f64)' keeps today's lowering, the case that broke frontier_format_any_union in pack 8. Trivial continuations (ELit, EB, EV, ESymbol) stay unwrapped as in v1. Checked without a build: every constructor and arity exists (E.EJoinPoint has 5 fields at core 52023; PatDefaultValue and PatRecordMembers* match the raw Pattern at 43910-43930), and the anchor matches once on both the tree core and the PACK-AFTER-NIGHT core. Gate (its own pack, not PACK-AFTER-NIGHT, because it changes peval for every or-pattern): Debug candidate; run.ps1 counts or_pattern.spi's arm body (3 copies -> 1 on C and Rust, exit 41); frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any compile; then a lib Cells sweep and -Suite all with DISAGREE 0, where only residuals with or-patterns change.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_9 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 9u32, "2026-10-22T13:38Z", "main", "2026-10-22 09:38 main: design notes for the clause-chain half (after v2 lands in PACK-NEXT-2), read from the tree core, no build. The clause chain is lowered at 53141/53144: List.mapFoldBack loop clauses (EPatternMiss (EV id)), where each clause's on_fail is EPatternMemo on_fail (the remaining clauses). The memo shares the E node in the prepass, but peval evaluates it once per failure path, which produces the nested-match staircase. A v3 would wrap that on_fail as v2 does for or-patterns: EJoinPoint (p r, on_fail, None, None, None) when on_fail is non-trivial (not EPatternMiss/ELit/EV), skipped when any remaining clause is type-directed (the same typeDirected walk). Better than the heuristic would be an explicit real-code flag: PartEvalPrepassEnv (52933) has only term and ty, and RawReal(r,a) -> f a (53350) drops the information. A field set by RawReal would let both v2 and v3 skip join points in compile-time code, but whole .spir modules (sm'_real.spir) may not pass through RawReal, so their entry path has to be traced first. The order is fixed: v2 has to prove itself in PACK-NEXT-2 (format_any samples, lib Cells sweep, DISAGREE 0). Then v3 is measured on x/apps/client args (1,243 lines before workarounds) as its own pack, because it touches every multi-clause match.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_10 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 10u32, "2026-10-22T21:09Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:09 main: or-join v2 is RED in PACK-NEXT-3's review. On lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_pattern.spi (Circle x | Square x | Triangle x => x * x + 7 * x - 3) the candidate (core 95AF71D2FE8AE0E6) fails with FatalError: Index was outside the bounds of the array, exit 5, on C. The installed compiler compiles it and the program exits 41. v2 had only ever been anchor-checked, so this review is its first execution. Probable cause from the patch: the shared continuation is wrapped in EJoinPoint(p r, on_succ, None, None, None) inside the pattern compiler, but that body reads x, which each alternative binds on its own. A join point's captured environment is fixed where it is created, so the alternatives' different bindings index past it. Next design: the join point must take the alternatives' bound variables as parameters (bind once, call with each alternative's values), or wrap only bodies that use no pattern-bound variable. Gate for v3: or_pattern.spi exits 41 with one arm-body copy, plus the matrix.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_11 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 11u32, "2026-10-22T21:20Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:20 main: correction to note 10's cause, from a code read (no build). Pattern variables come from one per-clause dictionary (cp's v x, core 53083: PatVar -> ELet(p r, v a, EV id, on_succ)), so x gets the SAME index in every alternative; the shared body does not see different bindings. A better fit for \"Index was outside the bounds of the array\": the lowering pass handles EJoinPoint(r,body,...) through scope env x (52735), and scope looks up per-node free-variable data computed by an earlier analysis (52466). v2 creates its EJoinPoint inside the pattern compiler (53130). If that analysis already ran, the new node has no scope entry, its var table is too small, and EV i -> env.term.var.[i] (52738) indexes past it. v3 options: (1) create the join point where the analysis sees it (wrap the or-pattern body before the free-variable pass, or run scope on the new node at creation); (2) reuse a construct the lowering already scopes, such as a let-bound closure called from each alternative. Next step, after the compile holds: a Debug candidate with v2 under SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET=0 to get the stack, confirming 52738 before writing v3.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_12 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 12u32, "2026-10-22T21:44Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:44 main: coverage gap. A census of or-pattern arms (| A | B => with an inner | and no ||; scratch or_pattern_census.py) finds 0 of 1,240 sample .spi files with an or-pattern arm, and 10 of 212 lib/spiral files, 2 of them binding variables. So the -Suite all matrix could not have caught v2's crash; only the dedicated probe did. Before v3: add a regression sample with a variable-binding or-pattern (or_pattern.spi's Circle x | Square x | Triangle x, exit 41), one sample per fixed bug, and gate v3 on lib Cells gates of the 2 binding files, since those are its real users.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_13 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 13u32, "2026-10-22T21:52Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:52 main: v3's lib gate target, made concrete: the only variable-binding or-pattern in lib/spiral is state_core.spi:594, | Some value, _ | _, Some (Ok (Some value)) =>, which binds value in both alternatives (exactly v2's crash shape). stream.spi:249/257 are wildcard-only (StreamNil, _ | _, StreamNil), the non-binding case. v3 gates: or_pattern.spi exits 41 with one arm-body copy, lib_gate state_core.spi and stream.spi green, and the new regression sample.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_14 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 14u32, "2026-10-22T21:57Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:57 main: regression sample added before next3b's matrix started: samples/or_pattern_bound_alternatives (or_pattern.spi: Circle x | Square x | Triangle x => x * x + 7 * x - 3, exits 41 from Square 4), the matrix's first variable-binding or-pattern. On next3b (no or-join) it is a plain new row expected to agree with exit 41; for v3 it is the guard that would have caught v2.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_15 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 15u32, "2026-10-22T22:08Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:08 main: v3 option (2) ruled out by a code read. \"Wrap only bodies that use no pattern-bound variable\" cannot be decided where v2 inserts the join point: at cp time the arm body is still an EPatternRef that the clause loop fills only after every pattern is compiled (core 53141-53152: \"I need to deal with the patterns first before replacing the strings in the body\"). Any v3 must therefore create the shared join point after the refs are filled (a post-pass over the lowered clause), or teach propagate/lower to scope a join point created inside cp. Both start from the Debug stack trace (next-session step 4).")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_16 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 16u32, "2026-10-23T13:45Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:45 main: v3 design from a code read (no build), replacing both v2 placements. Facts: in compile_pattern (core ~53054-53153) the arm body is an EPatternRef that is filled only after every clause is compiled. A join point created inside cp has no scope entry from the earlier free-variable analysis, so v2's crash came from creating EJoinPoint there. Every pass downstream, type inference included, already handles join points that come from the RAW tree: let functions become RawJoinPoint through join_point and adjust_join_point (core ~44497-44527), and 'let x = e in b' is RawMatch(r, e, [PatVar x, b]), which pattern_match turns into ELet. v3 is therefore a raw rewrite before type inference. A clause | P1 | P2 => body whose top-level pattern is a PatOr, whose alternatives bind the same variable set V and are not type-directed (v2's typeDirected walk: PatAnnot, PatExists, PatNominal, PatDefaultValue, PatFilledDefaultValue), whose body is not trivial (a literal, a variable or a symbol), and which sits outside real code becomes: let k = fun V => join body in match s with | P1 => k V | P2 => k V | other clauses. k is a fresh uninterned name like ' arg' in adjust_join_point, and V is a tuple when there are several variables. Type inference sees an ordinary let-bound join point, so scoping, free variables and codegen need no change, and each backend emits one body plus N calls. The rewrite belongs where the parser builds RawMatch (or one raw-tree pass after parsing that also gets the RawReal flag). Gates as before: samples/or_pattern_bound_alternatives exits 41 with one body copy, frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any compile, lib_gate state_core.spi and stream.spi, then -Suite all with DISAGREE 0. The clause-chain (wildcard staircase) half is the same rewrite applied to the remaining-clauses continuation, as a later pack.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_17 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 17u32, "2026-10-23T13:49Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:49 main: v3 implemented as %TEMP%\\pack-next7\\or-pattern-join-v3.ps1 and composed alone into pack-next8 (core 19B0B1D5F8629846 on next7 9CC2E91F). Two anchors: module OrPatternJoin before inl_or_let_process, and the 'match ... with' parser calling OrPatternJoin.lift instead of RawMatch. It parses under both defines with pair-drift clean. Shape: each clause whose top-level pattern is a PatOr, not type-directed, with a non-trivial body becomes 'let <uninterned ' or N'> = fun V => join body' around the match, and the clause body becomes the call. V is the first alternative's bound variables, which equal the second's (DisjointOrPatternVar enforces it), with unit for none and a pair chain for several. It is deliberately not the let-join form: let would PatDyn the arguments and stop static folding, while a plain fun whose body is a RawJoinPoint keeps static arguments specializing, and a dynamic scrutinee gives one body. The uninterned name can't shadow a user variable. Known risk for the gate: the rewrite runs at parse time without a real-code flag, so an unannotated or-pattern inside real code (.spir) would get a join point. The format_any samples and a lib Cells sweep (sm'_real.spir) are the check, and if they fail, the next step is the RawReal flag of note 9. Review rule (pack-next8, after next7): only outputs of samples containing an or-pattern arm may change; or_pattern_bound_alternatives exits 41 with one arm-body copy on C and Rust; frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any compile; lib_gate state_core.spi and stream.spi pass; DISAGREE 0.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_18 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 18u32, "2026-10-23T14:16Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:16 main: pack-next8 now also carries the inert hopacsota census (HOPACSOTA-P3), so its core is BF7EE0AD76C18036. The census is off without its environment variable, so the review rule for or-pattern v3 is unchanged.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_19 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 19u32, "2026-10-24T06:32Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:3x main: or-pattern join v3 reaches its first full gate. It came into pack 5g through the next8 base (5g = next9 stack + my fixes, core 1A297706E577BB03), so review-4 is its review. Its rule's parts the matrix cannot check are wired into review-4: %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\orjoin-check.ps1 runs with the reviewed candidate DLL (SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, which try.ps1 and Cells' toolchain.ex both honor), compiling lab or_pattern.spi to C and Rust and counting arm-body copies (rule: exit 41, 1 copy), then lib_gate state_core.spi and stream.spi in process mode (lib's only or-pattern users). The matrix covers or_pattern_bound_alternatives and frontier_format_any_union / frontier_join_format_any. review-4 is in the requeue manifest for tibia's block end.")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_20 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 20u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 11:48 (subagent): or-pattern join-point patch at pattern compile (lowering cannot add join points: scope table) + run.ps1 queued -> next: read the candidate run (3 copies -> 1) | lap 6 14:27 (subagent): bisect: or_join_patch is pack 8's regression (format_any samples; real-code type-annotated or-patterns); dropped from the pack -> next: redesign: skip annotated alternatives or thread a real flag; prove on format_any + lib sweep | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: or_join_patch dropped (format_any regression) -> next: redesign without type-annotated or-patterns")
inl note_of_rust_match_duplication_21 () = Note ("RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION", 21u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 helper patch record folded from the deleted history folder: patch lab/RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION/or_join_patch.py, state 'candidate queued (run.ps1)', proof: or_pattern body 3 copies -> 1")
inl spiral_export_pipe_forall () = Task ("SPIRAL-EXPORT-PIPE-FORALL", "Export of an annotated lambda whose body pipes, ((fun t => date_of_text t |> text_or_empty) : string -> string), fails 'Cannot apply a forall with a term' at the pipe; the same body as a named inl (text_or_empty (date_of_text t)) compiles; found in x/apps/client/checks 2026-10-16", TaskKind, P4, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,spiral,export", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_export_pipe_forall_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-EXPORT-PIPE-FORALL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: root cause Export missing from op_args_inferred (spiral_compiler.fs ~49323), so Infer never fills a forall used inside an exported lambda (Cannot apply a forall with a term). Fix pack-a/export-inferred.ps1; both repros compile on the candidate; no residual change in the full review. Regression case pack-a/export-contract-case.ps1 adds forall_in_export (a 4-line core-free program) to test-rust-exports.ps1: it fails on the installed compiler with Cannot apply a forall with a term and passes on the candidate (all export contracts pass). Lands with PACK-NIGHT-1017. Pack: night pack 10-17 (scripts %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\packs\\night-1017, each with -DryRun; final review single-flight-20261017-053315 on the exact pack: 6,906 rows, DISAGREE 0, residual-only Zig 457 and Rust 415, 3 intended Rust exit cells (shown as 2 NATIVE-DIFF rows); builds in single-flight, hopacsota and gpt5parallel; hopacsota inline and random:23 equal single-flight on every compile and residual cell; corpus 144 of 144; Zig at C speed on the bench).")
inl note_of_spiral_export_pipe_forall_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-EXPORT-PIPE-FORALL", 2u32, "2026-10-18T05:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 01:53 verified on x with the installed night-pack compiler: x/apps/client/checks exports (fun t => username_of_text t |> text_or_empty) again; the named-wrapper workaround is gone")
inl eoie_topology_crates () = Task ("EOIE-TOPOLOGY-CRATES", "eoie proxy source-topology C:\\home\\git\\x agrees on rust_file_max but reports cargo_crate_max=0: it only recognizes crates in its own layout, not a workspace of apps/<app>/<crate>/Cargo.toml with [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,eoie-dogfood,x", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_topology_crates_1 () = Note ("EOIE-TOPOLOGY-CRATES", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night, corrected root cause: the generic collector counts crates fine (topology_generic_packages); the verb source_topology_run calls the eoie-layout topology_collect, which only sees <root>/src/<pkg>/Cargo.toml, so x (apps/<app>/<crate>) gets cargo_crate_max=0. Fix: dispatch on layout; it sits in rust_global blob 3 (8,453 chars), so migrate the dispatch to granular Spiral first.")
inl note_of_eoie_topology_crates_2 () = Note ("EOIE-TOPOLOGY-CRATES", 2u32, "2026-10-18T12:14Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 08:14 new friction: eoie proxy source-topology C:\\home\\git\\x now exits 2 before counting anything: 'source-topology rejects symlink: C:\\home\\git\\x\\apps\\sim\\_build\\dev\\lib\\spiral_cells\\priv' (mix links path deps' priv into the gitignored _build). The walk should skip ignored build output (_build, deps, target) instead of rejecting the root")
inl note_of_eoie_topology_crates_3 () = Note ("EOIE-TOPOLOGY-CRATES", 3u32, "2026-10-18T13:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:35 main-thread: eoie's red CI (run 39106603979 on 75a19ac, 'source topology limit exceeded: rust_file_max=982', the rest of the line cut by the log width) reproduces locally today: eoie proxy source-topology C:\\home\\git\\eoie exits 2 with rust_file_max=982 (src/patch_compat_driver/compat_driver.rs, within the limit) and cargo_crate_max=1522 (crate rust_std_fs_mutation), limit 1000. The crate gate is what fails; rust_std_fs_mutation needs splitting into granular packages (eoie lane).")
inl note_of_eoie_topology_crates_4 () = Note ("EOIE-TOPOLOGY-CRATES", 4u32, "2026-10-18T14:00Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 10:00 _build fix in the typed model (eoie_source_topology_core/model.spi: source_directory MixBuildDirectory code 5, build_cache_evidence MixBuildOutput; ownership.rs gains '_build' in the reserved-name table and a_mix_build_directory_is_build_output): dev.ps1 -Package eoie-source-topology regenerates and passes 12/12. Not published: publishing would also bake the staged terminal.spi rewrite (another lane's, 609+/595-) into topology.rs, so the owner of that rewrite publishes both (dev.ps1 -Package eoie-source-topology -Publish) and rebuilds eoie.exe")
inl note_of_eoie_topology_crates_5 () = Note ("EOIE-TOPOLOGY-CRATES", 5u32, "2026-10-21T11:46Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:46 eoie team, closed by measurement: 'eoie proxy source-stats C:\\home\\git\\x --crates' (installed release 23) walks x in 1 s and reports cargo_crate_count=16, cargo_crate_max_lines=987 cargo_crate_max=apps/client/view, per-crate headroom lines, and no _build symlink rejection (note 2's failure is gone). The generic collector is the right verb for non-eoie layouts. source-topology stays eoie's own release gate by design: it checks eoie's src/<pkg> topology and writes eoie's state/authority_census.spi, and no x or tibia script calls it (grep of both repos: 0 hits). No code change.")
inl python_int_semantics () = Task ("PYTHON-INT-SEMANTICS", "Python backend integer semantics match C: wrap every fixed-width op, division and remainder toward zero", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,python,backend", "current", "current")
inl note_of_python_int_semantics_1 () = Note ("PYTHON-INT-SEMANTICS", 1u32, "2026-10-16T07:29Z", "main-thread", "03:29 main: diagnosed from codegenPython (spiral_compiler.fs ~175478-175501) and Python probe single-flight-20261015-175614 (39 unlisted DISAGREE): Add/Sub/Mult/Neg/ShiftLeft emit plain Python operators on unbounded ints (no i8..u64 wrap), integer Div is // and Mod is % (both floor; C truncates toward zero, so negatives differ). Failing families: small_int_ops, small_int_scalar, ebpf_arith/bits/conv/div/shift/uacc/umul, lua_wide_integers (wrap/div); managed_string_codepoints/utf8_boundaries/invalid_utf8_slice + string_utf8 (strings are not UTF-8 byte indexed); native_*scc*/recursive_tail_recursion (Python recursion limit); array_create_reads_zero (exit 705). Fix direction: per-width wrap helpers emitted on demand (spiral_wrap_i32 etc., keyed on the operand's YPrim), spiral_div/spiral_mod truncating, sys.setrecursionlimit + a big threading.stack_size main thread. The section maps to upstream's Python generator: keep the ws markers.")
inl note_of_python_int_semantics_2 () = Note ("PYTHON-INT-SEMANTICS", 2u32, "2026-10-16T10:35Z", "main-thread", "06:35 main: scoped in CodegenPython (spiral_compiler.fs ~175495-175546, upstream-mapped section, ws: edits): Add/Sub/Mult/ShiftLeft/Neg/BitwiseComplement emit bare Python ints (no width wrap); Div on ints is // (floors; C truncates toward zero); Mod is % (sign of divisor; C sign of dividend); TyConv is tyv(x) with no wrap or truncation. Plan: one prelude helper per op family emitted on use (Lua's spiral_w64 pattern): spiral_wrap(x, bits, signed) after Add/Sub/Mult/ShiftLeft/Neg/~ and int TyConv, spiral_div_trunc and spiral_mod_trunc for int Div/Mod; arrays already use cp.int32 etc. Prove with -Probe Python -Backend C,Python: the 39 unlisted DISAGREE rows should drop.")
inl note_of_python_int_semantics_3 () = Note ("PYTHON-INT-SEMANTICS", 3u32, "2026-10-16T10:43Z", "main-thread", "06:43 main: prototype proven on a source copy (tmp/pack-t/python-int.ps1; probe single-flight-20261016-063945): 10 of the 39 unlisted DISAGREE now agree (all the integer ones), ts_int_wrap Known -> agree, 0 listed regressions, 159 listed rows residual-only. The remaining 29 are other families (abi_external C ABI x9, dynamic_array x11, UTF-8 strings x4, deep tail recursion x4, array_create_reads_zero): split them out before closing this item. Not in the tree yet.")
inl note_of_python_int_semantics_4 () = Note ("PYTHON-INT-SEMANTICS", 4u32, "2026-10-16T11:57Z", "main-thread", "07:57 main: pack-1s installed both modes (07:23/07:31) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-073131, 6,685 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against the pack-1r bless 053218 overridden by probes 062511 (Kotlin), 061551 (Swift), 064814 (Python); the one extra row, Bend typecheck_long_let_body, exits 236 like C. ts_int_wrap Python Known -> agree. Python list 383 -> 405. Integer semantics now match C; the rest is PYTHON-BACKEND-GAPS / PYTHON-STRINGS-UPSTREAM.")
inl eoie_status_error_values () = Task ("EOIE-STATUS-ERROR-VALUES", "eoie agile set: invalid status error names the valid statuses", TaskKind, P4, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,agile,ux", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_status_error_values_1 () = Note ("EOIE-STATUS-ERROR-VALUES", 1u32, "2026-10-16T10:44Z", "main-thread", "06:44 main: agile set ... InProgress printed only 'eoie error: invalid agile status' (exit 2); the valid set Planned|Active|Blocked|Paused|Done is only in the usage line. The error should list it, like the usage does.")
inl note_of_eoie_status_error_values_2 () = Note ("EOIE-STATUS-ERROR-VALUES", 2u32, "2026-10-16T12:04Z", "main-thread", "08:04 main: done. eoie_agile_mutation/agile_mutation.spi agile_status_error names the valid set; regenerated with dev.ps1 -Package eoie-agile-mutation -Offline -Publish (1 owner, generated Rust diff is the one message line); build.ps1 -Test -Offline: 276 cargo results, 197 passed, 0 failed, Spiral test blocks 10/10, topology ok, eoie.exe redeployed 08:03. Live: agile set ... InProgress -> eoie error: invalid agile status (expected Planned, Active, Blocked, Paused or Done).")
inl python_backend_gaps () = Task ("PYTHON-BACKEND-GAPS", "Python backend: dynamic array runtime, UTF-8 byte strings, deep recursion, C ABI externals", TaskKind, P3, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,python,backend", "current", "current")
inl note_of_python_backend_gaps_1 () = Note ("PYTHON-BACKEND-GAPS", 1u32, "2026-10-16T10:45Z", "main-thread", "06:45 main: the 29 Python DISAGREE left after the PYTHON-INT-SEMANTICS prototype (probe single-flight-20261016-063945), stderr read on one per family: dynamic_array x11: NameError DynamicArrayReserve0 not defined (the C dynamic array runtime has no Python emission); managed_string/string_utf8 x4: IndexError string index out of range (Python indexes code points, C UTF-8 bytes: needs byte semantics like Kotlin spiralStringIndex); native_*_tail_recursion x4: RecursionError (needs sys.setrecursionlimit plus a big-stack threading main, like the other backends' 1 GB thread); abi_external x9: C ABI externals (likely out of scope for Python: mark Known or reject at compile time); array_create_reads_zero exit 705.")
inl note_of_python_backend_gaps_2 () = Note ("PYTHON-BACKEND-GAPS", 2u32, "2026-10-16T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 main: array_create_reads_zero fixed in the staged pack-1t (tmp/pack-t/python-zero-arrays.ps1: primitive arrays cp.zeros instead of cp.empty, which handed back reused memory); re-probe single-flight-20261016-064814 agrees, 28 left.")
inl note_of_python_backend_gaps_3 () = Note ("PYTHON-BACKEND-GAPS", 3u32, "2026-10-16T11:26Z", "main-thread", "07:26 main: UTF-8 family deferred by design check: lib/spiral sm'.spi Python arms are code-point based (find at 527, len at 991, slice through sm.slice at 276, split, startswith), so byte semantics in StringLength/StringIndex/StringSlice alone would mis-slice non-ASCII text in lib code. A fix must switch the primitives (helpers with an isascii fast path, surrogateescape round trip) and those arms together, then rerun the lib sweep on Python. Recursion family: staged tmp/pack-u/python-recursion.ps1 (sys.setrecursionlimit(1 << 30) at the entry; CPython 3.12 frames do not use the C stack): native_recursive_tail_recursion and native_managed_scc_tail_recursion exit 0 like C by hand; the 2 array SCC ones then hit the dynamic array gap. A 1 GB thread was rejected: Windows CPython caps threading.stack_size below 256 MB, and SystemExit inside a thread would lose exit codes.")
inl note_of_python_backend_gaps_4 () = Note ("PYTHON-BACKEND-GAPS", 4u32, "2026-10-16T11:26Z", "main-thread", "07:26 main: dynamic_array x11 and abi_external x9 are C-only samples by construction (their macros call the C runtime by name: DynamicArrayReserve0, ABI externals); in the pack-1r bless only their C rows run. They show as Python DISAGREE only because -Probe compiles every sample. Not a Python gap. Real remaining work: UTF-8 byte strings (4, needs lib arms) and the 2 SCC recursion rows (staged pack-u).")
inl note_of_python_backend_gaps_5 () = Note ("PYTHON-BACKEND-GAPS", 5u32, "2026-10-16T11:29Z", "main-thread", "07:29 main: the UTF-8 string family is PYTHON-STRINGS-UPSTREAM (existing item); this item keeps the recursion rows and the inventory.")
inl note_of_python_backend_gaps_6 () = Note ("PYTHON-BACKEND-GAPS", 6u32, "2026-10-16T13:46Z", "main-thread", "09:46 main: pack-1u installed both modes (09:16/09:21) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-092123, 6,742 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against the pack-1s bless 073131 overridden by probes 081908 (Kotlin), 082312 (Swift), 082906 (Bend), 084211 (Python). Harness Kotlin 444, Swift 443, Bend 401, Python 422.")
inl blog () = Task ("BLOG", "Spiral blog app (user request 2026-10-16): a static blog generator written in Spiral, an app like the X client (own repo beside spiral modeled on i574n/x: per-crate .spi modules, build.ps1 with Assert-SpiralBeside, e2e.ps1, Spiral test blocks, CI) published to GitHub Pages; posts are typed Spiral data (a block union: heading, paragraph, code, terminal or benchmark output, table, note, diagram, section rule) rendered to static HTML and an RSS feed; the look and voice copy bun.com/blog: index with a featured latest card, Everything/Articles/Releases tabs, posts grouped by year with date, type label, title, blurb and authors; post pages with a large title and underline bar, byline and date, an On this page table of contents, * * * section rules, hyperfine-style terminal blocks, comparison tables, warning notes, Conclusion and a call to action; Spiral's own name, logo and colors, no Bun text or assets. First post: the overarching pipeline, written like Behind The Scenes of Bun Install: .spi source -> the compiler (one 186k-line file, single-flight and gpt5parallel cores plus the hopacsota layer, 18 backends) -> the matrix with C as oracle (6,906 rows, accept rules) -> packs (review, install both modes, accept) -> Kino -> eoie (agile backlog, one-call CLI) -> Zed and the formatter -> CI -> apps (tibia, x, dice); every number from a real run (repo C:\\home\\git\\blog = i574n/blog; generic engine/ + site/ + posts/ so forks swap content; static HTML, no Leptos)", Feature, P1, D5, 25u32, Active, "", "spiral,app,blog,dogfood,main,lab-held", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_blog () = Planning ("BLOG", Unestimated, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_blog_1 () = Note ("BLOG", 1u32, "2026-10-17T01:44Z", "main-thread", "21:44 main-thread research. Style source bun.com/blog (179 posts, newest Bun v1.4.3 2026-10-10). Two post kinds: Releases (H1 version, byline, tabbed install + upgrade blocks first, no prose intro, one short lead sentence + code block per feature, benchmark tables with hardware noted) and Articles (the model for the pipeline post): Behind The Scenes of Bun Install (2025-09-10, ~6-7k words) opens with a benchmark claim then a history narrative; title-case section per stage (The Problem with System Calls, Async DNS Resolution, Binary Manifest Caching, Cache-Friendly Data Layout, Multi-Core Parallelism, Conclusion); short punchy sentences, bold key ideas, code with ❌/✅ comments, hyperfine Benchmark/Summary blocks, one comparison table, captioned diagrams, a platform warning note, respectful comparisons with other tools; ends on a one-line takeaway + link. Rewriting Bun in Rust (2026-07-08): first-person, admits false starts, Stats tables, Zig:/Rust: side-by-side code. Local prior art: x/apps/client (newest app layout, copy it); dice/ui (Leptos in Spiral, older era with rust_derive.ps1 post-processing, do not copy); publish like i574n.github gh-pages.yml (workflow.ps1 -> dist -> peaceiris). Pipeline numbers at 2026-10-16: spiral_compiler.fs 186,431 lines; backends F#, C, Rust, Delphi, Zig, TypeScript, Lua, Gleam, Lean, Bend, Kotlin, Swift, Python, Wasm, WGSL, Calyx, OpenQASM, eBPF; 855 sample dirs; harness 6,906 rows, last bless single-flight-20261016-145428; lib/spiral 195 .spi/.spir; eoie 492 .spi; backlog 296 items; today 8 packs (1s-1z). Open decisions for the start: repo name (user creates it on GitHub), Pages URL, renderer backend (Rust like x).")
inl note_of_blog_2 () = Note ("BLOG", 2u32, "2026-10-17T01:56Z", "main-thread", "21:56 main-thread, UI-stack decision (user question 2026-10-16): no Leptos for the blog, neither upstream nor a port. A blog is static: HTML is produced at build time, and the only interactive part (the Everything/Articles/Releases tabs) works as one page per tab or CSS-only, so there is nothing for a reactive framework to do. Of tachys' 20.7k code lines the static-HTML part is ssr 657 + html 7.5k, mostly element and attribute tables; a typed Spiral HTML model with escaping is a few hundred lines. Measured on the local clone (v0.8.11, 2025-10): the core a port would need is ~45k code lines (tachys 20.7k, reactive_graph 12.2k, router 7.9k, meta 1.2k), and upstream is not frozen (v0.8.22 on 10-05, v0.9.0-rc0 on 10-15 changes the signal traits). Interactive Spiral UIs are a separate item, SPIRAL-UI.")
inl note_of_blog_3 () = Note ("BLOG", 3u32, "2026-10-17T01:56Z", "main-thread", "21:56 main-thread, layout decision (user 2026-10-16: repo cloned at C:\\home\\git\\blog = i574n/blog, empty): the blog machinery is a generic app and the content sits apart, so a fork only swaps two folders. engine/ = generic Spiral (post model as a block union, HTML renderer, index with tabs and year groups, RSS, table of contents from headings, routes from slugs); site/site.spi = name, logo, colors, authors, base URL; posts/<slug>/ = post.spi (typed post data: title, date, kind Article|Release, authors, blurb, blocks) plus assets; scripts/ = build.ps1, workflow.ps1, e2e.ps1 as in x. Routes: / is the index, /<slug>/ is a post, /rss.xml; the first post is posts/<pipeline-slug>/ and is the only post at first. The generator runs natively (Rust like x) and writes dist/, which gh-pages publishes. Posts are Spiral data, so a broken link to a heading, a missing date or an unknown author fails the build.")
inl note_of_blog_4 () = Note ("BLOG", 4u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: C:\\home\\git\\blog\\engine (untracked) has html, post, page, index, site and build modules with 15 tests: site = shared layout + index.html + <slug>/index.html (a fork swaps the entries); build writes the tree to a temp dir and reads it back on Rust. Next: style.css, one package at the blog root with engine/ and posts/ as module folders (the x/apps/client layout: one packageDir, folder modules), then the first post. Its blocks are not postable yet (10 over 280 X weight).")
inl note_of_blog_5 () = Note ("BLOG", 5u32, "2026-10-18T12:36Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:36 main-thread engine grown (C:\\home\\git\\blog\\engine, untracked, tests written but not run yet, Cells verification queued after today's bless): post.block gains Terminal (command + output, prompt span), Table (head + rows, escaped cells) and Note (aside); page renders headings with section ids and an On this page table of contents (none without headings); rss.spi is an RSS 2.0 feed with dc:date (ISO dates, no RFC 822 day-of-week math); style.spi holds the stylesheet (Spiral palette, featured card, year groups, underline title bar, * * * rules, terminal blocks, tables, notes); site emits index.html, style.css, rss.xml, articles/index.html and releases/index.html (index.only filters by kind) plus one folder per post; pipeline.spi is the first post as typed data (one compiler file and two cores, 19 targets, C and Zig oracles, packs and blesses), every block under 280 characters. Next: run the 8 modules' tests through Cells, then the one-package layout (engine/ and posts/ folders) and build.ps1/e2e.ps1/CI like x.")
inl note_of_blog_6 () = Note ("BLOG", 6u32, "2026-10-18T12:57Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:57 main-thread: repo scaffold in x's shape (untracked, not run): scripts/common.ps1 (BlogRoot, SpiralRoot beside, Assert-SpiralBeside, Invoke-SpiralTests through spiral's apps/cells/spi/run_notebook.ps1 with the process compiler), scripts/init.ps1 (clones spiral beside, init-app-build), scripts/build.ps1 (the 9 engine modules' test blocks), scripts/workflow.ps1, and .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml (ubuntu + windows, rust, beam 27/1.18/gleam 1.19.1, zig 0.17.0, one step: pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1). Needs actions.lock generated like the other repos (gh actions-lock), then a generator main that writes the site into dist/ and a Pages publish job; first local run of build.ps1 after today's bless.")
inl note_of_blog_7 () = Note ("BLOG", 7u32, "2026-10-18T13:47Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:47 main-thread repair: my 08:3x scripted edits of engine/post.spi and site.spi passed -replace expressions straight into .Replace(...) calls, where PowerShell binds the comma-separated arguments into the -replace operator, so parts of both files became empty lines (post.spi lost its union and helper functions, site.spi two match cases). The user's 09:39 blog commit c8c3da8 carries that damage. Fixed in the tree: post.spi rewritten from e92c5af's intact content plus the Terminal/Table/Note blocks, site.spi's two cases restored. Verified: a probe package that builds site.files [pipeline.entry ()] compiles to Zig on the installed compiler (all engine modules typecheck); the test blocks still need their Cells run. Needs a commit.")
inl note_of_blog_8 () = Note ("BLOG", 8u32, "2026-10-18T15:57Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:57 main-thread: blog CI has never been green, and the cause is one line. engine/html.spi lacked the '///- --package .' first line that the other 8 modules (and x's modules) have. Under CI's runner (run_notebook.ps1, SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process) its test program therefore never loads package.spiproj's packages (|core-, spiral-), and both runs (13:43Z on c8c3da8 and 15:32Z on 7a41491) stopped at html.spi:2:10 ETY-UnboundModule on 'open testing', then 'unbound sm''. It's first in build.ps1's list, so no other module ran. The local mix spiral.notebook gate resolved lib anyway, which hid it. Fixed in the tree (directive added). Verifying with scripts/build.ps1, CI's exact path, through the heavy gate. Also new: scripts/publish.ps1 plus a CI publish step and push job (TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO), and a .gitignore (/dist/, /target/).")
inl note_of_blog_9 () = Note ("BLOG", 9u32, "2026-10-18T16:34Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 12:34 main-thread: verified locally through CI's exact path, scripts/build.ps1 (run_notebook.ps1 per module, SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process, on the tree lib incl. git-0e's conversion and the installed 10:28 compiler). All 9 modules pass, 25/25 tests, BLOG-OK. The html.spi '///- --package .' line is the fix; it needs the user's push, then CI should be green for the first time and the push job creates gh-pages.")
inl note_of_blog_10 () = Note ("BLOG", 10u32, "2026-10-19T12:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:13 helper-lane lap 1: verified state: blog CI is green (tests run on 5539b84, 2026-10-18 22:13Z, success) and Pages deploys, but https://i574n.github.io/blog/ serves only the dir-tree page of the source (scripts/publish.ps1 calls spiral publish-tree.ps1; gh-pages root holds .nojekyll, README.md, _github, engine, scripts, index.html = tree). The blog site itself is never generated: engine/package.spiproj lists html, post, page, index, rss, style, site, showcase, build (pipeline.spi is not a module), and build.spi has write_files only inside a test. Missing for a real site: a generator entry that calls site.files over the posts and writes dist/, publish of dist/ beside the tree page, and the engine/ vs posts/ vs site/ split (posts and site.spi still live in engine/). Uncommitted: gh-pages.yml rust channel stable -> nightly-2026-10-18 (someone's RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE edit).")
inl note_of_blog_11 () = Note ("BLOG", 11u32, "2026-10-19T13:06Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:06 helper-lane lap 2: the missing generator, written (uncommitted, unrun): engine/generate.spi (main: showcase.entries |> site.files |> build.write_files dist, exit 0) registered last in engine/package.spiproj; generator/Cargo.toml (bin blog-generate over ../engine/generate.rs, the x crate convention with [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output; chrono as the only dep, since lib file_system tests pull it); scripts/generate.ps1 (BuildSpiral -> cargo build --release -> run in the blog root, prints BLOG-GENERATED); scripts/publish.ps1 now renames publish-tree's dist/index.html to dist/tree.html (same folder, so its relative links still work) and then generates the site into dist/, so https://i574n.github.io/blog/ becomes the blog itself (site.base_url already assumes that root) with the source tree at /blog/tree.html; .gitignore gains /generator/target/. HEAVY QUEUE: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\blog\\scripts\\generate.ps1 (~10 min).")
inl note_of_blog_12 () = Note ("BLOG", 12u32, "2026-10-19T14:23Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:23 helper-lane lap 3: settled the engine/posts/site split by reading spiproj usage: a spiproj has one packageDir and resolves packages by name under it, so separate posts/ and site/ PACKAGES could not reference engine/ while packageDir points at spiral/lib. But module lists take folders (lib/spiral/package.spiproj lists rust/ with rust and rust_operators inside) and a module keeps its bare name, so the split is one package at the blog root: modules engine/ (html post page index rss style site build), posts/ (showcase), site/ (generate). Sources need no reference edits; what changes is the spiproj location (packageDir ../spiral/lib), each file's ///- --package line (.. instead of .), Cargo.toml entry/output and bin path (../site/generate.*), generate.ps1's engine path and build.ps1's module list. base_url (engine site.spi line 25) belongs in site/ with generate. Also verified for the queued run: generate.ps1 matches lib.ps1 BuildSpiral(SpiPath, OutPath, Name), and the full blog build passed 10:00-10:04 (BLOG-SHOWCASE-POST note 4). Not applied: blog/engine is in the 11:40 core-switch freeze and the generator is still unrun.")
inl note_of_blog_13 () = Note ("BLOG", 13u32, "2026-10-19T16:26Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 12:26 helper-lane: the engine/posts/app split is scripted as lab/BLOG/split.ps1 (dry run by default, prints the plan and the new root package.spiproj): one package at the blog root (packageDir ../spiral/lib, |core- and spiral-) with module folders engine/ (html post page index rss style site build), posts/ (showcase) and app/ (generate; named app because a folder site/ beside the module site would read ambiguously), each file's ///- --package . becomes .., and Cargo.toml, scripts/generate.ps1, scripts/build.ps1 and common.ps1's notebook output names follow. Not applied: it moves tracked files (git shows them as moves for the user's commit) and the queued generate.ps1 run still targets engine/generate.spi; apply with -Apply after that run, then rerun build.ps1 and generate.ps1.")
inl note_of_blog_14 () = Note ("BLOG", 14u32, "2026-10-19T22:03Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:03 helper-lane lap 6: ran the queued generate row under the gate (lab/BLOG/generate-run.ps1, log lab/BLOG/generate-20261019-1757.log, 210 s including 172 s waiting for the slot): blog/scripts/generate.ps1 printed BLOG-GENERATED dist/ (7 site files) on the installed pack 10 compiler, so the generator still builds and runs after the core switch. That was the gate before applying lab/BLOG/split.ps1 (engine/ posts/ app/ folders). The split moves tracked files, which shows up as renames in the user's commit, so it waits for the user's go and is not applied here.")
inl note_of_blog_15 () = Note ("BLOG", 15u32, "2026-10-22T05:37Z", "main", "01:36 main: lab run outputs moved to the run cache, per the lab rule that run outputs never sit in lab: 1 file(s) (logs, notebooks, diffs, metrics/gate/timing TSVs) are now under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\BLOG\\ at the same relative paths, sha256 checked. Scripts and probes stay in lab/BLOG until the item lands.")
inl note_of_blog_16 () = Note ("BLOG", 16u32, "2026-10-22T10:03Z", "main", "2026-10-22 06:03 main: deciding the split myself (memory decide-autonomously, 10-21: moves by rule are mine; the user commits). Rule: apply lab/BLOG/split.ps1 -Apply (engine/*.spi into engine/, posts/, app/, plus a root package.spiproj) only when (1) tonight's main-blog-regen2 job has finished with BLOG-GENERATED on the current layout (baseline output), (2) 'git -C blog status' shows no staged or modified file under engine/ (the user's commits come first), (3) after the move, scripts/generate.ps1 prints BLOG-GENERATED again and dist/ is byte-identical to the baseline output, apart from timestamps if any. If (3) fails, the move is reverted with the same script's inverse, and the reason is recorded. lab/BLOG/generate-run.ps1 is superseded by eoie job/window runs of generate.ps1 (tonight's launch uses that), so it is deleted when the folder drains.")
inl note_of_blog_17 () = Note ("BLOG", 17u32, "2026-10-22T18:16Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:15 main: the split rule of 06:03 was checked and is held at condition (2): git -C blog status shows 9 staged engine/ files (build, generate.spi, generate.rs, html, index, page, post, rss, site; generate.spi and generate.rs also modified after staging). The user's commits come first. The resume check is mechanical: rerun the rule when engine/ shows no staged or modified file.")
inl note_of_blog_18 () = Note ("BLOG", 18u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:29 (subagent): blocked: blog/engine in the core-switch freeze; generate.ps1 still queued -> next: after the all-clear: the queued generate run, then the folder split script | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): freeze lifted, but the folder split waits for the queued generate.ps1 run (it targets engine/generate.spi) -> next: after the generate run: apply the split script | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: generate.ps1 green on pack 10; split.ps1 waits for the user's go (moves tracked files) -> next: user go")
inl spiral_ui () = Task ("SPIRAL-UI", "Spiral-native reactive UI library (user question 2026-10-16, later, not on the blog's path): fine-grained signals and statically typed view trees in the style of Leptos' tachys and reactive_graph, designed for Spiral rather than ported line by line. Most of tachys (52 public traits, tuple and either_of impls, 32 macro_rules) exists to make Rust monomorphize a static view tree, which Spiral's partial evaluation does directly, so the expected size is a fraction of the ~45k-line Rust core. Browser target through the TypeScript backend (no wasm toolchain), Rust and wasm optional. Gate: dice/ui and one new app rebuilt on it, and js-framework-benchmark rows next to Leptos 0.9 on the same machine. Upstream Leptos is not frozen (0.9.0-rc0 2026-10-15), so it is a reference, not a dependency", Feature, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "", "spiral,ui,lib,main,lab-held", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_ui () = Planning ("SPIRAL-UI", Unestimated, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_ui_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:47Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:47 helper-lane lap 1: sized the gate's first consumer: dice/ui is 14 .spi files / 2,068 non-blank lines (transaction 414, dice_view 396, rpc 261, settings_view 223, use_transactions 210 ...) and calls 52 distinct leptos.* bindings 502 times. The surface is small and mostly two layers: view construction (element_to_view 98, text_fragment 74, div 22, element_to_fragment 22, td/th 19 each, tr 4, view_list_to_view 7, vec_to_view 5, on_click 3) and signals (signal_get 49, signal_get_untracked 37, new_arc_memo 17, signal_update 8, signal_set 7, create_signal 7, new_arc_rw_signal 6, use_context 8, arc_memo 4), plus log 23. So a Spiral-native UI that rebuilds dice/ui needs about 15 view primitives and 9 signal/memo/context primitives, not tachys' 52 traits; that is the concrete MVP list for the design.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:32Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:32 helper-lane lap 2: first executable sketch of the MVP surface (lab/SPIRAL-UI/ui.spi, about 45 lines): signal t = {get; set} over a mut cell, memo t = {get} (pull-based for now), union attribute (Class, Href, OnClick), union rec view (Text, Element of tag * attributes * children, Reactive of () -> view, Fragment), constructors text/element/reactive, and a let rec walk (count_elements / and let count_list). Probe main builds div[class app] { button[on_click: clicks + 1] { + }, reactive span }, sets the signal to 3 and returns count_elements * 10 + memo: C exit 36 (3 elements, memo 6), via try.ps1 + Invoke-Heavy. Covers the shapes behind dice/ui's top bindings (element_to_view, text_fragment, signal_get/set, new_arc_memo, on_click) at type level; missing for a real UI: dependency tracking (memo recomputation on signal change), effects, and an HTML/DOM renderer.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI", 3u32, "2026-10-19T15:15Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:15 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the next slice into lab/SPIRAL-UI/ui.spi (not yet run: the core-switch freeze stops bounded jobs until main's all-clear): signals are now push-based - each signal keeps a subscriber list (mut list of () -> ()), set notifies them (let rec notify), and create_memo source compute caches its value behind a dirty flag that the source's subscription sets, so get recomputes only after a change; plus an HTML string renderer (render_html / render_children / render_attributes: class and href become attributes, OnClick renders nothing, Reactive renders its current view). probe_main.spi counts recomputations through a mut counter: expected exit 1236 = HTML byte-equal to <div class=app><button>+</button><span>n</span></div> (1000) + 2 computes (200) + 3 elements (30) + doubled 6, with 5000 added if the memo recomputed without a change. Explicit source per memo for now; automatic dependency tracking (a current-observer stack) is the next step.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI", 4u32, "2026-10-19T16:00Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 12:00 helper-lane lap 5: next step written beside the explicit-source version: lab/SPIRAL-UI/ui_tracking.spi does automatic dependency tracking with a tracker record {observer : mut option of a mark-dirty callback}: a memo computes inside a scope that sets the observer to its own mark-dirty function (restoring the outer observer afterwards, so nested memos work), and every signal get subscribes the current observer, so a memo depends exactly on the signals it read. probe_tracking.spi: a memo over clicks, an unrelated signal changed in between; expected exit 2 (one computation at creation, none after the unrelated change, one after clicks changes, cached afterwards). Unrun until main's freeze all-clear; subscriptions are not yet deduplicated or dropped when a memo stops reading a signal.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI", 5u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run (try.ps1 C on the installed compiler, switched core): the push-based memo + HTML renderer probe exits 1236 as predicted (HTML byte-equal, 2 computations, 3 elements, doubled 6). The tracking variant failed to compile (ETY-ExpectedMutableLayout: a mut field inside an immutable record cannot be assigned); fixed by making the tracker itself a mut cell of the observer option, rerun pending.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI", 6u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:39 (subagent): blocked: push-based memo + HTML probe written, bounded jobs frozen -> next: after the all-clear: run probe_main (expect 1236) | lap 6 13:42 (subagent): probe 1236 PASS; tracking fixed (tracker as mut cell), rerun pending -> next: rerun probe_tracking (expect 2) | lap 6 13:49 (subagent): tracking probe PASS (2): automatic dependency tracking works -> next: effects + subscription dedup | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: probes pass (1236, 2) -> next: next widget")
