inl gadt_join_point_growing_index () = Task ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", "Compiler hang: a let rec join point over a runtime GADT value whose recursion grows the type index (term n -> term (succ n)) never finishes compiling, instead of sharing one join point across phantom indices or reporting polymorphic recursion", TaskKind, P1, D4, 40u32, Active, "", "compiler,gadt,bug,mega,main,lab-held", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index () = Planning ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_1 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 1u32, "2026-10-19T14:36Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:36 found by helper-lane-2 while sizing MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER's shared goal (substitution and renaming under binders in lean_cic, spiral_proves, zeta). Repros in spiral/lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX (installed single-flight compiler, try.ps1 -Backend C, bounded): debruijn_rename.spi, a well-scoped de Bruijn term union rec term n (Var of var n, Lam of term (succ n), App) with inl rec rename/lift and inl rec size over a static term, compiles and exits 5 (correct size after weakening). debruijn_size_runtime.spi, the same term made runtime (inl ~t) and size a let rec join point: TIMEOUT after 45 s (compiler killed; 60 s for the rename variant). gadt_same_index_runtime.spi, a let rec join point over a runtime union rec tree n whose recursion keeps the index: compiles, exits 3. So the hang needs both a runtime value and an index that grows in the recursive call; it looks like join-point specialization per index instantiation without a bound (succ n, succ (succ n), ...), although the index nominals are phantom (nominal succ n = ()) and the runtime layout is identical for every n. Expected: one join point for all n (erase phantom indices for specialization), or a fast EJP-style error naming the polymorphic recursion. Related but distinct: GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG (Done, the case-field type growth in ty_is_uninhabited). Owner: compiler lane (main)")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_2 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 2u32, "2026-10-19T14:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:39 lap 2 grain: triage: the hang is unbounded specialization, not a wrong result. size is a let rec join point; join points specialize per argument type, and every recursive call on term (succ n) has a new type, so the specialization set never closes (the fixed-index runtime case compiles because its key repeats); rename is an inl rec over a runtime ~t, which inlines without end. The compiler bug is the missing guard: partial evaluation should stop like EJP0040 does for dynamic ifs, by counting distinct specializations of one join-point body (or index-type nesting depth) and reporting the growing index with a hint (erase the index at runtime through an existential such as a term_dyn wrapper, or keep the recursion static). The runtime remedy for the lean_cic, spiral_proves and zeta roots is that erasure, independent of the guard Next: find where single-flight memoizes join-point specializations (join point dictionary keyed by body + argument types) and add a per-body distinct-key counter with an EJP report, as a candidate patch in lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_3 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:42 patch design located in single-flight PartEval (spiral_compiler.fs 158355-161335): (1) EUnbox's runtime case body (159931 run s a, and Unbox's 159962) is not wrapped in within_dynamic_if (159488, EJP0040, only if_'s two branches are, 159519-159520), so an inl rec recursing through a runtime match never reaches the nesting limit; wrap those calls so EJP0040 counts union-match branches too. (2) EJoinPoint' (159727-159764): dict is the per-(backend, body) specialization table; at the Owner branch add a distinct-key cap (SPIRAL_JP_SPECIALIZATION_LIMIT, default 1000) that raises RunawayUnrollDiagnostic.render with a new code and the hint erase the growing index at runtime (existential) or keep the recursion static. Constraint: PartEval is a SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL pair, and pair-drift.ps1 fails a build whose normal pair changed on one side only; routing the calls through PartEvalSeams would pass the check but spend hopacsota's G6 seam budget on a non-seam. So the patch either mirrors both guards on gpt5parallel's side (it already has jp_method_specialization_budget_exhausted at 41659) or waits for gpt5parallel's retirement (HOPACSOTA-P6).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_4 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:45 analysis note (helper-lane-2, for the compiler lane): why the runtime form cannot terminate today. A runtime value needs a residual layout for its type, and residual unions are monomorphized per type arguments; the type term zero has a Lam case whose field is term (succ zero), whose Lam field is term (succ (succ zero)), and so on, so the layout of term zero is an infinite family of union types. That is the same type-level growth GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG met (its fix capped nesting in ty_is_uninhabited for the inhabitedness check), but a runtime value additionally forces the layouts and a join point per instantiation, which the cap does not cover; the static repro avoids it because partial evaluation consumes the term before any layout exists. Two directions: (a) index erasure for runtime layouts when every index argument is a phantom nominal (nominal succ n = ()): one residual union per GADT head, cases tagged as usual, index checks already done by the type checker; (b) a bounded detection that reports EJP-style polymorphic recursion through a growing phantom index, naming the union and the field. (b) is the cheap correctness fix (no hang), (a) is what the mega roots' runtime substitution needs")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_5 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 5u32, "2026-10-19T18:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:39 lap 4 grain: helper lane 2 analysis (10:45) explains the non-termination: a runtime value needs a residual layout per type argument, and term zero has a Lam field of term (succ zero) whose Lam field is term (succ (succ zero)), so monomorphization never closes; the guard (cap distinct index instantiations per union) belongs in the PartEval pair, blocked by pair-drift until gpt5parallel retires Next: none until HOPACSOTA-P6, unless the guard fits a shared pre-PartEval section")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_6 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 6u32, "2026-10-21T22:23Z", "main", "18:23 code read (no compile): the non-termination is the eager layout recursion at PartEval's TUnion arm, single-flight spiral_compiler.fs:159909 (gpt5parallel's twin at 151407). Building YUnion maps every case payload through ty s v, and for term zero the Lam payload is term (succ zero), a new type at every level, so ty recurses through TUnion forever (recursive unions with fixed arguments terminate only because their instantiation repeats). Cheapest correct guard, mirrored on both sides of the pair, so pair-drift passes without spending a hopacsota seam: a union-nesting depth counter around the payload mapping at TUnion. On single-flight a plain ref local to the PartEval closure is enough, since its ty runs on one thread; on gpt5parallel the twin needs the depth carried in its environment record, because its ty runs on Hopac workers. Above SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT (default 256), raise a RunawayUnrollDiagnostic-style error naming the union, the case and the last two instantiations, with the hint from helper-lane-2 note 2 (erase the phantom index at runtime with an existential, or keep the recursion static). Unblocked enough to draft as lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX/union-depth.ps1 with both anchors; gate: the static repro unchanged, the runtime repro errors in < 5 s instead of hanging, and -Suite all DISAGREE 0.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_7 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 7u32, "2026-10-21T22:31Z", "main", "18:31 guard drafted: lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX/union-depth.ps1, 4 anchors, dry run matches each once; pair-drift on the patched copy is clean (both PartEval sides changed). Each side gets a UnionNesting module beside its DynamicIfNesting (SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT, default 256, ThreadLocal depth; ty recursion is synchronous per thread on both cores). At the TUnion arm, each case payload's ty s v runs inside the counter, and past the limit it raises EJP0041 through RunawayUnrollDiagnostic.render with the source frames, a headline naming the union case and the hint to keep the recursion static or erase the index with an existential. single-flight raises with raise_type_error and an empty trace, gpt5parallel with partEvalTypeError [], each as its EJP0040 does. Gate, fixed now: the lab repros debruijn_size_runtime.spi and debruijn_rename_runtime.spi end in EJP0041 within 30 s instead of hanging; gadt_same_index_runtime.spi and the static repros compile as before; -Suite all shows DISAGREE 0 with no residual change (256 levels is far above any legitimate union nesting in the samples, to be confirmed by the review). Follow-up: register EJP0041 in DiagnosticClassifier beside EJP0040 (7453/7480/7638/7661/7938) so a stall report classifies it. Rides the next pack.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_8 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 8u32, "2026-10-21T22:34Z", "main", "18:34 the classifier follow-up is now in the same patch: union-depth.ps1 has 9 anchors (EJP0041 literal, DiagnosticEJP0041 case, both code mappings, a classifyIngress branch as DiagnosticRunawayInlineUnroll + DiagnosticTerminalSemantic, then the 2 modules and 2 TUnion guards). Dry run matches each once; pair-drift on the patched copy is clean. Any other exhaustive match over DiagnosticCodeId that needs the new case shows up in the candidate build.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_9 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 9u32, "2026-10-22T00:24Z", "main", "20:24 pre-build check of the open question in note 18:34 (other exhaustive matches over DiagnosticCodeId): grep finds exactly 4 EJP0040 sites in spiral_compiler.fs (case 7480, code mapping 7638, text mapping 7661, classifyIngress 7938-7939) and union-depth.ps1 covers each with its EJP0041 twin, so no further match should break the candidate build; outside the core EJP0040 appears only in prose (FRONTIER.md, hopacsota PLAN.md, a mega sample README, NEXT.md, tmp/AGENTS.md:98). New companion lab/GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX/union-depth-doc.ps1 (dry-run, anchor unique) documents SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT and EJP0041 next to SPIRAL_IF_NESTING_LIMIT/EJP0040 in tmp/AGENTS.md; apply with the pack.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_10 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 10u32, "2026-10-22T06:05Z", "main", "02:05 main: union-depth.ps1 is fully in the PACK-AFTER-NIGHT core (pack-next carries it). All 5 EJP0041 blocks and both union-payload layout sites are present verbatim. The 2 counter blocks hold a __MODULE__ placeholder the script fills at run time, and module UnionNesting with SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT is at pack core 137748, used at 151463-151468. So the guard installs with that pack. At install: apply union-depth-doc.ps1 to tmp/AGENTS.md, then drain the folder. The debruijn_*/gadt_* repros: the hang case becomes an EJP0041 rejection, and adding it as a negative matrix row (ExpectedErrors pin) is the one regression sample this fix gets, after install.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_11 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 11u32, "2026-10-22T18:52Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:52 main: install steps of the 02:05 rule applied. (1) union-depth-doc.ps1 applied to apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md: SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT (default 256, checked in both cores at 137748 and 159661) and EJP0041 are documented beside EJP0040, as an unstaged edit over the user's staged file. (2) The one regression sample: samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime = debruijn_size_runtime.spi (a runtime term n with let rec size, the former 45 s hang), pinned in matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors with Contains 'has no finite layout' (UnionNesting.headline). Its first row comes from PACK-NEXT-3's review, and that row is evidence for this item, not for the pack: expected = a compile error containing the pin within the 20 s timeout. A timeout or a different error reopens the item with that text. Lab drains after that row: the other 3 repros (static rename exit 5, same-index runtime) are recorded in notes 1-2.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_12 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 12u32, "2026-10-23T07:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 03:11 main: lab drain: union-depth.ps1 and union-depth-doc.ps1 deleted, both landed (tree core has EJP0041 at 7455 and SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT in 4 places; apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md documents the limit). No script reads them (by-stem search of spiral *.ps1 with a positive control; only two scratchpad pack scripts of installed packs name them). The 4 .spi probes stay as reference for the open scope (one join point shared across phantom indices); the regression sample gadt_growing_index_runtime is an adapted copy of debruijn_size_runtime, not byte-equal, so that probe stays too.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_13 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 13u32, "2026-10-23T18:06Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:06 main: REOPENED by the 10-22 14:52 rule (a timeout reopens the item). The regression sample's first real rows (next4 review-2, run single-flight-20261023-135519): samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime times out at 20 s on C, Rust and Delphi ('no result within 20000 ms', one worker recycle each), so EJP0041's union-nesting cap does not catch this runtime-term shape (let rec size over a runtime term n), which is the former 45 s hang. next3b's earlier rows were a different error (a type error, from an older edit of the sample, which changed at 10-22 19:24). Per the rule this is evidence for this item, not for PACK-NEXT-4's verdict. Next: find where the runtime case grows (the join point for size specialized per index) with a bounded probe, then a cap there with the 'has no finite layout' headline.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_14 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 14u32, "2026-10-23T18:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:11 main: bounded probe (installed single-flight compiler, --backend C, 120 s bound, compile window): no output and no EJP0041 within 120 s, killed. Depth 256 cannot take that long, so the growth is NOT inside one union layout (where UnionNesting counts nested ty calls): each step is a new join-point specialization of size at term (succ^k n), laid out one level at a time, so the union depth never passes 1. Fix direction, rule written before drafting: cap the type depth of a join point's specialization key (the nesting of nominal applications like succ (succ ...)) at SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT and raise EJP0041 with the existing 'has no finite layout' headline there; adopt only if the sample then fails within 20 s with that text and the matrix shows no other row change.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_15 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 15u32, "2026-10-23T18:20Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:20 main: cap drafted per the rule above: %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\gadt-index-cap.ps1, in pack-next9 (core 2741D35BD11FF46B). Both UnionNesting modules (the pair stays symmetric; pair-drift 0 one-sided) gain applicationDepth (YApply nesting), tooDeep and indexHeadline ('specializing a join point at a type index nested more than N levels: ... has no finite layout', so the matrix.psd1 pin still matches). Each core's EJoinPoint' case checks its env_global_type before claiming the key and raises EJP0041 with the existing hint (SPIRAL_UNION_NESTING_LIMIT). TJoinPoint' (type-level join points) is left alone. Assumption to verify: the growing index reaches env_global_type (size's join point is built under its forall n); if it only travels in the argument's type, the review row stays a timeout and the check moves to the argument types. next9's review.ps1 prints the sample's rows with pinned=True/False; adopt per the rule (fails within 20 s with the pin, no other row change). The next9 Debug type-check build queued behind the 5f build covers the new code.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_16 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 16u32, "2026-10-23T19:27Z", "main", "2026-10-23 15:27 main: the cap WORKS. next9's Debug type-check build (core 1AF7692BCABBEEB8, build succeeded in 410 s) compiles samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime --backend C to 'error[EJP0041]: specializing a join point at a type index nested more than 256 levels: a type index that grows at every level (term n -> term (succ n)) has no finite layout', exit 5 after 10.9 s (Debug; Release is faster), against the installed compiler's 120 s with no result. So the growing index does reach env_global_type, and the matrix.psd1 pin ('has no finite layout') matches. Remaining per the rule: next9's review must show every backend row failing with the pin within 20 s and no other row change; it rides review-3's stack (next9 after next8).")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_17 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 17u32, "2026-10-23T21:45Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:45 main: every backend row now ends in the pinned EJP0041 on the next9 Debug build: C 10.9 s, Delphi 12.7 s, F# 10.8 s, Rust 21.5 s (exit 5, 'has no finite layout' in each). Rust is 1.5 s over the 20 s row timeout on a DEBUG build with cells-pack's suite running beside it; Release is faster, so the rule's 'within 20 s' is decided by review-3's Release rows, not by this probe. If Rust's Release row still times out, the cause is the depth (256 levels): the next lever is checking the specialization depth incrementally instead of recomputing applicationDepth per join point.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_18 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 18u32, "2026-10-24T03:49Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:49 main, corrected 01:19: samples/gadt_growing_index_runtime still hangs on the installed single-flight (B553EF6B78E6EE29) and on the 5f candidate: both TIME OUT at 300 s (probe job-1792813100262). The reason is that the cap (pack-next9 gadt-index-cap.ps1, proven on a Debug build at 15:27/17:45) was never part of 5f, so no bisect is needed. It now rides pack 5g (compose adds it; review-4 job-1792813775220), where the rule of note 15 decides: every backend row fails with the pin 'has no finite layout' within 20 s, and no other row changes.")
inl note_of_gadt_join_point_growing_index_19 () = Note ("GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX", 19u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 10:45 (subagent2): analysis for main: runtime layout of term zero is an infinite union family (monomorphized per index); fix options: phantom-index erasure (a) or bounded polymorphic-recursion error (b) -> next: main's lane")
inl cells_gate_machine_window () = Task ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", "Cells --gate takes a test-suite machine window from EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS and waits while a live GUI run or a compiler install holds the machine, instead of lanes asking in chat (split from CELLS-GATE 2026-10-19)", Feature, P1, D2, 30u32, Blocked, "EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS", "cells,testing,gate,one-call,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_gate_machine_window () = Planning ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_1 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 1u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 first grain, the interface this item needs from EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS: one call to request a window of class test-suite with an expected duration, granted or queued, and a release in the gate's teardown (spiral.notebook.ex cond, next to CompilerClient.shutdown, which also runs when the gate fails). The gate can estimate the duration from the file and test count; main's 10:51 data point: sm'.spi with 42 tests took 217 s on the warm daemon and 537 s with a process per compile. The conflicts that matter here are declared once: test-suite vs live-gui (the 10-14 reaper incidents) and test-suite vs compiler-install. Until that call exists, nothing changes in Cells. Status: blocked on the eoie lane.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_2 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 2u32, "2026-10-19T16:01Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:01 lap 7 status check: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS is still (EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS  Feature  P1  D4  0%  Planned), so the Cells side stays unstarted on purpose; the interface from note 1 is unchanged.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_3 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 3u32, "2026-10-19T16:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:43 lap 8 grain: placement decided while blocked: the machine-window acquire and release go into the wrappers that start a gate (the scaffold's scripts/gate.ps1 around its mix call, and Invoke-Heavy callers), not into mix spiral.notebook's gate branch (spiral.notebook.ex:10-19), because Cells does not call eoie (the same rule CELLS-NEW-PROJECT follows by printing the epic command instead of running it). The Cells side therefore needs no change; the wrapper needs one eoie call before mix (window test-suite with a duration) and one in its finally block. Still blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned, 0%)")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_4 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 4u32, "2026-10-19T17:19Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:20 lap 9 grain: planning set (estimate 60m, load light) and the duration input for the window request found: the gate CLI already writes --metrics <dir>/tests.tsv (notebook.ex:271-275, columns File Test Target Status Ms Memo), so the gate wrapper can request a test-suite window with expected duration = sum of Ms of the previous tests.tsv for the same paths (memo-hit rows count ~0), falling back to a fixed default on a first run; the wrapper passes --metrics into the per-run dir. Still blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned 0%): it unblocks with an eoie verb pair that grants or queues a class=test-suite window for N minutes and releases it")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_5 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 5u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:42 lap 10 grain: the window-duration half is written and self-tested: lab/CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW/expected-minutes.ps1 -MetricsDir <previous run> reads the gate's --metrics tests.tsv, sums Ms of rows whose Memo is not hit (Memo hit is the only value Cells counts, notebook.ex:214), times a 1.5 margin, ceil to minutes, at least 1; no table = 10 min first-run default. Self-test on a synthetic table (30 s run + 50 s hit + 50 s run) gives 2 min; a missing dir gives 10. The acquire/release half still waits on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned 0%): the gate wrapper will pass Minutes as the requested window length")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_6 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 6u32, "2026-10-19T17:58Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:58 lap 11 grain, an interim bridge until EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS exists: the scaffold's scripts/gate.ps1 (lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/patch/priv/new_project/scripts/gate.ps1) now runs the mix gate through spiral's own heavy gate (dot-sources apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 when the spiral checkout has it, Invoke-Heavy -Label cells gate <project>), so a gate waits for a free heavy slot instead of competing with suites; without heavy.ps1 it runs directly. Invoke-Heavy has no reentrancy marker (heavy.ps1 reads no env var), so a nested call would take a second slot or wait on itself: gate.ps1 takes -InsideHeavySlot to skip it, and spiral-new-run.ps1 (which already holds a slot) passes it; both parse with 0 errors; the ExUnit assertion (gate.ps1 mentions spiral.notebook) still holds. When EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS lands, the Invoke-Heavy call becomes the window acquire with expected-minutes.ps1's length")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_7 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 7u32, "2026-10-22T05:51Z", "main", "01:51 main: unblocked. EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS exists now (eoie window request/run/release, classes live-gui|test-suite|heavy-build|compile|compiler-install). The duration half is promoted: lab expected-minutes.ps1 is now apps/cells/spi/expected_minutes.ps1, re-self-tested (a synthetic 30 s run + 50 s hit + 50 s run gives 2 min; a missing metrics dir gives 10), and the lab folder is deleted. Remaining: lib_gate.ps1 and the scaffold gate.ps1 take a test-suite window of expected_minutes around their mix call. A wrapper can't tell it already holds a window: there is no env marker (checked eoie src), and every main job wraps lib_gate in 'eoie window run compile', so a nested request could wait on itself. Asked eoie for EOIE_WINDOW_HELD=<class>:<id> on window run children (and EOIE_HEAVY_HELD for job start --heavy). The wrapper change lands once that exists, with no lib_gate run in flight.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_8 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 8u32, "2026-10-23T21:32Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:32 main: the marker exists in eoie source (src/eoie_window_run/run.spi child_status sets EOIE_WINDOW_HELD on the window-run child) but not in the installed eoie.exe yet: 'eoie window run compile 1 -- pwsh -Command $env:EOIE_WINDOW_HELD' printed it empty at 17:3x. The wrapper change (lib_gate.ps1 and the scaffold gate.ps1 take a test-suite window of expected_minutes around their mix call unless EOIE_WINDOW_HELD is set) waits for the eoie release that carries it; re-probe with that one-liner first.")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_9 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 11:42 (subagent2): interface spelled out (test-suite window acquire with duration, release in the gate teardown; conflicts live-gui, compiler-install) -> next: blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS | lap 12 14:29 (subagent2): scaffold gate.ps1: -Path is [string[]], which pwsh -File passes as one comma string (a.spi,b.spi -> Resolve-Path fails); now splits on commas like try.ps1 -Modules; parse 0, split check resolves both files. Dep EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS still Planned 0% -> next: swap Invoke-Heavy for the window acquire when EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS lands | lap 13 14:41 (subagent2): blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned 0%, EOIE lane); interim bridge in the scaffold gate.ps1 stays -> next: unblocks: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS lands its window acquire verb | lap 14 15:03 (subagent2): blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS (Planned) -> next: EOIE lane lands the window verb | lap 16 16:06 (subagent2): blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS -> next: EOIE lane | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): blocked -> next: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS | lap 19 18:56 (subagent2): blocked -> next: EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS")
inl note_of_cells_gate_machine_window_10 () = Note ("CELLS-GATE-MACHINE-WINDOW", 10u32, "2026-10-25T00:01Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:01 main groom: status Active -> Blocked. Since the 10-19 lap 6 note, the only next step has been 'blocked on EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS', and that item is itself Blocked at 95% (eoie team). This resumes when it is Done.")
inl sim_in_spiral_bulk () = Task ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", "Bulk half of the xsim rewrite in Spiral, x lane: frames round trip, invariants, event glue, scenario and delivery generation compiled to Rust (and Gleam where pure), no Cells actor port needed", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "-", "sim,cells,rust,gleam,x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk () = Planning ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_1 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 1u32, "2026-10-19T19:47Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-19 15:47 x lane accepts helper lane 2's split (SIM-IN-SPIRAL lap 10, main agreed 15:46). Scope: slice 1 the frames round trip test (posts_of -> locate body_at -> assembled sources = file_cells, xsim cases 1-3), slice 2 invariants.ex as a named-violation decision union (lap 8), slice 3 rebuild/explode_posts glue (lap 9), then scenario/delivery generation in Rust under the 1k gate. The Elixir oracle is retired per slice, in the same change that reproduces its fixtures. The supervision half (World/Runner as gleam_otp) stays on SIM-IN-SPIRAL with the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR dep")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_2 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 2u32, "2026-10-19T20:13Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-19 16:13 slice 1 done (uncommitted in x): new Cells module apps/client/reading.spi takes the pure half of assemble (frame, cell_frames, Whole/Holed cell_reading, assembled) and the post-text reader locate used (frame_text_of: header parse + defused dots restored); assemble/assemble.spi and locate/locate.spi are JSON glue over it. Tests on rust + gleam agree: posted_cells_rebuild_exactly (posts_of then read back = file_cells over a link dot, a 600-weight CJK cell and a 360-weight word cell; xsim cases 1-3), posted_frames_keep_no_trailing_space, a_lost_frame_leaves_its_cell_holed. Round x-round-8b on pack 8b: Cells 11 modules 80 tests 0 failures, gate 16 crates 0 over (locate 570->591 from frame_text_of, the rest unchanged by 8b), x-compat 0 in 27 files, e2e 31+5. frames.ex can be retired once apps/sim calls the Gleam build of splitter/reading (needs the sim's Gleam build path, next). Slice 2 note: invariants.ex is pure but reads runner state snapshots (visible, starts, cancelled, evicted, results, wanted), so its oracle is hand-built snapshots until World/Runner exist; port it as a named-violation union over sorted pair lists")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_3 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 3u32, "2026-10-20T05:27Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 01:27 slice 2 written (uncommitted, verification queued): apps/client/invariants.spi ports xsim invariants.ex as backend-neutral Spiral (rust + gleam tests): snapshot record (visible/wanted/starts/cancelled/evicted/results/evicting as typed pair lists), union rule (5 named checks), union verdict = Held | Broken ids (first 5), injected baseline (string -> outcome), commute with the Elixir map semantics (extra ids give FAIL[] with no ids), and comparable's two regex rewrites as index/starts_with scanners that only slice on ASCII boundaries (spiral_cells[\\\\/]N-N -> spiral_cells/_, thread 'x' (N) panicked -> (_)); 9 tests. Oracle is hand-built snapshots for now; a cross-check needs xsim.run to dump snapshots. Slice 3 path (frames.ex retirement), checked in the compiler: the Gleam backend turns !!!!Export into pub fn wrappers (spiral_compiler.fs Gleam TyOp Export), so a Spiral entry exporting posts_of/file_cells/read_thread compiled to Gleam, built with gleam build, and put on the sim's code path lets Xsim call :module.fn directly (Spiral string = Erlang binary, list = Erlang list); then frames.ex is deleted in the change whose fixtures still match")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_4 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 4u32, "2026-10-20T08:06Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 04:06 slices 2 and 3 verified (x-chain4 Cells, x-chain5 builds; uncommitted): invariants.spi 9/9 on rust + gleam. Slice 3: Xsim.Frames no longer splits; its algorithm (posted units, defused dots, chunking, word cuts, header budget, file cells, thread posts) is the shared splitter.spi compiled to Gleam: apps/client/simframes/simframes.spi exports split_cell/file_cells/thread_posts, apps/sim/build.ps1 compiles it with BuildSpiral -Backend Gleam into apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames.gleam (841 lines) and runs gleam build; frames.ex (113 -> 64 lines) adds the ebins to the code path once (persistent_term) and keeps the Elixir glue (k/n tuples, restored, explode_posts). Gleam lists arrive as {tag_cons, head, tail} tuples ending in a nil atom, converted by shape not by tag name. splitter.spi gained framing {limit; label} so the sim's p-ids and test limits (20..120) go through the same code. Oracle step: all 11 sim fixtures (4 worlds + expected, thread-oauth1, prelude events + final) regenerate byte-identical from the Gleam build of the splitter, 39/39 ExUnit, corpus xsim.run commute ok, real-Cells run ok; xc post-thread still equals the sim's split, which now checks Rust vs BEAM builds of one source. Root Cargo.toml excludes apps/client/simframes (workspace glob)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_5 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 5u32, "2026-10-20T11:34Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 07:34 slice 4 written (verification waits for the Tibia live block, ends ~09:30): the scenario generator in backend-neutral Spiral. exsss.spi ports Erlang rand's exsss on u64 (splitmix64 seed58, xorshift116**, uniform_s with the truncated-range retry; both backends wrap u64: Rust wrapping_*, Gleam spiral_wrap_unsigned), with test vectors taken from erl itself (splitmix64_next(0), seed {1,8,16} state, ten uniform(8) draws, 1000/2^40/2 ranges, a second seed). personas.spi (persona union, seeded acts, break_source as an ASCII-boundary scanner for the regex (?<![\\d.])(\\d+)((?:\\.\\d+)?\\s*)$), scenario.spi (from_cells: trunk chaining, likes, unlikes, edits, breaking edits, persona streams, churn weave, in Elixir's draw order), schedule.spi (frame explosion through split_cell, jittered and duplicated deliveries merge-sorted by (time, index), scenario_stream = the xsim.events pipeline). simframes exports scenario_stream to Gleam (cells NUL-joined), Xsim.Spiral is the shared bridge, and test/spiral_test.exs is the oracle step: the Gleam build must reproduce fixtures/prelude.events.jsonl byte for byte and match the Elixir pipeline on posts.spi for two seeds with lurker/breaker/editor/fickle personas. events.spi also gained the xsim-events v1 decoder (Text/Whole/Null fields, round trip tests)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_6 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 6u32, "2026-10-20T14:52Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 10:52 slices 4-6 verified before retirement (x-chain16b + x-chain17): exsss, personas, scenario, final_state, world, invariants, schedule pass Cells on rust + gleam (exsss draws Erlang's numbers bit for bit; a PowerShell BigInteger replay of the port matched erl first). Sim ExUnit 44/44 with test/spiral_test.exs: the Gleam build of the Spiral scenario writes prelude.events.jsonl and prelude.final.json byte for byte, world_files writes all four e2e worlds and their expected programs byte for byte (fixtures regenerated by the Elixir oracle after this morning's prelude change), and Spiral equals the Elixir pipeline on two persona mixes and a two-thread corpus over two schedules (frames, breaking, churn, every persona). Retirement staged in the same change, verification pending (Tibia live block): Xsim.Scenario is a facade over Spiral (cells_raw/corpus_raw events through the xsim-events stream, rescheduled deliveries, break_source), personas.ex keeps only parse, x_world.ex is deleted (its JSON layout moved to Xsim.Json for xsim.frames), xsim.events and xsim.world call Spiral; xsim_test's XWorld case moved into world.spi. Perf: frame explosion and likes accumulate in reverse (were quadratic appends) for xsim.run's corpus")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_7 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 7u32, "2026-10-20T18:39Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 14:39 retirement verified (x-round19, sim 46/46): with Xsim.Scenario as a Spiral facade, xsim.run's full lib corpus keeps every invariant on both schedules (~45 s each, as before), all fixture guards match, thread_run proven both ways. Invariants retired too: Spiral invariants.spi reads the runner state as compact xsim-style JSON rows (visible with lazily computed baselines only where the key is wanted, wanted, start/cancelled/evicted tallies, results, broken ids, evicting) and answers check/commute/breaking/comparable; a side-by-side test against the old Elixir module (clean runs over 4 schedules, leak, recompile, stale and withdrawn faults, eviction, commute, breaking edits) agreed on every verdict, then invariants_elixir.ex and that test were deleted. Elixir left in apps/sim: runner, world, executors, daemon_memory, log, the facades and mix tasks (the supervision half + glue). Cells: all 39 notebooks pass except events' round trip, which times out at 300 s under load (rerun queued in x-chain20)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_8 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 8u32, "2026-10-20T19:52Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 15:52 x-chain20 green after the retirement: Cells 39/39 (events' round trip passes when not starved; its earlier failures were 300 s timeouts under load), client gate 16/0, x-compat 0 in 36 files, e2e 31+5, postable 1608 posts with 0 cells over one post, sim ExUnit 43/43, corpus run commutes, thread_run both ways. Decision: apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames.gleam is gitignored as a build artifact (21,213 lines / 1.17 MB of inl expansion, regenerated by apps/sim/build.ps1 before gleam build, unreviewable against the 1,000-line rule for crates); shrinking the Gleam output (let instead of inl on the shared pipeline) is the follow-up, measured by that file's size")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_9 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 9u32, "2026-10-20T20:12Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 16:12 Gleam output measured and shrunk: events.spi quoted and without_controls are let functions now (as inl, each JSON string field unrolled the 32-character control-escape fold): generated xsim_frames.gleam 21,213 -> 9,210 lines (1.17 MB -> 329 KB), encoded 7,240 -> 280 lines, simframes Gleam compile 34 s -> 8-11 s; events Cells no longer times out. x-chain21 green: Cells events/final_state/world/schedule, sim ExUnit 43/43 (fixtures byte-identical), corpus commute, thread_run both ways. Largest remaining Gleam functions: world_files 449, encoded 280, fields_from 279")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_10 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 10u32, "2026-10-20T23:56Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 19:56 Gleam output shrunk again (uncommitted, x-chain24 green: Cells final_state + world, sim fixtures byte-identical, ExUnit 43/43, corpus commute on both schedules, cells executor at concurrency 2 clean, thread_run both ways): final_state.spi laid_out is a thin inl over one let shared_layout that takes dyn items, brackets and level, so the JSON layout compiles once instead of at every call site (literal levels and bracket records would otherwise specialize a join point each). xsim_frames.gleam 9,210 -> 8,940 lines (329 -> 313 KB), world_files 449 -> 250; largest now encoded 280, fields_from 279")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_11 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 11u32, "2026-10-21T01:44Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 21:44 x-lane backlog refactor: dropped the X-CELLS-SPLIT dep (the shared-domain-first order is a sequencing preference, not a blocker; both progress in parallel and the dep hid this 90% story from agile next); estimate 240m for the remaining glue (Log codec facade, Gleam shrink of encoded/fields_from)")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_12 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 12u32, "2026-10-21T03:16Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-20 23:16 done (X-DONE deliberation 2): frames, scenario with personas and schedule, invariants and x_world run from Spiral through the Gleam build with each Elixir oracle retired in the slice that reproduced its fixtures; the pre-commit round (x-precommit, CI's build.ps1 without -fast) passes the sim end to end: ExUnit 43/43 with byte-exact fixture guards, corpus commute on both schedules, the real-Cells executor run and thread_run both ways. Log and the supervision half stay with SIM-IN-SPIRAL; the optional Gleam shrink of encoded/fields_from is not needed for done")
inl note_of_sim_in_spiral_bulk_13 () = Note ("SIM-IN-SPIRAL-BULK", 13u32, "2026-10-21T11:01Z", "x-lane", "2026-10-21 07:01 reverses the 10-20 15:52 decision (note 8), user: commit compiled files. apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames.gleam is committed like the client's 16 generated .rs files and marked linguist-generated=false in .gitattributes, so codegen changes show up as reviewable diffs in x; only frames/build/ (gleam's .beam and package output) stays ignored. The 10-20 reason (21,213 lines of inl expansion) is gone: 9,010 lines after the let/dyn shrinks, regenerated by apps/sim/build.ps1 on every build. Open question for later: the 1,000-line rule gates generated Rust crates only; the Gleam module is one 9,010-line file")
inl spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal () = Task ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", "Parser: a record field written as field = ;[ newline items newline ] |> f, with the closing bracket back at the field's column, fails to parse (Expected: } at the |>), and the whole top-level block is left out of its module, so a use elsewhere only says the module does not have the key; accept an operator continuation after a closing bracket at the field column, or name the offside rule in the message (repro lab/SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL/repro.spi)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,parser,diagnostics,main", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 1u32, "2026-10-19T23:01Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 19:01 helper-lane: cut down from main's failing_use_transactions.spi (dice ui line 184) to 6 lines, lab/SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL/repro.spi: inl render (v : i32) = { items = ;[ v ] |> am'.base } with ;[ on the field line and the ] line at the field's column. Using shapes.render from main gives 5:11 Expected: } (this block is left out of its module). Not a trigger: ;[ on its own line under the field (items = newline ;[ ... ] |> am'.base) parses and compiles, as does the same layout without the pipe. So the offside rule lets a bare ] return to the field column but not an operator after it. When the dropped block's module is another file, the only visible symptom is the module does not have the key: render at the use site, which is what main hit. Workaround: put ;[ on its own line or indent the ]. Note: an unused dropped block yields no error at all (exit 0), as the extracted real block showed.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 2u32, "2026-10-20T05:41Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-20 01:41 main, a second trigger of the same silent drop: a module-qualified constructor in a pattern (| Cons (xml.XmlElement (name, attributes, _), rest) =>) fails to parse; the block is left out of its module without an error, so a direct compile exits 0 and only the tests that use it fail, far away; with open xml and unqualified XmlElement the same code type-checks (and immediately showed 2 real type errors that had been hidden). Fix direction for both triggers: report the parse failure of a dropped block as an error at its own position, always, not only when the missing definition is used.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 3u32, "2026-10-21T03:24Z", "main", "23:24 root cause of the silent drop is in the host's DiagnosticRouter (Program.fs): ParserErrors for any module other than the entry only set 'related', which is printed only when a FatalError follows. A compile that succeeds, or whose entry fails with TypeErrors ('module does not have the key'), never shows the parse error. Fix written (lab SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL/related-parser-errors.ps1, host only, no exit-code change): other modules' ParserErrors print to stderr as 'warning: ParserErrors (...) (blocks that fail to parse are left out of their module)', and the entry's TypeErrors text gets the parse errors appended. The parser itself (accepting an operator after a closing bracket at the field column) stays open. Staged in the evening pack; the pack-candidate gate probes a package whose lib module has a broken block, with main using the good function and the broken one, installed vs pack.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 4u32, "2026-10-21T05:40Z", "main", "2026-10-21 01:40 main: parser half written: lab/SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL/field-operator-floor.ps1 (shared parser sections, both cores). Cause: operator_chain accepts a continuation operator only at or right of the column where the value expression started (blockParsingIndent i (<=) op), so after items = ;[ the floor is the ;[ column and '] |> f' at the field column + 2 ends the value. Fix: BlockParsingEnv.operator_floor, set by record field values (create/modify, both record forms) to field column + 1 and consumed by the first operator_chain (like continuation_column for match scrutinees): an operator may continue anywhere right of the field name, and the next field at the field column is never taken for a continuation. Only programs that failed to parse change. Regression sample staged (scratchpad pack-night/samples/record_field_operator_continuation): installed compiler gives main.spi:6:15 Expected: }. Goes in a night pack after the evening pack, with the matrix review.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 5u32, "2026-10-21T05:52Z", "main", "2026-10-21 01:52 main: host router fix gated on the exact evening pack (probe package: lib.spi with a good function and a block that fails to parse). main uses the good one: installed compiler exit 0 and no mention of the broken block; pack exit 0 plus 'warning: ParserErrors ... (blocks that fail to parse are left out of their module)'. main uses the broken one: both exit 5; the pack adds the warning line. Exit codes unchanged. Installs with the evening pack.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 6u32, "2026-10-21T07:20Z", "main", "2026-10-21 03:20 main: host warning for other modules' ParserErrors INSTALLED with the evening pack at 03:19. Parser half (field-operator-floor.ps1) waits for the night pack.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 7u32, "2026-10-21T09:39Z", "main", "2026-10-21 05:39 main: first field confirmation of the installed warning (tibia, 05:29): a parameter named 'open' (a keyword) in tibia src/hunting_pack.spi printed 'warning: ParserErrors ... Expected: ) (blocks that fail to parse are left out of their module)' at the exact lines, ahead of the downstream ETY-UnboundVariable errors that would otherwise have pointed only at the callers. Same class hit main at 05:2x (a 'join' parameter in the SEFAZ lab).")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 8u32, "2026-10-21T13:14Z", "main", "2026-10-21 09:14 main: night pack dry run clean on the installed base (core 084ABA2E2C4430C2, pair-drift clean, lint G1 596/G2 0). Corpus gate added BEFORE any install, because a parser change can break real code that no matrix sample has (lib-smoke rule): lab/SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL/night-gate.ps1 builds the Debug candidate, runs 'format-corpus flatten' (compareParses per file, G7-original-has-errors tally) over spiral lib/apps/samples + eoie + every tracked .spi of tibia, x, dice, alphabet and polyglot, with the installed compiler and with the candidate, diffs the listings, and compiles the repro sample with the candidate. Accept rule, fixed now: no file that parsed before stops parsing, every listing change is a file that failed before and parses now (explained by the fix), repro sample exit 0. Then pack-night.ps1 -Apply in an exclusive window. Runs after tibia's PR-17 block.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 9u32, "2026-10-21T17:59Z", "main", "13:59 night gate (job main-night-gate, 40 min; core 9E336ED57C8ACFE5 = field-operator-floor + GLEAM-STRING-LINEAR slice patch): Debug candidate built in 153 s; format-corpus flatten over 2010 rows gives identical listings for installed and candidate (32,925 bytes each, 0 differing lines; the tally regex no longer matches the current output, so only the listing diff counts). The gate's repro step pointed at a stale scratchpad copy (field-floor-check, package '|core-' = core not opened, so +. was unbound). The shipped sample record_field_operator_continuation (package 'core-'), compiled beside a core junction: candidate exit 0, C runs with exit 0; the installed compiler fails 'Expected: }' at 6:15. Rule met. night-gate.ps1 now points at scratchpad repro-root. Install (pack-night.ps1 -Apply, exclusive compiler-install) waits for my running lib-gate + SEFAZ v2 job and tibia's PR-19 block (~15:45-16:30); announced to peers before it starts.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 10u32, "2026-10-21T20:30Z", "main", "16:30 install order agreed with eoie: tibia's PR-19/PR-20 block until about 17:40, then eoie's r30–r33 combined release (about 58 min, to about 18:50; its checks ran on the current compiler), then my night-pack install (pack-night.ps1 -Apply: compiler-install, about 30 min, to about 19:20), then the review in a heavy slot (up to 90 min). Rule: don't queue compiler-install before eoie's release holds heavy-build (an older waiting exclusive request would block it); request only on eoie's 'released' message. The HOPACSOTA waits census runs in the other slot during eoie's release; SEFAZ variant 3 runs beside the review.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 11u32, "2026-10-21T20:40Z", "main", "16:40 pre-install review of pack-night.ps1 found a wrong baseline: without -Left, the review compared against the newest single-flight run dir, which is the 5-row -Filter rerun single-flight-20261021-042025, so every other row would have read as missing. The baseline is now the newest run with at least 7,000 result rows (today: single-flight-20261021-031858, the evening-pack review, 7,291 rows). Syntax-checked. Note for reading the review: that baseline predates today's lib/spiral sm'.spi changes (index_of, concat_list, concat_array); the matrix reaches lib in only about 19 rows, and any behavior change there gets checked against GLEAM-STRING-LINEAR.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 12u32, "2026-10-21T21:36Z", "main", "17:36 schedule change, rule fixed before measuring: eoie's r30-r33 release now expects 'released' around 19:40, so install-then-review (30 + 90 min) would end around 21:40 and leave a new compiler unreviewed overnight for every lane. The review moves BEFORE the install: scratchpad night-prereview.ps1 builds the night core 9E336ED57C8ACFE5 as a Release candidate in a private cache (candidates/night-release; Release builds are byte-reproducible across caches, AGENTS.md), then runs test.ps1 -Suite all -Native -Parallel 2 -ReuseNative with SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL set to it, with the regression sample in place, and compares with single-flight-20261021-031858. Install rule: DISAGREE 0, missing 0, every behavior-cell difference explained, and after pack-night.ps1 -Apply -SkipReview the installed SpiralCompilerCore hash must equal the reviewed candidate's (night-prereview.core.txt); on a mismatch pack-night runs the full review itself. Job main-night-prereview queued now, heavy, capped at 12 GB.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 13u32, "2026-10-21T23:20Z", "main", "19:20 PRE-INSTALL REVIEW PASSED (job main-night-prereview, 103 min incl. queue; Release candidate night-release, SpiralCompilerCore DA88EFB13FF61151 from core 9E336ED57C8ACFE5; -Suite all -Native -Parallel 2 -ReuseNative with SPIRAL_CC pinned; run single-flight-20261021-190054 vs single-flight-20261021-031858): rows 7,290 -> 7,294, missing 0, DISAGREE 0, 29 differing cells, all explained. 23 Gleam residuals = the GLEAM-STRING-LINEAR slice helper only (diffs checked: spiral_string_of_checked_bytes + spiral_string_starts_character added, the whole-slice to_string removed); 4 new rows = the regression sample record_field_operator_continuation on F#, C, Rust, Delphi; 2 behavior cells = conversation_negative_orphan_result F# and C compile timeout -> error, which reverts the baseline's own load timeouts (note 10-21 08:24) to the expected rejection. Peak memory 1.7 GB (capped run). Rule met. Install = pack-night.ps1 -Apply -SkipReview as soon as eoie messages 'released' (its r30-r33 release holds heavy slot 2); the install then checks that the installed SpiralCompilerCore hash equals DA88EFB13FF61151.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_14 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 14u32, "2026-10-22T00:09Z", "main", "20:09 install timing rule (fixed now): eoie now expects released at 20:20-20:30; the night-pack install (pack-night.ps1 -Apply -SkipReview, about 30 min) starts only if released arrives by 20:30, so it ends inside the 21:00 run; later than that, it goes to the next session unchanged (the pre-review and its core hash stay valid).")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_15 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 15u32, "2026-10-22T00:22Z", "main", "20:22 night-pack install deferred to the next session, by the 20:09 rule: eoie reported at 20:2x that the r30-r33 release will not install tonight (notebook phase 231/233: one infra failure, a Cells compiler daemon retired under low memory mid-call; one deterministic resume_cli assertion on an empty job home, fix ready; its single automatic retry will fail the same way, and eoie will not relaunch unattended). Order next session: eoie's release first, then pack-night.ps1 -Apply -SkipReview. The pre-review (single-flight-20261021-190054, DISAGREE 0, missing 0) and the candidate core hash DA88EFB13FF61151 stay valid as long as the night-pack core source is unchanged; -SkipReview checks the hash and falls back to a full review on any mismatch. Not installed tonight, also because installing during eoie's retry would swap the compiler under its Cells compiles.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_16 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 16u32, "2026-10-22T04:59Z", "main", "00:59 main: parser half INSTALLED with the night pack at 00:42 (pack-night.ps1 -Apply -SkipReview, single-flight core DA88EFB13FF61151 = the reviewed candidate, hopacsota AF323A03EE79F518). Both halves are now in shared spiral-bin: the host warning (evening pack) and operator_floor for record field values. The regression sample apps/compiler/tmp/samples/record_field_operator_continuation is in the tree (untracked, for the user's commit). The second trigger (a module-qualified constructor in a pattern) is QUALIFIED-UNION-CASES, not this item.")
inl note_of_spiral_diagnostic_piped_literal_17 () = Note ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-PIPED-LITERAL", 17u32, "2026-10-22T05:01Z", "main", "01:01 main: lab drained. night-gate.ps1 became apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/parser-corpus-gate.ps1 (-CoreSource, -Cache, -SampleDir), which diffs the format-corpus flatten listings of the installed compiler and a candidate over spiral, eoie, tibia, x, dice, alphabet and polyglot. The repro is the installed sample. The patch scripts are in the tree core (operator_floor at spiral_compiler.fs:44126). Folder deleted.")
