inl eoie_memory_features () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", "Mine every memory for eoie functionality that uses the type feature set, then implement it (user 2026-10-21 22:3x: 'pick from each memory what could be turned into an eoie functionality that leverages the typefeatureset then implement it to give even more value to claude, not to stuff everything into unformatted strings, drift')", Story, P1, D7, 40u32, Active, "", "eoie,memory,gadt", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_memory_features () = Planning ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", Minutes 720u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:30Z", "eoie", "22:30 pivot (to plan with more intelligence later, per the user): classify each memory as (a) enforceable behavior eoie can own (a verb, a check, a refusal, a typed default), (b) guidance that stays prose, (c) obsolete. Implement (a) with GADTs/existentials/reflection, then shrink the memory to a pointer at the feature. Precedents already in the tree or queue: read-clock-before-timestamps -> note-local-time (eoie stamps notes itself), decide-by-prewritten-rules -> experiment preregistration, no-sleep-polling/monitor-every-long-run -> job verbs with timeout and watcher (EOIE-JOBS). Candidates: stop-only-own-processes -> ps kill refuses PIDs outside its own job tree (OwnedPid|ForeignPid), copy-eoie-src-only -> a typed copy guard, pin-spiral-cc -> a toolchain profile type, 1000-line gate -> a standards check, eoie-generic-no-external -> a literal census check. Reflection can then list which memories are embodied by which verb and flag those still prose. Not a byte-identical string store.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_2 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 2u32, "2026-10-22T02:44Z", "eoie", "22:44 user 22:4x: 'return back the memories that can't be absorbed into eoie but are still useful to keep, don't lose useful stuff'. Checked: nothing was lost; the 17:11 import copied, all 75 imported memories are still in ~/.claude/projects/C--home-git/memory (none shrank, 10 grew, 7 new since). Rule for the pivot: a memory leaves the client folder only after its eoie feature exists and is installed; class (b) guidance stays in the client memory as is.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_3 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 3u32, "2026-10-22T03:13Z", "eoie", "23:13 classification of all 83 memories (draft in scratch memory-features/classification.md; moves to lab/EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES after release A): six feature families. F1 job supervision (retry-after-memory-kills, no-sleep-polling, monitor-every-long-run, stop-only-own-processes, sweep-leftover-shells, offscreen-muted-gui-runs...): job_outcome Exited|Reaped|TimedOut|Silent, retry_policy, pid_owner OwnedPid|ForeignPid, run_mode Visible|OffscreenMuted. F2 tree safety (cells-reads-live-tree, copy-eoie-src-only, dangling-check-by-stem): TreeRead lease refusing do edit, copy_scope, delete_clearance Cleared|Blocked refs. F3 standards rules indexed by language with an existential rule pack (lib-first, generic, 1k lines all languages, PowerShell glue, actions.lock). F4 toolchain profiles (pin-spiral-cc, kino-mix-erlang-path). F5 decision records (prewritten rules as typed items, release pipeline with a mandatory candidate phase). F6 mirrored repos (alphabet-mirrors-dice). 8 already embodied (read-clock -> note-local-time, ...), 43 stay as guidance, 2 to re-check (one-entry-no-native-wrappers, agent-sync-daily). Order by WSJF: F2 > F1 > F3 > F5 > F4 > F6.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_4 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 4u32, "2026-10-22T03:47Z", "eoie", "23:47 F2 tree lease designed in detail (scratch memory-features/f2-tree-lease.md): tree_use ReadsTree|ShapesTree, a TreeRead window class as the lease store (reuses holder-alive cleanup), tree_claim TreeFree|TreeHeld lease, edit_verdict EditAllowed|EditRefused lease. Releases (test-regeneration -Publish) and job start --reads-tree take the lease; do edit/write/replace/move refuse under it naming the holder; the edit hook checks the same registry; eoie tree copy with copy_scope SourceOnly|WithSpec; do delete takes only a Cleared value from eoie refs <stem>. Would have stopped tonight's mid-release lab edit and the earlier lab probe that cost a release.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_5 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 5u32, "2026-10-22T03:54Z", "eoie", "23:54 F2 started: the tree-lease model is written and green in the scratchpad (f2-lease/eoie_tree_lease/lease.spi, 3 Spiral test blocks pass via Cells): tree_use, tree_lease record, tree_leases list, tree_claim TreeFree|TreeHeld, edit_verdict EditAllowed|EditRefused, claim_of/edit_verdict_of/verdict_text. Lib-first, zero host macros (sm'.to_lower/replace/starts_with/ends_with/format, ++# from sm'_operators). Lessons: Cells finds lib/spiral by searching the notebook's ancestors (deps/polyglot/..., spiral/lib/spiral), so a package outside C:\\home\\git needs packageDir; string concat is ++# with open sm'_operators; a record update needs a bound base. Moves into eoie (lab then src) after release A; then the window-class store and the do-edit refusal.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_6 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 6u32, "2026-10-22T03:59Z", "eoie", "23:59 F2 model upgraded to the typefeatureset and green (3 tests): tree_lease is a GADT indexed by reads|shapes (ReadLease :: terms -> tree_lease reads, ShapeLease -> tree_lease shapes), a forall converter makes runtime rows (StoredRead|StoredShape), so only typed read handles can produce a blocking row. Census: gadt-case 2, forall 1, indexed-union 2, host-macro 0, string-concat 0. An existential registry was tried and dropped: existentials can't cross into runtime lists (Cannot dyn an existential into a runtime var); runtime data stays plain unions.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_7 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 7u32, "2026-10-22T04:00Z", "eoie", "00:00 F2 integration point found: eoie_do_edit/host.spi:32 commits every pending write in one Rust block (temporaries + rename), fed by decode.spi's record_list (Rc<Vec<Vec<Rc<str>>>> split on bytes 30/31: legacy encoded rows). Slice after release C: (1) land eoie_tree_lease (lab -> src, spiral- package), (2) a TreeRead window class in eoie_machine_windows as the lease store (holder-alive cleanup reused), (3) before the commit, claim_of over the stored leases for each pending path; EditRefused stops the whole batch with verdict_text, (4) test-regeneration -Publish takes a TreeRead lease on the repo root for its run. The commit blob itself is migration debt for EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION (encoded rows + a let-block).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_8 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 8u32, "2026-10-22T04:02Z", "eoie", "00:02 F2 store decision: reuse the window registry (a TreeRead class with the existing holder-alive cleanup and run-holder union from r35) instead of a new lease store; the lib's file_system.read_dir_names returns a Rust-only am'.vec of std_string, so a fresh store would mean lib-internal iteration for no gain.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_9 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 9u32, "2026-10-22T04:09Z", "eoie", "00:09 F2 interim protection installed (decided autonomously): a PreToolUse hook on Edit|Write|MultiEdit (~/.claude/hooks/eoie-tree-lease.ps1, settings backup in scratch) blocks an edit under any root named by a live lease file in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\tree-leases (root=, pid=, holder=, since=); a lease whose pid is dead is stale and removed. Release BC's job writes such a lease with its own pid for its whole run (regeneration hashes and notebook tests both read the tree). Tested: inside root blocked (exit 2, case/separators normalized), sibling prefix allowed, dead lease ignored and deleted, no leases allowed. Not covered: writes from shell commands (PowerShell WriteAllText) — the typed path (TreeRead window class + eoie_tree_lease crate + do-edit refusal + test-regeneration taking the lease generically) replaces this interim.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_10 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 10u32, "2026-10-22T04:59Z", "eoie", "00:59 lease hook extended to shell commands (the path of tonight's release-A-killing write): the PreToolUse matcher is now Edit|Write|MultiEdit|PowerShell|Bash; a command that names a leased root and contains a write verb (Set-Content, WriteAllText, Copy-Item, Remove-Item, robocopy, redirect...) is blocked while the lease is live. Tested 8 cases: Edit inside, WriteAllText into root and redirect into root blocked; read-only Get-Content, eoie verbs run from the root, writes elsewhere, -gt comparisons and lease-free sessions pass. Known gap: eoie's own write verbs (do edit) inside the root pass until the typed TreeRead path (release D) refuses them.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_11 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 11u32, "2026-10-22T05:02Z", "eoie", "01:02 lease hook made cheap and proven live: settings now run it as cmd /c if exist %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\tree-leases\\*.lease pwsh ..., so sessions pay pwsh startup only while a lease exists. Release ABC started 00:53 and holds two live leases (its job, pid 6572, and the regeneration -Publish, pid 71396), so both lease writers work. Against those real leases the hook blocks a PowerShell write into C:\\home\\git\\eoie (exit 2) and passes a read (exit 0).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_12 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 12u32, "2026-10-22T05:09Z", "eoie", "01:09 F1 job supervision designed in detail (scratch memory-features/f1-job-supervision.md, to lab after ABC): job_outcome Exited|Reaped|TimedOut|Stopped|Lost from the existing exit lines; liveness Progressing|Silent minutes with job start --silent-limit (busy-but-silent is a hang); retry_policy NoRetry|RetryWhen with job start --retry-reaped n exporting EOIE_RETRY_ATTEMPT; pid_owner OwnedBy|OtherJob|NoJob so ps kill refuses other authors' and job-less processes unless --theirs names the job; job sweep --mine; run_mode Visible|OffscreenMuted. Order: pid_owner > liveness > retry > sweep > run_mode.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_13 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 13u32, "2026-10-22T05:21Z", "eoie", "01:21 F5 decision records designed (scratch memory-features/f5-decision-records.md): decision_rule record (condition, check, tie-break, deadline) added with agile rule add before measuring and kept immutable; check_kind Measured|ThresholdAt|BeforeTime|AllOf; rule_outcome Applied|Waived|Expired via agile decide; agile rules lists open ones and the Stop gate blocks on rules past deadline without an outcome; a typed release_step sequence where Publish needs an Applied Candidate. Case study: tonight's R1-R11 lived as free-text notes and two were applied late (R2 broken by a shell write, R8 after a re-baseline).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_14 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 14u32, "2026-10-22T05:21Z", "eoie", "01:21 F4 and F6 sketched (scratch memory-features/f4-f6.md): F4 profiles as user-owned Spiral data (tool pins, env, PATH prefix), eoie env <profile> -- <cmd>, eoie env check with pin_drift Matches|Shadowed|Missing, job start --profile; F6 mirror pairs as data, eoie mirror check with mirror_state InStep|LeftAhead|RightAhead|Diverged. All six families now have designs; F2 is built (release D), the rest wait for build slots.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_15 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 15u32, "2026-10-22T05:23Z", "eoie", "01:23 F1 first piece built and green (scratch f1-owner/eoie_pid_owner/owner.spi, 2 test blocks via Cells, passed first run): process_row/job_record records, pid_owner OwnedBy|OtherJob|NoJob by walking parents up to a job supervisor (64-hop guard), kill_verdict KillAllowed|KillRefused with --theirs <job> as the only override and refusal texts naming the owner. Lib-first (++#), no host macros. Next F1 piece: liveness Progressing|Silent.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_16 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 16u32, "2026-10-22T05:25Z", "eoie", "01:25 F1 second piece green (scratch f1-owner/eoie_pid_owner/liveness.spi, 2 tests): output_watch from byte-count samples, liveness Progressing|Silent minutes, silence_verdict KeepRunning|StopSilent at a --silent-limit (0 = report only). Design corrected: no new job_outcome union, eoie_jobs already has job_settled (JobFinished|JobTimedOut|JobStopped|JobSpawnFailed|JobSkipped|JobLost|JobMemoryCapped); retry_policy NoRetry|RetryWhen n decides over it (JobLost from a reaped supervisor and JobMemoryCapped retry). Retry and wiring go into eoie_jobs itself, not a parallel type.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_17 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 17u32, "2026-10-22T05:27Z", "eoie", "01:27 F4 core built and green (scratch f1-owner/eoie_pid_owner/pins.spi, 1 test block, 5 cases): tool_pin record, found_paths in PATH order, pin_drift Matches|Shadowed first|Missing decided by the first copy on PATH (case and separators normalized), drift_text naming the shadowing copy (the mingw 16.2 vs scoop gcc 15.2 case from pin-spiral-cc).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_18 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 18u32, "2026-10-22T05:29Z", "eoie", "01:29 F6 core built and green (scratch f1-owner/eoie_pid_owner/mirror.spi, 5 cases): mirrored_file records with both hashes, mirror_state InStep|LeftAhead files|RightAhead files|Diverged, refuses_commit for anything but InStep (alphabet-mirrors-dice). Tonight's scratch models, all lib-first, no host macros, Spiral tests green: F2 lease (built into release D), F1 owner + liveness, F4 pins, F6 mirror; they move to lab/EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES with the D handoff and into real packages per family next.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_19 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 19u32, "2026-10-22T05:32Z", "eoie", "01:32 F5 core built and green (scratch f1-owner/eoie_pid_owner/decision.spi, 2 tests): rule_check AtMost|AtLeast|BeforeTime, decision_rule record with written and deadline times, rule_outcome Applied|Declined|Expired|Premature (a measurement older than the rule is refused; a decision after the deadline expires), outcome_text. Every family now has a green core model in the scratchpad (F1 owner + liveness, F2 lease, F4 pins, F5 decision, F6 mirror); F3 ships as standards rules in D.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_20 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 20u32, "2026-10-22T05:34Z", "eoie", "01:34 release E plan (next session; F1 pid ownership into ps kill): (1) job start records an author file from EOIE_AUTHOR or --as (job records carry no author today, so 'my jobs' cannot be decided); (2) eoie_process_table's stop path (stops_from -> stop_refusal_of Allowed pid) adds an ownership step: process rows from the existing listing host + job records (id, author, supervisor pid from the jobs registry) -> owner_of -> kill_verdict_of, refusing OtherJob and NoJob unless --theirs <job> names it; (3) eoie_pid_owner lands in src (lib-first package, standards.spi declaring HostFree + LibReferenced). Liveness wiring (job status/list print progressing|silent N min; job start --silent-limit) follows in the same release.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_21 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 21u32, "2026-10-22T08:14Z", "eoie", "04:14 F1 integration design, sized before drafting: eoie_process_table/process_table.rs is at 828 lines, so the owner walk (process rows + job records + parent walk, about 100-150 Rust lines) cannot be inlined into ps kill. Shape: the green scratch models (f1-owner/eoie_pid_owner: owner, liveness, pins, decision, mirror) become their own exporting crate eoie_pid_owner with one primitive boundary in the domain's words: eoie_pid_owner_of(pid, author) -> owner word (mine / other:<job id> / none). ps kill: stop_refusal gains OtherJob : string (refusal text names the owning job and its author), checked after OwnProcess/OwnAncestor; a --force flag is NOT added (killing another lane's job goes through that lane). Job records: job start writes an author file from env.try_get_environment_variable EOIE_AUTHOR (main landed it 04:0x; None leaves the file absent and the job counts as unowned, so old jobs stay killable by anyone, as today). Tests: ps_cli kill of a fixture job with another author is refused, own author allowed, unowned allowed. Needs: crate scaffold + owner growth row + compile preview; release F after D and E. Draft models carried in lab/EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN/night-2026-10-22/f1-owner.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_22 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 22u32, "2026-10-22T09:16Z", "eoie", "05:16 integration designs for the green F4/F5/F6 models (scratch f1-owner/eoie_pid_owner, carried in the night folder). F5 decision rules: tonight's R11-R14 were prewritten as free-text notes and applied by hand plus crons; as eoie: agile rule <root> <id> <rule-id> <check> [--by HH:MM] stores a typed decision_rule (check AtMost/AtLeast/BeforeTime, written_ms, deadline_ms) as its own state record (a backlog state schema addition, so it needs the state migration path, not note text), and agile decide <root> <id> <rule-id> <measured> evaluates it with the model's outcome_of: refuses a measurement older than the rule, expires after the deadline, and appends the outcome as a note. F4 pins (memory pin-spiral-cc: SPIRAL_CC must be scoop gcc 15.2 while mingw 16.2 is first on PATH): a pin check reading the first PATH hit per tool against a declared pin, reported by eoie machine watch and refused by the release prelaunch check (eoie release check, EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN note 45). F6 mirror (memory alphabet-mirrors-dice): mirror check <left-root> <right-root> <glob> hashes both sides and reports one-sided changes; the model's refuses_commit makes it a pre-commit refusal. Placement: each as a small exporting crate with a word boundary (owner, pin drift, mirror state), never inlined into near-gate crates (process_table 828, machine_watch 954).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_23 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 23u32, "2026-10-22T09:32Z", "eoie", "05:32 decision rule, written before checking (memory decide-autonomously: no user-decision lists; one-record-per-fact): an untracked file that copies another store's facts as strings is deleted when (1) the user called that shape drift, (2) the source of truth still holds every fact, and (3) a stem grep across eoie (src, scripts, compiler-contracts, spec, standards, lab) and the backlog finds no consumer. Subject: eoie/memory/claude.spi (213 KB, untracked, every Claude memory as a Memory GADT row of 8 strings, written 10-21 17:11 by the memory string-store slice r36, which was held as drift). If any check fails, keep it and record why.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_24 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 24u32, "2026-10-22T09:33Z", "eoie", "05:33 rule applied: all three conditions held, so eoie/memory/claude.spi was deleted (the untracked memory/ folder held only it). (1) the string-store shape was called drift by the user on 10-21; (2) ~/.claude/projects/C--home-git/memory still holds every fact; (3) no consumer: the only code that would read it is the default_store from lab/EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS/apply-memory-default-store.ps1, which never reached src (the r36 string-store slice was held), and the memory verbs take an explicit <data.spi> path. The typed direction stays EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES (F1-F6 models + verbs), not an imported copy.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_25 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 25u32, "2026-10-22T13:36Z", "eoie-lane", "09:36 eoie team, F1 wiring decided (stop-only-own-processes as one call, opt-in so no lane's plain kill changes): job start records the caller's EOIE_AUTHOR (any word; eoie names no lane) in jobs/<id>/author. 'eoie kill --mine <pid>...' allows a pid when it descends from the caller's agent session (the ps --mine scope that already exists) or when owner.spi's job_above finds a job whose author equals the caller's EOIE_AUTHOR. Otherwise it refuses with owner.spi's texts, unless --theirs <job-id> names the owning job. Plain kill is unchanged. With F's job host, supervisors hang under the host and not under the agent session, which is why the author field is needed (the session walk alone would refuse an agent's own detached job trees). Placement: the verdict lives in eoie_pid_owner as an export (process_table.rs is at 828; the owner fold plus job records would push it toward the gate), and process_table calls it once per pid. It lands after E2, with the harness adoption.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_26 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 26u32, "2026-10-22T13:56Z", "eoie-lane", "09:56 eoie team: F1 kill --mine, first half drafted as an anchor-safe slice in release H (apply-f1-ownership.ps1: new module eoie_process_table/ownership.spi; job_owner YourJob | TheirJob | NoOwningJob, mine_verdict, a 6-case test block registered; spiproj gains the module and spiral- when E's heal has not added it). It reuses machine watch's existing owner_walk (supervisor command line 'job supervise <id>') for the job lookup, so the lab owner.spi's parallel ancestry model stays as reference only. The second half (run.spi flags --mine/--theirs, rows sampled once, EOIE_AUTHOR written by job start) anchors on post-heal text and is written once GE2 is installed.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_27 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 27u32, "2026-10-22T15:57Z", "eoie", "2026-10-22 11:50: F2 tree-lease hook gap. A session started before the hook was installed (10-22 01:01) never loads it, so two edits landed under release F's lease. The hook itself blocks correctly (2-3 s, through its cmd wrapper). Typed follow-up: the tree-read window claim (release D) should also be checked by eoie's own write verbs, so the guard does not depend on a session's hook snapshot.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_28 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 28u32, "2026-10-22T17:14Z", "eoie", "2026-10-22 13:14 F6 integration drafted (scratch, synced to lab with the night folder): eoie mirror check <left-root> <right-root> <path>... as its own lib-only crate eoie_mirror (HostFree). The model is retyped from the 01:29 core: file_side Missing | Present (content, last_write_ms) replaces the empty-hash convention, which made an empty file look missing, plus file_drift SameOnBoth | LeftOnly | RightOnly | LeftNewer | RightNewer and mirror_state over a core list. Content equality replaces hashing (the lib crypto path's sha2 version is unproven in eoie). Directories are walked with an explicit worklist in sorted order. Exits 0 in step and 1 otherwise; model and CLI notebooks are registered. Dry run on the post-I tree is clean. Waits on lib file_system.last_write_ms (offered to main as apply-lib-last-write.ps1) and console.write_error_line (main's second landing).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_29 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 29u32, "2026-10-22T17:20Z", "eoie", "2026-10-22 13:20 F4 integration drafted: eoie env check <tool>=<pinned-path>... as a lib-only crate eoie_env_pins (HostFree). PATH comes from env.get_environment_variable split by platform.is_windows, and each tool is looked up as tool + platform.get_executable_suffix. pin_drift is Matches | Shadowed first | Elsewhere first | Missing; Elsewhere is new and separates 'only other copies' from 'none'. Exits 1 on drift. Model and CLI notebooks set the child's PATH over fixture tool stubs. Dry run clean in either order with F6's mirror slice. Waits only on lib console.write_error_line.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_30 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 30u32, "2026-10-22T18:28Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:28 x: candidate feature from memory ci-parity-toolchains (written today). Verb: 'eoie ci parity <repo>' reads the workflow's setup-* pins (setup-zig version, setup-beam otp/elixir/gleam, setup-rust channel or rust-toolchain.toml, setup-python). It compares them with what the local test harness resolves: the harness's own env override (SPIRAL_ZIG, SPIRAL_RUSTC, ...), else PATH through scoop shims (read the .shim target), and rustup's toolchain for the directory the harness runs in. It prints one row per tool (ci, local, match) and exits 1 on a mismatch, with the env line that would make a run match CI. Typed: a tool union (Zig | Rust | Otp | Elixir | Gleam | Python), each with its pin reader and local resolver. Evidence: today all local Cells runs used zig-dev 0.18 and rustc 1.88-nightly, while x CI pins 0.17.0 and nightly-2026-10-18 (X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED notes 32-33). Generic, with no repo names in the code: any repo whose workflow uses those actions.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_31 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 31u32, "2026-10-22T20:51Z", "eoie-lane", "16:51 F5 pure model drafted and tested (scratch f5/src/agile_decision_model; model only, like eoie_tree_lease, with standards.spi declaring HostFree and LibReferenced). rule_check AtLeast | AtMost | Before | Recorded is checked against measurement Number | Clock | Text: a kind mismatch is Unmeasurable, never a silent pass. The GADT typestate decision undecided -> decided (OpenRule/ClosedRule; decide and waive take only an undecided rule) stops a rule being decided twice, and stored_decision is the runtime row. rule_outcome is Applied (verdict, measurement) | Waived reason | Expired (decided after its deadline). overdue_rules is the fold the Stop gate needs. Its 3 test blocks use today's real rules: the sentinel-probe rule (10 against at least 14 fails; 14 holds) and 'no release launch after 19:00' (a late decision expires; a waived rule is not overdue). Cells notebook check: 3/3 pass on the first compile and again after re-laying lines over 120; eoie standards: 0 findings; census ratchet: no new-package problem. Next, a compile-release item: agile rule add/decide/rules over these rows, plus the Stop-gate reading.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_32 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 32u32, "2026-10-22T21:06Z", "eoie-lane", "17:06 F5 CLI placement, read from the code: an agile verb is a union case plus its spec record in agile_subcommand_domain/subcommand.spi (reflection-driven names and usage) and two arms in eoie_agile_mutation/agile_mutation.spi (the mutates table and the dispatch to a crate export). agile_mutation.rs is at 981 lines, so three verbs (rule add, decide, rules) do not fit as three cases. Plan: one family case AgileRule ('rule', shape 'add <root> <item> <id> --when <text> --check at-least|at-most|before|recorded <value> [--by HH:MM] | decide <root> <item> <id> --measured <value> | waive ... | list <root> [--overdue]'), which costs about 2 dispatch lines, routed to one export of a new crate agile_decision_rules. That crate opens agile_decision_model and parses its own sub-verbs, so agile_mutation never grows per sub-verb. Storage: typed rows in <root>/state/rules.spi (a Spiral data module like standards/census.spi: one 'inl rule_<id> () = DecisionRule (...)' per rule, outcome rows appended, never edited), so the Stop gate reads overdue rules with one 'eoie agile rule list <root> --overdue' call. Ordering: after GE2 and H (both touch agile crates), as its own release with candidate notebooks for the rule CLI.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_33 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 33u32, "2026-10-23T00:37Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:37 x: correction to note 30, plus a measured expected table for the 'ci parity' verb. The Rust half of note 30 is WRONG and would bake today's 15:18 failure into the tool. A plain 'rustc --version' shows rustup's default toolchain, but a harness may pass its own +toolchain to the rustup proxy (Cells does: rustc_args adds +nightly-2026-10-18 when rustc's path contains /.cargo/bin/ or /rustup/). So the local resolver must model the harness's argument building, not the tool's --version. Never suggest an env override that swaps the proxy for a direct rustc: the harness then passes '+nightly...' as a file name ('multiple input filenames provided'). Expected rows for the x repo on this box, 10-22 20:1x (ci / local / verdict): zig 0.17.0 / 0.18-dev from the PATH shim / MISMATCH, fixed by SPIRAL_ZIG=<scoop zig 0.17.0>; rust nightly-2026-10-18 (setup-rust x2 + rust-toolchain.toml) / the same through the proxy's +arg / match; otp 29 / 29.1.2 / match; elixir 1.20 / 1.20.4 / match; gleam 1.19.2 / 1.19.2 / match; python 3.12.10 / 3.15.0 on PATH, 3.12.10 under scoop python312 / MISMATCH, which matters only where python runs (x: the e2e stub; proof = run it on 3.12.10). Version matching is prefix-wise: a CI '29' matches a local '29.1.2'.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_34 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 34u32, "2026-10-23T15:58Z", "eoie", "11:58 eoie: re note 33 (x): the eoie ci parity draft now follows it: Rust is resolved the way a harness sees it (rust-toolchain file channel if installed, else rustup default; never 'swap the proxy' advice), Python is a tool, matching is prefix-wise at a dot (no dev build stands in), and mismatches print a fix line (SPIRAL_ZIG=<scoop zig 0.17.0> for x's zig row). Details on EOIE-CI-PARITY; ships in release M.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_35 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 35u32, "2026-10-23T18:11Z", "eoie", "14:11 eoie: today's memory lessons mined for features. (1) search-backlog-before-new (5 slips on 10-23: a mirror verb already in release I, a backfill rule already settled, do raw payloads already in the usage, plus 2 earlier) -> EOIE-AGILE-FIND (drafted) should also search the verb usage text and the scratch slice scripts, not only titles and notes: 'agile find <root> <words> --usage --slices <dir>', so one call answers 'is this already built, drafted or decided?'. (2) check-scripts-by-data-flow, extended today with 'a positive control must fail for the reason under test, through the bug's own path' -> the experiment ledger (EOIE-EXPERIMENTS) gains a typed control arm: a rule is not registrable until a control run on the old binary or fixture has failed with the expected verdict. (3) job run vs job start (agent-sync trick) -> eoie job doctor --env (drafted, release M) runs the detached path so nobody needs to remember the trick.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_36 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 36u32, "2026-10-23T21:36Z", "eoie", "17:36 eoie: mined today's new memory recompile-drafts-after-compiler-change into a typed feature: a release preview records the compiler hash it compiled with, and release launch refuses when the installed compiler differs (union preview_state = PreviewCurrent of hash | PreviewStale of old * new | NoPreview; the launch decision is total over it). Specified on EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN note 124, from today's case: a codegen regression made H's stamp_in a Rust E0507 hours after its last preview. Memory to eoie link: recompile-drafts-after-compiler-change -> EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN (EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_37 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 37u32, "2026-10-24T00:02Z", "eoie", "20:02 eoie: mined memory window-coordination ('read queue + announced blocks before heavy work') into EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE: announced exclusive blocks become typed rows in the window store (class, from, until, latest start), read by admission, release yield and wait-clear, instead of living only in peer messages. Evidence: tonight's 18:02 manual yield to tibia's block.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_38 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 38u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): JOB-4 (F1 retry-reaped with lower parallelism), TOOL-1 (F4 env profiles), EVID-7 (F6 mirror check). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_39 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 39u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 720m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D7 at 40%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_features_40 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES", 40u32, "2026-10-25T00:44Z", "eoie", "20:44 Lab drain: lab/EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES/classification.md moved into this note verbatim (the file is deleted; this note is the record):\n# EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES: classification of the 83 memories (2026-10-21 23:2x)\n\n(a) = eoie can enforce or do it, built on the type system; (b) = guidance that stays in the client memory; (c) = candidate obsolete, re-check before touching.\n\n## (a) Feature families\n\n### F1 Job supervision (eoie job / ps)\nMemories: retry-after-memory-kills, no-sleep-polling, parallel-while-waiting, monitor-every-long-run, sweep-leftover-shells, stop-only-own-processes, offscreen-muted-gui-runs, cmd-cwd-search-off, heavy-gate-lessons.\nTyped sketch:\n- `union job_outcome = Exited : i32 | Reaped | TimedOut | Silent : u32` (Silent = no output for N minutes: busy-but-silent is a hang).\n- `union retry_policy = NoRetry | RetryReaped : u8 * parallelism_step` drives `job start --retry-reaped`.\n- `union pid_owner = OwnedPid : job_id | ForeignPid` so `ps kill` refuses foreign PIDs by type.\n- `union run_mode = Visible | OffscreenMuted` for GUI clients (separate desktop, audio off).\n- `job sweep --mine` lists and stops leftovers owned by the caller's session.\n- Already embodied: windows, heavy gate, env_remove of NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath.\n\n### F2 Tree safety (do edit / copy / delete)\nMemories: cells-reads-live-tree, copy-eoie-src-only, dangling-check-by-stem, user-stages-mid-run, live-runs-alone.\nTyped sketch:\n- a `TreeRead` lease class held by test runs and releases; `do edit` returns `EditRefused (TreeLeased holder)` while it is held.\n- `copy_scope = SourceOnly | WithSpec` (never .cache/target) for scratch copies.\n- `delete_clearance = Cleared | Blocked : refs` from a cross-repo stem search; `do delete` takes only a Cleared value.\n- staged files reported as `StagedByUser` in `do git` output, never touched.\n\n### F3 Standards rules (eoie standards / edit hook)\nMemories: no-comments-self-explanatory, lib-first-all-projects, eoie-generic-no-external, eoie-granular-spiral, line-gate-all-languages, spiral-macro-gotchas (PowerShell glued statements, here-string traps), actions-lock-on-workflow-edits, code-era-quality, no-readme-for-comments.\nTyped sketch: `rule lang` indexed by language (Spiral, Rust, Gleam, PowerShell, Workflow) with an existential `rule_pack = exists lang. rule lang * lang_files lang`; findings carry the lib function to use. Feeds EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET and EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK.\n\n### F4 Toolchain profiles (eoie env)\nMemories: pin-spiral-cc, kino-mix-erlang-path, latest-of-everything (version check part).\nTyped sketch: `profile` record (compiler paths, PATH prefix, env overrides) named per task (matrix, kino); `eoie env <profile> -- <cmd>`; a profile check reports drift (mingw 16.2 first on PATH, choco OTP 26 first).\n\n### F5 Decision records (eoie agile)\nMemories: decide-by-prewritten-rules, decide-autonomously, release-candidate-cli-run, autonomous-stop-gate, backlog-always-has-cpu-light.\nTyped sketch: `decision_rule = { condition; check; tie_break; deadline }` stored on an item before measuring; `agile decide <id>` records the measured outcome against the rule; a release pipeline type with a mandatory candidate phase.\n\n### F6 Mirrored repos\nMemory: alphabet-mirrors-dice. Typed sketch: declared mirror pairs (data, generic) + `eoie mirror check <a> <b>` diffing plumbing files; refuses to report clean when one side changed alone.\n\n## Already embodied (memory can shrink to a pointer after the next install)\nread-clock-before-timestamps (note-local-time, r34), eoie-memory-symbiosis (agile links), scheduling-discipline (agile next roles), generic-tools-not-per-experiment (table/experiment verbs), eoie-one-call-tdd (spec/one-call.md + runner), agile-team-term (agile vocabulary), lab-in-repo (EOIE-LAB-AUDIT planned), one-record-per-fact (experiment import, partly).\n\n## (b) Guidance that stays in the client memory\nno-polls-during-runs, autonomous-e2e-fixing, experiment-until-sota, decisions-are-not-go, inner-jev-autonomy, decision-rounds-one-at-a-time, max-three-agents, classic-agents-visible, main-thread-works, use-full-timebox, trace-drift-before-pivoting, tokens-abundant-cpu-scarce, backlog-review-depth, pack-queued-builds, standardize-with-siblings, no-public-project-interaction, slide-with-spiral-main, fc1943s-dump-i574n-destructive, workspace-folder-stays, bold-native-goals, spiral-direction-2026-10, backend-targets-2026-10-07, core-migration-direction, priority-order, gpt5parallel-hopacsota, self-checking-samples-direction, regression-samples-allowed, accept-autonomously, lib-smoke-before-syntax, kino-copy-lib-resolution, formatter-keep-breaks, phantom-data-in-fsharp, x-compat-naming, x-api-credentials (secrets pointer: never into eoie), tibia-dogfood-project, tibia-other-session, lane-roles, agent-sync-daily, no-wsl-until-workflow-passes, backlog-in-eoie, eoie-first, eoie-embodied-memory, user-stages-mid-run (rule part).\n\n## (c) Candidates to re-check\none-entry-no-native-wrappers (F#/Fable entry may be moot under bold-native-goals' no-Fable direction), agent-sync-daily (agent-sync.md still read?).\n\n## Order (WSJF: weight = how often the rule is broken or costs a run, size = typed surface)\nF2 tree lease (broke releases twice) > F1 outcome/ownership (rustc kill hit main, reaped jobs) > F3 rules (feeds the hook) > F5 decision records > F4 profiles > F6 mirrors.")
inl tree_pages_v2 () = Task ("TREE-PAGES-V2", "Tree pages v2 (from the 10-21 review, TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO note 24): publish exactly the tracked files (git checkout-index) instead of the rsync allowlist that leaks build outputs on Linux and drops whole languages on Windows; a separate pages job per repo, independent of tests; deploy via actions/deploy-pages (no gh-pages history) or force_orphan; a pinned renderer release instead of building dir-tree-html main in every CI; the post-deploy check landed from lab; dir_tree_html.rs split under 1,000 lines", Story, P1, D4, 60u32, Active, "", "ci,pages,dir-tree-html,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tree_pages_v2 () = Planning ("TREE-PAGES-V2", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tree_pages_v2_1 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-V2", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:38Z", "x", "2026-10-21 22:38 x: filed from the review; measurements are in TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO note 24. Order by value: (a) checkout-index publish in spiral/scripts/publish-tree.ps1 (one script; every repo calls it), (b) the separate pages job, (c) deploy-pages or force_orphan, (d) renderer release + pin, (e) post-deploy check, (f) the crate split. (a) alone fixes the content of all 10 pages on their next deploy.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_v2_2 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-V2", 2u32, "2026-10-22T03:59Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:59 x: step (a) drafted and tested, not applied (spiral/scripts is main's). Draft: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--home-git\\b7595ac3-963d-4714-be58-0267aa270947\\scratchpad\\publish-tree-v2.ps1, a drop-in for spiral/scripts/publish-tree.ps1 with the same parameters. git checkout-index -a -f --prefix=dist/ exports the index; -Exclude patterns now drop any tracked path with a matching segment (eoie's '.cache' keeps working); -Include patterns now add untracked files with a matching name (polyglot '*.png', tibia '*.tsv','*.txt','*.lua','*.xml' keep their meaning); symlinks are dropped; dotfile renaming and the renderer-failure guard are unchanged; rsync, the 41-pattern allowlist, CYGWIN=noglob and the default exclude list are gone. Piping paths into checkout-index --stdin fails on Windows (pwsh appends CR: 'is not in the cache'), hence -a plus removal of excluded paths. Results with the local renderer: x 132 tracked files -> dist (0 .beam where the live page has 351 build outputs; all 19 .ex, which the allowlist drops on Windows), 4 s; spiral 10,234 files (534 .c, 467 .zig), 73 s on Windows, index.html 1,563 KB; a probe repo confirmed Exclude drops skipme/s.spi, Include adds an untracked b.tsv, an untracked c.beam stays out, and .gitattributes becomes _gitattributes. Note: the spiral test deleted a pre-existing, gitignored spiral/dist (an earlier local publish output; publish-tree itself wipes dist on every run).")
inl note_of_tree_pages_v2_3 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-V2", 3u32, "2026-10-22T04:01Z", "main", "00:01 main landed step (a) in spiral/scripts/publish-tree.ps1 (uncommitted for the user): x's git checkout-index draft with two mechanical fixes (the literal NUL byte in the exclusion split became the backtick-0 escape so git diffs it as text; CRLF to LF); token sequence identical to the draft x tested (424 tokens), so x's results stand (x 132 files in 4 s, spiral 10,234 in 73 s, Include/Exclude semantics on a probe repo).")
inl note_of_tree_pages_v2_4 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-V2", 4u32, "2026-10-22T04:02Z", "x", "2026-10-22 00:02 x: main landed step (a) as spiral/scripts/publish-tree.ps1 (uncommitted; NUL literal fixed to the backtick-0 escape, LF line endings). Rechecked the landed file on x: same result as the draft (132 tracked files + index.html, 0 .beam, 19 .ex). Live pages change on each repo's next deploy after the user commits spiral.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_v2_5 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-V2", 5u32, "2026-10-22T06:29Z", "x", "2026-10-22 02:29 x: x's .github/workflows/ci.yml push job gets main's deploy check, copied verbatim from spiral's gh-pages.yml: echo GITHUB_SHA > gh-pages/_deploy.txt before peaceiris, then a pwsh step that GETs https://i574n.github.io/x/_deploy.txt (cache-busting query) every 15 s for up to 6 min until it equals GITHUB_SHA, then requires the README section and viewport meta in the index. No new uses: (scripts/actions-lock.ps1: 8 actions locked, passes); YAML parses; today's live x page already carries both marks. Uncommitted, for the user.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_v2_6 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-V2", 6u32, "2026-10-22T10:00Z", "main", "2026-10-22 06:00 main: deciding (b), (c) and (d) myself (memory decide-autonomously), by one rule: pick the option that needs no repository-settings change and no new action dependency, with a tie going to the smaller workflow diff. (c) force_orphan: true on the existing peaceiris step. The alternative, actions/deploy-pages, needs each repo's Pages source switched to GitHub Actions in its settings, plus a new 'uses:' and actions.lock regeneration in every repo. Checked: all 10 tree-pages workflows (spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet, x/ci, eoie, tibia, blog, i574n.github, dir-tree-html) pass only github_token and publish_dir (no keep_files, no destination_dir). peaceiris already replaces the whole page content, so force_orphan only stops the gh-pages branch from accumulating history. (d) pin the renderer by commit SHA in the checkout step, not by a release, since cutting a release is a push and git actions belong to the user; it is bumped deliberately. (b) the separate pages job stays as it is, a push job that needs build. Pages still deploy only after the build, which keeps a broken tree off the page. Revisit only if a red build ever blocks a needed page.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_v2_7 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-V2", 7u32, "2026-10-22T10:01Z", "main", "2026-10-22 06:01 main: (c) APPLIED: force_orphan: true added after publish_dir in all 10 tree-pages workflows (spiral, polyglot (4-space YAML, indent 18), dice, alphabet, x/ci, eoie (no tree lease live), tibia, blog, i574n.github, dir-tree-html), one match per file. pyyaml check: each push job's peaceiris step now has force_orphan True. Uncommitted, for the user. (d) DEFERRED by a mechanical condition: the renderer is cloned at main only in CI (spiral/scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1 builds when no exe exists), and dir-tree-html has staged, uncommitted renderer changes while origin/main is c1363bb (10-19). publish-tree v2 was tested against a local renderer that may include them, so a pin now could break the next deploy. Resume condition: when 'git -C dir-tree-html status' is clean and main equals origin/main, add 'git -C \\ checkout --quiet <that sha>' right after the clone in dir-tree-html.ps1, then bump the SHA with every renderer commit.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_v2_8 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-V2", 8u32, "2026-10-22T11:40Z", "x", "2026-10-22 07:40 x: step (f) can close without a compiler change. x's scripts/rust-modules.ps1 (column-0 item parser; private fns go to <stem>_partN.rs with use super::*; types, pub fns and fn main stay in the root) splits dir-tree-html's generated dir_tree_html.rs, 2,774 lines, into a 33-line root plus parts of 950, 727, 379 and 701 lines. Every non-blank line is kept exactly once. Tested on %TEMP% copies, nothing in the repo touched: cargo check 0 errors 0 warnings; the dev builds of the split and unsplit copies both pass --self-test and render byte-identical HTML for x/scripts (4 KB) and x's 132 tracked files (19.6 KB). To apply: one line after BuildSpiral in dir-tree-html/build.ps1, pwsh -File <x>/scripts/rust-modules.ps1 -Source dir_tree_html.rs (or copy the script into spiral/scripts, since dir-tree-html has spiral, not x, as a dep). dir-tree-html and spiral/scripts are main's; x leaves the call to main.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_v2_9 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-V2", 9u32, "2026-10-23T12:35Z", "main", "2026-10-23 08:35 main: (d) still deferred by its own condition: dir-tree-html has staged gh-pages.yml and dir_tree_html.spi (HEAD = origin/main c1363bb), so the renderer pin waits for the user's commit. (f) decided: no per-repo post-processing script. x's scripts/rust-modules.ps1 would be a second copy of a method that GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES (P1, compiler: one entry's output written as several files under 1,000 lines) puts in the compiler, where every Spiral repo gets it (rule generic-tools-not-per-experiment). dir-tree-html's 2,774-line dir_tree_html.rs is listed there as a consumer, and this item's (f) closes when that lands.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_v2_10 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-V2", 10u32, "2026-10-25T09:58Z", "main", "10-25 05:58 main: (d) APPLIED by its resume condition (dir-tree-html clean, HEAD = origin/main = a643950e, 10-25 01:52). spiral/scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1 checks out $rendererCommit = 'a643950e0628bc57005c94a9db83edc05a4c66ea' right after the CI clone, with a throw on a failed checkout; it parses with 0 errors. Bump that SHA with each renderer commit. A local checkout that already exists is left as is. (f) re-decided under the 10-24 rules: no compiler or script splitting (5j removed the output splits; memory hand-modeled-build-units). dir_tree_html.rs (2,774 lines) meets the 1k gate by splitting dir_tree_html.spi by hand into units with exports, so (f) is a dir-tree-html authoring task, not GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES.")
inl note_of_tree_pages_v2_11 () = Note ("TREE-PAGES-V2", 11u32, "2026-10-25T17:03Z", "main", "10-25 13:03 main: (f) first unit drafted on x's hand-unit method (X-HAND-UNITS: crates split by hand, linked through !!!!Export with typed host calls by name). %TEMP%\\dir-tree-units\\split.ps1 -Root <repo> [-Apply] cuts dir_tree_html.spi (739 lines) into: shared.spi (the ++ operator, find_nl), markdown/markdown.spi (the markdown renderer, 314 lines; main exports dtl_markdown : string -> string; crate dtl_markdown) and the root (428 lines; readme_html calls dtl_markdown::dtl_markdown through one typed host call). It also adds the Cargo workspace member and dependency, the modules entries, and a BuildSpiral line. Rule, written before the run: GREEN iff the unsplit (reference) and split scratch copies both build and pass --self-test (which renders a README through the new crate boundary), every generated .rs is at most 1,000 lines, and the split binary writes byte-identical HTML to the reference for spiral/scripts, x/scripts and the dir-tree-html checkout. If a unit is still over 1,000, cut it next (inline renderer vs blocks) by the same method. Not in this cut, recorded for after: dir-tree-html builds against the compiler's samples core (packageDir deps/spiral/apps/compiler/tmp/samples), against lib-first, and that path breaks at COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE step 1; host_listing is a multi-statement Rust blob (GRANULAR-HEAL scope).")
inl eoie_lib_reuse () = Task ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", "Rework eoie onto lib/spiral and granular Spiral (user 2026-10-21 22:4x: 'gotta rework everything'; 'we have the damn sm in spiral lib, eoie should be reusing lib as much as possible instead of duplicating code everywhere')", Epic, P1, D8, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK,SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY,EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET,EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK,EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE,EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY,EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION", "eoie,style,lib,epic", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_lib_reuse () = Planning ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", Unestimated, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_1 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:41Z", "eoie", "22:41 baseline: eoie 47.4 KLOC Spiral, 29.6 host macros/KLOC, 12.1 Rc::<str>::from/KLOC, 3.3 $'{ let blocks/KLOC; tibia 0/0/0; x 16.3 macros confined to boundary modules. 0 eoie packages reference lib/spiral; x, polyglot, dice, alphabet and Spiral samples all do (packageDir + spiral-). Plan: (1) wire lib/spiral into eoie's build (workspace.ps1 makes a git-ignored src/spiral junction to the Spiral checkout's lib/spiral, the same shape the Cells mount uses) and into Cells runs; (2) a gate: no $' outside one eoie OS boundary package, no $'{ blocks, no Rc::<str>::from outside it, failing the build (EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET becomes a hard check); (3) shrink rust_std_string into calls of sm'/am' and then delete it; missing generic primitives go into lib/spiral, never into eoie; (4) migrate crate by crate after the r30-r36 train lands (pending slices anchor on today's text; migrating first breaks every anchor), each crate done when it has no macros outside the boundary and its tests pass. Pilot on agile_task_query started 22:38 (sm'.to_lower/to_upper/index_of/trim/split/starts_with + am'.any replacing three macros).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_2 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 2u32, "2026-10-22T02:43Z", "eoie", "22:43 pilot PASSES under Cells (query.spi unit tests 4/4): agile_task_query with spiral- as a plain sibling package (no packageDir: Cells resolves packageDir from its cells-mounts junction, so ../../../spiral/lib breaks; the mount already links spiral next to the package) and sm'.to_lower/to_upper/index_of/replace/starts_with replacing three host macros (6 -> 3 left: query_token, expect, i32 text). am'.any fails in rustc (SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY), worked around with sm'.index_of. Not left live: eoie's own build has no src/spiral, so the r30-r33 relaunch would break; live files restored, pilot saved in lab/EOIE-LIB-REUSE (apply-lib-pilot.ps1 refuses until src/spiral exists). Next: workspace.ps1 creates a git-ignored src/spiral junction to <bundle>/../../../lib/spiral (+ /src/spiral in .gitignore, census/harvest scans skip it), apply the pilot, test-regeneration -Filter agile_task_query -CargoCheck, check the generated .rs against the 1000-line gate.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_3 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 3u32, "2026-10-22T05:12Z", "main", "01:12 main: am'.any is portable now in tree lib/spiral/am'.spi, so it works on any backend that has array length and index_base. Test block for rust, fsharp and typescript; lib gate 11/11, 0 type errors (job main-am-any-gate). Next: sm'.listed and sm'.trim_end_suffix (sm'.contains already exists), gated as main-helpers-gate.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_4 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 4u32, "2026-10-22T05:26Z", "main", "01:26 main: sm' helpers gate, same harness fault (copy folder not named spiral); rerunning. Rule: on the same run as the tree baseline, every target passing on the tree passes on the copy, and listed_compares_trimmed_parts and trim_end_suffix_removes_every_trailing_copy pass on rust, fsharp and gleam. Then both land in tree lib/spiral/sm'.spi.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_5 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 5u32, "2026-10-22T05:39Z", "main", "01:39 main: eoie's 4th lib gap is answered. env.get_environment_variable (env.spi:53) is already a runtime read on 7 backends (Rust std::env::var). What's added is env.try_get_environment_variable var : option string, a portable wrapper (None when unset or empty), test try_get_environment_variable_is_none_when_unset on gleam, lua, fsharp, rust and typescript. Job main-env-gate runs after main-sm-gates; rule as for the sm' helpers (copy passes everything the tree passes, plus the new test). Landing in tree lib/spiral waits for eoie's 'release done' (ABC reads lib/spiral).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_6 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 6u32, "2026-10-22T05:46Z", "main", "01:46 main: second invalid run, now understood. Cells finds the lib and its real prelude (testing, rust_type) by looking for lib/spiral and similar paths under an ancestor of the file (notebook.ex @lib_rels), so a copy at <copy>\\spiral loses the prelude and every test fails with main_real.spir 'open testing' unbound. The tree baselines from the same runs pass: sm'.spi 43/43, env.spi 2/2. Copies are now <copy>\\lib\\spiral. lib_gate.ps1 throws on any other -Lib shape. Reruns: main-sm-gates2 (concat, helpers), then main-env-gate2.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_7 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 7u32, "2026-10-22T06:06Z", "main", "02:06 main: gates PASS on correct copies (job main-sm-gates2): balanced concat 43/43, 0 type errors, the same as the tree baseline; sm' helpers 45/45 (the tree's 43 plus listed_compares_trimmed_parts and trim_end_suffix_removes_every_trailing_copy), 0 type errors. Combined into the helpers copy (sm' E812C7BE301E6EC1). Slip: this was applied while main-env-gate2 was already running on that copy, so that env gate saw either sm' version. Both passed alone and env.spi uses neither change. Final check: main-sm-combined gates the combined sm'.spi after it. Landing into tree lib/spiral waits for eoie's 'release done'.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_8 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 8u32, "2026-10-22T06:11Z", "main", "02:11 main: env.try_get_environment_variable PASSES (job main-env-gate2 on the helpers copy): env.spi 3/3, 0 type errors (the tree's 2 plus try_get_environment_variable_is_none_when_unset on gleam, lua, fsharp, rust and typescript). It lands in tree lib/spiral with the sm' helpers after eoie's 'release done' and the combined sm' gate (main-sm-combined).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_9 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 9u32, "2026-10-22T06:18Z", "eoie", "02:18 measured progress (live tree -> scratch tree rE after releases ABC + D + E apply): packages on lib/spiral 1 -> 70 (of 374), on rust_std_string 116 -> 51, declaring HostFree 0 -> 22, concat5 uses 1165 -> 717. E carries the 67 healed crates and still needs its compile preview and the 1000-line gate check before it ships next session.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_10 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 10u32, "2026-10-22T06:18Z", "main", "02:18 main: the combined gate PASSES (job main-sm-combined, helpers copy with balanced concat + listed + trim_end_suffix + env.try_get_environment_variable): sm'.spi 45/45, env.spi 3/3, 0 type errors. Only sm'.spi (+50/-7) and env.spi (+17) differ from the tree, and the tree's sm'.spi still equals the gated base (F7D95B8A8410F714). Landing is %TEMP%\\land-lib.ps1, which refuses if either tree file changed since. It runs on eoie's 'release done' (ABC reads lib/spiral).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_11 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 11u32, "2026-10-22T06:43Z", "eoie", "02:43 heal-crate bug caught by an audit before any release: when a file's only open sm'_operators sat inside its 'test X static' block, the tool deleted the top-level open rust_std_string.model and its presence check matched the indented open, leaving 15 files using ++# with no top-level operator open (would have failed E's regen). Fix: the top-level open is replaced in place by open sm'_operators and dropped only when nothing uses ++#; the check is anchored at column 0. The E preview was rebuilt from the D tree in 20 s (apply-rE.ps1 now takes -Src): 0 files lack the open; the only other diffs are two block-string files the old preview had mangled. New hand-healed slice apply-heal-do-verbs.ps1: eoie_do_model gains query_verb_word/query_verb_named (reflection over the existing query_verb union); do read's verb if-chain, its two option pipe tables and its option record-update chain become query_option/query_filler unions with query_option_applied; do write reuses query_verb_named instead of re-listing the verbs. 74 crates healed in E. Compile check waits for a quiet machine (ABC release build running).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_12 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 12u32, "2026-10-22T07:03Z", "eoie", "03:03 E preview: two more heal-crate bugs fixed before any release: (1) a call whose only newlines sit inside a triple-quoted block was laid out multi-line with indent taken from inside the block (++# at column 0, ParserErrors in agile_note_model and agile_planning_model); layout now masks block literals. (2) eoie_agile_mutation reached 1004 .rs lines after the ++# heal (gate 1000): per the prewritten rule its heal-crate step is dropped from E, its planning-field pipe table still becomes a reflection over agile_planning_model.planning_field. Root cause sent to main: each ++# lowers to its own Rc::from(format!) binding on Rust, concat5 was one; fix belongs in codegen (inline single-use macro bindings) or a lib n-ary concat. New slice apply-heal-agile-words.ps1: agile list's status filter holds an agile_status, agile_status_open lives in agile_mutation_policy, ranking's open check reflects over it. Duplicate found: eoie_agile_mutation declares its own validation_result next to workspace_primitives.outcome's.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_13 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 13u32, "2026-10-22T08:01Z", "main", "04:01 main: LANDED in tree lib/spiral (uncommitted for the user): sm'.listed, sm'.trim_end_suffix, env.try_get_environment_variable, the balanced sm'.concat_list, and the new process module. Gates: sm'.spi 45/45 combined, env.spi 3/3, process.spi 5/5.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_14 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 14u32, "2026-10-22T08:06Z", "eoie", "04:06 main landed sm'.listed, sm'.trim_end_suffix, env.try_get_environment_variable in tree lib (04:0x). heal-crate.ps1 now maps trim_end_matches -> sm'.trim_end_suffix (same semantics: every trailing copy, empty suffix is a no-op) and pipe_member -> sm'.listed only over a whitespace-free string literal list (listed trims parts, pipe_member compares exactly; computed lists are refused for review). Hand slice apply-heal-comma-lists.ps1: table diff keys and planning tally deps become sm'.listed with a comma separator (trimmed comma lists, the behavior agile next's listed_in already implements by hand; listed_in itself stays because agile_task_next is at 993 lines). E rebuilt from the D tree: concat5 1165 -> 488 uses, packages on rust_std_string 116 -> 39, on lib 1 -> 82, pipe tables 22 -> 4 (command_spec_domain's positional route lists, hand-only), 4 near-gate crates skipped. Compile preview of E waits for the next session (no compiles from 04:00 through tibia's live block).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_15 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 15u32, "2026-10-22T08:09Z", "eoie", "04:09 preview caveat: the scratch D tree holds stale .rs for crates ABC regenerated or created (eoie_machine_watch watch.rs empty there, 954 live; eoie_do_write 683 vs 785; eoie_table_query 304 vs 534; eoie_read_query 939 vs 632), and heal-crate's 950 guard plus gate measurements read those files. No healed crate crosses 950 between the two, but eoie_do_write's healed size projects to ~963 from the live base. Next session builds E's preview from a copy of the live tree after the ABC tail installs. eoie_machine_watch stays on concat5 (954 live).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_16 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 16u32, "2026-10-22T08:10Z", "eoie", "04:10 one of the 4 remaining pipe tables: agile_subcommand_domain/subcommand.spi agile_subcommand_shape joins every spec name and shape into two strings and pipe_lookups by position, duplicating agile_subcommand_of right above it. Typed heal: match agile_subcommand_of input with AgileKnown subcommand -> (agile_subcommand_spec subcommand).shape, AgileUnknownName -> empty (and agile_subcommand_of's equals becomes =). Held: the package compiles into its consumers (agile_task_query, eoie_agile_mutation) and a 25-arm match costs more Rust than one pipe_lookup, with eoie_agile_mutation at 981 lines; do it with the near-gate set after main's codegen pack. The other 3 are command_spec_domain's positional route lists (hand-only, see spiral-macro-gotchas).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_17 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 17u32, "2026-10-22T08:26Z", "eoie", "04:26 census of the 39 packages still on rust_std_string in a composed copy (live + D + E, real .rs sizes): 21 heal now with tonight's mappings (wave E2, apply-rE2-wave.ps1, its own preview after E's); 12 near-gate skips (950-1000: job_gate 1000, memory 994, do_program 988, todo_lines 986, agile_mutation 981, task_listing 979, task_next 978, do_edit 976, memory_query 975, experiment_analysis 965, command_spec_domain 962, machine_watch 954); 3 unmapped names (agile_subcommand_domain pipe_lookup, authority_state_domain, eoie_experiment_stats); 2 computed lists (agile_memory_links, agile_todo_model). Rejected idea, measured: a static growth predictor (extra lines = ++# operators - concat5 calls) misses by -30..+148 lines against tonight's previews (do_write +178 measured vs +30 predicted; eoie_dev -28 vs +2), because sm' calls pull lib helpers and reflection lookups expand per call site. The compile preview stays the only gate measurement; the 950 rule stays the pre-filter.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_18 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 18u32, "2026-10-22T08:29Z", "eoie", "04:29 heal-crate fix: a file whose only rust_std_string open sits inside its test static block (authority_state_domain, eoie_experiment_stats) was refused because the open handling required a column-0 open; it now handles an open at any indent (replaced by open sm'_operators, dropped when nothing uses ++#; a top-level operator open is still inserted wherever ++# appears). Both join wave E2 (23 packages).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_19 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 19u32, "2026-10-22T08:31Z", "eoie", "04:31 wave E2 is 24 packages: the 21 from the census, authority_state_domain and eoie_experiment_stats (after the indented-open fix) and agile_memory_links (hand slice apply-heal-memory-links.ps1: memory slugs come from kebab-case file names, so sm'.listed's trimming changes nothing). agile_todo_model stays: it is a package compiled into agile_todo_lines (986 lines). The whole wave dry-runs clean on a composed copy of live + D + E.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_20 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 20u32, "2026-10-22T08:55Z", "eoie", "04:55 composition check (text only) on a fresh D-tree copy: apply-rE.ps1 then apply-rE2-wave.ps1 (24 packages + decimal parse 13 + string length 7 + path kind 8) compose cleanly. Totals after E+E2: concat5 1165 -> 232 uses, packages on rust_std_string 116 -> 14, packages on lib/spiral 1 -> 111. Gate numbers come only from previews (E first, then E2) on the live tree after the ABC tail installs.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_21 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 21u32, "2026-10-22T09:08Z", "eoie", "05:08 E's gate preview is scheduled for 06:32, after tibia's live block: preview-E-live.ps1 copies the live src (which already holds ABC's regenerated .rs, so sizes are real), applies D then E, and previews the 100 touched packages in a compile window (~18 min), reporting every .rs against 1000/950. Its text half was dry-run just now (D + E apply, 100 touched).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_22 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 22u32, "2026-10-22T09:16Z", "eoie", "05:16 preview scripts fixed before use: a touched set built only from packages whose own .spi changed misses the crates that compile a changed crate-less package (workspace_primitives, eoie_do_model, agile_mutation_policy...). preview-E-live.ps1 and the new preview-E2-live.ps1 add every crate whose package.spiproj lists a touched package; E's set grows 100 -> 120 (about 22 min in a compile window). E2's script applies E first and previews only what E2 changes on top.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_23 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 23u32, "2026-10-22T10:29Z", "eoie", "06:29 tibia's live block ended 06:28 (live-gui released, confirmed by tibia); E's gate preview started (preview-E-live.ps1: live src copy + D + E, about 120 touched crates and consumers, one compile window). Results go here and into the 07:00 report.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_24 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 24u32, "2026-10-22T10:33Z", "eoie", "06:33 E's gate preview was reaped by Claude Code's low-memory guard at about 06:31, after 5 of ~120 crates: at block end every lane started heavy work at once (x pre-commit, tibia gpu sim, four main jobs), free memory fell to 3.7 GB with both heavy slots held. Cleaned up: its compile window released (pid gone), the live src copy and the staged regeneration folder (verified mine by eoie_do_records, no process using it) removed. Not retried tonight, though retry-after-memory-kills says retry: the preview needs ~22 min, 27 min remained, and the machine stays saturated by six other heavy jobs, so a retry would be reaped again or miss 07:00. Next session: run preview-E-live.ps1 first thing, before heavy lanes start, or after the ABC tail; gate numbers stay pending until then.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_25 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 25u32, "2026-10-22T10:35Z", "eoie", "06:35 correction to note 24: the reap was not only the block-end stampede. I had taken the Stop gate's idle-stop path (three tool-free stops) while the preview ran, and the low-memory reaper only kills background shells of an idle session. Recorded in memory heavy-gate-lessons: while a job of mine runs, stay in the turn with light tool work.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_26 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 26u32, "2026-10-22T22:16Z", "eoie-lane", "18:16 next heal drafted (scratch apply-heal-console.ps1, text dry run on a GE2+H copy): 25 packages each define their own stdout helper as a println host macro, and lib console.write_line's Rust arm is exactly println of the value, so the helper becomes 'let name (~x : string) : () = console.write_line x' plus spiral- in the spiproj. Dry run: 23 packages healed; eoie_do_edit (976) and eoie_machine_watch (954) skipped by the near-gate rule; 16 inline println uses (not helpers) left for package-by-package healing. eprintln (12 copies) waits for main's console.write_error_line in the combined landing. Placement: its own wave after H, previewed with -CargoCheck over the 23 owners, since it touches crates GE2 and H do not regenerate. Also measured: workspace_primitives already holds stdout_line, stderr_line and unix_ms for 47 packages; the heal goes to lib, not there, because lib is the single source (lib-first).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_27 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 27u32, "2026-10-22T22:21Z", "eoie-lane", "18:21 apply-heal-console.ps1 now also covers the 12 eprintln helpers, behind a lib check: they move onto console.write_error_line only when lib console.spi defines it (main's combined landing). Dry runs on GE2+H copies: with today's lib the 12 stay as they are (reported); with a stand-in console.spi that defines write_error_line, all 12 heal. So the slice is correct before and after the landing, and needs no edit when the landing arrives.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_28 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 28u32, "2026-10-23T16:23Z", "eoie", "12:23 eoie: next heal drafted, scratch apply-heal-lines.ps1, from a census of host-macro helpers still copied across packages on the live tree (stdout_line 24, argument_count 22 and argument 18 were already covered by the console, argv, decimal-parse and integer-text heals; left: line_count 10, line_at 8, text_between 5, file_text 4, option_value 4). The line helpers come in two semantics (all lines, or the empty ones filtered out) and two return types. Lib gains sm'.line_count/line_at/nonempty_line_count/nonempty_line_at as named Rust one-expression primitives (the exact scanning expressions the copies use), plus sm'.lines/nonempty_lines (split once) for real per-package migrations; test blocks for both, in the lib-gaps slice. A first version mapped the copies onto sm'.lines plus am'.length/try_item, but callers use these helpers in loops (do_lab checks line_count every recursion step, lcov walks by line_at), so per-call array building would have turned those loops quadratic in allocations; the heal now maps onto the scanning primitives instead. Dry run on a src copy: 15 definitions in 8 packages (dev_spiral_tests, do_git, do_jobs, do_lab, do_write, handoff_render, lcov, repo_state), each keeping its semantics and return type; eoie_machine_watch (954) skipped by the near-gate rule; eoie_grep_query's multi-line copy is left for package-level healing. Lands after lib-gaps, in its own heal wave.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_29 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 29u32, "2026-10-23T17:50Z", "eoie", "13:50 eoie: lib-gaps gate with a tree baseline (job-1792773558090, process compiler, own MIX_BUILD_PATH, box at 100%). Tree sm'.spi (untouched lib) failed MORE targets than the copy: format and show and replicate_3 on rust, format_fixed_avoids_ties gleam, show_floats typescript. Copy sm'.spi with the lib gaps: 49/51, and the 2 reds (format_fixed_avoids_ties zig, show_floats zig, with delphi/lean not run) also fail on the tree (zig-dev locally vs 0.17.0 in CI). Tree env.spi 5/5, where the copy's earlier run had one typescript red in get_environment_variable: so these reds differ run to run under load. Every added test passes: process.try_capture_combined_list (rust), am'.growable/push_back, sm'.concat_array join, sm'.lines/nonempty_lines/line_count/line_at and nonempty variants, env.current_executable, testing.expect. Ready to land after main's lib land, with one more gate run on an idler box.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_30 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 30u32, "2026-10-23T21:51Z", "eoie", "17:51 eoie: from EOIE-USES-LIB's close check (live tree, after GE2's landing): 32 packages still list rust_std_string. 10 are named by a queued slice (H, J, K, heal-lines, agile-records, job-rechain, note-append); that is an upper bound, since being named doesn't mean the slice removes the dependency. 22 are covered by nothing queued: agile_note_model, agile_todo_lines, agile_todo_model, command_spec_domain, eoie_do_edit, eoie_do_facts, eoie_do_git, eoie_do_process, eoie_do_program, eoie_experiment_amend, eoie_experiment_analysis, eoie_experiments, eoie_job_supervise, eoie_machine_crowding, eoie_machine_watch, eoie_memory, eoie_memory_model, eoie_memory_query, eoie_receipt, eoie_style_census, eoie_table_query, workspace_primitives. Those are this item's next heal wave. Order by the 950-line gate (crates near 1000 lines first need a crate boundary, as with do_program at 988).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_31 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 31u32, "2026-10-23T22:08Z", "eoie", "18:08 eoie: heal wave 1 drafted (scratch heal-wave-1/apply-heal-wave-1.ps1; dry run clean and idempotent; typecheck waits for tibia's live block to end, since no compiles run during a block). eoie_receipt moves onto lib (++#, console.write_line, lib testing.expect, sm'.contains; spiral- added). eoie_job_supervise drops an unused open of rust_std_string.model. workspace_primitives measured_sizes uses ++#, with byte-identical output. replace_file_checked keeps its concat5 until lib has a process-id primitive, since touching it would mean migrating its pid host blob. Selection rule applied: packages whose own or inlining crates sit at 950+ generated lines are skipped (eoie_memory 994, do_program 988, do_edit 976, memory_query 975, experiment_analysis 965, machine_watch 954; eoie_table_query is inlined into analysis.rs at 965), because each ++# adds a Rust binding until RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE lands. Package listings stay until a dependents check (transitive resolution).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_32 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 32u32, "2026-10-23T22:18Z", "eoie", "18:18 eoie: measured with heal-crate.ps1 (its built-in gate rule: own or consumer crate projected within 5% of 1000 lines) over the 17 remaining uncovered packages on a post-O copy. Only 2 heal now: eoie_experiment_amend (1 file rewritten, spiral- referenced, HostFree + LibReferenced declared) and eoie_machine_crowding (0 rewrites, standards declared). 15 are skipped: own crates do_edit 976->1054, do_facts 937->973, do_process 903->987, do_program 988, experiment_analysis 965, experiments 949, machine_watch 954, memory 994, memory_query 975, style_census 923->986, agile_todo_lines 986; consumers agile_note_model and eoie_table_query (via experiment_analysis 965), agile_todo_model (via agile_todo_lines 986), eoie_memory_model (via eoie_memory 994). So the heal wave is blocked on RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE (main's codegen: inline single-use bindings, so a ++# chain costs what concat5 costs), not on drafting. Plan: wave 2 = heal-crate on those 2 plus heal wave 1, in the release after O; the rest re-run heal-crate the day RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE installs (the tool re-projects, so no per-package work is lost).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_33 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 33u32, "2026-10-24T00:09Z", "eoie", "20:09 eoie: lib-first heal of the most repeated host blob: stdout_line as $'println!(\"{}\", &*!text)' occurs once in each of 23 packages. A generated eoie do program (scratch heal-do-host/heal-stdout-line.do, one edit block per file with expect files 1 replacements 1) turns 20 of them into console.write_line text: ok files 20 replacements 20 in 54 ms on a copy of the real post-P tree. The 3 packages without spiral- (eoie_do_edit, eoie_do_facts, eoie_machine_watch) wait for a step that adds the lib reference; console.write_line compiles to the same println, so near-gate crates don't grow. heal-jobs-host.do dropped its own stdout rule (now 4 rules), so the two programs compose (both ok in sequence; a third, overlapping program refused fail-safe and was deleted). Typecheck tomorrow; ships after the train with the boundary work.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_34 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 34u32, "2026-10-24T00:10Z", "eoie", "20:10 eoie: second mass heal: the number-to-text one-liners count_text, decimal_text, i32_text and u64_text (to_string host blobs repeated across packages) become sm'.format value. sm'.format prints integers as bare digits (lib test: 8i32 shows as 8), and eoie's sequences receipts already rely on it. Generated program scratch heal-do-host/heal-number-text.do: 17 files, 17 replacements, ok in 40 ms, applied on top of heal-stdout-line and heal-jobs-host, so all three compose. Skipped: eoie_do_program and eoie_machine_watch (no spiral- reference) and eoie_jobs (its decimal_text sits in heal-jobs-host.do). Census method for the next ones: exact duplicate host one-liners (inl/let name ... = $'...' : type) grouped by text, highest counts first; all_digits (4x) is next and needs a lib sm'.all_digits or a fold over to_char_list.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_35 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 35u32, "2026-10-24T00:13Z", "eoie", "20:13 heal-do-host\\heal-stderr-line.do: 11 generated edit blocks swap the copied stderr_line host macro (eprintln through Rc str) for console.write_error_line text; every target package already lists spiral-; eoie_jobs left out because heal-jobs-host.do carries its copy. Applied on the compose copy after stdout-line + number-text: ok files 11 replacements 11 in 33 ms. Typecheck with the other heal programs after the train.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_36 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 36u32, "2026-10-24T00:16Z", "eoie", "20:16 heal-do-host\\heal-number-text-tail.do: the first number-text program only matched the .to_string().as_str() spelling; a regex census over integer-to-text one-liners with the plain (!value).to_string() spelling found 9 more packages, now sm'.format value. Composes on the compose copy after the other three programs (ok files 9 replacements 9). Left out: u64_parsed (2, parse without trim, try_convert goes through i64 so values above i64 max would differ), own_pid (2, lib has no process id: lib gap), text_between (4, total get vs lib substring that slices unchecked).")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_37 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 37u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "eoie", "20:17 correction to note 36: the tail program is 6 packages, not 9. eoie_do_program (compiled.spi) and eoie_machine_watch (jobs_watch.spi, run.spi) list only core- and rust_std_string, so sm'.format would not resolve. Dropped those 3 blocks and reverted them on the compose copy with the inverse program. A spiral- audit over all five heal programs now shows none targeting a package without spiral-.")
inl note_of_eoie_lib_reuse_38 () = Note ("EOIE-LIB-REUSE", 38u32, "2026-10-24T07:18Z", "eoie", "03:18 the five heal programs (stdout_line 20, number-text 17 + tail 6, stderr_line 11, eoie_jobs host 4 rules) still apply on the emulated post-R tree and form next-train stage S4 (scratch next-train\\apply-stage-S4.ps1, launch/build derived). Emulating the full chain HIJK..R + S1..S4 gives 37 ok, 0 FAIL. The post-block whole-train check regenerates and cargo-checks every crate they touch plus dependents before anything launches.")
inl spiral_lib_am_any () = Task ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", "lib/spiral am'.any emits source.any(...) on array_base, which the Rust backend represents as Rc<RefCell<Vec<_>>>: rustc E0599 no method named any", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,lib,eoie", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_lib_am_any () = Planning ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", Unestimated, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_lib_am_any_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:42Z", "eoie", "22:42 found by the EOIE-LIB-REUSE pilot (agile_task_query tag_listed through am'.any over sm'.split of the tags): Spiral typechecks, rustc fails at main.rs 'v3.any(|x| v4(x))'. Fix in lib/spiral/am'.spi (iterate the vector: borrow().iter().any) with a lib test block, then a Cells --gate run (lib edits). eoie works around it with sm'.index_of meanwhile.")
inl note_of_spiral_lib_am_any_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", 2u32, "2026-10-22T04:15Z", "main", "00:15 main fix in lib/spiral/am'.spi (uncommitted for the user): any was a Rust-only macro, source.any(|x| fn(x)), but array_base on Rust is an Rc RefCell Vec (ArrayIndex emits a.borrow()[i as usize].clone()), and .any is an Iterator method, hence E0599; no tracked caller existed (grep of all repos), eoie's lib pilot was the first. Now portable Spiral: a let rec loop over index_base up to the array length, true on the first element fn accepts. New test any (rust, fsharp, typescript): init 5 has an element equal to 3 and none above 10. Gate queued after the night install (job main-am-any-gate, lib_gate.ps1 on am'.spi).")
inl note_of_spiral_lib_am_any_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", 3u32, "2026-10-22T13:47Z", "eoie-lane", "09:47 eoie team: status check. Main's 00:15 fix is in the live lib (am'.any is a let rec loop over index_base, no Rust .any call), so the row's Planned 0% is stale. There is no job named main-am-any-gate in the local job registry, so I can't confirm the gate run from here, and the status stays main's call. Dependency to know: eoie release G (E's heal wave) maps rust_std_string pipe_member to sm'.listed, which is built on am'.any, so G's healed crates need this lib fix. Last night's scratch previews of those crates compiled after it landed. Committing the lib change before G installs keeps eoie's committed sources buildable from a clean checkout.")
inl note_of_spiral_lib_am_any_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", 4u32, "2026-10-23T10:55Z", "main", "2026-10-23 06:55 main: its own gate never ran (the 00:15 plan's job was never queued). Queued now as main-am-any-gate after the rustls check: lib_gate on lib/spiral am'.spi with the daemon retire floor at 1024 MB, raw output in runs/lab-outputs/SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY. Rule: the 'any' test (rust, fsharp, typescript) passes and 0 type errors, then Done.")
inl note_of_spiral_lib_am_any_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", 5u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming, corrected 00:04: main-am-any-gate finished 0 at 22:24 on the LIVE lib's am'.spi (50DC2DF31AFB1910): 12/12 tests, 0 type errors. The fix itself has been in the live lib since 10-22 00:15 (note 1), so there is nothing to land. The 23:08 'land next' reading was wrong.")
inl note_of_spiral_lib_am_any_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY", 6u32, "2026-10-24T04:04Z", "main", "2026-10-24 00:04 main: Done by note 4's prewritten rule (the 'any' test passes and 0 type errors): 12/12 on the live lib, 0 type errors.")
inl tibia_lib_migration () = Task ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", "apps/arena and apps/predict use Spiral's lib (user rule 2026-10-21 22:5x via eoie): reference it the way x, dice, alphabet and polyglot do (packageDir to spiral/lib plus spiral-), replace hand-rolled host macros with lib functions (sm', am', listm', optionm', resultm, seq, iter, math), and put any missing generic helper into lib/spiral (backend_switch shape), not into the app", Story, P1, D4, 91u32, Active, "", "tibia,lib,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_lib_migration () = Planning ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", Minutes 480u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:56Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 22:56 Census verified: apps/arena/package.spiproj has packageDir ../.. only and 169 host macros (\\$' / \\$\\\"), apps/predict 3, src 0. x's form is 'packageDir: ../../../spiral/lib' + 'spiral-'; check which path holds in tibia's CI (init.ps1 links deps/spiral) before copying it. Known lib bug: am'.any fails on the Rust backend (SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY); arena is Lua and predict is C, so check per backend. Done when both packages reference the lib, every remaining host macro is one the lib can't express (each recorded as a lib gap or kept as a client binding in client.spi), the arena Lua probes regenerate with a live smoke clear, predict reproduces the registered predictions (scripts/predict.ps1), and build.ps1 is green. Part of the Tibia retirement scope, and src/ gets a pass for listm'/optionm'/math over local helpers.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 2u32, "2026-10-22T03:21Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:21 Census (130 macros matched; eoie's 172 also counts escaped forms). About 65 are lib-replaceable: 40 string.format/printf (-> sm' format with ++#? and ++#; format is lib-tested on lua and c), 5 string compares (Spiral = and <>), 3 lengths, 5 tostring/tonumber, 2 os.getenv (lib env), 2 os.time, 1 math.random, the lua.spi concat and find, and 9 Lua table helpers (am'/listm' or keep). About 65 are OTClient bindings (g_game, g_map, creature methods such as (!c):getPosition()), which stay in client.spi. Structure finding: spiproj package names are plain identifiers resolved under one packageDir, or under the compiler dir with '|' (spiral_compiler.fs 178106 and 178324), so no package can reach both the lib and tibia's own src- package. Siblings avoid this by having one package per repo with the lib as packageDir and their code as modules. Test queued (job tibia-libtest, scratch copy in target/libtest): src/package.spiproj gets packageDir lib plus spiral-, arena keeps packageDir ../.. plus src-; does the include re-export spiral- to arena? If yes, migrate in place. If no, the standard form is one root package.spiproj (packageDir deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib, modules src/ + apps/arena/ + apps/predict/) replacing the three per-folder packages. Output-equality risk: probe log lines are parsed by analyses and eoie regexes, so every format change must keep the text byte-identical (check with lua54 on the compiled probes, then the live smoke clear). Mapping table: session scratchpad lib-census.tsv.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 3u32, "2026-10-22T03:31Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:31 Structure tests (scratch copies in target/, two compile jobs). (1) target/libtest: src/package.spiproj with packageDir lib plus spiral- compiles (competitor.lua builds through it), but arena, which includes src-, does not see the lib's modules: 'open sm'' resolves to something without format or ++#, probably the old core's sm'. Includes are not transitive. (2) target/rootpkg: one root package.spiproj (packageDir lib; modules src/ ..., apps/arena/ ..., apps/predict/ predict). Arena modules resolve each other, but src modules are unbound from apps/arena and apps/predict (policy, hunting_pack, mirroring_the_current_arena). Module names resolve in a module's own folder and its ancestors; dice's app.spi sees state only because state sits in an ancestor folder. So no spiproj today gives arena both tibia's src and the lib. Decision (rule: a missing capability goes to the tool first, as with eoie verbs): (A) a compiler change, either an included package that re-exports its own packages, or a package entry with a relative path; requested from main. Rejected for now: (B) nesting apps/arena and apps/predict under src/ (moving tracked folders and rewriting build paths for one repo). Status: Blocked on that compiler capability. Unblocked parts: the census, the mapping table, and the output-equality check plan.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 4u32, "2026-10-22T03:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-21 23:34 main took the compiler route (b) into tonight's pack (PACK-AFTER-NIGHT): a package entry starting with '.' is a path relative to the spiproj's folder, e.g. apps/arena/package.spiproj gets packageDir ../../deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib with packages |core-, spiral-, ../../src-. A non-include entry is named after its last segment; regression sample package_relative_path. Install in the morning, after eoie's release. Resume condition: main's message that the installed compiler has it. Then arena and predict move to that spiproj form; the ~65 lib-replaceable macros go per the census (note 2), keeping log text byte-identical (lua54 on the compiled probes, then a live smoke clear); predict must reproduce the registered predictions exactly. No restructure.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 5u32, "2026-10-22T05:09Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 01:09 eoie suggested a root AGENTS.md carrying the lib-first rule (eoie has one since tonight). Decision: not for tibia now. No sibling has one (x, dice and spiral checked), tibia is retiring its prose docs (DOCS-TO-CODE), and tibia already enforces such rules in code (build.ps1 refuses host calls in lua.spi; eoie standards checks src/standards.spi). When this migration lands, the rule goes into that check instead: every package.spiproj lists spiral-, and host macros only in client.spi. Revisit if the user standardizes AGENTS.md across repos.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 6u32, "2026-10-22T12:18Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:18 Prepared for the resume (no edit yet; main: install after main-pack-review3, then 'installed, go'). Target spiproj for apps/arena (and apps/predict likewise): 'edition: 3', 'packageDir: ../../deps/spiral/lib' (tibia's deps/spiral is the symlink init.ps1 creates to the spiral checkout; deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib also exists but goes through polyglot), packages '|core-', 'spiral-', '../../src-' (main's relative entry), modules unchanged. x's apps/client uses the same shape with '../../../spiral/lib'. Timing rule: apps/arena edits happen only outside live blocks (run-arena rebuilds the probes when apps/arena or src is newer than target/arena, so an edit during a block would compile mid-block); during today's PR-22/PR-23 block the macro replacements are drafted in a scratch copy under target/libmig and landed after 'block end'.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 7u32, "2026-10-22T12:20Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:20 Draft started in the scratch copy target/libmig (apps/arena, apps/predict, src copied; the tree is untouched). 61 string.format sites in apps/arena: scratch libmig-format.ps1 rewrote the 39 single-line ones that use only %s/%d into lib concatenation (text ++# s ++#? n, with @@ unescaped to @), left 1 (%+3d in scan.spi), and the rest are multi-line macros or sit inside OTClient binding blobs in client.spi (review by hand). Kept as host macros, recorded as lib gaps when the census is final: float and width formats (adjacency %.3f, measure %.1f%% and %.2f, scan %+3d). To check at the first compile after main's install: whether the modules need an open for the lib operators (x's prelude), and that ++#? prints an i32 exactly like %d on Lua (byte-identical log text, verified with lua54 on the compiled probes, then a live smoke clear).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 8u32, "2026-10-22T12:22Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:22 Draft progress in target/libmig (no compile yet): besides the 50 format sites, the 5 empty-string compares are Spiral (text = empty, x <> empty), and lua.spi's joined, shown and contains are now defined through the lib (a ++# b; empty ++#? n; sm'.contains needle text, whose Lua branch emits the same string.find(s, value, 1, true) ~= nil). Call sites unchanged. Left for after the install, each needing a compile to verify: summon.spi's order split (sm'.split plus am'), random_between (lib random), matched_number (tonumber of string.match; likely a lib gap), and the Lua table helpers in lua.spi (cells, sample tables: am'/listm' or kept as the probe's runtime boundary).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 9u32, "2026-10-22T12:24Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:24 Draft: apps/predict's 3 host macros (tab-separated integer printf rows) become console.write_line of a ++#? / tab-joined line in target/libmig (the lib's C writer prints strings with printf of %s plus newline); apps/predict also needs the edition-3 spiproj with spiral-. src has no local helpers that duplicate lib names (abs, min, max, clamp, list folds checked), so its pass has nothing to change. Verification after main's install: predict must reproduce the registered predictions exactly (scripts/predict.ps1), which also checks that ++#? prints an i32 like %d on C.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 10u32, "2026-10-22T12:25Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:25 Draft complete up to the compile (target/libmig): apps/arena and apps/predict package.spiproj in the target form (edition 3, packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib, |core- spiral- ../../src-), and every module using ++# opens sm'_operators like x's cli.spi (12 files). Risk to check first at compile: edition 3 may change how the existing arena code parses (x's edition-3 lessons are about naming, not syntax, but unverified for tibia). Landing order after main's install and outside live blocks: compile the draft copy, lua54 byte check of every probe's log lines against the current probes on the same input, predict reproduces the registered predictions, then copy into the tree, build.ps1, a live smoke clear, and the standards check gains the rule (every package.spiproj lists spiral-, host macros only in client.spi and lua.spi's runtime boundary).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 11u32, "2026-10-22T12:27Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:27 Edition-3 risk checked (scratch edition3-check.ps1, compile job tibia-edition3-check2): the current apps/arena with only 'edition: 3' added compiles all nine probe entries (competitor, measure, nexbot, path, random, scan, steps, summon, vbot) to Lua byte-identical to target/arena, failed 0 of 9. So the edition switch is a no-op for tibia's arena code; the migration's remaining risks are the lib resolution (needs main's install) and ++#? matching %d.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 12u32, "2026-10-22T12:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:28 ++#? vs %d checked (scratch showint-check.ps1, a standalone package on deps/spiral/lib with spiral-, compile job tibia-showint-check): 0, 123, -45 and 2147483647 joined with tabs and an @t+ suffix print byte-identically to %d on both Lua (lua) and C (gcc). So the converted format sites and predict's rows keep their text. With the edition-3 check (note 11), the only open risk left is the package resolution itself, which needs main's install.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 13u32, "2026-10-22T12:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 08:30 Census final for the draft (target/libmig): apps/arena host macros 173 -> 112 and apps/predict 3 -> 0 (steps.spi's three expression-argument format sites done by hand). What stays: 85 OTClient bindings in client.spi (g_game, g_map, creature methods; the probe's boundary), 20 Lua runtime helpers in lua.spi (cells, sample tables, stamps, counts; candidates for am'/listm' only if the Lua output stays equivalent, decided at the compile), and 7 others: adjacency %.3f, measure %.1f%% / %.2f and its Lua summary function, scan %+3d (lib has pad_left, but no sign flag), summon's order split and table reads (sm'.split has no Lua branch, so it stays). Lib gaps to file when landing: fixed-decimal float formatting (no %.Nf equivalent in sm'), signed width formatting, sm'.split on Lua.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 14u32, "2026-10-22T13:31Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 09:31 Unblocked: main installed PACK-AFTER-NIGHT (single-flight core 6446A71F2DCD9FA3, equals the reviewed candidate, DISAGREE 0; compiler-install window released 09:30). First step is compile job tibia-libmig-compile on the draft (nine probes to Lua with a lua parse check, plus predict to C). After that, the PR-22 + PR-23 live block. The draft lands after block end.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 15u32, "2026-10-22T13:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 09:34 Draft compiles on the installed compiler (job tibia-libmig-compile, 90 s): all nine probes compiled to Lua and parse (lua loadfile), predict compiled to C; failed 0 of 10. So main's relative entry ../../src- resolves alongside spiral-. The probes grew 5 to 29 percent (competitor 152963 to 174413 bytes) because ++# lowers to one .. per piece through a Mut cell, against one string.format before. The client runs LuaJIT (otclient-src CMake: find_package LuaJIT), and local lua is 5.1.5 with the same number model, so the earlier ++#? check ran under the client's semantics. Edge cases, measured on lua 5.1: tostring and %d agree on 0, -0, 7, -45 and 2147483647. They differ only for non-integral values (2.5 gives 2.5 vs 2) and values above 2^31 (%d wraps to -2147483648, tostring prints the value). Integer division compiles to math.modf truncation and negation goes through spiral_wrap_signed, which normalizes -0, so i32 values stay integral. Landing order is unchanged and starts after block end.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 16u32, "2026-10-22T13:42Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 09:42 Lib gaps filed: LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C (sm'.split has no Lua or C arm; summon's order split stays a host macro until it lands) and LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL (no %.Nf equivalent; adjacency and measure keep three host macros). Signed width is not a lib gap: scan's %+3d is now sm'.pad_left 3 with an explicit + for dy >= 0 in the draft, so the draft has 6 others left, not 7; compile-check after block end. Landing prep, also in the draft: scripts/predict.ps1 rewrites apps/predict's packageDir to the absolute lib path in its target/predict work copy (../../deps does not exist from there), and apps/arena/standards.spi declares the four default rules plus LibReferenced (the installed eoie still drops LibReferenced silently; eoie will flag unknown rules next release). Verify script is scratch libmig-verify.ps1: predict before/after byte compare, format of the draft, eoie standards, then the 10 compiles.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 17u32, "2026-10-22T15:31Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 11:31 Landing rule, written before the verify run: the draft lands in the tree only when all of these hold in one libmig-verify job: predict output byte-identical before and after (same row count), format --check exit 0 on the draft src, apps/arena and apps/predict after the format write, eoie standards on both apps with no finding, and libmig-compile with 0 of 10 failed. Then build.ps1 on the tree must pass; if it fails, the 17 files are restored from the pre-landing copy in target/libmig-restore and the failure is recorded. A live smoke clear waits for the next live window; until it passes, progress stays below 90.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 18u32, "2026-10-22T15:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 11:41 Verify job tibia-libmig-verify (11:34-11:40) passed every note-17 criterion: predict 400 rows byte-identical before and after; format write then --check exit 0 on draft src, apps/arena, apps/predict; eoie standards 0 findings on both apps (installed eoie still drops LibReferenced silently); libmig-compile 0 of 10 failed, every probe parses. LANDED the 17 files into the tree at 11:41 (originals in target/libmig-restore, no Cells reader active). Job tibia-libmig-after-land runs scripts/build.ps1 and re-runs predict on the landed (formatted) tree against the before output; a failure restores the originals.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 19u32, "2026-10-22T16:15Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:15 Smoke criterion, written before the smoke run (refines note 17, which said smoke clear): one PR-22-style hunter run on the landed probes passes when the probe works end to end, not when it clears (a single run clears about 72.5 percent of the time at the PR-22 rate): the row is written with an experience value, the log has the in-game line, at least one strike and the PC-7 chase lines, an end or cleared summary, and no Lua error line. Pass moves the item to 90; Done after one ordinary block on the landed probes shows no row or parse anomaly.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_20 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 20u32, "2026-10-22T16:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:34 Landing side effect found before it bit: the old apps/predict spiproj (packageDir ../..) stayed valid in any work copy with src beside it; the landed one (packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib) does not, since a copy under target/predict has no deps there. In the repo only scripts/predict.ps1 copies apps/predict, and it carries the rewrite to an absolute lib path (landed). The four scratch score scripts D37 runs (score-d20 predict, hunter-trace-d20, hunter-killspot, d21-retreat) got the same one-line rewrite; duo-predict has it. The relative ../../src- entry resolves from the spiproj (verify copied predict to a work dir and matched 400 rows), so work copies still compile their own patched src.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_21 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 21u32, "2026-10-22T16:39Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:39 Build check FAILED and the rule applied: job tibia-libmig-after-land (11:41-12:36) ran scripts/build.ps1 on the landed tree: 26 of 27 test files green (every src file), but apps/arena/lua.spi failed all 5 of its lua tests; predict on the landed tree stayed byte-identical (400 rows). By note 17 the 17 files were RESTORED from target/libmig-restore (12:38); the user index is untouched (it holds pre-PC-7 versions staged earlier; the worktree is back to the pre-landing state). Diagnosis (from the diff, not yet run): the landing put open sm'_operators ABOVE the ///- --package line in lua.spi, and Cells reads flags per cell, so the file's first cell lost its package; all 5 tests failing, including ones that touch none of the 3 changed functions, points to a file-level cause; no passing src or lib file puts an open above its package line. Re-land rule, written before any rerun: fix the draft (open after the static prelude), then the re-land passes when scripts/build.ps1 -Files lua is green (arena build, format check, Cells on lua.spi) and predict stays identical; src needs no rerun (unchanged since its 12:34 green). If lua.spi still fails, no re-land today and the cause goes here first.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_22 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 22u32, "2026-10-22T16:51Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:51 Re-land (job tibia-libmig-reland, 12:41-12:50) FAILED again and restored itself by rule note 21; predict stayed identical. The note-21 diagnosis was WRONG: Cells names the real cause. It mounts the arena package as cells-mounts/<hash>/arena, where the migrated spiproj's relative packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib resolves to cells-mounts/deps/spiral/lib (Package ... is not loaded, likewise src). The old spiproj had packageDir ../.. with plain src-, which the mount carries. The open-line move stays (harmless, and the pattern every passing file follows), but it was not the cause. Next: find how sibling packages that reference lib pass under Cells mounts, then a single-file Cells run on the draft before any third landing.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_23 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 23u32, "2026-10-22T16:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:54 Cause traced in Cells source (spiral apps/cells mounts.ex): declared_dependencies expands every packages entry relative to packageDir (package_base), while the compiler resolves a relative entry like ../../src- from the spiproj dir. With packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib the src entry lands at tibia/deps/src (missing, silently skipped). The mounted copy keeps the relative packageDir, which points into cells-mounts/deps (deps is in the mount skip set). x/apps/client passes only because it has no local sibling package. Reported to main (Cells owner) with a request for a supported spiproj form for lib plus a local sibling. PAUSED. Resume when Cells mounts such a package (main's fix or a documented form), then: draft spiproj in that form, a single-file Cells run on the draft lua.spi, then the note-21 re-land check. The draft stays in target/libmig (open-line move and the TIBIA_ARENA_CHARACTER patch included). The character patch also went onto the restored tree for today's duo, using lua.joined.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_24 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 24u32, "2026-10-22T16:56Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:56 main confirmed the Cells bug; no supported form exists today. The fix is staged as Cells-pack step 11: path-like entries (./ or ../) resolve from the spiproj dir as the compiler does, and a shadowed mount rewrites them to sibling names; its test mirrors the arena layout. It lands with the next Cells pack after the full suite, not today. main withdrew the junction workaround (it would put a link inside spirals real lib folder). Resume condition, exact: the Cells pack containing step 11 is installed; then the note-21 re-land check runs unchanged on the draft.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_25 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 25u32, "2026-10-22T17:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:26 Draft gains summon.spi on lib (needs LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C landed): orders_of, order_count and order_at drop their three Lua macros for sm'.split then am'.choose dropping empty parts, am'.length and am'.index (1-based callers shift by one). Checked in lua 5.1 against the old string.gmatch [^;]+ on 5 inputs (default order, two orders, ;a;;b;, empty, ;;;): identical parts and counts. Lands with the rest of the migration after the Cells pack.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_26 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 26u32, "2026-10-22T17:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:28 Draft gains adjacency.spi's last host macro on lib (needs LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-DECIMAL landed): the %.3f report line is ++#? concatenation plus sm'.format_fixed 3 over an f64 rate computed as Lua did (hits * 1000 / max(1, adjacent_ms), double division in Lua 5.1), and format_fixed's Lua arm is string.format itself, so the text stays byte-identical by construction. measure.spi's two report lines (percent .1f and .2f with Lua-expression arguments) are next once format_fixed lands.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_27 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 27u32, "2026-10-22T17:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:30 Draft gains measure.spi's two report lines on lib (format_fixed now landed): the swings line (percent .1f landed share, .2f mean landed) and the taken line (.2f mean) are ++#/++#? concatenation plus sm'.format_fixed over f64 values computed the way the old Lua expressions did (100 * landed / swings, total / landed, taken_total / count, 0 when the divisor is 0). Byte-compared in lua 5.1 against the old string.format calls on 7 inputs, including zero swings and zero counts: identical. With summon (note 25) and adjacency (note 26), the arena draft no longer needs a host macro for formatting or splitting; it lands after the Cells pack (note 24).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_28 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 28u32, "2026-10-23T11:11Z", "tibia-lane", "Retirement grooming 07:1x: in scope, about 2 h left once main installs the Cells pack with step 11 (external dependency, not scheduled by tibia): the note-21 re-land check (one job), then a live smoke clear inside any tibia block.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_29 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 29u32, "2026-10-23T11:47Z", "tibia-lane", "Draft drift found and fixed (07:3x-07:5x), before the pack lands. The 10-22 draft in target/libmig predated the tree's later changes: the @t and near logging in competitor.spi (10-22 20:3x and today's formatter wrap), the knight-from-env patch in probe, observer, path and random, and today's src/live.spi tests. Landing it as it was would have silently reverted them. Fix: a 3-way merge per migrated file (base target/libmig-restore, the pre-landing originals of 10-22; ours the tree; theirs the draft) gave 13 of 16 files clean. The 3 conflicts (two lines in competitor.spi, bot_profile in probe.spi) were resolved by keeping both intents, the lib concatenation plus the new @t and near fields; ++#? prints an i32 exactly like %d (note 12), so the log text stays byte-identical. The formatter was run on the result. Compile check (job tibia-libmig-rebase-compile on a scratch copy of the tree with the rebased files overlaid): 9 probes to Lua, all parse, and predict to C; failed 0 of 10. target/libmig now holds the rebased files (the 10-22 versions are backed up in session scratch libmig-draft-1022-backup). The note-21 re-land check is unchanged and still waits for the Cells pack with step 11, which main queued after tibia's PR-26 block. Its byte comparison now needs fresh references from today's tree probes.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_30 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 30u32, "2026-10-23T11:56Z", "tibia-lane", "Rebased draft verified 08:0x by the note-17 criteria that run before the pack (job tibia-libmig-rebase-verify, on a scratch copy of today's tree with the rebased files overlaid): predict 400 rows byte-identical before and after; the format write, then --check exit 0; eoie standards 0 findings on apps/arena and apps/predict; libmig-compile failed 0 of 10. The first standards pass found 996 and 111 lf-only findings, CRLF from my merge step ([IO.File]::WriteAllLines); the 30 files in the check copy and in target/libmig were normalized to LF, after which both apps have 0 findings. Only the Cells part of the re-land check (scripts/build.ps1 -Files lua on the landed tree) and the live smoke clear remain, both after the Cells pack with step 11.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_31 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 31u32, "2026-10-24T03:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-23 23:07 grooming: main installed a formatter-only compiler pack (B553EF6B78E6EE29) at 22:27. The rebased draft in target/libmig was compiled and verified at 08:0x, before it, so the note-21 re-land check starts with a fresh compile of the draft on the new compiler. Still gated by the Cells sibling mount (checked 23:0x: no :siblings in spiral apps/cells).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_32 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 32u32, "2026-10-24T05:05Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:05 tibia: the P10 change was carried into the rebased draft by a 3-way merge (base: the pre-P10 tree in scratch p10-backup; ours: the tree with P10 landed; theirs: target/libmig). probe.spi merged clean with LF endings. The draft's client.spi equals the base and is not in the landed file set, so its copy is just the tree version. A re-merge after the land gave byte-identical results, and the draft's previous versions are in scratch libmig-pre-p10-backup. The fresh compile and verify on main's 22:27 compiler is queued: job tibia-libmig-overlay-verify, on a %TEMP% overlay of today's tree plus the 17 draft files. It reports tree files newer than the draft, compares predict before and after, runs the formatter write and check plus eoie standards, and runs libmig-compile. At land time, libmig-land.ps1's 10-22 08:18 drift cut must be replaced by the 3-way merge check, since probe.spi now postdates it.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_33 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 33u32, "2026-10-24T05:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:26 tibia: the rebased draft is verified on main's 22:27 compiler (job tibia-libmig-overlay-verify, exit 0, on a %TEMP% overlay of today's tree plus the 17 draft files). No tree file is newer than the draft. Predict gives 400 rows, byte-identical before and after. The formatter write and check exit 0. eoie standards finds 0 problems on apps/arena (15 files) and apps/predict. libmig-compile failed 0 of 10 (9 probes to Lua, all parse, plus predict to C). This closes the grooming note's fresh-compile step. What remains is unchanged: the Cells part of the re-land check, which waits for the Cells sibling mount, then a live smoke clear.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_34 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 34u32, "2026-10-24T05:48Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:48 tibia: the land path is ready for the Cells pack. Main's cells-pack.ps1 step 11 is the relative package entries fix (lib plus a local ../ sibling under Cells), and main lands the pack right after PR-27's live block (CELLS-PACK-NEXT note of 01:13). So this item and TIBIA-CELLS-APPS unblock tonight. Scratch libmig-land.ps1 got two fixes before it runs. (1) The drift guard was a 10-22 08:18 date cut that P10 now trips. It is now a manifest: scratch libmig-merged-tree.tsv holds the sha256 of the 17 tree files as they were for the last 3-way merge, and land refuses if any differs (re-merge first). -Check runs only the guard: the positive control passes 17 of 17, and a negative control with probe.spi altered refuses naming probe.spi. (2) Land used to back up the tree into target/libmig-restore, which is also the 10-22 merge base, so landing would have overwritten the base. Backups now go to scratch libmig-land-backup, and land refuses if a backup already exists. After the pack lands, scratch libmig-reland.ps1 runs the land, then scripts/build.ps1 -Files lua, then predict byte-identical, and restores on any failure.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_35 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 35u32, "2026-10-24T07:27Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:27 tibia: after the user's 03:2x feedback (stop adding hand-written Lua; memory no-hand-crafted-lua), here is what the rebased draft still carries. $'...' macros per file: client.spi 86 (the OTClient bindings), lua.spi 20, competitor.spi 1, and every other arena file 0. lua.* helper calls outside lua.spi, about 190: native_table 39 (the cell type), joined 38, cell_value 35, new_cell 20, set_cell 19, new_table 7, contains 6, amount_at 4, add_to 1, the sample family (sample, sample_count, sample_total, sample_median, sample_max, samples_joined, labeled_sample, labeled_summary) 13, matched_number 2, random_between 2, and the stamp family 3. Next slice, rule written before drafting: (S1) joined becomes ++# and contains becomes sm'.contains (44 sites, mechanical). (S2) The cell family becomes a Spiral record with a mut field (value : mut i32, read .value, write with <-), which already compiles to Lua: lib step_runtime's spawn test runs on lua with mut record fields. So no new Lua arm is needed, and lua.native_table and new_table leave the arena. (S3) The sample, stamp, matched_number and random families each go to a lib home with a Lua arm only where lib has none (statistics over an array, sm' matching, lib random), checked one family at a time. Acceptance per family: predict 400 rows byte-identical, the 9 probes compile and parse, the formatter check, and the arena's Cells tests from TIBIA-CELLS-APPS green on lua and c. The slice starts after tonight's libmig re-land and the CELLS-APPS land, so a red never mixes with theirs. client.spi's game-API bindings stay as the declared boundary.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_36 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 36u32, "2026-10-24T07:35Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:35 tibia: S3 design per family, written before drafting (S1 and S2 are proven on temp copies; scratch lua-retire-s1.ps1 and lua-retire-s2.ps1, 33 and 74 sites). Samples (sample, count, total, max, median, joined): a mut (list i32) holding newest first, with core listm folds for count, total and max, sm'.concat_list over the reversed list for joined, and the median from am'.sort over the array form. If am'.sort_by's backend_switch has no Lua key, the fix is a lib Lua key pointing at its existing pure insertion sort (no hand Lua). Labeled samples: a mut list of (label, value) pairs; the summary groups by label after sorting (strings order through core comp) and formats with ++#. Stamps: a mut list of (id, at, kind), newest first, read with listm'.try_find_ (the runtime form). Counts (add_to, amount_at, keys 0-3): a fixed array i32 of 4 with set and index. matched_number (2 sites in measure): a pure Spiral parse of the digits between the two phrases using sm' functions, which already run on Lua (lua.spi's own contains delegates to sm'.contains and its lua test passes). random_between (2 sites in random): tibia's src/rng.spi, the pure Spiral generator the simulator uses, seeded from client.now_ms, which replaces math.random and math.randomseed. When S3 is done, lua.spi holds only the native_table type for client.spi's OTClient slots, and the module can be folded into client.spi.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_37 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 37u32, "2026-10-24T08:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:07 tibia: re-land attempt after PR-27 (job tibia-after-block): REFUSED by its own acceptance and restored. libmig-land landed the 17 files, then scripts/build.ps1 -Files lua failed all 5 lua.spi tests: 'Package .../cells-mounts/deps/spiral/lib/spiral is not loaded'. Predict was byte-identical. The runner restored all 17 files and the tree is as before. CELLS-APPS was not attempted. Cause: main's Cells pack (step 11) now mounts the siblings (Cells mount 17b254bb61370c49 has arena, spiral and src junctions), but it mounts arena itself as a plain junction, so its spiproj's packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib resolves from the mount to cells-mounts/deps/spiral/lib. A package like this needs the shadow kind with packageDir rewritten to .. and ../../src- to src-. Reported to main with the evidence; it's main's call or mine to patch mounts.ex. Still blocked on that Cells fix; everything else for the land is green (verify note 33, manifest guard, predict identical).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_38 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 38u32, "2026-10-24T08:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:17 tibia: the Cells blocker is being fixed by tibia (main agreed 04:1x). Cause: mounts.ex share_mounted_deps? compared the raw spiproj entries (spiral, ../../src) with the mounted sibling names (spiral, src), so a package with a ../ sibling was never shadowed and kept its relative packageDir. The fix maps the entries through mount_name (one anchored edit), plus a test in mounts_relative_test.exs (shared with arena, spiral and src mounted; not shared without src). Scratch cells-mounts-fix.ps1 (eoie do slice and undo; the dry run against the live apps/cells previews 2 replacements and writes nothing) and cells-mounts-gate.ps1 (a cells-root copy, then run_suite on the 3 mounts test files), as heavy job tibia-cells-mounts-gate. The land goes outside main-cells-suite-tree's run, then after-block reruns.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_39 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 39u32, "2026-10-24T08:48Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:48 tibia: LANDED in lib (S3 groundwork, note 36). am'.sort_by's Lua key now points at its existing pure Spiral insertion sort instead of the F# Array.sortBy macro, which couldn't run on Lua, and sort_orders_on_every_backend gains lua. Gate GREEN at 03:52 (12 of 12, 0 type errors, 42 rows 0 red); kit land at 04:47 after waiting out one Cells run, sha256 50dc2df3 to f624a66c, byte-identical to the gated candidate. Undo program: scratch am-sort-lua-land\\undo.do (expect files 1 replacements 2). It's pure Spiral, one backend key, within GRANULAR-HEAL.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_40 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 40u32, "2026-10-24T09:31Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:31 tibia: the Cells mounts fix gate is GREEN (job tibia-cells-mounts-gate-2, compile class): run_suite on a cells-root copy with the fix applied, 10 of 10 across mounts_relative, mounts_patched and mounts_name, including the new 'a package with a relative sibling shares the mounted deps, so it is shadowed' (spiral-bin\\cells-suite\\mounts-fix.log). The land job tibia-cells-mounts-land is running and waiting for two clear samples (other lanes' Cells erl runs were in flight at 05:30); tibia-after-block-2 (libmig re-land, then CELLS-APPS) follows it after-ok. The land result is recorded when it applies.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_41 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 41u32, "2026-10-24T09:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:54 tibia: the Cells mounts fix LANDED at 05:51 (job tibia-cells-mounts-land, after-ok on the green gate). It waited about 30 min for clear samples (eoie's sm'.spi lib gate, then another lane's run, then my own file_exists gate), then applied: mounts.ex sha256 544b12b3 to 1e6ed95f, byte-identical to the gated copy, and the new mounts_relative test. Undo: scratch cells-mounts-fix\\undo.do. main was told. tibia-after-block-2 (libmig re-land, then CELLS-APPS) started right after, as chained.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_42 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 42u32, "2026-10-24T10:11Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 06:11 tibia: LANDED. apps/arena and apps/predict are on Spiral's lib (job tibia-after-block-2, libmig-reland PASSED at 06:10). The 17 draft files replaced the tree's after the manifest guard confirmed they matched the last 3-way merge (17 of 17). scripts/build.ps1 -Files lua is green: lua.spi 5 of 5 under Cells on the migrated package, which mounts correctly now that the Cells mounts fix is in (the same check failed at 04:0x with 'package not loaded'). scripts/predict.ps1 gives 400 rows, byte-identical to scratch predict-before.tsv. Originals: scratch libmig-land-backup (libmig-land.ps1 -Restore puts them back). Left on this item: the live smoke clear (the next live window) and the Lua-retire slices S1-S3 (S1 and S2 proven; the chain runs after the file_system Lua arm lands; S3 is designed). Restage for the user: the 17 libmig files (apps/arena: adjacency, competitor, lua, measure, observer, package.spiproj, path, probe, random, scoreboard, steps, summon, scan, standards; apps/predict: package.spiproj, predict.spi; scripts/predict.ps1).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_43 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 43u32, "2026-10-24T10:20Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 06:20 tibia: S3, first family drafted: scratch lua-retire-s3a.ps1 makes adjacency's per-count table (lua.new_table, add_to, amount_at) a Spiral array i32 of 4. It starts as ;[ 0i32; 0i32; 0i32; 0i32 ], adds through set and index on the slot capped at 3 in Spiral, and reads with index. Proven by replaying the chain on a temp copy of the CELLS-APPS candidates (gate-via-lib, then S1, then S2, then S3a): every anchor matches once, no line is over 120, and adjacency.spi has 0 lua.* calls left. It applies after lua-retire-chain.ps1, with an adjacency Cells run (rate_line, count_line and hit_line tests on lua and c) as acceptance. The other S3 families (samples, stamps, matched_number, random) follow note 36.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_44 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 44u32, "2026-10-24T16:52Z", "main", "main 12:5x: the Cells tree suite with the staged mounts.ex fix (main-cells-suite-tree-2, log spiral-bin\\cells-suite\\main-tree.log) passed 308/311, and none of the 3 reds touches mounts: (1, 2) DocumentTest lib round-trips (conversation.spi double blank line before persist; json_real.spir and routes_real.spir lack the canonical heading and ///- --real marker), both red at HEAD 0ec501fb too, fixed token-neutrally as LIB-CELLS-ROUNDTRIP (queued with a document_test rerun, job main-serial-cells-roundtrip); (3) CellsLiveTest \"a print with no value still runs\" hit a rustc \"expected expression, found $\" at 11:2x that does not reproduce now (console.write_line compiles, builds and prints on the live lib).")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_45 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 45u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 19:45 Dry run of slice S3a (lua-retire-s3a.ps1) against HEAD 695c708 and against the working tree: all 4 anchors match once in both, and 3 lua.native_table fields remain in each (adjacent_ms, hits, last_tick, for the later slices). So whichever way the CELLS-APPS tree mismatch (CELLS-APPS note 20) is resolved, the Lua-retire chain doesn't need re-anchoring. It still waits on the RED file_exists arm.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_46 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 46u32, "2026-10-25T01:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 21:34 Lua file I/O lib slice in edition 3 (scratch lua-file-io-apply.ps1), replacing the RED lua-file-exists draft, whose root cause is corrected in TIBIA-PARTY note 90. It adds granular primitives in file_system.spi (:! lua_file, open, present, write, read_all, close, remove; one host call each, at most 120 chars), Lua arms for file_exists, write_all_text, read_all_text, append_all_text and file_delete with the logic in Spiral, and a cwd-independent ///> lua test that deletes, writes, checks existence, appends, reads back 'abc' and deletes. A 7-anchor eoie do program with undo, round-tripped byte-exact on a copy of the live edition-3 file. Gate: job-1792892069603-tibia-lib-lua-file-io-gate (heavy, kit gate with the fixed same-location baseline). It unblocks gate-via-lib and the Lua-retire S1-S3a slices here, and NO-HAND-LUA M1's trace-log append. LANDED 2026-10-24 22:07: gate-2 GREEN (job-1792892952996: 19 of 22 pass, 0 type errors; the 3 location reds are red on the same-location baseline too), applied by the kit land at 22:06 (job-1792893705330), lib file_system.spi 2A23F5F0 to 66D2992E, the gated candidate's hash. Main and x were told. gate-1 had failed only because the test sat above the definitions it calls, so it moved to the end of the file. Next: gate-via-lib.ps1 (anchors match 3 of 3 on the tree), then the Lua-retire S1, S2 and S3a slices.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_47 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 47u32, "2026-10-25T02:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 22:3x Landed in tibia as an unstaged layer over the user's staged 17 files (job-1792894637696-tibia-lib-lua-serial, exit 0). gate-via-lib: client.spi's hand-written g_resources.fileExists binding is gone, probe's gate check is now file_system.file_exists over the absolute gate path, and the harness passes that path. Lua-retire S1 (33 lua.joined/contains sites to sm' ++#), S2 (74 lua cell calls to mut cells) and S3a (adjacency counts to array i32) also landed. Checks: formatter clean, arena build LUA-OK for all 9 modules, lua.spi Cells 5 of 5. No file gained a dollar-quote macro or a lua. call; arena hand-written Lua went from 294 to 149 sites by count, and adjacency, observer, path, probe, scan, scoreboard and summon are at 0. git diff: 13 files, +129/-128. Left: the remaining S3 families (lua.sample_count, samples_joined, labeled_summary and the like in measure, steps, random, competitor; also GRANULAR-HEAL's lua.spi sites), about 75 min, and the smoke clear in the next live window, which is also the first live check of the gate path.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_48 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 48u32, "2026-10-25T12:08Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 08:08 S3 inventory (after S1/S2/S3a): 45 lua.* uses of 15 helpers in 5 files. Families: (1) samples, measure only: sample, sample_count, sample_median, sample_total, sample_max, samples_joined (11 uses), to become a Spiral accumulator (a list in a mut cell with count/median/total/max/joined), or lib statistics if a Lua-capable home exists; (2) labeled samples and stamps, steps only: labeled_sample, labeled_summary, stamp, stamp_at, stamp_kind (5), the same accumulator with labels; (3) random_between, random (2), to lib's RNG if it has a Lua arm, else a Lua arm there; (4) matched_number, measure (2), to lib string parsing; (5) native_table and new_table (24: client 11, competitor 2, measure 5, random 2, steps 4), creature tables keyed by id, to become a Spiral map. Family (5) also takes GRANULAR-HEAL's last 4 client.spi sites (instrument_game_calls, game_call_report, forget_dead, the monster list), which iterate those tables with pairs. Re-sized: about 2.5 h (was 75 min, which predated the client sites and family 5's count). Order by dependency: (1)+(2) first (measure and steps only), then (3) and (4), then (5) with the client sites, each a guarded slice with the arena build, lua Cells and, after pack 5l, competitor under the new Lua limits.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_49 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 49u32, "2026-10-25T13:18Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 09:18 S3 family 1 LANDED (job-1792932472660, unstaged): new apps/arena/samples.spi, a pure Spiral sample accumulator (mut list: sample, count, total, max from 0, median as sorted[floor((n+1)/2)] or 0, joined in insertion order), matching the hand Lua helpers exactly; measure.spi uses it for hits and taken (11 helper uses and 2 new_table). Proof: arena build LUA-OK for measure, formatter clean, Cells samples 3/3 (lua and c) and lua 5/5 with memo off. The first try restored itself on the formatter gate only (the renames lengthened a line); the slice now formats measure.spi itself. lua.spi's sample helpers have no callers left in measure; steps still uses labeled_sample/labeled_summary and the stamp helpers (family 2). Left: families 2 (steps), 3 (random_between), 4 (matched_number), 5 (native tables plus the 4 client sites), about 2 h.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_50 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 50u32, "2026-10-25T13:58Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 09:58 S3 families 2-4 in flight. (2) steps: labeled samples and per-key stamps added to samples.spi as Spiral association lists (labels ordered by core comp, since < is number-only; first try restored itself on that ETY constraint error), job-1792935902242. (3) random_between: lib common.random' had no Lua arm (fell to System.Random); slice random-lua-apply.ps1 adds lua_math_random (one host call) and a Lua arm with the bounds in Spiral (empty range returns min, like the Rust arm), plus a lua test; kit gate job-1792936186832 (LIB-RANDOM-LUA); tibia side s3-family3.ps1 (inclusive 0..3 and 1..8 become random' 0 4 and 1 9) waits on the lib land. (4) matched_number: new apps/arena/combat_text.spi (hit_amount, damage_taken over number_between, composed from sm'.index_of/substring/starts_with and convert.try_convert, all with Lua arms; tests mirror the Lua pattern incl. a later match after a non-fitting one), job-1792936644279 after family 2. Rule applied for (4): lib's only digit reader is xsd.digit_of inside the XSD domain module (main's P1 lane), so no lib move now; a generic sm' digit reader is a lib follow-up, not a tibia blocker.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_51 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 51u32, "2026-10-25T14:05Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 10:05 S3 family 2 LANDED (job-1792935902242, unstaged): steps.spi has 0 lua.* calls; labeled samples and per-key stamps live in samples.spi (Spiral association lists, labels ordered by core comp). lua.spi lost labeled_sample, labeled_summary, stamp, stamp_at, stamp_kind and their two tests. Proof: arena build LUA-OK (steps.lua 39957 bytes), formatter clean, Cells samples 6/6 and lua 3/3 with memo off.")
inl note_of_tibia_lib_migration_52 () = Note ("TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION", 52u32, "2026-10-25T14:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 10:28 S3 family 4 LANDED (job-1792936644279, unstaged): new apps/arena/combat_text.spi (hit_amount, damage_taken over number_between; composed from lib sm'.index_of/substring/starts_with and convert.try_convert) replaces both lua.matched_number calls in measure.spi; lua.spi lost matched_number and its test half (contains keeps a test). Proof: arena build green (13 LUA-OK), formatter clean, Cells combat_text 3/3 and lua 3/3 with memo off. Left in S3: family 3 (random, waits on the lib gate LIB-RANDOM-LUA) and family 5 (native tables plus the 4 client sites, waits on pack 5l for the healed client).")
inl eoie_heal_lib_link () = Task ("EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK", "eoie builds against lib/spiral: workspace.ps1 links src/spiral to the Spiral checkout's lib/spiral (the shape the Cells mount uses), /src/spiral git-ignored, census/harvest/grep scans skip it; then apply lab/EOIE-LIB-REUSE pilot and prove agile_task_query (test-regeneration -CargoCheck, 1000-line gate)", Story, P1, D3, 60u32, Active, "", "eoie,lib,heal", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_heal_lib_link () = Planning ("EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK", Minutes 60u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_heal_lib_link_1 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:57Z", "eoie", "22:57 first step of EOIE-LIB-REUSE; pilot already passes under Cells (EOIE-LIB-REUSE note 2).")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_lib_link_2 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 2u32, "2026-10-22T03:17Z", "eoie", "23:17 decision (R6, recorded before release C): the src/spiral link waits for a link-safe directory walk. eoie has 54 hand-rolled read_dir/walk sites in 39 files; census, standards, source topology, grep and the do verbs would all descend into a src/spiral junction and count or search lib files. Sequence: EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY gets one walk primitive that never follows junctions/symlinks, the scanners move onto it, then this link. Release C ships without it; the Cells-side pilot stays proven in lab/EOIE-LIB-REUSE.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_lib_link_3 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 3u32, "2026-10-22T03:40Z", "eoie", "23:40 redesign: no junction anywhere in eoie. test-regeneration already compiles in a staging copy (spiral-bin/eoie-regeneration/<guid>/eoie), and cargo builds the published .rs without needing Spiral packages, while Cells mounts link spiral next to each package. So the lib link is one staging step: after Copy-Workspace, copy <bundle>/../../../lib/spiral (top-level files + rust/) into the staged src/spiral; the live tree never holds the lib, scanners never see it, and Get-WorkspaceEntries' reparse-point refusal never fires. That removes the walk-primitive prerequisite noted at 23:17 (a link-safe walk is still wanted for EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY, using lib file_system.read_dir_names + path_buf_is_symlink). Proof running on a scratch copy (rL-tree): agile_task_query with the sm' pilot, regenerated through the patched staging, cargo check.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_lib_link_4 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 4u32, "2026-10-22T03:46Z", "eoie", "23:46 PROVEN on a scratch copy: test-regeneration with the lib staged as src/spiral (Spiral sources only: *.spi, *.spir, package.spiproj; target/node_modules/bench skipped) regenerated agile_task_query with the sm' pilot (24732 bytes) and cargo check passed; source topology with the staged lib stays ok (+1 Spiral-only package, limits ok). Shipping in release C as apply-lib-staging.ps1 (patches compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 + moves the pilot from lab/EOIE-LIB-REUSE into src/agile_task_query); C's candidate step adds next_cli and agile_cli. The boundary walk is no longer a prerequisite.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_lib_link_5 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 5u32, "2026-10-22T05:09Z", "eoie", "01:09 tibia mentions main is building a relative-package compiler feature (spiproj packages by relative path); when it lands it may replace the staging copy of lib/spiral with a declared relative package in each spiproj. Re-evaluate the staging step then.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_lib_link_6 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 6u32, "2026-10-22T05:37Z", "eoie", "01:37 proven in the real release: release ABC's regeneration staged lib/spiral as src/spiral and regenerated agile_task_query (sm' pilot) ok, 24732 bytes, identical to the scratch proof. Remaining for this item: ABC's cargo check, candidate notebooks (next_cli, agile_cli consume the query crate) and install.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_lib_link_7 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 7u32, "2026-10-22T16:56Z", "eoie", "2026-10-22 12:56 rule, written before measuring its effect: heal-crate's near-gate guard projects instead of reading today's size. A crate (or any direct consumer) is skipped when its widest generated .rs plus 3 lines per concat5 call in its .spi reaches 950 (a concat5 with n non-empty arguments becomes n-1 ++# bindings instead of one call, so at most +3 lines each). Trigger: eoie_experiments sits at 949 (one under the old 950 check) with 44 concat5 calls on the E2 list, so healing it could reach about 1080, past the 1000-line gate GE2's topology check enforces. A slice that anchors on healed text in a newly skipped crate fails its exact-once anchor; that crate's slice moves with it to after the codegen pack, and the wave never trims code to fit.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_lib_link_8 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 8u32, "2026-10-22T19:37Z", "eoie", "2026-10-22 15:37 first real compile of GE2+H (preview, 18 crates) found: (1) the argv heal turned inl argument helpers into wrappers of env.try_command_line_argument, so the lib call was expanded at every call site (process_table's pattern_from went 27 -> 94 lines; process_table 828 -> 1379). The argv, path-kind, integer-text and decimal-parse heals now emit let helpers; string-length keeps inl (same .len()). (2) heal-crate skipped every standards.spi as a rules declaration, which broke eoie_standards, whose standards.spi is the tool module. It now heals it as code when 'standards' is listed under modules, and never writes declarations over it. (3) eoie_do/run.spi lacked the eoie_do_model open after the program_family move (fixed in apply-do-verbs-crate). After crate splits for process_table (kill) and experiments (amend), every GE2+H crate previews under 1000 lines.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_lib_link_9 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK", 9u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 60m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 60%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl eoie_heal_edit_hook () = Task ("EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", "Edit-time drift check: a PostToolUse hook on Edit/Write of eoie .spi/.spiproj runs eoie standards on the change and blocks with the lib function to use (sm'/am'/listm'/...) when the added lines carry a host macro outside the boundary package, a $'{ let block, Rc::<str>::from, a rust_std_string call, or a spiproj without spiral-", Story, P1, D2, 90u32, Blocked, "RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE,EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", "eoie,heal,hook", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_heal_edit_hook () = Planning ("EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", Unestimated, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_heal_edit_hook_1 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:57Z", "eoie", "22:57 why: the harness tells agents to match surrounding code, and eoie's surrounding code is legacy (0/376 packages on the lib, 24.6 host macros/KLOC), so imitation is the drift; memory rules load at session start but the open file wins at edit time. The hook moves the rule to the moment of writing. Needs the user's approval: it edits ~/.claude/settings.json hooks (and the other clients' equivalents via EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS).")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_edit_hook_2 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 2u32, "2026-10-22T03:02Z", "eoie", "23:02 decided and installed (user 23:0x: 'try getting more autonomous in the decisions'): ~/.claude/hooks/eoie-style-check.ps1 as a PostToolUse hook on Edit|Write|MultiEdit in ~/.claude/settings.json (backup in scratch settings.json.before-style-hook.*). Scope: files under eoie/src/<package>/ ending .spi/.spiproj. Checks the ADDED text only (Edit new_string, MultiEdit edits, Write content): $'{ blocks, Rc::<str>::from, rust_std_string, any new host macro; a .spiproj without spiral-; plus eoie standards --ratchet on the package. Reports via exit 2 (feedback to the editing agent, not a hard block). Tested: macro edit -> 2 findings, clean sm' edit -> 0, tibia path -> ignored, lib-less spiproj -> flagged; about 0.6 s per call. Takes effect for sessions started after 23:02. Interim: the regex checks live in the hook until eoie's census carries them (EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET), then the hook shrinks to the eoie call.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_edit_hook_3 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 3u32, "2026-10-22T07:39Z", "eoie", "03:39 hook made gate-aware (rule agreed with main 03:0x, EOIE-LIB-REUSE note 12): in a package whose committed .rs is at 950+ lines, rust_std_string.model.concat5 is allowed (each lib ++# costs one Rust binding until codegen inlines single-use bindings); any other rust_std_string use is still blocked; packages under 950 get the lib advice incl. ++# from sm'_operators. Same 950 threshold as heal-crate.ps1's skip. Probed with synthetic Edit payloads: listing concat5 exit 0, listing trim exit 2, do_tables concat5 exit 2. Backup in scratch eoie-style-check.ps1.before-near-gate-*. Left: other clients' equivalents (EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPT); drop the near-gate branch when main's codegen pack lands.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_edit_hook_4 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK", 4u32, "2026-10-23T10:06Z", "eoie", "06:06 groom: both remaining parts wait on other items, so they are now deps: dropping the near-gate concat5 branch waits on RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE (main's codegen pack, 25% Active), and the other clients' hooks wait on EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS. The Claude Code hook itself is installed and gate-aware; nothing else is left to do here before those land.")
inl eoie_heal_boundary () = Task ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", "One thin OS boundary package for eoie (process, fs, env, clock, path) as named one-expression primitives, x-style (glyphs/wire/net); rust_std_string becomes calls into sm'/am' and is then deleted; generic gaps go to lib/spiral with backend_switch and lib tests", Story, P1, D4, 30u32, Active, "", "eoie,lib,heal", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_heal_boundary () = Planning ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_1 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 1u32, "2026-10-22T02:57Z", "eoie", "22:57 after EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK; the boundary is the only place a host macro may live.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_2 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 2u32, "2026-10-22T03:36Z", "eoie", "23:36 design input from a census of eoie's directory walks: 55 read_dir sites (47 plain listings, 6 recursive walks, 2 extension-filtered listings) in 39 files, none link-aware. One boundary module gives: entries <dir> -> list of a typed entry (FileEntry name | DirEntry name | LinkEntry name, never followed), a recursive walk built in Spiral over entries with a skip predicate (target, .cache, links), and an extension filter in Spiral. Host code stays one read_dir primitive. Scanners first (census, standards, source topology, grep/search, do read/facts/lab), then the rest; this unblocks EOIE-HEAL-LIB-LINK. Also eoie_job_gate sits at 1000 lines after tonight's gate fix: split the slot queue into its own crate before the next gate change.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_3 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 3u32, "2026-10-22T04:54Z", "eoie", "00:54 rust_std_string retirement map (89 of 637 eoie .spi files open it; counts are upper bounds, same-named locals included): concat5 1164 uses/134 files -> ++# chains (open sm'_operators); equals 185/46 -> =; replace 162/44 -> sm'.replace from into text (argument order flips); contains 134/42 -> LIB GAP (sm' has no contains; index_of >= 0 meanwhile); starts_with 76/39 and ends_with 34/14 -> sm'.starts_with/ends_with value text (order flips); trim 46/23 -> sm'.trim; pipe_member 28/16, pipe_lookup 3, pipe_index 1 -> LIB GAP (delimited membership, blocked on SPIRAL-LIB-AM-ANY); trim_end_matches 4/3 -> LIB GAP (suffix trim; sm'.trim_end takes chars). Order: add the three gaps to lib/spiral first (backend_switch + tests), then rewrite crate by crate with the argument-order flips checked by each crate's tests; measure concat5 -> ++# generated size on one crate first (concat5 is one host call; a ++# chain may inline more lines against the 1000 gate).")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_4 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 4u32, "2026-10-22T05:05Z", "eoie", "01:05 correction from main: sm'.contains already exists (all 8 backends), so contains (134 uses) maps today, not a lib gap; my 00:5x grep missed it. sm'.listed separator item text and sm'.trim_end_suffix suffix text are drafted by main as portable compositions with lib tests (rust, fsharp, gleam) and gate after the am'.any gate (main-helpers-gate); they land in tree lib/spiral and ship in main's next pack. The retirement map now has no open lib gaps once that gate passes.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_5 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 5u32, "2026-10-22T05:36Z", "eoie", "01:36 lib gap 4 (sent to main): lib/spiral env has no runtime environment read for Rust (environ_get is Python-only, get_environment_variable_comptime is option_env!). eoie has 132 std::env::var(_os) host reads that would move onto a portable env.var key : option string; release E (job author from EOIE_AUTHOR) needs it first.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_6 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 6u32, "2026-10-22T05:38Z", "eoie", "01:38 correction from main: lib/spiral env.get_environment_variable key : string (env.spi:53) reads at runtime on Rust, F#, Zig, Gleam, Lua, TypeScript and Python ('' when unset), so eoie's 132 std::env::var(_os) host reads can move onto it today and release E can record the job author from EOIE_AUTHOR. Main adds env.try_get_environment_variable : option string after release ABC ends (ABC reads lib/spiral, so main won't change its inputs mid-run). Second lib function I wrongly reported missing tonight (after sm'.contains): my lookups listed '^inl name' lines instead of searching the lib for the operation.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_7 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 7u32, "2026-10-22T08:33Z", "eoie", "04:33 census of eoie host macros (live src, read-only): 1500 macros in 123 packages. By surface: text ops 517 (split/trim/parse/format: lib sm' territory, not OS), other 387, fs 161, cross-crate extern calls 153 (legitimate boundaries), env 110, path 86, process 63, clock 23. Most-copied bodies across packages: args().nth(n) 20 pkgs, number to Rc<str> 20+18, args().count() 15, args().skip(2).count() 14, len() as i32 12, trim().parse::<u64>().unwrap_or(0) 12, the record decoder 10 (eoie_do_records in E absorbs it), Path::is_dir/is_file 9+9. Lib already covers: sm'.obj_to_string (numbers), sm'.length, file_system.path_buf_is_dir/is_file. Lib gaps (offered to main): command-line arguments (count, nth) in env, and a decimal parse returning option in sm'. Plan: the boundary package should hold only fs/env/process/clock/path primitives; the 517 text macros go to sm' instead (heal-crate-style mapping per recurring body). Top packages by macro count: eoie_jobs 62, eoie_cli_harness 51, eoie_machine_watch 47, eoie_do_edit 44, eoie_table_query 42.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_8 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 8u32, "2026-10-22T08:34Z", "eoie", "04:34 macro-to-lib mapping table for a future heal-macros tool (each row needs its semantics proven by a preview before use): (1) Path::new(x).is_dir() / .is_file() : bool -> file_system.path_buf_is_dir|is_file (file_system.new_path_buf (sm'.to_std_string x)): same std calls (PathBuf::from instead of Path::new), safe. (2) x.len() as i32 -> sm'.length x: UNVERIFIED, sm'.length delegates to core sm.length; must confirm it is byte length on Rust (str::len) before mapping, else non-ASCII text changes. (3) Rc::<str>::from(x.to_string()) -> sm'.obj_to_string x: type-directed in lib (string is identity, numbers via format); only for numeric x, which a text tool cannot see: map by hand per site or via a typed helper. (4) args().nth/count/skip(2).count and trim().parse::<u64>().unwrap_or(0): wait for main's lib answer (offered 04:33). (5) the record decoder: done by eoie_do_records (E).")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_9 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 9u32, "2026-10-22T08:46Z", "eoie", "04:46 correction from main: the decimal parse is NOT a lib gap. convert.try_convert : string -> option u (Rust arm (x).trim().parse::<u64>().ok(), plus Gleam/Lua/F#/Python/C/Delphi) with optionm'.default_value 0u64 reproduces the unwrap_or(0) helpers exactly (my census searched sm'/env/file_system only and missed convert.spi; lib-first means searching the whole lib for the operation). Slice apply-heal-decimal-parse.ps1 (in wave E2, after the heal loop) rewrites the trimmed helper bodies in 13 packages with the 950 guard (untrimmed sites in bundle_retention and dev_model stay: try_convert trims, so they would get more lenient). argv: main drafted env.command_line_argument_count () : i32, try_command_line_argument (index) : option string and command_line_argument index (index 0 = first user argument); mappings args().nth(n) -> try_command_line_argument (n - 1), args().count() -> command_line_argument_count () + 1, args().skip(2).count() -> command_line_argument_count () - 1; lands after its gate (before 05:30 or after 06:30).")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_10 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 10u32, "2026-10-22T08:47Z", "eoie", "04:47 mapping (2) verified in the compiler source: core sm.length is !!!!StringLength, which spiral_compiler.fs lowers to .len() on Rust (line 164419) and to UTF-8 GetByteCount on F# (163422), i.e. byte length on both, the same as str::len(). So x.len() as i32 -> sm'.length x is exact when x is a string parameter; it must NOT be applied to .len() on vectors (record_list, token_list, hunk_list: those stay boundary primitives or move to am'.length on typed arrays).")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_11 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 11u32, "2026-10-22T08:49Z", "eoie", "04:49 slice apply-heal-string-length.ps1 (wave E2): the 7 string byte-length helpers (eoie_cli_harness byte_count, eoie_guide/eoie_memory_model/eoie_table_logs/eoie_table_query text_length, eoie_table_measures text_bytes, workspace_primitives byte_len) become sm'.length over their string parameter; only string-typed parameters match, vector .len() macros are untouched. Generated code should be the same .len() call.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_12 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 12u32, "2026-10-22T08:52Z", "eoie", "04:52 slice apply-heal-path-kind.ps1 (wave E2): the 11 Path::new(x).is_dir()/is_file() helpers over a string parameter become file_system.path_buf_is_dir|is_file (file_system.new_path_buf (sm'.to_std_string x)); rule written before applying: this mapping grows each inl call site by about 2 Rust lines (String + PathBuf instead of a borrowed Path), so its guard is 900 lines instead of 950 (skips agile_todo_scan at 946). 8 packages heal on the E tree; the guard reads live sizes at release time.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_13 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 13u32, "2026-10-22T08:57Z", "eoie", "04:57 mapping (3) narrowed: 37 helpers turn an INTEGER-typed parameter into text via Rc::from(x.to_string()); lib sm'.obj_to_string routes non-nominal types to format_debug_real ({:?}), identical to Display for integers but NOT for floats (1.0 vs 1), so the one f64 site is excluded. Slice apply-heal-integer-text.ps1 drafted (matches integer parameter types only, 950 guard) and kept OUT of the waves until a compile confirms the Rust arm a plain integer reaches is {:?} (the nested typecase in sm'_real.spir is not conclusive by reading); the CLI notebooks would catch any printing change.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_14 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 14u32, "2026-10-22T08:58Z", "eoie", "04:58 argv mapping prepared for main's env API (not landed yet): 18 helpers wrap args().nth(i).unwrap_or_default(). nth counts the program name as 0, while env.try_command_line_argument starts at the first user argument, so the body becomes optionm'.default_value empty-string (env.try_command_line_argument (i - 1i32)) and is exact only when i >= 1. No call passes a literal 0; 22 calls pass a computed index (e.g. index + 1i32 while walking flags), which a slice must check by eye (they start at 2 or 3 in the verb parsers) before mapping. Slice to be written when env.spi lands.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_15 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 15u32, "2026-10-22T09:00Z", "main", "2026-10-22 05:00 main: lib env.spi gained command_line_argument_count () : i32, command_line_argument (index : i32) : string, try_command_line_argument (index : i32) : option string (index 0 = first user argument; Rust, F#, TypeScript, Python arms), landed EB6944E216A8EB3A after a 4 of 4 gate on a copy. Decimal parse needs nothing new: convert.try_convert : string -> option u64 already trims and parses on every backend.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_16 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 16u32, "2026-10-22T09:03Z", "eoie", "05:03 main landed env.command_line_argument_count / command_line_argument / try_command_line_argument in tree lib (index 0 = first user argument; gate 4/4 on rust, fsharp, python, typescript). Slice apply-heal-argv.ps1 (wave E2, after path kind): nth(i) helpers -> optionm'.default_value empty (env.try_command_line_argument (i - 1i32)) (all 22 computed call indices start at the verb parsers' offsets 2 or 3, none can be 0); args().count() helpers -> command_line_argument_count () + 1i32; args().skip(2).count() helpers -> user count minus 1 clamped at 0 (skip(2) never goes negative). Dry-run: 31 packages heal, the 3 near-gate crates skip. Risk for E2's preview: workspace_primitives has no crate of its own; its inl argument helpers expand inside consumer crates, near-gate ones included, which the per-package guard cannot see, so the preview must check every consumer. 61 other args() shapes (collect, skip(2).collect, any) remain for a later slice.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_17 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 17u32, "2026-10-22T09:10Z", "eoie", "05:10 fs census (161 fs macros, few repeats): read_to_string(..).unwrap_or_default() in 5+3 packages, create_dir_all(..).is_ok() in 4, write(..).is_ok() in 3, remove_dir_all/remove_file treated as success when the path is gone in 3+3. Lib file_system.read_all_text / write_all_text are NOT drop-in: their Rust arms unwrap (panic on error), while eoie needs total versions (empty text, or a success bool). Lib candidates for main's queue, low priority (about 21 sites): try_read_all_text : string -> option string, and write/create_dir/remove variants returning bool or a result. Until then these stay boundary primitives; the bigger fs wins are per-package boundary consolidation, not lib mapping.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_18 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 18u32, "2026-10-22T18:11Z", "eoie", "2026-10-22 14:11 mapping (3), integer to text, traced through lib source: sm'.obj_to_string x for a plain integer type takes obj_to_string_rust (show_composite_real is false), then sm'_real.spir obj_to_string_rust_real (not char, string or nominal), then format_debug_real's Rust arm (no option, tuple, array, list, seq or record case matches), then format_debug_other_rust, which for prim_type_is emits Rc::<str>::from(format!({:?}, x)). Debug and Display agree for i8..u64, so Rc::from(x.to_string()) -> sm'.obj_to_string x is exact for integer parameters; the f64 site stays excluded ({:?} prints 1.0, Display prints 1). apply-heal-integer-text.ps1 can join a wave after one preview compile confirms the emitted line in a generated .rs.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_19 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 19u32, "2026-10-22T22:10Z", "eoie-lane", "18:10 sizing input (text classification of the 1,502 live .spi lines carrying a host macro; a line can fall in several classes). Text work (Rc wraps, split, trim, replace, lines, lowercase, format) 670; numeric casts and parses 287; filesystem 262; collections (Vec, maps, RefCell, iter) 210; console io 138; env and args 137; process 79; clock 47; regex 31; unclassified 374. Text, numeric and collections (~60%) are not OS surface at all: they are lib/spiral's job (sm', convert, am', listm'), so the heal waves remove them. The real boundary is the fs + env + process + clock + console set (~660 lines, many duplicates across packages), which is what one OS boundary package must offer as named primitives. That is the target size for its first cut, not 1,502.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_20 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 20u32, "2026-10-22T22:11Z", "eoie-lane", "18:11 duplication measured: 491 single-quoted host macros touch an OS surface; with splice names normalized they are 338 distinct bodies. The top repeats are println (27x), argv nth (20x), argv count (15x and 15x with skip 2), eprintln (12x), is_dir (9x), so ~10 named primitives (stdout_line, stderr_line, argument n, argument_count, is_directory, file_text, ...) cover ~120 of the 491 uses at once. Many of the 338 are one-off composite blobs (read + parse + format in one macro), which split into a primitive plus lib code when their package is healed. First cut of the boundary package: the top ~25 bodies by count, then the rest package by package with the heal waves.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_21 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 21u32, "2026-10-23T12:32Z", "eoie", "08:32 scope decision by measurement (no compile): the top repeated OS bodies from the 18:11 count (println 27x, argv nth 20x, argv count 30x, eprintln 12x, is_dir 9x, ~100 uses) all have lib equivalents in the live lib today: console.write_line, console.write_error_line (main's 04:40 landing), env.try_command_line_argument, env.command_line_argument_count, file_system.directory_exists, plus file_exists and read_all_text. The packages healed tonight (eoie_crate_gate, eoie_table_text, eoie_notebook_progress, accept-review) use exactly these with no host macro. So the boundary package's scope shrinks to what lib/spiral lacks: process spawning with creation flags and job objects (eoie_jobs, eoie_job_host: CreateProcessW, parent attribute), process sampling (sysinfo), file times beyond last_write_ms, OS-specific job-object limits. Everything else heals by switching to the lib call package by package (the heal waves), not into a new eoie package. Next concrete cut: name those few primitives in one eoie_os package (one-expression each) and point eoie_job_host's launch at it.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_22 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 22u32, "2026-10-23T13:33Z", "eoie", "09:33 next cut drafted for release L, after start-under-shell: scratch start-under-shell/apply-host-uses-os-launch.ps1 replaces eoie_job_host's own 40-line CreateProcessW block (as HP lands it) with one call into eoie_os_launch::eoie_shell_parented_start, plus the Cargo dependency, so the job host and eoie process start-under-shell share one implementation. Dry run on HP's raw host_run.spi applies. Also verified: host_run.spi formatted by 5e equals the live file, so HP's landing needs no re-format (main's 5e pass found the other 46 eoie files unchanged too).")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_23 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 23u32, "2026-10-24T00:03Z", "eoie", "20:03 eoie: census snapshot of the live tree, post-GE2 landing (eoie standards --census src, 381 packages, 13 s): host-macro 721, wide-host-macro 187, let-block 175, rc-wrap 527, with host macros in 116 of 381 packages. Ranked by host + 3 x wide + 2 x let-block, the boundary work pays most in: eoie_jobs (39/14/11), eoie_cli_harness (24/13/14, test-only), eoie_do_edit (18/12/15, at 976 lines, so it needs a crate split first), rust_std_process (22/8/7, already a boundary-style package to absorb), eoie_do_process (14/9/7), eoie_do_facts, eoie_do_read, eoie_machine_windows (20/4/4), eoie_do_lab, agile_memory_links. Rule for picking the next boundary slice: the highest-ranked package whose crates sit under 950 lines, so eoie_jobs first (its blobs cover process liveness, file reads and env). Its first slice moves the process_alive, parent_pid and relaunched_as blobs into the boundary package's process module, as named one-expression primitives. Baseline kept for the ratchet: next session compares against these totals.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_24 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 24u32, "2026-10-24T00:06Z", "eoie", "20:06 eoie: first eoie_jobs heal written as an eoie do program, the first slice authored that way from the start (scratch heal-jobs-host/heal-jobs-host.do, 5 replace-once rules in one edit block with expect files 1 replacements 5). argument becomes env.try_command_line_argument (index - 1), the same mapping as eoie_do's host. decimal_i32 becomes convert.try_convert with default -1. decimal_text becomes sm'.format. stdout_line and stderr_line become console.write_line and write_error_line. Applied to a copy of the real post-P eoie_jobs: ok files 1 replacements 5 in 4 ms. jobs.rs is at 759 lines, well under the gate. The rest of host.spi (process_alive, parent_pid, container and job-object calls, newest_directories, supervisor_executable) are true OS calls and go to the boundary package. Typecheck tomorrow; it ships in the first release after the train, since HIJK..P all touch eoie_jobs.")
inl note_of_eoie_heal_boundary_25 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY", 25u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 180m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D4 at 30%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl generated_output_modules () = Task ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", "SUPERSEDED by SPIRAL-SHARED-SPECIALIZATIONS (not delivered): Compiler: write one entry's generated output as several files, each under 1,000 lines, so the user's 10-21 rule (1,000 lines per file in every language but Spiral) can hold for generated code: Gleam as granular packages, each with its own gleam.toml and path deps, built in parallel (SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES; module parts of one package were drift, flagged 10-24), Rust as mod files inside one crate, the same for other backends; the split follows the function graph (helpers and specializations grouped by their callers), with a stable order so outputs stay diffable", Story, P1, D6, 100u32, Done, "X-PARALLEL-BUILD,XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH", "compiler,codegen,gleam,rust,gate,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_generated_output_modules () = Planning ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", Minutes 480u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_1 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 1u32, "2026-10-22T03:23Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:23 x: evidence. (1) x's sim Gleam: one entry = 9,093 lines; per-export entries don't help (6 entries = 19,809 lines, 2.2x, each over 1,000 but one; SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES note 7); 60% of a package is anonymous method specializations (110 in xsim_world, 18 of 60+ lines). (2) dir-tree-html's generated dir_tree_html.rs is 2,774 lines (TREE-PAGES-V2). (3) x's Rust crates sit at 909-986 of the 1,000 gate (view 986), held there only by splitting crates by hand. So the 1,000-line rule for generated code needs the compiler to emit modules; entry-level splitting duplicates shared code. Consumers waiting: SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES, X-GATE-ALL-LANGUAGES, TREE-PAGES-V2 (f).")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_2 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 2u32, "2026-10-22T03:34Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:34 x: feasibility measured on the generated file itself (text-only, call graph from fn definitions and name( calls in xsim_frames.gleam): 9,093 lines = 293 fns (8,853 lines) + 240 lines of types, consts and imports; 608 call edges; Tarjan SCCs: 290 components, the largest 2 functions / 280 lines, none over 1,000. So the graph is almost acyclic, and Gleam's no-import-cycles rule is no obstacle: a topological packing gives 14 modules of <= 1,000 lines with types repeated per module, or 10 function modules plus one shared types module. Caveat: function values passed rather than called (closureN references) are not counted as edges; the compiler sees the exact graph.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_3 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 3u32, "2026-10-22T03:47Z", "x", "2026-10-21 23:47 x: executable spec for the Gleam half: x/scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 (SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES note 8) splits generated Gleam after the fact. Types and constructors go to <module>/types; functions are packed by call-graph SCCs, callee-first, into <module>/partN; the entry's exports stay in <module>; each module imports only the names it uses, unqualified. On xsim_frames: 12 modules, largest 957 lines, gleam build 0 errors, cold build about 30% faster (8.7-9.3 s vs 11.6-15.7 s). In the compiler the same split is cheaper (the call graph is exact, including function values), and the Rust half is simpler: modules of one crate may reference each other cyclically, so each part file needs only 'use super::*;' and pub(crate) items, with no SCC ordering. That would also remove x's crate headroom pressure (view 986/1000) and split dir_tree_html.rs (2,774).")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_4 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 4u32, "2026-10-22T06:41Z", "main", "02:41 main: design grounding from the compiler code (read only, no build). (1) The core already emits several files per build. BuildOk carries a list of {code; file_extension}, and the supervisor's BuildFile handler (spiral_compiler.fs ~187068) writes each as <source base><extension> through write_output_atomically_async, answering only after every file is written; CppCuda (4 files) and PythonCuda (2) use it today. (2) Every single-file backend goes through one function, `build codegen backend file_extension` (~184577: codegen, then UnusedGlobals.prune, then one GeneratedFile). That is the hook: after prune, a GeneratedOutputSplit stage for Rust and Gleam returns [main] or [main; main_part1; ...] by extension suffix (e.g. '_part1.rs'), so no supervisor change is needed. (3) The host is the real gap: compileOne (tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs ~498-622) knows multi-file outputs only through a STATIC multiFileSuffixes table (CppCuda, PythonCuda), waits for those suffixes to land and copies them to the output base, and the compile cache skips multi-file backends. A variable part count needs the core to write a manifest <base>.spiral-parts (one suffix per line) before answering; the host reads it in place of the static table and copies or cleans parts (stale main_partN files from a previous build must be deleted). (4) Language side: Rust parts become `#[path = \"main_part1.rs\"] mod part1;` with `use super::*;` and pub(crate) items, so cycles need no ordering (x note 23:47). Gleam parts are modules of the same package with x's SCC callee-first packing (x/scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 is the executable spec); a part's imports are only the names it uses. (5) Gate: split only above 1,000 lines, so every matrix row under the gate stays byte-identical, and the review must show only rows over 1,000 lines changing. Consumers: Cells (copies one output), x sim, dir_tree_html.rs (2,774 lines), x crates near 986/1,000.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_5 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 5u32, "2026-10-22T07:22Z", "main", "03:22 main: two findings that shape the Rust half. (1) A split by top-level items cannot shrink a fully inlined residual. The largest sample main.rs (mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/runtime_native, 4,638 lines) is one enum plus spiral_main spanning lines 9-4634, straight-line lets with no nested fns, so module parts would move nothing. The split helps method-rich outputs (x's sim: 293 fns, the largest SCC 280 lines). Fully inlined bodies need join points in the source (a `let` instead of an `inl`; eoie found the same lever, 'union reflection lookups must be let, not inl') or a later codegen pass that outlines long straight-line runs into helper fns. The gate's per-file report should say which case a file over 1,000 lines is. (2) Rust module mechanics, by Rust's privacy rules: each part is a child module, `#[path = \"main_part1.rs\"] mod part1;`, opening with `use super::*;`. A child sees its ancestors' private items and their private `use` imports through the glob, as `mod tests { use super::*; }` does. Items moved into a part become `pub(crate)` (a column-0 fn/struct/enum/type/const/static/impl-free item; impls need no visibility and can live in any part), and the root adds `pub(crate) use part1::*;`, so the root and the sibling parts see them. Cycles between parts are fine (no ordering), crate attributes (#![...]) stay in the root, and codegenRust emits no macro_rules (0 in the core), so nothing depends on textual order.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_6 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 6u32, "2026-10-22T07:23Z", "main", "03:23 main: note 5's module mechanics VERIFIED with rustc (edition 2021, a 3-file crate in TEMP, compile window): the root has a private 'use std::rc::Rc', a private fn root_helper, '#[path = ...] mod part1/part2' and 'pub(crate) use partN::*'. part1 calls root_helper and uses Rc through 'use super::*' and calls a part2 fn; part2 calls part1 back (a cycle); the root calls part2. It compiles clean and exits 6 as computed. So moved items need only 'pub(crate)'; root-private items and imports stay where they are.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_7 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 7u32, "2026-10-22T07:52Z", "main", "03:52 main: core half drafted and the algorithm validated without a compiler build. Patch: %TEMP%\\rust-module-split\\patch.ps1 (dry run by default; 3 anchors, each matching once on the tree and PACK-AFTER-NIGHT cores). It adds module RustModuleSplit in the shared CodegenUtils section next to UnusedGlobals, and edits both sides of the BuildFile pair (gpt5parallel ~184578, single-flight ~187286) so pair-drift stays clean. Rules: split only above SPIRAL_RUST_SPLIT_LINES (default 1000). Move only free functions ('fn' / 'pub fn' heads, never 'fn main'), because Rust struct fields and impl methods are private to their module and the root could not reach them from a part. Types, impls, thread_local!, use lines and crate attributes stay in the root. Moved fns get pub(crate); parts open with 'use super::*;' and are packed in order up to the limit; the root declares '#[path = \"<base>_partN.rs\"] mod partN; pub(crate) use partN::*;' where the first moved fn was. Validation (simulate.ps1, a faithful PowerShell port; rustc -O in a compile window): mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_bench (2,051 lines, 39 fns) becomes a 48-line root plus parts of 917/764/331 lines, compiles, exits 0 with stdout identical to the unsplit build; rust_static_closure_chain at a forced 100-line limit (6 parts) compiles and exits 40 like the original. REQUIRED host half (not drafted): BuildFile fills its IVar with every file's code joined by newlines, and for single-file backends compileOne (host Program.fs ~589-613) awaits or writes THAT text to the output path, so a split Rust main.rs would be overwritten by the concatenation. Fix: the core also emits a '.spiral-parts' manifest (one suffix per line) when it splits; compileOne reads it and takes the multi-file branch with those suffixes (copy every part to the output base, skip the compile cache), and deletes stale <base>_partN.rs and manifest files when an output is not split.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_8 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 8u32, "2026-10-22T07:53Z", "main", "03:53 main: the host half is drafted too, so the Rust split is complete on paper. Core patch: a split also emits '<base>.spiral-parts' (one part suffix per line). Host patch %TEMP%\\rust-module-split\\host-patch.ps1 (dry run; 2 anchors, each matching once on the tree's Program.fs). In compileOne, a fresh manifest (written during this build) routes the output into the existing multi-file branch with suffixes [.rs; parts; .spiral-parts], which waits for every part to land and copies them to the output base. The compile cache skips split outputs (its text would be the concatenation). When an output stops being split, a stale manifest at the source base and at the output base deletes exactly the files it lists, never a name pattern, so a user's own main_partN.rs is never touched. Gate plan for its pack: SPIRAL_RUST_SPLIT_LINES=100 over the Rust matrix rows, so most outputs split and every row keeps its native verdict; default limit: only outputs over 1,000 lines change; then x's crates and dir_tree_html.rs.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_9 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 9u32, "2026-10-22T08:10Z", "main", "04:10 main: measured on today's generated dice_contract.rs (3,106 lines, 129 top-level fns; largest closure7 451, method34 313, method29 237, closure76 231, closure4 197 lines). GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES' Rust split (simulate.ps1, limit 1000) gives a 126-line root plus parts of 895/980/873/244 lines: every file under the gate, with no hand-split crates. Not rustc-checked, because the contract needs near-sdk; the two std-only samples compiled and behaved identically (GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES note 7). A third consumer besides x's crates and dir_tree_html.rs.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_10 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 10u32, "2026-10-22T08:16Z", "main", "04:16 main: third rustc proof, and the first on a real tool. x's renderer dir_tree_html.rs (2,774 lines, 30 fns, std only) splits into a 33-line root plus parts of 993/682/378/700 lines. Whole and split both build with rustc --edition 2024 -O, and running each on apps/compiler/tmp/formatter gives byte-identical HTML (hash BC560C04A05C, 4,749 bytes, exit 0). TREE-PAGES-V2 (f) can use the compiler split instead of a hand crate split.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_11 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 11u32, "2026-10-22T11:49Z", "x", "2026-10-22 07:49 x: review of x/scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 as the spec for the Gleam half (read only). (1) Its edges over-approximate: any word that names a top-level fn counts as a call, which also covers function values passed rather than called. Parts are packed in Tarjan output order (callee-first) with every edge counted, so imports only point to earlier parts, types, or from the root to parts, and Gleam's no-import-cycle rule can't break. The compiler's exact graph keeps this as long as function-value references count as edges. (2) Exports and their transitive callers stay in the root, so part code never imports the root. (3) A false edge (a local variable or string word equal to a fn name) costs at most an unused-import warning. (4) Gap: a const whose value references a fn is not an edge; consts go to types.gleam, which imports no fn module. (5) Rust lesson from x's splitter, a PowerShell switch -Regex fall-through: an item matched by two kind rules was written twice ('fn main' in root and part, hidden by glob-import shadowing). The compiler split should assert that every top-level item lands in exactly one file (x checks non-blank line conservation).")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_12 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 12u32, "2026-10-23T12:35Z", "main", "2026-10-23 08:35 main: consumers waiting on this (each over 1,000 generated lines): dir-tree-html dir_tree_html.rs 2,774 (TREE-PAGES-V2 step f), dice ui 16,281 and alphabet documents 15,315 (EOIE-CRATE-GATE reports them OVER), and x's client crates (X-RUST-MODULES, interim script). The interim splitter's tested shape is root = types, uses and pub fns; parts = private fns with 'use super::*' and pub(crate); every non-blank line kept once. Its cargo check and byte-identical render on dir-tree-html are the acceptance test for the compiler version.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_13 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 13u32, "2026-10-23T12:52Z", "main", "2026-10-23 08:52 main: correction to my 08:35 note. The Rust half already exists in the pending pack's core: module RustModuleSplit (shared section; limit SPIRAL_RUST_SPLIT_LINES, default 1,000) is called from build for every .rs output in both cores. It moves private fns into <stem>_partN.rs ('use super::*', pub(crate)), adds '#[path] mod partN; pub(crate) use partN::*;' to the root, and writes a .spiral-parts manifest, which the host (Program.fs splitParts) copies beside the output and later cleans up. The installed tree core (25D2BE7C) lacks it (0 matches), which is why dice ui/src/main.rs (16,281 lines, generated 10-19) and alphabet apps/documents/documents.rs are still single files. Rule: after the 18AC install, regenerate those two tracked outputs with the installed compiler, check that every file is <= 1,000 lines with EOIE-CRATE-GATE, and the user commits the parts. dir-tree-html's 2,774-line output splits the same way, so TREE-PAGES-V2 (f) closes on regeneration. Left for this item: the Gleam half (modules of one package, callee-first packing, consts in types).")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_14 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 14u32, "2026-10-23T13:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:08 main: Gleam half implemented and proven, staged as pack-next7. (1) Core: %TEMP%\\pack-next7\\gleam-module-split.ps1 adds module GleamModuleSplit beside RustModuleSplit, an F# port of x's scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 (the spec named in note 4). splitWith takes a comparer, roots, module names and code. Byte check: with culture order and x's nested names it is byte-equal to the script on all 4 modules of both 1,000+ line matrix outputs (mega_brzozowski_derivatives inventory_closed_dfa_runtime 1,881 lines and runtime_native 1,362). The core uses ordinal order and flat sibling modules (<base>_types, <base>_partN), so the host's copy-beside-output works; roots are main plus the !!!!Export names in the entry .spi, as in x's script; SPIRAL_GLEAM_MODULE_PREFIX handles nested module paths; limit SPIRAL_GLEAM_SPLIT_LINES, default 1,000. Hooked in both cores' build next to the .rs split, and the patched core parses under both defines. Splits: 1,881 -> 110 + 86 + 803 + 937; 1,362 -> 135 + 57 + 952 + 258. (2) Plumbing: %TEMP%\\pack-next7\\gleam-parts-plumbing.ps1. Host Program.fs: the parts manifest names the output's own extension, not just .rs, and stale-part cleanup also runs for Gleam. test.ps1: Copy-GleamProgram copies the parts listed in .spiral-parts and renames part imports to the batch row module, both Gleam paths use it, a part-file build error is attributed to its row, and the native memo signature covers it (Gleam rows rerun once). 9 anchors match. (3) End-to-end in the matrix's Gleam shim: split and unsplit builds give equal exit and stdout on both samples, with the same warning count (10/10, 12/12, so no unused-import warnings), and the batch-renamed copy (spiral_row_0001_part1/2/types) builds. Review: next codegen pack after tibia's block. Expected residual changes: only those 2 samples' main.gleam (now a root + 3 new files each), with every Gleam row agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_15 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 15u32, "2026-10-25T00:06Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:06 main: the Gleam half was drift, and it entered here. Note 4 took x's scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 as the spec; that script closed SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES on 10-22 against the 1,000-line gate, while the user had asked for proper parallel-buildable packages with their own toml. x reopened SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES and made XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH P1 (packages vs parts, packages win unless they need compiler work). The title now names the asked-for shape. There is also a real bug: GleamModuleSplit.usedImports (pack 5i, line 162530) keeps an import in a part only when the part mentions '<last path segment>.'. lib emits 'import gleam/dynamic.{type Dynamic}' (sm'.spi last_index_of_gleam), so that import is dropped and a part using Dynamic unqualified fails to compile, the same break as X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS. The matrix missed it because no Gleam row over 1,000 lines reaches that lib function. Rule, applied: patching the filter would ship the drifted shape, so the Gleam split comes out of the install. %TEMP%\\pack-next5j\\no-gleam-parts.ps1 removes both build call sites (anchors 1/1, dry run clean). Gleam output stays one file and reports OVER honestly, and the Rust split stays. The Gleam half resumes with the benchmark's shape. If that is packages, a later pack deletes the module; if parts, it returns with import handling for '.{...}' and 'as' forms plus a regression sample.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_16 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 16u32, "2026-10-25T00:18Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:18 main: the premise is superseded (user 10-24 20:1x via x: splits belong at codegen as a granular crate/package graph, never post-hoc parts; 'verify if we really need this custom automatic splitting thing so we don't keep dead code'). Measured: no installed compiler writes parts (RustModuleSplit/GleamModuleSplit count 0 in the tree core; they exist only in pack 5i), so nothing regresses if both go. The consumers over 1,000 lines (dice ui 16k, alphabet documents 15k, dir_tree_html 2.7k, the mega samples) are already reported OVER today, and all have the same cause: one entry with everything inlined. Pack 5j drafted: %TEMP%\\pack-next5j\\no-output-parts.ps1 removes both split modules (253 lines, headings checked) and both build branches, and leaves no reference to the splits or .spiral-parts in the core. host-no-parts.ps1 removes the host's manifest reader and stale-parts cleanup (14 lines, apps/compiler/tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs) and leaves multiFile unchanged. Both dry-run clean. Paused behind X-PARALLEL-BUILD (codegen-level crate/package graph) and XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH; the remedy for big outputs is granular entries at the source.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_17 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 17u32, "2026-10-25T00:21Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:21 main correction to note 16 (found by x): 'nothing depends on the split' was wrong. The tree core counts 0, but the matrix's committed outputs depend on it: 29 tracked part and manifest files in 6 samples (user commit 0ec501fb, 10-24 05:02), written by a candidate core that had the split. 5j's install step now re-accepts those rows as single files and deletes the 29 files by the samples rule (COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS note 15, corrected). My check counted references in the compiler source and never asked git what the outputs held; the outputs of a compiler change are tracked files too.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_18 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 18u32, "2026-10-25T00:30Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:30 main: folded into SPIRAL-SHARED-SPECIALIZATIONS (x), so one record holds the fact. x's research (i574n.github/docs/research/parallel-builds-2026-10-24.md) contradicts this item's 20:0x title. Gleam 1.19 compiles packages one at a time: verified in project_compiler.rs, where compile_dependencies() is a plain 'for name in sequence' over toposort_deps with no threads. Only the BEAM step is parallel, across the modules of one package. So many modules in one package is Gleam's parallel shape, and every extra package is a barrier. Rust is the opposite: crates grouped by sharing unit, with modules inside. The successor lays out one target module per Spiral source module at codegen and emits shared specializations once. The post-hoc splitters are removed by pack 5j; their install steps live in COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS notes 15-16. The title is marked SUPERSEDED because eoie agile has no superseded/cancelled status (reported to eoie), and Done here does not mean delivered.")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_19 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 19u32, "2026-10-25T00:46Z", "x", "10-24 20:46 x: the research file note 18 cites (i574n.github/docs/research/parallel-builds-2026-10-24.md) is deleted (user: no new docs dirs in repos). Its sources are now X-PARALLEL-BUILD notes 5 (Rust) and 6 (Gleam and other generators).")
inl note_of_generated_output_modules_20 () = Note ("GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES", 20u32, "2026-10-25T00:53Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:53 main groom, lessons: (1) 'nobody uses it' needs two checks, references in the source AND tracked outputs (git ls-files): 29 committed sample outputs depended on a split no installed compiler had. (2) A build tool's parallel unit differs per backend: Gleam 1.19 compiles packages sequentially (project_compiler.rs compile_dependencies, a plain for over toposort_deps), Rust parallelizes crates. 'Packages' only means parallel for Rust. (3) Patching a drifted design's bug (the import filter) would have hardened the design; removing it waits for the measured shape instead.")
