inl note_of_lib_format_fixed_more_arms_10 () = Note ("LIB-FORMAT-FIXED-MORE-ARMS", 10u32, "2026-10-22T22:41Z", "tibia-lane", "LANDED 18:40 (eoie F end 18:39; main OK): lib_land.ps1 moved lib/spiral/sm prime 1DF55E5E16C9BD6F -> 47D67F79BEE7DEC5 (candidate 3: format_fixed Gleam and Zig arms call format_fixed_digits; both format_fixed tests run on gleam and zig too; gate 48/48, 0 unsupported). With the 17:13 landing, format_fixed now has exact arms for C, Gleam and Zig and native arms for F#, Lua, Python, Rust and TypeScript. Item done.")
inl eoie_window_wait_clear () = Task ("EOIE-WINDOW-WAIT-CLEAR", "eoie window wait-clear <class,...> [--settle <n>] [--max <min>]: block until no window of the given classes is held or waiting for n samples in a row, then print the list; and job status shows the windows held during a job (a contended flag), so agents stop writing their own window-sampling loops", Story, P3, D2, 60u32, Active, "", "eoie,gate,windows", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_window_wait_clear () = Planning ("EOIE-WINDOW-WAIT-CLEAR", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_window_wait_clear_1 () = Note ("EOIE-WINDOW-WAIT-CLEAR", 1u32, "2026-10-22T18:02Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:02 x: filed from two scratch scripts I wrote today instead of verbs (eoie-first). (1) block_end_watch.ps1 samples 'eoie window list' every 90 s until no live-gui or compiler-install window has been held or waiting for 2 samples in a row, so a background shell notifies the agent when a block ends. A block is often several live-gui windows back to back, hence the settle count. (2) run8_watch.ps1 samples 'eoie window list' every 5 min during a heavy job to classify the run as quiet or contended (any compile window held), because timing rules (X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED notes 27-28: --timeout thresholds of 135 s quiet vs 150 s contended) depend on it, and eoie keeps no window history. Proposed: 'window wait-clear' as in the title (exit 0 when clear, 1 at --max), and an append-only window event log (request, grant, release with timestamps) from which 'job status' derives windows_overlapping=<kinds:count>. Both are generic: no lane or project names. Until it ships, the scratch scripts stay in the session scratchpad only.")
inl note_of_eoie_window_wait_clear_2 () = Note ("EOIE-WINDOW-WAIT-CLEAR", 2u32, "2026-10-22T21:27Z", "eoie-lane", "17:27 pure model drafted and tested (scratch f5/src/eoie_window_settle_model, HostFree + LibReferenced), from x's two watcher scripts. settled forall sample. folds any sample_series with a busy predicate; sample_busy over window rows and watched classes is the window-list instance. settle_terms {clear_samples_needed; max_samples}, verdicts KeepWaiting | ClearNow | GaveUp, and run_contention Quiet | Contended busy_samples comes from the same pass, which is the 'contended flag' for job status. Tests: a one-sample gap between back-to-back live-gui blocks is not the end; a compile window does not count for a block watch; the watch gives up at its limit and reports Contended 3; a live window does not contend with a compile-only watch. Cells: 2/2 pass after annotating the recursive forall (ETY-ExpectedAnnotation on the first try); standards 0 findings. Next (compile release): 'eoie window wait-clear <class,...> [--settle n] [--max min]' sampling window list rows through this fold, and job status reading the contention.")
inl note_of_eoie_window_wait_clear_3 () = Note ("EOIE-WINDOW-WAIT-CLEAR", 3u32, "2026-10-23T06:17Z", "eoie", "02:17 eoie: verb drafted for release K (scratch window-wait/apply-window-wait.ps1, chained after the event log in apply-rK; dry run clean on the post-K tree). New crate eoie_window_wait (HostFree, LibReferenced, like eoie_window_claim): settle.spi is the Cells-checked model, and wait.spi samples live_rows every --every seconds (default 30), counts a sample busy when any row's class is in the comma list, and runs observed/verdict_of until the clear streak reaches --settle (default 2, exit 0) or --max minutes pass (default 60, exit 1). Unknown classes are refused. The receipt carries samples and quiet or contended busy_samples: x's contended flag for a heavy run. Wired into window_run's dispatch and usage, with eoie_machine_windows' Cargo edge and the workspace member. wait_cli tests a block that ends during the wait (busy, then cleared) and the unknown-class refusal. job status reading the contended flag from the event log comes after the log ships (EOIE-WINDOW-LIVE-QUEUE note 6).")
inl cells_memo_tool_identity () = Task ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", "Cells memo key ignores the zig (and gleam/erl/node) toolchain: a cell memoized under one Zig is a hit under another, so SPIRAL_ZIG changes and Zig bumps can report passes that never ran", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,memo,zig,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_memo_tool_identity () = Planning ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_1 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 1u32, "2026-10-22T18:18Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:18 x: found while checking x's CI against local runs (read-only). x CI and every sibling pin mlugg/setup-zig 0.17.0. Locally the scoop shim 'zig' points at zig-dev 0.18.0-dev.146 and SPIRAL_ZIG is unset, so every local ///> zig cell runs 0.18-dev (known and intended for the matrix, ZIG-FLOAT-FORMAT note 3). memo.ex keys entries with Keyword.take(opts, @tool_opts), and @tool_opts = [:print_code, :plot, :render_plot, :spiral, :python, :lua, :pwsh, :cc, :dcc, :rustc_bin] has no :zig. Toolchain.zig reads opts[:zig], then SPIRAL_ZIG, then PATH, outside the key. Gleam/erl and node are not in it either. Effect: an entry written by a zig-dev run is a hit under SPIRAL_ZIG=0.17.0 or after a Zig bump, so the cell is reported passed without running on that Zig, the false-green case for any CI-parity or toolchain-bump check. Fix direction: put the resolved executable path + version of each target's tool into the key (zig, gleam, erl, node, as already done for rustc_bin/cc), resolved the way Toolchain resolves them. Test: same cell, two SPIRAL_ZIG values -> two misses. Until then, x's CI-parity run uses SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 (X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED).")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_2 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 2u32, "2026-10-22T18:35Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:35 x: correction to note 1. rustc_bin and cc are keyed only when passed as opts. A rustc chosen through SPIRAL_RUSTC or PATH (Toolchain.rustc), or a cc through SPIRAL_CC or PATH, is not in the key either. This matters now: x's scripts/common.ps1 sets SPIRAL_RUSTC to the repo's rust-toolchain rustc (X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED note 34), so memo entries written by the earlier default rustc (nightly 2025-05-09) would hit under the new one. The fix direction covers every tool Toolchain resolves: key the resolved path + version, whatever the source (opts, env or PATH).")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_3 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 3u32, "2026-10-22T18:46Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:46 main: fix staged as Cells pack step 12 (%TEMP%\\cells-pack\\memo_tool_identity.py, 3 anchors, dry-applies on today's tree after steps 1-11). Memo.tool_identity(opts) replaces Keyword.take(opts, @tool_opts) in both the run context and code_result: the old opts part plus, for each of rustc, cc, zig, gleam, erl, bun, python, lua, wgsl_run, naga and pwsh, the executable Toolchain itself resolves (opts, then env, then PATH), stamped as {real path, size, mtime}. A scoop shim is followed through its .shim path= line, the same stamp test.ps1's native memo uses; spawning zig version on every key would cost a process per tool per run. Test memo_tool_identity_test.exs: two SPIRAL_ZIG files give two identities; an explicit zig: option wins over env; one shim retargeted gives a new identity. The key shape changes, so every existing entry misses once after the pack lands. It rides the Cells pack run after x's run 8 and my next3 review.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_4 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 4u32, "2026-10-22T19:22Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:22 main: related hardening from x's run 8 (x 15:2x): Toolchain's rustup_proxy? decides by a path substring (/rustup/), so a direct rustc.exe under scoop's persist\\rustup got +nightly-2026-10-18 prepended, which rustc then read as a filename. Fix for the next Cells pack: rustup_proxy? checks that the file is rustup's proxy (Path.basename is rustc or rustc.exe AND a sibling rustup(.exe) or a .cargo\\bin parent), not a substring of the path. Test: a fake rustc.exe under a persist\\rustup\\toolchains\\x\\bin path is NOT a proxy; one in .cargo\\bin next to rustup.exe is.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_5 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 5u32, "2026-10-22T19:27Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:27 main: rustup_proxy? hardening staged as Cells pack step 13 (%TEMP%\\cells-pack\\rustup_proxy.py plus rustup_proxy_test.exs). A rustc is a proxy when it is under /.cargo/bin/ or has rustup(.exe) beside it; the loose /rustup/ substring is gone, so x's persist\\rustup\\.rustup\\toolchains\\...\\bin\\rustc.exe runs as itself. The existing cells_test case (scoop .cargo\\bin path, no files on disk) keeps passing through the .cargo/bin rule. All 13 steps dry-apply on today's tree.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_6 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 6u32, "2026-10-22T19:41Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:41 x: concrete fix site. cells.ex run_builder puts List.wrap(Targets.tool_stamp(builder, opts)) into the code-result memo key, and targets.ex already has the mechanism: tool_stamp(%{tool: :wgsl}, opts) stamps wgsl_run and naga with file_stamp (path, size, mtime). Every other target falls into 'def tool_stamp(_builder, _opts), do: nil'. Fix: add clauses for :zig (Toolchain.zig), :rust (Toolchain.rustc plus the +toolchain argument rustc_args would add, since the proxy's file is identical across toolchains), :gleam and :erl (Toolchain.gleam/erl). Test: the same cell with two SPIRAL_ZIG values gives two misses.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_7 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 7u32, "2026-10-22T20:11Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:11 main: step 12 widened to every tool Toolchain resolves. dcc, fpc, dotnet (F# cells), spiral and plot join the stamped list, and lean is keyed as {stamped path, its +toolchain args}, since Toolchain.lean returns both and the args decide which Lean runs. workspace, package_dir and compiler_dll were already in the key through the compiler binaries and the core tree print. All resolvers are file lookups (no process spawned per key). The pack still dry-applies 14/14.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_8 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 8u32, "2026-10-23T00:38Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:38 x: correction to note 2's example. x's scripts/common.ps1 no longer sets SPIRAL_RUSTC (reverted 15:20, X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED note 44): local Rust cells run the PATH rustup proxy with Cells' own +nightly-2026-10-18, as CI does. Note 2's general point still holds: a tool picked by env or PATH must be in the key, as step 12 now does. For Rust, note 6's caution stays: the proxy's file stamp is the same for every toolchain, so the +toolchain argument must be part of the key.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_9 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 9u32, "2026-10-24T06:24Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:24 main: landed in apps/cells with the Cells pack (CELLS-PACK-NEXT, job main-cells-pack-land, exit 0). Done once the post-block suite run on the tree shows no new red.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_tool_identity_10 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY", 10u32, "2026-10-24T23:57Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:57 main groom: done by this item's own rule (no new red in the post-block suite run on the tree). Job job-1792852828683-main-serial-cells-suite-tree-2 (10:40-11:55, tree after the 02:23 Cells pack land) had 3 failures, all from before the pack. (1, 2) Spiral.Cells.DocumentTest lib round trips: the sm' orphan .spir headings, red at HEAD, fixed by LIB-SM-ROUNDTRIP at 17:47. (3) CellsLiveTest 'a print with no value still runs': RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG, which failed on the tree and in every pack before the land; its fix is in pack 5i.")
inl cells_timeout_headroom () = Task ("CELLS-TIMEOUT-HEADROOM", "Cells reports each notebook's timeout headroom (slowest cell ms vs the effective per-cell budget) in the spiral-tests file line, with a warn threshold, so repos stop keeping --timeout lists by hand", Story, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,timeout,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_timeout_headroom () = Planning ("CELLS-TIMEOUT-HEADROOM", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_timeout_headroom_1 () = Note ("CELLS-TIMEOUT-HEADROOM", 1u32, "2026-10-22T18:25Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:25 x: filed from X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED notes 8, 15-16 and 27-29. x keeps a per-notebook '--timeout 600000' list by hand. After each full run, a scratch script reads every .ipynb's duration_ms, takes the slowest cell, and applies thresholds (join at >= 135 s quiet / 150 s contended, leave below 100 s twice). Cells already has every input: each test's ms per target and the timeout that applied to it. Proposed generic feature: the 'spiral-tests file' JSON line gains 'slowest_ms' and 'timeout_ms' (the effective per-cell budget), plus 'headroom' = slowest/timeout. run_notebook prints one warning line when headroom passes a threshold (env SPIRAL_CELLS_HEADROOM_WARN, default 0.5). Any repo's build can then gate or log it with no per-repo script, and the --timeout choice stays in the source file's ///- line. x then retires its scratch revisit script and keeps only the rule in its item.")
inl note_of_cells_timeout_headroom_2 () = Note ("CELLS-TIMEOUT-HEADROOM", 2u32, "2026-10-22T18:47Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:47 main: design read from notebook.ex (no edit yet). The data is all there but not joined. Each test row's targets carry ms (target_rows, 232). The effective budget is computed only at dispatch (dispatch_spiral, 924: opts[:timeout] || positive_int(step.timeout) || @default_timeout 300000), and the report rows (report/3, 169) never see it. Plan: (1) evaluate/2 builds budgets = %{test.index => budget} with the same rule, from opts and the step's timeout; (2) report/3 puts \"timeout_ms\" on each test row and \"slowest_ms\" = max target ms; (3) summary_lines adds per file \"slowest_ms\", \"timeout_ms\" (the slowest test's budget) and \"headroom\" = slowest/timeout rounded to 2 places; (4) run_notebook prints one \"spiral-tests headroom\" warning line per file over SPIRAL_CELLS_HEADROOM_WARN (default 0.5). ci_gate.ps1 and lib_gate.ps1 parse the file line as JSON, so extra keys are harmless. Test: a notebook with a --timeout 1000 cell sleeping about 600 ms reports headroom >= 0.5 and the warning line. It rides the Cells pack after this one; P3.")
inl note_of_cells_timeout_headroom_3 () = Note ("CELLS-TIMEOUT-HEADROOM", 3u32, "2026-10-22T20:28Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:28 main: implemented as Cells pack step 15 (%TEMP%\\cells-pack\\timeout_headroom.py, 7 anchors, plus timeout_headroom_test.exs; dry-applies after steps 1-14). execute computes each spiral step's effective budget with dispatch's rule (opts timeout, else the cell's --timeout, else 300 s) BEFORE memo recall, so recalled cells keep theirs; ran carries :budgets. report rows gain timeout_ms and slowest_ms (the max target ms). The spiral-tests file line gains slowest_ms, timeout_ms and headroom from its tightest row (slowest/budget, 2 places), and a file at or above SPIRAL_CELLS_HEADROOM_WARN (default 0.5) adds one spiral-tests headroom line. ci_gate.ps1 and lib_gate.ps1 match only the file/total prefixes, and notebook_report_test's reports carry no budgets, so their [file, total] patterns still hold. Tests: 30 s of 300 s gives headroom 0.1 and no warning; 240 s gives 0.8 and a warning line.")
inl note_of_cells_timeout_headroom_4 () = Note ("CELLS-TIMEOUT-HEADROOM", 4u32, "2026-10-24T06:24Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:24 main: landed in apps/cells with the Cells pack (CELLS-PACK-NEXT, job main-cells-pack-land, exit 0). Done once the post-block suite run on the tree shows no new red.")
inl note_of_cells_timeout_headroom_5 () = Note ("CELLS-TIMEOUT-HEADROOM", 5u32, "2026-10-24T23:57Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:57 main groom: done by this item's own rule (no new red in the post-block suite run on the tree). Job job-1792852828683-main-serial-cells-suite-tree-2 (10:40-11:55, tree after the 02:23 Cells pack land) had 3 failures, all from before the pack. (1, 2) Spiral.Cells.DocumentTest lib round trips: the sm' orphan .spir headings, red at HEAD, fixed by LIB-SM-ROUNDTRIP at 17:47. (3) CellsLiveTest 'a print with no value still runs': RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG, which failed on the tree and in every pack before the land; its fix is in pack 5i.")
inl c_output_blank_lines () = Task ("C-OUTPUT-BLANK-LINES", "C backend emits no indentation-only lines: 45,538 of 138,961 lines (32.8%) across 507 samples/*/main.c are whitespace-only, every other backend has 0", TaskKind, P1, D1, 60u32, Active, "PACK-NEXT-4", "compiler,c,codegen,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_c_output_blank_lines () = Planning ("C-OUTPUT-BLANK-LINES", Minutes 30u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_c_output_blank_lines_1 () = Note ("C-OUTPUT-BLANK-LINES", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:15Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:15 main: cause found by code read. In CodegenC (core 174375), line' x s = line x (String.concat \" \" s) emits indentation plus an empty string whenever the statement group is empty: the refcount increments and decrements per bind (refc_varc incr/decr at 174633, two calls per statement, hence the paired blank lines), and empty decl_vars. The module already defines lineC, which skips \"\", but line' never used it. Fix staged: %TEMP%\\pack-next4\\c-blank-lines.ps1 routes line' through lineC (1 anchor, matches once on the next3 core). CodegenCpp has its own line' (175586), left alone (an upstream-mapped section, 16 rows). Pack PACK-NEXT-4, after next3's accept; rule fixed now: every C residual change is whitespace-only (git diff --ignore-blank-lines --ignore-space-at-eol is empty for each changed main.c), no other backend changes, DISAGREE 0, and native results equal the baseline row for row.")
inl note_of_c_output_blank_lines_2 () = Note ("C-OUTPUT-BLANK-LINES", 2u32, "2026-10-23T23:28Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:28 main: status fixed. The change is implemented and staged in PACK-NEXT-4, so it is Active, not Planned. The next4 composite is decided by review-3, requeued in %TEMP%\\cells-pack\\requeue-after-block.ps1 chain B after recompose-stack; this item's review rule (note 1) is applied there.")
inl zig_prelude_on_demand () = Task ("ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND", "Zig output emits only the prelude helpers it uses: today every main.zig carries the whole fixed prelude (~30% of the 121,843 Zig lines across 467 samples)", TaskKind, P2, D2, 60u32, Active, "PACK-NEXT-4", "compiler,zig,codegen,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_zig_prelude_on_demand () = Planning ("ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND", Minutes 60u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_zig_prelude_on_demand_1 () = Note ("ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:28Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:28 main: measured on the samples tree: about 36,957 of 121,843 Zig lines come before the first program item. The prelude (spiralWrite with the kernel32 externs, the panic handler, print/flush buffers, spiralFloatText, spiralPrintAny, spiralFail, spiralConv, spiralConcat, spiralStringSlice, spiralIndex, spiralNewArray, spiralCreate) is one multi-item text, which UnusedGlobals.prune keeps by design (it only drops single-item globals). Zig analyzes only referenced functions, so this is output size and readability, not compile time. Design: emit each helper as its own single-item global (fn name(...) text), so the existing prune drops unreferenced ones, transitively (spiralFail needs spiralFlush and spiralWrite); keep the panic override and the arena/gpa vars whenever any allocating helper survives. The same approach as Gleam's and Wasm's on-demand preludes. Rule: Zig residuals change only by dropped prelude lines (each changed main.zig minus its prelude equals the old one minus its prelude), every Zig row's native result is unchanged, and DISAGREE 0.")
inl note_of_zig_prelude_on_demand_2 () = Note ("ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND", 2u32, "2026-10-22T19:39Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:39 main: patch staged in PACK-NEXT-4 (%TEMP%\\pack-next4\\zig-prelude-on-demand.ps1, 3 anchors, each once on the next3 core). zigUsedPrelude splits zigPrelude into top-level chunks and drops every fn chunk whose name no longer occurs in the program body or in another kept chunk, iterating to a fixpoint. Vars, externs and the panic override always stay (they keep spiralPanic, spiralFlush and spiralWrite alive). codegenZig now builds the body first and returns zigUsedPrelude body + body. A Python mirror over the 467 samples/*/main.zig (the prelude is verbatim at the start of all 467): 121,843 -> 92,192 lines (24.3% fewer). Dropped helpers by file count: spiralPrint, spiralStringSlice, spiralPrintAny and spiralFloatText 459 each, spiralConv and spiralConcat 453, spiralFail 446, spiralNewArray 367, spiralIndex 349, spiralCreate 248. Rule as filed (only dropped prelude lines differ, native results unchanged, DISAGREE 0); the mirror script is scratch zig_prelude_mirror.py.")
inl note_of_zig_prelude_on_demand_3 () = Note ("ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND", 3u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:17 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 60 by the rule 'implemented and staged in a pack or queued gate, awaiting review'. Where: pack-next4 patch, reviewed by review-3.")
inl cells_unit_double_compile () = Task ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", "Cells may compile every unit-ending cell (all --test cells ending in _assert_eq) twice per target: wrap's generated i32 main fails on unit, then the retry appends 0i32 and recompiles", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,speed,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_unit_double_compile () = Planning ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_1 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:45Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:45 x: HYPOTHESIS from Cells' source (static, not measured): unit-ending cells compile twice per target. cell.spi wrap turns a cell's last top-level expression into 'inl main () : i32 = <expr>' (generated=1). Only a literal '()' line is rewritten to 0i32. A test cell ending in '... |> _assert_eq \"...\"' (unit), which is every x test cell, then fails attempt 1 with a unit/i32 mismatch. cells.ex compile/12 catches that (attempt == 1 and generated_main and Cell.unit_result?) and recompiles with '0i32' appended (attempt 2). If so, each such cell pays the full Spiral front end twice per target, and the compile phase (98% of run 5's cell time, X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED note 55) is about 2x what it needs to be. Cheap check (a compile window, one notebook): count SpiralCompiler process starts per test cell in process mode (Win32_Process start events filtered on SpiralCompiler.dll), expecting 2 per target if true. Fix direction (Cells): when the last expression's type is unit (or the cell is --test), emit '<expr>' followed by '0i32' up front, keeping the retry only for value cells.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_2 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 2u32, "2026-10-22T19:47Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:47 x: the check is queued (background waiter, x's scratch count_compiles.ps1). One prelude test cell (take_first_stops_at_the_limit, zig only, ends in _assert_eq) via run_notebook --test in process mode with the memo off, in a compile window, started when free memory is >= 3.5 GB and at most 1 compile window is held. A 200 ms poller records every dotnet process whose command line has SpiralCompiler.dll; grouping by the cell's spiral_cells dir separates other teams' compiles. Expected: 1 compile if the hypothesis is false, 2 if true. Result lands here.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_3 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 3u32, "2026-10-22T19:48Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:48 x: static chain complete (still unmeasured). directives.spi prepare (line ~70) calls cell.wrap on every cell's kept code, including --test cells (the flag is stripped first). cell.spi wrap makes the last top-level expression the body of 'inl main () : i32 =', rewriting only a bare '()' line to 0i32. cell.spi unit_result (line ~293) recognizes the compiler's 'Got: () / Expected: i32' pair. cells.ex compile/12 (line 622) then recompiles with '0i32' appended. Nothing on the test path appends 0i32 up front, so every --test cell whose last expression is unit (_assert_eq ...) should compile twice per target. Fix candidates, cheapest first: (a) in wrap, when the cell is a --test cell, emit the expression then '0i32' (test values are never shown); (b) for value cells, first ask the compiler for the expression's type (one pass), not compile-fail-retry. The runtime count (note 2) confirms or refutes it.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_4 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 4u32, "2026-10-22T19:54Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:54 main: confirmed by code read and fix staged as Cells pack step 14 (%TEMP%\\cells-pack\\unit_first.py plus unit_first_test.exs; it dry-applies after steps 1-13). cells.ex compile/10 always started at attempt 1 with the bare cell, and the attempt-1 error branch (622) recompiles with 0i32 appended when Cell.unit_result? matches. So every --test cell ending in a unit expression paid one failed compile (parse plus type check up to the main : i32 mismatch) before the real one. Now, for opts[:test_cell] with a generated main, compile/10 writes the 0i32-ended program first and compiles it as attempt 2 (no value shown, exactly what the retry produced). On success that is the only compile. On a timeout it returns it as before, and on any other error it restores the original text and runs today's attempt-1 path, so genuine errors are reported against the code as written. Tests: a unit --test cell reaches the compiler once, already ended by 0i32; a broken cell still reports its error. The speedup (x: compile is 98% of cell time) is measured by the pack's suite run against the baseline's per-cell compile_ms.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_5 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 5u32, "2026-10-22T19:55Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:55 x: fix sketch for main (text only, unverified). directives.spi pair parses '--test' and '--test static' and drops them ('pair rest print_code timeout packages'), so finish/cell.wrap no longer know a cell is a test. (a) Carry a test flag in Parsed, and in finish, for test cells, make the generated main end in 0i32 after the last expression (test values are never shown), so attempt 1 compiles. (b) A cheaper candidate: wrap already rewrites a bare '()' last line into 0i32 (cell.spi map_main), so appending a '()' line to a test cell's kept code might reuse that path. Whether the preceding assertion then stays inside main depends on how search_top chooses the main line, which needs checking. Wider scope: value cells whose last expression is non-i32 also go fail-then-retry (value_result? -> show_value_main), so any cell without an i32 result may pay two compiles. A one-pass design would generate the show_value main up front for value cells, if show_value can handle unit.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_6 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 6u32, "2026-10-22T20:16Z", "x", "2026-10-22 16:16 x: x's runtime count was cancelled, never run. Main confirmed the double compile by code read and staged the fix (pack step 14), so the count was only an extra 'before' figure. Its start condition (at most 1 compile window) went unmet for 30+ min while tibia held 2-3 compile windows, and it would likely have fired near 17:45, colliding with x's run 8 retry. Main's pack run measures the effect via compile_ms against its baseline.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_7 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 7u32, "2026-10-23T22:16Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:16 main: the nightpack run (job-1792766412460) found that step 14 also fired on .spi test blocks: their compiler-built main (inl _ = spiral_cells_test () then 0i32, or the wgsl status form spiral_cells_test ()) is already unit-safe, so appending 0i32 broke the wgsl status test and made an expected-fails test compile twice. unit_first.py now skips any program containing 'spiral_cells_test ()', so it only covers livemd --test cells built by cell.wrap, which is where the double compile is. Re-verified in main-cells-pack-2 (job-1792793750232).")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_8 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 8u32, "2026-10-24T06:24Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:24 main: landed in apps/cells with the Cells pack (CELLS-PACK-NEXT, job main-cells-pack-land, exit 0). Done once the post-block suite run on the tree shows no new red.")
inl note_of_cells_unit_double_compile_9 () = Note ("CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE", 9u32, "2026-10-24T23:57Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:57 main groom: done by this item's own rule (no new red in the post-block suite run on the tree). Job job-1792852828683-main-serial-cells-suite-tree-2 (10:40-11:55, tree after the 02:23 Cells pack land) had 3 failures, all from before the pack. (1, 2) Spiral.Cells.DocumentTest lib round trips: the sm' orphan .spir headings, red at HEAD, fixed by LIB-SM-ROUNDTRIP at 17:47. (3) CellsLiveTest 'a print with no value still runs': RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG, which failed on the tree and in every pack before the land; its fix is in pack 5i.")
inl eoie_tree_read_record () = Task ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", "One record for tree leases: release and regeneration scripts hold a tree-read window (eoie window run/request) instead of writing tree-leases/*.lease files; the PreToolUse hook and eoie's write verbs read the window store; the .lease directory goes away", TaskKind, P1, D3, 50u32, Active, "EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS", "eoie,lease,windows", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_tree_read_record () = Planning ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_1 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:48Z", "eoie-lane", "15:48 rule written before any probe: the cutover (scripts plus hook, no compile) is applied after F installs only if all of these hold on the installed binary, using a scratch folder as the leased root, so no one else's paths are touched: (1) 'window request tree-read 5 --label <scratch>' ok; (2) 'window list' shows it as tree-read with the label; (3) the drafted hook blocks an Edit inside <scratch> and allows one outside; (4) after 'window release' it allows the inside edit; (5) 'window run tree-read 30 --label <scratch> -- <long child>' with only that window-run pid (mine) killed: the next 'window list' prunes it (ProcessHolder orphan), so the hook allows; (6) hook p50 over 10 calls is at most 1.5x the current lease-file hook's p50 on the same inputs. If any fails, the .lease files stay and the failing case is noted here. Known gap found in reading: eoie_window_claim's claim_of reads stored_rows rather than live_rows, so 'window claim' still refuses for an orphaned window until it expires; the hook therefore reads 'window list' (which prunes), and the claim fix (claim over live_rows with an empty kept list) is a slice for the next compile release.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_2 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 2u32, "2026-10-22T19:55Z", "eoie-lane", "15:55 drafted in scratch tree-read/ (synced into the lab night folder after F's lease ends): (a) apply-tree-read-scripts.ps1 (no compile): workspace.ps1 gains Start-EoieTreeRead, which starts 'eoie window run tree-read 600 --label <root> -- pwsh -Command Wait-Process -Id <caller pid>', so the window ends with its caller, and if the holder itself is killed, the window-run pid dies and live_rows prunes it as an orphan ProcessHolder. test-regeneration -Publish calls it instead of writing regeneration-<pid>.lease. Dry run on copies: workspace.ps1 +14 lines, test-regeneration.ps1 5 lines -> 1, both parse. (b) the hook draft eoie-tree-lease.ps1: Edit/Write runs 'window claim <full path>'; on refusal it runs 'window list' (prunes) and claims again. Commands with a write verb parse 'window list' tree-read rows (label = root). A binary without claim (exit 2) allows. (c) apply-claim-live-rows.ps1 (compile): claim over live_rows. Anchor present in both the live tree and the GE2+H tree, so it is chained into apply-rH, and eoie_window_claim is added to H's regen filter. Left for the cutover: release-build-job-* call Start-EoieTreeRead instead of writing release-<pid>.lease; the rule's 6 probes after F installs. A trade-off to measure (case 6): the window label must be the bare root for claim to match, so a refusal names the window id, not the holder text ('eoie release F').")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_3 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 3u32, "2026-10-22T20:34Z", "eoie-lane", "16:34 the claim slice now carries its test: windows_cli gains a_tree_read_window_whose_holder_died_refuses_nothing (a '-run' tree-read row with a dead pid in the fixture store; the claim prints 'edit allowed' and the row is pruned). The row is built from Spiral strings, with no new host macro. It applies on both the live tree and the GE2+H tree. Release H's regen filter adds eoie_machine_windows, and its candidate notebooks add windows_cli.spi (4 instead of 3). job-script-check is ok.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_4 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 4u32, "2026-10-22T20:40Z", "eoie-lane", "16:40 placement for the write-verb half (eoie's own writes honoring the lease), read from the code: 'do edit' writes only through host.spi committed (one transactional host blob over the pending writes), and 'do write' through appended_to, copied_bytes and moved. A per-path claim check belongs in those write points, but do_edit.rs is at 976 lines (981 after GE2), and 'touching a legacy function migrates it' turns committed into a granular rewrite. A root-level check in eoie_do's dispatcher would be wrong the other way: a do root of C:\\work would refuse edits to a sibling repo while one tree under it is leased. Decision: the write-verb half waits for do_edit's boundary migration (EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY), which moves committed into the OS boundary package. There the claim export (a path-taking variant of eoie_window_claim_run) is one call per staged path. The hook half (scripts plus hook) does not depend on it.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_5 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 5u32, "2026-10-22T21:29Z", "eoie-lane", "17:29 the six probes of note 1 are scripted (scratch tree-read/probes.ps1; it parses), one call after F installs. EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME points at a scratch store, so the machine's shared window store is untouched, and the leased root is a scratch folder with a space in its name. Case 5 kills only the window-run process the probe itself started. Case 6 compares 10-call medians of the drafted hook and the installed lease-file hook on the same outside-path Edit payload.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_6 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 6u32, "2026-10-22T21:43Z", "eoie-lane", "17:43 baseline for probe 6, measured while F's real lease is live: the installed lease-file hook on an outside-path Edit has p50 2,003 ms over 10 calls (min 1,780, max 2,617). Almost all of it is pwsh startup, so the bar for the drafted hook is p50 <= 3,005 ms. An inside-tree Edit (eoie/README.md) is refused, exit 2. 'window list' costs ~760 ms, but the drafted hook calls it only on a claim refusal or for commands that contain a write verb.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_7 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 7u32, "2026-10-23T20:54Z", "eoie", "16:54 eoie: the six probes (scratch tree-read/probes.ps1) ran on the installed eoie, 6/6 PASS: request tree-read; list shows it with the leased path; the drafted hook blocks an inside Edit (exit 2) and allows outside (0); a release frees the inside (0); a killed window-run holder is pruned (inside 2 while held, 0 after the kill); hook p50 5,504 ms vs the current lease-file hook's 5,598 ms on a loaded box (bar 1.5x). Order for the swap, set now so protection never lapses: (1) release and regen scripts hold a tree-read window in addition to writing their .lease (both records for one release); (2) once one release ran with both, swap ~/.claude/hooks/eoie-tree-lease.ps1 to the window-reading draft, since every session on the box shares that hook; (3) the next release drops the .lease write and the tree-leases folder. Step 1 goes into the release scripts after GE2's train settles, not into a running release.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_8 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 8u32, "2026-10-23T22:28Z", "eoie", "18:28 eoie: step 1 drafted as scratch tree-read/apply-tree-read-step1.ps1 (dry run on copies of launch-O and launch-Q: rewritten, then idempotent). Each launch's release job start becomes 'job start --heavy <label> <ms> eoie window run tree-read <ms/60000+1> --label C:\\home\\git\\eoie -- pwsh -File release-build-job-X.ps1'. The window lives exactly as long as the release (window run's child; probe 5 showed a dead holder is pruned), and the exit code passes through. Not applied to tonight's train, by the 18:5x rule (train scripts stay frozen while it runs). Apply after P's acceptance, then step 2 (hook swap) after one release ran with both records.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_9 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 9u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): LEASE-7 (source freeze on paths while a reader such as a Cells run or a release regen is in flight). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_tree_read_record_10 () = Note ("EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD", 10u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 60m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 50%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl python_lazy_array_import () = Task ("PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT", "Python output imports cupy/numpy only when the program uses arrays: 357 of 451 samples/*/main.py never touch cp. yet pay the import at startup", TaskKind, P2, D2, 80u32, Active, "PACK-NEXT-4", "compiler,python,codegen,performance,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_python_lazy_array_import () = Planning ("PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT", Minutes 45u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_python_lazy_array_import_1 () = Note ("PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:50Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:50 main: measured. Every main.py has the fixed head try: import cupy as cp (CUDA probe) except: import numpy as cp; plus dataclass/typing imports. Only 94 of 451 programs use cp. in their body (39 use @dataclass, 255 the typing names). On this box (loaded, cupy installed) python -c pass takes 1,085 ms and import numpy 1,785 ms, so about 700 ms of each row's run is an unused array import, and with cupy present the CUDA runtime probe adds more. Fix: CodegenPython emits the cp block only when the body uses cp., and the dataclass/typing line only for what is used, the same on-demand rule as the other backends' helpers. Rule: Python residuals change only in those head lines, native results unchanged, DISAGREE 0; median Python native_ms before/after published.")
inl note_of_python_lazy_array_import_2 () = Note ("PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT", 2u32, "2026-10-22T20:00Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:00 main: patch staged in PACK-NEXT-4 (%TEMP%\\pack-next4\\python-lazy-array-import.ps1, 2 anchors once on the next3 core). CodegenPython no longer adds cupy_prelude before the body; after main_defs is written, it inserts the block at globals index 0 (its old position) only when globals, types, functions or main_defs match \\bcp\\. or \\bcuda\\b. Checked first: main_auto.py has no import and no Python cp/cuda use (its 3 cuda hits are inside the kernel string), no main.py body references cuda, and the matrix shim run_main.py does not use cp. The pack review checks that each changed main.py equals the old one minus exactly that block, with 94 of 451 programs expected to keep it.")
inl note_of_python_lazy_array_import_3 () = Note ("PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T06:02Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:02 main: measured the payoff on the matrix path (python run_main.py, SPIRAL_CUDA=0, ts_tuple_mixed, 4 rounds on a loaded box). With the numpy block 731-1,367 ms, without it 259-443 ms; python -X importtime puts import numpy at 570 ms cumulative. Dropping 'from main_auto import *' as well changes nothing measurable (266-511 ms). The reference run's Python run_ms median of 502 ms (22x C) is therefore mostly the numpy import that this pass removes from array-free programs.")
inl eoie_agile_find () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", "eoie agile find <root> <words>: one-call text search over titles, tags and notes (agile list has no text filter; grooming greps state/agile.spi by hand)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 60u32, Active, "", "eoie,agile,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_agile_find () = Planning ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_agile_find_1 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:55Z", "eoie-lane", "15:55 friction while grooming: to find which item owns tree leases, 'agile list' rejected --grep (no text filter exists) and I grepped the 4.3 MB state/agile.spi by hand with a regex window. Verb: 'eoie agile find <root> <words> [--open] [--team <word>] [--notes]' ranks items whose title, tags or (with --notes) notes contain every word (whole-word, case-insensitive), printing id, status, priority and the matching snippet. Typed: match_site Title | Tag | Note of note_number; reuse the whole-word scoring drafted for memory recall (lab/EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS recall coverage) instead of a second matcher.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_find_2 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", 2u32, "2026-10-22T20:01Z", "eoie-lane", "16:01 related, not a duplicate: EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES adds structured filters and named views (team, tag, prefix, open, ready, blocked, sort); this item is free-text search. If a view row can carry words, find becomes a --words filter on list, plus a find view, instead of a separate parser.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_find_3 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", 3u32, "2026-10-22T20:56Z", "eoie-lane", "16:56 pure model drafted and tested (scratch f5/src/agile_find_model, model only, HostFree + LibReferenced): backlog_item {id; title; tags; notes}; find_scope TitlesAndTags | WithNotes; match_site InTitle | InTag | InNote n; find_result Found score | Missed word, which names the first query word that hit nothing instead of returning an empty list. Words are whole-word and case-insensitive (lower-case, punctuation to spaces, lib sm'.split plus am'.any); a word scores 3 in the title, 2 in the tags, 1 in note n; blank words are skipped. Two test blocks run on today's lease item. Cells notebook check: pass on the first compile and again after re-laying; eoie standards 0 findings; census ratchet clean. Next (compile release): the agile CLI verb over state rows, sorted by score, with --notes and --open.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_find_4 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", 4u32, "2026-10-23T04:39Z", "eoie", "00:39 eoie: verb drafted as note 2 asked: a list/next filter, not a separate parser, since EOIE-AGILE-QUERIES' typed query flags are in the tree. Scratch agile-find/apply-agile-words.ps1, written against the post-GE2+H tree (GE2's heal wave rewrites query.spi; the dry run on that tree is clean). FlagWords :: query_flag string -> WithWords filter; words_hit keeps an item when every comma-separated word is a whole word, case-insensitive, of its title or tags (has_word copied from the Cells-checked agile_find_model). query_passes and the eoie_query_passes export gain the title; task_listing and next ranking pass row.title / task.title; the list and next usage shapes name --words (the usage test requires every flag). New test block words_match_whole_words_in_titles_and_tags (5 cases plus flag read and refusal). Note search (WithNotes) stays out: list rows don't carry notes. Risk, checked before it rides a release: task_listing.rs is at 979 and task_next.rs at 978 of 1000 lines; a compile preview after H decides, and if either crosses the gate, the callers' macro moves into a shared export instead of growing inline. Release: J, after I.")
inl note_of_eoie_agile_find_5 () = Note ("EOIE-AGILE-FIND", 5u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 60m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 60%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl eoie_ci_dev_workflow () = Task ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", "eoie CI is red since 10-18: test-dev-workflow 'Explicit Cargo target directory was not used' on both OSes, and the uncommitted lib staging in test-regeneration would add a 'Spiral lib not found' failure for its fixture bundle", TaskKind, P1, D3, 90u32, Active, "", "eoie,ci", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow () = Planning ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_1 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 1u32, "2026-10-22T20:10Z", "eoie-lane", "16:10 found while checking EOIE-PAGES-VERIFY (read-only gh): eoie gh-pages CI has failed on every push since dd48d2a (10-18 17:48, last green). 2e0fb13 and 40e71ad failed in build.ps1 ('Native Spiral developer workflow failed (exit 101)'). a831bad (10-19 22:09, the latest) fails on both ubuntu-24.04 and windows-2025 in compiler-contracts/test-dev-workflow.ps1:151, 'Explicit Cargo target directory was not used.': the regeneration with -CargoCheck -Test -SkipRelease -Publish -IncludeAuxiliary prints PASS, yet <fixture>/src/target/shared validation/debug/deps does not exist. Cause not yet found by reading; it needs a local run of test-dev-workflow (fake compiler fixture plus a one-crate cargo check, a compile-window job) after F. Second finding, which would break CI again once committed: the uncommitted lib staging in test-regeneration.ps1 throws 'Spiral lib not found' whenever <bundle>/../../../lib/spiral is missing, and test-dev-workflow's synthetic bundle has none. Fix drafted as scratch tree-read/apply-regen-lib-optional.ps1 (stage the lib when present; refuse only when a staged package.spiproj lists spiral- without it); a dry run composed with apply-tree-read-scripts on copies, and both files parse. Rule for the local run: apply both slices to a scratch copy of compiler-contracts plus build.ps1 and src/eoie_dev only (no .cache, no target), run test-dev-workflow.ps1 there under 'eoie job start --compile'; the fix is ready when it exits 0, and otherwise the first failing assertion's cause is recorded here.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_2 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 2u32, "2026-10-22T20:18Z", "eoie-lane", "16:17 cause found and confirmed by measurement: a831bad switched CI from Rust 1.88.0 to nightly-2026-10-18, the same commit where this failure first appears (the two earlier red runs failed differently, in build.ps1). Probe job job-1792700083598 (one-file crate, 'cargo check --target-dir T --all-targets'): nightly-2026-10-18 creates T/debug/build/probe/<hash> and T/debug/incremental, with no debug/deps and no .fingerprint (cargo's build-dir layout); 1.90 creates T/debug/deps. test-dev-workflow.ps1:151 asserted the old internal layout, and it is the only deps-path assertion in compiler-contracts, scripts or src. Fix drafted as scratch tree-read/apply-dev-workflow-layout.ps1: assert the profile folder T/debug, which both layouts create (not debug/incremental, since CI caches may set CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0). Dry run: 1 line changed, parses. With apply-regen-lib-optional this gives two slices; the local test-dev-workflow run (rule in note 1) verifies both after F.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_3 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 3u32, "2026-10-22T20:31Z", "eoie-lane", "16:31 rule from note 1 met: local run job-1792700833224 (scratch copies of compiler-contracts and build.ps1, existing workflow-driver eoie-dev, RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=nightly-2026-10-18 like CI, 185 s). (a) Current tree: fails at the first regeneration (2 PASS), consistent with the lib-staging throw. (b) lib-optional only: 6 PASS, then exactly CI's 'Explicit Cargo target directory was not used.' (reproduced locally). (c) Both slices: exit 0, 12 PASS. No fixture folders were left in TEMP. Ready: apply tree-read/apply-regen-lib-optional.ps1 and tree-read/apply-dev-workflow-layout.ps1 once F's lease ends; CI goes green on the next push if nothing else broke since a831bad (the user commits).")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_4 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 4u32, "2026-10-22T21:17Z", "eoie-lane", "17:17 applied to the live tree in the gap between F's runs (after-F-lease.ps1): test-regeneration lib staging is optional-unless-referenced, and test-dev-workflow asserts T/debug (diff: +19/-1 and +1/-1 against HEAD, including today's earlier range and lease edits). CI turns green on the user's next push if nothing else broke since a831bad.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_5 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 5u32, "2026-10-24T03:10Z", "eoie", "23:10 groom: no eoie CI run since 2026-10-20 02:10Z (gh-pages workflow 'tests' failure, run 39357467622). The last commit is a831bad (10-19); everything since is uncommitted local work, which is the user's to commit and push. So the 90% can't be confirmed until the next push; check that run before calling this done.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_6 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 6u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 30m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 90%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_7 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 7u32, "2026-10-25T17:39Z", "eoie", "13:39 kit groom: the CI failure changed. Run 40196034538 (i574n/kit tests, push to main 2026-10-25 13:05Z) fails on windows-2025 and Linux with 0 'Explicit Cargo target' and 0 'Spiral lib not found' lines.\n- New cause: native_selected_tests::selected_packages_preserve_native_build_contracts panics at eoie_dev/selected_tests.rs:24 because its fixture test fresh_cli_is_available can't find the CLI ('selected test requires the CLI', NotFound).\n- The fixture (eoie_dev/selected_tests.spi lines 42-55) expects <current_exe>/../../eoie(.exe), i.e. target/debug/eoie next to deps/. The client crate declares test-requires-cli=true, so the selected-test driver should build the eoie-cli bin before running the client's tests.\n- Next step: run the selected-test driver locally on the fixture with CARGO_TARGET_DIR set the way CI sets it, and compare where the bin lands with where current_exe/../.. points. Likely suspect: the driver builds the CLI into a different target dir or profile than the one the test runs from.\n- Progress stays 90% for the old cause; this new cause is a separate milestone (estimate 60m). It is a fix, not a feature, so the freeze allows it, but it rides SLATE's successor because the tree is in release.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_8 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 8u32, "2026-10-25T17:56Z", "eoie", "13:56 kit: likely root cause of the new CI failure (note 7).\n- In CI the test binaries use cargo's build-dir layout. The log shows 'Running selected_tests.rs (target\\debug\\build\\eoie-dev/3915de9c8a6f4b11\\out\\selected_workflow-3915de9c8a6f4b11.exe)', not target\\debug\\deps.\n- The fixture's client test looks for the CLI at current_exe/../../eoie(.exe). That is target/debug only in the old deps layout; under build/<pkg>/<hash>/out it points at a folder with no CLI, so NotFound.\n- The driver is fine: committed and live dev.rs are identical (682 lines, eoie-cli build path present), and it does build <target>/debug/eoie when test-requires-cli=true.\n- Local artifacts still use deps (last built 10-24 10:39), which is why it passes here. CI runs the same nightly-2026-10-18; what makes CI use the new layout is unconfirmed (no CARGO_BUILD_* other than JOBS in the log).\n- Fix draft (fixture only, no product change; the test text is a Rust string written into a temp project): scratch ci-cli-path\\fixture-cli-lookup.do. It finds the first ancestor of current_exe that holds eoie(.exe), which works in both layouts.\n- Next: lands after SLATE (editing the tree now would change measured content), then one CI run shows green or the next cause. A local repro needs a compile and can go to the laptop node.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_9 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 9u32, "2026-10-25T18:00Z", "kit2", "13:59 claimed by kit2 (88a146f8) per kit/kit2 split: reproduce the CI cargo build-dir failure on the laptop (full eoie test run = CPU this box lacks); kit's fixture-cli-lookup fix (ancestors().find is_file) lands after SLATE.")
inl note_of_eoie_ci_dev_workflow_10 () = Note ("EOIE-CI-DEV-WORKFLOW", 10u32, "2026-10-25T18:08Z", "kit2", "14:08 kit2: read-only config check toward the reproduce (no compile). No repo/CI setting forces the build-dir layout: src/.cargo/config.toml pins target-dir=target and the only CARGO_* in CI is CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 (gh-pages.yml). So the deps-vs-build-dir split is not an env/config toggle - it is toolchain/cargo-cache state. This box passes because its cached eoie artifacts (built 10-24) use the deps layout; CI fails on a clean build. Therefore the laptop, which has never built eoie, is the correct clean reproduce env (fresh cargo state matches CI). Queued for the fork-idle green light: offload a clean read-only selected-test run of eoie_dev via remote-run.ps1 (sync src minus target/.cache, RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=nightly-2026-10-18, CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2), expect the NotFound panic at selected_tests.rs:24, then apply kit fixture-cli-lookup on the mirror only and confirm green. In-tree fix still lands after SLATE.")
inl pack_next_4 () = Task ("PACK-NEXT-4", "Compiler pack after PACK-NEXT-3: output size and startup cost (C blank lines, Zig/Bend preludes on demand, Python lazy array import) plus formatter raw-block segments v2; composed by %TEMP%\\pack-next4\\compose.ps1 over the reviewed next3 core 95AF71D2FE8AE0E6", TaskKind, P1, D3, 60u32, Active, "PACK-NEXT-2", "compiler,codegen,formatter,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_pack_next_4 () = Planning ("PACK-NEXT-4", Minutes 180u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_pack_next_4_1 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 1u32, "2026-10-22T20:24Z", "main", "2026-10-22 16:24 main: contents, each with its own pre-written rule. (1) C-OUTPUT-BLANK-LINES: line' routes through lineC; C diffs whitespace-only. (2) ZIG-PRELUDE-ON-DEMAND: zigUsedPrelude; changed main.zig = old minus prelude helpers, mirror -24.3% lines. (3) BEND-BACKEND note 31: bendUsedPrelude; changed main.bend = old minus prelude defs, mirror -18.1%. (4) PYTHON-LAZY-ARRAY-IMPORT: the cupy/numpy block only when cp. or cuda is used; changed main.py = old minus exactly that block, 94 of 451 keep it. (5) FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS raw-block-segments v2: raw.spi lays out like plain.spi, 0 synthetic lines, formatter gate 0 beyond parentheses/whitespace. review.ps1 checks (1)-(4) mechanically over a before-snapshot of every main.c/zig/bend/py, plus DISAGREE 0, native results equal to next3 row for row, and the median native_ms for Bend and Python published. Starts only after PACK-NEXT-3 is accepted and installed, because compose refuses a changed next3 core.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_2 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 2u32, "2026-10-22T21:51Z", "main", "2026-10-22 17:51 main: rebased onto PACK-NEXT-3b (the or-join-free next3, core B9C5F83974DB245C) and composed end to end: c-blank-lines, zig-prelude-on-demand, bend-prelude-on-demand, python-lazy-array-import, raw-block-segments v2, and macro-continuations-kept (FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS note 51) all apply in sequence. Core CDCF5A72AFA1EC54, pair-drift 0 one-sided, lint G1 596, G2 0. review.ps1 now also checks that sm'.spi formats with 0 over-120 lines and every continuation line kept as written. It starts after next3b is accepted and installed (compose refuses a changed base).")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_3 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 3u32, "2026-10-22T22:06Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:06 main: next-session order for the main lane, written from today's state. (1) Read PACK-NEXT-3b's review (%TEMP%\\pack-next3b\\read.ps1 against pack-after-night's run, rules in PACK-NEXT-2 notes 5, 7, 9, 12). If green, install (adapt %TEMP%\\pack-next3\\install.ps1 to next3b: Base 25D2BE7C94943D8E, core B9C5F83974DB245C) and run a single-flight -Accept that reproduces the review row for row with SPIRAL_CC pinned. (2) PACK-NEXT-4 review (compose refuses anything but next3b's core; its build should reuse >= 20 shared ReadyToRun images, which closes BUILD-R2R-SHARED-CACHE). (3) The Cells pack verdict (targeted baseline of the failing files) and the combined lib landing (25 files) after eoie's past-lib-reads. (4) or-join v3: a Debug candidate with v2 for the stack, then the v3 design with or_pattern_bound_alternatives as its guard. (5) On a quiet box: the hopacsota waits census and COMPILER-PACK-SPEED-AB.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_4 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 4u32, "2026-10-22T22:56Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:56 main: recomposed, core B84CC78DC4162016 (pair-drift 0 one-sided, G1 596, G2 0). Now: next3b core B9C5F83974DB245C, then macro-unit-fit-dropped.ps1 (exact inverse; PACK-NEXT-2 note 18), c-blank-lines, zig and bend prelude on demand, python lazy array import, raw-block segments v2, macro continuations kept, and rust-let-immutable.ps1 (RUST-LET-IMMUTABLE). Since next3b is not installed, next4 is the pack to accept; its matrix compares with next3b's run. Review rules added before the run: Rust outputs differ only by let mut -> let; the formatter gate's automatic part must PASS, and it now fails on joined authored lines.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_5 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 5u32, "2026-10-22T23:06Z", "main", "2026-10-22 19:06 main: plus C-FUSE-DECLARATIONS, so the core is now 307C133544754112 (pair-drift 0 one-sided, G1 596, G2 0). The C rule in review.ps1 becomes 'beyond blank lines and declaration fusion 0' (self-tested on closure_curry against the mirror). Queued as job-1792709830384-main-pack-next4-review (heavy, after the next3b review).")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_6 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 6u32, "2026-10-22T23:54Z", "main", "2026-10-22 19:54 main: next3b's matrix (single-flight-20261022-184215: DISAGREE 0, native 6806 ran) exposed four defects that next4 inherited, all fixed before next4's matrix. (1) Bend: union-w64 re-declared w64_hi/w64_lo, which bendW64Prelude already defines; bend2 rejects the duplicate, so 11 Bend 64-bit rows were build-fail. New step bend-w64-projection-dedupe.ps1 deletes the copy. (2) Rust module split: 6 NATIVE-DIFF rows (main_part1.rs missing), because the host's stale-parts cleanup (Program.fs ~600) ran on every backend's compile of a sample and deleted the Rust parts written moments earlier. The cleanup now runs for backend = Rust only (tree host, before next4's host compile). (3) record_with_expected_base and gadt_growing_index_runtime used + and - under packages core-, which only binds +. and -.; fixed in the tree samples. (4) next4's first build failed on my c-fuse-declarations: the here-string dropped its final newline and glued 'let codegenC' to 'fused.ToString()'. Fixed, and both reviews now run fsc --parseonly under both defines (8 s) before building. Core now 67F2E4064FD4AFDE; requeued as job-1792712913136-main-pack-next4-review.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_7 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 7u32, "2026-10-23T00:15Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:15 main: order fixed before the run. Both matrices write outputs into the tree's samples/ and their mutexes are per cache, so next4 is requeued after the hopacsota promotion (job-1792714506844, --after job-1792711871311). Because the hopacsota matrix replaces next3b's outputs with pack-after-night's, next4's snapshot is pack-after-night's. That keeps the C, Zig and Python text rules exact (next3b touched none of them). For Rust and Bend the text check becomes informational; their acceptance is the matrix compare with next3b's run single-flight-20261022-184215 (DISAGREE 0, native equal row for row, no new build-fail).")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_8 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 8u32, "2026-10-23T01:48Z", "main", "2026-10-22 21:48 main: ZIG-CONST-LOCALS rides in next4 (pack-queued-builds: next4 hadn't started). The compose applies %TEMP%\\pack-next6\\zig-const-locals.ps1 after the bend dedupe. Core E1F6B0543BC3A1B2 parses under both defines, and codegenZig already uses Dictionary/HashSet unqualified. The Zig review rule becomes 'beyond the prelude and const locals 0'. zig-const-check.fsx strips the full prelude from each snapshot, applies the same zigConstLocals to the body in one fsi pass, and requires the new output to end with it. Self-test: a correct pair passes and an untransformed pair is flagged (checked 2, bad 1).")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_9 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 9u32, "2026-10-23T02:49Z", "main", "2026-10-22 22:49 main: KOTLIN-SWIFT-VAL-AT-ASSIGNMENT and TYPESCRIPT-CONST-LOCALS added (pack-queued-builds: next4 hasn't started). %TEMP%\\pack-next6\\kotlin-swift-ts-locals.ps1 defines valAtAssignment (before codegenKotlin; Kotlin pipes through it with 'val', Swift with 'let') and typeScriptConstLocals (before codegenTypeScript). Each F# pass is byte-equal to its Python mirror in fsi on 8/8/7 sample files. TS check: 30 most-changed transformed main.ts run under bun with the same exit and stdout as the originals (a reassigned const would throw). Core BCBBC43A71F1E5E6, pair-drift 0 one-sided, parses under both defines; Dictionary/HashSet already in scope at both sites. Review: snapshot adds main.kt/main.swift/main.ts, and locals_check.py re-applies the mirrors to each snapshot file and requires equality; self-test changed 417/416/423, beyond the rewrite 0. Expected: Kotlin -15.5% lines, Swift -15.4%, TS 84% let -> const.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_10 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 10u32, "2026-10-23T03:13Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:13 main: next4, the Cells pack and hopacsota are unqueued until eoie's job-host release installs (F6 or F7, about 23:15 to 00:30). Kept queued, next4 would have started right after next5b, during F7's 81-notebook test batch, with a parentless supervisor and on a box whose Cells daemon already retires on low memory. scratch start-chain.ps1 starts the chain fresh under the new binary (next4, then cells-pack, then hopacsota) and prints each supervisor's parent next to the job host pid. The user asked for no dangling processes tonight: scratch dangling-check.ps1 runs at each checkpoint (23:11: 0 dangling after stopping one orphaned compiler server).")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_11 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 11u32, "2026-10-23T03:21Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:21 main: LEAN-LET-AT-ASSIGNMENT added too (next4 is unqueued until eoie's install). %TEMP%\\pack-next6\\lean-let.ps1 defines leanLetAtAssignment before codegenLean and pipes codegenLean's output through it. The F# pass equals the mirror on all 452 main.lean in fsi. Core B7B131BE89E26F93, pair-drift 0 one-sided, parses under both defines; Dictionary/HashSet in scope. The review snapshot adds main.lean, and locals_check.py re-applies lean_let_mirror.py to it. Expected: -11.4% Lean lines, 49% of let mut become let. The matrix's Lean rows (lean --run elaboration) validate the output, since 'let' with a later ':=' would be a Lean error.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_12 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 12u32, "2026-10-23T03:30Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:30 main: RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE added (1,379 clones become moves; evidence on that item). Core F84F4569BC0035BE, pair-drift 0 one-sided, parses under both defines. Rust acceptance stays the matrix: DISAGREE 0, native equal to next3b row for row, no new Rust build-fail.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_13 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 13u32, "2026-10-23T03:50Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:50 main: plus PYTHON-DROP-DEL and GLEAM-TIDY-OUTPUT, core D38D865FFCB50E17 (parses under both defines). Install plan: once next5c's formatter review passes its rule, one final pack is composed (next4 latest plus then-habit, bare-join-head and chain-fill) and gets the full matrix review. Only that composite installs, after eoie's job-host release and tibia's block.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_14 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 14u32, "2026-10-23T04:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 00:37 composed with bend-prune after gleam-tidy; core 87EB43CF41975958 (was D38D865FFCB50E17); parse-only passes under both defines; Bend text rule moved from review.ps1 into locals_check.py (prelude file argument).")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_15 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 15u32, "2026-10-23T04:48Z", "main", "2026-10-23 00:48 plus lua-helpers after bend-prune; core 6D9273CFCCB09AEB parses under both defines; pack-next5d compose now expects this base.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_16 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 16u32, "2026-10-23T05:01Z", "main", "2026-10-23 01:01 plus wasm-fields after lua-helpers; core ECA129CF8D4A1EFE; pack-next5d compose expects it.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_17 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 17u32, "2026-10-23T05:09Z", "main", "2026-10-23 01:09 run-time census of the next3b reference run (single-flight-20261022-184215, run_ms median vs C): Lean 119x, Wgsl 133x, Gleam 34x, Bend 34x, Python 22x (next4's lazy array import targets it), the compiled backends within 1-2.4x. Probe: lean --run on a program that only returns 0 takes as long as array_i16 with the full prelude (4.4 s each on the loaded box), so Lean's cost is toolchain startup and a Lean prelude-on-demand would not move run_ms.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_18 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 18u32, "2026-10-23T05:54Z", "main", "2026-10-23 01:54 plus c-static (C-STATIC-FUNCTIONS) and census-per-statement after wasm-fields; core 4FBB760BD84BBCBF; pack-next5d compose expects it.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_19 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 19u32, "2026-10-23T08:47Z", "main", "2026-10-23 04:47 main: composed after tibia's block end: tail-moves (TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES; F# parallelMoves equals the mirror's steps on 165/165 sample blocks) and gleam-helpers (GLEAM-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND; F# gleamUsedHelpers equals the mirror on 447/447 outputs) after census-per-statement. next4 core 3D8553145D246A26. Composite with the formatter (pack-next5d: then-habit, bare-join-head, chain-fill, shape-cache): 7FCC891349EB1608, parse-only clean under SINGLE_FLIGHT and HOPACSOTA. The chain (review -> cells-pack -> hopacsota promotion) starts on eoie's 'release install done' (F9, expected 05:20-05:40), as agreed with eoie.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_20 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 20u32, "2026-10-23T09:06Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:06 main: post-F9 steps armed as a background waiter (eoie job wait F9, then scratchpad after-f9.ps1). They run only if F9 ends finished with exit=0, in this order: (1) the next5d reformat on spiral and eoie working files, holding every staged .spi (574 in spiral, 133 in eoie, re-read at run time) and skipping files changed since the review copy (apply-format's hash check, which also skips eoie's fresh lib landings); (2) spiral rust-toolchain.toml (nightly-2026-10-18, channel only) if absent; (3) start-chain.ps1 -Core on composite 7FCC891349EB1608: next4 review -> cells-pack -> hopacsota promotion, output to a file per the job-start pipe rule.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_21 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 21u32, "2026-10-23T09:26Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:26 main: install script staged: %TEMP%\\pack-next4\\install.ps1 (from next3b's). It refuses unless the tree core is still pack-after-night 25D2BE7C94943D8E (next3b never installed; next4 carries its fixed parts) and the composite on disk is exactly the reviewed 7FCC891349EB1608. Under an exclusive compiler-install window it copies the composite into the tree, builds single-flight and hopacsota, and exits 0 only if the installed single-flight SpiralCompilerCore equals the reviewed candidate. Rule for -Apply, unchanged from the pack rules: the review's automatic gate PASS, DISAGREE 0 on both oracles, every per-backend text rule at 0 beyond its rewrite, and the hand-read of the formatter hunks.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_22 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 22u32, "2026-10-23T10:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 06:11 main: composite 67EBF80563E2239A review (job-1792748200944). Release candidate built in 263.9 s. raw.spi lays out like plain.spi (widest 96 = 96, 0 synthetic lines). Macro continuations kept 12 of 12. Formatter candidate gate automatic part PASS: user, lib, synthetic, dice and eoie corpora at 0 files differing beyond parentheses and whitespace; 33 flagged joins, 0 of them authored; 0 joined authored lines that fit. The matrix compare against next3b's run single-flight-20261022-184215 runs next; the per-backend text rules (locals_check.py) follow it.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_23 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 23u32, "2026-10-23T13:33Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:33 main: the 67EBF805 review (job-1792748200944) reaches its 240-min limit at about 09:36. The formatter gate and the build passed; the matrix was at 3,752 of 4,988 native rows (through the 'r' samples) at 6-13 rows/min, because HP's cargo regen, x's Cells and tibia's agents shared the box. No verdict yet. Plan: restore the samples from the review's own 'before' snapshot (pack-next4\\restore-samples.ps1: 4,987 outputs plus 12 candidate part files from RustModuleSplit), so the rerun snapshots the installed state. Then rerun with a 6 h limit as main-pack-next4-review-2 (job-1792762224345, after tibia-block-pr26). -ReuseNative replays the 3,752 finished rows from the native memo, so the rerun runs about 1,240 rows plus compiles. The install of 18AC8014 (install.ps1, formatter-only delta over 67EBF805) waits for that verdict.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_24 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 24u32, "2026-10-23T13:58Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:58 main: post-block chain made serial, by my memory rule that Cells gates and cargo beside a matrix cost row timeouts (heavy-gate-lessons, 10-21/10-22 recurrences). After tibia-block-pr26 it runs main-pack-next4-review-2 (6 h), then main-lib-land-gate (it first restores the samples from review-2's 'before' snapshot), then main-lib-land, main-cells-pack, main-dib-suite, main-rustls-check, main-am-any-gate, and main-hopacsota-promotion (8 h), each --after the previous one. The promotion's matrix never overlaps another matrix on the shared samples tree. Manual steps in between, by rule: install 18AC8014 (install.ps1) once review-2's verdict passes, then the next7 review (Gleam split and closure factory), then next8 (or-pattern v3).")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_25 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 25u32, "2026-10-23T18:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:57 main: review-2 VERDICT: FAIL by the pre-written rule (DISAGREE 0). Run single-flight-20261023-135519 vs next3b's 184215: 277 Gleam rows DISAGREE (oracle agree -> DISAGREE, exit 1). Every other rule holds: C beyond blank lines/fusion/linkage 0, Zig const-check 467/0 bad, Kotlin/Swift/TS/Lean beyond their locals rewrites 0, Lua 0, Wasm 0, formatter gate PASS; compile-status changes vs next3b are all explained (GADT sample error->timeout = GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX evidence; portable_math_rounding Lean/Gleam/Lua timeout->ok; record_with_expected_base error->ok, a new sample). Informational per the rule text: Rust beyond let mut 307 (snapshot predates next3b + RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE), Bend 9 (w64), Delphi 1 (native_managed_array_tail_recursion), Python closures keep their dels (pythonWithoutDels does not reach closure bodies: an incomplete drop, harmless, to fix with the Gleam fix). CAUSE of the Gleam failure (found and proven in fsi): GLEAM-HELPERS-ON-DEMAND's gleamUsedHelpers ends a helper at the next '\\n}\\n', but StringBuilder.AppendLine writes CRLF on Windows, so the externals block ends in '}\\r\\n' and the search for the unused spiral_wrap_unsigned ran on to main's closing brace: the 'helper' removed was everything through main, then spiral_wrap_signed went unused too; output = 'import gleam/int\\r\\n' (18 bytes; the sample's own repro with the next4 dll gives exactly that). Fix in %TEMP%\\pack-next6\\gleam-helpers.ps1: prune (code.Replace('\\r\\n', '\\n')); fsi on a CRLF case: 224 -> 19 bytes before, 224 -> 151 after (only the unused helper goes). Consequence: next4 (and the 5f composite on it) is not installed today; the user's formatter fix goes in as a formatter-only core instead (pack-fmt-only: the installed tree core with only 5f's Formatter section, delta Formatter-only, building now as job-1792781232696). next4 needs a recompose with the fix and a new review.")
inl note_of_pack_next_4_26 () = Note ("PACK-NEXT-4", 26u32, "2026-10-23T19:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 15:51 main: after the formatter-only install (tree core now 19F5F752485517B1 = pack-after-night + 5f's Formatter section), pack-next4\\install.ps1's Base pin moved from 25D2BE7C to 19F5F752 so the reviewed 5f composite (5f 821195F8CC1C5E10 = next4 with the Gleam CRLF fix + the same 5f Formatter section) can install over it. review-3 (job-1792782626942, queued at the chain end) reviews that composite, records its candidate in pack-next5f\\dll.txt, and restores the samples tree after itself; install only on its pass.")
