inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_1 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:54Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:54 main: from x's read-only scan (ureq PR #1218 lists the three fixed versions). Applied as semver-compatible lockfile bumps, with nothing built yet: spiral/workspace/Cargo.lock (rustls 0.23.34 -> 0.23.46, rustls-webpki 0.103.7 -> 0.103.16, webpki-roots 1.0.3 -> 1.0.10; the 0.26.11 webpki-roots shim stays) and polyglot/apps/chat/Cargo.lock (0.23.45 -> 0.23.46, 0.103.15 -> 0.103.16, 1.0.9 -> 1.0.10). Open: dice/Cargo.lock reaches only rustls 0.23.23 and stays on rustls-webpki 0.102.8 (an older line the fix isn't on), so it needs a real dependency upgrade, probably held by the near-sdk pins (see RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE's dice/contract note). eoie's vendored syn lock is probably not built; eoie's call. Verify: build spiral/workspace and polyglot chat on the pinned nightly in a quiet window, before the user's commit.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_2 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 2u32, "2026-10-23T06:44Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:44 main: dice Cargo.lock moved (unstaged, lockfile only): rustls 0.23.16 -> 0.23.46, rustls-pki-types 1.10.0 -> 1.15.1, rustls-webpki 0.102.8 -> 0.103.16, webpki-roots 0.26.6 -> 0.26.11 (the shim) + 1.0.10. Plain 'cargo update -p rustls' stopped at 0.23.23, which looked like a pin, but --precise 0.23.46 resolves cleanly: it was cargo's minimal-change choice while rustls-pki-types stayed at 1.10, not a constraint. alphabet: apps/documents/Cargo.lock has no rustls, rustls-webpki or webpki-roots entries, so nothing to mirror. Open: one quiet-window build each of dice, spiral/workspace and polyglot/apps/chat on the new locks.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_3 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 3u32, "2026-10-24T00:18Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:18 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 60 (bumps applied in the spiral/workspace, polyglot chat and dice Cargo.lock files). The three quiet-window verify builds are main-rustls-check, requeued in %TEMP%\\cells-pack\\requeue-after-block.ps1 chain B after review-3.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_4 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 4u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: main-rustls-check timed out (124) at 22:16 and the cause is not rustls: dice's cargo check --locked exited 101 after 3051 s on near-workspaces v0.11.1's build script (custom build command failed), with rustls 0.23.46, rustls-webpki 0.103.16, webpki-roots 0.26.11 and 1.0.10 resolved. Next: read the near-workspaces build output and fix the dice build, then rerun with a longer limit.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_5 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 5u32, "2026-10-24T03:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:57 main: cause of the 22:16 failure found, and it isn't rustls. dice's contract/tests crate (dice_contract_tests) depends on near-workspaces 0.11.1, whose build script fetches the NEAR sandbox binary, which has no Windows build; CI runs that crate on Linux. The check now runs dice with --workspace --exclude dice_contract_tests, at Normal priority (BelowNormal plus CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 took 3051 s and starved), keeps each repo's full cargo log in spiral-bin\\runs\\rustls-check, and exits with the failure count. Requeued as main-rustls-check-2.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_6 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 6u32, "2026-10-24T13:11Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:11 main: main-rustls-check-2 was stopped at 09:10 by me. It held heavy slot 2 for 1 h 40 min at about 0.01 cores, inside openssl-sys's vendored nmake build for polyglot apps/chat (single-threaded cl /Zs dependency scans), while heal gates waited. Rerun rule: run the three cargo checks as a light-slot job, or point openssl-sys at a prebuilt OpenSSL (OPENSSL_DIR) so chat does not build it from source; dice and spiral/workspace finished their part before the stop.")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_7 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 7u32, "2026-10-25T07:40Z", "main", "10-25 03:40 main: the 10-24 logs re-read. spiral/workspace finished cleanly (51 min; its lock hasn't changed since 10-22 23:52), so it's done. dice did NOT pass: dice_contract fails E0308 on native, because on non-wasm SpiralNearVec is a Vec shim while the hand-written State wraps near-sdk's Vector. So the contract compiles only for wasm32, which is what CI builds. chat failed in near-workspaces' sandbox install. rustls reaches these repos only through near-workspaces -> near-sandbox-utils -> binary-install -> ureq, so the test crates are the real check. Its build.rs skips the sandbox download when NEAR_SANDBOX_BIN_PATH is set. main-rustls-check-3 (job-1792913855712, compile gate) runs dice native minus the contracts, both contracts for wasm32, and both test crates with NEAR_SANDBOX_BIN_PATH set plus OPENSSL_NO_VENDOR=1 and OPENSSL_DIR=scoop openssl (no vendored nmake build).")
inl note_of_rustls_advisories_2026_10_8 () = Note ("RUSTLS-ADVISORIES-2026-10", 8u32, "2026-10-25T07:53Z", "main", "10-25 03:53 main: DONE. main-rustls-check-3 (job-1792913855712), cargo check --locked against the bumped locks (rustls 0.23.46, rustls-webpki 0.103.16, webpki-roots 0.26.11 + 1.0.10):\n- dice native minus the contracts: exit 0 (256 s).\n- dice_contract_tests: exit 0 (280 s). With NEAR_SANDBOX_BIN_PATH set, near-workspaces' build.rs skips the sandbox download that has no Windows build.\n- chat_contract_tests: exit 0 (190 s), with OPENSSL_NO_VENDOR=1 and OPENSSL_DIR=scoop openssl, so no nmake build.\nThose test crates are the only rustls consumers (ureq <- binary-install <- near-sandbox-utils). spiral/workspace was already clean on 10-24 with an unchanged lock.\nThe 2 wasm32 contract checks failed only because --all-targets drags the contracts' native test code (near_workspaces, tokio) into the wasm build. It's a flag artifact, not a lock problem; CI builds the contract lib for wasm without --all-targets.")
inl ci_actions_latest () = Task ("CI-ACTIONS-LATEST", "CI action pins to the latest majors in our repos: checkout v6.1.0 -> v7.0.1, upload-artifact v5.0.0 -> v7.0.2, download-artifact v6.0.0 -> v8.0.2, setup-python v6.3.0 -> v7.1.0 (tibia's CI already runs these)", TaskKind, P2, D1, 60u32, Blocked, "", "ci,actions,deps,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ci_actions_latest () = Planning ("CI-ACTIONS-LATEST", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_ci_actions_latest_1 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LATEST", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:56Z", "main", "2026-10-22 23:56 main: from x's read-only release check. Scope: the gh-pages.yml workflows of spiral, polyglot, dice, alphabet (mirrors dice), blog, dir-tree-html and i574n.github. Each repo has .github/workflows/actions.lock, so every bump needs 'gh actions-lock' run in that repo (extension v0.2.0 installed), then 'gh actions-lock --no-fix' to verify (memory: a new uses: fails at startup until the lock is regenerated). HELD: the user has gh-pages.yml staged in polyglot, dice, alphabet, blog, dir-tree-html and i574n.github ('M ' at 23:57), and spiral's has unstaged edits. Staged files are the user's, so this runs after the user's commit, all seven repos at once.")
inl note_of_ci_actions_latest_2 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LATEST", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:11 main: staged %TEMP%\\ci-actions-latest\\apply.ps1, dry run by default. It skips any repo whose gh-pages.yml is staged and runs gh actions-lock, then --no-fix, after each edit. Latest releases read today with gh api (repos/<action>/releases/latest): checkout v7.0.1, download-artifact v8.0.2, upload-artifact v7.0.2, and setup-python v7.1.0 (published 2026-10-22) as the item says, plus four the item didn't list: setup-dotnet v5.4.0 -> v6.0.0, setup-go v5.6.0 -> v7.0.0 (alphabet), wtfjoke/setup-tectonic v3.1.5 -> v4.0.6 (alphabet, i574n.github), and awalsh128/cache-apt-pkgs-action from the unpinned @master to v1.6.3 (alphabet). Current already: setup-beam v1.24.1, setup-zig v2.2.1, setup-rust v1.3.0, setup-bun v2.2.0, gh-pages v4.1.0, install-poetry v1.4.2. Dry run: spiral would take 5 bumps, and polyglot, dice, alphabet, blog, dir-tree-html and i574n.github are HELD because the user has them staged. By the item's rule all seven go together after the user's commit; major bumps (dotnet 6, go 7, tectonic 4) get checked by the first CI run.")
inl note_of_ci_actions_latest_3 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LATEST", 3u32, "2026-10-23T22:43Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:43 x: x joins this item for X-DEPS-LATEST step 4. Its workflow is .github/workflows/ci.yml (not gh-pages.yml), with actions.lock beside it. It has the same four stale pins (checkout v6.1.0, download-artifact v6.0.0, upload-artifact v5.0.0, setup-python v6.3.0) plus python-version '3.12.10', which the plan moves to '3.15' (e2e stub only). The other four actions (setup-beam v1.24.1, setup-zig v2.2.1, setup-rust v1.3.0, actions-gh-pages v4.1.0) are current. Suggestion so one script serves all repos: a -Workflow parameter on %TEMP%\\ci-actions-latest\\apply.ps1 (default gh-pages.yml), plus a python-version pin. x's ci.yml is staged by the user right now, so the script's HELD guard applies; nothing to run for x until the user commits.")
inl note_of_ci_actions_latest_4 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LATEST", 4u32, "2026-10-24T00:19Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:19 main: x's suggestion applied to %TEMP%\\ci-actions-latest\\apply.ps1: -Workflow <file> (default gh-pages.yml) and -PythonVersion <v> (rewrites python-version: values and keeps their quoting), and -Repos splits on commas, so it works under pwsh -File. Dry runs: -Repos x -Workflow ci.yml -PythonVersion 3.15 -> HELD (the user has x's ci.yml staged); -Repos spiral,dice -> spiral 5 bumps (checkout v7.0.1, download-artifact v8.0.2, setup-dotnet v6.0.0, setup-python v7.1.0, upload-artifact v7.0.2), dice HELD (gh-pages.yml staged). Applied per repo after the user commits the staged workflows; each run regenerates actions.lock.")
inl note_of_ci_actions_latest_5 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LATEST", 5u32, "2026-10-24T00:20Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:20 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 60 (the script is staged and dry-run clean). The remaining step waits on the user's commits of the staged workflows in dice, x and the other HELD repos.")
inl note_of_ci_actions_latest_6 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LATEST", 6u32, "2026-10-24T06:17Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:17 main: Active -> Blocked. Rechecked: spiral is now HELD too (the user has spiral's .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml staged), like dice and x. The script never edits a staged workflow; each repo's bump, plus its actions.lock regeneration, runs after the user commits.")
inl eoie_heavy_one_limit () = Task ("EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", "heavy jobs have two limits: eoie's timeout and Invoke-Heavy's 240-minute watchdog (heavy_wrapper drops the timeout)", TaskKind, P1, D1, 60u32, Blocked, "EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN", "eoie,jobs,process", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_heavy_one_limit () = Planning ("EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", Minutes 15u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_heavy_one_limit_1 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:56Z", "eoie", "23:56 eoie: found when F7 started (23:51): 'Invoke-Heavy: acquired slot 2 ... (held at most 240 min)'. eoie_machine_windows heavy_wrapper takes the job timeout and discards it (inl _ = timeout), so every --heavy job gets the gate's default MaxMinutes 240 (SPIRAL_HEAVY_MAX_MINUTES unset). The heavy-queue watchdog kills the job's whole process tree at that deadline, whatever eoie's limit says. F7 (limit 6 h) would have been killed at 03:52, possibly mid release build. Done tonight: stopped F7's own watchdog, pid 46596, verified as a child of F7's heavy wrapper 120228 with deadline 03:51:58; F7's eoie supervisor still enforces its 6 h limit. Fix drafted for release H: tree-read/apply-heavy-one-limit.ps1 (wrapper passes -MaxMinutes timeout/60000+1, the test's fake gate takes the parameter, and the heavy-job CLI test asserts '-MaxMinutes 1 {' and 'held at most 1 min'). Dry run on the GE2+H copy is clean; chained into apply-rH.")
inl note_of_eoie_heavy_one_limit_2 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: the slice tree-read\\apply-heavy-one-limit.ps1 (run by HIJK's apply-rH) left heavy_wrapper's concat5 with 4 arguments; the Q typecheck caught it 22:41 in crowding.spi. Fixed on the slice (backup .before-arity-fix); the Q typecheck passes 10/10 since. Ships with HIJK.")
inl note_of_eoie_heavy_one_limit_3 () = Note ("EOIE-HEAVY-ONE-LIMIT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T11:23Z", "eoie", "07:23 groom: blocked on the train. The only work left is to install stage HIJK and run its after-install check, so 'agile next' must not hand this out as ready work. Back to Active when stage HIJK installs.")
inl x_paging_guard () = Task ("X-PAGING-GUARD", "x client: pagination stops on a repeated next_token and at a page cap, so an API cursor loop cannot spin xc or burn paid reads", Story, P2, D2, 85u32, Active, "X-DONE", "x,client,api", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_paging_guard () = Planning ("X-PAGING-GUARD", Minutes 40u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_1 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-10-23T03:58Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:58 x: found by a read-only review. paging.spi:14-17 'paged' calls fetch with each next_token until the token is empty, with no guard. A cursor that repeats (the API returning a token already seen, or a cycle) makes xc thread and every paged read loop forever, spending paid X API reads on each request. The tests (paging.spi:24-38) cover two pages and an error, not a loop. Fix in the domain: keep the seen tokens; a repeat returns a typed error (e.g. CursorRepeated token) naming the page count; add a page cap (a named constant or a parameter, a few hundred for xc's thread reads) that returns CursorBudget. Tests: a pager that returns 't1' forever must stop with CursorRepeated after 2 fetches; a pager with an endless fresh token must stop at the cap. Also minor: 'listm.append acc [p.body]' is O(n^2) over pages; cons and reverse once.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_2 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:06 x: a draft fix is at C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-PAGING-GUARD.md (not compiled). It adds a cursor_halt union (CursorRepeated / CursorBudget), a walk state record, a 500-page budget and a 'walker' record so paging stays generic over the caller's error, plus 4 tests with hand traces. Its post weights were checked without a compiler via 'xc post-thread --dry-run' on paging_draft.spi: 22 posts, max weight 248, no cell split (the first version had one 301-weight definition and was split). Measured constraint for wire.spi: union failure (224 chars) and describe_failure (263) have no room for an inline-record case or one more arm, so the draft names a halted_cursor record and suggests passing whole records to the describers. Open at compile time: top-level 'let rec ... and ...' and mixed record punning.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_3 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:33Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:33 x: the three Spiral drafts (paging, token, invariants) pass x-compat with 0 findings ('mix xsim.x_compat' on the extracted .spi files, Cells' checker for hashtag, mention, cashtag and autolink hazards). Their post weights were checked before with xc post-thread --dry-run. Cost note: a fresh MIX_BUILD_PATH made mix compile kino, spiral_cells and xsim (about 107 .ex files) first; that ran outside any live block or compile window.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_4 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:05Z", "x", "2026-10-23 05:05 x: the draft now COMPILES, partly verified. C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_paging_guard.ps1 applies it to a client package (each anchor asserted exactly once). On a scratch copy (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck\\client, packageDir made absolute; x's tree untouched), the Spiral compiler built thread/thread.spi to Rust in 15 s (49 KB) under 'eoie window run compile' (a window granted at 4.1 GB free, then released). The cursor messages, the conversation resource and the 500 budget appear in the output, so the new paging walk, wire's CursorHalted / cursor_text and thread's halted_in were type-checked. Two fixes came from the run-up: the names stepped / finished / continued clashed with exsss.spi:54 and splitter.spi:104/109 and are now walk_step / walk_bodies / walk_on. NOT yet verified: the 4 new paging test blocks (Cells only) and cargo build of the generated crate. Both go in the first pre-commit after applying.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_5 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_paging_guard_6 () = Note ("X-PAGING-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl x_token_refresh_safe () = Task ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", "x client: xc refresh never loses the rotated refresh token (atomic replace of the env file; new tokens kept somewhere on write failure)", Story, P2, D2, 85u32, Active, "X-DONE", "x,client,auth", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_token_refresh_safe () = Planning ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_1 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:00Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:00 x: found by a read-only review of token/token.spi. 'refreshed' reads the env file, then 'exchanged' posts the refresh token to X, which rotates it server-side (X's OAuth 2.0 refresh tokens are single-use), then 'saved' writes the file. write_text (line 20) is std::fs::write: it truncates and then writes, with no temp file and rename. On failure, line 72 returns only 'could not write <path>' (exit 4), and the new access and refresh tokens are dropped. After that, the file holds the now-invalid old token (if the open failed) or a truncated file (if the write failed midway), and only a fresh browser OAuth authorization recovers. Fix: write to <path>.tmp in the same folder, flush, then std::fs::rename over the original (rename replaces an existing target on Windows too). If that fails, write the new pair to <path>.renewed next to it and name that file in the exit-4 message, never print secrets to the console (memory x-api-credentials). Minor: split_on on '\\n' keeps '\\r' on CRLF env files and rewritten() rejoins with '\\n', so a CRLF file comes back mixed. Keep the file's line ending. Tests: a read-only target path keeps the old file intact and writes .renewed; a CRLF file round-trips.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_2 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:08Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:08 x: correction to note 1's CRLF point. key_line (token.spi:45-47) keeps a trailing '\\r' on the lines it replaces, so an existing CRLF file keeps its endings for the two token keys. Only a key that with_key APPENDS (the file had no X_OAUTH2_REFRESH_TOKEN line yet) gets a bare '\\n'. So it is minor: append with the file's own line ending. The lost-token issue stands unchanged.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_3 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:09Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:09 x: a draft fix is at C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE.md (not compiled). It adds boundary primitives synced_write (File::create + write_all + sync_all) and renamed (std::fs::rename, which replaces on Windows and Unix); 'replaced' writes <path>.tmp and renames it over; on failure, saved -> kept_aside writes <path>.renewed and names it, never printing secrets. Appended keys keep the file's line ending, and a test plan is included. Post weights checked with 'xc post-thread --dry-run': 11 posts, max 267, no cell split (the first version's kept_aside spilled and was split into set_aside / token_lost). Open at compile time: '&&' short-circuit on the Rust backend, and the '?' inside the closure of the one-expression primitive.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_4 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:07Z", "x", "2026-10-23 05:07 x: the draft now COMPILES, partly verified. C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_token_refresh_safe.ps1 applies it with asserted anchors; on a scratch copy (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck2, x untouched) the Spiral compiler built token/token.spi to Rust in 15 s (26 KB) under an eoie compile window. sync_all, std::fs::rename, the .renewed sidecar and the 'authorize again' message are in the output, so synced_write / renamed / file_replaced / kept_aside / line_end were type-checked. A pre-run name check renamed 'replaced' to 'file_replaced' (world.spi:113 has a 'replaced'; token.spi does not open world, but the clash was a trap). NOT yet verified: rustc on the generated code (the '?' inside the closure of synced_write) and a test of the failure path. Both go in the first pre-commit after applying.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_5 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 5u32, "2026-10-24T07:07Z", "x", "10-24 03:07 x review (CPU-free): the atomic replace holds on Windows. file_replaced = synced_write(path.tmp) (File::create + write_all + sync_all) and then std::fs::rename(tmp, path). On Windows, std's rename is MoveFileExW with MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING, and the temp file sits in the same directory, so it's a same-volume replace; a crash leaves either the old file or the complete new one. The known failure (the destination opened without FILE_SHARE_DELETE, e.g. by an editor) makes rename return Err, and the draft then keeps the renewed tokens in the aside file and prints where (line 34), so the rotated token is never lost. Nothing to change. Directory fsync doesn't apply on NTFS.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_6 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 6u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_token_refresh_safe_7 () = Note ("X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl eoie_job_env () = Task ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", "job host: a job runs with the host's environment, not its caller's; record the caller environment at start and adopt it in the supervisor", TaskKind, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "EOIE-JOB-HOST", "eoie,jobs,process", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_job_env () = Planning ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", Minutes 40u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:02Z", "eoie", "00:02 eoie: found reading host_run.spi:22. The host spawns 'eoie job supervise <id>' with its own environment, which comes from whichever caller started the host (idle-out 10 min), and nothing in the start request carries the caller's variables. The 10-21 binary spawned supervisors from the caller, so variables set before 'eoie job start' (SPIRAL_CC for matrix runs, SPIRAL_ZIG, CARGO_BUILD_JOBS, SPIRAL_HEAVY_MAX_MINUTES, EOIE_HEAVY_*) reached the job; with F installed they silently stop reaching it, and one caller's variables leak into every job while its host lives. Peers were warned at 00:0x with the workaround (set variables inside the job script). Rule, set before writing: P0 because F changes behavior for every lane at install. Fix in GE2: launch writes the caller's environment to the job folder (env, NUL-separated KEY=VALUE), and the supervisor adopts it before the gates (set listed variables, remove unlisted ones except EOIE_JOB_*), so the window gate, the slot gate and the child all see the caller's environment. Test: two starts under one fixture home with different values of a variable; the second job must print its own value (it fails today, since the host keeps the first caller's environment).")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:24Z", "eoie", "00:24 eoie: drafted. (1) lib env.spi: environment_snapshot (NUL-separated KEY=VALUE of every variable) and environment_restored snapshot kept_prefix (set listed variables, remove unlisted ones unless their name has the prefix); Rust only, other targets fail loudly, round-trip test block (scratch job-env/apply-lib-env-snapshot.ps1). (2) eoie (job-env/apply-job-env.ps1, applied after GE2's E2 wave): launch writes job_file env from env.environment_snapshot; eoie_jobs job_environment_adopted restores it keeping EOIE_JOB_* and deletes the file (file_system.file_delete), so secrets don't sit in job folders past the start; supervise_job calls it first, before the after-wait, the gates and the spawn. (3) stop_cli gains each_job_runs_with_the_environment_of_its_own_caller: two starts under one fixture home with EOIE_ENV_PROBE=first, then second; each job's stdout must be its own value, and the env file must be gone. The chain G, E2, job-env, H dry-runs clean on a src copy. launch-GE2 refuses until lib env.spi has environment_restored. Order: Cells-gate the lib file alone after F8's 'past lib reads' and main's landing, land it, then launch GE2.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 3u32, "2026-10-23T05:26Z", "eoie", "01:26 eoie: second host-inheritance problem, measured. The host also inherits its creator's priority class. Windows gives a child the creator's class when that class is BelowNormal or Idle and no class flag is passed, and host_run.spi spawns the host with 0x0100_0208 (no class flag). Probe with F6's debug candidate from a BelowNormal pwsh: 'caller priority: BelowNormal; host priority: BelowNormal'. Every heavy job's script runs BelowNormal (Invoke-Heavy), so a host first started from inside one (F8's own acceptance step right after install) would run every lane's later jobs BelowNormal, tibia's live client included. Fix for GE2: job-env/apply-host-normal-priority.ps1 adds NORMAL_PRIORITY_CLASS (0x0100_0228 / 0x0000_0228), chained in launch-GE2 and its prelaunch. GE2's probe now blocks unless the candidate host started from its BelowNormal job is Normal. Tonight's mitigation: scratch host-hygiene.ps1 runs after F8 installs. An idle host is stopped and a fresh one started from a clean Normal shell, which also drops F8's environment (CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2, the Cells retire floor 2048, SPIRAL_HEAVY_MAX_MINUTES); a busy host gets PriorityClass Normal in place, and I rerun once it idles. Both paths were tested on a fixture home with the debug candidate.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 4u32, "2026-10-23T05:31Z", "eoie", "01:31 eoie: order decided. The lib gate (scratch job-env/libgate-copy.ps1: copy the landed lib, apply env snapshot/restore and last_write_ms, lib_gate.ps1 -RawOut on env.spi and file_system.spi) runs only when no live-gui window holds or waits, per memory live-runs-alone. tibia's night block has been waiting since 00:16 and starts when F8 and main's next5d release their heavy windows, so the gate runs after the block, around 06:30. GE2 can't launch during the block either (launch-GE2 refuses on an exclusive window), so nothing is lost. Sequence: F8 installs -> host-hygiene -> post-F -> main's lib landing after my 'past lib reads' -> block -> lib gate -> land my lib parts on main's landed tree -> launch-GE2.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_5 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 5u32, "2026-10-23T07:31Z", "eoie", "03:31 eoie: the three lib slices (env snapshot/restore, file_system.last_write_ms with the temp-path test fix, file_system.append_all_text) now append at the end of env.spi and file_system.spi instead of anchoring on headings, so main's 25-file landing (which touches file_system.spi) can't break their anchors. Everything they use is defined earlier in the file. Each is idempotent (a rerun writes nothing), and a dry run of all three on lib copies puts them at the tail in order.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_6 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 6u32, "2026-10-23T08:43Z", "eoie", "04:43 main landed its 25-file lib at 04:40:05 (hashes verified: env E5F81A293E209276, file_system 4756D432D99475E4, package.spiproj EA124BCBC41E4722). My 4 lib slices are gating on a fresh copy (libgate-copy -Tag land). Landing rule set before the result: lib_land.ps1 -Apply only if all 3 gates pass, after F9 prints 'release install done' or settles (F9 reads lib/spiral during its stop_cli compile and regen steps), and only while the tree hashes still equal the copy's (lib_land refuses otherwise -> re-gate). Then preview-GE2 (now with the no-inherit slice), candidate pre-run, launch-GE2.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_7 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 7u32, "2026-10-23T08:50Z", "eoie", "04:50 lib gate on main's landed lib (copy libgate-land): csv.spi 2/2 pass; file_system.spi's new tests pass, and 3 old tests fail only because the scratch copy has no workspace/ or README.md above it (find_parent: 'No parent for dir workspace ... until c:\\'); env.spi FAILED an_environment_snapshot_restores_to_itself (snapshot after restore differed). Cause: Rust's Windows vars_os yields hidden per-drive keys like '=C:' (std finds the separator from position 1), but environment_restored split pairs with split_once('='), so '=C:=C:\\x' became an empty key, was filtered out of the wanted set, and the removal loop then deleted =C: (a real bug: every GE2 supervisor would have dropped its per-drive cwd variables). Fix in apply-lib-env-snapshot.ps1 (backup .before-hidden-keys): the separator is searched from the second char (char_indices, byte-safe), keys starting with '=' are never removed or set, and set_var runs only when the value differs (an identity restore changes nothing). Re-gate on the same copy with repo markers added (libgate-regate.ps1: env.spi + file_system.spi). Landing rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_8 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 8u32, "2026-10-23T08:56Z", "eoie", "04:56 re-gate on the same copy: env.spi 5/5 PASS with the hidden-key fix. file_system.spi still 17/20: find_parent (fsharp), find_parent_2 (rust), trace_file (fsharp) assert the found root folder is named spiral or polyglot ('_assert_in { x = [libgate-land]; items = [spiral; polyglot] }'), so no copy outside a folder named spiral can pass them; my last_write_ms and append_all_text tests are among the 17 passes. Rule set before the next run: after F9 settles, gate file_system.spi once more from a copy rooted at a folder named spiral (scratch libgate-spiral\\spiral\\lib\\spiral, with workspace/ and README.md). Land if 20/20, or if only those 3 fail with errors naming none of my functions; otherwise fix first.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_9 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 9u32, "2026-10-23T09:04Z", "eoie", "05:04 after-install-GE2.ps1 gains GE2's core checks on the INSTALLED binary: EOIE-JOB-ENV (fixture home: first job starts the host without EOIE_ENV_PROBE, then the variable is set and a second job must echo probe=adopted-42), EOIE-JOB-HOST handles (pipe-release-check -Eoie installed) and breakaway (nested-host-check -Eoie installed). Positive control planned right after F9 installs: run it against F9's binary, where all three must FAIL (host without env adoption, inherited handles, no BREAKAWAY_OK); the old 10-21 binary would pass the env probe vacuously (no host), so F9's binary is the right control.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_10 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 10u32, "2026-10-23T09:44Z", "eoie", "05:44 all lib gates pass: env.spi 5/5, file_system.spi 20/20 (from a copy rooted at a folder named spiral, so the 3 location tests pass too), csv.spi 2/2. Landing (lib_land, tree hashes E5F81A29/4756D432/EA124BCB/absent -> 93FB080E/90E5CD2E/DC64AFD4/318ED8DC) waits for a gap with no Cells run reading the lib: 2 Cells VMs (main/tibia jobs) started 05:42; asked main for the gap. GE2's prelaunch with -Originals is clean, and GE2's rebase dry run is clean; launch-GE2 refuses until environment_restored is in the lib.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_11 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 11u32, "2026-10-23T10:40Z", "eoie", "06:40 lib landed 06:38:09 through land-lib-batch.ps1 in main's gap (fiscal gate stopped early, no spiral.notebook erl alive): env.spi 93FB080E4D303EB4, file_system.spi 90E5CD2E1531EAF9, package.spiproj DC64AFD4CC940A37, csv.spi 318ED8DC3D54C836, all equal to the gated copy; main verified them in the tree. launch-GE2's lib precondition (environment_restored) now holds. GE2's heavy preview started as job-1792751959794-eoie-preview-GE2 (supervised by host 34888, 2 h limit, through fmt-rebase); next: candidate pre-run, then launch-GE2.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_12 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 12u32, "2026-10-23T14:53Z", "eoie", "10:53 eoie: INCIDENT, measured. From about 09:52 to 10:50 every DETACHED job's window gate was granted at once. Host 29276 (HP install) was spawned from my release script after an in-process one-call runner pass. Runner case 40 sets EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME to a fixture registry, and the runner's finally cleared only EOIE_JOBS_HOME, so the variable leaked into the release script and from there into the shared host. Supervisors inherit the host's environment, so their gates read an empty registry. Effect: my eoie-preview-GE2 ran 10:26:15-10:40:00 during tibia's PR-26 live block (runs 2-4 flagged by tibia; no other session's job ran in the span, confirmed by main, tibia and x). Diagnosis trap: job run supervises in the caller's shell and so looked correct; only job start reproduces the host path. Proof: a detached job's 'window check compile' said free, and its cmd /c set showed EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME=...eoie-one-call-31592-40-windows. Mitigation at 10:50, nothing stopped: the empty fixture dir became a junction to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\windows; a detached check now reports the live-gui hold. Source fix at 10:5x (lab one-call-runner.ps1): snapshot the caller's EOIE_*/SPIRAL_* variables per case, restore them in finally, and remove the -windows fixture dir; verified by an in-process full pass (38/40; EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME and EOIE_JOBS_HOME unset afterwards, sentinel kept, 0 leftover dirs). Permanent fix stays this item's GE2 slice (supervisor adopts the caller env before the gates). The host env also carries CARGO_BUILD_JOBS from the same script. Rule for GE2's acceptance: a detached 'window check' during a held live-gui window must report blocked.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_13 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 13u32, "2026-10-23T16:30Z", "eoie", "12:30 eoie: residual symptoms on host 29276 until GE2 installs (detached 'cmd /c set' vs a shell): CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 is set for every detached job (from my HP release script), so detached cargo builds machine-wide run at -j2; EOIE_LEASE_POLICY (renewing in agent shells) is absent, so agile writes from inside detached jobs use the strict default. EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME is neutralized by the 10:50 junction. A new host only spawns for a new binary, so these stay until GE2's install brings its host, which adopts each caller's environment per job anyway.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_14 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 14u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): PROC-8 (one capture contract: cwd, env, unset), TOOL-1 (profiles passed to every job, with EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES F4). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_15 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 15u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: GE2's job-env work (job_environment_adopted in eoie_jobs, env.environment_restored from lib) inlines ~121 generated lines into every crate that calls it, and pushed eoie_job_supervise to 1062 lines, one of GE2's four topology refusals. The supervise split (crate-splits\\supervise-split.do) moves the call behind a new eoie_job_env crate; keep future environment helpers there.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_16 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 16u32, "2026-10-24T04:12Z", "eoie", "00:12 status: code landed with GE2 (15:00) and ships in the GE3 release queued at 00:05 (job-1792814703946); GE3's acceptance runs the EOIE-JOB-ENV probe (a job started after the host sees the caller's variable). Progress 50 -> 80. Open question for after the install: host 29276 still carries CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 from the HP release script (note of 12:30), so detached cargo builds machine-wide run at -j2. Main's chains keep that host busy, so it never idle-exits and the leak outlives installs. Check after GE3: does environment_restored also remove variables absent from the caller's snapshot (if not, the adopted environment still has CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2)? If not, either restore exactly (unset extras) or let EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT's per-owner hosts retire 29276. Measure with a detached 'cmd /c set CARGO' job after GE3 installs.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_17 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 17u32, "2026-10-24T04:13Z", "eoie", "00:13 answer to the 00:12 question, read from lib env.spi environment_restored: it restores exactly. It removes every variable missing from the caller's snapshot (except '=' keys and the kept EOIE_JOB_ prefix), then sets the snapshot's values. So after GE3 installs, each new supervisor adopts its caller's environment exactly, and host 29276's CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 stops reaching jobs started by the new CLI, even while that old host keeps serving. Post-install check stays: a detached 'cmd /c set CARGO' job from a shell without the variable must print nothing.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_18 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 18u32, "2026-10-24T11:22Z", "eoie", "07:2x groom: the eoie_job_env crate (environment adoption, environment_restored over lib env) is in the live tree since GE3 landed at 00:05 (ok  files 5 searched  1 matched  matches 2  15ms  07:22:28-04:00) and installs with GE3's release, which is in build/tests now (regen 122/122 at 06:42). Done = installed, plus its after-install check: a detached job sees the caller's variables, not the host's.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_19 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 19u32, "2026-10-24T11:23Z", "eoie", "07:23 groom: blocked on the train. The only work left is to install GE3's release and run its after-install check, so 'agile next' must not hand this out as ready work. Back to Active when GE3's release installs.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_env_20 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-ENV", 20u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "eoie", "19:45 groom: the release condition is met. GE3's rerun (GE3b, job-1792850533961) installed at 11:46, and after-install-GE3 passed 12/12. Done.")
inl bend_prune_if_params () = Task ("BEND-PRUNE-IF-PARAMS", "Bend lifted if-helpers (name_ifN) take only the variables their branches use: 44.2% of their parameters are unused (18,376 of 41,604 over 4,101 helpers), and pruning to a fixpoint leaves 14,213 parameters; -4.7% of Bend output bytes", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "PACK-NEXT-4", "compiler,bend,codegen,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_bend_prune_if_params () = Planning ("BEND-PRUNE-IF-PARAMS", Minutes 90u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_bend_prune_if_params_1 () = Note ("BEND-PRUNE-IF-PARAMS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 00:25 main: measured with scratch bend_if_params_census.py; mirror bend_prune_mirror.py, 38 s over 470 main.bend after replacing per-name regexes with one call scan per iteration. Rule: a parameter of a def <x>_ifN(...) that its body never names (the condition c is always kept) is removed from the def and from every call with a matching argument count; repeat to a fixpoint, since a parameter only forwarded to a pruned helper becomes unused in turn. Example closure_curry: spiral_main_if1(v3, v0, v1, v2, v3) becomes spiral_main_if1(v3). Check: the 15 most-pruned programs run under bend2 (bun) with the original exit code and stdout. Better done in codegenBend (pass only the branch's free variables) than as a text pass; the mirror is the reference either way. Not in next4 yet: it needs an F# port, a byte check, and Bend's ~200 matrix rows.")
inl note_of_bend_prune_if_params_2 () = Note ("BEND-PRUNE-IF-PARAMS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 00:37 patched into pack-next4 as pack-next6\\bend-prune.ps1 (definition before codegenBend; body piped through bendPruneIfParameters before bendUsedPrelude). Core 87EB43CF41975958 parses under SINGLE_FLIGHT and HOPACSOTA. Review rule in locals_check.py: before starts with the full prelude and after ends with the mirror-pruned body; self-test on 417 tree samples accepts 417 (305 bodies pruned).")
inl note_of_bend_prune_if_params_3 () = Note ("BEND-PRUNE-IF-PARAMS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T06:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:53 main: bend2 run-time probe of the 6 most-pruned programs (bun bend2 main.ts as test.ps1:668, second of 2 rounds) is INCONCLUSIVE: it overlapped the start of tibia's live block at 02:50. The swings are too large to be the change: utf8_normalization 2,781 -> 1,251 ms, canonical_equal 8,136 -> 4,470, but the two mega_brzozowski programs 24.2 -> 32.7 s and 25.0 -> 36.3 s, total 65.6 -> 80.2 s. Exits equal (0, and 1 for the invalid sample). Rule for the rerun on a quiet box: 3 alternating rounds; call it a speedup or slowdown only if the per-program median moves at least 10% in the same direction in 2 of 3 rounds. Correctness does not rest on this; it rests on the 417/417 mirror equality, the bend2 runs of 15 programs and the matrix review.")
inl note_of_bend_prune_if_params_4 () = Note ("BEND-PRUNE-IF-PARAMS", 4u32, "2026-10-25T13:20Z", "main", "10-25 09:19 main groom: DONE by its rule (implemented, reviewed, installed). The slice's bendPruneIfParameters is in the installed tree core apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs. Negative control: the 5l'-only luaSpillLocals is absent, so the grep tells installed from staged. It has passed every accepted review since, latest the 5j accept single-flight-20261025-010749 (DISAGREE 0). Uncommitted until the user commits the compiler layer.")
inl x_emoji_weight () = Task ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", "x: emoji sequences weigh 2 as one entity, as twitter-text v3 counts them (x counts per code point: thumbs-up with skin tone = 4, a ZWJ family = 11)", Story, P3, D3, 85u32, Active, "X-DONE", "x,glyphs,weights", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_emoji_weight () = Planning ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_1 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:25Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:25 x: found while measuring X-E2E-NON-ASCII's draft texts. twitter-text's config/v3.json has emojiParsingEnabled: true and defaultWeight 200 (scale 100), and js/src/parseTweet.js:37-53 extracts emoji entities with twemoji-parser, adds defaultWeight ONCE per entity and skips its length. So a skin-tone thumbs-up and a 4-person ZWJ family each weigh 2. x's weight (glyphs.spi, per code point by v3 ranges) gives 4 and 11 (measured with xc post-thread --dry-run), and glyphs.spi's weights_follow_x asserts thumb = 4. No backlog note shows a live X measurement behind that 4. Effect: safe but not exact. x over-counts emoji, so posts split early (never over 280), and x-compat's one-post-per-definition check can flag emoji-rich definitions that X would accept. Fix: x already segments graphemes (unicode-segmentation, gleam string graphemes), so a grapheme cluster that contains an emoji code point weighs 2 in total. The tests become thumb = 2 and the ZWJ family = 2, and keep U+2014 + U+200B = 2. Before changing, check twemoji-parser's entity rules against grapheme clusters for flags (regional-indicator pairs) and keycaps, and ideally confirm one value against X's live counter when the user allows a write.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_2 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:26Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:26 x: scope beyond x's glyphs.spi. The same per-code-point model lives in spiral/apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/x_compat.ex:112-113 (weight = sum of code_point_weight over the charlist, ranges from the profile) and in the compiler's XCompat overweight check (spiral_compiler.fs:183089: 'CJK and emoji weigh 2'). The profile x/postability/x.json describes weights only as code_point_weight ranges, so it cannot express 'an emoji entity weighs default_weight once'. Fix order: add an emoji-entity rule to the profile schema (e.g. post.emoji_entity_weight: 2, segmented by extended grapheme clusters), then x's glyphs.spi, Cells' x_compat.ex and the compiler read it. The Cells and compiler parts are main's (told 2026-10-23).")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_3 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:48Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:48 x: a design draft (steps, edge cases, tests; no code yet) is at C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-THREAD-RESUME-and-X-EMOJI-WEIGHT.md.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_4 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 4u32, "2026-10-23T12:49Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:52 x: RULE, written before the code. A grapheme cluster is an emoji entity (weight 2 in total) when it has more than one code point and ONE of these holds: (a) its first code point is >= U+1F000 (pictographs, regional-indicator flag pairs, tag flags, ZWJ sequences, skin tones); (b) it contains U+FE0F and its first code point is in U+2000..U+2BFF or is U+00A9, U+00AE, U+3030, U+303D, U+3297 or U+3299 (text-default symbols made emoji by VS16); (c) it contains U+20E3 and starts with 0-9, # or * (keycaps). Every other cluster keeps the per-code-point weight, so the change can only LOWER a weight, and only for multi-code-point emoji clusters. Deliberately unchanged: a lone (c) or (R) without VS16 stays 1, and a letter + U+FE0F stays 3, because twemoji does not parse those as emoji and lowering them could produce a 281-weight post. Code shape: glyph_weight g = if emoji_entity g then 2 else plain_weight g, with two one-expression primitives (first_code_point, code_point_count) on both backends. VS16/keycap checks use sm'.index_of. splitter's posted_unit and budget_for switch to glyph_weight. Expected tests: thumb + skin tone 4 -> 2, the ZWJ family 11 -> 2, heart + VS16 4 -> 2, keycap 1 5 -> 2, JP flag 4 -> 2. Unchanged: decomposed e 2, a + VS16 3, CJK 2, U+2014 + ZWSP 2. Cells' x_compat.ex and the compiler's XCompat stay main's (note 2).")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_5 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 5u32, "2026-10-23T13:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:27 x: the glyphs half PASSES. Cells on scratch copy C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-emoji\\client (x tree + apply_emoji_weight.ps1): glyphs.spi 8/8 on rust + gleam, including emoji_entities_weigh_two (thumb + skin tone, ZWJ family, heart + VS16, keycap 1, JP flag all = 2) and other_clusters_keep_code_point_weights (decomposed e 2, a + VS16 3, CJK 2, lone (c) 1, a 1). first_code_point and code_point_count agree on both backends. splitter.spi (units use glyph_weight) is running. Landing still waits for main's x_compat.ex / XC005 side (note 2), so the checkers agree with x.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_6 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 6u32, "2026-10-23T13:29Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:29 x: splitter.spi with glyph_weight units PASSES 7/7 (rust + gleam) on the same scratch copy, so the whole x-side draft is green. Emoji-free sources keep their weights by construction (glyph_weight only differs for multi-code-point emoji clusters), and no existing splitter test changed.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_7 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 7u32, "2026-10-23T14:19Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:19 x: open question from note 4 CLOSED against twemoji-parser's source (src/lib/regex.js, read-only). Bare (c), (R) and TM are NOT entities: their only branch is '(?:[(c)(R)\\u2122\\u265f]\\ufe0f)', where FE0F is mandatory. Keycaps match with or without FE0F ('[#*0-9]\\ufe0f?\\u20e3'), which rule (c) already covers: it only needs U+20E3 in the cluster. Safety argument completed: the only emoji in x's weight-1 ranges (0-4351, 8192-8205, 8208-8223, 8242-8247) are (c) and (R), and those need FE0F, which rule (b) weighs 2. (Double-exclamation U+203C and U+2049 are 8252 and 8265, already weight 2.) So no lone code point weighs more on X than in x, and the draft can only remove over-counting, never cause a 281-weight post.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_8 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 8u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_emoji_weight_9 () = Note ("X-EMOJI-WEIGHT", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl x_lookup_limits () = Task ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", "x client: by-ID lookups are chunked to X's 100-item limit instead of truncating posts silently and sending every user ID in one request", Story, P2, D2, 85u32, Active, "X-DONE,X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", "x,client,api", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_lookup_limits () = Planning ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_1 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:39Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:39 x: found by a read-only review. X API v2 lookups (/2/tweets?ids=, /2/users?ids=, /2/users/by?usernames=) take at most 100 items per request. (1) refs.spi:36 posts_by sends 'take_first 100 wanted': it respects the limit but silently drops every referenced post after the first 100 at each depth level, with no failure, note or count, so a large timeline shows references as missing with no reason. (2) users.spi:53-54 by_ids (called from xc.spi:138 with refs' 'unnamed' author list, which grows with the timeline) puts every ID into one /2/users?ids= request, so past 100 unnamed authors X rejects it and with_users returns the failure for the whole timeline run. (3) users.spi:23 'named' does the same for CLI usernames (only matters past 100 typed names). e2e's worlds stay far below 100, so nothing exercised it. Fix: one chunking helper (chunks of 100 over a list, results merged in order, the first failure kept) used by all three. Tests: 250 IDs -> 3 requests (100, 100, 50) against a counting fake fetch, and a refs level with 150 wanted posts fetches all 150. Cost note: more requests means more paid reads, which is the API's real price for the data.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_2 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:40Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:40 x: correction to note 1, point (3). CLI usernames cannot exceed the limit: cli.spi:265 rejects more than lookup_limit () = 100 accounts per run, and --per-account is validated to 5..100 (cli.spi:167, tested at :424). So the scope is (1) refs' silent truncation and (2) users by_ids with refs' unnamed authors, which the CLI cannot bound because the list comes from the API's referenced posts. The chunking helper can reuse lookup_limit () from cli.spi (move it to wire.spi, next to the paths, so all three share one constant).")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_3 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T04:41Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:41 x: a draft is at C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-LOOKUP-LIMITS.md (not compiled). It moves lookup_limit () to wire.spi and adds chunked/dropped plus all_got (the first failure wins, otherwise results in order). refs gets posts_chunks so every chunk is fetched and absorbed, users by_ids goes per chunk with merged found/unknown lists, and the tests are listed. Post weights via xc post-thread --dry-run: 5 posts, max 238, no split.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_4 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:21Z", "x", "2026-10-23 05:21 x: the refs half of the draft COMPILES. C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_lookup_limits_refs.ps1 (asserted anchors) adds after_first / chunked / all_got to wire.spi after 'type got r'. 'dropped' was renamed after_first because world.spi:105 has one. It replaces refs' posts_by (take_first 100) with posts_chunks (chunked (lookup_limit ()) + all_got; refs opens cli directly) and makes deepened take got (list fetched), with next_of_all / absorbed_all. On a scratch copy (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck5, x untouched), refs/refs.spi compiled to Rust in 15 s (47 KB) under an eoie compile window. Not done: the users by_ids half and the counting-fetch tests (chunks of 100/100/50, 150 wanted posts absorbed); both come with applying. Tonight's compile checks stopped here, at 2.4 GB free.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_5 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 5u32, "2026-10-23T12:07Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:07 x: the users half COMPILES too. C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_lookup_limits_users.ps1 (applied after the refs script) adds one primitive, wire.json_text (serde Value to text), and rewrites users.by_ids: ids_chunks (chunked by lookup_limit ()), one request per chunk, all_got, then merged_users joins every chunk's 'data' and 'errors' arrays into one document. by_ids' contract (one body for xc's users_entry) is unchanged, so xc.spi needs no edit, and users_of_doc keeps reading 'errors' for unknown ids. Both scripts applied in sequence to a scratch copy (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck6): users/users.spi compiled to Rust in 24 s, with the merge in the output. Still at apply time: tests with a counting fake fetch (250 ids -> 3 requests; 150 wanted posts all absorbed).")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_6 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 6u32, "2026-10-24T03:55Z", "x", "23:55 x: lib-first follow-up, drafted to apply AFTER this item's two drafts and AFTER lib part B (listm' skip_/chunks_) is live: programs-x-lookup-lib\\land.eoie (eoie do, 3 files, 4 replacements). It deletes wire.spi's after_first/chunked/all_got. refs.spi and users.spi call listm'.chunks_ and resultm.collect_list (part A) directly; these are the only 3 call sites of the 9 references. Semantics are the same for n > 0. For n <= 0, x's chunked never terminates (after_first 0 returns the list unchanged), while lib chunks_ returns [items]. That's latent today (lookup_limit is 100). collect_list is all_got's exact contract (first Error, otherwise the values in order). Dry-run fits the landing-trial tree. Check job-1792814090102 (--after-ok the part B lander) applies it to %TEMP%\\x-glyphs-check and gates wire, refs and users.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_7 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 7u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_8 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1). Not closable on that alone: the counting-fake-fetch tests promised in n4/n5 are not in HEAD (git grep finds no chunk test). Left: refs test (150 wanted posts, all absorbed) and users test (250 ids -> 3 requests of 100/100/50), written against lib listm'.chunks_ once lib part B lands (n6 program), so they test the final code.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_9 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 9u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "x", "10-24 19:44 x: UNBLOCKED: lib part B (listm' skip_/chunks_) is live since 17:58 (held-files runner, job-1792875049850). Next, CPU-light to draft: programs-x-lookup-lib (n6) on the real tree, then the two counting-fake-fetch tests against listm'.chunks_ (users: 250 ids -> 100/100/50 requests; refs: 150 wanted posts all absorbed). They gate together with X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS' verify-landing5.")
inl note_of_x_lookup_limits_10 () = Note ("X-LOOKUP-LIMITS", 10u32, "2026-10-25T06:05Z", "x", "10-25 02:05 x: lib-first follow-up and the promised tests, written by hand in edition-3 spelling (the n6 eoie program anchored on edition-2 text, now stale). wire.spi drops after_first/chunked/all_got. refs.spi gets chunk_fetched (fetch : post_ids -> got r) = listm'.chunks_ (lookup_limit ()) |> each fetch |> resultm.collect_list, and posts_chunks calls it with get_raw. users.spi's ids_chunks uses listm'.chunks_ and by_ids uses resultm.collect_list. Semantics are identical for n > 0 (lib skip_/chunks_ are x's after_first/chunked moved to lib; collect_list is all_got with its accumulator inside). Tests: refs a_lookup_level_fetches_every_wanted_post (150 wanted posts through a counting fake fetch give request sizes [100; 50], all 150) and users user_ids_chunk_to_the_lookup_limit (250 ids give [100; 100; 50]). They run in verify-landing10's notebook stage (job-1792904826458), whose crate regen at 01:51 predates these 02:02 edits.")
inl x_read_budget () = Task ("X-READ-BUDGET", "x client: xc timeline reports how many paid API requests and posts it read, and '--max-following all' over a large follow list needs an explicit bound", Story, P2, D2, 85u32, Active, "X-DONE,X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", "x,client,api,cost", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_read_budget () = Planning ("X-READ-BUDGET", Minutes 40u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_read_budget_1 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:42Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:42 x: found by a read-only review. Only post creation is priced (splitter.spi:152 post_price_thousandths = 15, shown by post-thread --dry-run). Reads have no count or estimate. 'xc timeline --following USERNAME --max-following all' reads one timeline per followed account, plus the reference and user lookups, so an account that follows thousands makes thousands of paid requests in one run, with nothing printed before or after. The default (20) is safe; 'all' is the risk. Fix: (1) every read command ends with one line 'N requests, M posts read' (count in transport, where get_raw is), (2) 'all' resolves to the real following count first and refuses above a named cap (e.g. 200) unless --max-following is given as a number. Price per read is left out on purpose: X's current read pricing must come from X's docs when this is done, not from memory.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_2 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:48Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:48 x: a draft is at C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-READ-BUDGET.md (not compiled). Every request passes xc_net::xc_send (transport.spi:11 -> net.spi sent_of -> exchange) or xc_post_form, so one AtomicU32 there counts a run's requests: counted () before exchange, an xc_requests export. xc prints '--- N API requests, M posts read' on stderr (keeps 'xc thread ID > file' clean). '--max-following all' is rejected above following_cap () = 200 unless a number confirms it. Tests: an e2e count for 'xc timeline alice' = 1 and the cli cap. Post weights: 5 posts, max 212. Open: how net.spi may declare the static under the style rules.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_3 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 3u32, "2026-10-23T09:19Z", "x", "2026-10-23 05:19 x: the net part of the draft COMPILES. It settles the open question of note 2: a top-level Rust static is declared the way glyphs.spi declares Gleam externals, through an inl calling global (request_counter ()). The increment stays one expression typed () via drop(XC_REQUESTS.fetch_add(...)). C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_read_budget_net.ps1 adds request_counted () to exchange and form_exchange (every request) and exports xc_requests. On a scratch copy (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck4, x untouched), net/net.spi compiled to Rust in 14 s: 'static XC_REQUESTS' appears exactly once (global emitted once despite 2 callers), fetch_add twice, and the xc_requests export once. The helper was renamed counted -> request_counted after the run (posts.spi:231 has a 'counted'; nothing opens net, so the compiled result stands). Still to draft at apply time: xc.spi's stderr cost line and the --max-following all cap.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_4 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 4u32, "2026-10-23T11:09Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:09 x: grooming: P3 -> P2. Unbounded paid reads are money loss, which X-DONE note 55's order puts in the top tier, next to X-PAGING-GUARD.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_5 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 5u32, "2026-10-23T12:12Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:12 x: the xc half COMPILES with the net half. C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_read_budget_xc.ps1 (after apply_read_budget_net.ps1) adds requests_made (calls xc_net::xc_requests) and cost_noted, which writes 'xc: N API requests, M posts shown' on stderr at the end of shown_timeline, and 'xc: N API requests' after a thread read. On a scratch copy (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck7) xc/xc.spi compiled to Rust in 20 s with both in the output. CORRECTION to note 2: e2e does compare stderr. e2e.ps1:37 builds each step's text from stderr lines then stdout lines, so the cost line changes every read step's expected output in the README tutorial. Applying it therefore means e2e.ps1 -Accept plus a reviewed README diff. Not drafted yet: the --max-following all cap in cli.spi.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_6 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 6u32, "2026-10-23T12:15Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:15 x: the --max-following all cap COMPILES too, so the item is fully drafted (counter, cost line, cap). C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_read_budget_cap.ps1 changes users.followed: with 'all' (cap < 0) it pages up to following_cap () + 1 = 201 accounts instead of the i32 max, and if more come back it refuses (exit 4) with '--max-following all would read more than 200 timelines; pass a number to confirm'. A numeric --max-following stays the explicit confirmation. No extra API call: the following list is paged anyway. Applied together with both lookup-limit scripts (all three touch users.spi at different anchors) on C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-draftcheck8: users/users.spi compiled to Rust in 32 s with the message in the output.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_7 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 7u32, "2026-10-23T13:28Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:28 x: renames in the drafts, made after their compile checks: users' following_cap () -> following_read_limit () (cli.spi has a union named following_cap that users opens; types and terms don't collide, but it read as one name for two things), and xc.spi's requests_made -> api_requests_so_far (net.spi has its own requests_made, which xc reaches only as xc_net::xc_requests). Pure renames with no other change; recompile both at apply time. apply_all_check.ps1 confirms all 11 client drafts apply together cleanly.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_8 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 8u32, "2026-10-23T17:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:30 x: CORRECTION to note 6. The drafted cost line says 'M posts shown', but this item promises posts READ, and they differ: reference lookups return billed posts that never become timeline lines, and with X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1 (below 5, X still returns and bills 5 per account) the shown count would understate paid reads further. Draft apply_read_budget_posts_read.ps1 (after read_budget_xc and per_account_min_1): digest gains posts_read bundle, the sum over primary and lookup entries (not user entries) of each response's data plus includes.posts, i.e. what X returned. It is exported as xc_posts_read, and xc's line becomes 'xc: N API requests, R posts read, M shown'. Test: posts_read_counts_what_x_returned (primary_bundle -> 1). As before, the line goes to stderr, so the README read steps change at e2e -Accept.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_9 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 9u32, "2026-10-23T17:36Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:36 x: posts-read follow-up compiles: xc.spi with all 14 drafts applied (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-allcheck) builds to 831 lines of Rust carrying the xc_posts_read call and the 'posts read' text. The digest test is queued (self-gating chain10). It is also in land_all, after per_account_min_1.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_10 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 10u32, "2026-10-24T07:06Z", "x", "10-24 03:06 x review (CPU-free, during tibia's block): the request count stays exact under tonight's lookup work. net.spi counts at the transport: exchange (line 57) and form_exchange (71) each call request_counted once. Chunked by-ID lookups (X-LOOKUP-LIMITS, and its lib-first follow-up with listm'.chunks_) therefore count one paid request per 100-item chunk. patient/patient_bodied (24-28) only set a 30 s global timeout and http_status_as_error(false), with no retry loop, so ureq sends one HTTP request per exchange and no request goes uncounted. The timeline report's request number is a true upper bound of billed reads.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_11 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 11u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_12 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 12u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_read_budget_13 () = Note ("X-READ-BUDGET", 13u32, "2026-10-24T21:02Z", "x", "10-24 17:02 x: verify3 e2e failures from this item: (1) extras 'xc timeline busy'/'gateway' now start with 'xc: 2 API requests, 0 posts read, 0 shown' before the 429/502 line, and e2e.ps1's hard-coded expectations predated the cost line. (2) The '--max-following all' check expected max_results=1000, but this item's bound reads following_read_limit()+1 = 201 accounts, so the stub's pages are 201 then 199&pagination_token=p2. Both are now expected in e2e.ps1 (unstaged). (3) Grammar: 'xc: 1 API requests' after xc thread 999. xc.spi cost_noted now goes through requests_counted (1 -> 'API request'). The README answers come from the next e2e -Accept and get reviewed by diff. Gate: job-1792875692136-x-verify-landing4.")
inl x_thread_resume () = Task ("X-THREAD-RESUME", "x client: xc post-thread can resume an interrupted thread (and paces to X's per-user posting limit) instead of forcing delete and repost", Story, P3, D3, 85u32, Active, "X-DONE,X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", "x,client,api,cost", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_thread_resume () = Planning ("X-THREAD-RESUME", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_1 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 1u32, "2026-10-23T04:43Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:43 x: found by a read-only review. post-thread (cli.spi:74, write.spi) posts every post in sequence, each replying to the previous, with no pacing and no resume flag. On a failure mid-thread (e.g. a 429 from X's per-user posting limit on a large file; the README says xc's own source travels as a thread, about 1,644 posts for the whole client), it reports the IDs posted before the failure (write.spi:56, good) but cannot continue. The only path is delete-thread plus a full repost, paying again (.015 per post by splitter.spi's price) for the posts already made. Fix: 'xc post-thread --resume LAST_ID FILE' reads the thread back (the xc thread machinery), checks that the posted prefix equals the file's first k posts byte for byte, and continues at post k+1 replying to LAST_ID; it refuses on any mismatch. Separately, pace creates against X's documented per-user write limit (take the numbers from X's current docs at implementation time, not from memory) and stop cleanly with the resume command printed when the window is used up.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_2 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 2u32, "2026-10-23T04:48Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:48 x: a design draft (steps, edge cases, tests; no code yet) is at C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\X-THREAD-RESUME-and-X-EMOJI-WEIGHT.md.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_3 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 3u32, "2026-10-23T12:59Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:59 x: code draft of the pure half (not compiled yet; queued). C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_thread_resume_plan.ps1 adds to write.spi: union resume_plan (ResumeAfter {posted; remaining} | PostDiffers {at; planned; found} | MorePostedThanPlanned n), resume_from (compares found thread texts, oldest first, with the planned posts by exact equality; wire already unescapes on read and splitter already trims), resume_refusal, resumed_posting (starts chained from After LAST_ID with the line 'resumed after post k of n'), resume_written, plus 3 tests (half-posted resumes, a changed post 2 refuses, a longer thread or no thread refuses). Plumbing still to draft, in order: (1) cli: 'post-thread --resume LAST_ID FILE' -> command ResumeThread {last; path}; (2) args: kind 10, payload LAST_ID + newline + path; (3) thread.spi: a new export xc_thread_texts(base, bearer, id) giving the texts of the chain from its root down to id, oldest first, built from the same pages that xc_thread_ids reads; (4) xc.spi: run_resume_thread splits the file with framed, reads the found texts and passes {last, planned, found} as JSON to xc_write kind 10; (5) write.spi: thread_written dispatches kind 10 to resume_written. e2e test: a stub world with posts 1..3 of a known 6-post file, then '--resume <id 3>' posts 4..6 and a tampered post 2 is refused.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_4 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 4u32, "2026-10-23T13:12Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:12 x: DECISION, CLI shape: 'xc post-thread --resume ROOT_ID FILE' instead of LAST_ID. Reason: thread_bodies searches the conversation from the given post's created_at (search_of: start_time = whole_second created_at), so reading from LAST_ID would miss every earlier post. ROOT_ID is the first 'posted' line and what delete-thread already takes. The resume then finds the chain's end itself, so the user never has to work out which post came last. Plumbing drafted: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_thread_resume_plumbing.ps1, applied after apply_thread_resume_plan.ps1. It touches cli (ResumeThread (root, path), a usage line, a parse test), args (kind 10, payload root + newline + path), thread.spi (chain_of: the root is the post whose parent is outside the set; follow the single reply; refuse on a branch with 'the thread branches after post X'; a new export xc_thread_chain returns [{id, text}] oldest first; test a_thread_chain_runs_from_its_root_to_the_last_reply), xc.spi (run_resume_thread: file check, bearer check, xc_thread_chain, then xc_write kind 10 with {planned, found}) and write.spi (resume_of: last id = the last found id, then resume_written). Applied on scratch copy C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-resume\\client. Cells queued for write, thread and cli. xc.spi needs the staged crate build (compile_check.ps1). Still to do: the e2e step with a half-posted thread in the stub world.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_5 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 5u32, "2026-10-23T13:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:27 x: the drafted code PASSES Cells except xc.spi, which needs the staged crate build. On C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-resume\\client (x tree + apply_thread_resume_plan.ps1 + apply_thread_resume_plumbing.ps1): write.spi 5/5 (rust), thread.spi 2/2 (chain root -> last reply, branch refusal, empty), cli.spi 18/18 (rust + zig, with the --resume parse test). Left: xc.spi compile (compile_check.ps1 after the live block), then a release xc.exe and the e2e fixture C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\e2e_resume.ps1. That fixture is already built and inspected: posts 9101-9103 = the first 3 of checks.spi's 9 framed posts with conversation and reply refs, 9201-9202 with post 2 changed, and 2 tutorial steps before 'Renewing the OAuth 2.0 token'. Also: apply_all_check.ps1 in the drafts folder applies all 11 client drafts, in landing order, to one fresh copy: 11/11 apply, and 0 new cross-file duplicate names after renaming following_cap -> following_read_limit (users; cli has a type of that name) and xc's requests_made -> api_requests_so_far (net has a term of that name).")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_6 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 6u32, "2026-10-23T13:55Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:55 x: desk-review fix in apply_thread_resume_plumbing.ps1 (after the 2/2 Cells pass, so thread.spi is queued again after tibia's block). chain_of took the FIRST post whose parent is outside the found set as the root. An edited post breaks that: keep latest drops the old version, so a child that replied to the old id also looks like a root. The texts would then mismatch and refuse (safe), but with a misleading 'post k differs'. Now chain_of collects every outside-parented post: exactly one is the root, two or more refuse with ChainRoots ('post X replies to a post outside the thread's chain (an edited or deleted post?); --resume needs one unbroken chain'). The test gains a 'second root 5' case. apply_all_check still applies 11/11 with no new clashes.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_7 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 7u32, "2026-10-23T15:28Z", "x", "2026-10-23 11:28 x: Cells caught my note-6 change: thread.spi 1 of 2 failed (rust), actual 'second root 1', expected 'second root 5'. In [5; 3; 1; 2], post 5 (parent 9 missing) and post 1 (the real root) are both outside-parented, and ChainRoots named whichever came second in list order, the real root. Fixed so the refusal names the ORPHAN reply (a post whose parent is missing; orphan / stray_of), which is the post the user must look at. Re-queued as job x-resume-thread-2 on a freshly rebuilt x-resume copy; the queued xc compile reads the same copy.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_8 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 8u32, "2026-10-23T15:57Z", "x", "2026-10-23 11:57 x: xc.spi with both resume drafts COMPILES (eoie job x-resume-xc, BuildSpiral Rust, 14 s, 42,262 bytes). The output carries the xc_thread_chain call and the refusal texts, so the whole draft type-checks end to end: cli, args, thread, write, xc. Left: thread.spi's re-test after the orphan fix (re-queued in a self-gating chain, because the eoie-queued run was refused by a foreign Cells run), then a release build and the e2e fixture.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_9 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 9u32, "2026-10-23T16:29Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:29 x: thread.spi with the orphan fix PASSES 2/2 (rust; self-gating chain, 11:58). The whole draft is now verified on scratch: write 5/5, thread 2/2, cli 18/18, xc.spi compiles. Progress 50 -> 70 (verified on a copy; the rest is landing + a release xc + the e2e fixture apply_e2e_resume.ps1, both in land_all).")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_10 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 10u32, "2026-10-23T23:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:52 x: the pacing half of this item, decided and DRAFTED. X's per-user posting limits depend on the tier (from 17 posts per 24 h up to per-15-min windows), and net.spi returns no rate-limit headers, so an in-process sleep would be uninformed and could last a day. With --resume in place, pacing = stop cleanly and say exactly how to continue. C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_thread_resume_hint.ps1 -Client <apps/client> (after land_all's fit, anchored on post-fit write.spi + README): posting gains resumed : bool; stopped takes the posting and ends with '; continue with: xc post-thread --resume ROOT FILE' (ROOT = the first posted id) on a fresh thread, or '; continue with the same --resume command' on a resumed one, where the first new id isn't the root. resumed_line is split out to keep resumed_posting's cell at 228 (it was 282 > XC005's budget). Tests (429 failure): the fresh hint names root 1; the resumed hint says to repeat; no hint when nothing was posted. Touched cells weigh 259 or less. README's resume paragraph now says the error ends with the command. Validated on a fresh post-fit copy; the write.spi Cells check (on landing-trial-4's copy) is queued after tibia's block; joins land_all only after it passes.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_11 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 11u32, "2026-10-24T07:08Z", "x", "10-24 03:08 x review (CPU-free): the drafts match the 19:52 decision. Pacing = stop at the 429 and end with the exact 'xc post-thread --resume <root>' (apply_thread_resume_hint.ps1:67-90), and no draft has a fixed sleep or rate constant, which is right: X's per-user window can't be known locally. Follow-up idea, not in scope and not drafted: X's 429 carries x-rate-limit-reset (epoch seconds), so the hint could print 'resume after HH:MM'. net.spi's http_reply is (status, body) only (call_reply/send_reply drop headers), so that needs a third field read via r.headers().get(\"x-rate-limit-reset\") in both arms, plus a wire failure field. Small, but it touches every exchange's tuple; worth it only if threads hit the limit in practice.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_12 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 12u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_13 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 13u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl note_of_x_thread_resume_14 () = Note ("X-THREAD-RESUME", 14u32, "2026-10-24T21:02Z", "x", "10-24 17:02 x: verify3 (job-1792840102746, 07:08) e2e FAILED on both resume steps: 9101 and 9201 printed 'no posted thread found before the resume point'. Root cause: thread.spi chain_json built the found list with wire.quoted_text, a test helper that wraps in quotes without escaping. Post texts carry newlines and quotes, so the resume payload was not valid JSON, parse_json gave nothing, planned and found were both empty, and resume_from 0 Nil Nil = ResumeAfter 0. The unit tests covered chain_listed but never chain_json. Reproduced at 16:5x with the release xc.exe against e2e/stub.py: xc thread 9101 reads the chain and --dry-run plans 9 posts, but --resume refuses. Fix (unstaged): chain_json uses wire.json_string (serde), the escaper that assemble/locate already use, plus test a_chain_keeps_multiline_quoted_texts (chain_json, then parse_json, gives back the text). Gate: job-1792875692136-x-verify-landing4.")
