inl note_of_wasm_prelude_fields_on_demand_1 () = Note ("WASM-PRELUDE-FIELDS-ON-DEMAND", 1u32, "2026-10-23T05:01Z", "main", "2026-10-23 01:01 fd_write imported but never called in 423 of 444 tree outputs, prelude data unused in 437; mirror wasm_fields_mirror.py: 2,812,732 -> 2,714,570 bytes (3.5%). Rule: an import stays when proc_exit or reached; a prelude data segment at N stays when 'i32.const N' appears in the emitted globals, functions or reached helpers (addresses are only used as literals; user data starts at 72). wasmtime runs of 60 changed outputs (40 smallest, 20 largest) equal before and after (2 differ only in the error text path and backtrace offset, exit 1 both). Pass pack-next6\\wasm-fields.ps1 in pack-next4, core ECA129CF8D4A1EFE parses under both defines; review rule in locals_check.py.")
inl tail_call_parallel_moves () = Task ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", "Self tail calls in Delphi, Zig, Lean and Wasm assign parameters by parallel-move resolution (temps only on cycles) instead of copying every argument through a temp", Story, P3, D2, 80u32, Active, "PACK-NEXT-4", "team:main,codegen,backend:delphi,backend:zig,backend:lean,backend:wasm", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tail_call_parallel_moves () = Planning ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_1 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 1u32, "2026-10-23T05:09Z", "main", "2026-10-23 01:09 census: 431 temp copies in 111 of 411 Delphi outputs; the same two-loop pattern (all args into temps, then temps into params) sits at the 4 self-loop emitters in codegenDelphi, codegenZig, codegenLean and codegenWasm. P3 because the native compilers coalesce these moves (FPC least so), so it is mostly output size and readability. Review needs temp-name canonicalization (temp numbers come from decls.Count, so removing one renumbers later temps).")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_2 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 2u32, "2026-10-23T07:02Z", "main", "2026-10-23 03:02 main: written for Delphi and Lean (the only backends with samples; Wasm has 0 such blocks in the tree samples, Zig is left alone because its review rule is a const-locals F# check that a second rewrite would entangle). Census: Delphi 115 blocks / 313 temp copies in 21 files, Lean 50 blocks / 187 copies in 33 files; exactly 1 block per backend has a real cycle (native_managed_array_tail_recursion: v1 <- v2, v2 <- v1), which keeps one temp. Patch pack-next6\\tail-moves.ps1: a shared ParallelMove union + parallelMoves (free move first in parameter order, else save the first pending target to a temp slot and redirect its readers) before CodegenDelphi, and the two self-loop emitters. Mirror tail_moves_mirror.py: Delphi 52,828 -> 52,120 lines (temp declarations dropped per function; the cycle reuses the parameter's own temp declaration, so the type matches), Lean 53,885 -> 53,619. Review rules wired: main.pas compared with temp names canonicalized by first occurrence (numbers come from decls.Count), main.lean as lean-let(moves(before)). Held for tibia's live block (no compiles of any size): the fsi check of parallelMoves against the mirror's step lists, composing into next4, the parse-only check, and fpc/lean runs of the changed programs.")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_3 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 3u32, "2026-10-23T08:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 04:57 main: native proof after tibia's block end. Delphi: all 21 changed programs (mirror output vs tree output) build with fpc -O2 -Mdelphi as test.ps1 does, and run with equal exit and stdout, 0 broken; that includes native_managed_array_tail_recursion's real cycle. Lean: 6 programs (the cycle sample + 5) under lean +v4.35.0-rc4 --run, all equal. F# parallelMoves equals the mirror's steps on 165/165 blocks. Composed into next4 (3D8553145D246A26) and the composite 7FCC891349EB1608; the matrix review is the end-to-end check.")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_4 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 4u32, "2026-10-23T14:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:23 main: tail-moves v2 is staged as %TEMP%\\pack-next8\\tail-moves-v2.ps1 and composed into pack-next8, which is now core D78DE6536D4AF893 (or-pattern v3 + hopacsota census + this). Eight self-tail emitters always saved every argument to a temp and then copied: Zig (tmpN = vI), Wasm (local.set tmp), WGSL (let spiral_t), Kotlin (val tN_I), Swift (let tN_I), OpenQASM and eBPF (statement temps), and TypeScript (a { let t0 ... } block for any call with 2 or more parameters). Each now goes through pack-next6's shared parallelMoves (MoveVar, SaveTemp, MoveTemp), so a temp is declared only where a parameter is overwritten while another move still reads it, and TypeScript opens its block only for such a cycle. C is excluded on purpose: its self_continue runs the refcount release between saving and assigning, so direct moves would decref the new values. Rust already destructures a tuple, Python assigns a tuple, and Lua recurses. Census of the tree outputs (tail_temp_census.py): temp saves that become direct moves, zig 437, ts 361, kt 198, swift 198, wgsl 41, qasm 25, ebpf 27 (0.3-0.9% of each backend's lines; the Wasm pattern didn't match, so its count is unknown). It parses under both defines with pair-drift clean. Review rule for next8: residual changes confined to self-tail blocks in these 8 backends plus or-pattern samples (v3); DISAGREE 0; every self-tail block's moves are a correct parallel assignment (native results equal).")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_5 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 5u32, "2026-10-23T14:26Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:26 main: pack-next8 also gets ts-dead-break.ps1. TypeScript switch cases (int switch and union unbox) emitted 'break;' after a case body that already ended in return, continue tail or throw: 669 dead breaks over the 449 tree main.ts. A falls_through helper in the TypeScript codegen scope reads the last emitted line by walking back from the end of the StringBuilder, which is linear and avoids a whole-text ToString, and skips the break only after those three terminators. A body ending in a nested '}' keeps its break, which stays safe. It parses; the composed next8 core is printed by compose.ps1. Review rule: TypeScript residuals differ from the baseline only by dropped 'break;' lines and the self-tail moves; native results are unchanged.")
inl note_of_tail_call_parallel_moves_6 () = Note ("TAIL-CALL-PARALLEL-MOVES", 6u32, "2026-10-23T14:32Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:32 main: pack-next8's review is staged as %TEMP%\\pack-next8\\review.ps1 (parse, build, snapshot, -Suite all -Native -ReuseNative, rule check) with next8_rules.py as its output rule. Samples whose main.spi holds an or-pattern arm may change on any backend (v3). Everywhere else only these backends may change: zig, ts, kt, swift, wat, wgsl, qasm and ebpf, by removing temp saves, temp declarations and temp moves (plus TypeScript braces and dead breaks) and adding direct vN = vM moves; and lua, by removing 'local __v = { vN }' and its __v[1] reads. Any other change is a violation. Checked on a hand-made case: the TypeScript self-tail block made direct with its breaks dropped passes, and a stray literal change is flagged. Order: after the next7 review and its accept, so next8's snapshot is the next7 state; DISAGREE 0 and native results equal are required as usual.")
inl c_static_functions () = Task ("C-STATIC-FUNCTIONS", "C output gives internal linkage (static) to every function it defines, and to their forward declarations, except main", Story, P1, D1, 80u32, Active, "PACK-NEXT-4", "team:main,backend:c,codegen,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_c_static_functions () = Planning ("C-STATIC-FUNCTIONS", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_c_static_functions_1 () = Note ("C-STATIC-FUNCTIONS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T05:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 01:51 generated C is always one translation unit (matrix, tibia predict.ps1, shims are -include headers that define only their own static helpers). 2,852 lines of 511 outputs gain static; prototypes of external functions (no definition in the file) and user globals are left alone, because the pass runs on fwd_dcls + types + functions only. gcc 15.2 -O2 A/B on 40 hashed-random samples: compile time -25% (198 s -> 149 s) and -16% with the order swapped (137 s -> 115 s), exe bytes -0.5%, 0 broken, 0 run differences; plus all 65 samples with CFlags rules, c-shim.h or prototypes: 65 same. F# pass equals the mirror on 511/511. In pack-next4 (core 366DF6250C393FFE, parses under both defines); review rule re-applies the same regex.")
inl formatter_speed () = Task ("FORMATTER-SPEED", "SpiralCompiler format runs in time linear in the file size: a 287 KB generated module takes 555 s today (about 90 ms per line)", Story, P1, D3, 85u32, Active, "", "team:main,formatter,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_formatter_speed () = Planning ("FORMATTER-SPEED", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_formatter_speed_1 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 1u32, "2026-10-23T06:12Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:12 measured on generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi (FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS note 2). Shapes are parsed per block (layout -> Joined -> blockShapes over the block's own lines), so a per-file quadratic is not obvious from the code; each block may parse up to 4 candidate layouts (preferred, plain, habits, chain fill). Next: a scaling probe on the first quarter of the same file (about 140 s means linear with a large constant, about 35 s means quadratic in file size), then a sampled profile of one run.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_2 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 2u32, "2026-10-23T06:15Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:15 main: scaling probe: the first quarter of generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi (1,600 of 6,216 lines, 70 KB) took 169 s wall and 37 s CPU, against 555 s wall for the whole file, so time grows linearly (3.3x for 4x the lines). Wall time is mostly BelowNormal starvation on the loaded box (4.6x the CPU time). The real cost is a constant of about 23 ms of CPU per line, roughly 150 s of CPU for this module. Next: a sampled profile on a quiet box (dotnet-trace or a stopwatch build) to find which of flatten / blockShapes re-parses / layout candidates dominates.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_3 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 3u32, "2026-10-23T06:47Z", "main", "2026-10-23 02:47 main: profiled (dotnet-trace, scratch tool path, on the quarter file): 94% of the worker thread is sameParse -> blockShapes -> shapeOf -> shapeSteps, which renders each parsed block to a shape string by F# reflection. Per node it ran isPosition (IsRecord + GetRecordFields + sort) and up to 3 GetUnionFields (foldedConcat, rawSeqHalves, then the node itself). Fix: fmt-fix\\shape-cache.ps1 classifies each runtime type once (ShapeKind in a ConcurrentDictionary: position, enum, union with PreComputeUnionTagReader + per-case PreComputeUnionReader, tuple/record precomputed readers, pair, array, enumerable) and the helpers match on it. The type-test order is unchanged. Equivalence and speed (fsi harness over the installed SpiralCompilerCore's own parse results: 60 lib/spiral files + the fiscal quarter, 2,252 blocks): 2,252 shapes byte-equal, rendering 378,818 ms -> 2,175 ms (174x). Composed into pack-next5d after chain-fill; composite 372640E632978A3E parses under both defines. The next4-composite review's formatter gate is the end-to-end check.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_4 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 4u32, "2026-10-23T09:46Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:46 main: first end-to-end number from the composite's formatter gate (same box, same run, user corpus 84 files): installed 87.5 s, candidate with the shape cache 55.8 s (-36% wall, 1.57x). Smaller than the 174x on shape rendering alone, because a whole run also pays process start, lexing, parsing and layout per file, and the user files are small. On the large generated module (where shapes were 94% of the profile) the gain should be close to that share; a timed rerun of generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi with the installed composite measures it.")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_5 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 5u32, "2026-10-23T09:49Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:49 main: end-to-end on the large generated file, same box, same minute, BelowNormal, first 1,600 lines of the saved original generated_mdfe_v1_00.spi. Installed formatter: 100 s wall, 34.1 s CPU. Composite candidate (67EBF80563E2239A, shape cache + the reviewed habits): 10 s wall, 5.0 s CPU. That is 6.8x less CPU and 10x less wall time, with byte-identical output. Per line: 21 ms down to 3.1 ms of CPU, so the 300k generated lines drop from about 1.8 h to about 16 min of CPU, which makes FISCAL-GENERATOR-FORMATS' format-after-generate step viable once the composite installs. (A first attempt timed a 3-line placeholder: the running fiscal family gate had replaced the live module, so that number was discarded.)")
inl note_of_formatter_speed_6 () = Note ("FORMATTER-SPEED", 6u32, "2026-10-23T09:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 05:51 main: composite review's formatter gate (job-1792748200944, same run, same box), installed vs candidate. user 84 files: 87.5 s vs 55.8 s. lib 82 files: 274.5 s vs 44.5 s (6.2x). synthetic 10 files: 2.7 s vs 3.5 s (tiny files, process start dominates). Every corpus is at 0 files differing beyond parentheses and whitespace. The lib figure matches the 6.8x CPU on the generated module: larger files gain the most.")
