inl planning_of_eoie_job_host_inherit () = Planning ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", Minutes 30u32, Load Heavy, Unassigned)
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T09:27Z", "eoie", "05:27 split from EOIE-JOB-HOST (closed: the parent fix is verified live). Four of tonight's five inheritances ship in GE2 as slices (job-env/apply-host-normal-priority, apply-host-neutral-cwd, apply-container-breakaway, apply-host-no-inherit); the fifth, the environment, is EOIE-JOB-ENV. Gate: after-install-GE2.ps1's handles and breakaway probes, which FAIL on F9's binary (positive control 05:17) and must PASS after GE2. Note: the 05:08 formatter pass moved anchors; GE2 now goes through fmt-rebase/fmt-rebase.ps1.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T10:56Z", "eoie", "06:56 user 06:5x: 'can't you prioritize making eoie processes spin up with a parent? this is sloppy work, asking this for days'. Drift traced: I had declared the job host a root by design and counted that as done; the requirement is every eoie process has a live parent. Measured 06:53: the host (34888) is the only eoie process with a dead parent (the exited eoie.exe call). Fix proven in two lab runs (scratch parent-proof/): CreateProcessW with PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_PARENT_PROCESS = the user's shell (GetShellWindow -> explorer.exe, alive for the logon) gives the child a live parent, Normal priority, no job object, and from INSIDE an eoie job (no BREAKAWAY_OK) the child still broke away and outlived the job (job membership follows the specified parent). Rule set before building: ship as hotfix release HP ahead of GE2 with the 3 host-launch slices (normal priority, neutral cwd, CreateProcessW no-inherit + the parent attribute, falling back to no attribute only when there is no shell window). Install only if the candidate host's parent is a live explorer.exe, supervisors are host-parented, nested-host-check and pipe-release-check PASS, and eoie_job_host/eoie_jobs notebooks pass. Live check after install: 0 eoie processes with a dead parent.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T12:39Z", "eoie", "08:39 HP candidate: 'candidate host 117792 parent 8628 explorer.exe', so the fix works in the real build. But HP's host-probe gate saw 'probe supervisor 114932 parent 0' and will block publishing: its probe job (ping -n 4, ~3 s) ended before the parent lookup on this loaded box, and a vanished process reads as parent 0. Reproduced with the same candidate binary in a fixture home: the supervisor stays alive and parented by the host (104676) on every read. Recovery rule (like F9 after F8): fix the probe (a 30-s job, and a missing row fails as 'supervisor gone', never as a parent mismatch), stop HP, run HP-tail on the same tree: skip regen and cargo check only if HP's two landed files still hash as landed and src is otherwise unchanged since HP's launch; rerun the debug build (incremental), candidate probes, notebooks, parity, runner, lab checks, release build, publish.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 4u32, "2026-10-23T13:08Z", "eoie", "09:08 HP-tail candidate: host probe PASS (supervisor parented by the host), nested start PASS (a job started inside another job outlives it: the job-object escape works), but pipe-release FAIL ('did not return within 20 s'). Measured with the same candidate binary: standalone the redirected start returned in 3 s with the new host alive; inside an eoie job PASS in 7.7 s. So the 20 s limit was a load artifact (a fresh home's first start copies the 63 MB debug binary; the box was busy at 08:49). pipe-release-check now waits 60 s; it still requires the host to be alive at the end, so a pipe held until the host dies still fails. HP-tail stopped, HP-tail2 (same script, same unchanged tree) holds slot 2; no live slot ticket was waiting (all queued jobs are --after chains), checked before the stop.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_5 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 5u32, "2026-10-23T13:29Z", "eoie", "09:29 HP-tail2: candidate host under explorer.exe and the probe supervisor parented by the host again, but its probe start took 23772 ms against the 5000 ms gate. Measured right after with the same candidate: 6 fresh-home starts 2160, 953, 1034, 1977, 1859, 946 ms (median 1859), and the installed binary 2629, 1999, 2611, 1821, 991, 1971 (median 1999): no regression, a transient spike under load. Gate rule changed: a probe start over 5000 ms takes one more sample, and the gate blocks only if both are slow. HP-tail2 stopped; HP-tail3 (same tree, same script with the retry) holds slot 2; no queued job without an --after chain was waiting.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_6 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 6u32, "2026-10-23T13:56Z", "eoie", "09:56 HP installed 09:51 (eoie.exe 9AEE40C7). Install rule met: candidate host parent was a live explorer.exe (72740 under 8628), probe supervisor host-parented, nested and pipe checks PASS, eoie_job_host and eoie_jobs notebooks 2/2. Live after install, after-install-HP.ps1 6/6 at 09:53: host 29276 parent 8628 explorer.exe, Normal priority, 3 new supervisors all live under the host, pipe released, nested start outlives its job, 0 dead-parent rows started after the install. 1 dead-parent process left: the pre-install host 34888, which still serves main's queued tickets and exits when they drain. Closing condition: the strict audit (live-parent-audit.ps1) shows 0 once 34888 exits. Container breakaway still ships in GE2. One-call spec on the installed binary 37/40, 38/40 after the runner setup fix (case 36).")
inl note_of_eoie_job_host_inherit_7 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-HOST-INHERIT", 7u32, "2026-10-23T18:55Z", "eoie", "14:55 eoie: done. The pre-install host 34888 (dead parent) took its idle-exit path at 14:51:47, 11 minutes after its last job (main's review 2) finished; host.pid already named 29276, so it removed nothing. The strict audit the watcher ran at 14:52 found 18 eoie processes, 0 with a dead parent: host 29276 under explorer, every supervisor under 29276 (all started by the installed host), and 3 CLI calls under their callers' pwsh. Every eoie process now has a live parent, the job host included.")
inl lib_testing_expect () = Task ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", "lib testing gains expect condition with a message", TaskKind, P2, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,eoie,testing", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_testing_expect () = Planning ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", Minutes 20u32, LoadUnset, Unassigned)
inl note_of_lib_testing_expect_1 () = Note ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T09:28Z", "eoie", "05:28 measured: 11 scratch models and 50 eoie src files each define 'inl expect (condition : bool) (~detail : string) : () = if condition then () else failwith detail' locally; lib/spiral testing.spi has _assert_eq/_assert_in/... but no boolean check that carries a message (my eoie_notebook_progress model failed its typecheck at 05:11 for lacking it). Lib-first: add testing.expect (backend-generic: failwith), with a test block, then the copies drop theirs as they are touched. Lands with a lib gate like the others; coordinate the landing with main.")
inl note_of_lib_testing_expect_2 () = Note ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T09:50Z", "eoie", "05:50 slice drafted: scratch job-env/apply-lib-testing-expect.ps1 (end-of-file append to testing.spi, guarded on an existing 'inl expect (condition : bool)', rust+fsharp test block); dry run on a copy applies and is idempotent. Not in tonight's gated batch (that one is already gated and waiting for its landing gap); it goes in the next lib gate, then the copies drop their local expect when touched.")
inl note_of_lib_testing_expect_3 () = Note ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T15:08Z", "eoie", "11:08 eoie: bundled into lib-gaps/apply-lib-gaps.ps1, which now runs this slice first, so testing.expect lands in the same lib landing and gate as process.try_capture_combined_list, am'.growable/push_back, sm'.concat_array's Rust join and env.current_executable (after main's main-lib-land, which main agreed to). The f6 lib copy carries it, and its notebook check runs after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_lib_testing_expect_4 () = Note ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", 4u32, "2026-10-23T21:34Z", "eoie", "17:34 eoie: lib-gaps (testing.expect plus am'.growable/push_back, the sm' line primitives, the sm'.concat_array Rust join, process.try_capture_combined_list and env.current_executable) is rebased on main's two lib landings of today. A dry run on a fresh copy, after checking it against main's posted hashes (sm' A4FD87585FDCC6A5, process D4025378EE51F5FE), applies clean and keeps main's portable_index_of. Lib gate on that copy: process 6/6, am' 12/12, testing 8/8, env 6/6, sm' 50/52 (show_floats and replace_regex red). Land rule, written before the baseline: land only if both reds are also red on the untouched lib (baseline gate running since 17:26; main reports replace_regex red in its own baseline), no Cells erl.exe runs, and the gated hashes still hold. land-lib-gaps.ps1 enforces all three; its controls refuse with no baseline, refuse when the baseline has only replace_regex, and accept with both reds, up to the Cells guard.")
inl note_of_lib_testing_expect_5 () = Note ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", 5u32, "2026-10-23T21:56Z", "eoie", "17:56 eoie: landed in spiral/lib/spiral by land-lib-gaps.ps1 with all three conditions met. Baseline: the untouched lib's sm' fails show_floats and replace_regex too (47/49), the same reds as the dry copy (50/52; the 3 extra tests are the new primitives, all passing). No Cells erl.exe was running, and the gated hashes held. 5 of 5 files equal the gated dry copy; backup in scratch lib-gaps/before-land-175558; main told. N's lib needs (am' push_back, sm' nonempty_line_at, concat_array signature) are now in the tree.")
inl note_of_lib_testing_expect_6 () = Note ("LIB-TESTING-EXPECT", 6u32, "2026-10-23T22:25Z", "eoie", "18:25 eoie: done. The title's deliverable (lib testing gains expect condition with a message) is in the live lib since 17:56 (testing.spi: inl expect (condition : bool) (detail : string) : () = if condition then () else failwith detail), lib-gated, with the sm' reds equal to the untouched baseline. Lib edits need no install step. Callers move onto it package by package: heal wave 1's eoie_receipt test is the first (release Q).")
inl eoie_grep_regex_hint () = Task ("EOIE-GREP-REGEX-HINT", "eoie grep hints at --regex when a literal needle with regex syntax matches nothing", TaskKind, P3, D1, 50u32, Active, "", "eoie,grep,friction", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_grep_regex_hint () = Planning ("EOIE-GREP-REGEX-HINT", Minutes 20u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_grep_regex_hint_1 () = Note ("EOIE-GREP-REGEX-HINT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:07Z", "eoie", "06:07 friction measured on myself: eoie grep over the backlog state with the needle 'single-use binding|codegen pack' (and 'near-gate|zzz') answered 'ok ... 0 matched', because the needle is literal without --regex, while 'near-gate' alone matched 12 times; I nearly concluded absence. Rule: when a search ends with 0 matches, no --regex was given, and the needle holds a pipe, .*, brackets, ^ or $, the receipt adds 'hint: the needle is literal; pass --regex to use it as a pattern'. No other behavior change. Test: a grep CLI notebook case with an alternation needle over a fixture that holds both words.")
inl note_of_eoie_grep_regex_hint_2 () = Note ("EOIE-GREP-REGEX-HINT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T10:10Z", "eoie", "06:10 slice drafted: scratch grep-hint/apply-grep-regex-hint.ps1 (typed needle_hint union NoNeedleHint | LiteralLooksLikePattern, hint only when total is 0 and --regex is absent; eoie_grep_query gains spiral- for sm'; grep_cli test with 'needle|three' literal -> hint, --regex -> matches 3 and no hint). Anchors are on main's pre-format originals (grep.spi is in the 05:08 formatted set), so it lands through fmt-rebase; prelaunch with -Originals is clean. Ships with the small post-GE2 release (accept-review, notebook progress), not HIJK.")
inl note_of_eoie_grep_regex_hint_3 () = Note ("EOIE-GREP-REGEX-HINT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T10:17Z", "eoie", "06:17 re-anchored: the slice now matches the post-G healed grep.spi (receipt line with ++#, open sm'_operators already present, added only if missing). Composes in the release L dry chain on GE2 -> H,J,K. Ships in release L (launch-L.ps1).")
inl note_of_eoie_grep_regex_hint_4 () = Note ("EOIE-GREP-REGEX-HINT", 4u32, "2026-10-23T18:10Z", "eoie", "14:10 eoie: TDD case written, spec/one-call.md case 44 (eoie grep notes.txt 'one|two' over a two-line fixture). On the installed binary it is red at exactly the hint ('ok  files 1 searched  0 matched  matches 0  2ms ...' vs the same with '  hint: the needle is literal; pass --regex to use it as a pattern'), so the rest of the receipt already matches byte for byte; it turns green when L installs the slice.")
inl eoie_adoption_habits () = Task ("EOIE-ADOPTION-HABITS", "eoie adoption lists habit calls per habit so the guide targets the largest", Story, P2, D2, 60u32, Active, "", "eoie,adoption,guide", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_adoption_habits () = Planning ("EOIE-ADOPTION-HABITS", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_adoption_habits_1 () = Note ("EOIE-ADOPTION-HABITS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:29Z", "eoie", "06:29 why: the adoption sweep says 19,447 of 32,687 shell calls in the last 8 days were habit calls, but not WHICH habits, so the guide cannot target the biggest ones. Today habit_named (eoie_adoption host.spi:69) is one host-macro blob that answers a bool over 18 words (get-content, select-string, get-childitem, set-content, add-content, out-file, foreach-object, measure-object, copy-item, remove-item, move-item, test-path, start-sleep, robocopy, findstr, python, python3, py.exe, plus [io.file]). Design: a typed habit union with one case per habit and its word (union_reflection for the lookup, like agile_task_query's flags), a per-habit tally in model.spi, and 'eoie adoption --habits' printing habit TAB calls TAB share, sorted by calls; the blob migrates into the union (AGENTS.md: touching legacy migrates it), word-boundary matching moves to lib sm' calls. Rule for the guide: its next section covers the habits that together make up 80% of habit calls in the --habits table, largest first. Tests: model test over synthetic commands, adoption CLI notebook with a fixture transcript.")
inl note_of_eoie_adoption_habits_2 () = Note ("EOIE-ADOPTION-HABITS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T10:37Z", "eoie", "06:37 pure model passes (scratch f5/src/eoie_adoption_habits_model, notebook check habits5): habit union with one word per case (19 cases incl. python3, py.exe and [io.file]), whole-word matching on lib code points (python3 is not python, my-get-contents.ps1 is not get-content), one count per habit per call, text table of the habits that occur in union order. Typecheck lessons recorded in memory (precedence of |> not with &&; annotate lambda params for record fields). Next: the --habits flag in eoie_adoption with the blob migrated into this union, after GE2.")
inl note_of_eoie_adoption_habits_3 () = Note ("EOIE-ADOPTION-HABITS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:57Z", "eoie", "07:57 wired: scratch adoption-habits/apply-adoption-habits.ps1 (on the raw chain after G's heal) adds habits.spi (the passing model + habit_any), threads a typed tally_mode (ByDay | ByHabit) through calls_tallied/files_tallied/client_tallied, prints 'habit TAB calls' largest first under --habits with receipt 'mode=habits days=n habits=k', switches classify to habits.habit_any and removes the 18-word host blob habit_named (migration; same word-boundary semantics). Full L dry chain applies; on that tree classify.spi (the migration's regression net) and habits.spi pass. First run hit the here-string trailing-newline trap twice (two lines glued); fixed. Ships in release L with an after-install probe; closes on PASS.")
inl sim_x_feature_parity () = Task ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", "x sim: extend the simulator until it covers every X feature x uses, so the whole client can run against the sim (start only after all the sim's Elixir is rewritten in Spiral)", Feature, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "SIM-IN-SPIRAL", "x,sim", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_x_feature_parity () = Planning ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", Minutes 630u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_1 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:41Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:41 x: user request 2026-10-23 06:3x: 'extend sim so we reach full x feature parity, but only after all the elixir got rewritten in spiral'. Gate: deps SIM-IN-SPIRAL (the remaining Elixir half: World/Runner supervision, mix tasks); do not start while any apps/sim Elixir remains. Starting point for the inventory when it starts: today the sim models post, reply (parent), edit (revisions), delete, like/unlike, and frames (threads split into posts, edits of frames). xc also uses timelines per account with paging, following lists, users lookup by name and id, posts lookup by id with referenced posts (quote, reply, repost), conversation search for threads, OAuth 1.0a / 2.0 auth and token refresh, post and delete writes, and X's text rules (weights, autolinks, HTML escaping, trailing-whitespace trim), which e2e's Python stub covers today. First step: list each xc feature against the sim with a covered/missing column, then add the missing ones as sim slices in Spiral, each with its parity test against the stub's current behaviour, until e2e can run against the sim instead of the stub.")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_2 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:34Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:34 x: PARITY TARGET inventory (planning only; work still waits for SIM-IN-SPIRAL). x's model of the X API today is the e2e Python stub (apps/client/e2e/stub.py), so 'full x feature parity' means the sim serves at least what the stub serves. GET: /2/users/by (usernames), /2/users (ids), /2/tweets (id lookup, 100 per call), /2/tweets/:id, /2/tweets/search/recent (7 days) and /search/all (30 days, start_time), /2/users/:id/following (paged by next_token, following_page_size), /2/users/:id/tweets (timeline, exclude=replies,retweets). POST: /2/tweets (create, reply via reply.in_reply_to_tweet_id, assigns ids, conversation_id and replied_to refs), /2/oauth2/token (refresh rotation, client Basic auth). DELETE: /2/tweets/:id (own posts only). Cross-cutting: 4 auth contexts (app bearer, user bearer, OAuth 1.0a signature check, client Basic) and the 403 for app-only on writes; problem+json errors (400/401/403/404); edit_history_post_ids; HTML escaping of & < > on output; public_metrics; url entities with expanded/display/title; referenced posts and their expansions; a request log the e2e asserts on. GAP found while reading: the stub caps text at 280 Python characters (code points), not X's weighted count, so it accepts a 200-CJK post that X would refuse at weight 400. The sim should enforce the weighted rule (glyphs plain_weight + X-EMOJI-WEIGHT's entities) and return X's 400 problem. Its world model (inbox/scheduler) already has posts, edits, likes and deletes; users, following, timelines, search windows and auth are what remains to model.")
inl note_of_sim_x_feature_parity_3 () = Note ("SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY", 3u32, "2026-10-24T11:21Z", "x", "10-24 07:21 x: estimate by rule, not by guess: 90m per X API endpoint family that the client calls and the sim does not serve (a sim handler, a world-model field, and one e2e row that runs the client against the sim). Code check on HEAD 6ef9c48: apps/client/wire.spi calls 7 families (/2/tweets lookup, /2/tweets create+delete, /2/tweets/search/all, /2/users lookup, /2/users/by, following, user timeline), and apps/sim mentions no /2/ path, so 7 x 90 = 630m. Load is medium: each family needs one sim compile plus one client e2e, not a matrix. Re-size after SIM-IN-SPIRAL lands, because the Spiral world may change the per-family cost.")
inl sim_world_spiral () = Task ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", "x sim: port the runtime world model (apps/sim/lib/xsim/world.ex, 127 lines: delivery dedup, pending buffer, frame gathering, rekeying, demand, dirty set) to a pure Spiral module with test blocks; no supervision involved", Story, P1, D4, 70u32, Active, "LIB-DICTM", "x,sim,spiral", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_world_spiral () = Planning ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_1 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:52 x: split out of SIM-IN-SPIRAL after the 10-22/23 night retrospective. world.ex is a pure state machine (apply_delivery, drain, gathered, rekey, wanted_key, take_dirty) and has no Spiral counterpart (client world.spi is the posting side). Nothing in it needs gleam_otp actors or KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, so marking all of SIM-IN-SPIRAL Blocked hid this work from the ready list for two nights. Shape: unions for the 7 delivery kinds (log.ex @fields), a record world, exhaustive matches, the oracle being the current Elixir via the existing ExUnit and corpus-commute fixtures until the Spiral module replaces it. Until the user's commit lands, work in a scratch copy with an anchored apply script (memory verify-drafts-on-scratch-copies).")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_2 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:09Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:09 x: grooming, the user's 10-23 directions applied to the first rewrite step. (1) BACKEND-BALANCE: write it backend-neutral and test it on rust AND gleam; it runs inside the Gleam runner and on Rust for batch sweeps. (2) Lib first: build on listm' / sm' / resultm and LIB-PAR-MAP; anything generic it needs (a map/set over keys, a dedup set) goes into lib/spiral first, not into x. (3) No host blobs: pure Spiral domain (unions for the delivery kinds, records), with any Gleam boundary through granular primitives (LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 2), never global or rust_global code.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_3 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:16Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:16 x: plan and rules for today's run (until 18:00), written before any code. FACTS: world.ex needs a map (posts, keys, frames, demand, clock), a set (seen, dirty) and SHA-256 (rekey: first 16 hex chars of sha256(parent_key, 0, source)). lib/spiral has none of these backend-neutrally: mapm wraps Rust/F# HashMap/BTreeMap only, crypto's sha256 has F#/Python/Rust arms only, and there is no gleam/dict or gleam_crypto binding. STEPS: (1) draft lib modules in a lib scratch copy, granular per the user's 10-23 direction (one nominal per foreign type, one one-expression primitive per foreign function, no blobs). They are a persistent map (Rust std BTreeMap via persistent copy-on-insert or im-style; Gleam gleam/dict), a set on top of it, and a sha256-hex (Rust sha2 crate; Gleam gleam_crypto hash Sha256 + bit_array base16). Test blocks on rust + gleam, plus a fixed-vector sha test ('abc' -> ba7816bf...). (2) Port world.ex to a new module (name chosen after a clash scan) on top of them, with unions for the 7 delivery kinds and records, plus test blocks ported from xsim_test's World cases. (3) Compile-check both arms (rust crate entry, gleam via simframes-style entry) under eoie compile windows at >= 3.5 GB free. (4) Cells test runs only when no other Cells job is active. RULES: never touch x's staged tree or lib/spiral itself (scratch copies plus anchored apply scripts); hand the lib drafts to main for landing (main owns lib) and keep x's port on them; fallback if the map binding stalls past 13:00: an association-list map behind the same interface, swapped later. Progress is recorded here with timestamps.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_4 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-23T11:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:27 x: step 1 done for the map: LIB-DICTM drafted and passing on rust + gleam under Cells. Correction to note 3: SHA-256 is NOT a gap; lib crypto.hash_text is already backend-neutral (Gleam via an @external to erlang crypto:hash, plus Rust, Zig, F# and TS arms, fixed-vector tests). Next: the world.ex port on dictm + crypto.hash_text, before tibia's live block at about 09:40 (no compiles during it).")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_5 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-23T11:46Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:46 x: step 2 done in draft: world.ex is ported to Spiral as module 'inbox' (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\inbox.spi; scratch package C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\client = x client + dictm + inbox, x untouched). It reuses events.spi's delivery/event unions and splits state into small records (intake, graph, reactions, tallies) so each definition fits one post. Under Cells (process compiler, memo off, no other Cells job active): 6/6 test blocks pass on rust -d sha2 AND gleam (217 s). They cover dedup, a child before its parent waiting then landing, frames in any order rebuilding the post, an older unlike not undoing a newer like, an edit rekeying children, and keys_match_the_elixir_world: both backends give exactly Elixir's keys (022a6979e6dab7aa / 451526e1c47e6023, computed with the same :crypto.hash(:sha256, [parent_key, 0, source]) on elixir 1.20.4). Fixed on the way: 6 name clashes renamed (edited, frame_at, gathered, posted, step, wanted); x-compat autolinks on .social and .parts fields (now reactions / frame_parts); a tuple accumulator in a fold (see LIB-DICTM note 2). Next: a parity run against the Elixir World on real event streams, then hand dictm to main and the module to x's tree after the user's commit.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_6 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-23T11:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:52 x: PARITY with the Elixir World on a real event stream, both backends. C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\gen_parity.exs loads the actual apps/sim/lib/xsim/world.ex + frames.ex (Code.require_file), replays apps/sim/fixtures/prelude.events.jsonl (65 deliveries: 19 frame, 22 edit_frame, 20 like, 4 unlike) through Xsim.World and writes a Spiral test that rebuilds the same deliveries with typed constructors and asserts Elixir's results. Spiral's inbox gives exactly the same on rust -d sha2 and gleam (Cells, 96 s): tallies framed=6 edits=6 dups=14 buffered=0, all 6 post keys (p0=3f5400c8092236b1 ... p5=f86e168f9079f0b8) and the same 6 wanted keys. inbox gained frame_from (a frame with a parent) and edit_frame_at. The generated parity test stays scratch tooling (its fixture literal would not fit x-compat's one-post rule); the generator is the reusable part. Slip: this run overlapped eoie's notebook check by about a minute because my script printed the foreign runner instead of refusing; eoie was told, and the script now refuses (exit 75) while any other run_notebook is active.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_7 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-23T13:49Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:49 x: dependency recorded: LIB-DICTM. inbox.spi (and scheduler.spi on top of it) use dictm, and by lib-first dictm lands in lib/spiral (main's), not as a copy in x. LANDING PLAN once main lands dictm: inbox.spi, scheduler.spi, runner_checks.spi and the parity tests (inbox_parity, scheduler_parity, checks_parity) join apps/client's package next to invariants/world/scenario, where the sim's Spiral already lives (simframes exports). The parity tests' generators (gen_parity.exs, gen_runner_parity.exs, gen_checks_parity.exs) become mix tasks in apps/sim (xsim.parity), so the fixtures are regenerated from the Elixir oracle rather than hand-kept, until each Elixir module is retired. All sources are in C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_8 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-23T13:51Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:51 x: the generator mix task is drafted and checked: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\xsim.parity.ex (Mix.Tasks.Xsim.Parity, 'mix xsim.parity --events FILE --out DIR'). It replaces the three loose generators (gen_parity / gen_runner_parity / gen_checks_parity .exs). It reads the stream through the real Xsim.Log.decode, runs the real World, Runner (fake executor) and Invariants.check, and writes inbox_parity.spi, scheduler_parity.spi and checks_parity.spi with their headers, so no PowerShell assembly is left. Checked by loading the task source next to the real xsim modules (check_parity_task.exs, Elixir only) on prelude.events.jsonl: 65 deliveries, 6 posts, 6 visible. The fixture is byte-equal to the hand-verified inbox_parity.spi, and every final expectation equals the Cells-verified files. checks_parity keeps only the real Invariants.check test; the row-text copy test is dropped, because the real oracle supersedes it. Lands into apps/sim/lib/mix/tasks with the Spiral modules (note 7).")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_9 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-23T14:31Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:31 x: landing script for note 7's plan: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_sim_modules.ps1 [-Go]. It copies the 10 Spiral sim modules (inbox, inbox_parity, scheduler, scheduler_parity, runner_checks, checks_parity, tree_memory, sim_report, sim_args, sim_run) from x-world\\client into apps/client, lists them after invariants in package.spiproj (dependency order) and in build.ps1's Invoke-SpiralTests notebook list. Guards: it refuses (exit 75) until lib/spiral has dictm.spi (lib-first: no x copy of dictm) or while x has staged changes, and refuses if any module is missing or already present. Dry run now: refused on the dictm guard, as expected. xsim_entry is left out: as an entry it needs its own crate folder with Cargo.toml and a workspace entry, which come with the X-BACKEND-MAP decision, not this landing.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_10 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-23T19:25Z", "x", "2026-10-23 15:25 x: after the formatter pass (inbox 161 and scheduler 219 lines re-laid), the dictm no-host-callback rework, sorted Gleam keys and the sim_run import fix, everything passes again on rust + gleam: inbox 6/6, inbox_parity 1/1, scheduler 5/5 (with a_crashed_task_is_a_visible_crash_result and peak_results), scheduler_parity 1/1, checks_parity 2/2. Parity with the real Elixir World, Runner and Invariants holds on the formatted sources, so the formatter changed no meaning there. Remaining in chain9: tree_memory, sim_report, sim_args, sim_run, xsim_entry and the lib drafts.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_11 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-24T07:26Z", "x", "10-24 03:26 x: landing state of the 17 Spiral sim modules (apply_sim_modules.ps1). Its 'x has staged changes' refusal is replaced by an unstaged check on package.spiproj/build.ps1 only, per the new rule that work lands as an unstaged layer over the user's staged commit (memory land-drafts-in-the-repo). Dry run now refuses only on the real blocker: lib/spiral has no dictm.spi yet (main's main-lib-dictm-land-2, in main's lib chain, paused for tibia's PR-27 block). After dictm lands: run with -Verdicts <tonight's chain logs> (chain21c passed scheduler_parity, runner_checks, scenario_checks, runner_shell and executors; the rest are in requeue_after_block's chain21d), then -Go.")
inl note_of_sim_world_spiral_12 () = Note ("SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-25T10:24Z", "x", "2026-10-25 06:24 -04:00 x: xsim.parity LANDED (unstaged) in a new shape. It no longer writes .spi files with the fixture inlined (that broke the 280-weight cell rule: inbox_parity's literal list weighed 10,222). 'mix xsim.parity --events FILE --out FILE' writes one labelled line per expectation (tallies, keys, wanted, wanted_count, visible, visible_count, checks_fake, checks_wrong) from the real Xsim.World, Runner (fake executor) and Invariants.check. apps/sim/build.ps1 regenerates it after xsim.events and compares it with apps/sim/fixtures/prelude.parity.txt via Confirm-Fixture (-Accept rewrites). The Spiral parity modules hold no data: they read ../sim/fixtures/prelude.events.jsonl (the one recorded stream; one record per fact) and look up each expectation by label. The row-text test is dropped, as the draft said. A new test, the_prelude_fixture_is_a_canonical_stream, checks that Spiral's encoder re-emits the Elixir stream byte for byte. First run on the current stream: deliveries=61 posts=6 visible=6, dups=12 buffered=1. The old hand-kept expectations were for an older 65-delivery stream, the reason to generate them.")
inl sim_runner_core_spiral () = Task ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", "x sim: port the runner's pure scheduling core (retarget, release/acquire refcounts, doom/expire/evict, queue, harvest bookkeeping from runner.ex) to Spiral as a state machine over events; the task spawning stays a thin shell", Story, P1, D5, 70u32, Active, "SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL", "x,sim,spiral", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_sim_runner_core_spiral () = Planning ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_1 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:52 x: split out of SIM-IN-SPIRAL with SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL. In runner.ex only start_queued (Task.async), harvest (Task.yield_many) and Task.shutdown touch processes; everything else is pure bookkeeping that can be modeled as 'state + event -> state + commands' (StartTask key, CancelTask key) in Spiral now. The effectful shell that executes those commands is what waits for KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (gleam_otp). Folds in X-SIM-RUNNER-CRASHES's crash result as a domain case.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_2 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:53Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:53 x: design and rules, written before code. Module 'scheduler' (name after a clash scan) on top of inbox. STATE (small records): the inbox; running (dict key -> owner ids) and the queue (list key); refs (key -> count), post_key (id -> key), owner (key -> id); results (key -> task_result); doomed (key -> deadline) with a clock; visible (id -> key, result); counters (starts, cancelled, evicted, cancels, rescues, recomputes, peaks). INPUTS: a delivery; a task finished (key, result); a task crashed (key, reason); the drain end. OUTPUTS: commands StartTask {key; sources} and CancelTask key, which a shell executes. The shell is Elixir Task today and gleam_otp later via LIB-OTP-ACTORS; it is not part of this item. Faults as values: cancel on/off, rerun_completed, evict Off | Now | Grace n, naive visibility, max_concurrency. Every runner.ex function maps 1:1 (retarget, release, acquire, doom, expire, rescued, evict, requeue_completed, start_queued, reconcile, settle). TESTS: a synchronous driver that runs StartTask at once with a fake executor (the same digest as Executors.fake: sha256 of the sources joined by NUL, first 12 hex), and a deferred driver that completes tasks only at drain, which exercises cancellation. PARITY RULE: compare against the Elixir Runner on the settled visible map (id -> key and result) and wanted keys, which are deterministic. Do not compare starts/cancels, which depend on Elixir task timing. Compile/test windows: before 09:40 or after tibia's block (about 12:10).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_3 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-23T12:03Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:03 x: drafted as module 'scheduler' (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\scheduler.spi, on top of inbox): every runner.ex function 1:1 as small pure functions over records (task_work, owner_map, life_cycle, run_counts), task_command StartTask/CancelTask as outputs, faults as values (cancel, rerun, eviction NoEviction/EvictNow/EvictAfter n, naive, limit), plus sync_run and deferred_run drivers and 4 test blocks (computed once, withdrawn demand cancels, same-source roots share one task, eviction then relike recomputes). A clash scan renamed command/settled/started (cli, invariants, refs). Type-checks and compiles to Rust (24 s, 304 KB) and Gleam (19 s, 140 KB) via a check entry. Cells runs are waiting for tibia's Cells run to finish (my script refused twice, as it should). Parity test generated from the real Elixir Runner (gen_runner_parity.exs, Code.require_file of frames/world/executors/runner, Executors.fake): on prelude.events.jsonl it settles 6 visible posts with keys and fake outputs (26 starts and 1 cancel there, which by the rule are not compared); scheduler_parity asserts the same settled map on both backends.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_4 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-23T13:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:06 x: PARITY PASSES. Cells (rust + gleam, process compiler, memo off): C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\client\\scheduler.spi 4/4 and scheduler_parity.spi 1/1. On the recorded prelude stream (65 deliveries), the Spiral scheduler's settled visible map equals the Elixir Runner's: 6 posts, each with the same source key and the same fake executor digest. Two fixes got there: (1) a test used {plain_faults () with ...}, a parse error, because record update needs a bound name, so it is now inl plain = plain_faults (); (2) sync_run and deferred_run now bind the delivery list dynamically (inl ~deliveries = l). Before that, a static 65-element fixture made the partial evaluator unroll the whole fold with every inl scheduler step inlined, and the cell hit Cells' 300 s timeout. Lesson for SIM-IN-SPIRAL: step functions over recorded streams take dynamic input. Next (in flight): runner_checks.spi, the typed bridge that replaces invariants.ex (scheduler state -> invariants.spi snapshot, rows sorted by id, no text rows), and checks_parity.spi, which compares the bridge's findings with (a) checked_text over the Elixir row text and (b) the real Xsim.Invariants.check, for a matching and a wrong baseline (expected 'sequential_equivalence=FAIL[p0,p1,p2,p3,p4]'). Both pass on gleam and fail on rust only because of the dictm fold bug (LIB-DICTM note 5).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_5 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-23T13:44Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:44 x: progress set to 60%, by the rule used for every x item today: verified on a scratch copy = 70%, and the last 30% is applying it in the real tree plus the full test run. This one sits 10 points lower until checks_parity passes on rust after the dictm fix (queued for after tibia's block, with the new crash-result test).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_6 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-23T13:57Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:57 x: xsim.run's pure half drafted as C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\sim_report.spi (queued for Cells after tibia's block). summary_line prints the same 'XSIM seed=.. schedule=.. ... peak_beam_mb=.. ms=.. <findings>' line as Mix.Tasks.Xsim.Run.summary, field for field in the same order, so xsim.diff and the logs keep working when the entry switches to Spiral. commuted_runs folds runs_commute over consecutive schedules (Enum.chunk_every(2, 1)), and verdict_line gives 'XSIM commute=.. result=pass|fail'. The scheduler gained run_counts.peak_results (max results size, updated in reconciled like runner.ex's reconcile). peak_beam_mb and ms come in through run_shape from the shell, since they are host measurements. Caught before compiling: 'x |> listm.append y' is y ++ x, so the fields are now joined by explicit nesting. 2 tests pin the line format and pass/fail.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_7 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-23T18:56Z", "x", "2026-10-23 14:56 x: checks_parity PASSES 2/2 on rust + gleam (self-gating chain9, 14:42, after the dictm no-host-callback fix and the formatter pass). On the recorded 65-delivery stream, runner_checks' findings equal both Elixir oracles on both backends: checked_text over the Elixir row text, and the real Xsim.Invariants.check, for a matching baseline and a wrong one ('sequential_equivalence=FAIL[p0,p1,p2,p3,p4]'). Scheduler parity passed earlier, so the runner core and its invariant bridge are verified on scratch. Progress 60 -> 70 (rule: verified on scratch; landing waits for LIB-DICTM, via apply_sim_modules.ps1).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_8 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-23T23:04Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:04 x: PORTING BUG found by desk check (mapping xsim_test.exs case 51 'forgetting finished results recompiles shared prefixes'), fixed in scratch, check queued after tibia's block. Elixir's Runner re-queues completed tasks (rerun_completed) only in the per-delivery reconcile; drain reconciles with rerun: false (runner.ex:166), and harvest doesn't reconcile at all. scheduler.spi's reconciled always read faults.rerun and is also called after every completion batch (executed_all, the crash test helper, runner_shell's Finished handler), so any run with rerun = true would re-queue forever and never settle. The parity tests all use plain faults, which is why it went unseen. Fix: reconciled_with (rerun) as the core, reconciled = per delivery (faults.rerun), drained = after completions (rerun false); executed_all, crash test and runner_shell use drained. With rerun false both are identical, so the parity results are unaffected (scheduler_parity rerun first in the queue). Regression tests added to runner_checks: rerunning_finished_results_recompiles_shared_prefixes (rerun -> one_compile_per_prefix FAIL, plain -> ok) and naive_visibility_without_cancelling_surfaces_a_stale_revision (case 39; a newest-first execution run stands in for Elixir's slow old revision: naive + no cancel -> no_stale_results FAIL[p0], plain -> ok).")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_9 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-23T23:28Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:28 x: scheduler.spi gained an executor parameter for the synchronous runner: executed_by exec / executed_all_by exec / sync_run_with exec, with sync_run = sync_run_with fake_result (fake_result = the old inline {Done; fake_out}). Needed by the ported Elixir case 223 (an honest executor that errs on broken sources). Behavior for existing callers is unchanged; scheduler + scheduler_parity rerun first after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_10 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-24T00:37Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:37 x: scheduler.spi PASSES 5/5 on rust + gleam (chain20, 20:31-20:36) with both of tonight's changes: the rerun fix (reconciled_with / reconciled per delivery / drained after completions) and the executor parameter (executed_by / executed_all_by / sync_run_with). scheduler_parity (the Elixir oracle) is next in the chain; runner_checks then carries the regression tests for the rerun bug.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_11 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-24T00:40Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:40 x: scheduler_parity PASSES after tonight's changes (chain20, 20:36-20:39): the settled view still equals the real Elixir Runner's on the recorded 65-delivery stream. With rerun = false, reconciled_with is identical to the old reconciled, as predicted. runner_checks (the regression tests for the rerun bug) is next.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_12 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-24T01:02Z", "x", "2026-10-23 21:02 x: runner_checks PASSES 9/9 on rust + gleam (chain20, 20:44-20:5x): the 4 existing tests plus tonight's 5 runner-level ports of Elixir cases. Case 51, rerun recompiles shared prefixes, now settles and fails one_compile_per_prefix as Elixir does, which confirms the drain fix. Also case 39 naive stale revision, case 57 withdrawn demand, case 190 grace window and case 143 saved-stream replay. With scheduler 5/5 and scheduler_parity, the fixed core is verified on scratch. scenario_checks and everything after it in the queue didn't start before the 20:50 cutoff; re-run per X-DONE n68.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_13 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-10-24T03:51Z", "x", "23:51 x: chain21 (job-1792811662170) scheduler.spi: all 5 Rust cells timed out at 300 s. It passed 5/5 at 20:31. The box was saturated (CPU 100%, five heavy/compile windows across main, tibia and eoie), and main's listm-runtime land (23:05) changed listm'/seq runtime paths. RULE, written before the retry: retry_timeouts.ps1 (job x-chain21-retry, after chain21) reruns every notebook whose failures are all timeouts. Pass means contention. A timeout again means a regression, and I bisect against a pre-23:05 lib copy (x-libprog-roundtrip, 21:24, has the pre-tonight state) before anything that depends on scheduler lands.")
inl note_of_sim_runner_core_spiral_14 () = Note ("SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-10-24T04:44Z", "x", "10-24 00:44 x: rule applied (n13). The timeout-only retry of scheduler.spi PASSED (job-1792813911883 attempt 2, 316 s, 00:43), so chain21's five 300 s timeouts were contention, not a regression from main's listm-runtime land. No bisect needed. The rest of chain21 is requeued as x-chain21c on the mutex-guarded compile-class gate.")
inl eoie_crate_gate () = Task ("EOIE-CRATE-GATE", "eoie crate gate <root> [--enforce] [--limit 1000]: count the generated output of every Cargo.toml with [package.metadata.spiral] anywhere under root (not only apps/), report per crate, and with --enforce fail on any crate over the limit or on finding none", Story, P1, D2, 60u32, Active, "", "team:eoie,eoie,gate,rust", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_crate_gate () = Planning ("EOIE-CRATE-GATE", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_crate_gate_1 () = Note ("EOIE-CRATE-GATE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:58Z", "main", "2026-10-23 06:58 main: requested from CRATE-GATE-HARDEN. Reference behaviour is x/scripts/gate.ps1 (entry/output metadata, -Limit, -Enforce, vacuity). It needs repo-wide discovery, because dice keeps its crates in ui/ and contract/. eoie source-topology already counts .rs per crate dir, so the verb can extend it. First users: x (replaces its gate.ps1), dice and alphabet (report-only until their 15-16k-line monoliths are split).")
inl note_of_eoie_crate_gate_2 () = Note ("EOIE-CRATE-GATE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:38Z", "eoie", "07:38 drafted: scratch f5/src/eoie_crate_gate_model (model.spi pure: [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output parsing that stops at the next section, CRLF-safe line counts, within/OVER/no-output verdicts, enforce outcomes incl. the vacuity rule; gate.spi lib-only: repo-wide walk skipping dot folders, target, node_modules, _build, deps and lab, --limit via a code-point digit parser) typechecks and passes 2 test blocks against the live lib. Verb slice crate-gate/apply-crate-gate.ps1 adds VerbCrate (eoie crate gate <root> [--enforce] [--limit n]), usage, the spiral-tests registry entry (appended at the list tail, order-independent), eoie_cli dep and workspace member; prelaunch clean on a live copy. Ships in release L (candidate notebook + baseline merge added).")
inl note_of_eoie_crate_gate_3 () = Note ("EOIE-CRATE-GATE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:42Z", "eoie", "07:42 main's ruling applied: under --enforce a crate with no generated output fails (GateFailedMissing n: 'n crate(s) have no generated output, so generation did not run or failed'), since enforcement runs right after generation; without --enforce it stays the 'no generated output yet' report. Order of refusals: vacuous, then over, then missing. Model test extended (enforce fails on missing; report-only passes), package passes against the live lib. dice ui (16,281) and alphabet documents (15,315) stay report-only until split.")
inl note_of_eoie_crate_gate_4 () = Note ("EOIE-CRATE-GATE", 4u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): CENSUS-2 (per-crate audit, thresholds from project policy, one count shared with topology). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl backend_balance () = Task ("BACKEND-BALANCE", "Every project compiles each component to the backend that suits it: Gleam (BEAM) where Erlang's runtime pays (supervision, many concurrent long-lived actors, timeouts, monitors, hot reload, distribution), Rust where native speed pays (data-parallel work with rayon, short-lived CLIs, native libs, tight memory)", Epic, P1, D7, 41u32, Active, "LIB-PAR-MAP,LIB-OTP-ACTORS,X-BACKEND-MAP,EOIE-BACKEND-MAP,TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", "spiral,lib,x,eoie,tibia,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_backend_balance () = Planning ("BACKEND-BALANCE", Unestimated, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_backend_balance_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T10:59Z", "x", "2026-10-23 06:59 x: user request 2026-10-23 06:5x: 'everything that can leverage erlang goodies compile to gleam and the rest rayon etc compiles to rust, all projects gotta reach the perfect balance between gleam and rust compilations; x is a good playground, tibia and eoie we haven't thought about'. RULE, written before any classification. A component goes to GLEAM when two or more hold: (G1) it lives for minutes or longer and holds state; (G2) it runs many independent activities at once (clients, jobs, fetchers, simulated users); (G3) it must supervise, restart, time out or monitor other processes or OS children; (G4) its parts talk by messages; (G5) hot code reload or multi-node distribution would remove real pain. A component goes to RUST when one holds: (R1) CPU-bound work over independent items that rayon can split (seeds, files, schedules, cells); (R2) a CLI invoked per command, where BEAM's cold start (measure it; ~0.5-1 s) is too slow; (R3) it needs native libraries (TLS, GPU, SIMD, compression); (R4) peak memory matters more than throughput. TIES and everything else: keep the current backend. Domain code stays backend-neutral (backend_switch only at boundaries), so a component can be compiled both ways and its Cells test blocks must agree on rust and gleam. Every switch lands only with a measurement before and after (wall time, cold start, peak memory; an existing benchmark if there is one) and the result in the item. GPU work stays WGSL and C stays where the matrix needs it (tibia). Children: LIB-PAR-MAP and LIB-OTP-ACTORS (lib groundwork), then X-BACKEND-MAP (playground first), EOIE-BACKEND-MAP and TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:00Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:00 x: R2 measured, 5 runs each, on this box while eoie's F9 and main's corpus checks ran. 'erl -noshell -eval halt().' (OTP 29) took 2430, 2603, 2056, 1913 and 1110 ms. Rust CLIs: 'xc --help' 145, 34, 87, 71, 53 ms; 'eoie agile status' 45, 51, 73, 70, 160 ms. BEAM cold start is 15-50x a Rust CLI, so per-command tools stay Rust, and BEAM components must be long-lived services (a host or daemon) that the Rust CLI talks to, the way Cells keeps a warm compiler daemon (KINO-WARM-RUNNER). Re-measure on a quiet box before any decision rests on the exact factor.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_3 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T00:42Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:42 x: progress bookkeeping by rule, not a new estimate. The epic's progress is the mean of its five deps' recorded progress: X-BACKEND-MAP 30, EOIE-BACKEND-MAP 15, TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP 80, LIB-PAR-MAP 50, LIB-OTP-ACTORS 50 -> 45. Status Planned -> Active, because all five are Active. Recompute it the same way when a child moves.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_4 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 4u32, "2026-10-24T03:36Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-23 23:36 tibia: recomputed by note 3's rule after TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP moved to 90 (Blocked on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR's runtime-process arm; its classification, host core, executor, actor wrapper and Rust launcher are landed, TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note 16): mean of 30, 15, 90, 50, 50 = 47.")
inl note_of_backend_balance_5 () = Note ("BACKEND-BALANCE", 5u32, "2026-10-24T11:21Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 07:21 tibia: recomputed by note 3's rule after TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP was re-sized to 60 (its OS-port arm entered scope, TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note 19): mean of LIB-PAR-MAP 50, LIB-OTP-ACTORS 50, X-BACKEND-MAP 30, EOIE-BACKEND-MAP 15, TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP 60 = 41.")
inl lib_par_map () = Task ("LIB-PAR-MAP", "lib/spiral: a backend-neutral parallel map (par'.map / par'.each over independent items) with a Rust arm on rayon and a Gleam arm on BEAM processes, so data-parallel code is written once and compiled to either", Story, P1, D4, 50u32, Active, "GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", "lib,spiral,main,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_par_map () = Planning ("LIB-PAR-MAP", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_par_map_1 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:00Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:00 x: BACKEND-BALANCE groundwork. Today lib/spiral has no parallel primitive and no rayon arm: eoie reaches rayon only through raw host-code blobs (eoie_do_read/host.spi:36, eoie_grep_query/search.spi:51, eoie_bundle_retention/retention_terminal.spi:7), which eoie's own style contract asks to remove. Shape: backend_switch with Rust = rayon par_iter over a Vec, results in input order; Gleam = one process per chunk (chunk count = schedulers), monitored, results collected in order, the first crash surfaced as a typed error; any other backend = the sequential map (same results, so tests agree everywhere). Test blocks on rust + gleam: order kept, a raising element is reported, an empty list works. Benchmark both arms on one CPU-bound workload (e.g. x's splitter over every client cell) against the sequential map.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_2 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:30 main: design read (code + probe). The Rust blocker is Send. Spiral's Rust output is Rc everywhere: strings are Rc<str>, closures are Rc<dyn Fn> built by a per-closure factory 'fn name(free vars) -> Rc<dyn Fn(..)>' (codegenRust closure, core ~164974). A closure with no free vars keeps one copy per thread in a thread_local. rayon needs Send + Sync, so today's eoie blobs convert to String/Vec inside host code. Probe (scratchpad par-probe/main.rs, rustc -O, std::thread::scope with 4 and 2 chunks): calling the FACTORY fn pointer inside each worker (fn() -> Rc<dyn Fn> is Send + Sync) type-checks and runs. Results come back in input order: i32 squares, Rc<str> strings deep-copied at the boundary as String, and empty input. Plan, decided by rule: (1) compiler, Rust only: a macro escape that renders a closure term as its factory call text (name plus captured args) when every free var is a Send type (primitive scalar or string-by-copy), and refuses at compile time otherwise. That puts soundness in the compiler instead of an unsafe Send wrapper, which would be UB once a capture is an Rc cloned on two threads. (2) lib par'.map/par'.each: Rust arm = rayon par_iter over chunks, each worker calls the factory once; element and result types limited to scalars and strings, copied at the boundary. Gleam arm = one process per chunk (chunks = schedulers_online), each sends back its index with list.map(chunk, f), collected in order under a monitor, so a crash surfaces as Error. Every other backend = the sequential map. (3) Test blocks rust + gleam: order kept, empty list, crash reported; then the benchmark from the first note. Step 1 is a pack item (codegenRust only, matrix review); the Gleam arm can draft now. Not started in lib/spiral: tibia's live block 09:40-12:30, and lib lands only in gaps.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_3 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:43Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:43 main: Gleam arm reworked onto the user's granular direction (LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 2): scratchpad par-lab/par'.spi is now pure Spiral over par-lab/erlang_process.spi primitives (new_subject, spawn_unlinked, monitor, select_map, select_specific_monitor, selector_receive_forever, exited_normally, schedulers_online). Typed union par_event = ChunkDone (index, values) | WorkerEnded exit_reason. One worker per chunk (chunk count = schedulers_online). It collects until every result and every Down has arrived, so no stray monitor message is left in the caller's mailbox, then concatenates in index order, and an abnormal Down fails with failwith. No global blob: the only globals are imports and one @external line per BIF. Tests: chunks_split_evenly, chunks_empty (all backends), map_keeps_order (gleam/rust/fsharp), map_empty, map_strings. Not compiled yet; it needs GLEAM-ERLANG-1X (the 1.x API names) and a compile gap. Arrays live in the process dictionary on Gleam, so items and captures must not hold arrays; this mirrors the Rust arm's Send rule.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_4 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 4u32, "2026-10-23T11:48Z", "tibia-lane", "Use case from tibia-lane (07:5x, TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note 5: tibia's largest R1 win). (1) Trainer inner loop: network.evolve (src/network.spi 162-181) scores 64 offspring per generation, each fitness candidate independent; fitness = worth o camp (network_tactic w) first count (hunting_pack), and each call runs about 50 training clears. Shape: par'.map over offspring indices 0..63 giving i32 scores, then the existing sequential max (keeps the first best on ties, so the result stays bit-identical to the sequential evolve). The closure captures camp (a record of unions, i32 and nested arrays such as kinds and layout), o (a union) and the parent weights (array i32); it returns i32. (2) Held-out scoring: clear_ledger_of over 100 seeds is a fold of per-seed ledgers, so par'.map per seed plus a sequential sum. Today: single-threaded C, about 43-67 min per M1 arm on this 8-thread laptop. Constraints tibia needs: results in input order; deterministic (no shared RNG; tibia keys every roll by seed, tick, slot and purpose); captured values that are arrays of i32 and records of unions crossing the thread boundary; the C oracle and the Rust arm agreeing in Cells. A first consumer test: evolve on a 4-gene toy fitness, par versus sequential, bit-identical best genes.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_5 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 5u32, "2026-10-23T12:06Z", "main", "2026-10-23 08:06 main: Rust half drafted. (1) Compiler: %TEMP%\\pack-next6\\rust-closure-factory.ps1. Two anchors, each matching once on the next5e core, which parses under both defines. A macro term preceded by the text '__spiral_closure_factory ' renders as its closure's factory fn name (closure_names via closure_of_var, the same map the Export op uses for 'a closure without captures'). The marker text is stripped, and a captured or unknown closure is a codegen error naming the reason. (2) lib arm, par-lab/par'.spi map_on_threads: one expression on std::thread::scope with chunks = len.div_ceil(available_parallelism). Each worker calls (factory())(x.clone()) and the results are flattened in chunk order. It's std-only because Cells builds Rust cells with bare rustc (cells.ex rustc_args, no crates), so rayon can't link in test blocks; a rayon arm waits for a cargo-built consumer. rustc enforces Sync on items and Send on results, so Rc<str> items are refused at compile time (sound). Strings therefore stay gleam-only in the tests until a String boundary copy exists. The pack carrying (1) goes into the next codegen review (matrix: no residual changes, since no sample uses the marker), and lib par'.spi lands with GLEAM-ERLANG-1X in a gap.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_6 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 6u32, "2026-10-23T14:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:06 x: (1) CAVEAT for the Rust arm in note 5, from today's measurement (LIB-DICTM note 5): Spiral compiles a closure's record, tuple and unit parameters FLATTENED into separate Rust arguments, while map_on_threads calls (factory())(x.clone()) with one value. So par'.map over items that are tuples or records (tibia's use case passes offspring indices, fine; a (seed, schedule) pair would not be) fails with E0057 'this function takes N arguments but 1 was supplied'. Either restrict the item type to single-value types at the type level, or have the arm call through a Spiral-side adapter closure that takes the whole item as one i32 index and looks it up in an array captured by the factory. The index form also suits the 'no captures' factory rule, because the array becomes the map's own argument. UNVERIFIED: whether a closure RETURNING a record or tuple also changes shape on Rust. Today's evidence covers parameters only (dictm's folds returned lists), so probe a record-returning closure in the arm's first test. (2) x's first consumer, for the measured switch in X-BACKEND-MAP note 2: replaying N schedules of one corpus is N independent scheduler.sync_run calls. Item = schedule seed (i32), result = a scheduler record (or its XSIM summary line, a string), order kept, 2 to ~20 items of seconds each. The commute check over consecutive results stays sequential after the map.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_7 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 7u32, "2026-10-23T18:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 14:30 main: on rayon (BACKEND-BALANCE R1 names it): par'.map's Rust arm uses std::thread::scope with chunking, not rayon, because Cells compiles Rust cells with bare rustc and no crates, so a rayon arm could not be gated by the lib's own test blocks. A rayon variant fits cargo consumers only: a separate par'.map_rayon whose arm is '(*!native).par_iter().map(|x| (__spiral_closure_factory !fn())(x.clone())).collect()' (the closure-factory marker gives each worker its own closure, since Spiral closures are Rc and not Send), proven through a consumer's cargo build (x is the playground per BACKEND-BALANCE). Not drafted today; the std arm lands first with today's lib landing.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_8 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 8u32, "2026-10-23T20:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:37 main: par'.spi NOT landed: its Rust arm needs the __spiral_closure_factory marker that pack-next7's codegen implements (the installed compiler emits it verbatim: E0425 + a parse error), and F# also fails map_keeps_order; Gleam passes now. Kept in %TEMP%\\lib-land-1023\\lib\\spiral\\par'.spi (out of the land copy's package.spiproj). Next: land with next7's install, after an F# look.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_9 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 9u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:17 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 50. par' is drafted (lib-land-1023), and its Rust arm waits for pack-next7's closure factory (rust-closure-factory in pack-next6/7). It lands after that install.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_10 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 10u32, "2026-10-24T04:04Z", "x", "10-24 00:04 x: for main's 'F# look' (n 16:37): queued a Cells run of the draft par'.spi on a live-lib copy (%TEMP%\\x-par-fsharp, par' listed last) as job-1792814654580-x-par-fsharp-probe. Its F# arm is the plain listm.map fallback, so the failing map_keeps_order output should show whether F# trips on the module (Gleam-only union par_event over erlang_process types) or on the arm. The Rust failure there is the known missing closure-factory marker and isn't news. The result goes into this note's follow-up.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_11 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 11u32, "2026-10-24T05:36Z", "x", "10-24 01:36 x: F# look ANSWERED (n10's probe, rerun as job-1792817012060, 01:19). The draft par'.spi on a live-lib copy (par' listed last) ran 5 tests and failed 2: map_empty and map_keeps_order, both only on rust (the known missing __spiral_closure_factory marker). F# map_keeps_order PASSES on today's lib and compiler (single-flight B553EF6B78E6EE29), and gleam passes everything. So 16:37's F# failure doesn't reproduce; the only blocker left is pack-next7's closure factory for the Rust arm.")
inl note_of_lib_par_map_12 () = Note ("LIB-PAR-MAP", 12u32, "2026-10-24T05:40Z", "main", "2026-10-24 01:40 main: thanks to x's n11 (F# passes; only the Rust closure-factory marker is missing). pack-next6's rust-closure-factory.ps1 was in no composed stack (next4 took other next6 slices, not this one); it composes cleanly on 5g and is now in pack 5g's compose (core AEC3FF009A141AB8). It rides review-4 (job-1792813775220), and after the install par'.spi lands through the gate kit with its rust tests.")
inl lib_otp_actors () = Task ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", "lib/spiral: typed actors and supervisors with a Gleam arm on gleam_otp (actor, supervisor, subject, monitor, timeouts) and a deterministic single-threaded arm elsewhere, so long-lived concurrent components are modeled once in Spiral", Story, P1, D5, 50u32, Active, "GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", "lib,spiral,main,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_otp_actors () = Planning ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:00Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:00 x: BACKEND-BALANCE groundwork, the G-side of the rule. lib/spiral/process.spi covers only OS-process capture on Gleam (gleam/erlang/process); there is no actor, supervisor or monitor primitive. Shape: a typed message protocol per actor (a GADT of requests with their reply types), start / call / cast / stop, supervisors with restart strategies (one_for_one first), timeouts as values. The Gleam arm uses gleam_otp. The deterministic arm (Rust and the rest) runs actors as a step loop with a mailbox, so the same actor code is testable without BEAM and agrees on test blocks. Relate to LIB-REACTORS (reactors map onto actors on BEAM) and KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (Kino's Elixir supervision is the first big consumer). Check gleam_otp's current release and API before designing (memory latest-of-everything).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:07Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:07 x: user direction 2026-10-23 07:0x: 'we gotta master gleam's subject api granular modeling from spiral though, to not do mess like rust_global that eoie indulged in'. Design constraint: model gleam/erlang/process and gleam/otp granularly in Spiral, one nominal per Gleam type (Subject(msg) as a phantom-typed nominal over the message type, Pid, Monitor, Selector(payload), Timer, ExitReason, actor Started/Next) and one named one-expression primitive per Gleam function (process.new_subject, send, receive with a timeout, call, try_call, selector select/select_map, monitor, link/unlink, send_after, cancel_timer). No $'{...}' or multi-statement host blocks and no global blobs beyond the imports. The typed layer on top (actor protocols as GADTs of request -> reply, supervisors) is pure Spiral built from those primitives. Mirror Gleam's real API: read gleam_erlang's and gleam_otp's current docs and releases first. Test blocks on gleam for each primitive, so the binding is proven granular, not just compiled.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_3 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:47Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:47 main: API read from hexdocs (current: gleam_erlang 1.3.0, gleam_otp 1.3.0). process: Subject, Pid, Monitor, Selector, Timer, Name, Down = ProcessDown(monitor, pid, reason) | PortDown, ExitReason = Normal | Killed | Abnormal(dynamic), 44 functions (spawn, spawn_unlinked, new_subject, send, receive(from, within), receive_forever, call, monitor, select_map, select_specific_monitor, selector_receive(_forever), send_after, cancel_timer, link, kill, trap_exits, named subjects). actor: Builder(state, message, return), Next, Started(pid, data), StartError = InitTimeout | InitFailed(String) | InitExited(ExitReason); new, new_with_initialiser, on_message, start, continue, stop, stop_abnormal, with_selector, named, call, send. static_supervisor: new(OneForOne | OneForAll | RestForOne), add(ChildSpecification), restart_tolerance(intensity 2, period 5 by default), auto_shutdown, start, supervised. supervision.ChildSpecification(start, restart Permanent | Transient | Temporary, significant, child_type Worker(shutdown_ms) | Supervisor) with worker, supervisor, restart, significant, timeout, map_data. Drafts in scratchpad par-lab, granular per the user's direction: erlang_process.spi (8 nominals, 22 one-expression primitives, 4 gleam tests) and otp_actor.spi (4 nominals; new, on_message, continue, stop, stop_abnormal, start, started_data, call, send; counter_actor_counts gleam test). Next, in order: static_supervisor + supervision bindings, then the typed layer in pure Spiral (a protocol = GADT of request -> reply, start, call, cast, a supervised child), then the deterministic arm (mailbox queue + step loop, call = enqueue + run until the reply). Blocked only on compiling: GLEAM-ERLANG-1X (dep) moves the cells to 1.x. Open question for the compile check: whether a curried Spiral closure is fn(a) -> fn(b) -> c in Gleam; the drafts assume curried (!handler(state)(message)).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_4 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 4u32, "2026-10-23T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:49 main: drafted par-lab/otp_supervisor.spi: nominals supervisor_builder, supervisor, child d; Spiral unions strategy (OneForOne | OneForAll | RestForOne) and restart (Permanent | Transient | Temporary), each mapped to its Gleam constructor by a compile-time match. Primitives new, restart_tolerance, add, start, supervised, worker, with_restart, with_shutdown, plus the gleam test supervisor_starts_a_worker. Typed-layer design question for the first compile experiment: BackendSwitch types every arm and requires them equal, so a reply handle inside a message type has to be ONE Spiral type on every backend. The candidate is nominal reply_to r = backend_switch { Gleam: process.Subject(r); others: a Spiral ref (option r) }, as listm' list'_switch does for List(t). The experiment settles whether a switch field may name a Spiral type rather than host text. If it can't, reply handles become an index into an actor-local reply table, a pure Spiral i32 on every backend, with the Gleam arm keeping a Subject per pending call.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_5 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 5u32, "2026-10-23T12:37Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:38 x: evidence for main's open question in note 3. Yes, a Spiral closure reaches Gleam curried: fn(a) -> fn(b) -> c, applied as f(a)(b). The draft neutral map C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\dictm.spi folds with the Gleam arm 'dict.fold(!d, !init, fn(acc, k, v) { !f(acc)(k)(v) })', and its tests passed 3/3 on gleam (plus 6/6 for inbox.spi on top of it), so the curried shape your drafts assume (!handler(state)(message)) is right. RUST CAVEAT for the deterministic arm: in a closure that reaches a Rust host splice, a tuple parameter is flattened into separate arguments and a unit () parameter is dropped. dictm's fold over set k = dict k () therefore broke on Rust with 12x E0057 (wrong argument count) when the splice called (!f)(acc)(k)(v) with v : (). The fix was a separate fold_keys primitive whose closure never takes the unit. For otp_actor: a handler or message that is () or a tuple must not be called positionally from a Rust splice; keep the splice's closure types free of () and tuples, or wrap them in a record or nominal.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_6 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 6u32, "2026-10-23T13:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:06 x: widening note 5's Rust caveat. Not only () and tuples: RECORDS are flattened too. On Rust every compound parameter of a Spiral closure becomes separate arguments, so a host splice that calls (!handler)(state)(message) breaks as soon as state or message is a record, tuple or unit. Hit today through dictm.fold over record values (LIB-DICTM note 5). For the deterministic arm, keep the actor step loop in pure Spiral (mailbox list + a match on the message), and keep $'...' to primitives that never call back into Spiral closures. On the Gleam arm, actor.on_message(!handler) with curried whole values is fine.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_7 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 7u32, "2026-10-23T13:13Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:13 main: typed-layer design, settled with x's 09:xx finding (Rust flattens a closure's record, tuple and unit parameters, so no host splice may call a closure with user-chosen types; Gleam closures are curried f(a)(b), which confirms the drafts' !handler(state)(message)). Decision: no uniform handle type across backends. That would need the reply_to switch experiment of note 4, and on Rust it would put closures with user types behind splices. Instead, domain code is generic over a runtime record: an actor is { init : s; handle : s -> m -> step s } with union step s = Continue : s | Stop, and a runtime h r is a record of Spiral functions { spawn; send; call } over a handle type h and a reply type r. Domain functions are written forall h r against it, and the entry picks the runtime in one run_target: Gleam passes the otp_actor-backed runtime (h = process.Subject(m), r = process.Subject(reply)), and every other backend the deterministic step runtime (pure Spiral: mailbox list and state in Spiral refs, call = enqueue plus drain to the reply, no splice calls a closure). Each arm returns the domain result, so BackendSwitch's equal-type rule holds on the result, not on the handles. Messages that carry replies are parameterized by r (union counter_message r = Add : i32 | Read : r). Next: draft step_runtime.spi and a counter domain test that runs on gleam, rust and c with the same expected value.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_8 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 8u32, "2026-10-23T13:15Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:15 main: drafted scratchpad par-lab/step_runtime.spi following note 7. Types: union step s = Continue : s | Stop; type actor s m = { init; handle : s -> m -> step s }. The deterministic runtime is pure Spiral, with mut cells for state, mailbox and a draining flag: spawn; drain (a join-point loop; a send from inside a handler only enqueues, so there is no reentrancy); send; reply; call, which enqueues a request carrying a reply cell, drains, and fails if the actor stopped without replying. otp_start adapts the same actor spec to gleam_otp (on_message, Continue -> continue, Stop -> stop). The counter domain counter_total forall h r takes start, post, ask and answer, and the test counter_counts_on_every_backend picks the otp runtime on Gleam and the step runtime elsewhere, with ///> gleam rust c and expected 7. stopped_actor_ignores_later_messages runs on the default target. Not compiled: it waits for GLEAM-ERLANG-1X and a compile gap after tibia's block. The first compile also answers whether a record type with mut fields can be built with { state = mut ...; ... }.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_9 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 9u32, "2026-10-23T13:20Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:20 x: requirements from the first consumer, the xsim runner shell (SIM-IN-SPIRAL note 20), mapped onto main's par-lab drafts (otp_actor, step_runtime: actor s m = {init; handle}). The pure scheduler (x-world scheduler.spi) is the actor STATE. It already emits StartTask {key; sources} / CancelTask key commands and takes finished_task key result, so the shell is: union runner_message = Delivered delivery | Finished {key; result} | Crashed {key; reason} | Settle (reply to scheduler). Primitives it needs beyond the current drafts: (1) the actor's own subject inside handle, so an executor can send Finished back: gleam_otp actor.new_with_initialiser (the draft has only new); (2) spawn_unlinked + monitor, with a selector mapping the Down message to Crashed for the task's key: runner.ex turns a Task exit into status :crash (select_specific_monitor or select_map in the draft's erlang_process); (3) kill(pid) for CancelTask (runner.ex uses Task.shutdown(task, :brutal_kill)), so the shell keeps key -> pid in its state; (4) send_after for pace_ms and per-task timeouts. Deterministic arm: run the executor inline and feed Finished immediately, which is exactly scheduler.sync_run (parity with the Elixir Runner already passes), so step_runtime's counter shape fits as is. Test plan when it compiles: the same prelude stream through the Gleam shell and through sync_run must settle to the same visible map.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_10 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 10u32, "2026-10-23T13:44Z", "tibia-lane", "tibia-lane 09:4x: requirements from the second consumer, the tibia arena host (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note 6), mapped onto main's step_runtime draft (actor s m = {init; handle}). The pure core is tested and landed: apps/host/arena_host.spi, step : host -> host_event -> host * list host_command (events WindowGranted, WindowLost, WorldReady, WorldCrashed, RunEnded outcome, RunTimedOut; commands StartWorld, RestartWorld, ResetCharacter, StartRun stage run, StopRun, Record fate, ReleaseWindow), over block_plan's crash rule. Four traces are Cells tests on lua and gleam that replay an event list, which is exactly what the deterministic arm should run. Needs beyond the drafts: (1) handle emits commands, so either handle returns step s plus a command list that the runtime hands to an executor, or the executor is a second actor the handler sends to (x's StartTask/CancelTask is the same shape, so one pattern would serve both). (2) OS processes as ports with an exit status delivered as a message: canary.exe and mariadbd for the world actor, and target/offscreen/offscreen.exe (apps/offscreen, landed today: one OTClient run on a hidden desktop, exit 0 with a pid/exit line, 124 on timeout) for the run actor. A port exit maps to WorldCrashed or RunEnded. (3) A timer (send_after) for RunTimedOut, cancellable when the run ends first. (4) A monitor on the block actor, so the eoie window is released when the block ends however it ends (WindowLost covers revocation). (5) The window request itself is an eoie CLI call, so a port with a typed reply parsed from its key=value line. No BEAM-only types in the domain: the pure step stays as it is, and only the executor is per-backend.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_11 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 11u32, "2026-10-23T20:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:37 main: LANDED 16:36: erlang_process.spi (4/4 on gleam), otp_actor.spi (1/1), otp_supervisor.spi (1/1), step_runtime.spi (2/2, the cross-backend step runtime), listed in lib package.spiproj. Defects the gate found in my drafts, fixed before landing: step_runtime assigned mut fields through a non-mut record (it type-failed the whole package), erlang_process.down_reason used field access on the 1.x Down union (now a case), and the supervisor test left the message type open.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_12 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 12u32, "2026-10-23T21:00Z", "tibia-lane", "Finding from the first consumer, tibia-lane 17:0x: on the Gleam backend a Spiral array created in the caller cannot be read inside an otp_actor. tibia's scratch arena-actor/arena_host.spi wraps the arena host's step function as a step_runtime.actor (state host * trace, messages Event or Trace r, read back with call), modelled on counter_total. Gleam via step_runtime.otp_start fails with: exception error: no function clause matching array:is_fix(undefined) (array.erl:463), then a perform_call panic (gleam/erlang/process.gleam:626). The host state holds runs : array i32 (block_plan.stages_of), built in the test process as spec.init, and Spiral arrays on Gleam live in the creating process's dictionary, so the actor process sees undefined. The 6 non-actor tests pass on lua and gleam (Cells job tibia-host-actor). Options as I see them: (1) document that actor state must hold no Spiral arrays or refs (consumers use lists or records), or (2) have otp_start take an init thunk evaluated inside the actor (new_with_initialiser, which x also asked for), or (3) copy arrays across the spawn. tibia will move the host's run plan to a list next session unless (2) lands first. Repro: tibia scratch arena-actor (package with block_plan and arena_host, test the_host_step_runs_inside_an_actor_and_gives_the_same_trace_on_every_backend). Update 17:1x: building the array inside the actor works. tibia's host_actor keeps its run plan as a list and builds the host on the first message, 7 of 7 green on gleam and lua (job tibia-host-actor2), landed in apps/host/arena_host.spi. main landed option (2) as otp_actor.new_with_init at 17:1x, and tibia moves to it next session.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_13 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 13u32, "2026-10-23T21:01Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:01 main: accepted tibia's first-consumer finding (array state crosses the actor's process boundary: Gleam arrays live in the creating process's dictionary, array:is_fix(undefined)). Fix chosen: an init-thunk constructor, otp_actor.new_with_init (fun () => state) over gleam_otp actor.new_with_initialiser, so the state is built inside the actor process (x asked for the same); plus a test whose state holds an array, gated on gleam. First lib item of the next round.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_14 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 14u32, "2026-10-23T21:15Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:15 main: tibia's array-state finding FIXED the same day: otp_actor.new_with_init (timeout_ms : i32) (init : () -> s) over gleam_otp actor.new_with_initialiser runs init inside the actor process and returns its subject. Test array_state_is_built_inside_the_actor (state ;[4; 5; 6], length back through call = 3) passes on gleam; the file's gate is 2/2 (lesson: a Cells test sees only the definitions above it, so the test sits at the end of the file). Landed 17:14 as otp_actor.spi 200738F70462FE88; tibia and x told.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_15 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 15u32, "2026-10-23T21:16Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:15 x: consumer note after main's new_with_init (state built in the actor process). The sim shell (x-world runner_shell.spi) still needs the actor's OWN subject in its handler, for workers to send Finished back. Today an Adopt message right after start delivers it, which leaves a short window where state.me is None (dispatched skips spawning then). Proposed extension: the init thunk receives the subject, as gleam_otp's initialiser does (fun (me : subject m) => state). Then Adopt and the None case disappear.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_16 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 16u32, "2026-10-23T21:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:25 main: x's follow-up landed too (17:21, otp_actor.spi 6525F5CCD4F86A0F): new_with_self (timeout_ms : i32) (init : erlang_process.subject m -> s) gives the initialiser the actor's own subject (gleam_otp new_with_initialiser), so workers can report back without an Adopt message. Test the_initialiser_receives_the_actors_own_subject; gate 3/3 on gleam. tibia's executor will use it for OS port exits.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_17 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 17u32, "2026-10-23T21:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:27 x: note 15's request is done: main's new_with_self (6525F5CC) gives the initialiser the actor's own subject. runner_shell has switched; its gleam check is queued.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_18 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 18u32, "2026-10-23T23:55Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:55 main: status fixed by the item's own two parts. The Gleam arm landed on 10-23 (erlang_process, otp_actor with new/new_with_init/new_with_self/on_message/call/send, otp_supervisor; all gates green), so this is Active 50, not Planned 0. Left: the deterministic single-threaded arm for the other backends (Rust first, the end-product target): the same builder API over an in-process mailbox queue run to quiescence, plus tests that pass on rust and gleam with the same expected text.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_19 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 19u32, "2026-10-23T23:56Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:56 main: design for the single-threaded arm (no code yet; it needs compile iterations). (1) Types: erlang_process' nominals are bare Gleam types (subject t = process.Subject); each becomes a backend_switch type record, as dictm does, with a Rust arm. Rust subject t = Rc<RefCell<SpiralMailbox<T>>>, where SpiralMailbox holds a VecDeque<T> queue, an Option<Rc<dyn Fn(T)>> deliver hook and a delivering flag, emitted once through global. (2) Semantics, deterministic run to quiescence: send with no deliver hook pushes to the queue; with a hook it calls deliver unless delivering is already set, in which case it pushes, and after the handler returns it drains the queue in FIFO order, so a handler that sends to itself never recurses. receive pops the front or returns Error at once: timeouts are meaningless in one thread, and an empty mailbox after quiescence is a definite timeout. receive_forever on an empty mailbox fails with a deadlock message instead of hanging. (3) otp_actor on Rust: builder = the initial-state thunk plus the handler. start creates the subject and installs deliver = (state in Rc<RefCell<s>>, handler) mapping Next to Continue(s') or Stop. After Stop the hook is removed and later sends just queue, matching a dead actor's mailbox. call builds a reply subject, sends request reply_subject, and pops the reply, which the synchronous delivery has already put there; an empty reply mailbox is the timeout outcome. (4) spawn on Rust runs the work closure to completion at once, so a spawned process that blocks in receive is the deadlock failure above; monitors report Normal when the work returns and Abnormal(text) on failwith (catch_unwind). (5) Test plan: the existing gleam tests (counter, array state, new_with_self, supervisor restart) gain '///> rust' with the same expected values; supervisor restart on Rust re-runs the child init after a failwith inside the handler. Rust is first because it is the end-product target; other backends follow the same mailbox shape.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_20 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 20u32, "2026-10-23T23:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:57 main: first step of the single-threaded arm drafted as a standalone Rust probe (scratch mailbox-probe: main.spi plus run.ps1, queued in requeue-after-block.ps1 as main-probe-rust-mailbox). It contains the SpiralMailbox struct, spiral_mailbox_new and spiral_mailbox_send. send pushes, then delivers through the hook unless delivering is already set, and drains FIFO with the borrow released before each handler call. new_subject, send, receive_or and on_deliver exercise it. The main checks FIFO receive (3 then 4), the empty mailbox, and a handler that sends to itself 3 times before replying elsewhere, which needs the reentrancy guard. Expected exit 30143. If it holds, the mailbox becomes erlang_process' Rust subject arm (backend_switch type record), and otp_actor's Rust start installs on_deliver over Rc<RefCell<state>>.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_21 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 21u32, "2026-10-24T00:32Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:32 main: next step staged after the probe. %TEMP%\\lib-otp-single\\apply.ps1 (6 anchors, dry run clean on the tree) gives erlang_process' subject and received backend_switch type records: Rust subject = Rc<RefCell<SpiralMailbox<T>>> and received = Option<T>, with the mailbox helpers emitted through global, byte-identical to the probe's. new_subject, send, receive (an empty mailbox after quiescence is the timeout outcome) and received_value get Rust arms; their Gleam arms are unchanged. It applies only if main-probe-rust-mailbox (job-1792801895047) exits 730143, and then goes through lib-gate-kit on erlang_process.spi with '///> rust' added to send_then_receive and receive_times_out.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_22 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 22u32, "2026-10-24T00:40Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:40 main: the Rust mailbox probe passed on its first real run (job-1792802071939, exit 730143): FIFO receive, the empty mailbox, a handler sending to itself 3 times drained without recursion, and a counter actor answering call with 7 after Add 3 and Add 4. So %TEMP%\\lib-otp-single\\apply.ps1 (8 anchors now, with '///> rust' on send_then_receive and receive_times_out) is queued: main-lib-otp-single-gate (job-1792802374879) after chain A's fiscal gate, then main-lib-otp-single-land (--after-ok). Next after it lands: otp_actor's Rust start over on_deliver and Rc<RefCell<state>>, as the probe's start_actor did.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_actors_23 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-ACTORS", 23u32, "2026-10-24T03:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:37 main: tibia's 23:3x finding closes a gap I left at 17:15. new_with_init fixed array state for otp_actor callers, but step_runtime.otp_start still called otp_actor.new spec.init, so state built in the caller died at the actor boundary for every step_runtime user. Fix drafted as %TEMP%\\lib-step-init\\apply.ps1 (dry run clean on the live lib, 7 anchors): step_runtime.actor's init becomes () -> s, spawn runs it, otp_start uses new_with_init 10000i32 spec.init, and the lib call/init test timeouts go from 1000 to 10000 ms (tibia's arena_host timed out at 1000 ms under load). Breaking for callers: tibia's arena_host.spi:216 needs init = fun () => (plan, None, ''); tibia was told. Queued: job-1792812964392-main-lib-step-init-gate then job-1792812965354-main-lib-step-init-land, at the end of main's lib chain.")
inl x_backend_map () = Task ("X-BACKEND-MAP", "x: map every x component to Gleam or Rust by BACKEND-BALANCE's rule, then switch them one at a time with a measurement each (x is the playground for the rule)", Story, P1, D4, 30u32, Active, "", "x,backends,sim", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_backend_map () = Planning ("X-BACKEND-MAP", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_backend_map_1 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:01Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:01 x: first classification by the rule (G1-G5 / R1-R4), to be confirmed by measurement before each switch. RUST, keep: (1) xc and all client crates: per-command CLI (R2, measured 34-145 ms vs BEAM 1.1-2.6 s) plus TLS (R3). GLEAM: (2) the sim runner shell, which starts, cancels, times out and harvests executors (G1, G2, G3, G4): gleam_otp via LIB-OTP-ACTORS, replacing Elixir Task (SIM-IN-SPIRAL); (3) the daemon-memory sampler, a periodic monitor of an OS tree (G1, G3); (4) the future X API server: the sim serving xc and e2e instead of the Python stub, i.e. many concurrent requests and rate-limit windows (G1, G2, G4; SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY); (5) a future long-running 'xc watch' over many accounts with backoff (G1, G2, G3), as a Gleam service the Rust CLI talks to. BOTH, one Spiral source with both arms: (6) the sim world model and runner core (SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL, SIM-RUNNER-CORE-SPIRAL) run inside the Gleam runner by default, and on Rust for batch work; (7) corpus commute, scale ladders and schedule sweeps (many independent schedules and seeds) go Rust + LIB-PAR-MAP (R1) for batch runs, measured against today's Elixir run time; (8) splitter/weights stay dual (already tested rust + gleam); a rayon arm for bulk postability over 36 files only if a measurement shows a gain. KEEP: invariants, digest and view (small inputs, no rule fires). Order: (2) and (6) with the Spiral rewrite, then (7) once LIB-PAR-MAP lands, then (4) with feature parity. Record each switch's before/after numbers here.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_2 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:32Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:32 x: RULE for switch (2), the runner shell, written before any measurement. Candidates: E = today's Elixir Xsim.Runner + Executors.fake; G = the Gleam shell (LIB-OTP-ACTORS actor over scheduler.spi) with the same fake executor (fake_out: sha256 of the sources, first 12 hex); R = scheduler.sync_run compiled to Rust (the deterministic arm, no concurrency) as the floor. Inputs: the prelude stream (65 deliveries), sim-stress.world.json's stream, and a 50k-event corpus from the scenario exports (seed 1). Each run on a quiet box (no live block, no other heavy job), 5 runs per candidate per input, rotating order. Measure wall time from process start (BEAM boot included for E and G), peak working set of the process tree (100 ms sampler) and the settled visible map. GATE: G is only eligible when its visible map equals E's on all 3 inputs (parity first). SWITCH G in for E when G's median wall is <= E's on the 50k corpus and its peak memory is <= E's + 25%. Otherwise keep E and record why. R is the floor, not a candidate for the shell (rule R2 does not apply to a long-lived host). If R beats G by more than 10x on the 50k corpus, open a follow-up to run batch replays (commute, schedule sweeps) on R + LIB-PAR-MAP instead. Results go in this item, numbers and all.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_3 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 3u32, "2026-10-23T13:53Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:53 x: component (3), daemon_memory.ex, split by the rule. PURE (drafted, queued for Cells after tibia's block): C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\tree_memory.spi, with memory_reading {samples; last_mb; procs; peak_mb}, sample_of (one 'procs mb' line), read_samples (an incremental fold, so a reader can pass just the new bytes), daemon_pid_of (the Cells daemon's 'spiral-cells<TAB>version<TAB>pid' hello) and 3 tests. ACTOR (Gleam, G1 + G3): a periodic timer (send_after interval) that reads new samples and answers peak calls; it finds the daemon pid by the TCP hello, retrying on a timer. SAMPLER (the measured part): today a pwsh child (Get-CimInstance loop, ~1 s per sample, a whole PowerShell per run). The R side of the rule says this is a native-library job (R3): one Rust primitive 'tree working set of pid' that walks the process table once per sample, which eoie already does (eoie_process_table, machine watch). By lib-first it should move to lib/spiral (process.spi has Gleam OS-process capture only) and be shared by eoie's machine watch and this actor, through the Gleam arm calling it as a NIF-free Rust CLI per sample or a long-lived Rust sampler emitting lines. Measure the pwsh sampler vs the Rust one (CPU per sample, sample period jitter) before switching, by the note-2 style rule.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_4 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 4u32, "2026-10-23T16:44Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:44 x: component (8) measured: the postability pass (build.ps1:42-51, one 'xc post-thread --dry-run' process per file) over 36 files / 1,644 posts. Wall 62.0 s, 45.4 s and 11.4 s in 3 runs (box loaded by two heavy builds, so very noisy). Per-file times don't follow size: frames.spi (0.7 KB, 6 posts) took 2.4 s, the same as glyphs.spi (6.6 KB, 49 posts), and 'xc --help' alone took 933 ms. So process start under contention dominates, not splitting. Consequence for the rule: a rayon arm inside the splitter (R1) can't pay here. The win is ONE process for all files: a batch form 'xc post-thread --dry-run FILE...' (or an export the build calls once) removes 35 of 36 process starts. Parallelism across files inside that one process comes second, and only if a quiet-box measurement then shows splitting time worth it. Re-measure both forms on a quiet box before choosing (rule from note 2: decide from medians, not loaded-box numbers).")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_5 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 5u32, "2026-10-23T16:49Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:49 x: the batch form from note 4 is drafted, so the quiet-box comparison has both arms: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_batch_dry_run.ps1 (applies after the thread-resume drafts). 'xc post-thread --dry-run FILE FILE...' becomes cli PreviewThreads (list), args kind 11, and xc run_preview_threads, which prints '=== <path>' before each file's preview and returns the worst exit code (1 for a missing file). Kind 11 is dispatched explicitly, because run_thread_kind's final else POSTS, so a missing branch would have turned a batch dry run into a real thread post. Parse test: 'post-thread --dry-run prelude.spi glyphs.spi'. NOT in land_all: per note 2's rule it lands only if the quiet-box measurement (36 single-file calls vs one batch call, 5 runs each, medians) shows a gain. Then build.ps1's postable loop becomes one call, split on '=== '.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_6 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 6u32, "2026-10-23T16:53Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:53 x: the quiet-box comparison is ready to run, pending a batch-capable xc. The sampler is C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\measure_postable.ps1 -Exe <xc.exe> (36 single-file calls vs one batch call, 5 rounds, alternating order, medians, free memory at the end). Record it with eoie, not ad hoc: 'eoie experiment register x/target/ledger X-POSTABLE-BATCH --test median --metric ms --group arm --arms single,batch --runs 5 --decision <switch to batch when its median is at least 30% below single's>', then analyze. The batch-capable binary comes cheaply after x-landing-trial: apply apply_batch_dry_run.ps1 to the trial copy and run an incremental 'cargo build --release' there.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_7 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 7u32, "2026-10-23T17:40Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:40 x: experiment pre-registered with eoie (no ad-hoc rule): X-POSTABLE-BATCH in x/target/ledger (welch on ms, arms single,batch, 5 runs, registered 17:39Z). Decision: batch lands when median(batch) <= 0.7 x median(single) and the 95% CI excludes 0; else keep single calls and close component 8. measure_postable.ps1 writes postable-batch.tsv (arm, round, ms) for 'eoie experiment analyze'.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_8 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 8u32, "2026-10-23T18:25Z", "x", "2026-10-23 14:25 x: component (3)'s pure core PASSES. Cells on x-world tree_memory.spi (after the formatter pass): 3 tests on rust + gleam (samples_fold_into_the_peak, readings_continue_across_reads, the_daemon_hello_names_its_pid). Left for (3): the Gleam actor (LIB-OTP-ACTORS) and the Rust tree sampler (the lib-first move of eoie's process-table walk).")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_9 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 9u32, "2026-10-23T21:34Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:34 x: component (3)'s Gleam actor is DRAFTED, not yet checked: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\memory_watch.spi. It is an otp_actor.new_with_self actor (main's 6525F5CC) with watch_message = Tick | Peak (subject memory_reading). A Tick folds one sample text through tree_memory.read_samples and re-arms itself with send_after every_ms; Peak answers a call with the reading. The sampler is injected as a 'sample : () -> string' thunk, because file_system.read_all_text has no Gleam arm (Zig/F#/Rust only), and so the actor stays neutral between note 3's sampler options (pwsh, Rust tree walk, port). Test: two Ticks over '3 120\\n5 340\\n', then a Peak call expects samples=4 last=340 procs=5 peak=340. Next: add it after tree_memory in the x-shell package and run Cells on gleam (needs a free slot). Lib gap for main: read_all_text Gleam arm (simplifile or file:read_file).")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_10 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 10u32, "2026-10-23T21:47Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:47 x: component (3)'s Gleam actor PASSES: memory_watch 1/1 on gleam (chain15, 17:45). Two Ticks fold through tree_memory and a Peak call answers samples=4 last=340 procs=5 peak=340. Left for (3): the real sampler (note 3's pwsh vs Rust tree-walk measurement) and a Gleam file or port source for the sample thunk.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_11 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 11u32, "2026-10-23T22:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:52 x: switch (2)'s G candidate gets a runnable entry, DRAFTED: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\xsim_shell_entry.spi (gleam; staged in the x-shell package). 'xsim-shell --replay STREAM [--max-concurrency N]' decodes an xsim-events stream (events.decoded_stream) and settles it through the OTP shell (runner_shell over scheduler.spi, fake executor). It prints 'XSHELL visible=N results=M'. Replay keeps note 2's inputs identical for E and G (Elixir: mix xsim.run --replay STREAM), which sidesteps the cells-parsing mismatch of SIM-IN-SPIRAL n40. Parity for G holds through the_otp_shell_settles_like_sync_run plus sync_run's Elixir parity (scheduler_parity). Tests: replay settles (visible=2), argument checks. main needs LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS (argv + read_all_text on Gleam) before it can run; the tests don't touch it. Still open for the measurement: a gleam run/escript launcher and the 100 ms tree-memory sampler (memory_watch can serve once it has a real sample source).")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_12 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 12u32, "2026-10-23T22:53Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:53 x: launcher skeleton for switch (2)'s G arm, staged (not run): C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\shell-launch\\ (gleam.toml at lib's pins: gleam_stdlib 1.0.5, gleam_erlang 1.3, gleam_otp 1.3). launch.ps1 -Stream FILE [-MaxConcurrency N] compiles xsim_shell_entry.spi with lib.ps1 BuildSpiral -Backend Gleam into src/xsim_shell.gleam (the pattern x/apps/sim/build.ps1:31-33 uses for frames), gleam builds it, then times one 'gleam run -m xsim_shell -- --replay FILE' from process start (BEAM boot included, as note 2's rule requires) and prints the XSHELL line + wall_ms. Run order once LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS lands: 1 run as a smoke test, then the note-2 protocol (5 runs per candidate per input, rotating, quiet box).")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_13 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 13u32, "2026-10-23T23:54Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:54 x: RULE for component (7), written before any measurement. Candidates: E = today's 'mix xsim.run' corpus run (build.ps1:112: the lib corpus, --frames --duplicates 0.15 --personas lurker=2+fickle=3+editor=2+breaker=2 --evict --breaking 1 --churn 2 --users 3 --edits 3); R1 = Spiral 'xsim run' (xsim_entry, Rust, schedules sequential); R2 = R1 with its schedule loop over lib par' (LIB-PAR-MAP, after next7). Input: the same corpus at --schedules 8 (enough schedules for parallelism to show), quiet box, 5 runs per candidate, rotating order, wall from process start, peak working set by a 100 ms tree sampler. PARITY GATE (first): per schedule, the XSIM summary lines must be equal except the peak_beam_mb and ms fields; the commute verdict lines too. This needs R1 to take cells exactly like Elixir (SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY's shared parser), so (7) is blocked on it, not on speed. SWITCH build.ps1's corpus run E -> R1 when R1's median wall <= E's median. ADOPT R2 over R1 when R2's median <= 0.7 x R1's median at 8 schedules with identical lines; otherwise keep R1 and record why. Results go here.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_14 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 14u32, "2026-10-23T23:55Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:55 x: notes 2 and 13's rules are now registered in eoie's ledger (C:\\home\\git\\x\\target\\ledger, 23:55Z, before any outcome): X-SHELL-SWITCH (welch on ms, arms elixir,gleam), X-CORPUS-R1 (elixir,r1) and X-CORPUS-R2 (r1,r2). eoie's median test is one-arm, so component (7)'s three-way comparison is two two-arm experiments. The decisions quote the notes' medians, thresholds and parity gates verbatim; analysis goes through 'eoie experiment analyze' on the measured TSVs.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_15 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 15u32, "2026-10-23T23:58Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:58 x: component (3)'s actor made incremental, ready for a real sample source (a sampler appending 'procs mb' lines to a file, read through LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS's Gleam read_all_text). memory_watch keeps consumed : i32 and folds only the text after it. A partial last line (the sampler mid-write: '5 34' would parse as 34 MB) waits for its newline; a shrunken text (a restarted sampler) is read again from the start. lib's sm'.last_index_of has no Gleam arm, so the tail is found with pieces '\\n'. Tests: two ticks over the same complete text fold once (samples=2); a partial last line is held back (samples=1). This replaces the 17:45 test that expected samples=4; that run passed with the old non-incremental fold. Queued after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_16 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 16u32, "2026-10-24T00:32Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:32 x: component (3) sample source drafted and measured (one data point, CPU-light box). C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\tree_sample.ps1 -Root <pid> prints memory_watch's 'procs mb' line for a process tree: one CIM snapshot, a breadth-first walk over parent ids with a seen-set (guards pid reuse loops), and '0 0' for a missing root. Results: my shell's tree '2 143', explorer's tree '61 4881', a missing pid '0 0'. Cost: one sample = 2,266 ms wall, almost all pwsh startup + Get-CimInstance. That's too slow for note 2's 100 ms sampler, and supports note 3's plan of a native tree walk (the lib-first move of eoie's process-table walk). Wiring to try: memory_watch's sample thunk = process.try_capture_combined_list 'pwsh' ['-NoProfile';'-File';'tree_sample.ps1';'-Root';pid] (Gleam arm drafted in LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS batch 5), appending to the text it folds.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_17 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 17u32, "2026-10-24T00:39Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:39 x: memory_watch's sample source is now a union (x-world draft; the x-shell copy is updated after chain20 so the running chain isn't disturbed): GrowingText (a file a sampler appends to, folded incrementally, partial last line held back) | FreshLine (a command printing one 'procs mb' line per tick, each line folded). tree_sampler script root = FreshLine over process.try_capture_combined_list pwsh tree_sample.ps1 -Root <pid> (Gleam arm from LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS batch 5; the inl is reached only once wired, so the live lib's missing arm can't break tests). New test: a fresh-line source counts every tick (samples=2). I caught a semantic slip while writing it: the incremental fold alone would read a fresh line per tick as 'nothing new'.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_18 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 18u32, "2026-10-24T03:13Z", "x", "23:13 x: component (3) lib-first now that LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS is live. memory_watch.complete_lines is sm'.substring 0 (sm'.last_index_of newline + 1), which closes n15's 'last_index_of has no Gleam arm' gap. grown's tail cut uses sm'.substring too; x prelude's pieces/cut/rest_from are gone from this file (X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE direction). Units checked at the compiler: Gleam StringLength is bit_array.byte_size, so length, substring and last_index_of all count bytes on both backends. The no-newline case falls out of -1 + 1 = 0. Copied over x-shell\\client (the n68 step); Cells check job-1792811606552-x-memory-watch-check runs after the regexp bisect.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_19 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 19u32, "2026-10-24T04:39Z", "x", "10-24 00:39 x: component (3) lib-first VERIFIED. memory_watch 3/3 on gleam (job-1792812757759, 00:37, 76 s) with complete_lines = sm'.substring 0 (sm'.last_index_of newline + 1) and the sm'.substring tail cut. The x-shell package compiles again after cells_executor's nominal moved to $'##(Bool, String)'.")
inl note_of_x_backend_map_20 () = Note ("X-BACKEND-MAP", 20u32, "2026-10-25T15:52Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:52 -04:00 x: component (3)'s native sampler, checked before drafting (search before 'new'). lib has no process-table code (grep sysinfo/Toolhelp over lib/spiral: 0). eoie has three process-table walks: eoie_process_table/host.spi (ancestor_pids and process_stopped, as multi-statement rust blocks of the old era), eoie_jobs and eoie_machine_watch/host.spi process_rows_sampled. The eoie team is reworking that sampler right now under EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH (n2: cores = cpu_usage/100 * cpus, one shared scan for ps, watch and health). Decision: x does NOT draft its own walk. The lib-first target is ONE lib module ('process_table': snapshot, row ids, parent, resident bytes as one-call sysinfo primitives, with the tree fold in Spiral) that eoie's watch and x's memory_watch both call. It is drafted after eoie's sampler fix lands, so the cpu attribution fix is not forked. Until then memory_watch keeps its injected FreshLine source (tree_sample.ps1, ~2.3 s per sample, n16), which is fine for the shell's minutes-long runs and not for a 100 ms sampler. Dep noted: EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH.")
inl eoie_backend_map () = Task ("EOIE-BACKEND-MAP", "eoie: map eoie's components to Gleam or Rust by BACKEND-BALANCE's rule. Its job host, machine windows and process watching are the strongest BEAM candidates in any repo; its CLI verbs stay Rust", Story, P1, D5, 15u32, Active, "", "eoie,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_backend_map () = Planning ("EOIE-BACKEND-MAP", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_backend_map_1 () = Note ("EOIE-BACKEND-MAP", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:01Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:01 x (filed at the user's request, 2026-10-23: 'tibia and eoie we haven't thought about what could be gained there'; the eoie team decides). Survey: 378 packages, all Rust (269 ///> rust targets, no gleam). First classification by the rule. RUST, keep: (1) every CLI verb (agile, do, read, grep, table, experiment, standards). Per-command use, measured 45-160 ms for 'agile status' vs a 1.1-2.6 s BEAM cold start (R2). (2) Scans and bulk work (grep/read search, style census, coverage, bundle zip/deflate, table query) stay Rust (R1, R3). Their rayon use moves from host-code blobs (eoie_do_read/host.spi:36, eoie_grep_query/search.spi:51, eoie_bundle_retention/retention_terminal.spi:7) to LIB-PAR-MAP, which also serves eoie's own style contract. GLEAM candidates: (3) the job host and its supervisors (eoie_jobs, EOIE-JOB-HOST), machine windows and slot arbitration (eoie_machine_windows), machine watch, process observation and streaming (eoie_machine_watch, eoie_process_observation, eoie_process_streaming). These meet G1, G2, G3, G4 and G5. OTP supervision trees answer tonight's failure modes directly: supervisors lost with their caller, nested job starts dying with an outer job (GE2), windows held by jobs still waiting on --after, restarts after a crash. Hot code reload (G5) would let a release install a new host without stopping running jobs, which today's releases avoid by waiting for or stopping jobs. The Rust CLI keeps talking to the host over its existing socket, so commands stay fast. KEEP: agile state as files (it must work with no service running). Next step: a measured prototype of the window/slot arbiter as a gleam_otp actor (LIB-OTP-ACTORS) behind the existing protocol, comparing admission latency, memory and crash recovery with today's host.")
inl note_of_eoie_backend_map_2 () = Note ("EOIE-BACKEND-MAP", 2u32, "2026-10-23T12:01Z", "eoie", "08:01 eoie team decision rule, written before any prototype. Baseline measured now (fixture windows home, installed binary, 5 runs): a waiting window request learns of a release by polling, release-to-grant 1853, 1791, 71, 1750, 1680 ms (median 1750, max 1853; the 71 ms run polled right after the release). The arbiter prototype (machine windows + heavy slots as one Gleam actor behind the existing CLI, per x's 07:01 survey) is adopted only if all of these hold on the same box, measured the same way: (1) release-to-grant median <= 100 ms and max <= 250 ms; (2) the actor's idle CPU <= 0.5% of one core over 10 min and resident memory <= 60 MB; (3) every existing windows_cli and run_cli notebook passes against it unchanged; (4) a killed actor is restarted by its supervisor and the window list it rebuilds equals the files on disk. Any miss keeps the Rust file arbiter, and its polling becomes a file-change notification instead (a smaller fix for the same latency). The job host stays Rust for now: it was just fixed in Rust (hotfix HP, live parent under the user shell), and BEAM's 1.1-2.6 s cold start would sit on every first job start of a session unless the actor is already warm.")
inl tibia_backend_map () = Task ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", "tibia: map tibia's components to Gleam or Rust by BACKEND-BALANCE's rule. The live arena's orchestration is a BEAM candidate; the simulator stays native (C oracle, WGSL GPU), with Rust + rayon for batch sweeps", Story, P1, D5, 75u32, Blocked, "LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", "tibia,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_backend_map () = Planning ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", Minutes 390u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:01Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:01 x (filed at the user's request, 2026-10-23: 'tibia and eoie we haven't thought about what could be gained there'; the tibia team decides and measures). Survey: 28 src modules plus 17 app files; targets today c 167, wgsl 28, lua 9, rust 2, zig 1 (C is the oracle, WGSL the GPU path, Lua the live OTClient entry). First classification by the rule. KEEP: (1) the rules and simulator modules on C, as the oracle and for speed, and gpu_sim on WGSL; (2) the Lua probes inside OTClient (the client's own language). RUST + LIB-PAR-MAP candidates: (3) batch work over independent seeds, policies or spots: hunt_sim / pack_sim sweeps, policy search, model-vs-live prediction tables (R1). A rayon fan-out over seeds on the Rust arm, measured against today's single-threaded C runs, could cut experiment wall time by about the core count. GLEAM candidates: (4) arena orchestration: start and supervise the game server, N OTClient clients with probes, and the competitor bots (vBot, NexBot), time out and restart them, and collect observer and scoreboard events as messages (G1, G2, G3, G4). A crash or hang of one client becomes a supervised restart instead of a lost block. (5) The OT Agent Arena as a public benchmark service that schedules runs across machines (G5, distribution). Measure first: how much live-block time goes to client hangs, restarts and manual cleanup today (the arena logs and PR items have the history), and the seeds/s of a batch sweep on C vs Rust + rayon.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:03Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:03 x: first measurement, from tibia (night of 10-22/23): 30 live runs with 0 crashes and 0 restarts. The only process losses were two low-memory reaps of waiter shells, and job-host jobs were unaffected. That weakens candidate (4) for now: supervision of clients (G3) pays only if hangs or restarts cost live-block time, and tonight they cost none. By the rule, (4) moves to Gleam only if a longer sample shows that cost, or if the benchmark-service need (5, G5 distribution) becomes real. (3), Rust + rayon for batch sweeps, is unaffected and is now the stronger first experiment.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:05Z", "tibia-lane", "tibia-lane 07:1x: the arena orchestration is in the retirement scope (TIBIA note 41), judged by dogfood value rather than by a measured hang rate. Inventory to map: live/*.ps1 (about 530 lines in 10 scripts; run-policy-scenario.ps1 188, run-arena.ps1 87, run-offscreen.ps1 67), the night-block chaining logic (scratch night-block.ps1), the crash-rerun rule from the pre-registrations, the server-probe lifecycle (-ServerProbe), and eoie window requests. The baseline measurement x suggested (hang and restart time per live block) still runs first, as the before number.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 4u32, "2026-10-23T11:11Z", "tibia-lane", "Retirement grooming 07:1x: in scope as filed, about 4 h: the hang and restart baseline over the next live blocks plus the classification. Implementing what the map chooses (rayon sweeps, an OTP arena) is sized by that classification and is not in this estimate.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 5u32, "2026-10-23T11:21Z", "tibia-lane", "Classification by BACKEND-BALANCE's rule (note 1), with before-numbers. tibia-lane 07:3x.\n(1) src rules and simulators (geometry, combat, hunting_pack, pack_sim, and the rest): KEEP. They are the C oracle, the WGSL GPU twins and the Lua live target, which the rule keeps (\"GPU work stays WGSL and C stays where the matrix needs it\").\n(2) apps/arena probes: KEEP Lua (they run inside OTClient's Lua host).\n(3) apps/map: KEEP Rust (R1 and R3: a one-shot parse of canary.otbm and appearances.dat).\n(4) apps/predict sweeps and the AGENT trainers (network.evolve, evolve_cem over population x training seeds; held-out scoring over 100 seeds): RUST plus rayon by R1, CPU-bound over independent seeds, through LIB-PAR-MAP. Before-number: single-threaded C; the M1 network.evolve arm took about 43 min (job tibia-agent-m1-trace-evolve, 05:11-05:53, beside main's heavy jobs) and about 67 min under heavier load (run 4); held-out scoring of one tactic takes about 50 s (job tibia-agent-clock: 4 tactics in 206 s with compile). The C build stays as the oracle for the same code (Cells agreement c = rust).\n(5) live/ orchestration (about 530 lines of PowerShell: run-policy-scenario, run-arena, run-offscreen, block, world): GLEAM by G1 (a block lives 1-3 h and holds state: window, server, DB, probe, results), G3 (it starts, times out and restarts OS children: canary.exe, mariadbd, OTClient on an off-screen desktop) and G2 (the duo runs two clients beside the server). Per x's note 2 (BEAM cold start 1-2.6 s), it would be a long-lived arena host that the CLI (block.ps1 or eoie) talks to, not a per-command tool. Before-numbers from the night block (results/*-leadtrace.tsv and *-pr23b.tsv, row-to-row minus time in game): orchestration overhead per run about 43 s on the fixed 300 s hunting-ground hunts (12 percent), about 61 s on cyclops arena runs, rough on hunter runs. 0 crashes, 0 hangs and 0 restarts in 30 runs; 2 waiter shells were reaped under low memory, outside the arena. The block's largest cost was about 4 h queued for a live-gui window behind heavy builds, which is eoie admission, not the arena. So the gain to measure after a switch is per-run overhead (a warm host instead of a cold restart per run) and the crash-rerun rule built in, not hang time.\n(6) Analysis readers (scratch read-trace.ps1, p5-eval.ps1): RUST via eoie verbs (R2; TOOLS-TO-EOIE, EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES).\nNext, as children of this item: (4) waits on LIB-PAR-MAP and is the bigger win for laptop time; (5) waits on LIB-OTP-ACTORS. Neither switch lands without its after-measurement.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 6u32, "2026-10-23T11:50Z", "tibia-lane", "Arena host design (07:5x, for (5) of note 5, on LIB-OTP-ACTORS primitives as main read them: gleam_erlang 1.3.0 and gleam_otp 1.3.0). Each responsibility of live/*.ps1, mapped:\n(1) arena_host, a static_supervisor (RestForOne): world first, then block. A world crash restarts the block; a block crash leaves the world up.\n(2) world, an actor that owns mariadbd and canary.exe as OS ports (today world.ps1 and run-policy-scenario.ps1 start them and stop canary by process name; here the port's own pid is the handle, which ends kill-by-name). Its messages are a typed union: Start, ResetCharacter (the DB reset SQL of run-policy-scenario), InstallProbe path, RemoveProbe, Restart (canary restarts per run today; whether a warm canary survives a DB reset is the after-measurement), Health.\n(3) offscreen_launch stays a Rust CLI by R3 (Win32 CreateDesktop and CreateProcess, today in run-offscreen.ps1), started by the run actor as a port and exited by it. It is the native boundary the rule names.\n(4) run, an actor started per run with restart Transient and restart_tolerance intensity 1. That is the pre-registered crash rule (\"a crash is rerun once, then counted missing\") as supervisor policy instead of a manual checklist step. It holds the timeout as a send_after timer value (today -TimeoutSeconds plus wall-clock caps), reads the client log, writes the results row, and keeps the probe trace (today the -ServerProbe finally block).\n(5) block, an actor that asks eoie for the live-gui window (eoie stays the Rust CLI by R2, called as a port) and sequences runs and stages (today block.ps1 and the hand-written night-block chaining), releasing the window on its own termination via a monitor, the guarantee PowerShell's finally gave only within one script.\n(6) The probe Lua (apps/arena) and the server probes stay as they are; the host only installs and collects them.\nTyped protocols as GADTs of request to reply per actor, once LIB-OTP-ACTORS lands its reply_to handle. The deterministic arm (actors as a step loop) gives Cells tests without BEAM: a run that times out is retried once and then counted missing; a world restart re-resets the character; a block that ends releases its window.\nBefore-numbers to beat (note 5): about 43 s of overhead per hunting-ground run, 0 hangs in 30 runs. After-numbers to measure: overhead per run with a warm world, and crash-rerun latency.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 7u32, "2026-10-23T12:17Z", "tibia-lane", "First host code LANDED 08:2x: apps/host (new package; packageDir ../../deps/spiral/lib with |core- and spiral-, no sibling entry, so it mounts in Cells without step 11). block_plan.spi is the deterministic core of the run and block actors of note 6. It types run_outcome (Completed or Crashed, read from the experience field as run-arena writes it), run_fate (Counted, Rerun or Missing; fate_of is the pre-registered crash rule), progress over stages, and stage_step (InstallProbe, Runs, RemoveProbe, KeepTrace) with probed_stage, plain_stage, runs_of and probe_left_installed. Gate green from the tree (job tibia-host-gate2): scripts/build.ps1 -Files block_plan, 4 tests, 12 of 12 cells on c, lua and gleam; standards 0 findings. Tibia's first Spiral code tested on the Gleam backend. scripts/build.ps1 now gates apps/host (formatter check and Cells source list). Next in scratch: character.spi (level-derived stats, the reset statement and the canonical kit, each byte-identical to run-policy-scenario.ps1 lines 50-56, plus the duo config edit as a typed result), then the monster mix and summon orders (lines 30-34).")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 8u32, "2026-10-23T12:28Z", "tibia-lane", "08:3x. (1) apps/host/character.spi LANDED, gate green (job tibia-host-gate3: 4 tests, 11 of 11 cells). It holds the level-derived stats; the reset statement and the canonical kit, both byte-identical to run-policy-scenario.ps1 lines 55-56 (the kit's 609 characters compared to the script programmatically); and the duo config edit as a typed result (Raised text or LimitsMissing) instead of a throw. The duo test targets lua and gleam because sm'.replace has no C arm (known, CONVO-IN-SPIRAL note 29). (2) Row extraction stays in eoie: eoie table logs with Write-ArenaRow's patterns (--last experience, cleared_ms, lowest_health, actions_allowed, actions_dropped; --count potions and deaths; --sum gold) reproduces all 30 rows of last night's three result tables, 0 of 240 fields different. So the run actor writes rows by calling eoie (R2, the existing generic verb) instead of a Spiral regex port; Get-ForeignActivity's two columns are the only part not yet covered. (3) The comparison caught a false record of mine: PC-8's RESULT row said every run cleared, but the hunter runs cleared 7 of 10 (three hit the 200 s cap). Corrected in docs/BENCHMARK.md. (4) scenario.spi (monster mix, summon orders, BadOrder) is in Cells on lua and gleam. sm'.contains has no C arm, filed as LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 9u32, "2026-10-23T12:40Z", "tibia-lane", "08:4x. apps/host now has 4 modules, all derived from live/ PowerShell and checked against it. (1) scenario.spi LANDED, gate green (job tibia-host-gate4: 2 tests, 4 of 4 cells on lua and gleam): the monster mix (one kind with a count, or 'troll:2, cyclops:1'), summon orders byte-identical to run-policy-scenario.ps1 line 34, total count, and BadOrder naming the part when a count is not a number (the script throws mid-run). (2) Rows: eoie table logs plus arithmetic now reproduces every Write-ArenaRow column, the two Get-ForeignActivity columns included (0 of 30 rows different; three --count and four --last patterns). The arithmetic is apps/host/activity.spi (foreign_potions, own_actions, foreign_actions as an option, since PowerShell's null meant no allowed/dropped line); 3 of 3 cells on c, lua and gleam, LANDED, gate green (job tibia-host-gate5: 1 test, 3 of 3 cells). (3) So the run actor's logic exists as typed, tested Spiral for the crash rule, the stage plan, the character reset, the kit, the duo config, the mix, the orders and the row arithmetic; only process control (canary, mariadb, OTClient off-screen, eoie window calls) waits for LIB-OTP-ACTORS and the Rust offscreen_launch helper.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 10u32, "2026-10-23T13:47Z", "tibia-lane", "09:4x. Two more pieces of the arena host LANDED, gate green (job tibia-host-gate6: formatter clean, arena_host 4 of 4 tests, offscreen 3 of 3; eoie standards 0 findings). (1) apps/host/arena_host.spi: the host deterministic step loop of note 6, step : host -> host_event -> host * list host_command over block_plan crash rule. Four event-list traces as tests on lua and gleam: a clean block resets the character before every run and releases its window; a timed-out run is stopped, rerun once, then counted missing; a world crash restarts the world and resets the character before the rerun; a lost window ends the block and later events change nothing. (2) apps/offscreen: note 6 item 3, the off-screen launcher as a Spiral-to-Rust CLI (std only, plain rustc like apps/map; build.ps1 -Check builds target/offscreen/offscreen.exe and checks it). It replaces run-offscreen.ps1 inline C#: flags as a typed parse result (MissingExe, MissingValue, BadTimeout, UnknownFlag), run-offscreen.ps1 report lines byte for byte, and each Win32 call (CreateDesktopW, CreateProcessW, WaitForSingleObject, TerminateProcess, GetExitCodeProcess, CloseHandle) as its own small primitive. Native check: cmd.exe exit 7 reported, ping killed at 2 s with exit 124 and both lines, missing --exe exits 2. Not wired into live/ yet: run-offscreen.ps1 moves onto it after the block, with one probe run as the check (draft in scratch). Tibia requirements for the executor are posted on LIB-OTP-ACTORS (note 10). Left on this item: the Gleam executor (waits on LIB-OTP-ACTORS), the predict/trainer move to Rust (waits on LIB-PAR-MAP), and the after-measurements of note 5.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 11u32, "2026-10-23T15:28Z", "tibia-lane", "11:3x. live/run-offscreen.ps1 now runs on the Rust launcher (target/offscreen/offscreen.exe, built by apps/offscreen/build.ps1 if missing): same parameters and output lines, and the inline C# is gone. Checked after the block, without a live window: cmd.exe exit 5 reported; ping killed at 2 s with exit 124 and both lines; default desktop tibia-probe; -WorkingDirectory passed through; and the duo ally argument shape --user-dir=\"<path with a space>\" reaches the child intact, so the command line matches the old string-built one. The first real OTClient run on it is PC-10's first run (next live window); a failure there is a crash for the block's crash rule, and I restore the old script (scratch copy offscreen/run-offscreen.before.ps1) before rerunning.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 12u32, "2026-10-23T15:34Z", "tibia-lane", "11:4x. apps/host/arena_host.spi gained the deterministic executor of note 6, LANDED, gate green (job tibia-host-gate7: 6 of 6 tests on lua and gleam, formatter clean). A scripted world (Finishes outcome, TimesOut, CrashesWorld) answers each host command (StartWorld and RestartWorld give WorldReady, StartRun takes the next scripted reaction, and an empty script gives WindowLost), and simulated runs the host loop until no event is left, so a whole block runs closed-loop with no BEAM. Tests: a 3-run block with a timeout and a world crash gives the exact command trace expected by hand, and a world that stops answering ends the block. This is the deterministic arm LIB-OTP-ACTORS asks for (its note 10): the same step function, with the executor as the only per-backend part.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_13 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 13u32, "2026-10-23T20:38Z", "tibia-lane", "Blocker cleared 16:3x: main landed LIB-OTP-ACTORS' first modules in spiral lib (erlang_process, otp_actor, otp_supervisor, step_runtime, with process.spi and platform.spi on gleam_erlang 1.x; Cells pins gleam_erlang 1.3, gleam_otp 1.3, stdlib 1.0.5). The arena host's Gleam executor (note 6, requirements on LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 10) can now be built on them in the next session: the step function of apps/host/arena_host.spi as the actor's handle, its scripted executor as the deterministic arm (step_runtime), and an otp_actor executor that owns canary/mariadbd ports and target/offscreen/offscreen.exe runs. par' (LIB-PAR-MAP) is not landed yet: it needs a compiler pack. Also landed: sm'.spi C arms (LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM), so apps/host/scenario.spi's tests can add the c target.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_14 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 14u32, "2026-10-23T21:15Z", "tibia-lane", "17:1x: apps/host/arena_host.spi gains host_actor and host_trace: the arena host's step function as a step_runtime.actor (state: the run plan as a list, the host built inside the actor on its first message, and the trace; messages Event or Trace r, read back with call), the first consumer of main's LIB-OTP-ACTORS. One test runs the same event list through a real gleam_otp actor (step_runtime.otp_start, otp_actor.send and call) on gleam and through spawn, send and call on lua, and both give the identical command trace; Cells 7 of 7 on lua and gleam (job tibia-host-actor2), landing gate job tibia-host-gate8. The list plan works around Gleam arrays dying at the process boundary (LIB-OTP-ACTORS note 12); main landed otp_actor.new_with_init at 17:1x, so next session the plan can stay an array with the thunk form. Next for the executor: the OS-port side (canary and mariadbd ports, offscreen.exe runs) on erlang_process. Gate: tibia-host-gate8 failed only the actor test on gleam (callee did not send reply before timeout, the 1000 ms call, under load after main's 17:14 otp_actor landing); the rerun tibia-host-gate9 is 7 of 7 green with no memo. So the 1000 ms call can time out on a loaded laptop: next session either raises it or moves to new_with_init, so a CI run under load does not flake.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_15 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 15u32, "2026-10-23T22:03Z", "tibia-lane", "Dependency 18:1x: the executor's OS-port side (offscreen.exe per run, canary and mariadbd per block) needs a lib runtime-process Gleam arm (spawn_executable port with exit status), which KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR already plans as a lib gap (its 10-16 spike used a 30-line Erlang FFI); tibia's needs are recorded there as a second consumer. The pure host loop, its deterministic executor and the actor wrapper are landed; only the port arm waits. CORRECTED 2026-10-23 23:4x: the gap is smaller than written. lib/spiral/process.spi (main, landed 10-22) already has capture_status_within program args ms on rust and gleam: a spawn_executable port with exit_status, a deadline, and a tree kill (taskkill /T) past it, returning the exit code or -1. Run in a worker process (erlang_process.spawn) that sends the result to the host actor, it covers the per-run offscreen.exe call (its paths have no spaces, and offscreen.exe takes --directory, so the space-split args and the missing cwd option do not matter). What lib lacks is only a port handle for the per-run canary.exe and the block's mariadbd: open, await exit, and kill on demand from another process (the prelude has spiral_process_open_with and spiral_process_kill_tree, but no public start/stop pair).")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_16 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 16u32, "2026-10-24T03:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-23 23:30 Night item 4 of the 10-23 report: host_trace's gleam arm now calls otp_actor.call with 10000 ms instead of 1000 (apps/host/arena_host.spi 255), so a loaded CI laptop does not flake the actor test (gate8's one timeout); job tibia-night-full-build has arena_host 7 of 7 green on lua and gleam with it, on main's 22:27 compiler. The move to otp_actor.new_with_init is not made: the host is already built inside the actor on its first message (runs_of_plan), so the array never crosses the boundary, and new_with_init would only help if step_runtime.actor carried an init thunk (init : () -> s), a lib change to step_runtime and otp_start that every step_runtime user shares. Passed to main as information (step_runtime.otp_start evaluates spec.init in the caller, so any array state dies at the boundary for every user). BACKEND-MAP's in-scope part is the classification plus the landed host core, executor, actor wrapper and launcher; only the OS-port arm remains, waiting on KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR's runtime-process arm (note 15). UPDATE 23:4x: main took the finding as its own gap (otp_start never switched to new_with_init): its lib-step-init change gives step_runtime.actor an init thunk (init : () -> s), otp_start on otp_actor.new_with_init 10000, and every lib call timeout 10000 ms; gate job-1792812964392-main-lib-step-init-gate, land job-1792812965354-main-lib-step-init-land, which also flips arena_host.spi's init line to the thunk in the same land so the tree stays green. After it lands, tibia drops the workaround with scratch host-init-patch.ps1 (dry-run clean against main's flipped line): state host * string, the host opened by the init thunk inside the actor, then the arena_host gate.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_17 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 17u32, "2026-10-24T05:35Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:35 tibia: the dependency moved from the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR epic to LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE, the one primitive note 15 names (start with a log, OS pid, await exit within a deadline, tree kill by pid). Its lab is green on rust and gleam, and its lib gate is queued after PR-27's live block (LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE note 2). Once it lands, the remaining OS-port arm is the block's world owner on BEAM: start mariadbd and canary with logs, await the world line, and kill both trees at block end or on a crash.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_18 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 18u32, "2026-10-24T08:59Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:59 tibia: the consumer is proven. world_port's 2 gleam tests pass against the healed process module (job tibia-world-port-cells, on a %TEMP% copy of apps/host with a lib copy holding the healed process.spi after file_system): a fake python server reports WorldReady, then WorldCrashed after world_stopped; and the owner restarts and releases its server. A plain compile of world_port as the entry exits 0 but checks nothing, because a module without main compiles the package's main (memory gotcha updated), so the Cells run is the real check. The land plan is one replace, not a delete then re-add: lib process.spi becomes the healed module, moved after file_system (scratch process-replace-apply.ps1, proven on a copy, kit gate tibia-lib-process-replace-gate on its 5 tests), and in the same session world_port.spi joins tibia apps/host (package.spiproj module list) as its first caller, so rule (4) holds for every new definition. The 6 uncalled definitions disappear in the replace.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_19 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 19u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 07:19 tibia: grooming re-size. The 240 min estimate covered the classification plus the host core, executor, actor wrapper and launcher, all done. The OS-port arm entered scope when LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's handle and the world_port owner were proven (note 18): joint land about 30 min, executor wiring (arena_host's StartWorld, RestartWorld and ReleaseWindow driving world_obeyed, events from world_event_within into the host actor) about 120 min. Estimate 390, progress 60 (from 90), still Blocked on LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's multi-file land.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_20 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 20u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 19:44 host-init done in the tree. apps/host/arena_host.spi (modified 09:43) has init = fun () => plan, None, '', matching lib step_runtime.spi line 11 'type actor s m = { init : () -> s; ... }' (lib modified 17:18). The note 16 pending patch is applied. Still unproven: no arena_host build since then, so the next host compile in a free window confirms it.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_21 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 21u32, "2026-10-25T14:50Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 10:50 host-init is proven, so note 20's open point is closed: target/ci-metrics/cells-arena_host/tests.tsv (written 2026-10-24 23:43, after arena_host.spi's last change at 23:31) has all 7 arena_host tests passed on lua and gleam, including the_host_step_runs_inside_an_actor_and_gives_the_same_trace_on_every_backend and a_scripted_world_drives_a_whole_block_through_the_executor. Still blocked only on LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE, whose anchored lib slice is in kit gate job-1792938470349; after it lands, the joint land wires the executor's world port to process.start_logging and await_exit_within.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_22 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 22u32, "2026-10-25T15:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:00 joint land queued behind the lib gate: job-1792940329591 (process-handle-serial.ps1) runs the kit land of LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE-SPLIT, then world-port-land.ps1, which adds apps/host/world_port.spi (the note 18 owner respelled to edition 3 with format --spelling symbols; database_answers dropped since nothing calls it) after arena_host in the host package and runs Cells world_port and arena_host with memo off, restoring both on red. Left after it: the executor wiring (StartWorld, RestartWorld and ReleaseWindow through world_obeyed, world_event_within feeding the host actor), about 120 min.")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_23 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 23u32, "2026-10-25T15:02Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:02 Executor wiring design, written before drafting; it applies once world_port is in the tree (job-1792940329591). New apps/host/world_executor.spi after world_port, with the logic in Spiral over lib process and file_system. (1) executor record: world programs (world_port.world_programs), a database admin program plus args for running SQL, the run program (the offscreen client) plus its directory, run deadline, world-start deadline and block log path. (2) executed x current command : option world * list host_event. StartWorld and RestartWorld go through world_port.world_obeyed, then world_event_within is polled in 50 ms steps until WorldReady or WorldCrashed or the start deadline (deadline passed = WorldCrashed, which the host answers with RestartWorld). ReleaseWindow goes through world_obeyed with no event. ResetCharacter runs character.reset_sql through the database program with process.capture_status_within: exit 0 = no event, otherwise WorldCrashed. StartRun (stage, run) runs the client with capture_status_within under the run deadline: -1 = RunTimedOut, exit 0 = RunEnded (block_plan.outcome_of_experience of the experience line read from the run log), other codes = RunEnded Crashed. StopRun does nothing, because capture_status_within already killed the run tree at its deadline. Record fate appends fate_text to the block log. (3) driven h pending current: the simulated loop with executed in place of reacted, so the trace text and the host step stay the ones the 7 arena_host tests pin. (4) Acceptance: one gleam test over fake programs (python world printing the online line, a python run printing an experience line, a python database exiting 0) whose trace equals simulated's for the same reactions; a second where the fake run sleeps past its deadline and the trace shows stop, rerun. Lua is out: the executor runs on BEAM (BACKEND-BALANCE: supervision and ports).")
inl note_of_tibia_backend_map_24 () = Note ("TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP", 24u32, "2026-10-25T15:07Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:07 Executor drafted to note 23's design: scratch world-port-draft/world_executor.spi (134 lines, edition 3): executed maps each host command to its program (world_obeyed plus a 50 ms start poll, reset_sql through the database program, the client run under capture_status_within, Record appending fate_text to the block log); driven mirrors simulated's FIFO queue; two gleam tests over python fakes (a 3-run block whose trace equals simulated's and whose block log reads counted x3; an always-sleeping run giving stop, rerun, stop, missing per stage). It lands as step 3 of job-1792940329591 (world-executor-land.ps1, Cells world_executor and world_port, restore on red).")
inl x_generic_to_lib () = Task ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", "x: move x's generic (non-X) code into lib/spiral, so other projects reuse it; x keeps only X-specific domain", Story, P1, D4, 55u32, Active, "X-DONE", "x,lib,cleanup", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_generic_to_lib () = Planning ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_1 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:07Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:07 x: user direction 2026-10-23 07:0x: 'all projects should also reuse as much lib as possible, also moving generic code to there'. Candidates found in x (a quick lib grep found no equivalents; confirm each before moving): (1) oauth1.spi: RFC 3986 percent-encoding, the OAuth 1.0a signature base string and HMAC-SHA1 header. Generic HTTP auth, no lib equivalent. (2) glyphs.spi: grapheme segmentation and NFC on rust + gleam. Generic Unicode, while X's weight table stays in x. (3) paging.spi: the cursor walk with repeat and budget guards (X-PAGING-GUARD's walker), a generic paginated fetch. (4) X-LOOKUP-LIMITS' chunked / after_first / all_got: generic list and result helpers. (5) events.spi's JSON string unescape: the lib needs a full decoder (X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE note 3). (6) prelude's list/result helpers, already covered by X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE. Rule per move: the lib version lands first with lib test blocks on every backend x declares (rust, gleam, zig where used), and the lib corpus check runs (memory lib-smoke-before-syntax); then x switches to it in one change and deletes its copy. Lib edits are main's domain, so coordinate each landing with main and never edit lib while a Cells job reads it (memory cells-reads-live-tree).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_2 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 2u32, "2026-10-23T12:55Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:55 x: text primitives that are generic and belong in lib/spiral (main's domain; x keeps local copies until then). x's glyphs.spi has two-armed (Rust + Gleam) one-expression versions of char_of, graphemes (first_grapheme), plain code-point weight, nfc and blank, and the X-EMOJI-WEIGHT draft adds first_code_point and code_point_count. lib/spiral/xml.spi already has code_point_at, code_point_width, code_point_count and code_point_string, but Rust only (the other arms failwith) and inside the xml module. Suggested shape: one lib text module (graphemes, code points, nfc) with Rust + Gleam arms each. xml.spi would call it, and x's glyphs would drop its copies. Names already clash: x's draft and lib xml both define code_point_count, harmless only while x never opens xml.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_3 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 3u32, "2026-10-23T13:31Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:31 x: concrete draft for main: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\unicode.spi (next to dictm.spi, same scratch package). It has char_of, first_grapheme_bytes, graphemes, first_code_point, code_point_count and nfc: each a one-expression primitive with Rust (unicode-segmentation / unicode-normalization / chars) and Gleam (gleam/string, erlang unicode) arms, and no host splice calls a Spiral closure (LIB-DICTM note 5's rule). 3 tests: graphemes_hold_clusters, code_points_count_scalars_not_bytes, nfc_composes. Not run yet; queued for after tibia's live block. Once it lands in lib/spiral, x's glyphs.spi keeps only the X-specific parts (weights, emoji_entity, defused dots, blank/trim helpers), and xml.spi's Rust-only code_point_* can call it.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_4 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 4u32, "2026-10-23T14:03Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:03 x: SUPERSEDED: this duplicated X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE notes 1 and 5 (2026-10-22 23:20 and 2026-10-23 00:31), which own the prelude-to-lib audit, including the split_on bug and the gaps (bind, take, split_list). Their call is the more careful one on spread: keep it until listm'.collect stops being quadratic, rather than swapping it directly as this note first said. What is new here, on top of them: a 4th gap, the strict decimal parse 'text -> option i32' (count_of_text; convert.parsed_integer and convert.i32 don't fit). See note 5 for the code.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_5 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 5u32, "2026-10-23T14:04Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:04 x: code for X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE's lib additions (that item owns the plan): C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\lib_gaps.spi, pure Spiral, queued for Cells rust + gleam after tibia's block. take -> listm'.take; bind -> resultm.bind; split_list -> sm'.split_list (whole separator width, which fixes the split_on bug recorded there); decimal_i32 -> convert.decimal_i32 (digits only, exactly int32-bounded). 4 tests.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_6 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 6u32, "2026-10-23T21:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:57 main: x handed off the draft unicode.spi (char_of, graphemes, first_code_point, code_point_count, nfc; 3/3 PASS) in C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft for main to land. Landing rule: Cells gate on the tree-lib baseline subset rule, after the tonight chain; rebase over eoie's 17:56 lib-gaps land (sm', am', process, testing, env changed) first, since x's lib_gaps.spi also touches listm'/sm'.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_7 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 7u32, "2026-10-23T22:12Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:12 x: step drafted, client OS boundary through lib: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_os_boundary_lib.ps1. It applies AFTER land_all's fit step, anchored on post-fit text, because args.spi and xc.spi are hash-guarded by the fit. args: process_args uses env.command_line_argument / command_line_argument_count (drops arg_count/arg_at, no other users). xc: write_line -> console.write_line, write_error -> console.write_error_line (same println!/eprintln! arms), file_text -> file_system.read_all_text (callers check is_file first, so the panic-on-missing arm is fine), is_file -> file_system.file_exists (exists && is_file, the same as Path::is_file). Dry-applied to post-fit copies: every anchor found once, 0 std/println splices left in args+xc, touched cells weigh 241 and 71 (XC005 budget ~270). Stays as is: token.spi's read/write, which must be total (X-TOKEN-REFRESH-SAFE); lib's are panicking, a gap already in EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY n17. Also the xc_* cross-crate calls, which are x's own glue. Compile check (args + xc on a fit copy) queued for after tibia's live block; it joins land_all only after passing.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_8 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 8u32, "2026-10-23T22:39Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:39 main: the landing draft of x's unicode.spi is %TEMP%\\lib-unicode\\lib\\spiral\\unicode.spi, with apply.ps1 (new module, listed last). Changes from x's draft: lib layout (/// # unicode, test unicode static, /// ### headings), and the x-world package line dropped. graphemes is now one arm per backend, linear: Rust UnicodeSegmentation::graphemes(..).rev().fold with Cons prepends; Gleam list.fold(list.reverse(string.to_graphemes(s))). x's version re-sliced the string once per grapheme, quadratic on Rust, and also needed first_grapheme_bytes, which is gone. Each backend arm sits inline in its backend_switch instead of rust_/gleam_ wrapper functions, and test helpers are prefixed unicode_. Test headers follow the lib convention ('///> rust -d unicode-segmentation', no versions, as in crypto.spi); the graphemes test also checks that the first cluster is 'a' (core listm has no head, so a local match). Gate: lib-gate-kit -Name LIB-UNICODE -Files unicode.spi, chain A in requeue-after-block.ps1 for tibia's 'block end'.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_9 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 9u32, "2026-10-23T22:48Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:48 x: candidate (1) DRAFTED for main: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\oauth1.spi, a lib-layout module (/// # oauth1, test oauth1 static, /// ### headings, arms inline in backend_switch, as main restyled unicode). Lib grep (positive control: crypto.hash_text found) shows no percent-encoder, HMAC, SHA-1 or base64 in lib. Primitives with Rust + Gleam arms: percent_encode (RFC 3986 unreserved), hmac_sha1_base64 and sha1_hex. Gleam uses OTP crypto:mac/hash + bit_array base64/base16, so no new hex dependency. Pure Spiral parts: parameter_string (sorted by comp/lt_is like am'), signature_base, signing_key, protocol_params, signature and authorization_header. Nonce and timestamp are parameters, so the module is pure and x keeps its clock/nonce source. Tests on rust + gleam: unreserved encoding (with UTF-8 é), Twitter's published signing example to its documented signature hCtSmYh+iHYCEqBWrE7C7hYmtUk=, and sha1('abc'). Both expected values were recomputed independently with .NET HMACSHA1/SHA1 at 19:0x. Listed in lib-draft, queued for after tibia's block. Once landed, x's oauth1.spi keeps only clock_seed/unix_seconds/fresh_nonce and calls lib's authorization_header (an apply draft follows the landing).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_10 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 10u32, "2026-10-23T22:49Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:49 x: candidate (3) DRAFTED for main: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\cursor_walk.spi (lib layout). It's the generic cursor-paginated fetch from x's paging.spi as it stands after X-PAGING-GUARD: cursor_walker e = {halted : cursor_halt -> e; budget; fetch : string -> result cursor_page e}, walked_from/all_pages, and it stops on a repeated next_token (CursorRepeated) or at the page budget (CursorBudget). It's one let rec with explicit matches, so it doesn't depend on resultm.bind from the unlanded lib_gaps draft; listm'.exists' does the repeat check. The 2 tests carry over x's 4 walker cases on rust + gleam (follow tokens, failed page, repeated cursor, budget). x keeps whole_second (X's search timestamp rule) and its 500-page budget; paging.spi then wraps lib's walker (an apply draft follows the landing). Queued after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_11 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 11u32, "2026-10-23T22:50Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:50 x: candidate (4) DRAFTED for main: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\list_more.spi. These are X-LOOKUP-LIMITS' generic helpers, meant to land as listm'.skip_ (x's after_first), listm'.chunks_ (x's chunked; n <= 0 yields one chunk instead of looping forever) and resultm.collect_list (x's all_got: Ok list in order, or the first Error). The names were renamed at 19:1x to main's rule that runtime let rec list loops take a trailing underscore. Lib grep (positive control: listm'.try_find_ found) shows only am' array versions (am'.skip, am'.vec_chunks), nothing for lists. Kept separate from lib_gaps.spi because main is rebasing that draft over eoie's 17:56 lib-gaps landing. 2 tests on rust + gleam. Queued after tibia's block. Once landed, wire.spi's three helpers are deleted and call sites renamed (an apply draft follows the landing).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_12 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 12u32, "2026-10-23T23:19Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:19 main: unicode draft revised for one record per fact. lib/spiral/xml.spi already has Rust-only code_point_count and code_point_string (plus code_point_at, code_point_width and code_point_text, which xpath_eval, xsd_regex, json_schema and xsd_emit use). unicode is now listed right before xml in the lib modules, and xml.code_point_count and code_point_string delegate to unicode.code_point_count and unicode.char_of (conv). Their names stay for the callers, Rust behaviour is identical (from_u32 -> '' when invalid), and they gain Gleam arms. The gate covers unicode, xml, xpath_eval and xsd_regex.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_13 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 13u32, "2026-10-23T23:20Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:20 x: per main's 19:1x placement review, the oauth1/cursor_walk/list_more drafts are replaced by ONE landing patch: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\landing\\apply_x_lib_drafts.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. It adds crypto.sha1_hex/hmac_sha1_base64 (generic crypto in its existing home), sm'.percent_encode (beside encode_uri_component), and listm'.skip_/chunks_ APPENDED at the end of listm'.spi after main's take_ (Spiral resolves top-down; main's LIB-GAPS-X land rewrites collect and appends take_, so it lands first, and a precondition refuses with no writes until take_ exists). Also resultm.collect_list (anchored with its inl line, since unwrap_err is a prefix of unwrap_err'), and new modules cursor_walk + oauth1 (oauth1 calls crypto.* and sm'.percent_encode). Lines are at most 120 with \\ continuations. Proof copy %TEMP%\\x-libcopy2 built in tree order: mine alone refused (crypto.spi hash unchanged), then main's %TEMP%\\lib-gaps-x\\apply.ps1 -Apply, then mine cleanly (listm' take_ 766 < skip_ 791 < chunks_ 797). Cells after tibia's block: x-libcopy2\\check\\x_lib_check.spi + oauth1.spi + cursor_walk.spi in the copy. Loose drafts lib-draft\\oauth1/cursor_walk/list_more.spi are superseded (kept as reference until main lands).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_14 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 14u32, "2026-10-23T23:35Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:35 x: x-side follow-up for candidate (4) DRAFTED: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_wire_lib_helpers.ps1 -Client <apps/client> -Lib <lib/spiral>. It applies after land_all's fit step, anchored on post-fit wire.spi. It's a no-rename delegation: after_first -> listm'.skip_, chunked -> listm'.chunks_, all_got -> resultm.collect_list (the accumulator contract kept: Ok (rev acc ++ l); callers in refs.spi/users.spi pass Nil). One deliberate difference: x's chunked looped forever on n <= 0, lib's chunks_ returns one chunk (callers pass lookup_limit () = 100). It refuses until the lib has skip_/chunks_/collect_list. Trialled: applied to a post-fit wire.spi copy against %TEMP%\\x-libcopy2; refused against the live lib. Compile check runs once main lands, on the real lib.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_15 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 15u32, "2026-10-23T23:36Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:36 x: paging.spi -> lib cursor_walk DRAFTED at 19:4x: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_paging_cursor_walk.ps1 -Client <apps/client> -Lib <lib/spiral>, applied after X-PAGING-GUARD's draft. It replaces paging's walker block (located by its boundaries, verified by its SHA-256, so a drifted text refuses) with aliases: type page = cursor_page, pager e = string -> result cursor_page e, page_budget, and an all_pages that calls cursor_walk.all_pages. whole_second and paging's tests stay; walk_with uses cursor_walk.walked_from with a literal trail. Only thread.spi and wire.spi name the types (step-1 grep: thread 14/17/31, wire 58/403-408); both get 'open cursor_walk' BEFORE 'open paging'. Order matters: lib's all_pages takes a walker record, and Spiral lets the later open win silently, so paging's all_pages must come later. Also renamed lib cursor_walk's test helpers with the module prefix (cursor_walk_halt_text, cursor_walk_two_pages), since wire defines its own cursor_halt_text. Trialled on copies against %TEMP%\\x-libcopy2: applied cleanly; the only line over 120 in paging is the pre-existing 'broken' test helper. Refuses until lib has cursor_walk.spi. Cells on paging/wire/thread run once main lands.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_16 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 16u32, "2026-10-23T23:39Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:39 x: the lib module in the landing patch is renamed oauth1 -> oauth (functions and types unchanged). x has its own apps/client/oauth1.spi, and no x module shares a name with a lib module today, so how Spiral resolves a package-vs-lib module name clash is untested. A lib oauth1 could break x's build the moment main lands it, before x migrates. Rebuilt x-libcopy2 in tree order (main's lib-gaps-x, then the patch): clean; the queue now checks x-libcopy2\\lib\\spiral\\oauth.spi. When x migrates, its oauth1.spi keeps the clock/nonce source and calls oauth.authorization_header.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_17 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 17u32, "2026-10-24T00:41Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:41 main: unicode's module position moved before json_scan (lib package.spiproj line 42), not before xml (97). json_scan's \\u branch (JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES) will call unicode.char_of, and xml's delegation still works because xml comes later. unicode needs only sm'_operators (line 26) and core listm/sm, so the move is safe. The apply's dry run is clean; its gate runs in chain A.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_18 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 18u32, "2026-10-24T00:46Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:46 main: unicode.char_of moved to the run_target form, the JSON-UNICODE-ESCAPES prerequisite: Gleam, Rust, C (the probed ({ }) UTF-8 encoder), Fsharp (ConvertFromUtf32 with the surrogate check) and TypeScript (String.fromCodePoint, written without '!(' since ! starts macro interpolation), plus '| _ => failwith', so every backend compiles it. Risk to watch: no lib function yet uses '| C =>' in a run_target match (0 of 31 Gleam/C/TS case uses are C), though the backend union has a C case. The unicode gate compiles the C test, so if C does not resolve the gate is red and the land is skipped.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_19 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 19u32, "2026-10-24T01:11Z", "x", "2026-10-23 21:11 x: CORRECTION by user direction (21:0x): \"why we're delegating to main lib work? makes no sense, one of my goals of inventing eoie is the atomic batched patches, so all lanes can go ham, also with automatic backups etc\". The earlier note here saying \"Lib edits are main's domain, so coordinate each landing with main\" was my own unexamined assumption, not a rule. From now on x lands its own lib changes through eoie do atomic batched edits (expect counts, all-or-nothing, sha256 per file) plus the lib Cells gate. Coordination with other lanes is about order and live-tree reads only, not ownership. x takes over landing of its own drafts: the x landing patch (crypto/sm'/listm'/resultm + oauth + cursor_walk) and LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS's 7 batches. Main keeps LIB-GAPS-X, which x's patch builds on (take_).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_20 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 20u32, "2026-10-24T01:12Z", "x", "2026-10-23 21:12 x: landing split, agreed with main. dictm, unicode and try_find' stay in main's chain A, already gated and rebased (no re-owning work in motion). x lands its own work as eoie do programs, one per change, each followed by the lib Cells gate on the touched modules. Part A, independent: LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS batches 1-7, crypto sha1_hex/hmac_sha1_base64, sm' percent_encode, resultm collect_list, new oauth + cursor_walk. Part B: listm' skip_/chunks_, after LIB-GAPS-X's take_ is in the tree. Constraint: lib has to be quiet (no Cells reader); main's baseline/cells-pack/dib reruns read it for hours, so part A lands in the first quiet window of 20+ min.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_21 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 21u32, "2026-10-24T01:36Z", "x", "10-23 21:18-21:22 incident (as of 21:36): a control run of land_lib_part_a.ps1 applied part A to the live lib, because the runner had no target param and its reader check only matched run_notebook.ps1. Reverted with reverse eoie do programs; restore round-trip proven (0 differing); main confirms no reader was hit. Runner rebuilt: mandatory -Target, -Live required and must match; ONE eoie do batch (33 replacements, 7 files, 38 ms) plus generated undo.eoie; the guard checks eoie window claim (tree-read leases), running lib/cells eoie jobs, erl/beam/Spiral-dotnet, live-gui windows and lib command lines, then again before applying. Controls on a scratch probe all failed for the tested reason (no -Live -> 64; dummy reader, tree-read lease -> 75; broken anchor -> nothing written, 0/1138 changed). Order vs main-lib-gaps-x: commutes (main appends bind/split_list/take_, rewrites listm collect; part A anchors mid-file). Queued: job-1792805715663-x-lib-part-a-land (after main-float-wire-gate), then job-1792805730458-x-lib-part-a-gate (--after-ok --heavy). Part B (listm skip_/chunks_) waits for main-lib-gaps-x-land (take_).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_22 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 22u32, "2026-10-24T01:39Z", "x", "Part B ready as one eoie do program (%TEMP%\\x-world\\lib-draft\\programs-part-b\\land.eoie): it inserts skip_/chunks_ + test right after main's take_ definition, anchored on 'taken n l'. The anchor encodes the dependency: on the current lib it refuses (nothing written), and on a scratch lib with main's lib-gaps-x applied it lands at listm' lines 774/780. Land it through the guarded runner after job-1792801901862-main-lib-gaps-x-land is ok, then gate listm' with Cells.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_23 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 23u32, "2026-10-24T02:29Z", "x", "Part A LANDED in live lib 22:06:07 (as of 22:29): one eoie do batch, 7 files, 33 replacements, plus new oauth.spi/cursor_walk.spi. job-1792807012027-x-part-a-lander landed it on attempt 13; earlier attempts were refused while main fmt-only-gate, erl and Spiral dotnet readers were active, and the apply-time re-check caught an erl that started mid-run. Guard fixes on the way: own job excluded via the supervisor pid in the eoie job folder (it had flagged itself at first); dotnet build tools (MSBuild/fsc) no longer count as readers; SpiralCompiler.exe added. Gate job-1792807567547 ran on compiler B553EF6B78E6EE29 (slot at 22:20:39, after the 22:18 install): x_lib_check 2/2; gleam_os_check 8/9, the failure being the notebook's Rust test missing -d regex (normalize_path's regex dependency predates this change); notebook fixed, recheck job-1792808951052. Undo: programs-landing\\batch\\undo.eoie plus removing the 2 new files.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_24 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 24u32, "2026-10-24T02:35Z", "x", "Part B armed (22:35): generic land_lib_program.ps1 (mandatory -Program/-Target, -Live must match, guard dot-sourced from land_guard.ps1, rehearsal on a fresh copy, guard re-check before apply), plus programs-part-b\\undo.eoie. Scratch tests on a copy taken after part A landed: without take_ it rejects and writes nothing; -Live on a non-live target gives 64; with gaps-x applied it lands; undo restores byte for byte; dot-sourced guard names a planted reader (75). Launch when main confirms take_ is live: eoie job start x-part-b-lander 14400000 pwsh -NoProfile -File land_when_quiet.ps1 -Program programs-part-b\\land.eoie -GateNotebook C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral\\listm'.spi -Label x-lib-part-b.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_25 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 25u32, "2026-10-24T03:02Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:02 main: main's gaps-x lib slice LANDED in live lib/spiral (job-1792810371034-main-lib-gaps-x-land-2, exit 0): listm' take_ (line 766) and linear collect, resultm.bind (545), sm'.split_list (2400, let rec), sm'.decimal_i32 (2425). Gate job-1792801901497 was GREEN on the post-install compiler (B553EF6B78E6EE29), on a lib copy taken at 22:24:53 that already held x's Part A: every candidate red was also red on the unchanged lib. x's Part B (skip_/chunks_) is unblocked; x was told at 23:00.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_26 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 26u32, "2026-10-24T03:45Z", "x", "23:45 x: x-side switch for candidate (2), now that main's lib unicode.spi is live (20:45). One eoie do program, %TEMP%\\x-world\\lib-draft\\programs-x-glyphs-unicode\\land.eoie (anchors generated from the staged file's text). It makes glyphs.char_of, graphemes and nfc one-line delegations to unicode.char_of, unicode.graphemes and unicode.nfc, and drops x's rust_/gleam_ wrappers plus the per-grapheme re-slicing walk (quadratic on Rust; the lib's version is linear). Names stay, so the 8 modules that open glyphs don't change. The lib arms are character for character the same as x's (char_of, nfc) or the same segmentation (graphemes, extended). Dry-run fits both the staged tree and the landing-trial tree (after apply_emoji_weight; its anchors are disjoint). Cells glyphs + splitter on %TEMP%\\x-glyphs-check: job-1792813502665. The x tree lands only when the user does. Left for this item after that: oauth1/paging/lookup switch to lib oauth, cursor_walk and chunks_ (apply_paging_cursor_walk is drafted; chunks_ waits for part B).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_27 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 27u32, "2026-10-24T03:48Z", "x", "23:48 x: x-side switch for candidate (1). programs-x-oauth1-lib\\land.eoie (generated from the staged text) makes x's oauth1.spi a thin layer over lib oauth. type oauth1 = oauth.oauth1_credentials (same fields), pair = oauth.oauth1_pair, percent = sm'.percent_encode (the same Rust arm character for character, plus a Gleam arm), fresh_nonce = crypto.sha1_hex (clock_seed ()), oauth_header = oauth.authorization_header (same protocol-param order). It keeps x's clock_seed and unix_seconds. It drops x's own hmac/base64/sorting/base-string code and the digest_bytes nominal: outside callers (transport, wire, net, token, write) use only percent, unix_seconds, fresh_nonce, oauth_header, oauth1 and pair, and wire's 'quoted' is its own JSON helper. Tests: x's percent test is the same fact as lib's percent_encode_keeps_only_unreserved, so it's dropped; the published-example checks live in lib oauth; x keeps a_fresh_nonce_is_forty_hex_digits. Dry-run fits the staged tree. Cells oauth1 + wire on %TEMP%\\x-glyphs-check (which also has the glyphs switch): job-1792813707329.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_28 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 28u32, "2026-10-24T05:00Z", "x", "10-24 01:00 x: oauth1 switch, first Cells run: a_fresh_nonce_is_forty_hex_digits failed on Rust, E0433 'cannot find crate sha1'. fresh_nonce now goes through crypto.sha1_hex, so the test needs '///> rust -d sha1' (lib oauth's own tests declare -d hmac sha1 base64). This was a directive bug in my draft; the code was fine. Fixed in programs-x-oauth1-lib\\land.eoie and the check copy, and the program still fits the staged tree. Recheck queued after the running wire check.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_29 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 29u32, "2026-10-24T05:04Z", "x", "10-24 01:04 x: oauth1 switch, what each check covers. Only net.spi:85 (signed_header, exported as xc_oauth1_header) calls oauth_header, and no Cells test does. wire.spi uses only the oauth1 type. So the delegation's Rust path is proven by net's cargo build, not by Cells. That build compiles the same calls against the same pins: lib crypto.hmac_sha1_base64 / sha1_hex use x's exact APIs (<hmac::Hmac<sha1::Sha1> as KeyInit>::new_from_slice, Mac::update/finalize, base64 Engine::encode STANDARD; lifted from x), and net's Cargo.toml already pins hmac 0.13, sha1 0.11 and base64 0.23. Add 'cargo check -p xc_net' on the check copy to this item's gate before landing.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_30 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 30u32, "2026-10-24T05:06Z", "x", "10-24 01:06 x: glyphs -> lib unicode delegation, glyphs.spi 8/8 PASS (job-1792817008488, rust + gleam) on %TEMP%\\x-glyphs-check. splitter.spi (the heavy glyphs user) is running next.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_31 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 31u32, "2026-10-24T05:36Z", "x", "10-24 01:36 x: glyphs -> unicode delegation VERIFIED: glyphs 8/8 and splitter 7/7 (rust + gleam, job-1792817008488). oauth1 delegation: wire 23/23 with exit 0. Its 4 failed_targets entries (zig x3 and 'rust -d serde_json' on the negative tests) match every earlier wire run back to the staged tree (10-22 09:23), so they're pre-existing and not from the delegation. oauth1.spi's own recheck (sha1 directive) is queued.")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_32 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 32u32, "2026-10-24T06:08Z", "x", "10-24 02:08 x: oauth1 -> lib delegation VERIFIED in Cells. The oauth1.spi recheck with '///> rust -d sha1' passes (job-1792818046562), and wire 23/23 matches its baseline. Both x-side switches (glyphs -> unicode, oauth1 -> oauth/crypto/sm') are now ready-to-land programs for the user's x landing; oauth1's landing gate also takes 'cargo check -p xc_net' (n29). Left for this item: paging -> cursor_walk (apply_paging_cursor_walk, drafted) and lookup -> chunks_ (waits for part B).")
inl note_of_x_generic_to_lib_33 () = Note ("X-GENERIC-TO-LIB", 33u32, "2026-10-24T06:16Z", "x", "10-24 02:16 x: paging -> lib cursor_walk, now against the LIVE lib (cursor_walk landed in part A, 22:06). apply_paging_cursor_walk.ps1 -Lib C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lib\\spiral applies cleanly to a copy of the landing-trial client (%TEMP%\\x-paging-check; the post-X-PAGING-GUARD walker hash matched). Cells paging + wire + thread\\thread queued: job-1792822553404. This one has its own copy, so the lookup check's wire edits on x-glyphs-check stay independent.")
inl eoie_next_fits_now () = Task ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", "agile next ranks only what fits the machine now so agents never idle on shells", Story, P0, D3, 85u32, Active, "", "eoie,agile,scheduling,friction", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_next_fits_now () = Planning ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_1 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:18Z", "eoie", "07:18 user 07:1x: 'we have backlog for days of work, categorizations considering weights, duration, machine load, etc. why you're still waiting for shells? that means eoie is flawed and opus unable to properly manage development' and 'put in the backlog to fix this both in eoie and claude memory, this makes the experience of using this tool so annoying'. Measured flaw: agile next ranks by WSJF only and has no --load filter (agile list has --load light, agile next does not), so while a heavy job held the slots, next kept offering compile-bound items and the agent idled on watchers. Fix: (1) agile next gains --load light|medium|heavy|none like list; (2) agile next --fits reads the machine (eoie window list: held heavy slots, free memory) and keeps only items whose load fits now (both heavy slots busy or free memory under the heavy floor -> light and none only), printing 'fits now: <load classes>' in its receipt; (3) items with load unset count as light only when their next step needs no compile; a groom pass sets load on the top 30 eoie items (drafts/reviews light, previews/releases heavy); (4) the Stop hook's ready list comes from 'agile next --fits' instead of the plain WSJF list, so an idle stop is refused while a fitting item exists. Done when: with both heavy slots held, 'agile next . --team eoie --fits' lists only light/none items and the hook quotes that list. Tests: next_cli cases for --load and --fits over a fixture windows home.")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_2 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:27Z", "eoie", "07:27 part 1 drafted and chained into release J (scratch next-fits/apply-next-load.ps1, prelaunch clean on a live copy): agile next --load light|medium|heavy|none[,...] filters the ranking through the same planning filter agile list uses (agile_planning_filters export), comma list = any, invalid word refused with a named usage; next_cli test covers light, light+heavy and a refused word. Part 4 is live now, without waiting for a release: the shared Stop hook (~/.claude/hooks/autonomous-gate.ps1, backup in scratch backups/) checks eoie window list, and when both heavy slots are held it quotes the team's 'agile list --open --load light --sort priority' rows from the FIRST block, not only after repeated idle stops (17 eoie rows parsed in a test). Left: part 2 (--fits reading the machine inside eoie) and part 3 (groom load on the top 30 items so the light list is complete).")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_3 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 3u32, "2026-10-23T11:28Z", "eoie", "07:28 part 3 groomed: load set on 40 open P0-P2 eoie items by one rule, load = machine cost of the item's NEXT step (heavy: release, preview or full regen; medium: a compile-window check; light: design, draft, review, grooming; none: waits outside the machine). heavy 7, medium 4, none 1, light 28. agile list --team eoie --open --load light now lists 45 rows instead of 17.")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_4 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 4u32, "2026-10-23T12:16Z", "eoie", "08:16 part 2 drafted for release L (scratch next-fits/apply-next-fits.ps1, after K because K threads min_free through verdict_text): eoie_window_query exports eoie_fitting_loads, which asks the window admission verdict itself whether a compile window and a heavy-build would be granted now (heavy also needs a free heavy slot) and answers light,none[,medium][,heavy]; agile next --fits ranks with that load list and the receipt names it (load=...). Full chain dry run on GE2's raw output: H, I, J, K (76 writes) then L with 6 slices (34 writes) apply clean; on that tree subcommand.spi, eoie_adoption classify.spi, eoie_crate_gate model.spi and agile_task_next ranking.spi (whose package includes task_next.spi) pass. Found on the way: release I adds VerbEnv/VerbMirror after VerbResume, so the crate-gate slice now anchors on the VerbResume line alone.")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_5 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 5u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): PROC-7 (supervision never starved: control-path capacity, heavy admission under caps), HEALTH-2 (effective capacity and worker attribution). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_6 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 6u32, "2026-10-24T07:02Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:02 main: a concrete gap hit at 01:2x during tibia's live block: 'agile next . --team main --load light 20' is rejected (unexpected argument --load; next takes --as/--team/--tag/--prefix/--open/--ready/--blocked), while 'agile list' accepts --load. During a live block only light work is allowed, which is exactly when an agent asks next for light items; I fell back to reading the whole ranked list. Smallest step toward this item: accept --load on next with list's semantics, and let a held live-gui window imply --load light.")
inl note_of_eoie_next_fits_now_7 () = Note ("EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW", 7u32, "2026-10-25T08:00Z", "eoie", "04:00 eoie: both parts are in the live tree (landed with LAND33, carried through the splits): part 1, agile next --load <loads> (loads_pass in agile_task_next; flag parsing in eoie_agile_next_args/args.spi), and part 2, --fits (args.spi asks eoie_window_query's eoie_fitting_loads: light,none, plus medium when a compile window would be granted, plus heavy when a heavy-build would be granted and a slot is free), with the next_cli tests. Main's 03:02 case is covered by --fits: a held live-gui window blocks compile and heavy admission, so --fits gives load=light,none. Both ship in SLATE. Left: the post-install CLI check (agile next . --team eoie --fits during a held window shows load=light,none) and the consolidated PROC-7/HEALTH-2 capacity notes.")
inl lib_dictm () = Task ("LIB-DICTM", "lib/spiral: dictm, a persistent map and set with one source for Rust (Rc<BTreeMap>, copy on update) and Gleam (gleam/dict); drafted and test-passed by x, to be landed by main", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,spiral,main,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_dictm () = Planning ("LIB-DICTM", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_dictm_1 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:27 x: drafted for SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL (world.ex needs maps and sets; lib had none that is backend-neutral: mapm wraps Rust/F# HashMap only). Draft: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\dictm.spi. API: empty, insert, remove, has, present, find (option), size, fold (order unspecified across backends), updated, keys; set = dict k () with set_empty/add/drop/has/size/items. Granular per the user's 10-23 direction: one foreign type per backend via a switch record, every function one backend_switch arm with a single expression, no blobs. Facts found on the way: Spiral closures reach Gleam curried (fn(Int) -> fn(Int) -> Int, measured with a probe), so fold calls !f(acc)(k)(v); '!=' inside a host splice breaks the parser (PartialEq::ne instead). Verified: compiles to Gleam and Rust (11-12 s), and Cells runs its 3 test blocks (insert/find/remove, order-free fold, set add/drop) on rust + gleam: 3/3 passed (process compiler, memo off, 78 s, no other Cells job active). Landing: copy into lib/spiral, drop the '--package .' line, run the lib corpus check; main owns the landing window. Note: in an edition-3 package x-compat flags 'dict.new' as an autolink (harmless in lib).")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_2 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:46Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:46 x: cross-backend caveat found by the inbox port. On Rust, Spiral flattens a closure's tuple parameter into separate arguments, so dictm.fold with a TUPLE accumulator, e.g. fun (a, b) k v => ..., breaks on Rust ('this function takes 2 arguments but 1 argument was supplied') while Gleam works. Options for landing: document 'accumulator must not be a tuple' with a test, or make the Rust arm pass a record. Callers today use an i32/list/dict accumulator, or fold to an index and look it up.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_3 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 3u32, "2026-10-23T12:09Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:09 x: second Rust-only closure-shape finding, from scheduler's tests. Spiral's Rust output DROPS unit (()) parameters from closures, so dictm.fold over a set (dict k (), value ()) called f(acc)(k)(v) on a closure that takes no v: 'this function takes 0 arguments but 1 argument was supplied' (12x), while Gleam was fine. Fix in the draft: a value-free primitive fold_keys (Gleam dict.fold with fn(acc, k, _) calling f(acc)(k); Rust keys().fold calling (f)(acc)(k.clone())), with keys and so set_items built on it. The set test now also checks set_items' length. Landing rule: any dictm or other host-splice binding that calls a Spiral closure must avoid tuple-typed and unit-typed parameters on Rust; both shapes need a lib test.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_4 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 4u32, "2026-10-23T12:41Z", "x", "2026-10-23 08:41 x: fold_keys landed in the draft and PASSES. Cells on C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\dictm.spi: 3/3 on rust + gleam (process compiler, memo off). New primitive: fold_keys f init d, with Gleam 'dict.fold(!d, !init, fn(acc, k, _) { !f(acc)(k) })' and Rust '(*!d).keys().fold(!init, |acc, k| (!f)(acc)(k.clone()))'. keys is defined through it, so set_items no longer passes a unit value through a Rust closure; the set_add_drop test now also checks listm.length (set_items s) = 2. Cause: on Rust, a closure's unit () parameter is dropped when it reaches a host splice, so the old keys (= fold with a () value) failed with E0057 wrong argument count in every user of set k = dict k (). Open for main when this enters lib/spiral: dictm.keys order is sorted on Rust (BTreeMap) but unspecified on Gleam (Erlang maps are sorted only up to 32 keys). Either sort keys in the Gleam arm, which needs an order on k, or document keys as unordered and sort at call sites that print.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_5 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 5u32, "2026-10-23T13:06Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:06 x: the root cause is wider than note 4's unit-parameter case. On Rust, Spiral compiles a closure with each record or tuple parameter FLATTENED into separate arguments (a visible_entry {key; result {status; out}} value became 3 arguments), while a host splice calls the closure with one value per parameter. So ANY $'...' that calls a Spiral closure with a user-chosen type breaks on Rust with E0057 once that type is a record, a tuple or unit. Gleam is unaffected (curried, whole values). runner_checks hit it through dictm.fold over a dict string visible_entry. Fix in both drafts (lib-draft and the client copy): no host splice calls a closure with user-chosen types. keys is now the only callback, and its closure is dictm's own fixed fun (acc : list k) key => Cons (key, acc) (keys come out descending on both backends, as before). fold is plain Spiral: keys |> listm.rev |> listm.fold with present lookups, ascending on both backends. fold_keys is removed. One constraint remains: k itself must be a single-value type (string or ints), which is true for every current user. RULE for lib reviews (LIB-OTP-ACTORS too): a host splice may call a Spiral closure only when the module fixes the closure's parameter types to non-compound ones. Retest queued.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_6 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 6u32, "2026-10-23T14:21Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:21 x: note 4's open key-order question, drafted (both copies, queued for Cells after tibia's block). The Gleam arm of keys now sorts with Erlang's lists:sort/1 (a '@ external' named dictm_term_sorted) before folding into the Spiral list. Erlang term order is total and generic, so no comparator closure crosses the boundary, and for same-typed keys it equals Rust's Ord (binaries and strings bytewise, integers numerically). Both backends now give the same descending list for any size; before, Erlang maps were sorted only up to 32 keys. New test keys_are_sorted_past_32_entries: 40 keys '1000'..'1039', first '1039', last '1000', length 40. fold (Spiral over keys) therefore visits ascending on both backends, so the scheduler's queue order is backend-independent.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_7 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 7u32, "2026-10-23T14:40Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:40 x: name history, so landing doesn't confuse it. The OLD fork core had a module named dictm (Python-only: rm-list buckets, hash_cython; CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE notes 2, 4, 5), and candidate v1 removed it (note 11: 'dictm*, dictm_utils/*' removed). Today's core (VS Code Plugin/core: arraym, base, core, dep_optionm, limit, listm, loop, monad, optionm, sm) has no dictm, so lib/spiral's dictm.spi clashes with nothing. It is a new neutral module, not a port of the old one.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_8 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 8u32, "2026-10-23T21:37Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:37 x: dictm draft 4/4 on rust + gleam (chain11 + chain14). keys_are_sorted_past_32_entries had failed only on zig, because it lacked the ///> rust/gleam targets the other tests carry (dictm has no Zig arm). Targets added.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_9 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 9u32, "2026-10-23T21:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:57 main: x handed off the draft dictm.spi (BTreeMap / gleam dict, sorted keys, 4/4 PASS) in C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft for main to land. Landing rule: Cells gate on the tree-lib baseline subset rule, after the tonight chain; rebase over eoie's 17:56 lib-gaps land (sm', am', process, testing, env changed) first, since x's lib_gaps.spi also touches listm'/sm'.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_10 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 10u32, "2026-10-23T22:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:30 main: the landing draft of x's dictm.spi is in %TEMP%\\lib-dictm\\lib\\spiral\\dictm.spi. It follows the lib's layout (/// # dictm, open sm'_operators, then test dictm static, one /// ### heading per definition). It drops x-world's '///- --package .' line and the prose header, whose 'iteration order unspecified' contradicted the sorted keys. keys now ascends: Rust keys().rev() with Cons prepends; Gleam list.reverse(lists:sort(...)). fold drops its listm.rev, and the test is renamed keys_ascend_past_32_entries with expected first 1000 and last 1039. Rust insert and remove are clone-then-insert/remove blocks instead of rebuilding the map through an iterator chain. No line is over 120 characters. mapm checked: Rust-only mutable HashMap/BTreeMap from the old era, no overlap. Gate (%TEMP%\\lib-dictm\\gate.ps1, a fresh tree-lib copy plus dictm in package.spiproj, lib_gate -File dictm.spi): GREEN only if every test row passes, at least 4. land.ps1 lands only on GREEN with no Cells erl in flight; it copies dictm.spi and appends '    dictm' to the lib's modules. Queued in requeue-after-block.ps1 (chain A, last), which runs on tibia's 'block end'.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_11 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 11u32, "2026-10-23T22:36Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:36 main: correction to note 10: the dictm gate is the generic lib-gate-kit\\gate.ps1 -Name LIB-DICTM with %TEMP%\\lib-dictm\\apply.ps1 (adds dictm.spi and appends '    dictm' to the modules), and it lands via lib-gate-kit\\land.ps1. The one-off gate.ps1 and land.ps1 are deleted. Verdict rule: every row passes, or every red is also red on the tree baseline; for a new module the baseline is empty, so every row must pass.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_12 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 12u32, "2026-10-24T00:28Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:28 main: lib-gate-kit gate.ps1 hardened after a data-flow trace, before its first run. (1) A header-only tests.tsv (0 rows, e.g. a load failure) counted as green, since Import-Csv gives an empty array, not null; 0 rows is now RED. (2) A stale verdict-<Name>.txt from an earlier run could let land.ps1 land after a gate that crashed before writing; the verdict file is now deleted at the start. (3) A new module's baseline (the file is absent from the tree lib) made lib_gate throw; it is now an empty baseline, so a new module must pass every row.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_13 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 13u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: gate job main-lib-dictm-gate finished 0 (GREEN) at 22:21; the land refused at 22:21 on a Cells erl still alive (pid 30280), the race land.ps1 now waits out (two clear 20 s samples, up to 90 min). Requeued as job-1792811312648-main-lib-dictm-land-2, after the lib chain's tail (job-1792810377882-main-lib-otp-single-land) so no gate copies the lib mid-write. dictm.spi is a new file, so the 22:21 verdict still holds.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_14 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 14u32, "2026-10-24T08:42Z", "x", "10-24 04:42 x: LIB-DICTM rebuilt to GRANULAR-HEAL n1 at main's request (main withdrew main-lib-dictm-land-2 over 5 violations). Slice: %TEMP%\\x-world\\lib-draft\\dictm-granular\\dictm.spi; census finds 0 sites, and Cells is 5/5 rust + gleam (job-1792831094255). Rust: Rc<BTreeMap> with copy-on-write in Spiral (rust_owned = Rc::unwrap_or_clone, rust.to_mut, then one-call $'!m.insert(!key, !value)' / $'!m.remove(&!key)', then rust_new_rc), and one-call reads (contains_key; get(&k).cloned() as option' |> optionm'.unbox; len as convert.unativeint |> convert.convert : i32; keys().cloned().collect() as am'.vec |> am'.vec_fold'). Gleam: dict.get as result' |> resultm.unbox; keys = dict.keys -> the lists:sort extern -> listm'.fold'. New test an_old_version_survives_an_insert. Lessons on the way: the !\\\\ splice drops unit arguments (a dict used as a set rendered `$2`), and an unpinned convert.convert or new_b_tree_map leaves value-restriction metavars. Sent to main for gate + land; SIM-WORLD-SPIRAL's apply_sim_modules follows.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_15 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 15u32, "2026-10-24T18:57Z", "main", "main 15:0x: x's dictm granular rewrite (%TEMP%\\lib-dictm-granular) gated GREEN (10:38, main-serial-heal-gates) and LANDED 13:52 through main-serial-lands (dictm added to lib/spiral/package.spiproj after oauth). The x-side consumers can drop their copies now.")
inl note_of_lib_dictm_16 () = Note ("LIB-DICTM", 16u32, "2026-10-24T23:59Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:59 main groom: done. The item's scope was 'drafted and test-passed by x, to be landed by main', and the land happened at 13:52 (main-serial-lands; gate GREEN 10:38; dictm listed in lib/spiral/package.spiproj). Moving the consumers is x's own work and is tracked in x's items.")
inl spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple () = Task ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", "An unparenthesized comparison piped into an assert (x = y |> _assert_eq z, which parses as x = (y |> ...)) reaches codegen and fails with a misleading \"main of a Zig program returns ... not a tuple\" instead of a type error", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,compiler,c,zig,cells,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple () = Planning ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", Unestimated, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:31Z", "tibia-lane", "Found by tibia-lane 07:2x in src/live.spi; bisected 08:0x in a scratch package (scratch repro-curried/repro.spi, Cells c target, packageDir lib plus spiral-). The module main returns a 2-tuple of closures, f (a : i32) (b : i32) : i32 = a + b and g. Results, one cell each: f one 2 |> _assert_eq 3i32 passes; a local untyped curried helper (inl call a b = f a b) passes, so it was NOT the cause, despite the first version of this note; f one 2 % 2 |> _assert_eq 1i32 passes; (f one 2 % 2 = 1) |> _assert_eq (g one) passes; f one 2 % 2 = 1 |> _assert_eq (g one) FAILS; f one 2 = 3 |> _assert_eq true FAILS. Both failures: (Mix) FatalError: The main of a Zig program returns a single value (an i32 exit code, unit, or one value that is discarded), not a tuple. So |> binds tighter than =, and x = y |> _assert_eq z parses as x = (y |> _assert_eq z). The defect: that misparse reaches codegen and reports a Zig main tuple, instead of a type error at the comparison (the right side is unit, or the assert gets the wrong type). Minimal repro: a test cell whose body is 1 = 1 |> _assert_eq true. Fix candidates (compiler lane): a type error at =, or a lint for an unparenthesized comparison feeding |>. tibia wraps the comparison in parentheses or binds it to a typed local first.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:47Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:47 main: code read and a staged probe. Cells compiles a test cell once (cells.ex 605-633). On a unit-result diagnostic it retries with '0i32' appended; on a value result it retries with show_value_main, which binds the body and prints it. The Zig 'main returns ... not a tuple' error comes from codegen on the first compile, so the defect sits in type inference or partial evaluation, which let 1 = (1 |> _assert_eq true) through as a tuple-valued main, not in Cells. Probe staged (%TEMP%\\curried-main-probe, a package over lib; run.ps1 runs mix spiral.notebook --gate, output to the run cache): four test cells on c and zig, (1) the misparse 1i32 = 1i32 |> _assert_eq true, (2) the parenthesized form, (3) the bare mismatch 1i32 = (), and (4) 1i32 |> _assert_eq true. Whichever of (3)/(4) compiles without a type error names the culprit: equality across types, or _assert_eq across types. Runs in the first compile gap after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T15:38Z", "tibia-lane", "Team tag tibia -> main (tibia-lane 11:4x): tibia found it, the fix is a compiler change, which is main's lane. tibia works around it with typed locals.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 4u32, "2026-10-23T21:56Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:56 main: probe queued tonight as job-1792770218847 (after the hopacsota promotion): %TEMP%\\curried-main-probe (probe.spi, 4 test blocks on c and zig: 1i32 = 1i32 |> _assert_eq true, the parenthesized form, 1i32 = (), and 1i32 |> _assert_eq true), run through mix spiral.notebook --gate; output in spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE. Reading rule: if the unparenthesized form still reaches codegen ('The main of a Zig program returns ... not a tuple') while 1i32 = () gives a type error, the fix is a type check of the comparison's operands before codegen, with the unparenthesized form as the regression sample.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 5u32, "2026-10-24T00:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:51 main: status 0 Planned -> 20 Active: the probe (4 cells on c and zig, decides whether equality or _assert_eq lets the mismatch through) is requeued as job-1792801907956 in chain B; its reading rule is in note 4.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 6u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming, corrected at 23:51: main-curried-main-probe (exit 0) does NOT reproduce the misleading codegen error. It stops with 'FatalError: File main has a type error somewhere in its path' at probe.spi:4:20, the |> of cell 1 (1i32 = 1i32 |> _assert_eq true, which parses as 1i32 = (1i32 |> _assert_eq true)). All 4 cells fail only because one type error fails the whole file in that run. The 23:08 reading ('reproduces the bug') was wrong.")
inl note_of_spiral_test_local_curried_main_tuple_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-TEST-LOCAL-CURRIED-MAIN-TUPLE", 7u32, "2026-10-24T03:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:51 main: closed by note 4's prewritten reading rule. The fix was needed only if the unparenthesized form still reached codegen ('The main of a Zig program returns ... not a tuple'). On the installed single-flight (B553EF6B78E6EE29, probe ran 22:25) it is a type error at the misparsed |> instead, which is the requested behavior; some change in the tree since tibia's 10-23 07:2x find fixed it. tibia: reopen if your typed-locals workaround still hits the codegen message on the current compiler.")
inl gleam_erlang_1x () = Task ("GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", "Gleam cells and lib on gleam_erlang 1.3 and gleam_otp 1.3: bump the cell project pins (targets.ex) and migrate lib process.spi/platform.spi to the 1.x process API, with a granular erlang_process binding", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,spiral,gleam,main,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_gleam_erlang_1x () = Planning ("GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", Minutes 120u32, LoadUnset, Unassigned)
inl note_of_gleam_erlang_1x_1 () = Note ("GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:42Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:42 main: filed from LIB-PAR-MAP/LIB-OTP-ACTORS groundwork. Current releases (hexdocs, 10-23): gleam_erlang 1.3.0 (published 2025-08-03, latest on hex) and gleam_otp 1.3.0. Cell Gleam projects pin gleam_erlang '>=0.34.0 and <1.0.0' (apps/cells lib/spiral/cells/targets.ex:344) and gleam_stdlib 0.57. The 1.x process API renames what lib process.spi uses: selecting_anything -> select_other, select_forever -> selector_receive_forever, select(selector, ms) -> selector_receive(from, within). selecting(subject, f) -> select_map; monitor_process -> monitor, with Down = ProcessDown(monitor, pid, reason: ExitReason Normal|Killed|Abnormal). gleam/erlang/os is gone (platform.is_windows imports it: give it an os:type external first). Plan: (1) lib migrations on 0.34 first, where an equivalent exists, so the pin bump is a no-op diff; (2) bump targets.ex to gleam_erlang >= 1.3.0 and < 2.0.0, gleam_otp >= 1.3.0 and < 2.0.0, and stdlib to latest; (3) the granular binding scratchpad par-lab/erlang_process.spi (one nominal per Gleam type, one one-expression primitive per function, 4 gleam test blocks) moves into lib. Gate: a lib Cells --gate run on gleam + the KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR process_capture tests, before and after. Cells and lib edits happen only outside tibia's live blocks.")
inl note_of_gleam_erlang_1x_2 () = Note ("GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", 2u32, "2026-10-23T11:46Z", "main", "2026-10-23 07:46 main: staged %TEMP%\\gleam-erlang-1x\\apply.ps1 (dry run by default). Five anchors, each matching once on the live tree: the targets.ex @gleam_deps pins (stdlib >=1.0.5, gleam_erlang >=1.3.0, new gleam_otp >=1.3.0, envoy >=1.2.0, gtempo >=7.4.0, each <2/<8); process.spi selecting_anything -> select_other, select_forever -> selector_receive_forever, select(selector, left) -> selector_receive; and platform.is_windows reading os:type through one @external instead of gleam/erlang/os. Compatibility read from the hex release requirements: gleam_erlang 1.3.0 needs stdlib >=0.53; gary 1.1.0 needs stdlib >=0.34 and <2; gtempo 7.4.0 needs stdlib >=0.60, gleam_time 1 and tzif 1. The matrix Gleam shim (tests/native-shims/gleam) already resolves stdlib 1.x and its rows pass, so codegen output is stdlib-1 clean. No other repo uses the 0.x names (grep with *.ex positive control). Landing rule: a gap with no live block and no Cells erl.exe running. Apply, then lib_gate on process.spi and platform.spi (gleam), then par-lab/erlang_process.spi's 4 gleam tests in a scratch package. Revert if any gleam row fails that passed before.")
inl note_of_gleam_erlang_1x_3 () = Note ("GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", 3u32, "2026-10-23T13:27Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:27 main: post-block lib landing staged in %TEMP%\\lib-land-1023. The copy is lib/spiral at 09:2x plus apps/cells targets.ex, with tree hashes in tree-hashes.tsv and targets-tree-hash.txt. On it: 23 formatter restores (pack-next5e, from the pre-05:08 originals); GLEAM-ERLANG-1X (process.spi, platform.spi, targets.ex pins); LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM as sm-c-arms.ps1 (portable_matches_at and portable_index_of helpers before index_of, one-line C arms in index_of, contains, starts_with and ends_with, and test searches_agree_on_c for c, gleam and rust after contains, with tibia's cases; reproducible byte for byte); and the five new modules erlang_process, otp_actor, otp_supervisor, step_runtime and par' in package.spiproj. Jobs, all after tibia-block-pr26: main-lib-land-gate (job-1792761989271; gate.ps1 runs a Cells copy with the new pins and gates 8 files from the copy lib), then main-lib-land (job-1792761990973), then cells-pack, then hopacsota. Land rule, written before the gate (land.ps1): the formatter restores land on their hash guards. process and platform plus targets.ex land only if both gates pass. sm' lands only if its gate passes. The five new modules and package.spiproj land only if all five pass and the 1.x pins landed. 'Pass' means failed 0, discovered > 0 and no target-not-passed row. lib_land refuses any file whose tree hash moved since the copy.")
inl note_of_gleam_erlang_1x_4 () = Note ("GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", 4u32, "2026-10-23T20:04Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:04 main: lib land of 16:02 (job-1792766411950, after a re-gate of the files the first gate run poisoned): LANDED 12 formatter restores (convert, lustre, parsing, reflection, state_core, testing, trace, leptos/leptos, leptos/ui/accordion + tab_link, near/wallet near_wallet, rust/testing) and seq.spi + listm'.spi (LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS, gates 26/26 and 23/23). The 1.x set is HELD. Its gate found three real defects, all fixed in %TEMP%\\lib-land-1023 since: (1) process.spi still called atom.create_from_string (7 sites; gleam_erlang 1.x names it atom.create) -> gleam build failed on all 5 process tests; (2) erlang_process.down_reason read event.reason, which Gleam rejects on the 1.x Down union (ProcessDown | PortDown) -> now a case over both constructors; (3) otp_supervisor's test left the actor message type open (ETY-ValueRestriction) -> annotated (_ : i32). Also fixed: step_runtime.spi assigned mut fields through a non-mut record (it now passes 2/2), which had failed every file of the first run. Still red: par' map_keeps_order/map_empty on rust and fsharp (the thread-scope arm). Next: one gate pass over process, erlang_process, otp_supervisor, par' (plus the rest of the set) on the land copy, then land the set with targets.ex.")
inl note_of_gleam_erlang_1x_5 () = Note ("GLEAM-ERLANG-1X", 5u32, "2026-10-23T20:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:37 main: LANDED 16:36: process.spi (D4025378EE51F5FE, Gleam 5/5 after the atom.create rename), platform.spi (B8CCBD05C66A0BB0), and apps/cells targets.ex (7FC4A09F9B047C1C: gleam_erlang >= 1.3.0, gleam_otp >= 1.3.0, stdlib >= 1.0.5, envoy >= 1.2.0, gtempo >= 7.4.0). No x, tibia or eoie source calls gleam_erlang directly (ripgrep, positive control on lib), so every consumer reaches 1.x through these lib modules. Closed.")
inl lib_contains_c_arm () = Task ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", "lib/spiral sm'.contains, starts_with, ends_with and index_of have no C arm (backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral on the c target); pure-Spiral C arms drafted and tested", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,spiral,c,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_contains_c_arm () = Planning ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", Unestimated, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl note_of_lib_contains_c_arm_1 () = Note ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", 1u32, "2026-10-23T12:28Z", "tibia-lane", "Found by tibia-lane 08:3x: apps/host scenario.spi (monster mix parsing) failed on the c target at gcc with backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral; sm'.contains has arms for Gleam, Lua, Fsharp, Python, Rust, TypeScript, Zig and Lean, not C. sm'.split and am'.index have C arms now (LIB-SPLIT-LUA-C). Likely fix: a C arm as strstr(haystack, needle) != NULL over the String* ptr fields, the pattern of the other C string arms; plus a c target on contains' own test. tibia's host targets gleam and lua meanwhile, so nothing waits on it.")
inl note_of_lib_contains_c_arm_2 () = Note ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", 2u32, "2026-10-23T12:34Z", "tibia-lane", "C arms DRAFTED and tested 08:3x (tibia-lane; scratch c-arms/carms.spi). Same style as sm'.split's existing C arm: plain Spiral over core sm.length and sm.index, no host text. matches_at search s at compares the needle character by character from at. index_of = the first at from 0 with at + m <= n where matches_at holds, else -1. contains = index_of >= 0. starts_with = m <= n && matches_at at 0. ends_with = m <= n && matches_at at n - m. Lengths must be bound to i32 locals: an inline comparison of two generic sm.length results fails with ETY-ValueRestriction. Cells, 3 of 3 cells (c, lua and gleam) on one test against the lib contract: index_of on \"a.b%c\" gives 1 for \".\", 3 for \"%\", -1 for \"y\", 0 for the empty needle and 2 for \"b%c\"; contains, starts_with and ends_with each pass, fail and handle the empty value. To land through lib_land (main's process; main holds lib writes during tibia's live block): add C = fun () => <the scan> to each of the four backend_switch blocks, and a c target on their test. Note that index_of returns int there; the scan returns i32, so cast or annotate to match the signature.")
inl note_of_lib_contains_c_arm_3 () = Note ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", 3u32, "2026-10-23T13:27Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:27 main: post-block lib landing staged in %TEMP%\\lib-land-1023. The copy is lib/spiral at 09:2x plus apps/cells targets.ex, with tree hashes in tree-hashes.tsv and targets-tree-hash.txt. On it: 23 formatter restores (pack-next5e, from the pre-05:08 originals); GLEAM-ERLANG-1X (process.spi, platform.spi, targets.ex pins); LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM as sm-c-arms.ps1 (portable_matches_at and portable_index_of helpers before index_of, one-line C arms in index_of, contains, starts_with and ends_with, and test searches_agree_on_c for c, gleam and rust after contains, with tibia's cases; reproducible byte for byte); and the five new modules erlang_process, otp_actor, otp_supervisor, step_runtime and par' in package.spiproj. Jobs, all after tibia-block-pr26: main-lib-land-gate (job-1792761989271; gate.ps1 runs a Cells copy with the new pins and gates 8 files from the copy lib), then main-lib-land (job-1792761990973), then cells-pack, then hopacsota. Land rule, written before the gate (land.ps1): the formatter restores land on their hash guards. process and platform plus targets.ex land only if both gates pass. sm' lands only if its gate passes. The five new modules and package.spiproj land only if all five pass and the 1.x pins landed. 'Pass' means failed 0, discovered > 0 and no target-not-passed row. lib_land refuses any file whose tree hash moved since the copy.")
inl note_of_lib_contains_c_arm_4 () = Note ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", 4u32, "2026-10-23T15:38Z", "tibia-lane", "Team tag tibia -> main (tibia-lane 11:4x): main lands the arms in its post-block lib landing (note of 09:27); tibia's drafts are in that staging.")
inl note_of_lib_contains_c_arm_5 () = Note ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", 5u32, "2026-10-23T20:04Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:04 main: lib land of 16:02 (job-1792766411950, after a re-gate of the files the first gate run poisoned): LANDED 12 formatter restores (convert, lustre, parsing, reflection, state_core, testing, trace, leptos/leptos, leptos/ui/accordion + tab_link, near/wallet near_wallet, rust/testing) and seq.spi + listm'.spi (LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS, gates 26/26 and 23/23). sm'.spi (the C arms) HELD: the copy's sm' gate wrote no report file (its 47/48 run printed but the RawOut is missing), so the baseline-subset rule had nothing to compare; the tree baseline's reds are replace_regex on gleam and rust -d regex. Next: re-gate sm'.spi alone on the land copy, then land it if its reds are a subset.")
inl note_of_lib_contains_c_arm_6 () = Note ("LIB-CONTAINS-C-ARM", 6u32, "2026-10-23T20:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:37 main: LANDED 16:36 in spiral/lib/spiral/sm'.spi (A4FD87585FDCC6A5): C arms for index_of, contains, starts_with and ends_with plus portable_matches_at/portable_index_of and the searches_agree_on_c test. Gate 48/49 on the land copy; its one red, replace_regex on gleam and rust -d regex, is red on the tree lib too (baseline run), so it lands by the subset rule. Closed; tibia confirmed (16:3x).")
inl eoie_start_under_shell () = Task ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", "eoie process start-under-shell starts a daemon with the user shell as its live parent", Story, P2, D2, 60u32, Active, "", "eoie,process,parent", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_start_under_shell () = Planning ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_start_under_shell_1 () = Note ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", 1u32, "2026-10-23T12:50Z", "eoie", "08:50 requested by main (CELLS-DAEMON-PARENT): one generic verb that starts a long-lived process with a live parent, so Cells' CompilerDaemon (and any other daemon) stops running parentless without a second Win32 launcher. Shape: eoie process start-under-shell <program> [args...]: CreateProcessW with PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_PARENT_PROCESS = the user shell (GetShellWindow -> explorer.exe), handle inheritance off, NORMAL priority, TEMP cwd, detached; prints pid=<n> parent=<shell pid> and exits 0; falls back to a plain detached start (reported as parent=none) only when there is no shell window. Reuses the launch primitive HP ships for the job host (one primitive in the eoie_os boundary package per EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY's 08:32 scope), so the host and this verb share code. Test: start a ping under the shell, read its parent (explorer.exe), stop it. Priority P2, after HP-tail and the release chain.")
inl note_of_eoie_start_under_shell_2 () = Note ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:04Z", "eoie", "09:04 drafted for release L: new OS boundary crate eoie_os_launch. launch.spi emits the launch function once through Spiral global (top-level Rust: CreateProcessW with the shell as parent attribute, the same fallbacks as the job host, returns pid and parent; unix keeps a Command in its own process group) plus a one-expression primitive; run.spi is lib-only (env args, console). Verb: VerbProcess -> 'eoie process start-under-shell <program> [args...]' printing 'pid=<n> parent=<shell pid> program=...'. The Rust compiled standalone and started ping under explorer.exe (reported parent 8628 = actual parent explorer.exe); both Spiral files parse with the 5e formatter, which left the global Rust byte-identical. Prelaunch clean after two checker fixes (a triple-quoted global block and a formatter-wrapped macro were read as code); the improved checker still passes GE2's chain. after-install-L probes it (parent must be explorer.exe). Next: the job host calls this function instead of its own copy.")
inl note_of_eoie_start_under_shell_3 () = Note ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", 3u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: user direction 10-23 22:1x is that eoie processes belong to the session that started them, not explorer (EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT). This verb stays for daemons that must outlive any session, but should gain a start-under-owner form on the same eoie_parented_start primitive (owner pid instead of the shell window), and callers should pick it unless outliving the session is the point.")
inl note_of_eoie_start_under_shell_4 () = Note ("EOIE-START-UNDER-SHELL", 4u32, "2026-10-24T04:34Z", "eoie", "00:34 found and fixed a certain stage-L build failure before L launched. launch.spi put its Rust in a top-level 'global' block of a non-entry module, which codegen drops: the regenerated os_launch.rs was 4,623 bytes with no eoie_shell_parented_start*, so run.spi's call failed E0425 under real cargo (the 09:04 check compiled the Rust by hand, outside Spiral). Fix: start-under-shell\\eoie_os_launch\\launch.spi now puts the global inside shell_parented_started's body (move-global-into-body.ps1; Rust byte-identical; backup .before-global-move). Verified: regen + cargo check of eoie-os-launch + eoie-job-host on the emulated post-R tree, PASS 00:33, os_launch.rs 13,334 bytes. This edits stage L's source while the train runs (GE3 queued); justified because L would otherwise fail deterministically, and the change was emulated and compiled before L launches.")
inl eoie_slot_file_settled () = Task ("EOIE-SLOT-FILE-SETTLED", "job slot files record the settled state instead of a phantom waiting ticket", TaskKind, P2, D2, 50u32, Active, "", "eoie,jobs,slots", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_slot_file_settled () = Planning ("EOIE-SLOT-FILE-SETTLED", Minutes 40u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_slot_file_settled_1 () = Note ("EOIE-SLOT-FILE-SETTLED", 1u32, "2026-10-23T13:08Z", "eoie", "09:08 found while checking slot tickets before stopping HP-tail: 28 job folders whose job has settled (finished, stopped, lost, expired) still have a slot file saying 'waiting ticket=...'. The supervisor writes 'waiting' while queued and 'ready' on admission, but nothing rewrites it when a job settles before or without admission, and old-binary jobs never wrote 'ready'. Anything that reads slot files (window-slot-order.ps1, a human checking the queue) sees phantom waiters. Fix: the supervisor writes the slot file's final state when the job settles ('settled ticket=... state=<state>'), and window list (or job list --queue) shows the live ticket queue from the queue directory, not from job folders. Test: a queued heavy job stopped before admission leaves 'settled', not 'waiting'.")
inl note_of_eoie_slot_file_settled_2 () = Note ("EOIE-SLOT-FILE-SETTLED", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:12Z", "eoie", "09:12 slice drafted for release L (scratch slot-settled/apply-slot-settled.ps1, on G's healed slot.spi): the slot loop's stop and expiry branches write 'settled ticket=<t> state=stopped|expired-in-queue' to the slot file before returning, so a job that ends in the queue no longer leaves a phantom waiting ticket. Anchored on the two return lines (layout-independent); dry run on the chain's raw slot.spi applies and is idempotent. The other half (a live queue view from the queue directory) stays open.")
inl x_stub_weighted_limit () = Task ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", "x e2e: the stub refuses posts over X's weighted 280 (code-point weights + emoji entities) with X's 400 problem, instead of capping 280 Python characters, so e2e catches a splitter that emits an overweight post", Story, P3, D2, 85u32, Active, "", "x,e2e,weights", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_stub_weighted_limit () = Planning ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_1 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T13:34Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:34 x: found while inventorying the stub for SIM-X-FEATURE-PARITY. stub.py create() checks len(text) > 280 in code points, so a 200-CJK post (X weight 400) is accepted, and e2e can never catch an overweight frame. The stub's weight must be an independent implementation (Python, from twitter-text v3's ranges and emoji entities), not a call into x's glyphs, so it stays an oracle. Test: one tutorial or e2e-only step posting a text that is 141 CJK characters gets the 400. Land after X-EMOJI-WEIGHT, so both sides count emoji the same way.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_2 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 2u32, "2026-10-23T13:43Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:43 x: drafted and verified red/green (no compile: release xc.exe + the Python stub). Oracle: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\x_weight.py, an algorithm independent of x's glyphs: NFC first as twitter-text does, then regex-matched emoji entities (flag pairs, keycaps, pictograph or VS16-symbol sequences with skin tones, tags and ZWJ joins) at 2 each, then per-code-point v3 ranges. Its self-test agrees on 14 of 14 cases (thumb + tone 2, ZWJ family 2, heart + VS16 2, keycap 2, JP flag 2, decomposed e 1 after NFC, (c) 1, a + VS16 3, 140 CJK 280, 141 CJK 282). apply_stub_weighted_limit.ps1 inlines it into stub.py (the stub runs with python -I, so no sibling import), changes create() to x_weight(text) > 280 with the same 400 message, and adds a README step after 'posted 9001': xc post ('日' * 141); $LASTEXITCODE. GREEN (patched stub): the step answers 'xc: X API answered 400 for /2/tweets: Invalid Request: text must be 1 to 280 characters' and 4. RED (same README, original stub): 'posted 9002' and 0. Both copies: accept and verify have 0 failures, so no other step changed and the refused post uses no id. Note: x's counts are >= the oracle's for every case (x doesn't NFC; it counts a decomposed e as 2), so the stub never refuses a post x's splitter produced. Harness: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\e2e_stub_check.ps1.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_3 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 3u32, "2026-10-23T16:39Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:39 x: known simplification in the drafted oracle (x_weight.py): X weighs every URL as 23 (t.co, twitter-text's transformedURLLength), but the oracle counts a URL's real characters. So it is STRICTER than X for URLs longer than 23 and laxer for shorter ones. No tutorial step hits it today: framed thread posts defuse dots (no links form), and e2e's single posts carry no URLs. If a future step posts a link, add twitter-text's URL rule to the oracle first (its validUrl regex is large; a scheme-and-host subset covers x's cases), or the stub will refuse posts X accepts.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_4 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 4u32, "2026-10-24T04:07Z", "x", "10-24 00:07 x: n3's known gap CLOSED for scheme URLs. x_weight.py gains twitter-text's transformedURLLength rule: each http(s):// URL weighs 23 whatever its length, and trailing . , ! ? ; : ) ] ' quote stay outside it. Emoji and code-point weights apply to the text between URLs (plain_weight). Seven new self-test cases make 21/21. Positive control: the old rule weighs the same three URL cases 61, 11 and 16, the new one 27, 23 and 25. Still NOT covered: bare domains ('example.com' weighs 11 here, 23 on X, since twitter-text links them through its TLD list). x's own posts defuse dots (defused_dot), so only a human-typed bare domain would hit it. apply_stub_weighted_limit.ps1 inlines the file up to its __main__ block. A simulated inline into the staged stub.py compiles (py_compile 0), and the new names (plain_weight, URL, TRANSFORMED_URL_LENGTH) don't clash: stub.py's 'url' is a lowercase local.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_5 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 5u32, "2026-10-24T07:10Z", "x", "10-24 03:10 x: n4 CORRECTED by a cross-check against x's own weighting (X-EMOJI-WEIGHT's Spiral cases agree with the oracle on every shared case). The 00:05 URL regex matched DEFUSED links: x writes dots in posts as ZWSP + '.' (defused_dot), Python's \\s doesn't match U+200B, so 'https://x<ZWSP>.co/abc' was weighed 23, while X doesn't link it (a host containing U+200B isn't a valid domain) and counts its characters. The regex now requires a real host (label(.label)+ over [a-z0-9-], case-insensitive) before an optional path. Two new defused cases make 23/23. Positive control: URL.search finds 'https://x.co/abc' but not its defused form. The simulated inline into the staged stub.py still compiles.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_6 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 6u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_stub_weighted_limit_7 () = Note ("X-STUB-WEIGHTED-LIMIT", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl eoie_job_cells_gate () = Task ("EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE", "eoie job start waits, instead of failing, while another team's Cells notebook run is active (a 'cells' slot or --wait-free <process pattern>), so agents stop hand-rolling busy loops around run_notebook.ps1", Story, P2, D3, 20u32, Active, "", "eoie,jobs,cells,friction", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_job_cells_gate () = Planning ("EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_job_cells_gate_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T13:52Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:52 x: friction measured today. To keep my Cells checks off other teams' runs I wrote three ad-hoc gates: cells_when_free.ps1 (poll every 60 s, give up after N min), cells_chain.ps1 (poll, retry on refusal, and a named mutex after two of my own chains started in the same second at 09:18:40-41), and cells_check.ps1, which refuses with exit 75 when a run_notebook.ps1 outside its own process tree is alive (the 07:52 overlap with eoie's notebook check is why). eoie job start --compile does not know about Cells runs started outside eoie jobs (tibia's and main's run_notebook calls), so a queued job either overlaps them or fails with 75 and stops the --after-ok chain. Wanted: job start waits in its queue while a run_notebook.ps1 / spiral.notebook process not in its own tree is alive (re-checked on each slot grant), shows 'waiting: foreign Cells run pid N (label)' in job status and machine watch, and has a --slot-within deadline as today. Then every agent's Cells check is one 'eoie job start --compile' call, and these scripts are deleted.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_cells_gate_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T15:57Z", "x", "2026-10-23 11:57 x: measured cost today. I queued 21 compile-class jobs right after tibia's 11:22 block end, each running a Cells notebook through a foreign-run refusal (exit 75). eoie admitted them while other teams' run_notebook.ps1 were alive (eoie's own f6 scratch notebooks among them), so 19 of 21 finished as exit 75 within ~30 min without testing anything. Only the non-Cells xc compile and one early prelude run did real work. Workaround now: a detached self-gating chain (poll until no foreign runner, a named mutex, retry on 75), i.e. the hand-rolled gate this item asks eoie to own. With the requested wait-in-queue behaviour, the 21 jobs would have run in sequence with no resubmission.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_cells_gate_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-CELLS-GATE", 3u32, "2026-10-23T18:21Z", "eoie", "14:21 eoie: design chosen and pure model drafted (scratch f6/src/eoie_wait_free; typecheck queued behind main's matrix). Of the item's two options, a box-wide exclusive 'cells' window class would serialize every team's notebook runs, which is a policy change for main and tibia. '--wait-free <pattern,...>' is opt-in and generic, so it comes first: job start writes the patterns to the job file; the supervisor's gate samples processes (eoie_machine_watch's sysinfo rows) every 5 s and stays queued while a process OUTSIDE the job's own tree has a command line containing any pattern; job status shows 'waiting free of a foreign run pid=N (command)' (with EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR's waiting_for); --slot-within keeps its deadline. Model: wait_free_state_of (rows, own root pid, patterns) -> MachineFree | ForeignRun (pid, command), with tree membership by bounded parent walk; tests: a foreign run_notebook.ps1 holds the job, the job's own run never does, comma pattern lists, and empty patterns never wait. Then x's three scripts become 'eoie job start --compile --wait-free run_notebook.ps1,spiral.notebook <label> ...'. Placement: the flag goes into K's typed start-option fold, so this lands after K. Today's cost, from x: 19 of 21 queued Cells jobs ended exit 75 without testing anything.")
inl eoie_job_rechain () = Task ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", "one-call re-chaining of queued jobs: eoie job after <queued-id> <predecessor-id> [--ok] re-points a queued job's --after (label, timeout, gate and command kept; refused for a running job), and eoie job chain <after-id> -- <label> <timeout> <program> [args] -- <label> ... starts N jobs each after the previous one and prints every id", Story, P2, D3, 50u32, Active, "", "eoie,jobs", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_job_rechain () = Planning ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_job_rechain_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", 1u32, "2026-10-23T14:13Z", "eoie", "10:13 from main (10:1x): re-chaining queued jobs costs a stop and a fresh start per job, plus relaunching every job behind it; done 7-8 times on 10-23 (around HP, tibia's block, serializing a chain), each time retyping program and args and re-reading new ids. Builds on job start --after/--after-ok (EOIE-JOBS note 31). Rule: every verb one call (EOIE-ONE-CALL). Shape to decide when drafting: re-point by editing the queued job's after file under the jobs home vs stop+restart with the same command record; the first keeps the id, which is what callers hold.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_rechain_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:34Z", "eoie", "10:34 eoie: part 1 drafted, scratch job-rechain/apply-job-rechain.ps1 (dry run clean on copies). eoie job after <queued-id> <predecessor-id> [--ok] rewrites the waiting job's after file in place, so the id main holds stays valid. Refusals in a fixed order: unknown job, unknown predecessor, self, not started with --after, no longer waiting, and a cycle (the predecessor's own after chain, walked up to 64 steps, leads back to the job). The waiter (eoie_job_after) re-read nothing before: it read the after file once. It now re-reads every 250 ms beat, and the ok-mode decision uses the predecessor that actually settled; the file is migrated off its two host macros onto lib split/format (AGENTS.md: touching a legacy function migrates it). Model: typed facts record -> rechain_outcome union (HostFree crate eoie_job_rechain, model tests). CLI test in stop_cli: re-point a job from a 20 s predecessor to a 0.5 s one with --ok, finishes within 15 s while the old predecessor still runs, plus the cycle, not-chained, unknown and settled refusals. Part 2 (job chain) not drafted yet. Checks wait for tibia's block.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_rechain_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", 3u32, "2026-10-23T14:38Z", "eoie", "10:38 eoie: part 2 drafted in the same slice. eoie job chain [--ok] [<after-id>] -- <label> <timeout-ms> <program> [args...] -- ... parses into a typed chain_plan (head: optional --ok and starting job id; links split at --, each needs label, timeout, program) and starts each link as job start --after(-ok) <previous id> through the running binary (lib gap env.current_executable added to lib-gaps), so the launch logic stays in one place and --heavy/--compile inside a link keep working. It prints each start receipt, then 'eoie job chain ok jobs=N ids=a,b', and stops at the first link that does not start, naming the ids started before it. Model test covers head, links, short link, refusals and id parsing; CLI test chains two jobs with --ok and waits on the second. Checks queued with the other f6 drafts for after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_eoie_job_rechain_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOB-RECHAIN", 4u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): JOB-2 (chains that stop on the first failing prerequisite; per-stage receipts go to EOIE-BUILD-PHASES). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl lib_listm_runtime_paths () = Task ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", "lib/spiral listm': seq.memoize calls the static try_find on a runtime list (should be try_find_); map4 has no runtime variant; optionally give the static/runtime pairs exists'-style auto-switching so the wrong pick can't happen", Story, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,spiral,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths () = Planning ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_1 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T14:18Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:18 x: found by a read-only scan of listm'.spi (inl rec bodies without var_is): map4 (line 31), rec (49), try_index_of (122), try_find (174), zip_with (226). exists' (334) shows the pattern: inl rec body ... and inl loop list = if var_is list |> not then body f list else join body (join f) (dyn list). Over static lists the bare inl rec folds away at compile time; over a runtime list the partial evaluator unrolls without end (derived from the code, the same failure mode as x's scheduler_parity 300 s timeout this morning). Callers in spiral lib/apps, eoie/src, x/apps, dice, alphabet and tibia/src: only seq.memoize (seq.spi:259) calls listm'.try_find, on *state, a runtime list. seq.memoize has no test (seq's test at :276 exercises memoize_) and no caller (lib uses stream.memoize), so it is latently broken, not failing. Draft of the fixed try_find with a runtime-list test: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\find_runtime.spi (try_find_any, queued for Cells rust + gleam). X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE note 9 needs the try_find fix before x's first_where can move to lib.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_2 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:22Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:22 x: CORRECTION to note 1, which I filed before I knew lib's convention. A trailing underscore names the RUNTIME variant: try_find_ (line 182), try_index_of_ (133) and zip_with_ (234) are let rec loops, and try_item has a sibling too, so the plain inl rec names are static by design, not broken. What remains: (1) seq.memoize (seq.spi:259) uses the static try_find on *state, a runtime list: a one-character fix to try_find_ (latent: no test or caller); (2) map4 has no runtime variant; (3) optional, the real foot-gun: a caller has to know the convention, and exists' shows the alternative (one name, var_is -> join). The find_runtime.spi draft (try_find_any) is that auto-switching form, if you want it. Priority stays P3 (latent only).")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_3 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T14:33Z", "x", "2026-10-23 10:33 x: one-step patch for main: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_listm_runtime.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>, with anchored edits (each must match exactly once). (1) seq.spi: memoize and memoize_ were identical except try_find vs try_find_. Since state is a mut cell, memoize can never have a static use, so the patch removes its body and adds 'inl memoize seq = memoize_ seq' after memoize_: one implementation, name kept. (2) listm'.spi: map4_ (let rec), the runtime sibling of map4, following the _ convention. Dry-applied to copies of seq.spi and listm'.spi: both edits apply and read correctly. Not compiled (lib edits are yours to gate); the seq memoize test already exercises memoize_.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_4 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 4u32, "2026-10-23T15:50Z", "main", "2026-10-23 11:49 main: x's apply_listm_runtime.ps1 applied unchanged to main's lib land copy (%TEMP%\\lib-land-1023\\lib\\spiral; originals in pre-listm-backup), plus a runtime test for the new sibling: test map4_ sums four dyn lists [1;2;3] to [4;8;12]. Rides today's lib gate and landing (serial chain after next4 review-2 and the 5f build): gate.ps1 now runs seq.spi and listm'.spi too, and land.ps1 lands each only when its own gate report passes. listm'.spi was also a formatter-restore file, so it is taken out of the ungated restore path and out of the 5f refresh (which would have overwritten the patch with a formatted original); its 5f layout difference, if any, waits like process.spi's. The tree-hashes guard covers both files. Option (3), exists'-style auto-switching, not taken: it changes every static caller's residual, a separate measured item if wanted.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_5 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 5u32, "2026-10-23T20:04Z", "main", "2026-10-23 16:04 main: LANDED 16:02 in spiral/lib/spiral: seq.spi (memoize delegates to memoize_, gate 26/26) and listm'.spi (map4_ runtime sibling + its dyn-list test, gate 23/23), landed hashes seq 1C930A681DE388AA, listm' 1D764CD9C6598857. Closed: (1) and (2) are in; (3), exists'-style auto-switching, stays not taken (it would change every static caller's residual; a separate measured item if wanted).")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_6 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 6u32, "2026-10-23T20:33Z", "x", "2026-10-23 16:33 x: main reports apply_listm_runtime.ps1 LANDED in spiral/lib/spiral at 16:02 (seq.memoize delegates to memoize_; listm'.map4_ added). Left on this item: only the optional auto-switching try_find (find_runtime.spi draft), at main's discretion.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_7 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 7u32, "2026-10-23T21:57Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:57 main: x handed off the draft find_runtime.spi + apply_listm_runtime.ps1 (auto try_find, memoize -> memoize_, map4_; 2/2 PASS) in C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft for main to land. Landing rule: Cells gate on the tree-lib baseline subset rule, after the tonight chain; rebase over eoie's 17:56 lib-gaps land (sm', am', process, testing, env changed) first, since x's lib_gaps.spi also touches listm'/sm'.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_8 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 8u32, "2026-10-23T22:43Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:43 main: x's apply_listm_runtime.ps1 is already in the tree: seq.memoize delegates to memoize_ (seq.spi:266) and listm'.map4_ exists (listm'.spi:39). A dry run on copies fails its first anchor for that reason. What is new in find_runtime.spi is the auto-switching find. Instead of a third variant (try_find_any next to try_find/try_find_), listm'.try_find itself now switches: a static list unrolls as before (inl rec body), and a list that var_is reports dynamic goes through a join point over dyn. Draft %TEMP%\\lib-listm-runtime\\apply.ps1 (2 anchors, dry run ok) adds x's two cases as try_find tests on rust and gleam. Gate: lib-gate-kit -Name LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS -Files listm'.spi seq.spi, chain A in requeue-after-block.ps1. Its anchors don't overlap LIB-GAPS-X's listm' edits, so both land in either order.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_9 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 9u32, "2026-10-23T23:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:08 main: correction to note 8. listm' naming convention: a plain name is the static inl rec form, a trailing underscore the runtime let rec loop, and a prime the var_is auto-switch (exists' at listm'.spi:355). So plain try_find stays as it is, and x's auto-switching find lands as a new try_find' with exists' shape, added after the try_find test. Tests are try_find_prime_searches_a_runtime_list_at_run_time and try_find_prime_folds_a_static_list: lib test names have no primes, and Cells matches '^test \\w+'. The apply script is rewritten (additive, dry run ok).")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_10 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 10u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: gate GREEN at 23:05 (seq.spi 26/26, 42 rows, red 0) on a lib copy that already held LIB-GAPS-X (take_ landed 23:00) and LIB-UNICODE; its land (try_find_prime) is running in the requeued chain.")
inl note_of_lib_listm_runtime_paths_11 () = Note ("LIB-LISTM-RUNTIME-PATHS", 11u32, "2026-10-24T03:12Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:12 main: LANDED in live lib/spiral (job main-lib-listm-runtime-land, exit 0) after its 23:05 GREEN gate on a lib copy holding gaps-x and unicode. try_find' is in listm'.spi (line 210, line 211), shaped like exists'.")
inl lua_single_scrutinee () = Task ("LUA-SINGLE-SCRUTINEE", "Lua union matches read a single scrutinee directly instead of wrapping it in a fresh table per match (557 of 559 __v tables over the tree main.lua)", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,lua,codegen,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lua_single_scrutinee () = Planning ("LUA-SINGLE-SCRUTINEE", Minutes 30u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lua_single_scrutinee_1 () = Note ("LUA-SINGLE-SCRUTINEE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T14:28Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:28 main: codegenLua's TyUnionUnbox emitted 'local __v = { vI }' and read __v[1] in every guard and payload, so each match allocated a table (a GC cost on Lua 5.1/WoW hot paths) and used one of the 200 local slots. Census: 559 such tables over the tree main.lua, 557 of them with one scrutinee. Staged %TEMP%\\pack-next8\\lua-single-scrutinee.ps1 (3 anchors, each matching once): with one scrutinee the guard reads 'vI ~= nil and vI.tag == ...' and the payload 'local vJ = vI._k', and only several scrutinees keep the table. Guards are evaluated in order before any body runs, so reading vI directly is equivalent. It parses, and is composed into pack-next8 (pack-next8 core A2FAD36DFC8E7141). Review rule: Lua residuals lose exactly those table lines and __v[1] reads, and every Lua row agrees with C.")
inl note_of_lua_single_scrutinee_2 () = Note ("LUA-SINGLE-SCRUTINEE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:17 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 60 by the rule 'implemented and staged in a pack or queued gate, awaiting review'. Where: pack-next8 patch, reviewed after next7.")
inl note_of_lua_single_scrutinee_3 () = Note ("LUA-SINGLE-SCRUTINEE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T07:27Z", "main", "10-25 03:27 main: DONE by its rule. The single-scrutinee arm ('let scrutinee (idx : int)', with 'local __v' only for several scrutinees) is in the installed tree apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs and in the 5k core: re-running pack-next8\\lua-single-scrutinee.ps1 refuses with 'already applied'. Every pack since has carried it through review with oracle DISAGREE 0, including 5j's accept today (native 6833 ran, Lua rows agree), so 'every Lua row agrees with C' holds. Still uncommitted in spiral; the user commits.")
inl c_nullary_case_singleton () = Task ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", "C heap unions build each nullary case (UH0_Nil()) by malloc + refcount instead of sharing one static immortal object", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "compiler,c,codegen,performance,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_c_nullary_case_singleton () = Planning ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", Minutes 120u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_1 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 1u32, "2026-10-23T14:30Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:30 main: found reading samples/native_closure_union_rec/main.c. UH0 * UH0_Nil() { malloc; tag = 1; refc = 1 }, so every list end and every payload-free case pays a malloc and a free through UHDecref0. A census of the tree main.c files finds 46 nullary heap-union constructors in 19 files. The design must keep the decref path branch-light: (a) a static object per nullary case with an immortal refcount, where UHDecref0 never frees an object whose refc is above a sentinel bound, so the static's refc starts at INT32_MAX / 2 and increments wrap safely below the sentinel; or (b) the tag lives in the pointer for nullary cases (a tagged-pointer null per case), which changes every switch on x->tag. Rule, written before measuring: pick (a) unless the benchmark shows decref overhead above 2% on a refcount-heavy sample (bench-pair -Native on mega_brzozowski_derivatives runtime_native and native_closure_union_rec, 5 rounds, QuietCores 2); adopt only if allocation-heavy samples speed up by 5% or more and every C row stays bit-identical in exit and stdout. Same question for Zig, which allocates per box.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_2 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 2u32, "2026-10-23T14:58Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:58 main: option (a) drafted, %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\c-nullary-singleton.ps1 (1 anchor in union_tmpl's heap constructor), composed as pack-next9 on next8 (3957189B) -> core 86448170CAD44C75, hopacsota lint G1/G2 pass. A nullary UH case emits 'static UHi x = { .refc = INT32_MAX/2, .tag = t }; return &x;'. No decref sentinel is needed: C refcounts are single-threaded and balanced, so the static's count cannot reach 0; the code read found no refc == 1 reuse path and free only inside UHDecref. Pending (CPU, after tibia's block): fsc --parseonly, then the pre-written rule from the 10:30 note (bench-pair -Native on mega_brzozowski_derivatives and native_closure_union_rec, 5 rounds, QuietCores 2; adopt on >=5% on allocation-heavy samples with every C row bit-identical). Zig question still open.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_3 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 3u32, "2026-10-23T23:42Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:42 main: mirror survey across backends on samples/native_closure_union_rec. Rust already shares nullary cases (a thread_local Rc, cloned), and F#, Lean, Gleam and Python use plain constructors or values. Swift's case is a struct, so no allocation. Kotlin (class UH0_Nil instantiated per use) and Lua (function Uh0_Nil() return { tag = ... } end, a new table per call) allocate per nullary case like C did. Mirror drafted as pack-next10 on next9: %TEMP%\\pack-next10\\nullary-mirrors.ps1 has 4 anchors, each matching once on the tree and next9. Kotlin: object instead of class, and the zero and box sites name the object without (). Lua: the heap case becomes NAME = (function() local shared = {...} return function() return shared end end)(), so no new chunk-level local, staying under Lua 5.1's 200-local limit, and the call sites are unchanged. Lua stack unions (Us) and TypeScript are left as they are: whether anything mutates or compares those tables or objects by identity is unverified. Review rule (next10_rules.py, checked with positive and negative controls): a changed main.kt or main.lua must equal its snapshot with exactly these rewrites applied, no other file changes, and native results and DISAGREE 0 as baseline. Compose and review wait for the next9 review.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_4 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 4u32, "2026-10-23T23:48Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:48 main: Wasm added to the pack-next10 mirror. Its bump allocator never frees, so a nullary case allocated per use (alloc 8, store the tag) was also an unbounded leak in loops. Now each distinct tag gets one 8-byte static data segment [tag][pad], interned like string literals at data_end before heap_start is computed. Objects with the same tag are byte-identical and immutable, so tag alone is the key across unions. Boxing a nullary case returns (i32.const at). The patch has 6 anchors (Kotlin 3, Lua 1, Wasm 2), each matching once on the tree. The patched F# was read on a scratch copy: the arm is if/else with the allocation path under else, and nullary_object sits after intern. The rule for main.wat is that allocation calls must not increase, with the matrix's native check (DISAGREE 0) deciding.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_5 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 5u32, "2026-10-23T23:51Z", "main", "2026-10-23 19:51 main: Delphi added to pack-next10 (7 anchors now). Its nullary heap constructor did Result := TUH0.Create per call, and none of the 412 generated main.pas files frees anything, so every Nil leaked an object. Now: var <case>_shared: TUHn = nil; plus a constructor that creates it on first use and returns it. FPC/Delphi allow an initialized global var between routines. Stack unions (TUS, records) are unchanged. next10_rules.py predicts the main.pas rewrite exactly and was checked on the real sample text (1 file within the rule, 0 violations). Order: next10's review compares against the installed outputs, so it runs only after next9 is reviewed and installed; review.ps1 and compose.ps1 are in %TEMP%\\pack-next10.")
inl note_of_c_nullary_case_singleton_6 () = Note ("C-NULLARY-CASE-SINGLETON", 6u32, "2026-10-24T00:00Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:00 main: TypeScript added to pack-next10 (8 anchors). It was held back on an identity risk, now settled by a code read: TS's EQ lowering (a === b, core 174081) serves primitives only. Union equality reaches codegen as a multi-scrutinee unbox comparing tags (v{a}.tag === v{b}.tag at 173971), so no union object is ever compared by identity. A nullary case now emits const <case>_shared: U = { tag: i }; and the function returns it; the types are readonly already. next10_rules.py predicts the main.ts rewrite and was checked on the real sample (1 file within the rule). Python is skipped on purpose: a fieldless NamedTuple per call is cheap, and Python is the slowest target anyway. Swift's nullary case is a struct (no allocation), and Rust already shares a thread_local Rc.")
inl x_per_account_min_1 () = Task ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", "x client: xc timeline --per-account accepts 1..100 (minimum 1 instead of 5); below 5 it still asks X for 5 (the API floor) and keeps only the newest N per account", Story, P1, D2, 85u32, Active, "", "x,client,cli", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_per_account_min_1 () = Planning ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_1 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 1u32, "2026-10-23T16:22Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:22 x: user request 2026-10-23 ~11:58: 'make --per-account have a minimum of 1 instead of 5'. Today cli.spi validates 5..100 (usage line :81 '5..100 (default 5)'; the test at :422 per_account_below_the_api_minimum_is_rejected expects 'rejected: --per-account expects a number from 5 to 100, got 4'), because X's GET /2/users/:id/tweets takes max_results 5..100, and args.spi:38 passes per_account straight through as max_results. DESIGN: (1) cli accepts 1..100, with the usage line and rejection text saying 1 to 100; (2) the request asks for max(5, N) (a wire-side constant named for the API floor); (3) after fetching, each account's posts are cut to its newest N before merging, so --limit and --per-author keep their meaning; (4) tests: the cli test pins 1 accepted and 0 rejected, and a unit test checks the per-account cut (N=1 out of 5 fetched keeps the newest). e2e gets one tutorial step, 'xc timeline --per-account 1 alice bob', showing one post each. COST NOTE for the README line: below 5, X still bills the 5 posts it returns, so --per-account 1 trims what is shown but doesn't cut paid reads (X-READ-BUDGET's cost line would show it). Lands after X-DONE with the other client drafts; the draft and verification go on a scratch copy while the tree is staged.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_2 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 2u32, "2026-10-23T16:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 12:30 x: drafted as C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next\\drafts\\apply_per_account_min_1.ps1 (in land_all and apply_all_check: 12 of 12 drafts apply together, no new name clashes). cli: range 1..100, the usage line says 'posts shown per account, 1..100 (default 5; X sends at least 5)', and the test now rejects 0 and accepts 1 ('accounts alice per=1 ...'). wire: requested_posts n = max(timeline_floor () = 5, n) in posts_path, plus a test that n=1 asks for max_results=5. fetch: each primary entry carries 'keep' = N, and refs are taken only from the kept posts, so a smaller N also means fewer reference lookups. digest: kept_posts cuts each account's primary posts to the first N (X and the e2e stub both return newest first; the stub sorts by created_at descending), and a test checks keep '1' -> 1 of 2, '' -> all. Negative control already in hand: today's stub answers 400 to max_results < 5, so without the floor the e2e step would fail. Cells on cli, wire, fetch, digest queued (self-gating chain8).")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_3 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 3u32, "2026-10-23T17:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:27 x: Cells on the scratch copy (C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-permin\\client, x tree + this draft): cli.spi 18/18 PASS, including per_account_takes_one_and_rejects_zero ('0' rejected as 1 to 100, '1' accepted as per=1), and wire.spi 23/23 PASS, including posts_below_the_api_floor_ask_for_five (max_results=5 for N=1). fetch and digest (the keep cut) are next in the same chain.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_4 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 4u32, "2026-10-23T17:36Z", "x", "2026-10-23 13:36 x: VERIFIED on the scratch copy: fetch.spi PASS and digest.spi 2/2 PASS (keep_cuts_each_account_to_its_newest: keep '1' gives 1 of 2, '' gives all), alongside cli 18/18 and wire 23/23. Also compiled with all 14 drafts applied together (xc.spi -> 831 lines of Rust, under the 1,000-line gate). Left: landing after the user's commit, then e2e -Accept for the README step (covered by the queued x-landing-trial).")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_5 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 5u32, "2026-10-24T03:10Z", "x", "23:10 x grooming: Active -> Blocked, by the same rule as X-CLIENT (10-21), so the stop gate's ready list stops offering it. Its own n4 says all that's left is landing after the user's commit, then e2e -Accept. Unblock when the user commits x.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_6 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 6u32, "2026-10-24T07:24Z", "x", "10-24 03:24 x: LANDED in C:\\home\\git\\x's working tree (unstaged layer over the user's staged commit; X-DONE n74). Left: run8 on the real tree after tibia's block (N8), then the user commits.")
inl note_of_x_per_account_min_1_7 () = Note ("X-PER-ACCOUNT-MIN-1", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:19Z", "x", "10-24 07:19 x: in HEAD 6ef9c48 (user commit 05:20). x-verify-landing2 (07:08) compiled all 16 crates and passed the line gate, then failed only on x-compat. The 3 findings came from my prelude step 1 and the heal: autolinks listm.map/resultm.map in prelude.spi, and wire.spi replied_to_in at weight 290 > 271. All 3 were fixed unstaged at 07:05, and x-compat now finds 0 in 36 files. Done = job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3 exits 0 (regen, gate, x-compat, release build, e2e -Accept, scripts/build.ps1).")
inl eoie_queued_jobs_in_host () = Task ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", "queued jobs wait as job files held by the host, not as one resident supervisor each; the supervisor starts when the job's after-chain clears and its gate grants", Story, P0, D4, 60u32, Blocked, "EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN", "eoie,jobs,process", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host () = Planning ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_1 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 1u32, "2026-10-23T16:30Z", "eoie", "12:30 eoie, from the user's 12:2x question ('a shit ton of eoie processes, is our product stable?'). Census 12:25 (scratch eoie-process-census.ps1): 26 eoie processes = 2 hosts (the pre-install one draining) + 18 supervisors + 6 job waits; 12 of the 18 supervisors serve QUEUED jobs (main's 9-job serial chain, my GE2 chain, a raw gate run, a typecheck), each polling its after file every 250 ms or its gate every 5 s. Measured over 10 s: all 25 used 672 ms CPU (0.84% of the box) and 161 MB. No leak: every waiter and supervisor targets a live job, every after-chain points at a live job, and the only dead parent is the draining host. Design: the host already runs; it can hold the queue (after-waits and gate polls in one loop) and spawn a job's supervisor at grant, so idle queued jobs cost no process. Rule before building: adopt it if a 12-job queue drops eoie processes by 12 and CPU by at least half, with the stop_cli, after and windows CLI tests unchanged.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_2 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 2u32, "2026-10-23T17:05Z", "eoie", "13:05 eoie: decision model drafted and passing (scratch f6/src/eoie_job_queue, HostFree, LibReferenced): a queued_job (stop request, NoChain or Chained predecessor, mode, predecessor state) maps to KeepWaiting | StartSupervisor | SettleStopped | SettleSkipped detail, with the same rules and skip detail as eoie_job_after's after_outcome_of today ('--after-ok: <id> ended exit N'). The host's loop would fold this over its queued job files every beat and spawn a supervisor only on StartSupervisor; a serial chain behind a running job costs 0 processes (test). Gate waits (slot and window) stay in the supervisor for now, since they need a live process holding the ticket. Next: the host loop (eoie_job_host host_run), after release M.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_3 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 3u32, "2026-10-23T17:12Z", "eoie", "13:12 eoie: host design, read from eoie_job_host/host_run.spi (host_served drains request files and starts a supervisor per request; supervisor_requested waits 5 s for the supervisor pid, then falls back to a detached start). (1) On a request whose job has an after file, the host evaluates eoie_job_queue's step_of from the job files: KeepWaiting writes shell-host/held/<id> and starts nothing; StartSupervisor starts the supervisor as today; SettleStopped and SettleSkipped write the job's exit line exactly as eoie_job_after does (143 stopped, 125 skipped with the same detail). (2) Each 500 ms beat the host re-evaluates only shell-host/held/ (no scan of the whole jobs root), so the held set lives in files and survives a host restart or upgrade; the host is not idle while held/ is non-empty. (3) supervisor_requested treats a held marker as taken and returns 'held', so job start prints supervisor_pid=held and never falls back to a detached supervisor for a held job. (4) job stop needs no change: its stop file is read by step_of. eoie_job_after stays for the fallback path and older hosts. Tests: a three-job serial chain shows one supervisor process at a time; stop of a held job settles 143; --after-ok skip detail unchanged; a host restart re-holds from held/. Risk: host_run is what HP fixed today, so this lands only after GE2's acceptance and with HP's 6 checks rerun.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_4 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 4u32, "2026-10-23T20:25Z", "eoie", "16:25 eoie: host loop drafted and typechecked; it ships as release P (after O, before N), its own release because it changes the host every job depends on. Slice: scratch queued-in-host/apply-queued-in-host.ps1 + blocks/queue.spi. eoie_job_host gains queue: step_of over a queued_job read from the job files (after = predecessor TAB mode; predecessor state via job_state_now, so a missing predecessor reads as lost, exactly like eoie_job_after). host_served: a request whose predecessor is live is recorded as <jobs>/held/<id> (its executable) instead of starting a supervisor. Each 500 ms beat, held_served re-evaluates held/: KeepWaiting writes the job's heartbeat_ms, which keeps readers from declaring it lost after 15 s; StartSupervisor starts it; a stop writes 143 and an --after-ok on a failed predecessor writes 125, with eoie_job_after's exact details. Held jobs count as live, so the host does not idle-exit. supervisor_awaited returns SupervisorHeld 'held' (or 'settled'), so job start prints supervisor_pid=held. Notebooks on the post-O tree pass (16:13 and 16:17): queue (4 model tests plus a read-only IO probe), host_run (probe reaching host_run and supervisor_requested), host_model. after-install-P on today's eoie: the held check fails as it must (supervisor_pid=131976, 1 supervisor); run-after-head, skip detail ('--after-ok: <id> ended finished exit 3'), stop 143 ('stopped on request while waiting for <id>') and HP 6/6 pass, and P must keep them passing. Today's details match the model's strings exactly. The train order is now GE2, HIJK, L, M, O, P, N.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_5 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 5u32, "2026-10-23T21:05Z", "eoie", "17:05 eoie: review of P against every verb that touches a queued job, read in the post-O tree. job stop writes the stop file and waits 15 s for a settle (eoie_job_control control.spi:47-53), and the host settles a held job within one 500 ms beat, so it works unchanged. job amend refuses only once a pid file exists, and a held job has none, so the new limit is read when its supervisor starts. job after (M) rewrites the after file, which the host re-reads every beat. job wait and status read state, and the beaten heartbeat keeps the job queued. Known risk, accepted for P with a follow-up before P installs: if the host process dies while jobs are held, nobody beats them and the first reader marks them lost after 15 s, whereas today each queued job's own supervisor would outlive a host crash. Follow-up slice: job status, list and wait call the existing host-start path (supervisor_requested's HostMissing branch) when held/ is non-empty and host.pid is dead, so a reader revives the queue instead of burying it. P's acceptance gains a probe: kill the isolated-home host with a held job, run job status, and the job must still finish.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_6 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 6u32, "2026-10-23T21:07Z", "eoie", "17:07 eoie: enforced mechanically: launch-P refuses until queue.spi carries the revival follow-up (marker held_revived), and P moved to the end of the train (GE2, HIJK, L, M, O, N, P; N rebases on O again, P on N), so P's refusal ends the train after N instead of stranding N. P can't reach install tonight in any case (GE2's regen alone runs past 18:00).")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_7 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 7u32, "2026-10-23T21:26Z", "eoie", "17:26 eoie: the follow-up from note 5 is now in P's slice, so launch-P's gate (held_revived) passes. (1) A held job is never declared lost by silence: in job_state_now's lost branch, a job with a held/<id> file reads Live JobPending, since it has no process that could be lost. This removes the 15 s race in which a reader would bury a held job before a revived host beats it. (2) eoie_job_host exports eoie_job_host_revive (held_revived: idle | running | revived | error). It starts a host through the existing detached path when held/ is non-empty and host.pid is dead, and job status and job wait call it first (eoie_jobs already depends on the eoie-job-host crate, so no cycle). Checks on a tree rebuilt as post-M plus O plus P (17:12 and 17:23): host_run and queue notebooks pass, and the jobs.spi probe reaches job_status and job_waited with only the standalone link error (eoie_job_host crate). after-install-P gains 'held: survives a host crash': kill the isolated home's host while a job is held, wait 20 s (past the old silence limit), run job status, and the job must finish exit 0. A slip caught in my own slice: a here-string without its trailing newline merged two statements into one line (it parsed, but the second swap never ran). Found by checking the dry-run output, not the parse.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_8 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 8u32, "2026-10-24T09:20Z", "eoie", "05:1x eoie, user asked again ('damn we have a lot of eoie processes up, is this drift or intended?'), the second time after 10-23 12:2x. Census: 61 eoie processes = 1 host + 56 supervisors + 3 caller waiters + 1 window run. 8 jobs run; 52 are queued, 34 on --after (release P's held queue removes these) and 18 on a slot or window gate (P keeps those in the supervisor). Each idle supervisor costs ~7 MB plus a ~7.5 MB conhost (stage R removes the conhost): ~800 MB in all on a memory-tight box. Priority raised P2 -> P0 because the user repeated the request; the gate-wait remainder is EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_9 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:22Z", "eoie", "07:2x groom: estimate set from what remains, not from the item text. Release P carries the held queue in tonight's train, behind GE3 -> HIJK -> L -> M -> O -> N -> Q. Left: P's install and its after-install check (a queued --after job shows no supervisor; job stop, amend and after on a held job keep their receipts), about 30 min of check time once P lands.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_in_host_10 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST", 10u32, "2026-10-24T11:23Z", "eoie", "07:23 groom: blocked on the train. The only work left is to install release P and run its after-install check, so 'agile next' must not hand this out as ready work. Back to Active when release P installs.")
inl eoie_jobs_doctor () = Task ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", "eoie jobs doctor: one call that audits every eoie process and job (host liveness and parent, supervisors whose job settled, job waits on settled or missing jobs, after-chains to missing jobs, window rows of dead holders, detached environment vs caller) and exits 1 with one row per finding", Story, P1, D3, 50u32, Active, "", "eoie,jobs,process", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_jobs_doctor () = Planning ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_1 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 1u32, "2026-10-23T16:30Z", "eoie", "12:30 eoie: today's checks were hand-rolled scripts (scratch eoie-process-census.ps1 for the user's 12:2x stability question; the detached 'window check' and 'cmd /c set' probes that found the EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME leak at 10:4x). By the generic-tools rule each method must be an eoie command, not a scratch script. Typed findings: DeadParent pid | SettledButAlive job | WaitOnSettled job | ChainToMissing job | OrphanWindow id | HostEnvDiffers names (the detached environment probe; today it would have shown EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME, CARGO_BUILD_JOBS and a missing EOIE_LEASE_POLICY). The census today: 0 findings except the draining host. Spec examples: today's 10:4x leak (HostEnvDiffers EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME) and 12:25 census.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_2 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 2u32, "2026-10-23T16:38Z", "eoie", "12:38 eoie: pure model drafted and passing (scratch f6/src/eoie_jobs_doctor, HostFree, LibReferenced). role_of reads a command line into JobHost | JobSupervisor job | JobWaiter job | WindowHolder | OtherCall. process_findings: a dead-parent host counts only when it serves no job (IdleOrphanHost), because a host draining after an install is by design; a supervisor of a settled job is SettledButSupervised, and a waiter on a settled or missing job is WaitOnSettled/WaitOnMissing. chain_findings: ChainToMissing. drift compares a detached job's environment with the caller's over the steering prefixes (EOIE_, SPIRAL_, CARGO_, RUSTUP_; EOIE_JOB_* excepted) and reads today's leak as +EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME +CARGO_BUILD_JOBS -EOIE_LEASE_POLICY. Tests: the 12:25 census (draining host, no finding), an idle orphan host, stale waiters, roles, chains, drift. Next: the verb crate, with snapshots through eoie_machine_watch's sysinfo rows, job state from the job files, and the detached env probe as a 'cmd /c set' job.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_3 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 3u32, "2026-10-23T16:59Z", "eoie", "12:59 eoie: verb drafted as 'eoie job doctor [--env]' (scratch job-doctor/apply-job-doctor.ps1; crate eoie_jobs_doctor: model + check + entry). check.spi reads the eoie processes through eoie_machine_watch's sysinfo snapshot (role and parent liveness), job standing from the job files, and the after-chains from every job folder. --env runs the same 'cmd /c set' (or 'env') attached and as a detached job and diffs the variable names, which avoids parsing the NUL-separated snapshot. Notebooks: model and check pass on f6 with the real legacy packages copied in. The slice's anchors are order-independent with job-rechain (both orders tried: union tail JobStop, JobAfter, JobChain, JobDoctor, JobUnknown); job-rechain's case anchor was made order-independent for this. In release M: 8 candidate notebooks, baseline merge, and acceptance 'job doctor --env' must print ok, which proves the env-leak class fixed end to end once GE2's caller environment is in.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_4 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 4u32, "2026-10-23T17:25Z", "eoie", "13:25 eoie: companion slice from the same question (scratch waiting-for/apply-waiting-for.ps1, in release M): job status/list/wait print waiting_for=after:<id> | window:<class> | heavy-slot after state=queued. A queued job no longer looks the same whether it is stuck or correctly waiting on its predecessor. gate_queued became a typed gate_wait union over the same job files (lib sm' calls instead of rust_std_string); the stop_cli after test asserts the predecessor. eoie_jobs model notebook and the doctor's check notebook pass with it; all four M slices applied together on a src copy (union and routes consistent). after-install-M asserts 'state=queued waiting_for=after:<slow id>' for a chained job.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_5 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 5u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): TOOL-4 (helper and daemon lifecycle: stale compiler servers reaped by identity, with a leak receipt). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_6 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 6u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "eoie", "23:06 groom: add the unwatched-jobs check from the 22:14-22:45 audit (EOIE-JOBS note 49). A live job is unwatched when no job wait/run process waits on it or on any successor reachable through after links. Print the sample time. Also flag supervisors whose conhost exists (until EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE ships) and hosts anchored outside their owner session (until EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT).")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_7 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 7u32, "2026-10-24T05:59Z", "eoie", "01:59 case from main (01:5x): a Spiral compiler server (dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --server-tcp ...spiral-cells-<erlpid>-<n>.sock --stall-seconds 120) outlived its parent and its erl. The pid in the socket name no longer existed, and it sat at 0 CPU for 25+ min despite --stall-seconds 120 (it later exited by itself). Doctor should flag compiler servers whose socket's erl pid is dead and that have idled past their own stall limit, suggest 'quit' on the socket, and attribute them by start time against the job registry.")
inl note_of_eoie_jobs_doctor_8 () = Note ("EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR", 8u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 60m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 50%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl x_format_client () = Task ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", "x: format apps/client and apps/sim's Spiral with the canonical formatter after the drafts land, so eoie standards' line-width 120 passes without an x override", Story, P3, D2, 0u32, Planned, "X-DONE,X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", "x,format,standards", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_format_client () = Planning ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_format_client_1 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 1u32, "2026-10-23T18:21Z", "x", "2026-10-23 14:21 x: decision on eoie's question (EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS note 4 follow-up): x declares NO LineWidth override. 120 is the formatter's own limit (memory formatter-keep-breaks: it re-lays only what exceeds 120), so x's 136 over-wide client lines are unformatted code, not a different standard. ORDER: after land_all.ps1 -Go, because the 14 drafts are anchored on today's exact text and formatting first would break their anchors (the reformat-peer-repos lesson, applied to my own patch chain). Steps: SpiralCompiler format on each .spi (copy first, read 3 files by hand), byte-compare the generated Rust and Gleam before and after (tibia did that for 15 outputs), then Cells and eoie standards (expect 0 line-width findings, and 0 test-targets findings once eoie's release M ships).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_2 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T00:43Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:43 x: scope update from the 10-23 evening drafts, so the formatting pass covers everything that lands. Post-land scripts touch apps/client args/args.spi, xc/xc.spi, write/write.spi (+ README), wire.spi, thread/thread.spi, paging.spi, prelude.spi. Lib-dependent delegations touch the same files. apply_sim_modules adds 17 sim modules (inbox ... xsim_shell_entry) and apply_xsim_crate adds xsim/xsim.spi. Order: format only after all of them land and pass, then x-compat (XC005) on the formatted cells, because re-laying can move cell weights. Rules from memory formatter-keep-breaks: never join authored lines, re-lay only lines over 120, and apply to unstaged files only.")
inl note_of_x_format_client_3 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "x", "23:08 x grooming: main installed a formatter-only compiler pack at 22:18 (B553EF6B78E6EE29, single-flight): no parens around join heads, 'then' placement follows each file's habit, deep-indent room max 40. Run this item with that compiler (or newer), and compare against a format of the same tree with it, not against earlier 10-23 formats. Still waits for the user to land x's queued drafts.")
inl note_of_x_format_client_4 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 4u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "x", "10-24 19:45 x: grooming: the condition 'after the drafts land' is MET (HEAD 6ef9c48, user commit 05:20). What's left is the unstaged fix layer: x-compat fixes, chain_json, e2e expectations, plural, and the frames imports. Start when verify-landing5 passes and the user commits that layer. Its anchors are the last chain this pass could break. Use main's newest formatter pack (n3).")
inl note_of_x_format_client_5 () = Note ("X-FORMAT-CLIENT", 5u32, "2026-10-25T03:53Z", "x", "10-24 23:53 x: measured input for this item. The installed formatter (10:08 build) on x's edition-3 text changes 40 of 44 files and makes 28 cells overweight, mostly records exploded to one field per line and long signatures re-laid with deep indentation. So the format pass here must come with per-cell splits until x-compat and postable are both 0, plus a check that no authored line is joined (0 files lost lines in the measured run).")
inl rust_fnmut_capture_move () = Task ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", "Rust codegen: a vec_fold' closure that reuses its seed literal moves a captured variable (E0507 cannot move out of v4, a captured variable in an FnMut closure); regression since 10-22 13:50 (eoie EOIE-LOG-SEQUENCES note 20)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 60u32, Active, "", "compiler,rust,codegen,regression,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move () = Planning ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_1 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 1u32, "2026-10-23T19:38Z", "main", "2026-10-23 15:38 main: filed from eoie's report with its repro (session scratch fc1568a6\\f6\\src\\fold_capture_repro\\repro.spi: two rust -d regex tests, named_group/literal_group, both E0507). Hypothesis to test first: the seed '' and the '' inside the closure share one CSE'd variable, which the FnMut closure captures and then moves into optionm'.default_value' instead of cloning. Bisect against the cores installed since 10-22 13:50 (pack-after-night 25D2BE7C is the tree core now); the fix gets the one regression sample (the literal_group shape).")
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_2 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 2u32, "2026-10-23T21:35Z", "main", "2026-10-23 17:35 main: narrowing by code read (no build): (1) the E0507 closure is not a Spiral closure. codegenRust emits those as Rc<dyn Fn> with captured variables kept live (closure: live_binds (fun i -> captured.Contains i)), so the FnMut must be the Rust closure written inside lib am'.vec_fold''s macro arm, where a spliced variable is opaque to liveness. (2) live_binds itself is old (git log -S: 76f3989c, 10-06), so it is not the regression. The window is 10-22 13:50 to now, i.e. the pack-after-night install (tree core 25D2BE7C, now 19F5F752 with only the formatter changed since). Bisect next round: compile eoie's repro with the next3 and pack-after-night candidate DLLs (both still in spiral-bin/candidates) and diff the vec_fold' call site's Rust; a plain listm.fold version of the shape compiles fine (scratch fnmut probe), so the macro arm is the place.")
inl note_of_rust_fnmut_capture_move_3 () = Note ("RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T06:23Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:2x main: ROOT CAUSE found, and it is lib, not the compiler. A probe package with eoie's literal_group shape, compiled to Rust by night-release (10-21), pack-after-night and the installed compiler, gives byte-identical output (scratch fnmut-bisect), so no compiler change in the window caused it. The generated fold closure reads `let mut v14: Rc<str> = v13.unwrap_or(v3);`: v3 is the fold's seed literal (the inner \"\" default is the same CSE'd value), captured by the FnMut closure, and lib optionm'.default_value''s Rust arm `!x.unwrap_or(!value)` moves it (E0507). Note 1's CSE hypothesis holds. Fix (%TEMP%\\lib-default-clone\\apply.ps1, dry run clean): the Rust arm becomes `!x.unwrap_or_else(|| !value.clone())` (a lazy clone; every Spiral Rust value is Clone), plus the regression test default_value_inside_a_fold_closure_keeps_the_captured_seed in sm'.spi (rust -d regex, eoie's shape). Gate and land after tibia's PR-27 block. It likely surfaced on 10-22 because a lib change began CSE-sharing the literal, not because the arm changed.")
inl rust_emit_unit_arg () = Task ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", "A Rust emit snippet given a unit argument keeps its literal $0, so rustc fails (Cells: a print with no value)", Story, P2, D2, 40u32, Active, "", "compiler,rust,cells,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_emit_unit_arg () = Planning ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_1 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 1u32, "2026-10-23T22:19Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:19 main: the Cells live test 'a print with no value still runs' fails on the tree and in every pack (CELLS-PACK-NEXT notes 11, 12, 16): rustc rejects SpiralKinoShow(&$0). Root of the literal: inlineRustEmits (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs:163964 splitArgs) maps an args text of '()' to no arguments, and the $k evaluator (164012) keeps m.Value when k >= args.Length, so a unit argument leaves $0 as written. Fix A (compiler): splitArgs returns ['()'] for '()', so $0 becomes () (valid Rust; other $k unchanged). Fix A alone does not pass the test: Cells' split_output (apps/cells/spi/cell.spi:226) would read 'SPIRAL_CELLS_VALUE:()' as a value, and the test expects none. Open question B: why does a unit cell reach show_value_main at all? cells.ex compile/12 tries Cell.unit_result? (append 0i32) before value_result? (show_value_main), and unit_pair accepts Got () / Expected i32 with padding skipped. So either the error text no longer matches (restore_type_lines?), or the wrap already emits a show_value main. Rule: B is decided by one probe run after tibia's block (that cell's compile output with attempt numbers). If unit_result? misses, fix its match. In both cases also land A, since any emit snippet given a unit argument hits it.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_2 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 2u32, "2026-10-23T22:24Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:24 main: Fix A staged in pack-next9. Patch: %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\rust-emit-unit-arg.ps1 (splitArgs '()' -> ['()']; anchors once on both the tree and next8). compose.ps1 runs it after gadt-index-cap, and next9 now composes to 5124A93187EEF889 (lint G1/G2 clean). Regression sample samples/rust_emit_unit_arg is staged in %TEMP%\\pack-next9\\samples, not in the tree, so review-3 (next4 composite, no fix) never sees it. next9's review.ps1 copies it in after the snapshot (remove it if next9 is rejected) and prints its row: Rust must pass with 42, i.e. a unit's size_of_val is 0. next9_rules.py now accepts a main.rs change only when the old text with $0 replaced by () equals the new.")
inl note_of_rust_emit_unit_arg_3 () = Note ("RUST-EMIT-UNIT-ARG", 3u32, "2026-10-24T23:57Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:57 main groom: still the only non-lib red in the Cells suite on the tree (cells-suite-tree-2 11:55). Fix A rides in pack 5i (5h is built on pack-next9). Done once 5i is installed and the Cells live test passes on the tree.")
inl lib_gleam_os_arms () = Task ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", "lib/spiral: Gleam arms for portable lib functions BEAM components call (env argv; file_system read/write/append/exists/delete/basename/is_dir; sm'.last_index_of), from a census of 50 portable functions without one", Story, P1, D2, 85u32, Active, "", "lib,backends,gleam", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_gleam_os_arms () = Planning ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_1 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 1u32, "2026-10-23T22:27Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:27 x: found while building Gleam components for BACKEND-BALANCE. The sim's OTP shell, memory_watch and a future Gleam xsim entry (needed for xsim diff/thread_run, which call Cells' evaluate on the BEAM) all hit these five functions having no Gleam arm (Rust/F#/Zig/TS only). memory_watch works around it with an injected thunk. Previously only a mention in X-BACKEND-MAP n9, which main read as already tracked; this item is the record. x will draft the arms in x-world/lib-draft for main to land, as with today's dictm/unicode handoff.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_2 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 2u32, "2026-10-23T22:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:30 x: arms DRAFTED as an anchored patch, C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_os_arms.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. env: count = list.length(init:get_plain_arguments()), item i = charlist.to_string of the i-th, or '' past the end (Rust returns unwrap_or_default ''). file_system: read_all_text = file:read_file + bit_array.to_string, with let assert (panics on error like Rust's unwrap); write_all_text = file:write_file, asserting ok; file_exists = filelib:is_regular (an existing regular file, the same as Rust's exists && is_file). Helpers are env_gleam_argv () and file_system_gleam_files () (global blocks, as process.spi does). Dry-applied to a lib copy: 5 arms, every anchor unique. Proof set up on a PATCHED lib copy (%TEMP%\\x-libcopy\\lib, real folder, no junction), check package %TEMP%\\x-libcopy\\check: write/exists/read round trip + argv. The same test is also run on the LIVE lib (%TEMP%\\x-libcopy\\control) as the positive control, which must fail at the missing Gleam arm. Both queued after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_3 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 3u32, "2026-10-23T23:30Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:30 x: fixed before any run. The draft put file_system_gleam_files () before read_all_text (~634), but file_exists (572) and write_all_text (611) call it too, and Spiral resolves top-down, so those arms would not have compiled. The helper now goes before file_exists, anchored with its let line, since file_exists is a prefix of file_exists_content. The proof copy %TEMP%\\x-libcopy was rebuilt from the live lib and re-applied: helper at 571, users at 612/654/678; no new lines over 120 in env.spi or file_system.spi. The check + live-lib control still run after tibia's block.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_4 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T00:02Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:02 x: one more missing Gleam arm, same theme (found while making memory_watch incremental): sm'.last_index_of has Zig/Rust arms and an F#-style default (.LastIndexOf), so on Gleam it fails. Draft C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_last_index.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral> adds a Gleam arm returning a BYTE offset, like sm'.index_of's Gleam arm (bit_array.byte_size of the head) and Rust's rfind. It uses Erlang string:find(S, Search, trailing): total bytes minus the suffix's, -1 on nomatch, and the length for an empty search. That's also right for overlapping patterns ('aa' in 'aaa' = 1), where binary:matches' non-overlapping scan would give 0. Applied to the OS-arms proof copy %TEMP%\\x-libcopy; the check package gains last_index_of_gives_the_last_byte_offset_on_the_beam (abcb/b = 3, aaa/aa = 1, z = -1, '' = 4), and the live-lib control must fail it.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_5 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 5u32, "2026-10-24T00:05Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:05 x: CENSUS of lib functions with backend arms but no Gleam arm (core modules, Rust-only signatures filtered out): 50 with portable signatures (string/i32/bool/list), mostly file_system (25), env (6), sm' (5: line_count, nonempty_line_count, line_at, nonempty_line_at, last_index_of), console colors, date_time, process.try_capture_combined_list. A BEAM component that calls one fails at runtime. Second batch DRAFTED: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_os_arms2.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral> adds Gleam arms to get_file_name (filename:basename), directory_exists (filelib:is_dir), file_delete (file:delete, errors ignored like Rust's traced path) and append_all_text (file:write_file with [append], asserting ok like Rust's unwrap). It has its own helper before get_file_name (line 414), because those functions come before the first batch's helper. Skipped get_extension on purpose: its backends already disagree (Rust gives 'spi' and panics without an extension; F# gives '.spi'), so a Gleam arm would have to pick a contract; that needs a decision first. Applied to %TEMP%\\x-libcopy after batch 1 + last_index_of: helper 414, users 457/534/827/1386, no new lines over 120. Check test paths_append_and_delete_on_the_beam added (live-lib control must fail).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_6 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 6u32, "2026-10-24T00:07Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:07 x: batch 4 DRAFTED: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_line_arms.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. It adds Gleam arms to sm'.line_count, nonempty_line_count, line_at and nonempty_line_at, whose only arm was Rust's .lines(), with the same semantics: split on '\\n', strip one trailing '\\r' per line, no final empty line after a trailing newline, 0 lines for ''. The helper lines_gleam () sits before line_count (line_count has no heading, so the anchor is its inl line). Applied on %TEMP%\\x-libcopy after batches 1-3 (helper 683, users from 712). Check test line_helpers_follow_rust_lines_on_the_beam: 'a\\r\\nb\\n' has 2 lines and line 1 = 'b'; '' has 0; nonempty in 'a\\n\\nb' = 2 and line 1 = 'b'.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_7 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 7u32, "2026-10-24T00:08Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:08 x: batch 5 DRAFTED: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_try_capture.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. It gives process.try_capture_combined_list a Gleam arm built from process.spi's own port helpers: a missing program gives None (os:find_executable is checked first, because open_port with 'false' would crash, while Rust's output() returns Err), a non-zero exit gives None, and otherwise stdout + stderr (stderr_to_stdout). One known difference: invalid UTF-8 output gives None on Gleam where Rust gives lossy text. Risk stated: the arm returns a Gleam #(Bool, String) typed as Spiral bool * string, which assumes Spiral pairs are native Gleam tuples; the check settles it (if not, switch to a nominal + accessors like x's cells_run). Use case: memory_watch's sampler can run a sampling command per tick on the BEAM. Applied on %TEMP%\\x-libcopy; check test: a missing program is None.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_8 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 8u32, "2026-10-24T00:10Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:10 x: batch 6 DRAFTED: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_stat_arms.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. get_current_directory gets file:get_cwd (asserting ok like Rust's unwrap), and last_write_ms gets file:read_file_info with posix time, mtime x 1000 and 0 when missing (Rust's unwrap_or(0)). Two differences, recorded: Erlang's cwd uses forward slashes on Windows ('c:/...') where Rust prints backslashes, and the mtime is whole seconds where Rust has milliseconds; both are fine for change detection, not for path equality across backends. Skipped on purpose: get_full_path (Rust canonicalize adds Windows' \\\\?\\ prefix; the contract needs a decision) and get_temp_path (OS-specific env-var rules). Helper file_system_gleam_stat () sits before get_current_directory (494; users 540, 1396). Check test: cwd non-empty, mtime > 0 for a fresh file, 0 for a missing one. Applied on %TEMP%\\x-libcopy with batches 1-5.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_9 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 9u32, "2026-10-24T00:13Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:13 main: get_extension contract decided by rule (x asked). The arms disagree (Rust Path::extension -> 'spi' and a panic when there is none through optionm'.unwrap; F# Path.GetExtension -> '.spi' or ''), and the function has 0 callers in lib/spiral, eoie/src and x/apps, so it can be fixed without breaking anyone. Rule: total (no panic) and the majority of standard libraries (Python os.path.splitext, Node path.extname, Go filepath.Ext and .NET all return the leading dot, and '' when there is no extension; Rust is the outlier). Contract: '.spi', or '' for no extension. So the Rust arm becomes extension().map_or('', |e| '.' + e) and the Gleam arm follows the same contract, with a test over 'a/b.spi' -> '.spi', 'a/b' -> '' and 'a.b/c' -> '' (a dot in a directory name only).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_10 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 10u32, "2026-10-24T00:15Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:15 x: get_extension folded into batch 2, per main's 20:1x decision (it has no callers in lib, eoie or x; Python, Node, Go and .NET all give the leading dot or ''). The Rust arm becomes extension().map_or('', |e| '.' + e) instead of unwrap (which panicked); the Gleam arm is filename:extension of the basename with one leading dot stripped, because Erlang gives '.bashrc' for a dotfile where Rust gives None. Test on rust + gleam: a/b.spi -> .spi, a/b -> '', a.b/c -> '', .bashrc -> ''. %TEMP%\\x-libcopy rebuilt from the live lib with all six batches in order: all applied, no new lines over 120. That makes 18 functions covered; get_full_path and get_temp_path still need a contract decision.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_11 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 11u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:17 x: get_extension's F# arm aligned too (main's follow-up): an explicit Fsharp arm returns empty for a dotfile (the file name starts with a dot and has no later dot; IndexOf with a string, ordinal), else Path.GetExtension, which by default gives .bashrc. The string overload avoids a quoted char literal, which would close the Spiral macro string early. The test targets rust, gleam and fsharp, so the three arms are pinned together. Proof copy rebuilt with all six batches; no new long lines.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_12 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 12u32, "2026-10-24T00:22Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:22 x: analysis for the two undecided contracts (main decides; evidence from this machine). Both have callers only inside file_system.spi (6 and 8 hits, mostly its own tests), so a contract change is cheap, as with get_extension. (1) get_temp_path: .NET GetTempPath returns WITH a trailing separator (C:\\Users\\...\\Temp\\); Rust std::env::temp_dir follows GetTempPath2 on Windows and TMPDIR or /tmp elsewhere. Proposal: return the directory without a trailing separator on every backend (trim it in the F# arm), and for Gleam look up os:getenv TMPDIR, then TEMP, then TMP, falling back to /tmp. (2) get_full_path: today the Rust arm canonicalizes existing paths, which requires existence, resolves symlinks and adds Windows' \\\\?\\ prefix, and normalizes lexically only for missing paths; .NET GetFullPath is purely lexical (x/../y -> <cwd>\\y, no filesystem access). Proposal: purely lexical everywhere (absolute against cwd, . and .. removed, no symlink resolution, no \\\\?\\), i.e. the .NET behavior, the same for existing and missing paths. Gleam: filename:absname, then fold the segments to drop . and resolve .. . Either way, a test pinning x/../y and a missing path on all arms.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_13 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 13u32, "2026-10-24T00:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:23 main: get_temp_path and get_full_path decided by the get_extension rule (total; the majority of standard libraries; callers only inside file_system.spi). (1) get_temp_path: no trailing separator on every backend. Python tempfile.gettempdir, Node os.tmpdir and Go os.TempDir omit it; .NET GetTempPath is the outlier. Lookup order on Gleam: TMPDIR, TEMP, TMP, then /tmp (Python's order), so the F# arm trims the trailing separator. (2) get_full_path: purely lexical everywhere (absolute against cwd, . and .. removed, no symlink resolution, no \\\\?\\ prefix), as Python os.path.abspath, Node path.resolve, Go filepath.Abs and .NET GetFullPath are. Rust's canonicalize is the outlier and fails on missing paths. Test: x/../y and a missing path, on rust, gleam and fsharp.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_14 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 14u32, "2026-10-24T00:26Z", "x", "2026-10-23 20:26 x: batch 7 DRAFTED per main's 20:2x decisions: C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-world\\lib-draft\\apply_gleam_path_arms.ps1 -Lib <lib/spiral>. (1) get_temp_path: no trailing separator on every backend (new pure without_trailing_separator, keeping a lone root); Rust and F# trim, and Gleam looks up TMPDIR, TEMP, TMP (empty counts as unset), then /tmp (Python's order). (2) get_full_path: purely lexical. The Rust arm drops its canonicalize branch and always takes its existing lexical branch (extracted programmatically from the live text and dedented, refusing if the arm's shape drifted). </> is PathBuf::join, so absolute inputs still replace cwd. The Gleam arm is filename:absname, then a fold dropping . and empty segments and resolving .. ; F# keeps GetFullPath (already lexical). Recorded difference: Gleam joins with / on Windows, like get_current_directory. Test on rust + gleam + fsharp: x/../y ends in y with no .., a missing path ends in its name, and the temp path has no trailing separator. %TEMP%\\x-libcopy rebuilt with all 7 batches in order: all applied; helpers precede users (env 408 < temp 435; paths 450 < full_path 1091); no new lines over 120. 20 functions covered now.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_15 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 15u32, "2026-10-24T01:11Z", "x", "2026-10-23 21:11 x: CORRECTION by user direction (21:0x): \"why we're delegating to main lib work? makes no sense, one of my goals of inventing eoie is the atomic batched patches, so all lanes can go ham, also with automatic backups etc\". The earlier note here saying \"Lib edits are main's domain, so coordinate each landing with main\" was my own unexamined assumption, not a rule. From now on x lands its own lib changes through eoie do atomic batched edits (expect counts, all-or-nothing, sha256 per file) plus the lib Cells gate. Coordination with other lanes is about order and live-tree reads only, not ownership. x takes over landing of its own drafts: the x landing patch (crypto/sm'/listm'/resultm + oauth + cursor_walk) and LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS's 7 batches. Main keeps LIB-GAPS-X, which x's patch builds on (take_).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_16 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 16u32, "2026-10-24T03:06Z", "x", "23:06 x: batches 1-7 are LIVE in lib/spiral since 22:06:07 (inside x part A's single eoie do batch; see X-GENERIC-TO-LIB n23). Gate on the new compiler: oauth 2/2 and cursor_walk 2/2 pass (the first oauth timeout was contention); x_lib_check 2/2; gleam_os_check 8/9. The open failure is the Gleam target of full_path_is_lexical_and_temp_path_has_no_trailing_separator: gleam build stops at 'Unknown module gleam/regexp'. Only sm'.replace_regex imports gleam/regexp (its Gleam arm), reached through file_system.normalize_path, and statically neither new Gleam arm calls it. Bisecting with two one-call probes (%TEMP%\\x-gleam-regexp-probe, job-1792811193870-x-gleam-regexp-bisect). Rules: if the probe that fails (get_full_path or get_temp_path) shows the regexp import, fix that arm with an atomic patch; if neither fails, it's the combined notebook, so add the gleam regexp dependency to the test.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_17 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 17u32, "2026-10-24T03:35Z", "x", "23:35 x: bisect DONE. probe_temp_path passes on Gleam; probe_full_path fails with the regexp import. Cause, by four compile probes in %TEMP%\\x-gleam-regexp-probe: the dead Rust arm of get_full_path calls the top-level let functions get_current_directory and normalize_path. Their Gleam-side globals leak into Gleam builds (normalize_path brings sm'.replace_regex's gleam/regexp), while an inl callee in a dead arm doesn't leak. Compiler finding sent to main. Lib fix (programs-normalize-prefix\\land.eoie + undo): normalize_path strips the literal \\\\?\\ prefix with starts_with + substring instead of replace_regex, plus a test normalize_path_drops_the_verbatim_prefix (rust, no -d regex). On a patched copy the probe no longer imports regexp. RULE, written before the Cells results: land when (a) gleam_os_check on the patched copy is 9/9, (b) the new test passes, and (c) file_system.spi's failing tests on the patched copy are a subset of those on an unpatched copy with the same layout (x-normalize-base). Otherwise don't land, and fix first.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_18 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 18u32, "2026-10-24T04:09Z", "x", "10-24 00:09 x: side finding from the bisect. lib sm'.replace_regex's own test runs on '///> gleam' with no dependency directive, but its Gleam arm imports gleam/regexp, which Cells' Gleam build doesn't include by default (probe_full_path's failure). Red/green probes queued, job-1792814947532 (%TEMP%\\x-regexp-directive-probe): the same assertion bare vs '///> gleam -d gleam_regexp=\">= 1.1.0 and < 2.0.0\"' (base.spi's -d form). RULE, before results: bare fails on regexp and directive passes -> land the directive on sm'.spi's replace_regex test via the guarded runner, gated on that test. Both pass -> Cells already provides it, so re-examine tonight's failure. Directive fails -> the -d syntax is wrong; read Cells' directive parser before touching lib.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_19 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 19u32, "2026-10-24T04:59Z", "x", "10-24 00:59 x: ORDERING. main's %TEMP%\\lib-fs-primitives\\apply.ps1 (replace_checked, is_within and find_on_path, appended to file_system.spi; is_within calls normalize_path) waits for the normalize_path land. When x-lib-normalize lands (decide3 job-1792817005040 -> lander), send main a one-liner. main takes the file_system.spi:889 repo-coupled cwd test fix itself (not folded into mine, so my program stays byte-identical to the proof).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_20 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 20u32, "2026-10-24T06:08Z", "x", "10-24 02:08 x: n18's rule applied. The bare probe fails on Gleam with 'Unknown module gleam/regexp' and the same assertion with '///> gleam -d gleam_regexp=\">= 1.1.0 and < 2.0.0\"' passes, so landing the directive on sm'.spi's replace_regex test. It goes via the guarded lander (programs-regexp-directive\\land.eoie + undo, dry-run fits the live lib, job x-regexp-directive-lander), gated on sm'.spi through the mutex + 3.5 GB floor gate. (probe job job-1792817005684-x-regexp-directive-probe3)")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_21 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 21u32, "2026-10-24T06:19Z", "x", "10-24 02:19 x: proof3 result. gleam_os_check is 9/9 on the patched copy (rule a). The patched file_system.spi ran 21 tests and failed 3: find_parent (fsharp), find_parent_2 (rust) and trace_file (fsharp). The new normalize_path_drops_the_verbatim_prefix test is not among them. PREDICTION, written before the baseline result: all three fail the same way on the unpatched twin. find_parent/_2 walk up from the source or current directory for a 'workspace' folder and README.md, which don't exist above a %TEMP% lib copy, and trace_file writes under get_workspace_root's layout search. None of them reaches the \\\\?\\ prefix branch. If the baseline differs, the decision job's rule (c) refuses the land, and I investigate before any retry.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_22 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 22u32, "2026-10-24T08:47Z", "x", "10-24 04:47 x: TAKING the file_system.spi GRANULAR-HEAL slice at main's request (7,928 violating chars). It's drafted on a lib copy with the normalize_path patch already applied (%TEMP%\\x-fs-heal\\lib), so its anchors match the tree after the normalize land, and it lands after it. The globals with bodies: env (temp_dir_from), paths (lexical_full_path, dotted_extension, append_text), stat (cwd, mtime_ms), files (read_text, write_text), plus walk_dir_filter's poll (Rust), file_exists, and Rust's MAIN_SEPARATOR cast, symlink cfg blocks and mtime. FS-1 (programs-fs-heal-1, 4 replacements) moves the 3 gleam_paths bodies into Spiral over the externs, which stay. gleam_segment_step plus a listm.fold over sm'.split_list of absname gives lexical_full_path; basename, a Spiral leading-dot strip and the extension extern give dotted_extension; append goes through gleam_written + coerce + gleam_atoms_equal, and failwith keeps the old let-assert behavior. Check: gleam_os_check on the copy, job-1792831611429.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_23 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 23u32, "2026-10-24T08:54Z", "x", "10-24 04:54 x: FS-1 VERIFIED. gleam_os_check on the healed copy passes 9/9 on rust, gleam and fsharp (job-1792831611429), covering full path, extension and append. FS-2 (programs-fs-heal-2, 10 replacements) is applied to the copy: the shared nominals (gleam_dynamic, gleam_atom, gleam_charlist) move above gleam_env; temp_dir_from becomes a Spiral let rec over one-call getenv/coerce/charlist primitives; cwd and mtime_ms go through resultm.result' + unbox, with the posix-option tuple written as ##( in the macro and * 1000 in Spiral; read_text and write_text go through result' + unbox and the ok-atom check. Its check is job-1792832046429.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_24 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 24u32, "2026-10-24T09:01Z", "x", "10-24 05:01 x: FS-2 VERIFIED. gleam_os_check on the healed copy passes 9/9 on rust, gleam and fsharp (job-1792832046429), covering temp path, cwd, mtime and the file round trip. With FS-1 + FS-2 every Gleam global in file_system.spi holds only externs and imports (rule 3): the bodies of temp_dir_from, lexical_full_path, dotted_extension, append_text, cwd, mtime_ms, read_text and write_text are now Spiral over one-call primitives. Ready to land in order after the normalize land (anchored on the post-normalize text): programs-normalize-prefix, then programs-fs-heal-1, then programs-fs-heal-2. Left for FS-3: the F# macros (interpolated messages, REPL let-exports), Rust walk_dir_filter's async filter, the MAIN_SEPARATOR cast, the symlink cfg blocks, and the mtime/append chains.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_25 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 25u32, "2026-10-24T09:02Z", "x", "10-24 05:02 x: land chain queued, all guarded (reader guard + rehearsal on a fresh copy + gleam_os_check on the live lib): normalize lander job-1792828314873 (waits for eoie GE3's live-lib phases) -> --after-ok job-1792832494176-x-fs-heal-1-lander -> --after-ok job-1792832495716-x-fs-heal-2-lander. Each lander's guard also waits for the previous slice's own gate, which reads the lib, so every slice lands on a verified base. Message main at the normalize land (their fs-primitives append follows).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_26 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 26u32, "2026-10-24T09:09Z", "x", "10-24 05:09 x: FS-3 under main's rule readings (GRANULAR-HEAL n5): symlinks use cfg-gated helper fns (same name and signature, one-call bodies); F# REPL export lines are host declarations, so they stay; walk_dir_filter is x's (later, over main's async primitives). FS-3a (programs-fs-heal-3): Rust last_write_ms = metadata/modified/duration_since bridges + ms in Spiral (0 on any error); Rust append_all_text = OpenOptions open + write_all bridges, with failwith keeping the old unwrap panics. Check job-1792832705648. FS-3b (programs-fs-heal-4, dry run 6/6): directory/file_create_symbolic_link Rust arms call spiral_fs_symlink_dir/_file, cfg-gated globals for windows (symlink_dir/symlink_file) and unix (symlink), behind a rust_link_done bridge + failwith; and the all-string F# interpolations become Spiral ++# (find_parent's message, new_file_uri, trace_file's file name with sm'.obj_to_string guid). Left: read_link's mixed-type messages (ints and exceptions; they need the lib formatter, so not a mechanical swap), trace_file's %A, and walk_dir_filter.")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_27 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 27u32, "2026-10-24T09:37Z", "x", "10-24 05:37 x: FS-3a VERIFIED (job-1792832705648). The whole file_system.spi on the healed copy fails exactly the 3 layout tests, the same set as the unpatched baseline (find_parent, find_parent_2, trace_file); gleam_os_check passes 9/9. Its lander, job-1792834592721, is chained --after-ok the FS-2 lander. FS-3b (programs-fs-heal-4: symlink cfg helpers + string messages) is applied to the copy; its check is job-1792834585180 (same two notebooks, same baseline rule).")
inl note_of_lib_gleam_os_arms_28 () = Note ("LIB-GLEAM-OS-ARMS", 28u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "x", "10-24 19:44 x: held-files runner (job-1792875049850, exit 0) LANDED normalize-prefix and fs-heal-1..3 on live file_system.spi (18:28; gate %TEMP%\\x-libcopy\\control\\gleam_os_check.spi passed after each). fs-heal-4 was applied only to the copy %TEMP%\\x-fs-heal-4-recheck-10241828\\spiral: file_system.spi failed 21/21 tests, 0 passed, on every target (rust, fsharp, gleam, zig). That's a whole-file failure, not the baseline subset (find_parent, find_parent_2, trace_file), so FS-4 stays unlanded. Next (CPU-light first): diff the copy's file_system.spi against live and read the first compile error in that run's per-target output. 21/21 points at a parse/type error the program introduces, not test semantics. main got 'file_system.spi done for this round' for its edition-3 respelling, so FS-4 will be redrafted in edition-3 spelling after main's pass.")
