inl compiler_dead_arm_globals () = Task ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", "Globals from a dead backend arm leak into the output when the arm calls a top-level let: the let callee's specialization globals (imports, helpers) survive though only the live arm is emitted (x 10-23 23:3x, Gleam, compiler B553EF6B78E6EE29)", Story, P1, D5, 75u32, Active, "PACK-NEXT-2", "compiler,backends,gleam,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals () = Planning ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", Minutes 300u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T03:37Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:37 main: repro from x in %TEMP%\\x-gleam-regexp-probe (BuildSpiral -Backend Gleam). probe_arms: the dead Rust arm calls an inl that emits global import, no leak. probe_vc: the dead Rust arm calls the let file_system.get_current_directory, which leaks gleam/erlang/charlist plus file_system_gleam_stat's helpers. probe_vd: the let file_system.normalize_path leaks gleam/option and gleam/regexp (not in Cells' Gleam deps, so gleam build fails). probe_va leaks all three; probe_vb (trivial Rust arm) leaks none. The generated get_full_path_0 body is clean, so it looks like a let's specialization (with its globals) memoized while a dead arm was evaluated speculatively. Milestones: locate where top-level let specializations register globals during backend_switch / run_target arm evaluation 90 m; make globals belong to the emitted specialization only (collect per join point, keep the ones reached from the live program) 120 m; minimal regression sample from probe_vd 30 m; pack review 60 m. x's lib side (normalize_path without regex) is a separate workaround that x lands.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T04:32Z", "main", "2026-10-24 00:32 main: cause and fix. In the BackendSwitch op, a non-current arm is validated in an env rebuilt as {s with seq; cse; backend; i} that kept s.globals, so everything the dead arm recorded went into the live scope's globals list. That includes the bsv and key markers of let callees specialized there and the globals of their own nested arm validations. Single-flight registers a bsv marker via key_body first, and the rebuild keeps its child list. Fix (slice %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\dead-arm-globals.ps1, 3 sites: 2 parallel-core, 1 single-flight): the validation env gets globals=ResizeArray(), so the validated arm's globals are discarded; the bsv marker stays, with no children, and a join point first specialized inside a dead arm keeps its own child list under its key marker, so a later live KeyHit still brings its globals. Regression sample (staged; review-4 installs it into tmp/samples before its run): gleam_dead_arm_let_globals, core only, a let gleam_marked whose Gleam arm emits global 'import spiral_dead_arm_marker', called only from picked's Rust arm. Positive control on the installed compiler (job main-dead-arm-positive-control-8): the Gleam output contains the marker import, so gleam build would fail on an unknown module; the Rust output does not. Expected after the fix: Gleam builds and exits 6. In pack 5g with the Rust self-rebind clone fix, Bend balanced if-heads and segment-head-relaid; review job main-pack-next5g-review-4.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 3u32, "2026-10-24T09:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 05:37 main: pack 5g review-4 (core 64902F6956ECA45B; run single-flight-20261024-043256 vs 20261022-184215) is NOT acceptable. The automatic part passed (formatter corpus, DISAGREE 0; Bend build-fail -> ran across many rows; record_with_expected_base and portable_math_rounding fixed), but 5 Gleam rows regress from native ran to build-fail with \"Unknown module\" in 10-48 ms: mega_brzozowski_derivatives indexed_dfa_state_cardinality, inventory_closed_dfa_runtime, runtime_bench, runtime_native, and mega_omniledger_erp_kernel runtime_native. 447 Gleam residuals changed and 440 still run, so a few large programs lose an import or module reference; the dead-arm-globals slice is the prime suspect (it changes which globals the validation env keeps). Probe queued: main-pack5g-gleam-probe (job-1792834615254) compiles indexed_dfa_state_cardinality on Gleam with the installed and candidate compilers and diffs their imports; the sample folder is restored from a snapshot afterwards.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T09:39Z", "main", "2026-10-24 05:39 main: hypothesis, written before the probe result: the globals index is per list (GlobalsIndex.get s.globals), so de-duplication does not leak between the arm env and the live env. What does leak is evaluation memoized by join points and type join points: a join point (or type join point) first evaluated while validating a NON-current arm records its body once, its global emissions go to the discarded list, and later live uses hit the cache without replaying those globals. Before the slice, the leak into the live list masked this. Fix options, in order of preference: (a) cache entries keep the globals their first evaluation emitted and replay them into the caller env on a hit; (b) non-current arm validation uses a separate join point cache. The probe decides whether an import is what goes missing.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 5u32, "2026-10-24T09:52Z", "main", "2026-10-24 05:52 main: probe result (main-pack5g-gleam-probe, outputs in %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\gleam-probe): installed main.gleam has 1 import (gleam/int) and builds; the candidate main.gleam has 3 imports (main_types, main_part1, main_part2) and LOSES `import gleam/int`, so the build stops with Unknown module. The global is emitted while something is first evaluated in a context whose globals are now discarded, and later live uses do not re-emit it: the memo hypothesis of note 4. Decision by the review rule: pack 5g is not accepted as is. Next (compiler, main): find where the join point / type join point caches store their first evaluation (the top-level join_point_method / join_point_closure dictionaries, PartEvalSeams), record the globals that evaluation emitted with the cache entry and replay them into the caller env on a hit. Positive controls: this sample on Gleam (must import gleam/int again) and x's gleam_dead_arm_let_globals (must keep the dead arm's globals out). Then re-run review-4 for 5g' (5g + the replay).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 6u32, "2026-10-24T09:57Z", "main", "2026-10-24 05:57 main: mechanism found by reading the 5g core (BackendSwitch, ~line 1611xx in %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\spiral_compiler.fs). Validating a non-current arm (say Fsharp on a Gleam build) reaches nested backend_switches whose GLEAM arm is then validated as non-current with backend=Gleam. Join points evaluated there fill the (Gleam, body) method cache (JpBodyOwnerIdentity includes backend'), and their `global \"import gleam/int\"` lands in the slice's throwaway list. The live Gleam compile later hits that cache entry and never re-emits the import, hence Unknown module. Before the slice, the leak into the live list hid this. Fix design (one rule, no replay machinery): a validation env keeps the LIVE root globals when the arm's backend equals the build's target backend, and a throwaway list otherwise. Implementation: a ThreadLocal root (backend, globals) recorded when RustGapTrace.validating goes 0 -> 1, then `globals = if backend = root backend then root globals else ResizeArray()` in the 3 validation envs (single-flight + 2 parallel). Positive controls: gleam_dead_arm_let_globals (Rust/C target, so the Gleam marker stays out) and mega_brzozowski indexed_dfa_state_cardinality on Gleam (gleam/int is back). The cost is a possible unused target-language import, which Gleam only warns about.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 7u32, "2026-10-24T15:26Z", "main", "main 11:3x: implemented note 6 as pack 5h, plus a second leak found while reading the parallel core. (a) note 6: each peval keeps validation_root_globals (set when its root LangEnv is built); a backend_switch validation env uses the live root globals when its arm backend = env.backend (the target) and a fresh list otherwise, so a nested target-backend arm validated under a dead arm (the 5g Gleam case) emits import gleam/int into the live program. (b) parallel cores add a global only if s.globals_seen.TryAdd succeeds (spiral_compiler.fs ~128580); 5g swapped globals to a throwaway list but kept the LIVE globals_seen, so a global first emitted in a dead-arm validation was marked seen and then skipped by the live emission. 5h gives non-target validation envs their own (globals, globals_lock, globals_seen) triple. Slice %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\dead-arm-root-globals.ps1 replaces dead-arm-globals.ps1; %TEMP%\\pack-next5h\\compose.ps1 composes it on pack-next9 (core 02815A83BA3AEA8B). Queued job main-serial-pack5h: parse both defines, build candidate, positive control gleam_dead_arm_let_globals on Rust and C (marker must stay out), decisive mega_brzozowski Gleam probe (import gleam/int must be back). Full matrix review only after both pass.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 8u32, "2026-10-24T15:42Z", "main", "main 12:0x: correction to note 7: the first 5h compose (02815A83BA3AEA8B) was corrupted (the slice's Swap helper printed its status into the returned text, so line 1 of the core held status lines; the queued parse check would have refused it). Fixed (status to Out-Host) and recomposed before the build took its slot: pack-next5h core F3DFDD955559E904, line 1 #nowarn 40, validation_root_globals at 7 sites (2 declarations, 2 root sets, 3 validation envs).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 9u32, "2026-10-24T16:41Z", "main", "main 12:4x: pack 5h build+probe (12:13): candidate built (306 s); positive control gleam_dead_arm_let_globals keeps the dead Gleam arm's marker out of Rust and C; the mega Gleam row still reported build-fail Unknown module, BUT the cause is the matrix harness, not the core: 5h's output for that row is split (main.gleam + main_types/main_part1/main_part2.gleam, the pack-next7 Gleam module split), main_part1 now DOES import gleam/int (the note-6 fix works), and the 4 files build and run in a scratch Gleam project. test.ps1's Gleam native tier copied only main.gleam: the batch renames it spiral_row_NNNN and the per-row path copies it alone, so main_types was unknown. Fixed in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1 (uncommitted, for the user): Gleam rows with .gleam parts skip the batch (every split row has a module named main_types) and the per-row path clears stale main_*.gleam parts and copies the row's parts beside main.gleam. 5g's 5 Gleam mega build-fails were probably the same harness gap on top of the real lost-import bug. Re-probe queued (main-serial-pack5h-probe-2); then the full matrix review of 5h.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 10u32, "2026-10-24T20:07Z", "main", "main 15:5x: re-probe with the test.ps1 Gleam split-part fix (main-pack5h-gleam-probe-2 rerun 15:38-15:42): mega_brzozowski indexed_dfa_state_cardinality on Gleam builds and runs exit 0 with BOTH the installed and the 5h candidate (DISAGREE 0); the runner's exit 1 is the probe script's robocopy restore code. Before the full review I found 5h would revert a tree change: pack-next9's base predates the 10-24 09:25 fmt-next install (arm-lambda-opener, FORMATTER-LAMBDA-NEXT-LINE); the tree's other later install (pack-fmt-only) only spliced 5f's Formatter section, which next9 already holds. Pack 5i (%TEMP%\\pack-next5i\\compose.ps1) = 5h + arm-lambda-opener + the pack-spelling slices (format --spelling verb, tight-first respelling); its Formatter section equals the tree's except 5h's own segment-head-relaid. Full review queued: main-serial-pack5i-review (%TEMP%\\pack-next4\\review-5i.ps1: build, formatter gate, matrix -Suite all -Native, output rules). Acceptance by the review rule, then install.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_11 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 11u32, "2026-10-24T21:41Z", "main", "main 17:4x: pack 5i review done (main-serial-pack5i-review, 16:06-17:13, exit 0). Formatter candidate gate, automatic part: PASS. Matrix run single-flight-20261024-164057: native 6,833 ran, 9 build-fail, oracle agree 6,106, DISAGREE 0. Against the 10-22 reference run single-flight-20261022-184215: 115 behavior cells changed (not residual), DISAGREE on the right 0. Visible so far: Bend rows going from build-fail to ran and agreeing (array_i64, array_i64_at1, array_u64, closure_pair_i64, ...), and gadt_growing_index_runtime C/F# oracle WRONG-ERROR -> blank (the tree's 10-23 GADT type-check hang; noted for 5g, not caused by this pack). The output-rule text checks compare against an older snapshot and are informational per the rule text. The candidate is recorded in %TEMP%\\pack-next5i\\dll.txt. By the accept rule, install only after every one of the 115 cells is reviewed and explained (compare-runs -Show all), then the determinism check: next block's first job. 5i also carries the format --spelling verb, so installing it lets the edition-3 apply use the installed compiler.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_12 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 12u32, "2026-10-24T21:43Z", "main", "main 17:4x: all 115 changed cells of the 5i review are reviewed and explained, with no REGRESSED or DISAGREE. (1) Bend: 12 samples build-fail -> ran, oracle agree (balanced if-heads), including native_closure_union_rec exit 70 and printf_portable with stdout. (2) Rust: the 5 mega runtime rows (brzozowski indexed_dfa_state_cardinality, inventory_closed_dfa_runtime, runtime_bench, runtime_native; omniledger runtime_native) and typecheck_long_let_body (exit 236) go build-fail -> ran, agree (self-rebind clones; this is the BACKENDS-SOTA 'Rust 3 of 8 mega' gap). (3) record_with_expected_base: compile error -> ok on F#, C, Rust and Delphi, agree (C: no-zig). (4) portable_math_rounding: Lean, Gleam and Lua compile timeout -> ok, agree. (5) gleam_dead_arm_let_globals: new rows (the regression sample). (6) gadt_growing_index_runtime: oracle WRONG-ERROR -> blank on 4 backends, the tree's 10-23 type-check hang (5g review-4 note), not this pack. Still needed before install: the hopacsota-side run of 5i (both cores DISAGREE 0) and the determinism check, then install at a block start when no gate reads the installed compiler (fiscal cte-os-3, the edition-3 proof and the lib gates are running now).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_13 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 13u32, "2026-10-24T23:45Z", "main", "2026-10-24 19:45 groom: 75%. Pack 5i (5h + arm-lambda-opener + spelling verb) single-flight review exit 0, DISAGREE 0, 115 cells explained. Left: read the hopacsota-side run (main-serial-pack5i-hopacsota), run the determinism check, install at a block start.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_14 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 14u32, "2026-10-25T00:06Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:06 main: the install vehicle is now pack 5j = 5i + no-gleam-parts.ps1 (see GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES): 5i's Gleam module split drops '.{...}' imports from parts. The slice changes only the '.gleam' branch of build. So the 5j review rule: build 5j, rerun the Gleam rows whose 5i run wrote main.spiral-parts, and require each to agree with 5i's stdout as one file; every other row's output is unchanged by construction. Then the determinism check and install as before. The running 5i hopacsota review stays valid for every non-Gleam-split row.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_15 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 15u32, "2026-10-25T00:18Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:21 main (corrected; x found the error): 5j removes both output splits (Rust and Gleam; see GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES). Install-step rules: (1) Compose and build 5j, then rerun the 6 samples whose committed outputs are split: mega_brzozowski_derivatives/{indexed_dfa_state_cardinality, inventory_closed_dfa_runtime, runtime_bench, runtime_native}, mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/runtime_native, typecheck_long_let_body. Each Rust and Gleam row must agree with 5i's stdout as one file. Re-accept those rows by the accept rule (single-flight review, every changed row explained, DISAGREE 0, an accept run that reproduces the review). (2) The 29 split files are TRACKED (user commit 0ec501fb, 10-24 05:02 'tests'), not untracked as the first version of this note said: 6 main.spiral-parts, 6 main_part1.rs, the Gleam parts and main_types.gleam (git ls-files lists exactly these). By the samples rule (one committed copy of each output, the compiler's output replacing the previous one), they become stale once 5j writes single files. After the accept run, delete exactly those 29 from the working tree and leave the deletions unstaged for the user. (3) At install, apply host-no-parts.ps1 and revert test.ps1's parts hunks (Get-OutputParts, the compile-memo .parts dir, partsKey, the main_*.gleam copy) back to HEAD's behavior. Get-OutputParts also caught 451 Python main_auto.py files (the 'Python + Cuda' multiFile output), which HEAD never collected; that is MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO's question, not 5j's. (4) Message x, tibia and eoie before touching the core or the host. The install waits for eoie CLEAN's 'lib-free:' (note 16).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_16 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 16u32, "2026-10-25T00:19Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:19 main: install timing rule from eoie. CLEAN's regen, candidate notebooks and spiral tests all run on the installed SpiralCompiler.dll and finish before CLEAN prints 'lib-free:'. So the 5j install waits for that line, the same hold as the edition-3 land. A compiler-install window taken before CLEAN launches makes CLEAN wait instead. 5j isn't built or reviewed yet, so the window isn't taken: the install happens after lib-free.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_17 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 17u32, "2026-10-25T04:40Z", "main", "2026-10-25 00:40 main: 5j REVIEW GREEN (job-1792900139397, 00:29, exit 0). Composite 8E893BAAE47D1D2A (5i plus no-output-parts, test-header-words and test-symbol-lexer; lint G1/G2 clean); reviewed SpiralCompilerCore BA0949F4EE7399DE. DISAGREE 0; native results equal next3 row for row; formatter gate PASS. Every text-check count equals 5i's except Gleam 'beyond locals' 29 -> 23: the 6 split samples are single files again, as planned. The candidate is recorded in pack-next5j\\dll.txt. install-5j.ps1 dry run is OK: base tree core BEECFE432427C57A (the 09:25 install), and the post-build hash check against the reviewed core is the determinism check. The host-no-parts slice and the test.ps1 revert stay out of this install, because they'd change the reviewed build; they go in the next pack. Waiting on x, tibia and eoie for a quiet window, then: install, accept run, then delete the 29 tracked part files.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_18 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 18u32, "2026-10-25T05:06Z", "main", "2026-10-25 01:06 main: 5j INSTALLED (job-1792904479028, window win-1792904480020; tree core 8E893BAAE47D1D2A, single-flight core 2CC30D887CF85F24, hopacsota core A2C4D1FAAB168E4A). The file-hash check said 'differs from the reviewed BA0949F4EE7399DE' (exit 3). The analysis: both DLLs are 12,128,256 bytes, with 53 differing bytes in 7 runs, all inside the PDB identity records (debug directory stamps @184776, the CodeView PDB GUID @987592, PdbChecksum @987736). The MVID is IDENTICAL (4bf3840d-2864-620b-bdb6-1c29a7c317ea), and in a deterministic build that's the content hash of metadata and IL, so the installed code is the reviewed code. Likely cause: a source path in the PDB (candidate cache vs tree). install-5j.ps1 now accepts an equal MVID with PDB-only differences. Next: tell the lanes, then the accept run (6 single-file samples), then delete the 29 tracked part files (left unstaged for the user).")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_19 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 19u32, "2026-10-25T07:18Z", "main", "10-25 03:18 main: 5j ACCEPT done (job-1792904859558, run single-flight-20261025-010749): native 6833 ran, DISAGREE 0, 7352 rows. Matches review single-flight-20261025-000845 row for row except 1 cell: mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/all_terminals Fsharp exit, review 1 vs accept timeout (a load timeout on the F# tier). Both the review and the 10-20 baseline say 1, so EXPECTED.tsv keeps the reviewed 1 (raw accept kept as EXPECTED.tsv.accept-5j-raw; new hash 77512DAEA5CC1050). Part files: only 6 tracked main_part1.rs were left (no main.rs references them, and git grep finds no consumer outside samples, with a positive control), so I deleted them, unstaged (copies in %TEMP%\\pack-next5j\\deleted-parts). The '29' in n18 counted Gleam parts too, and those are no longer tracked.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_20 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 20u32, "2026-10-25T08:22Z", "main", "10-25 04:22 main: 5k REVIEW done (job-1792908855610, run single-flight-20261025-032812, core 3DC18876AFBE138E, reviewed SpiralCompilerCore 75272FE5C7F5443F). Formatter gate PASS on user/lib/synthetic/dice/eoie (0 files differ). Against today's 5j accept (single-flight-20261025-010749), row for row: 7352 rows, 0 missing, 209 differing cells = 208 Rust residuals (the closure guard: functions that hold a closure keep the clone instead of moving, as designed for E0507) + the F# mega_omniledger all_terminals load-timeout cell flipping back to the baseline's 1; 0 behavior cells otherwise, DISAGREE 0. Install queued: job-1792916161438 (install-5k.ps1 = install-5j.ps1 with base 8E893BAAE47D1D2A, composite 3DC18876AFBE138E, MVID-equal acceptance). Then: tell eoie (holds SLATE/publishes on 5k), x, tibia.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_21 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 21u32, "2026-10-25T10:34Z", "main", "10-25 06:34 main: PACK 5l composed: %TEMP%\\pack-next5l\\spiral_compiler.fs = the 5k core 3DC18876 + test-header-symbol (stage 3, FE738C3B) + tibia's LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS pair (lua-do-blocks + lua-spill-locals, 375E1C9A) + tibia's C-PRIM-TO-STRING (!!!!PrimToString for C) = composite 04823272F73F534A. Each slice applied with all its anchors matching once, in sequence, from pack-local copies. Review: job-1792924381126 (review-5l.ps1), after the 5k install: the pack-next4 matrix review, then the lib Lua check. The check runs the 12 lib files that have ///> lua tests on the candidate and reruns red files on the installed compiler; only candidate-only reds fail. After install: gate tibia's lib-obj-to-string-c.ps1 (sm'.obj_to_string C arm), which needs PrimToString.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_22 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 22u32, "2026-10-25T11:02Z", "main", "10-25 07:02 main: the 5k install (job-1792916161438) REFUSED by its own base check: the tree core is 17D970FE7D26D27B, not 5j's 8E893BAA. x's x-compat change (xCompatDefusedAt/xCompatPostedLink, xCompatTopLevelBlocks rewritten; 38+/11-, written 05:50, outside codegen) was installed into the tree meanwhile. That skipped the 5l review (after-ok). Decision: no separate 5k rebase plus re-review. 5l is recomposed on the tree core: 17D970FE + the 5k closure guard (4B67A297) + stage 3 + LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS + C-PRIM-TO-STRING = 5l' 0C48169521E5384F. Its diff from the first 5l is exactly x's delta. One review (job-1792926015134) and one install then cover 5k and 5l.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_23 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 23u32, "2026-10-25T14:30Z", "main", "10-25 10:30 main: 5l' REVIEW (run single-flight-20261025-081136, composite 0C48169521E5384F) vs today's 5j accept: 7352 rows, DISAGREE 0. 442 differing cells: Lua residual 229, Rust residual 208 (the 5k guard), the F# all_terminals load-timeout cell, and ONE behavior regression: samples/mega_omniledger_erp_kernel/runtime_native Lua ran/agree -> build-fail 'main.lua:3196: main function has more than 200 local variables' (LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS: the do-block temporaries land in main's frame and the spill doesn't cover main). Not installed. Decision (keep SLATE unblocked, tibia's pair returns when fixed): 5l'' = tree 17D970FE + 5k guard + stage 3 + C-PRIM-TO-STRING = 34519B923366CCEC; review job-1792938510698 (lib Lua check skipped, since Lua codegen is unchanged). Stopped the 5l' review during its lib Lua check (samples already restored; only logs written after that). tibia and kit told.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_24 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 24u32, "2026-10-25T14:52Z", "main", "10-25 10:52 main: pack 5m staged (%TEMP%\\pack-next5m). tibia's fixed LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS pair: the do-blocks emit bare 'local vN' at chunk level; spillChunkLocals counted only 'local x =' lines, so chunk main never hit its trigger. Fix: count bare declarations, and drop a spilled bare declaration after the rename. tibia's chunk-spill-check reproduces 'more than 200 local variables' with the old regex and runs clean with the fix. The pair dry-runs clean on the 5l'' composite. 5m = installed 5l'' core + the pair + HOPACSOTA-P2's published-ready.ps1 (5/5 on 5l'). Review requirements: the omniledger runtime_native Lua row back to ran/agree, and the lib Lua check (12 files) on the candidate.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_25 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 25u32, "2026-10-25T17:50Z", "main", "10-25 13:50 main: 5l'' REVIEW done (job-1792938510698, run single-flight-20261025-123908, composite 34519B923366CCEC, reviewed SpiralCompilerCore CDD68DC3EE829A67): DISAGREE 0. Outputs changed: Rust 208 beyond let mut, the same 208 the 5k review attributed to the closure guard (n20). C, Zig, Python, Kotlin, Swift, TS, Lean, Gleam, Bend, Lua, Wasm and Pascal changed 0 beyond their known rewrites. So every changed row is explained. Install queued: job-1792950541607 (install-5k.ps1 -Base 17D970FE7D26D27B -Composite 34519B923366CCEC -CoreDir pack-next5l -Wait until-granted). Fixes to the install script, found while queueing: (1) its 120-minute window wait would expire behind x's 250-minute compile window, so it now takes -Wait; (2) it checked the base core only BEFORE the wait, so an edit by another lane during a long wait would have been overwritten; it now re-checks after the grant and refuses if the core moved. x and kit told.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_26 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 26u32, "2026-10-25T18:20Z", "main", "10-25 14:20 main: 5l'' INSTALLED (job-1792950541607, window granted after x released its compile window and kit's SLATE gate passed). Tree core 34519B923366CCEC; single-flight SpiralCompilerCore CDD68DC3EE829A67 = the reviewed candidate; MVID 7fd00bff-ba14-fc03-1b62-5a896ccd06ed (kit's SLATE trigger); hopacsota core 7CECE03C8FB4BB12. kit, x and tibia told. Next: pack 5m composed over 34519B92: tibia's fixed LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS pair, HOPACSOTA published-ready, the citation-comment strip (strip-doc-citations.ps1). Gate: the omniledger runtime_native Lua row ran/agree, plus the lib Lua check. Per memory recompile-drafts-after-compiler-change, the queued lib gates and heals now run on the new compiler; their verdicts already target it.")
inl note_of_compiler_dead_arm_globals_27 () = Note ("COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS", 27u32, "2026-10-25T19:06Z", "main", "10-25 15:06 main: pack 5m COMPOSED: %TEMP%\\pack-next5m\\spiral_compiler.fs A29F241986A441F3 = installed 5l'' core 34519B92 + HOPACSOTA published-ready (lab/HOPACSOTA-P2/published-ready.ps1, all 6 anchors matched once) + tibia's LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS pair (lua-function-limits.ps1) + strip-doc-citations (24 blocks, 86 comment lines). pair-drift: 0 pairs touched on one side only. Review script review-5m.ps1 re-enables the lib Lua check (every lib .spi with a ///> lua target, candidate vs installed) and reads the omniledger runtime_native Lua row (5l'': ran/agree; it must stay ran/agree). Toolchain change, rule written before the run: fiscal moved this machine's fpc to trunk 3.3.1-21298 today, so Delphi native rows rerun on a new fpc and test.ps1 will print a toolchain diff. A Delphi row that differs from the 5j accept is attributed to the pack only if it also differs from a same-fpc rerun of the installed 5l'' compiler (test.ps1 -Backend Delphi with the installed DLL). Otherwise it's the toolchain, and the next accept takes -AcceptToolchain after its own review. Rows of every other backend gate as before (DISAGREE 0, every changed residual explained).")
inl eoie_note_edit_expect () = Task ("EOIE-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", "agile note-edit refuses to rewrite the wrong note: --expect-at <ISO> or --expect-prefix <text> must match the note being replaced, and the receipt prints the replaced text's first 80 chars, so an off-by-number edit is refused instead of silently overwriting history", Story, P1, D2, 80u32, Active, "EOIE-AGILE-NOTE-EDIT", "eoie,agile,consolidated", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_note_edit_expect () = Planning ("EOIE-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_note_edit_expect_1 () = Note ("EOIE-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T04:14Z", "main", "2026-10-24 00:14 main: hit tonight. Correcting a grooming note, main ran note-edit FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL 9 meaning the newest note (26), and silently replaced a 10-22 note about Delphi build outputs. Recovered byte-identical only because the user had staged agile.spi (git show :docs/backlog/state/agile.spi). The receipt printed at=2026-10-22T06:59Z, which would have shown the mistake if it had been checked first. Milestones: optional --expect-at and --expect-prefix guards in note_request_of 30 m; the receipt shows the old text head 15 m; one-call tutorial case (edit the newest note by --last) 30 m; release 15 m. A --last shorthand (edit the newest note) would remove the counting entirely.")
inl note_of_eoie_note_edit_expect_2 () = Note ("EOIE-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T05:03Z", "main", "2026-10-24 01:03 main: draft ready as scratch note-edit-expect\\apply.ps1 (dry run on live eoie src: 5 anchors x1). note_guard union NoGuard | ExpectedAt | ExpectedPrefix plus a note_edit_options record; guard_problem is checked before the author check and before any write; a recursive flag parser takes --as, --expect-at and --expect-prefix in any order, replacing the legacy positional --as check; the usage shape is updated. Handed to the eoie team for their train's preview and typecheck (the tree is leased during releases).")
inl note_of_eoie_note_edit_expect_3 () = Note ("EOIE-NOTE-EDIT-EXPECT", 3u32, "2026-10-25T13:57Z", "eoie", "09:57 eoie: main's draft is landed in the live tree and ships in SLATE (the parked note-edit-expect-rebased stage passed the S-stage rule: its crates measured at or under 950 once eoie_cli was fixed; it is in the 5l' full measure, all 234 crates at or under 1000 and cargo check green in one workspace). It adds the note_guard union (NoGuard | ExpectedAt | ExpectedPrefix), --expect-at <ISO> and --expect-prefix <text> parsed in any order beside --as, and the guard checked before the author check and before any write. Left: after install, a CLI run: note-edit with a wrong --expect-at is refused with nothing written, a right one edits, and --expect-prefix behaves the same.")
inl lib_process_handle () = Task ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", "lib/spiral process: a started-process handle for long-lived children (start with stdout and stderr to a log file, OS pid, await exit within a deadline, tree kill by pid from any process), with Rust and Gleam arms", TaskKind, P1, D3, 65u32, Active, "", "lib,process,gleam,tibia,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_process_handle () = Planning ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", Minutes 210u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_1 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T05:19Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:19 tibia: created for TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note 15 and KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR note 21 (both name this gap; a backlog search for process, port and spawn found no item, and WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB's 10-24 survey moves fs and PATH primitives, not this one). Estimate by milestones: lab draft with 2 test blocks green on rust and gleam, 60 m; one eoie do land program plus undo, then the lib gate on process.spi, 60 m; notes and the consumer hook, 30 m. Design: nominal started switched per backend (Rust Rc RefCell Child, Gleam a port plus log path). Owner rule on both backends: only the starting process awaits, because BEAM sends port messages to the owner and Rc is not Send. Gleam drains port data into the log on each await, so await 0 drains and polls. Tree kill is by pid, so a supervisor can kill from another process.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_2 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T05:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 01:34 tibia: milestone 1 is done. Lab spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE is green, 2 of 2 tests on rust and gleam (job tibia-lib-process-handle-lab-3). The tests are a_started_program_logs_and_reports_its_exit and a_running_program_is_killed_by_its_pid. Run 1 lacked the test process static block, so _assert_eq was unbound. Run 2 was green on rust, but the gleam quick test hit badarg in lib's own capture_stdout_list. Its catch-all selector took a {'EXIT', Port, normal} left by the finished port when the test process traps exits. The fix is erlang unlink on the port at start, since the handle owner should not get exit signals from its child. The BEAM await reads only its own port's messages (select_record keyed on the port), so an actor using the handle keeps its other mail. Milestone 2 is in progress. The land program is scratch process-handle-land.ps1: one eoie do edit of process.spi with 3 anchored replaces (Gleam helpers into process_gleam_prelude, the started type and 4 functions before the tests, and the 2 tests at the end), plus a reverse undo.do. It refuses the live path without -Live (control checked), round-trips byte-exact on a copy, and adds no line over 120. The kit gate is queued as heavy job tibia-lib-process-handle-gate (lib-gate-kit gate.ps1 with adapter process-handle-apply.ps1, Files process.spi). It runs after PR-27's live block, then kit land.ps1, which waits for no Cells or live-gui reader.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_3 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T06:22Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 02:22 tibia: the candidate lib process.spi is green, 9 of 9 on rust and gleam (job tibia-process-handle-candidate-2: lib_gate.ps1 -Compiler process on a full lib copy with the 4-edit land applied; a_missing_or_failing_program_captures_nothing stays rust only). Changes since note 2, all driven by tests. (1) start_logging takes a directory first, because canary must run in its own folder and KINO's plan lists cd too (Rust current_dir, BEAM {cd, Dir}). The test a_program_runs_in_its_directory runs python in .. and compares its getcwd with the caller's parent, as a bool so both backends print the same line. (2) On BEAM, await keeps the exit code in the owner's process dictionary, keyed by the port. Exited stays exited, as Rust's try_wait does; before this a second Gleam await after exit waited out its deadline and returned None. (3) A pre-existing lib bug: lib's capture functions left their port linked, so in a process that traps exits the port's {'EXIT', Port, normal} reached the next capture's catch-all reader, which raised badarg (two captures in one test reproduce it). The fix is one home: erlang unlink inside spiral_process_open_with, which covers every lib process function, so start_logging's own unlink is dropped. The land program is now 4 anchored replaces. It round-trips byte-exact and adds no line over 120. The heavy kit gate is tibia-lib-process-handle-gate-2 (scratch kit copy that calls lib_gate with -Compiler process, because the resident daemon failed to start 01:43-02:0x until main's Cells pack). It runs after PR-27's block, then the kit land.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_4 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 4u32, "2026-10-24T07:47Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:47 tibia: kit gate GREEN at 03:40 (job tibia-lib-process-handle-gate-2: candidate process.spi 9 of 9 on rust and gleam, 0 type errors, 17 rows 0 red; the 4-edit land on a fresh lib copy took sha256 a2d87547 to b8bada74). Land queued as job tibia-lib-process-handle-land (scratch kit land.ps1; it waits for two clear 20 s samples with no Cells erl and no live-gui window, then applies). The undo program is scratch process-handle-land\\undo.do (expect files 1 replacements 4).")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_5 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 5u32, "2026-10-24T07:53Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:53 tibia: LAND HELD by GRANULAR-HEAL (user P0, 10-24 03:3x; LIB-MACRO-HEAL rule 5: a new lib def with logic in a macro is a review failure from now on). The gate was GREEN 9 of 9 (note 4), but the slice adds logic in host text. Its Rust arms are multi-statement blocks (the Command chain with File::create and try_clone, the try_wait deadline loop, the cfg taskkill/kill branch), and its Gleam side adds function bodies (start_logging, await with the process-dictionary cache, drain, kill_pid_tree) to process_gleam_prelude, a blob the census already lists. I stopped job tibia-lib-process-handle-land before it applied; lib process.spi is unchanged. Rework, rule-first: every host contact is one named primitive of one call or expression, at most 120 chars, with no casts, arithmetic or flow (open_port, port_info os_pid, unlink, erlang get and put, the selector receive, file write_file with append, os cmd; Command::new, current_dir, args, stdio, spawn, try_wait, id, thread sleep, Instant now), and the deadline loop, the drain loop, the exit cache and the platform choice are Spiral. The same 3 tests must pass on rust and gleam. I asked main for the primitive names its process.spi heal will use, so the two build on one set. Progress back to 60: the behaviour is proven, the shape is not.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_6 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 6u32, "2026-10-24T08:23Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:23 tibia: GRANULAR-HEAL rule (4) applies. A caller search finds 0 callers outside process.spi for its 5 functions and the prelude, in every repo (positive control: the names match in lib process.spi and the two labs). So process.spi's heal is a deletion (slice (a), after the Cells mounts fix lands), and this item's handle lands only together with its first caller, the world_port owner in tibia apps/host (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP, scratch world-port-draft). The granular form is proven in spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL (run 3 compiling) and lands with that consumer. main was told and can keep the capture functions if it knows of a coming consumer.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_7 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 7u32, "2026-10-24T08:37Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:37 tibia: the granular form is GREEN. Lab spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL (job tibia-lib-process-heal-lab-4) passes 5 of 5 on rust and gleam: capture_status_within with list args (quick exit 0, past deadline killed, -1) and the handle (logs and reports exit, with exit kept on a second await; killed by pid from outside; runs in its directory, checked by a marker file written in ..). A rule scan of the module: 70 macros, 0 violations (each one expression, at most 120 chars, no casts, arithmetic or flow), and the Gleam global holds 13 @external declarations and no function bodies. Getting there took 4 runs: a != splice (escaped as \\!=), core length and index on array_base (removed with words_of when args became lists), and a cleanup pass that also caught its own over-deletion. It ran on the default compiler daemon, which confirms main's Cells pack fixed the 01:43 start failures. Per rule (4) the module lands only with its first caller, tibia's world_port owner (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP); slice (a) deletes today's uncalled process.spi (gate tibia-lib-process-delete-gate).")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_8 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 8u32, "2026-10-24T08:59Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:59 tibia: plan changed from delete-then-re-add to one replace, since the consumer is proven (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP note of 2026-10-24 04:59). The deletion gate is stopped; replace gate tibia-lib-process-replace-gate runs the healed module's 5 tests in a lib copy. The land goes with world_port into tibia apps/host in one session.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_9 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 07:00 tibia: per the user's review (GRANULAR-HEAL note 6: every lego piece in its proper file, even at 3x the lines), the replace slice will split by subject before it lands, instead of one granular process.spi. Port primitives (port nominal, port_open, port_unlink, port_os_pid, port_close, port_selector, the message and event accessors) go in a new lib erlang_port.spi after erlang_process. The process dictionary get and put go in erlang_process.spi (a per-process subject). The Gleam file append goes in file_system.spi. Monotonic ms and the Rust Instant and elapsed seconds go in date_time.spi. os_cmd joins env or platform with its kin. The Rust command and child primitives go in a new rust command module beside lib's rust modules. process.spi keeps only the Spiral logic (start_logging, started_pid, await_exit_within, kill_tree_of_pid, capture_status_within) and its 5 tests, placed after all of those in package.spiproj. The lab proof (5 of 5) carries over, because the primitives are unchanged and only their homes move; each new home gets its own small test. The process.spi replace gate (stopped at 06:05) is rebuilt as that multi-file slice in the morning, then lands with world_port.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_10 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 10u32, "2026-10-24T11:18Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 07:18 tibia: grooming re-size by milestones after the user's 06:56 review (split by subject, note 9). Done: lab proof, granular, 5 of 5 on rust and gleam, about 60 min. Left: the multi-file slice (new erlang_port and rust command homes, plus additions to erlang_process, file_system, date_time, and env or platform, each with a small test; process.spi keeps only the Spiral logic), its kit gate and land, about 120 min; then the joint land with world_port into tibia apps/host, about 30 min. Total 210 (from 150), about 30 percent done; progress lowered from 75, because the shape that lands changed even though the behaviour is proven.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_11 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 11u32, "2026-10-24T20:44Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 21:09 Edition-3 lib: main landed the respelling around 20:43 (pre-land copy %TEMP%\\edition3-backup-lib-20261024-204332). At 20:5x tibia released its holds on spiral\\process.spi and spiral\\file_system.spi, so main's hold.txt is empty and main respells both in its second pass without waiting on tibia. Rewriting the tibia drafts for edition 3 is tibia's job: lua-file-exists-apply's anchors (let file_exists, the arm and test lines) and process-handle-land. The lab module lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL/process_healed.spi was respelled with format --spelling symbols at 20:5x; its 5 tests passed but Cells raised a FatalError at the first top-level ':!'. Cause, found by main: Cells' cell.spi:45 and memo.ex:544 classify definitions by keyword prefix, which affects 34 lib files; main is fixing it. Repro: lab/E3-NOMINAL-PROBE. The lab module is back on its edition-2 text (hash A1C7A1B6), copy in scratch edition2-drafts. Once main's Cells fix lands, respell it again and re-run the lab job. UPDATE 2026-10-24 21:21: main's pass 2 landed at 21:16. lib process.spi and file_system.spi are now edition 3 (live residual check green), and the Cells ':!' keyword fix is in the tree. main is verifying it with lab/E3-NOMINAL-PROBE as the positive control, then running lib gates on otp_actor.spi and channel.spi (job-1792891232334-main-serial-cells-e3-verify). Keep lab/E3-NOMINAL-PROBE until main reports green. Next for tibia: re-anchor lua-file-exists-apply and process-handle-land on the live edition-3 text, and once main is green, respell the lab module again and re-run it. UPDATE 2026-10-24 21:35: main's Cells fix is green, and lab/E3-NOMINAL-PROBE is deleted. The lab module is respelled to edition 3 (hash 965DC6CD) and passes 5 of 5 with SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 (job-1792891735514-tibia-lib-process-heal-lab-e3b, exit 0). The lua-file-exists draft is superseded by lua-file-io (TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION note 46). Left here: the multi-file split by subject, written in edition 3.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_12 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 12u32, "2026-10-25T11:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 07:03 Split design refined by a one-home check against the edition-3 lib. lib/spiral/erlang_term.spi already provides term (Dynamic), atom, of and cast (the identity coerce both ways), tuple, equal, apply (any Erlang module:function(args)) and charlist. So the lab module's own Gleam glue (dynamic, gleam_atom, gleam_pair, gleam_dynamic_text/i32/strings, dynamic_i32, dynamic_is_undefined, and its spiral_process_coerce/element externals) is replaced by erlang_term calls rather than moved. Of the 14 @external declarations in process_gleam_declarations, os:cmd, erlang:open_port, port_info, port_close, unlink, monotonic_time, get, put and file:write_file become typed wrappers over erlang_term.apply in their subject homes, so most of the block disappears. Homes: erlang_port.spi (new, after erlang_term and erlang_process): port nominal, open (spawn_executable with options), os_pid, close, unlink, selector and receive with message/event accessors (Gleam process selector calls stay one-call macros). erlang_process.spi: dictionary_get/put. date_time.spi: monotonic_ms (Gleam) beside the Rust Instant it already has, plus elapsed seconds. file_system.spi: the Gleam append through apply 'file' 'write_file' with [append]. An env/platform home: os_cmd. A new rust command module beside lib's rust modules: command new/arg/dir/quiet/no_input/logging/output/spawn and the child try_wait/wait/kill/id primitives. process.spi keeps start_logging, started_pid, await_exit_within, kill_tree_of_pid, capture_status_within and the 5 tests, after all of those in package.spiproj. Written in edition 3; each home gets a small test; the gate is the kit with the same-location baseline. About 150 min left. REVISED 2026-10-25 07:09 after two more one-home checks. (1) lib/spiral/runtime.spi already owns the Rust command family (process_command over std::process::Command, new_process_command, process_command_args, process_stdio, process_output, exit status, stdin/stdout/stderr), so the lab's rust_command_* primitives become additions to runtime.spi in its existing style (current_dir, null stdio, log-file stdio, spawn, child try_wait/wait/kill/id), not a new rust command module. (2) lib/spiral/date_time.spi already has stopwatch over std::time::Instant with stopwatch_elapsed_milliseconds, which replaces the lab's rust_now and rust_elapsed_seconds, so no new timing primitive on Rust. Draft started in spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT: erlang_port.spi built entirely on erlang_term (apply, tuple, atom, of, cast, list_term) plus 4 one-call Gleam process/result macros and an identity Spiral closure as select_record's mapper; its gleam test opens erl -noshell -eval halt(3) as a port, reads os_pid and waits for exit_status 3 (job-1792926397205). STYLE 2026-10-25 07:11: runtime.spi's existing Rust builders (process_command_args, _stdout, _current_dir, _spawn) are older-era drift. They move a std::process::Command by value through 'true; let mut !c = !c' statement macros, which GRANULAR-HEAL forbids copying. A one-call macro can't mutate a moved value, so the additions use the lab's shared shape: a nominal over Rc<RefCell<std::process::Command>> with one-expression steps (drop(!c.borrow_mut().stdout(...)) and similar), placed in runtime.spi beside the old builders. Healing those builders belongs to GRANULAR-HEAL's lib pass, not this slice. FINAL MAP 2026-10-25 07:16 (each checked against lib): file_write's append and truncate reuse file_system.append_all_text and write_all_text, whose Gleam arms exist (port output is a BEAM binary, so a cast to string suffices), with no new primitive. monotonic_ms becomes a Gleam arm on date_time's stopwatch (erlang:monotonic_time in milliseconds), which generalizes it instead of adding a parallel clock. os_cmd goes in platform.spi (os_platform's home) through erlang_term.apply 'os' 'cmd' with a charlist, converted back with unicode:characters_to_binary. dictionary_get/put go in erlang_process.spi through erlang_term.apply 'erlang' 'get'/'put'. Net new lib surface: erlang_port.spi, the shared_command block in runtime.spi, 1 stopwatch arm, 2 dictionary functions, 1 platform function; everything else reuses lib. Lab drafts: spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT/erlang_port.spi and shared_command.spi (tests running, job-1792926858525).")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_13 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 13u32, "2026-10-25T12:20Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 08:20 SPLIT PROVEN in lab (job-1792929533406, 10/10 with memo off): spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-SPLIT holds erlang_port.spi (proven alone on gleam: erl port, os_pid, exit_status 3), shared_command.spi (proven alone on rust: spawn, id, exit code 4), split_misc.spi (dictionary get/put and os_command_output pass on gleam; the clock test had a precedence bug in the test, fixed) and process_split.spi (the 5 original behaviour tests: quick exit, deadline kill, logging, kill by pid, working directory). Because Cells compiles a single file as the main package, sibling lab modules aren't visible, so the proof ran on process_split_all.spi, the four concatenated with module prefixes stripped. In lib they are separate modules, as planned. Both backends pass all 5 behaviour tests on the new homes (erlang_term-based ports, the shared command, date_time.stopwatch for Rust timing, threading.sleep, file_system append and write for the Gleam log). Next: the anchored lib slice in edition 3. It adds erlang_port.spi (new module after erlang_term and erlang_process), runtime.spi's shared_command block, erlang_process's dictionary_get/put, platform's os_command_output, date_time's monotonic milliseconds (as the stopwatch Gleam arm or a named function), and replaces process.spi's body with process_split's logic and its 5 tests. Kit gate with the same-location baseline, then land; then the world_port joint land into tibia apps/host (BACKEND-MAP).")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_14 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 14u32, "2026-10-25T14:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 10:28 Anchored lib slice ready and round-tripped byte-exact on a copy (scratch process-handle-apply.ps1; land.do 13 replacements in 6 files plus copy.do for the new erlang_port.spi; undo.do restores all 6 by hash, then erlang_port.spi is removed by literal path). Two design corrections from reading the live lib: (1) module order: erlang_term and erlang_process sat at 111-112, after platform, date_time and process, so they move up right after sm'_operators (erlang_term, erlang_process, erlang_port; neither has lib callers outside host strings), and process moves below file_system_operators (its started handles log through file_system; no lib module calls process.). (2) process.spi is merged, not replaced: the capture family (capture_stdout, _list, combined, try_capture_combined_list, used by eoie) stays; capture_status_within becomes the granular erlang_port/runtime version with list arguments (no callers outside lib tests), start_logging, started_pid and await_exit_within are added, and the prelude loses the status-only Gleam glue (now, wait, kill_tree, status_within and 5 externals, plus the unused int import). Homes: os_command_output in platform, monotonic_milliseconds in date_time (Gleam arms, failwith elsewhere), shared commands in runtime before its main. Kit gate job-1792938470349 over process, erlang_port, erlang_process, erlang_term, platform, date_time, runtime; the kit now snapshots its baseline at gate start.")
inl note_of_lib_process_handle_15 () = Note ("LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE", 15u32, "2026-10-25T16:22Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 12:21 Both kit gates queued earlier failed before testing anything: lib_gate.ps1 needs the file name with its extension ('no common under -Lib ... a missing file runs zero tests and reads as green'), and the kit passed bare module names. Fixed in the kit (gate.ps1 appends .spi when no extension), so the later time_span and date-casts gates are unaffected. The drafts also moved to list sugar per the new list-sugar rule (process tests, world_port, world_executor; the heal slice converts the 3 pre-existing capture tests and enables gleam on the missing-program test). Requeued as one serial runner, job-1792945250381: random gate -> lands -> family 3 -> dead helpers; process gate -> split land -> world_port -> world_executor; then the process heal gate and land.")
inl python_nullary_shared () = Task ("PYTHON-NULLARY-SHARED", "Python output shares one instance per nullary union case (UH0_End_shared) instead of building a NamedTuple per construction, mirroring next9/next10's nullary singletons on C, Zig, Kotlin, Lua, Wasm, Delphi and TypeScript", Story, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "PACK-NEXT-2", "compiler,python,codegen,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_python_nullary_shared () = Planning ("PYTHON-NULLARY-SHARED", Minutes 60u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_python_nullary_shared_1 () = Note ("PYTHON-NULLARY-SHARED", 1u32, "2026-10-24T06:42Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:5x main: drafted and composed into pack 5g. %TEMP%\\pack-next5g\\python-nullary-shared.ps1 (3 anchors, the construction one scoped to module CodegenPython, because the CUDA backend's Python host has the same TyUnionBox text). Every nullary heap/stack union case gets `<Case>_shared = <Case>()` right after its NamedTuple class, and a nullary construction emits `<Case>_shared` instead of `<Case>()`. Nullness is read from the case's payload array (as next9 does), not from the argument text. NamedTuple values are immutable, so sharing is safe, and `case <Case>():` patterns still match the class. Rides review-4 (job requeued after tibia's block); the matrix's Python rows are its behaviour check, and main.py diffs should change only at nullary constructions.")
inl lib_macro_heal () = Task ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", "Heal lib/spiral's logic-in-macros drift (user 10-24: like eoie's rust_global): 69 definitions hold control flow inside \\$'...' text (59,479 chars, 22.6 % of lib is macro text), plus helper preludes kept as global blobs; host code shrinks to named one-call primitives and the logic moves into Spiral over lib", Epic, P0, D7, 0u32, Planned, "", "lib,spiral,heal,macros,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_macro_heal () = Planning ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", Minutes 2400u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_1 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T07:08Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:08 main: user direction 2026-10-24 03:0x: \"we started ruining lib's code stuffing a lot of code inside macros, same way eoie drifted with rust_global, unhealable drift that we're still dealing with there. look at async.start_child_rust geez, prioritize healing ALL of this\". Census (scratch macro-census.ps1 over 107 lib .spi/.spir files): macro text is 193,899 of 856,114 definition chars (22.6 %); 962 definitions use a macro; 69 hold LOGIC inside a macro (3+ statements or 2+ control keywords), 59,479 chars (list in scratch macro-logic.tsv). Largest: sm'_real.format_real 12,866 (171 lines), sm'_real rec 4,109, runtime.execute_with_options_rust 2,276, leptos svg loading render 2,168, async.start_child_rust 2,072, date_time.time_span_format 1,908, trace.trace 1,505, unicode.char_of 1,365, trace.init_trace_state 1,184, sm'.index_of 1,141, common.random' 1,114, base.try_finally 1,102, convert.convert_i32_base 1,072, guid.new_raw_guid 1,045, resultm.unwrap_err 996, base.try_unit 989, resultm.get 968, file_system.walk_dir_filter 952, sm'.split 948, async.catch_rust 905. Not counted as logic but in scope: Gleam helper preludes kept as global blobs (process.process_gleam_prelude 5,839, file_system.file_system_gleam_paths 1,550). Out of scope: F# export `main` stubs (one let per exported name) and one-call backend arms ($'Async.Sleep').\nRULE, written before any heal: (1) host code stays only as a named primitive whose macro is ONE host call or expression, at most 120 chars, and does no control flow; (2) everything else (statements, loops, matches, panic and timeout handling, helper preludes) is Spiral over lib, with the primitives as its only host contact; (3) order: async.start_child_rust first (user-named), then by logic chars descending, a file's defs together when they share primitives; (4) acceptance per heal: every test the def had passes on every backend it had, the generated output for its tests is reviewed (no behavior change), the lib Cells gate is green, and no new macro exceeds rule (1); (5) a new lib def with logic in a macro is a review failure from now on. Progress = healed logic chars / 59,479, plus the 2 preludes.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_2 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T07:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:37 main: under GRANULAR-HEAL; rule extended by the user's cast remark (env.command_line_argument_count Rust arm (std::env::args().count() as i32) - 1): no as-casts and no arithmetic in macro text either. Rule (6): authorship is irrelevant (user 03:3x: don't keep old code just because I wrote it). Census now 715 sites, 60,477 chars; top files sm'_real.spir 44/15,447, file_system 55/7,928, sm' 69/6,033, trace 27/5,649, date_time 43/2,373, base 48/2,331. start_child_rust: 0 callers in every repo since the 10-23 unused removal took start_child/start_child_timeout, so rule (4) deletes it; catch_rust and run_synchronously (used by catch' and start) are healed first.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_3 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-24T08:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:17 tibia: process.spi's heal moved to tibia, prelude included, by main's handover (04:0x), and it absorbs LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's slice. Lab spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL/process_healed.spi is the whole module rewritten under this item's rule. The Gleam global keeps only imports and @external declarations (coerce, open_port, element, find_executable, halt, port_info, port_close, unlink, os cmd, monotonic_time, get, put, file write_file). Every host contact is a named primitive of one expression: nominals dynamic, port, bits, gleam_strings, port_options, selector, received, plus rust_command, rust_output, rust_try_output, rust_file, rust_instant, started. The verbs follow main's suggested shape (port_open, port_unlink, port_os_pid, port_selector, selector_receive, file_write, os_cmd, rust_command_new and friends, rust_command_spawn, rust_child_exited and rust_child_code). The logic is Spiral: the argv filter, the collect and drain loops, the exit cache, the deadline loop, and kill_tree_of_pid choosing taskkill or kill through platform.is_windows. It carries the module's 6 existing tests plus the handle's 3, on the same backends. Lab run 2 is queued after run 1's two parse and type errors (a != splice and an array type in words_of).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_4 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-24T08:32Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:32 tibia: rule (4) agreed with main (04:2x). Slice (a) removes lib process.spi: all 6 of its definitions have 0 callers in every repo. Scratch process-delete-apply.ps1: one eoie do edit drops process from package.spiproj, both files are backed up for undo, and the file is removed by literal path. Its guard refuses if any lib code outside macro text still calls process.*; the first runs flagged 15 lines, all host text (TypeScript process.stdout and argv, Zig std.process, Gleam process.*), and the strip now covers single-line and unterminated multi-line macros. Proven on a lib copy: process.spi removed, spiproj lists runtime then file_system. Kit gate job tibia-lib-process-delete-gate (gates runtime.spi to show the package still loads); the land goes after the Cells mounts fix. The lab is trimmed to what lands later with world_port: capture_status_within (now taking list args) plus the handle, 5 tests, with no unused definitions left (two cleanup rounds; a removal that ate 4 adjacent nominals was caught and restored). Lab run 4 is compiling.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_5 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-24T08:40Z", "main", "2026-10-24 04:40 main: heal slices drafted and queued as gate-kit gate+land pairs (land waits for Cells erl, live-gui windows and now running eoie-release-* trains, which read the live lib): (1) LIB-UNICODE-GRANULAR %TEMP%\\lib-unicode-granular (char_of validity + UTF-8 encode in Spiral, one host call per arm; new width test) job-1792829476802; (2) LIB-ENV-ARGV %TEMP%\\lib-env-argv-granular (host_argument_count/host_argument one-call arms, offsets and +/- in Spiral, Gleam fn body gone) job-1792829682792; (3) LIB-ASYNC-GRANULAR %TEMP%\\lib-async-granular (poll/context/waker/panic-hook primitives; run_synchronously via rust.loop, catch_rust via a capture-style poll_fn, ignore/sleep via new_future_move; start_child_rust deleted by rule 4; threading.sleep cast -> conv; 3 new Rust tests) job-1792830257981; (4) RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE %TEMP%\\lib-default-clone (default_value' Rust clones via rust.clone; Lua/Python/TS branch in Spiral over one-op is_none') job-1792830472144; (5) LIB-FLOAT-NATIVE %TEMP%\\lib-float-native (format_real: 11 float arms -> one float_native.shortest call; new module float_native.spi: is_nan/is_infinite/native shortest one-calls, precision search for Lua/C/Delphi and scaling search for Lean in Spiral; composes the float-wire slice; gated on all 10 float-wire files, superseding float-wire gates 2-4) job-1792831183650. Withdrawn: lib-otp-single (Rust mailbox blobs), dictm land (x rebuilt it granular, 0 violations, pending its Cells check), reactor-actor Gleam clock arm.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_6 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-24T10:27Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:27 main: more slices drafted and queued: LIB-SM-LINES-GRANULAR (line_count/line_at/nonempty_* over split/ends_with/slice, last_index_of and percent_encode in Spiral; Gleam fn bodies gone), LIB-SM-INDEX-GRANULAR (index_of on 6 backends, substring casts; composes sm-lines), LIB-BASE-TRY-GRANULAR (one home for the Rust panic primitives; catch_unwind_rust, try_unit, try_finally in Spiral; async rebased onto it), LIB-CRYPTO-GRANULAR (Gleam fn bodies inlined as call expressions; hmac block as primitives), LIB-TRACE-GRANULAR (TS clock, NEAR chunk cast), LIB-XML-GRANULAR (code_point_at/width; composes unicode). The queue is collapsed into serial runners (eoie's process-pile request): main-serial-old-lib-chain, -heal-gates, -heal-lands (dependency order), -other-heavies, -xml; manifests and logs are scratch serial-*.json/.log. Census correction: my first census read only tracked files; with untracked lib modules it is 738 sites / 69,262 chars (new: process.spi 7,304, which tibia is replacing; unicode 600; xml 149; erlang_process/otp_actor/otp_supervisor are thunks around one call, allowed by rule 2, except erlang_process's ProcessDown/PortDown case bridge). Left for later, each needing a platform test first: trace's Rust local clock and NEAR log helper, env.environment_restored's Rust block, sm's Rust lines/nonempty_lines closures.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_7 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-24T10:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:37 main: next lib target by size (not started; plan written for the next pass). guid.spi: (1) new_raw_guid's Zig and Rust arms do the v4 bit layout and hex formatting in host text. Move it to Spiral as guid_v4_text (a b : u64) : string, with the masks, shifts and version/variant bits in Spiral bit ops and hex text from a Spiral digit loop, reusing sm's hex_digit once LIB-SM-LINES lands. (2) Per backend, only random_u64 stays host. Zig keeps `var spiral_random_state` as a declaration, the xorshift step moves to Spiral over a state get/set primitive pair, and the OS seed becomes a rule-3c platform helper. Rust uses RandomState hasher primitives (new/build_hasher, write_u128, finish), each one call, with the SystemTime nanos through unwrap_or_default. Tests: guid's rust/zig/fsharp/gleam rows plus a format check (8-4-4-4-12, version 4, variant 8-b).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_8 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-24T10:41Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:41 main: guid plan of note 7 drafted after all (%TEMP%\\lib-guid-granular): guid_v4_text and guid_hex in Spiral; Rust random through one-call hasher primitives (rust_unix_nanos, build_hasher, write_u128/u64, finish); Zig keeps zig_random_u64 for now (its xorshift/seed declaration is the remaining guid site). Serial runner main-serial-guid (gate on guid.spi, then land).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_9 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-24T15:41Z", "main", "main 12:0x: process.spi (user 06:56 list: the multiline block) heal A queued as job main-serial-process-a (gate files process.spi,runtime.spi). The Rust arms now compose the user's own runtime.spi Command lego (new_process_command, process_command_args, setters, spawn, output/status accessors) plus new one-call pieces in runtime.spi: Stdio::null, Command::output, try_wait, kill, wait, exit; process_command_stdout/stderr now use their stdio argument (they ignored it and always piped). Argument filtering, success, UTF-8 decoding and the deadline loop (core loop.while' over try_wait and date_time.stopwatch, no raw-brace injection) are Spiral. Probe on a lib copy: Rust compile + rustc + run gives (true, 2 a b, warned, None, None, 0, -1) = every test expectation. Census note: macro-rule.ps1 missed these arms (it does not follow \\ continuations), so its lib count is low. Heal B next: the 165-line Gleam program in process_gleam_prelude becomes one-call @external primitives with the port/selector logic in Spiral over erlang_process (needs module order: process after erlang_process, or a new erlang_port module).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_10 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 10u32, "2026-10-24T15:47Z", "main", "main 12:1x: correction to note 9: process slice A is withdrawn and its job stopped before it took a slot (no live change). process.spi's heal was handed to the tibia team at 04:0x (note 3) and rule (4) already decided it: 0 callers outside process.spi in every repo, so today's module is deleted (tibia-lib-process-delete-gate) and the GREEN granular module in spiral/lab/LIB-PROCESS-HEAL lands with its first caller (TIBIA-BACKEND-MAP). I missed note 3 because I searched the backlog for the file name and not the item tree. The slice A runtime pieces (Stdio::null, Command::output, try_wait, kill, wait, exit) stay as a draft in %TEMP%\\lib-process-granular-a for whoever lands the lab module's Rust half.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_11 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 11u32, "2026-10-24T16:11Z", "main", "main 12:4x: run-the-binary probes ahead of the queued heal gates (CPU minutes instead of gate slots). base-try (queued in main-serial-heal-gates) had 2 hidden failures, both fixed in %TEMP%\\lib-base-try-granular before its slot: (1) Rust: catch_unwind_rust returned rust_caught (std::thread::Result, not Clone) out of rust_quietly's if, and Spiral clones branch results, so rustc E0599 x4; the conversion now happens inside the quiet thunk. (2) Lua: the healed arms ended in $'nil' : t, a bare nil statement Lua rejects; restored the original IIFE (function() return nil end)() (one expression, so within the rule). Probe results: Rust (Some 7, Some -1, true, true) with 0 stderr lines; Lua (true, true). sm-lines (RED 11:55: lines code used split before its definition, and arraym.length) fixed and requeued as main-serial-sm-lines-2 after Rust+Gleam probes matched every expectation.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_12 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 12u32, "2026-10-24T16:18Z", "main", "main 12:2x: sm-index (queued in main-serial-heal-gates) would have thrown at apply: its lines/nonempty_lines anchor was the pre-format text, but LIB-FORMAT-IDLE (landed 08:55) wrapped nonempty_lines' macro with a \\ continuation; and moving lines/nonempty_lines below the healed helpers stranded the test lines_follow_rust_line_rules above them. The apply now cuts the span by position (inl lines .. that test, checked by content markers) and moves the test with the definitions. Composed with sm-lines on one fresh copy: order split 1389, line_parts 1455, lines 1482, test 1500, line_count 1509. Probe: Rust ((2, 2, -1, 1), (bcd, ), 3, 2, a, b, 0) and Gleam ((2, 2, -1, 1), bcd, ) match every expectation; Lua has no substring arm on the tree either (gap, not a regression: the test runs on gleam/rust/fsharp only).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_13 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 13u32, "2026-10-24T17:43Z", "main", "main 13:4x: date_time.spi heal 1 (rule (4) order after sm') queued as job main-serial-date-time-span: hours/minutes/seconds/milliseconds Python, Rust and TypeScript arms are one host call each (seconds/microseconds field, num_hours..num_milliseconds, the ms number) with the /, % and casts in Spiral (conv; annotated : i32 per arm since backend_switch record arms do not unify). Probes per accessor, each in its own program: TypeScript (node) 10/11/12/13 after widening the ms arm to i64 (conv of a 3.4e10 ms span to i32 wrapped to -355); Rust through a chrono cargo crate built --offline 10/11/12/13. Python not run locally (its generated main imports a main_auto harness module), covered by the gate's cuda target. Unified unicode landed 12:51 (GREEN 12:48, live = draft).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_14 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 14u32, "2026-10-24T18:08Z", "main", "main 14:0x: date_time heal 2 queued (job main-serial-date-time-span-2): Rust tick math as one-call num_seconds/subsec_nanos with the scaling in Spiral (rust_time_delta_ticks, used by total_seconds and days), add_days' cast through conv, stopwatch_elapsed_milliseconds from as_secs/subsec_millis (no u128 as i64), F# to_timestamp and time_span_of_micros arithmetic in Spiral. Two new tests (spans_convert_to_seconds_and_days, add_days_moves_the_timestamp; F# and Rust): the first exposed a PRE-EXISTING bug, total_seconds truncated to whole seconds (n / 10000000 in i64 before the f64 conversion: 1.5 s gave 1.0 on Rust and F#); fixed as (conv n : f64) / 10000000f64; its one caller converts to i32 anyway. Probes per body: Rust (chrono 0.4.45 cargo crate, --offline) and F# (dotnet fsi) give (1.5, 1.0), 1792152000000000, (1791892800000000, 0); the stopwatch reads 25..5000 ms after a 30 ms sleep. Lua and Python/Cuda arms left for a slice that can run them.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_15 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 15u32, "2026-10-24T18:18Z", "main", "main 14:1x: unicode heal LANDED 12:51 (GREEN 12:48). xml heal run 5 RED 13:57 was my own cross-slice break: the unicode heal changed rust_char_code's argument to the new rust_scalar nominal (Spiral char is u8 in Rust), and the xml slice still annotated option' char. Fixed (rust_scalar in the xml helpers and its unicode-presence check), plus a Zig fix found by the probe: convert.convert i32 -> convert.unativeint is identity on Zig (try_convert has no Zig mapping for the nominal), so the index stayed i32; the Zig helper now indexes with conv to u64 (Zig coerces u64 to usize). Probe: Rust (1, 5) and (2, 1); Zig (2, 1). code_point_count has no Zig arm on the tree either (same error on the baseline; the xml Zig tests do not call it). Requeued as main-serial-xml-6. Lib gap noted: convert.convert to unativeint on Zig.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_16 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 16u32, "2026-10-24T18:42Z", "main", "main 14:4x: the post-land document_test (main-document, 14:25) is still 15/17: today's lands broke the Cells livemd round trip of two more files, which the lib gate never checked: async.spi (the async heal's primitives block ends without the blank line before /// ### ignore) and the sm'.spi / sm'_real.spir pair (a missing blank line before /// ### (~#?) and a .spir heading sequence that no longer mirrors the .spi's format_fixed_digits). Fixes: lib-async-roundtrip queued (token-neutral blank line); the sm' pair waits until the 4 pending sm'.spi slices land (canonicalizing it now would move their anchors). The gate kit now runs %TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit\\roundtrip.exs on every touched .spi/.spir (candidate and tree): a file that round-trips on the tree and not on the candidate turns the verdict RED. Positive control: tree async.spi differs, fixed copy ok, json.spi ok.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_17 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 17u32, "2026-10-24T20:28Z", "main", "main 16:3x, correction to note 16 for the sm' pair: not a regression from today's lands. HEAD fails the same document_test; the lib-pair loop stops at its first failure, so sm' surfaced only after json/routes landed. Cause: sm'_real.spir kept 7 empty mirror headings with no .spi counterpart (index_of_char, is_white_space, builder_append_line, char_is_alphanumeric, os_string_as_ref, base64_decode, utf8_decode; their .spi sections were removed before HEAD). apps/cells/spi/document.spi interleave walks the .spir headings and, when one is absent from the rest of the .spi, appends the remaining .spir cells at the end, so the canonical form was not even a fixed point. Slice %TEMP%\\lib-sm-roundtrip\\apply.ps1: drop only empty unmatched .spir headings (refuses if one has content), then write Cells' canonical pair (a fixed point; code lines unchanged, checked; the installed formatter leaves it unchanged; idempotent). Queued: main-serial-sm-roundtrip (gate + land). Rule for later pairs: every .spir heading needs a .spi twin, or the interleave appends the rest.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_18 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 18u32, "2026-10-24T21:49Z", "main", "main 17:5x: LIB-SM-ROUNDTRIP landed (gate GREEN 17:15, land 17:47). Live check: the tree's sm'.spi / sm'_real.spir pair is a Cells canonical fixed point and round-trips byte for byte through livemd (both hashes equal), which is what the document_test lib-pair loop asserts. The next pair in that loop, if any, is the next thing to look at in the post-land document_test.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_19 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 19u32, "2026-10-25T11:39Z", "x", "2026-10-25 07:39 -04:00 x: two observations from x's code_points draft (X-GRANULAR-HEAL n10), both in lib/spiral/unicode.spi after the 10-24 unicode heal. (1) first_scalar's Gleam arm is still one macro chaining four host calls: option.from_result(result.map(list.first(string.to_utf_codepoints(s)), string.utf_codepoint_to_int)). With x's draft primitives gleam_codepoints and gleam_codepoint_code, it becomes Spiral over a host-list head bridge. (2) grapheme_list's Rust arm is a graphemes(..).map(..).collect() chain inside Rc::new; x's rust_scalars uses the same chars().collect() shape, so whichever rule decides that one (iterator chain = one host expression or not) applies to both. x lands code_points and is_white_space into unicode.spi with its gate; it doesn't touch first_scalar or grapheme_list.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_20 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 20u32, "2026-10-25T15:26Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:26 tibia takes date_time.spi's time_span_format, its largest host-text logic (rule 4: chars descending). Its TypeScript and Rust arms (two 6-line host programs computing days, hours, minutes, seconds and 100 ns ticks with sign, c format and hh/mm/ss substitution) become one Spiral time_span_ticks_text over i64 ticks, with one primitive per backend: rust_time_span_nanoseconds (num_nanoseconds) and typescript_time_span_milliseconds (the number itself); ticks = nanoseconds / 100 or milliseconds * 10000 in Spiral. Equivalent for whole milliseconds, which is all TypeScript's Date subtraction gives. New test time_span_format_custom_and_negative (rust, typescript; F# excluded because .NET rejects hh-mm-ss as a custom format) pins hh-mm-ss substitution and a negative c format. Slice scratch time-span-heal-apply.ps1 (2 replacements, round trip byte-exact); kit gate job-1792941907856 on date_time, land job-1792941929670 after it. Its anchors don't overlap LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's monotonic_milliseconds insertion, so either can land first.")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_21 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 21u32, "2026-10-25T15:32Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:32 date_time.spi second slice, rule (2) casts: the Rust arms of min_value, max_value, unix_epoch, date_time_milliseconds and date_time_utc chained chrono calls with 'as u32' casts inside host text. They become Spiral over five primitives (naive_date_rust; rust_naive_date year month day; rust_date_at, rust_date_at_milli and rust_date_at_nano, one chrono call each) with the casts as conv in Spiral; 0 'as u32' left. New ///> rust -d chrono test calendar_constructors_agree_on_rust (date_time_utc and date_time_milliseconds at 1970-01-01 equal unix_epoch; min_value and max_value run). Slice scratch date-casts-heal-apply.ps1 (7 replacements, round trip byte-exact; anchors don't overlap the time_span or monotonic slices). Gate then land in one job: job-1792942299778 (kit gate-then-land.ps1, the reusable runner from now on).")
inl note_of_lib_macro_heal_22 () = Note ("LIB-MACRO-HEAL", 22u32, "2026-10-25T18:14Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 14:14 Both date_time gates went RED for kit reasons, and the cells show what each slice actually did. (1) Kit bug: gate.ps1 piped lib_gate into Select-Object -First 16, which stops the upstream script once 16 lines match, so with many reds lib_gate died before copying tests.tsv (the metrics dirs were left in %TEMP%); fixed by capturing the whole lib_gate output to <raw>.gate.log before filtering. (2) The surviving tests.tsv: time_span slice: time_span_format and _2 'failed' only on typescript, as 300 s cell timeouts (compile 190-209 s of each cell under load), while _3 and the new custom-and-negative test passed on rust and typescript; date-casts slice: its new test had a real type error ('=' needs primitive types; date_time is a backend_switch host type), now rewritten to compare chrono %f text for utc, milliseconds 250, unix_epoch, min_value and max_value. The other reds (unix_epoch cuda, date_time_milliseconds fsharp/cuda, to_timestamp*, hours, minutes) flip between the two runs, i.e. load. New gate rule, written before the next run: a candidate-only red counts only if it is red again when its test runs alone (lib_gate.ps1 gained an optional -Test list for --test; additive). Both slices now run in serial-b (job-1792945250381) after the process steps.")
inl granular_heal () = Task ("GRANULAR-HEAL", "Heal host-text drift in every project once and for all (user 2026-10-24 03:0x-03:3x: lib drift like eoie rust_global, unhealable; look at async.start_child_rust; even casts should go through Spiral's type system; the whole purpose of lib is to be granular; groom the backlog to heal this once and for all in all projects including eoie; make it properly granular even if it takes five times more tokens or calls): every Spiral project ends with host macros as one-call primitives and its logic in granular typed Spiral, with a lint that keeps it there", Epic, P0, D7, 30u32, Active, "GRANULAR-LINT,LIB-MACRO-HEAL,EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION,DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL,ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL,APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL,POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL,X-GRANULAR-HEAL,TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", "granular,macros,lib,eoie,x,tibia,dice,alphabet,polyglot,apps,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_granular_heal () = Planning ("GRANULAR-HEAL", Minutes 9600u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T07:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:37 main: Rule (pre-written, applied mechanically): (1) a host macro ($'...', $\"...\", global, rust_global) is a named primitive holding ONE host call or expression, at most 120 chars, with no as-casts, no arithmetic, no if/match/loop/let/;/blocks; type text in type position is exempt. (2) casts go through conv and the convert types, arithmetic and control flow are Spiral, a host sum type is bridged to a Spiral union by one primitive per constructor and per test. (3) global/rust_global blocks keep only host declarations Spiral cannot express (imports, extern items); function bodies move to Spiral over lib. (4) order per project: file by violating chars descending; a definition with 0 callers by stem across all repos is deleted instead of healed. (5) slice acceptance: tests pass on the same backends, generated output reviewed, the file's census count drops, the lint baseline only shrinks. (6) authorship is irrelevant: user-era and agent-era code are healed alike, the style is what is kept; extra primitives, lines, calls and model hits are the accepted price.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T07:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:37 main census (scratchpad macro-rule.ps1 over tracked .spi/.spir; flow regex also counts -> in type text, so flow is an upper bound): sites/chars - eoie/src 786/1,050,302 (202 blocks, 247 casts); spiral/lib 715/60,477 (40 blocks, 69 casts, 106 arith); dice 79/7,187; spiral/apps 63/2,875; alphabet 34/1,340; x 32/2,916; polyglot 13/3,476; tibia 5/232; fiscal/src and blog 0.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-24T08:20Z", "main", "2026-10-24 04:20 main: rule (2) refinement, written before its first use (async heal): Rust has no expression that extracts a payload from a host sum type, so a bridge primitive may hold ONE match whose arms only map host constructors to Spiral-visible constructors (e.g. Poll::Ready(v) => Some(v), _ => None), with no other logic. A closure inside a macro is allowed only as a thunk around ONE call that the host API requires as a closure (catch_unwind(AssertUnwindSafe(|| poll)), set_hook(Box::new(|_| ()))). Identity retags between two lib types with the same host type ($'!x' : t) are type bridges, as in the user-era rust.from_ref. Everything else stays under rule (1).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-24T08:56Z", "main", "2026-10-24 04:56 main: rule (3b), written before its first use (base.try_*): when a host offers an exception construct only as syntax (F# try/with/finally, Python try/except/finally), ONE such construct whose every body is a single call is allowed, as an expression where the host has one (F#) or as a one-construct helper def where it does not (Python). Lua pcall and Rust catch_unwind are calls, so their results go through one-call primitives and the branching is Spiral. Known trade-off recorded with the heal: the Rust quiet catch swaps the panic hook per call instead of the old once-installed thread-local counter, so two threads catching at the same moment can leave the silent hook installed; Spiral Rust programs that catch across threads should be checked when they appear.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-24T09:07Z", "main", "2026-10-24 05:07 main: rule (3c), answering x on FS-3: a cfg-gated host declaration is allowed when Spiral cannot express the platform split (Rust type-checks both arms of a runtime cfg!() branch, so platform-only APIs need #[cfg]): every variant has the same name and signature and a body of ONE call (e.g. #[cfg(windows)]/#[cfg(unix)] spiral_fs_symlink_dir), and the result handling stays in Spiral. REPL/module export bindings (let name x = !name x) are declarations, not flow. Wrapper types (SpiralAssertSend) are declarations if their impls hold no logic.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-24T10:56Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:56 main: process.spi's big multiline block is the user's example of drift: granular even at 3x the time and lines, every lego piece in its proper file (tibia's process replace slice follows this) (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)")
inl note_of_granular_heal_7 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-25T07:11Z", "main", "10-25 03:11 main: LESSON from LIB-ZIG-INDEX-OF: a heal that rewrites a backend arm must run a test declaring that backend; sm'.index_of's Zig arm was healed into a Rust-only call and passed the gate because its test lists gleam/fsharp/cuda/rust/typescript only. Rule for heal drafts: for each Zig/Lua/Gleam/C arm touched, grep the nearest test's ///> lines for that target, add it when missing.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_8 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-25T15:13Z", "x", "2026-10-25 11:13 -04:00 x: x's part of the heal, re-censused on tracked .spi (scratchpad x-heal-census.ps1). Rule (1) leaves 3 sites flagged, and the census missed Gleam 'case' as flow, which adds 3 more. All of them are in glyphs.spi except wire.spi:42, a single serde_json::json! call whose braces are a JSON literal, which is one call under rule (1), so it stays. Rule (3) adds glyphs.spi's Gleam global function bodies: x_light, x_white, x_spaced, plus the binary:split extern and its XSplitScope type. Healed in ONE draft, the code-points patch (scratchpad\\code-points\\apply.ps1, lib unicode plus x glyphs, dry run on copies clean). plain_weight = a Spiral fold over lib unicode.code_points with a Spiral light/code_point_weight. blank = unicode.is_white_space over code points, the union of Rust's trim() set and the Gleam x_spaced set. split_all = lib sm'.split_list on both backends, which replaces the Gleam binary:split bridge, its three case macros and listed_pieces. glyphs.spi goes 190 -> 134 lines with 0 host macros and 0 globals, lines over 120 go 9 -> 4, and nothing outside glyphs calls the removed names (grep over C:\\home\\git, positive control: glyphs.spi itself). Noted, not changed: prelude.split_on skips i+1, so it is only right for 1-byte separators; sm'.split_list uses the separator's width. Folding it into lib is X-LIB-FIRST-PRELUDE's job. Gate when lib is free of Cells readers: Cells on lib unicode (rust+gleam), then x glyphs, splitter and reading (rust+gleam). After that x has 0 rule-1/rule-3 sites.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_9 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-25T15:17Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 11:17 lib process.spi heal drafted and queued (GRANULAR-HEAL in lib, after LIB-PROCESS-HANDLE's split). Scratch process-heal-apply.ps1 (4 replacements in process.spi and runtime.spi, round-tripped byte-exact on a post-split copy) deletes the Gleam prelude (about 130 lines of Gleam source in a global string: externals, open/collect/captured/try_capture glue) and the four host-text Rust blocks of capture_stdout, capture_stdout_list, capture_combined_list and try_capture_combined_list, and rebuilds them in Spiral over the split's primitives: one captured record from captured_by (Rust: runtime shared command output; Gleam: erlang_port with binary, exit_status, args and optional stderr_to_stdout, collected until exit_status), required exits 3 on failure like before (new exit_with: std::process::exit / erlang:halt), try_capture returns None when the program is missing or fails. argument_separator goes (no callers outside process.spi). runtime's command_output becomes Rc-wrapped so it can be read several times, plus ran, code, stdout and stderr readers (one expression each). process.spi: 413 to 318 lines, host macros 17 to 8 (all one expression), Gleam globals 7 to 0, lines over 120 3 to 0. API unchanged for eoie's try_capture_combined_list callers. Gate and land: job-1792941398810 (kit gate on process and runtime, then land), queued after the split's serial.")
inl granular_lint () = Task ("GRANULAR-LINT", "eoie lint macros <paths>: classify every host macro by GRANULAR-HEAL rule (1)-(3) (long, flow, cast, arith, block), per-repo baseline file so only NEW violations fail, JSON/TSV out; wired into the PostToolUse style check and the lib gate kit acceptance, so drift cannot re-enter", Feature, P0, D4, 55u32, Active, "", "granular,macros,eoie,lint,hooks,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_granular_lint () = Planning ("GRANULAR-LINT", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_granular_lint_1 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 1u32, "2026-10-24T07:37Z", "main", "2026-10-24 03:37 main: prototype is the scratchpad macro-rule.ps1 classifier (regexes for flow/cast/arith; tracked files via git ls-files; build dirs skipped). Port it to Spiral in eoie as a generic verb (no project names), drop the -> false positive by skipping type-position macros, then baseline each repo with the GRANULAR-HEAL census.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_2 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:35Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:35 main: lessons for the verb, from the prototype: (1) count untracked files too. New agent modules are untracked until the user commits, and my tracked-only census missed 23 lib sites / 8.8k chars (process.spi, unicode, xml, the otp modules). (2) Do not walk untracked trees with git ls-files --others in repos whose large caches are not gitignored (eoie's 5.5 GB .cache took over 30 min); take the package manifests' module lists, or skip known cache dirs before listing. (3) The flow regex has false positives on type text (Rc<dyn Fn()>, -> in signatures), data literals ((char[]){ !b, 0 }) and allowed thunks; classify by AST position (type vs term macro) and by the rule 2/3 exceptions instead of regexes. (4) Report per definition and per added line of a diff, so a slice's check covers only what it adds (slice-macro-check.ps1 in scratch does that for apply scripts).")
inl note_of_granular_lint_3 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 3u32, "2026-10-24T21:10Z", "main", "main 17:2x: draft one-call case for the verb, in eoie/spec/one-call.md form, offered to the eoie team to adopt (their spec file; nothing edited there). Fixture lib/sample.spi lines: (1) inl ok (x : i32) : i32 = $'!x'  (2) inl widen (x : i32) : i64 = $'!x as i64'  (3) inl pick (b : bool) : i32 = $'if !b { 1 } else { 0 }'  (4) inl sum (a : i32) (b : i32) : i32 = $'!a + !b'  (5) nominal callback = $'Rc<dyn Fn()>'. Command: eoie lint macros . lib. Expected, one row per violation, then the receipt: 'lib/sample.spi:2  widen  cast   $'!x as i64'' / 'lib/sample.spi:3  pick   flow   $'if !b { 1 } else { 0 }'' / 'lib/sample.spi:4  sum    arith  $'!a + !b'' / 'failed  files 1  macros 5  violations 3  new 3  <elapsed>  <clock>'. Line 5 is the positive control for lesson 3: -> and dyn in a TYPE-position macro (nominal body) is not flow. Companion case: the same command with --baseline lint/macros.tsv listing rows 2 and 3 prints only row 4 and 'failed ... violations 3  new 1'; with all three listed it ends 'ok ... new 0'. Classes from the prototype (scratch macro-rule.ps1): long = text over 120 chars, flow = if/else/match/for/while/loop/let/return/fn/=>/->/; or a braced statement in a TERM macro, cast = 'as T', arith = an operator between splices or literals, block = a triple-quoted global/macro; rule 2/3 exceptions (named one-expression boundary primitives) come from the package standards.spi, not from regexes.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_4 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 4u32, "2026-10-25T07:46Z", "eoie", "03:46 eoie: verb core built TDD in eoie/src/eoie_lint_macros (HostFree, LibReferenced, standards 0 findings). model.spi holds unions macro_place (term/type) and macro_class (long/block/flow/cast/arith), records host_macro, violation, known_violation and lint_tally, and the pure classifier. lint.spi walks the tree (skipping target, node_modules, _build, deps, bin, obj and dot-dirs; untracked files count, per lesson 1, and no git listing, per lesson 2) and builds the report. Spec cases 48/49 adopted into eoie/spec/one-call.md. Both notebooks pass: main's 5-line fixture gives exactly the 3 expected rows and the receipt head; type-position macros (nominal, after : or ->) are not flow, per lesson 3; string literals and a deref are clean; and a temp-tree e2e run gives new 3, then new 1 against a baseline. Change from main's draft: the baseline is the verb's own output (eoie lint macros . lib > lint/macros.txt, keyed by file+name+class, line numbers ignored), because the style ratchet refuses tab-encoded rows in a new package. Left: a crate plus a CLI route (lint verb, help line), after SLATE; standards.spi exemptions for named boundary primitives (rule 2/3) need a declaration in eoie_standards first; per-diff reporting (lesson 4); wiring into the PostToolUse check. Finding for eoie_standards: host-free counts \\$' inside string literals as a host macro, a false positive that forced fixtures through a marker.")
inl note_of_granular_lint_5 () = Note ("GRANULAR-LINT", 5u32, "2026-10-25T08:07Z", "eoie", "04:06 eoie: lesson 4 done: report only what a change adds. A file named on the command line counts whatever its extension, and a .diff or .patch contributes only its added lines (+, not the +++ header; context and removed lines are skipped), so a slice check is 'eoie lint macros . change.diff'. Test a_diff_counts_only_its_added_lines passes, and both notebooks and standards are green (0 findings). Spec case 48's note documents it. CLI wiring is drafted (scratch clean\\lint-verb\\apply-lint-verb.ps1, all 4 anchors present once): a crate with entry.spi exporting eoie_lint_macros_run, VerbLint with its help line, the eoie_cli_model route and the eoie_cli dependency. It applies right after SLATE.")
