inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T11:34Z", "main", "10-25 07:34 main: pass 1 queued (job-1792928007912) with a generic tool, %TEMP%\\granular-heal\\plain-macros.ps1 -Spi -Output -Name [-Livemd]. It rewrites plain macro strings ($'$\"text\"', no braces, escapes, splices or quotes) into Spiral strings, and passes only if (a) with -Livemd, the livemd exported to the spi before healing AND the healed livemd exports to the healed spi byte for byte, and (b) the compiled output is byte-identical before and after. Otherwise it restores. apps/spiral: spiral.livemd is the source of truth (build.ps1 exports it first), and both files have 86 such sites, most of them the generated .fsproj template. Its 216 macro lines also hold splice and escape sites for later passes. The tool is a candidate for an eoie verb next to GRANULAR-LINT's 'eoie lint macros' (rule: generic tools, not per-experiment scripts).")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-25T11:46Z", "main", "10-25 07:46 main: pass 1 re-planned; the first job (job-1792928007912) was stopped while still queued, with spiral.spi untouched (216 macro lines). Finding: the .fsproj template is dead code. persist_code_project is called only by publish_code (only by publish_file, 0 callers) and persist_file (0 callers); publish_project and read_file are reached only from those. A grep of all 11 repos (excluding generated .rs/.fsx/.fs/.ipynb) finds the names only in apps/spiral/spiral.spi and .livemd, with a positive control; SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR note 1 (10-17) already called it dead. Step 1, job-1792928699219: drop the 6 definitions (persist_code_project..publish_file, the contiguous block before '## rust') from spiral.spi (151 lines) and spiral.livemd (256 lines); get_package_dir stays, since line 270 uses it. Spiral emits only reachable code, so the proof is byte-identical spiral.rs plus the livemd export reproducing the spi. This removes 41 macro lines. Step 2, job-1792928704408 (after-ok): heal the 59 plain macro strings left. FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER: the dotnet publish / fsproj path in apps/spiral is gone with step 1.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-25T12:38Z", "main", "10-25 08:38 main: FOUND a drift from the 10-24 edition-3 landing. apps/spiral/spiral.livemd is the build's source of truth (build.ps1 exports it with --export-only before compiling), but respell-repo.ps1 only touches .spi/.spir, so the livemd stayed in edition-2 words. The next apps/spiral build would have silently reverted spiral.spi to edition 2. The same shape applies to spiral/apps/wasm/spiral_wasm.livemd and polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd. Fix: %TEMP%\\granular-heal\\livemd-from-spi.ps1 regenerates each livemd from its edition-3 spi through Cells and applies only if the new livemd exports back to the spi byte for byte. apps/spiral dry run GREEN; the apply is job-1792931767062; wasm and perf dry runs are queued. Decision, by the '.spi as notebook' direction (memory self-checking-samples-direction): the regenerated notebook uses Cells' canonical cell layout (40 smart cells, was 64; headings and prose identical except one), so the hand cell boundaries aren't kept. The one cell dropped is '### escape_cell (test)', a static test cell that defines escape_cell and asserts nothing. It never reached the spi (the old export dropped it too), and a grep of all 9 repos finds it only in that livemd, so it's dead under the same rule as the publish chain. Then the dead-publish drop and the plain-string heal are requeued after it.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-25T15:35Z", "main", "10-25 11:35 main: step 1 GREEN (job-1792931858307): the 6 dead publish definitions are dropped from spiral.spi (151 lines) and the regenerated spiral.livemd (211 lines); the livemd export reproduces the spi byte for byte, and spiral.rs is byte-identical (B5D5A1E4A436DEDA). Step 2, the plain-string heal on the 59 remaining sites, runs next (job-1792931861319).")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-25T17:23Z", "main", "10-25 13:23 main: step 2 (heal-plain-3, job-1792931861319) RED was the byte-identical rule, not a behaviour change. spiral.rs 1,459,426 -> 1,455,245 B. Read: the heal turns 59 plain macro pieces into Spiral strings, and the compiler then (a) shares identical literal constants within a function (the Cargo.toml writer reuses the earlier '[dependencies]' and '' bindings, so the standalone literal count drops 3 -> 2 with the same text written) and (b) folds adjacent plain pieces of ++# chains statically, so the concat trees differ in shape. With literal bindings inlined per function and vN normalized, 128 of 20,6xx lines still differ, all concat-shape. So text equality can't prove this class of heal; dice's attribute heal is the same class. Rule for heal-plain-4, written before the run: plain-macros.ps1 -RunCheck <project build.ps1>. If the output is byte-identical: GREEN as before. Otherwise GREEN iff the project's own release gate passes on the healed tree. For apps/spiral that is build.ps1's run check: --help, export, the export of every livemd compared with the previous CLI, and the rust/cuda/lua/gleam runs, which write Cargo.toml and gleam.toml through exactly the healed writers. build.ps1 ships the CLI only on pass. On any failure, the sources and spiral.rs are restored and the previous CLI stays.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-25T17:32Z", "main", "10-25 13:32 main: the Cells trailing-markdown fix is RED and withdrawn (job-1792933087976; snapshot restored, document.spi has no diff, keep_docs is back). Dropping keep_docs from export_text broke 3 suite tests: 'the export equals spiral export on the dice and alphabet notebooks', 'the export keeps directive lines ... follows the CLI export's markdown rules', and the _real.spir interleave round trip. So 'a trailing markdown cell after the last code cell is not part of the spi' is a rule Cells and the spiral CLI export share, not a Cells bug. Consequence for polyglot Perf.livemd (livemd-from-spi dry RED: 2 inserted lines): its trailing markdown can't be regenerated from the spi by design. The fix is in the notebook: move that trailing text above the last code cell (a heading section's prose), then regenerate. Rule for that edit: livemd-from-spi round-trips byte for byte, and the moved prose is unchanged as a line multiset.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_7 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-25T18:04Z", "main", "10-25 14:04 main: polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd must NOT be regenerated from Perf.spi. It is a mixed notebook: 0 spiral fences, 5 elixir smart cells holding the Spiral code, and 13 fsharp cells (the original F# perf tests, e.g. returnLettersWithOddCountTests' solutions). The spi export carries only the Spiral cells, so livemd-from-spi would drop every F# cell (47,168 -> 30,026 bytes). Its dry run was RED only on the trailing results block, and only because it compared spi with spi. Rule added to livemd-from-spi: refuse a livemd that has any code fence other than spiral/elixir-smart-cell. Perf's fix is an in-place respell of the edition-2 words inside its Spiral smart cells (respell-repo over .livemd cell bodies), proven by: the livemd exports to an spi equal to today's edition-3 Perf.spi, and every non-Spiral cell is byte-identical.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_8 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-25T18:21Z", "main", "10-25 14:21 main: polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd respelled IN PLACE and landed unstaged (105+/105-, previous copy in %TEMP%\\perf-respell\\Perf.livemd.previous). Method (%TEMP%\\perf-respell\\respell-cells.ps1): export the livemd (670 lines); respell that export with the pack-spelling candidate's 'format --spelling symbols', which keeps the line count (670 = 670); locate each elixir smart cell's body (first to last non-blank line) in order in the export; put back the same range of the respelled export. 5 Spiral cells, 4 respelled; every fsharp cell, markdown line and cell frame byte-identical (checked by masking the cells). Proof: the respelled livemd exports to an spi byte-identical to the respelled export. The committed Perf.spi differs from it only by the installed formatter's doc-comment re-spacing plus the trailing results block (no code-only edits lost). Side finding for FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS: that formatter collapsed the column alignment inside /// markdown tables (e.g. 'abc        | abc' -> 'abc | abc') and rewrapped table rows over 120; comment interiors should be kept as written (F7).")
inl polyglot_granular_heal () = Task ("POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL", "Heal polyglot host macros per GRANULAR-HEAL (13 sites, 3,476 chars: chat_contract.spi 4/2,076, lib/math/math.spi 9/1,400)", Story, P2, D3, 10u32, Planned, "", "granular,macros,polyglot,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_polyglot_granular_heal () = Planning ("POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_polyglot_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("POLYGLOT-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T07:48Z", "main", "10-25 03:48 main: classified, nothing edited. chat_contract.spi: 3 type aliases (12/152/171) and 3 one-call sites (81 panic_str, 108 get.cloned, 182 get.cloned) are already granular. The 5 multi-statement Rust blocks (39 alias validation, 65 CID varint encoding, 122/131 alias-map update, 161 account lookup) are the real heal. Proof: a wasm32 build plus chat_contract_tests, which need the NEAR sandbox and so run on Linux (CI); WSL waits for the workflow per the standing rule, so this item pairs with a CI run. math.spi: 9 complex operators (10-85) are one host call each, so they stay. The heal candidates are the 3 println! traces (96, 254, 263) -> console.write_line with format_debug (same {:?} text, if format_debug's Rust arm is format!(\"{:?}\")), plus the exit at 555. The pyo3 glue (137-162, 274-284) stays as host calls; 276-284 opens a closure across macros, which is the real design smell. Proof: the math build runs the Rust+pyo3 tests, which needs a Python env.")
inl x_granular_heal () = Task ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", "Heal x host macros per GRANULAR-HEAL (32 sites, 2,916 chars: client/glyphs.spi 14/1,149, net/net.spi 6/682, wire.spi 7/520, locate, oauth1, token)", Story, P1, D3, 85u32, Active, "X-DONE,X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS", "granular,macros,x", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_granular_heal () = Planning ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T07:50Z", "x", "10-24 03:50 x: started, rule from GRANULAR-HEAL n1 applied mechanically. Own census (lib-draft\\macro_census.ps1: length > 120, 'as' cast, arithmetic, flow; ';' inside string literals gives false positives): staged tree 21 sites / 2,443 chars. Tonight's landing in x's working tree already removed 591 chars: the glyphs -> unicode and oauth1 -> lib delegations dropped x's char_of/graphemes/nfc wrappers and the hmac/sha1/base64/percent macros. Now 18 / 1,852. Batch 1, programs-x-heal-1\\land.eoie (3 files, 7 replacements, dry-run fits x's tree): glyphs first_code_point/code_point_count -> lib unicode (2 cast sites gone); net status_of = ($'!r.0' : u16) |> conv and requests_made = ($'...load(Relaxed)' : u32) |> conv; wire number_of = ($'..as_i64().unwrap_or(0)' : i64) |> conv, count_of = map_or(0, Vec::len) typed convert.unativeint |> convert (no closure), item converts i to unativeint before the call (no cast in the macro), unescaped becomes 3 sm'.replace calls (no macro). On those 3 files: 13/1,406 -> 7/1,027. Cells glyphs + wire + net on %TEMP%\\x-heal-check: job-1792828153670. It lands into x after the landing verify (so the verify reads a stable tree), then gets verified again. Next: glyphs plain_weight/blank (they need a code-point list: a lib unicode.code_points primitive per backend, then the weight ranges in Spiral), net call_reply/send_reply (the match on the ureq Result becomes a result bridge per constructor), wire's replied_to search (Spiral over json helpers), token synced_write, oauth1 clock_seed, locate.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T07:53Z", "x", "10-24 03:53 x: batch 2 (programs-x-heal-2, net/net.spi, applies after batch 1). The 200-char match macros in call_reply/send_reply become a bridge in rule (2)'s shape: nominals ureq_response / ureq_failure / ureq_outcome, one-call primitives called / sent_with / outcome_ok (is_ok) / outcome_response (unwrap) / outcome_failure (unwrap_err) / response_status (u16 |> conv) / response_body / failure_text, and reply_of in Spiral (if/else, with r used once per branch after the is_ok borrow, so Rust's moves hold). resultm.unbox was not used because Spiral unions derive Clone and ureq's Response isn't Clone. http_reply is now a Spiral i32 * string, so status_of/body_of are pattern bindings, not macros. net.spi: 6 sites -> 1 (the jiff timestamp map_or left). No Cells test reaches this path, so the check is a regenerate of the net crate plus cargo check -p xc_net on %TEMP%\\x-heal-check: job-1792828390876.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-24T08:00Z", "x", "10-24 04:00 x: batches 1+2 COMPILE on Rust. Regenerating the net crate and running cargo check -p xc_net on %TEMP%\\x-heal-check (job-1792828507946, 2m56s) exits 0 with the ureq result bridge, http_reply as a Spiral tuple, and the conv/convert.convert casts. The first try failed: bare `convert` in wire.spi resolves to lib's convert MODULE (ETY-ModuleMustBeImmediatelyApplied / ExpectedSymbolAsModuleKey), so batch 1 now writes convert.convert (memory spiral-macro-gotchas). There are 2 existing warnings, both from request_counted's drop(...) of a Copy u32. Next heal for it: ($'XC_REQUESTS.fetch_add(1, std::sync::atomic::Ordering::Relaxed)' : u32) |> ignore, which removes the drop and the warning. Still pending: the Cells check of batch 1 (glyphs, wire and net notebooks, i.e. the gleam paths), job-1792828153670.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-24T08:11Z", "x", "10-24 04:11 x: batches 1-3 APPLIED to C:\\home\\git\\x (working tree, unstaged) at 04:10. Batch 3 (programs-x-heal-3): oauth1 clock_seed/unix_seconds as dependency-free std::time one-call primitives (system_now, since_epoch, whole_seconds, sub_micros, process_id) with the micros arithmetic in Spiral (lib date_time would have pulled in chrono, which x's crates don't depend on); token synced_write as create/write_all/sync_all one-call primitives with the order and short-circuit in Spiral (the file's own file_text bridge style); net request_counted as fetch_add typed u32 |> ignore (drops the drop(Copy) warning). The crate check (cargo check -p xc_net -p xc_token on the healed copy) exits 0. x census: 6 sites / 795 chars, from 21 / 2,443 on the staged tree. Left: glyphs plain_weight (rust + gleam) and gleam_blank, which wait for main's unicode heal + code_points; net's jiff format_timestamp map_or; wire's replied_to search; locate's flat_map. The queued x-verify-landing pre-commit will verify the healed tree.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-24T08:16Z", "x", "10-24 04:16 x: batch 4 APPLIED to x (programs-x-heal-4). wire replied_to_in is a Spiral scan over count_of/item/at_key, with no macro left; net local_minute bridges the Option<jiff::Timestamp> (instant_found/instant_value) plus one-call system_zone/zoned_in/minute_text, with the fallback in Spiral; locate's posts_of_pages became page_posts : list json (collect id + spread over field \"data\"), and its only caller maps framed_of over the list, so the rebuilt JSON array is gone. Crate check of net, locate and token exits 0, and the regenerated net.rs/locate.rs carry no old macro text. x census is now only the 3 glyphs sites, which wait for main's unicode heal and then code_points.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-24T08:19Z", "x", "10-24 04:19 x: batch 1 Cells (job-1792828153670): wire 23/23 (failed_targets same as baseline), net 2/2, glyphs 6/8. The 2 glyphs failures (graphemes_hold_clusters, a_line_break_pair_is_one_grapheme) are both gleam and both 300 s cell timeouts (cell started 03:53 with main's chain, eoie's GE3 and tibia's gate running). Not caused by the heal: batch 1 doesn't touch graphemes, lib unicode.spi is unchanged since 20:45, and the same glyphs + lib unicode passed 8/8 at 01:22. Rule (timeout-only -> rerun once; a pass means contention, a repeat timeout is a real defect): retry job-1792829946596.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_7 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-24T08:25Z", "x", "10-24 04:25 x: rule applied (n6): the glyphs timeout-only retry PASSED 8/8 (job-1792829946596, 304 s), so the two gleam grapheme timeouts were contention. Heal batches 1-4 are verified: Cells glyphs 8/8, wire 23/23, net 2/2, plus crate checks for net, token and locate.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_8 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:22Z", "x", "10-24 07:22 x: census on HEAD 6ef9c48: 3 sites left (glyphs plain_weight rust+gleam arms, gleam_blank), down from the title's 32. All 3 wait on main's lib unicode heal plus code_points. One side effect fixed: the heal's wire.replied_to_in loop (replacing the serde macro) went 19 over x-compat's post weight, so it was hoisted at 07:05 into a top-level first_reply_from (unstaged) and x-compat is at 0. Gate: job-1792840102746-x-verify-landing3.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_9 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-24T23:46Z", "x", "10-24 19:46 x: gate moved. verify3 failed e2e (fixed: chain_json, expectations), and verify4 stopped at the sim frames build (X-SIM-FRAMES-GLEAM-IMPORTS) before x-compat ran. So first_reply_from's hoist is proven only by the 07:05 local x-compat (0 in 36 files). The gate for it is now verify-landing5. The 3 remaining sites still wait on main's unicode heal; main is respelling sm'/listm' to edition 3 now, so check for that heal after the respelling.")
inl note_of_x_granular_heal_10 () = Note ("X-GRANULAR-HEAL", 10u32, "2026-10-25T11:34Z", "x", "2026-10-25 07:34 -04:00 x: the last 3 sites are DRAFTED; the lane that needs a lib primitive lands it (no wait on main). Patch: scratchpad\\code-points\\apply.ps1 (-Lib/-Glyphs; a dry run on copies applies cleanly). lib unicode gets code_points (gleam: string.to_utf_codepoints + utf_codepoint_to_int as one-call primitives; rust: chars().collect() into list' plus rust_char_code; order via listm'.rev'/fold' in Spiral, as graphemes does) and is_white_space (the Unicode White_Space set in Spiral, the set Rust's trim uses and that x_spaced spelled in Gleam), each with a rust+gleam test. x glyphs: rust_weight/gleam_weight/x_light become light + code_point_weight + plain_weight over unicode.code_points, and rust_blank/gleam_blank/x_white/x_spaced become one blank over unicode.is_white_space: one Spiral rule for both backends, no macro left. Also checked: the 17 sim modules about to land carry 0 host macros (positive control: the original cells_executor had 18). Applies after serial3 releases the live tree; gate: Cells lib unicode + x glyphs, splitter, reading (the weight users) on rust and gleam.")
inl tibia_granular_heal () = Task ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", "Heal tibia host macros per GRANULAR-HEAL: client.spi 22 sites (3 slices proven, 22 to 9), lua.spi 10 (retired by LIB-MIGRATION S2/S3), offscreen.spi 9 (done 10-24)", Story, P2, D2, 75u32, Active, "TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION,LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS", "granular,macros,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_granular_heal () = Planning ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", Minutes 420u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T07:40Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 03:40 tibia: census by GRANULAR-HEAL rule 1 as written (one host call or expression, at most 120 chars, no casts, no arithmetic, no flow: if/elseif/for/while/local/function/return, and/or/not fallbacks, ;). Over every tibia .spi in the tree it finds more than the umbrella's 5 sites, because Lua (function() ... end)() blocks and x or fallback count as flow. Per file: client.spi 22, lua.spi 10, offscreen.spi 9 (Rust Win32 blocks), and the other arena files 1-4 each (adjacency 1, competitor 2, measure 3, observer 3, path 2, random 2, scoreboard 1, steps 4, summon 1). Plan, rule-first, ordered by what's already in flight: (a) the non-client arena files: the LIB-MIGRATION rebased draft already has 0 macros there and re-lands tonight after PR-27; competitor's last one ($'(!text) == \"\"') goes in slice S1 (note 35). (b) lua.spi's 10 are retired as pure Spiral by LIB-MIGRATION S2 (mut cells) and S3 (lists, arrays, src/rng), with no macro left but the native_table type. (c) client.spi's 22 OTClient bindings: each becomes one-call primitives (one getter per host value, nil bridged to option by one primitive per case) plus Spiral logic, the biggest first (instrument_game_calls 1101 chars, the inventory report 809, the monster list 388, footing_at 269, the attack lines 227 and 152, login 198, bot settings 189 and 156, position hooks 156, chase 126, protection zone 125), each with a lua Cells test from TIBIA-CELLS-APPS. (d) offscreen.spi's 9: Rust Win32 calls as one-call primitives with the struct and loop logic in Spiral (rule 3 keeps extern declarations). Estimate by milestones: (c) 22 x 12 min, about 4.5 h; (d) 9 x 15 min, about 2.3 h; (a) and (b) are counted in LIB-MIGRATION. Total about 420 min, replacing the 45 min first guess.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T08:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:34 tibia: rule (4) census for client.spi: all 73 definitions have callers in apps/arena, apps/host or src (enable_client_bot is used inside client.spi), so nothing is deleted and (c) is a real rewrite. The pattern, set on the largest testable site, footing_at (269 chars): five one-call primitives, map_tile x y z = g_map.getTile({ x = !x, y = !y, z = !z }), tile_present = (!t) ~= nil, tile_walkable = (!t):isWalkable(), tile_floor_change = (!t):hasFloorChange(), tile_walkable_through = (!t):isWalkable(true); then a pure Spiral footing_of present walkable floor_change walkable_through : footing (the if-chain of Unknown/Free/Ramp/Occupied/Blocked), with footing_at s = footing_of over the primitives. footing_of replaces CELLS-APPS's footing_of_code split (the code numbers existed only to cross the macro boundary), and the client Cells test moves to footing_of's 5 cases. The other 21 sites follow the same shape: one getter primitive per host value, nil bridged by a ~= nil primitive, and the logic and string building in Spiral. Verification needs the Cells mounts fix (the arena package can't mount until it lands), so (c) starts after it.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-24T08:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:41 tibia: (d) offscreen.spi design, drafted in scratch offscreen-heal/win32-section.spi and NOT landed. Rule 3 keeps one declaration global: unsafe extern 'system' blocks with safe fn items (Rust 2024), handles typed i64 so no as-casts remain, and the two #[repr(C)] structs with pointer fields as usize filled through .addr(), so #[derive(Default)] works. Out-params go through Cell::as_ptr, and the cwd fallback is Spiral over file_system.directory_get_parent. Two blockers found while drafting. (1) offscreen's 3 Cells tests cover only parsing and report text, and nothing exercises the Win32 calls, so a heal could compile and still break every live run. A Windows-only behaviour test comes first (create and close a desktop; start cmd /c exit 3 hidden, wait, read exit 3), gated off on Linux. (2) Pointer lifetime: a pointer taken in one primitive and used by a later call can dangle if Spiral's Rust codegen passes the Vec<u16> as a clone. Rule for this file: every pointer is derived inside the same host expression as the call that uses it (CreateProcessW gets its wide strings and its inline StartupInfo literal in one expression, with short parameter names to stay under 120 chars). Start (d) after the behaviour test exists.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-24T08:55Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 04:55 tibia: (d)'s first blocker has a draft: scratch offscreen-heal/win32-behaviour-test.spi (one rust test, a_hidden_desktop_runs_a_program_and_reports_its_exit_code). It creates the 'spiral-offscreen-test' desktop, starts cmd.exe /c exit 3 on WinSta0\\spiral-offscreen-test with start_on, and checks wait_for returns 0 within 10 s and exit_status is 3, then closes the process and the desktop. Off Windows it passes trivially through platform.is_windows, since CI runs Linux. It lands first, against today's (unhealed) offscreen.spi, and must pass there before any heal; then the granular rewrite must keep it green. It needs a compile slot and a window that's free of live-gui (it creates a desktop), so it waits for a quiet moment after tonight's queue.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-24T09:03Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:03 tibia: (d)'s safety net is in place. The Win32 behaviour test passes against today's unhealed offscreen.spi (job tibia-offscreen-behaviour, 4 of 4 with the 3 existing tests): a hidden desktop is created, cmd.exe /c exit 3 runs on it, wait_for returns 0 and exit_status returns 3. The pointer-lifetime worry from note 3 is answered by inlining: every primitive is an inl, so macro text is substituted at its call site with the caller's own variable (wide_start application is application.as_ptr() on the same live Vec), so no clone happens; a pointer can dangle only across a let function or a closure, and the section has none. The long macros are shortened (create_process_at takes a null *const u8 parameter instead of repeating std::ptr::null()). The healed copy (scratch offscreen-heal/offscreen-healed.spi: the Win32 section replaced, plus the behaviour test) scans 0 macro violations, with the declarations as rule 3 allows, and runs as job tibia-offscreen-healed.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-24T09:10Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:10 tibia: (d) LANDED. apps/offscreen/offscreen.spi's Win32 section is granular (job tibia-offscreen-land, exit 0): one declaration global (unsafe extern 'system' with safe fn items typed i64, and the two repr(C) structs with usize pointer fields and Default), 0 macro violations, every call a one-expression primitive, and the cwd fallback and branching in Spiral. Acceptance before the land: the healed copy passed 4 of 4 under Cells (job tibia-offscreen-healed), including the new Win32 behaviour test (hidden desktop, cmd /c exit 3, wait 0, exit 3), which also passed on the unhealed file (job tibia-offscreen-behaviour). The land script checked the source against the base hash, backed up the source and offscreen.exe (scratch offscreen-heal\\offscreen-backup-20261024-050832), ran the formatter check, rebuilt target/offscreen/offscreen.exe (25,379 bytes of Rust), and smoke-ran live/run-offscreen.ps1 on cmd.exe /c exit 0: pid 70784, exit 0 on desktop tibia-probe. Left: (c) client.spi's 22 sites (after the Cells mounts fix), while (a) and (b) ride LIB-MIGRATION. Restage for the user: apps/offscreen/offscreen.spi.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-24T09:34Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:34 tibia: (c) first slice drafted: scratch client-heal-footing.ps1 heals footing_at, the largest client.spi site with a pure part (269 chars of Lua flow). Five one-call primitives (tile_at, tile_present, tile_walkable, tile_floor_change, tile_walkable_through_creatures) plus a pure footing_of over the four facts, replacing CELLS-APPS's footing_of_code, with the client test moved to footing_of's 5 cases (Free, Ramp, Occupied, Blocked, Unknown). Proven on a copy of the CELLS-APPS candidate client.spi: both anchors match once, footing_of_code is gone, and no line is over 120. It applies after the CELLS-APPS land (tibia-after-block-2), then a client Cells run on lua and c.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-24T09:38Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:38 tibia: (c) second slice drafted: scratch client-heal-batch2.ps1 heals player_zone (zone-check primitives plus Spiral branching), attacking_short and attacking_described (creature_present plus the existing name, health and spot getters, and spot_text and target_line built with ++#; client.spi now opens sm'_operators), and chasing (ChaseOpponent, DontChase, get and set chase mode primitives, compared in Spiral). Applied after client-heal-footing.ps1 on a copy of the CELLS-APPS candidate: every anchor matches once, no line is over 120, and client.spi's violating macros drop from 22 to 17. Both slices run after the CELLS-APPS land, then a client Cells run on lua and c, plus the arena build and its LUA-OK parse check. Next by size: instrument_game_calls (1101 chars; it wraps g_game functions to count calls, the hardest to split) and the inventory report (809).")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-24T09:43Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-24 05:43 tibia: (c) third slice drafted: scratch client-heal-batch3.ps1 heals the and/or fallback sites. env_text and env_i32 are now Spiral over lib env.get_environment_variable, plus a tonumber primitive (lib's own os.getenv or \"\" fallback belongs to LIB-MACRO-HEAL). player_present is player_exists && game_online. positioned_monster and live_monster moved below the creature getters and compose creature_is_monster, creature_dead and creature_positioned. parity_summary, mute (g_sounds present, disable, stop) and write_log_line (probe log missing, start, text, with the trailing newline added in Spiral) are healed too. Applied after the footing and batch2 slices on a copy of the CELLS-APPS candidate: every anchor matches once, no line is over 120, and client.spi's violating macros go from 22 to 9. Order constraint: env_text needs lib env, which the arena package reaches only after the libmig re-land (packageDir lib, spiral-), so the order is libmig, then CELLS-APPS, then the three client slices, then the client Cells run and the arena build. Left: 9 sites, mostly multi-statement blocks (instrument_game_calls, the inventory report, the monster list, forget_dead, login, the position hooks, the bot settings).")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 10u32, "2026-10-25T06:30Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 02:30 The client slices are re-derived for the staged base and edition 3: footing's anchor is now the staged inline footing_at (no footing test without CELLS-APPS), and batch2's sm'_operators header goes before the first nominal. Method: apply to the words original (respell-repo's original copy), then format --spelling symbols. Control check: respelling the unhealed original reproduces the tree byte for byte. Result: client.spi violating macros go from 22 to 9, no line over 120, lua. helper calls 11 to 11; the file is in scratch heal-e3. Found and fixed in batch2 and batch3: PowerShell here-strings drop the final newline, so five edits joined lines (gotcha in memory; the first land's parse error came from this, not from 5j). The land (job-1792909058769, restored itself) is blocked by LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS: with inl composites competitor.lua exceeds 200 locals, and with let composites it exceeds 60 upvalues. It resumes when that lands, from the let variant (scratch client-let.spi), since functions are the better granular shape. Remaining after that: the 9 multi-statement sites.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 11u32, "2026-10-25T10:06Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 06:06 Batch 4 drafted (scratch client-heal-batch4.ps1, edits on the edition-3 healed client-let.spi; result client-let-b4.spi). step: 8 one-call direction constants plus walk_direction, chosen by a Spiral if-chain over a local 'toward' predicate (negative literals written 0 - 1). disable_client_bot and enable_client_bot: bot_settings_node, presence check, empty node, mark disabled/enabled, store and save primitives composed in Spiral (current_bot_settings). on_position_change: the one-expression curried adapter 'function(c, n, o) (!f)(c)(n)(o) end' plus two single connect calls. Type-checked on the scratch tibia root (scan and path compile against it); no line over 120. Census 9 to 6, where one of the 6 is that adapter, flagged only for the word function and the same branch-free shape as hooks.spi's arity adapters. Real sites left: instrument_game_calls, the inventory report, the monster list, forget_dead (pairs over lua.native_table, which belongs to LIB-MIGRATION's S3 family) and the login connect with its onLoginError adapter. Lands with batches 1-3 once LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS is installed.")
inl note_of_tibia_granular_heal_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-GRANULAR-HEAL", 12u32, "2026-10-25T12:06Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 08:06 Batch 5 drafted (scratch client-heal-batch5.ps1; result client-let-b5.spi): log_in's connect becomes login_error_handler and text_message_handler (one-expression adapters with the tostring conversions) plus connect_game_events (one connect call with a table constructor). Type-checked on the scratch tibia root (scan and summon compile), no line over 120. With batches 1-5, client.spi goes from 22 violating sites to 4. All 4 iterate lua.native_table counters with pairs/sort (instrument_game_calls, game_call_report, forget_dead, the monster list), so they move to TIBIA-LIB-MIGRATION's S3 family, where lua.native_table itself retires. GRANULAR-HEAL's own client.spi part is drafted in full and lands (client-let-b5.spi over client.spi, guarded with arena build plus lua Cells) once pack 5l with LUA-FUNCTION-LIMITS installs; competitor needs those limits either way.")
inl eoie_queued_jobs_no_process () = Task ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", "gate-waiting jobs hold no process either: the host also holds jobs waiting for a heavy slot or a window (18 of 52 queued at 05:1x) and starts their supervisor when the gate grants; the remainder of EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST, whose release P covers --after waits", Story, P0, D4, 30u32, Active, "EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST,EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT", "eoie,jobs,processes", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_queued_jobs_no_process () = Planning ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_no_process_1 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T09:14Z", "eoie", "05:1x eoie, measured on the user's question ('a lot of eoie processes up, drift or intended?'): 61 eoie processes = 1 job host + 56 job supervisors + 3 caller-side waiters + 1 window run. Only 8 jobs are running; 47 are queued (main 36, x 11, eoie 5, tibia 3, counting running) and each queued job holds a resident supervisor (~7 MB) plus its hidden conhost (~7.5 MB): 48 idle supervisors, ~800 MB together on a memory-tight machine. Cause: job start spawns the supervisor at once, and the supervisor itself waits for --after, the slot and the window (job chaining 10-21, note 31 on EOIE-JOBS). It was a convenient implementation, never a design goal, and it drifted as queues grew to 40+ jobs. Rule (acceptance written before work): a queued job is only a record in the jobs home; the per-owner host starts its supervisor when the job becomes runnable (predecessor settled, slot and window granted). Done = with N queued and R running jobs, the eoie process count is hosts + R supervisors (+ caller waiters), measured by job doctor; queue order, --after/--after-ok semantics, timeouts counted from start and 'job stop' of a queued job all keep their current receipts. Pairs with EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE (stage R: no conhost per supervisor) and EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT (S1: hosts under the owner session, not explorer).")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_no_process_2 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T09:21Z", "eoie", "05:2x eoie: narrowed after a search I should have run first. EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-IN-HOST already holds --after-chained jobs without a process (release P, tonight's train; queue.spi step_of). This item is now only the gate waits: the host decides slot and window admission itself (the window store and heavy slots are files it can read on each 500 ms beat) and starts the supervisor holding the granted lease, so no process waits for a gate. The acceptance in note 1 is unchanged.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_no_process_3 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 3u32, "2026-10-24T10:06Z", "eoie", "06:0x eoie: user follow-up ('i thought you were going to heal the processes when i asked, help me here'). Healed what can be healed live: my 9 queued checks are now one serial runner (job-1792836186900-eoie-serial-checks; eoie processes 74 -> 66), and main (40 queued), x (4) and tibia (3) were asked to requeue serial chains the same way. Main fixed the starvation source the same hour (Invoke-Heavy runs heavy at Normal). Gap found in eoie: 'job chain' (one-call case 42) starts N jobs with N resident supervisors, which is the pattern that piles up processes. Until P lands, a chain should be ONE job that runs its links in order, each under its own window. Add to this item: 'job chain --serial' (or the chain default) runs the links inside one supervisor, keeping per-link receipts.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_no_process_4 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T10:17Z", "eoie", "06:1x eoie: live heal measured. Every lane collapsed its queued chains into serial runner jobs: eoie 9 -> 1, main 40 -> 4, tibia stopped 1 independent gate, x had no serial chain. eoie processes 74 -> 28, supervisor conhosts 56 -> 22, free memory 1.3 -> 1.7 GB. The runner pattern (one job, steps in a foreach, each under its own window run) is the interim rule until release P, stage R and this item land.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_no_process_5 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 5u32, "2026-10-24T10:31Z", "eoie", "06:3x eoie: drafted 'job chain --serial', the one-call form of the runner pattern every lane used by hand tonight. Scratch: job-chain-serial/job-chain-serial.do, 11 edits, dry run clean on post-R. Model (eoie_job_rechain/model.spi): a typed chain_head {mode, first, serial, runner} parsed by a fold over --ok/--serial/--run-serial/<after-id>. links_timeout sums the link limits (one job's limit; a non-numeric limit refuses). serial_start_arguments builds ONE 'job start [--after...] <first>-serial <sum> eoie job chain --run-serial [--ok] -- <links>'. run_arguments makes each link 'job run <link>' (in-process supervision, so each link keeps its own id and receipts). continues_after: --ok stops at the first failure, plain continues. Runner (chain.spi): serial_ran runs the links in order and ends 'eoie job chain ok serial ran=N failed=M' or 'stopped serial ... skipped=K'. Usage updated in both places. A test block covers the start arguments, the sum, the refusals and the stop rule. Typecheck appended to runner eoie-serial-checks-2. Effect: a waiting chain costs 1 supervisor instead of N, today, without the host changes.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_no_process_6 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 6u32, "2026-10-24T11:18Z", "eoie", "07:1x groom: 'job chain --serial' moved to its own ready item EOIE-JOB-CHAIN-SERIAL, since it doesn't wait on the host held queue. This item keeps only the gate waits (slot and window) held by the host.")
inl note_of_eoie_queued_jobs_no_process_7 () = Note ("EOIE-QUEUED-JOBS-NO-PROCESS", 7u32, "2026-10-25T11:28Z", "eoie", "07:28 eoie design, written before code, grounded in eoie_job_host/queue.spi (step_of over queued_job.wait: NoChain or Chained(id, mode, PredecessorLive|PredecessorSettled) gives KeepWaiting / StartSupervisor / SettleSkipped / SettleStopped; host_run's held_from loop beats held files and starts supervisors through eoie_job_queue_decide). (1) Model: queued_job gains a gate field, union start_gate = NoGate | HeavySlot | CompileWindow : u64 (minutes) | HeavyWindow : u64, read from the job record that job start already writes (shape, slot_minutes, min_free). (2) step_of becomes chain first, then gate: a settled-or-absent chain with an ungranted gate is KeepWaiting, so no supervisor exists while a job waits on a slot or window. (3) Grant without a race: the host claims the window or slot itself (the same admission verdict eoie_window_query uses; heavy also needs a free heavy slot), then starts the supervisor with the claim id in an env var; the supervisor adopts the claim instead of requesting again, and releases it on exit as today. If adoption fails (a stale claim), the supervisor requests normally, which only costs a wait. (4) Order: held jobs are granted in queue order per class, so a later small compile cannot starve an earlier heavy job (the current per-supervisor polling has no global order). (5) Acceptance, unchanged from note 1: with N queued and R running, eoie processes = hosts + R supervisors (+ caller waiters) per job doctor; receipts for queue order, --after/--after-ok, timeout from start, and job stop of a queued job unchanged; plus a new test: two queued heavy jobs and one free slot give exactly one supervisor. Lands after S1 (owner-parent hosts), which lands after SLATE.")
inl lib_otp_pins () = Task ("LIB-OTP-PINS", "lib/spiral: confirm the gleam_otp and gleam_erlang versions lib pins and that process.Name and the factory supervisor exist there; bump to the newest releases if not (prerequisite for the OTP pattern items)", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,gleam,otp,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_otp_pins () = Planning ("LIB-OTP-PINS", Minutes 30u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_otp_pins_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PINS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). The survey could not find lib's gleam.toml pins within its search depth.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_pins_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-PINS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:26Z", "x", "10-24 06:26 x: DONE. The pins aren't in lib/spiral's gleam.toml: Cells supplies Gleam deps (apps/cells targets.ex:344-345: gleam_erlang >=1.3.0 and <2.0.0, gleam_otp >=1.3.0 and <2.0.0), and the warm build resolves gleam_erlang 1.3.0 and gleam_otp 1.3.0. Both are the newest stable on hex.pm (gleam_otp 1.3.0 from 2026-08-09, gleam_erlang 1.3.0 from 2025-08-03), so no bump is needed. Checked in the resolved sources: gleam_erlang's process.gleam has pub type Name, new_name and named_subject (LIB-OTP-NAME can start), and gleam_otp ships factory_supervisor.gleam beside static_supervisor (LIB-OTP-FACTORY-SUPERVISOR can start).")
inl lib_otp_name () = Task ("LIB-OTP-NAME", "lib/spiral: wrap process.Name(msg) as a typed name handle that survives a supervisor restart, and address supervised actors by name instead of by subject", Story, P1, D3, 40u32, Active, "LIB-OTP-PINS,LIB-OTP-ACTORS", "lib,gleam,otp,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_otp_name () = Planning ("LIB-OTP-NAME", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). A Subject is tied to one pid and dies with it; Name is the stable address 'let it crash' needs. Highest leverage, so it comes first.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:32Z", "x", "10-24 06:32 x: drafted as programs-otp-name\\land.eoie (2 files, 4 replacements, applied to a fresh lib copy %TEMP%\\x-otp-name). erlang_process gains nominal name t = process.Name(t) and one-call new_name / named_subject / register (process.register's Result(Nil, Nil) through the module's received () + received_value -> bool); otp_actor gains named (actor.named(builder, name)) so a supervised actor can be started under a stable name. New gleam test a_named_subject_reaches_the_registered_process registers self under a fresh name, sends through named_subject and receives the message back. Check: Cells erlang_process.spi on the copy, job-1792837873588. It lands through the guarded lander once green.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_3 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 3u32, "2026-10-24T15:55Z", "main", "main 12:2x: the erlang_process half of this item is folded into ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA's rewrite (%TEMP%\\lib-erlang-process-sota, gate+land job main-serial-erlang-process-3), so your programs-otp-name erlang_process.spi replacements would no longer find their anchors: the rewrite renames receive -> receive_within and selector_receive -> selector_receive_within (returning the Spiral union receipt t = Got | TimedOut) and drops received/received_value (host_received + receipt_of). Carried over unchanged in meaning: nominal name t, new_name, named_subject, register (now host_received () |> host_received_is_ok, no match on a received option), and your test a_named_subject_reaches_the_registered_process on receive_within + receipt_value (passes locally on Gleam: True, Some 5). Your otp_actor.named part is untouched by the rewrite; land it alone once ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA lands.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_name_4 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-NAME", 4u32, "2026-10-24T16:40Z", "main", "main 12:4x: the erlang_process half landed with ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA (12:39): nominal name, new_name, named_subject, register and your test are live. Land only the otp_actor.named part now (actor.named(builder, name) over erlang_process.name).")
inl lib_otp_typed_call () = Task ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", "lib/spiral: a typed request/reply protocol as a GADT (req r = request constructors indexed by their reply type; call : name (req r) -> req r -> r), so gen_server-style calls are checked end to end", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "LIB-OTP-NAME", "lib,gleam,otp,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_otp_typed_call () = Planning ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_otp_typed_call_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). The biggest safety win; the same idea as x's reads r. No new host types needed.")
inl note_of_lib_otp_typed_call_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-TYPED-CALL", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:37Z", "x", "10-24 06:37 x: design sketch, written before code. A protocol is a Spiral GADT whose constructors carry their reply type, e.g. `union req r = | Get :: req i32 | Put : i32 -> req () | Name :: req string`. The server keeps ONE subject for the erased envelope `envelope = exists r. req r * erlang_process.subject r` (an existential, so a single mailbox serves every request). The client side is `call (to : erlang_process.name envelope) (timeout_ms : i32) (q : req r) : option r`: make a fresh reply subject r, send (q, reply), and receive on reply with the timeout, so a Get can only be answered with an i32. The handler side is `handled (q : req r) : r` per constructor, a GADT match where each arm returns that arm's r, wrapped by `serve` into otp_actor's on_message. That gives gen_server:call checked end to end with no new host types: everything stays erlang_process subject/send/receive plus LIB-OTP-NAME. Test: a counter server with Get/Increment/Reset whose wrong-typed reply fails to compile (a negative check by hand) and whose calls return the right values on gleam. The x analog is the client's reads r GADT, the same pattern on the HTTP side.")
inl lib_otp_factory_supervisor () = Task ("LIB-OTP-FACTORY-SUPERVISOR", "lib/spiral: wrap the gleam_otp factory supervisor and its child-spec type for dynamic children (simple_one_for_one), next to the static supervisor already in lib", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "LIB-OTP-PINS,LIB-OTP-ACTORS", "lib,gleam,otp,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_otp_factory_supervisor () = Planning ("LIB-OTP-FACTORY-SUPERVISOR", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_otp_factory_supervisor_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-FACTORY-SUPERVISOR", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). Needed for per-connection and per-job workers, e.g. the x sim runner shell (SIM-IN-SPIRAL).")
inl note_of_lib_otp_factory_supervisor_2 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-FACTORY-SUPERVISOR", 2u32, "2026-10-24T10:37Z", "x", "10-24 06:37 x: API read from the pinned gleam_otp 1.3.0 source (gleam/otp/factory_supervisor.gleam, 463 lines). Planned lib surface, mirroring otp_supervisor/otp_actor and one call per primitive: nominals factory a d = factory_supervisor.Supervisor(a, d), factory_builder a d = Builder(a, d), factory_message a d = Message(a, d); worker_child / supervisor_child (template : a -> otp_actor.start_result d, passed as a closure VALUE, as otp_actor.on_message already does, so no closure body sits in a macro); named (builder, erlang_process.name (factory_message a d)), which builds on LIB-OTP-NAME; restart_tolerance, timeout, restart_strategy (otp_supervisor's restart union); start -> start_result (factory a d); supervised -> child spec, so a factory can sit under a static supervisor; start_child (factory, argument) -> start_result d; count_children -> i32; get_by_name (name) -> factory a d. Gleam test: a named factory starts 3 echo workers, count_children = 3, each worker answers a call. First consumer: the x sim runner shell's per-job executors (SIM-IN-SPIRAL).")
inl lib_otp_selector_union () = Task ("LIB-OTP-SELECTOR-UNION", "lib/spiral: make the selector the primary receive: map several subjects, monitor Down messages and trapped exits into one Spiral message union (typed selective receive)", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "LIB-OTP-ACTORS", "lib,gleam,otp,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_otp_selector_union () = Planning ("LIB-OTP-SELECTOR-UNION", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_otp_selector_union_1 () = Note ("LIB-OTP-SELECTOR-UNION", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:23Z", "x", "From the 10-24 agent survey of Erlang/OTP patterns worth modelling in Gleam (lib has erlang_process subject/pid/monitor/selector, otp_actor builder/next/started, otp_supervisor static). Selective receive is Erlang's real strength; a raw receive on one subject throws it away.")
inl lib_otp_pg () = Task ("LIB-OTP-PG", "lib/spiral: process groups (Erlang pg) as a typed group handle for broadcast and fan-out (gossip peers, pub/sub)", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "LIB-OTP-NAME", "lib,gleam,otp,backends", "current", "current")
