inl note_of_bang_macro_cleanup_1 () = Note ("BANG-MACRO-CLEANUP", 1u32, "2026-10-24T13:18Z", "main", "2026-10-24 09:18 main: inventory of !!!! uses outside core (lib, eoie src, x, tibia, fiscal; lab and deps excluded), with the core alias each one has. Export 596: no core alias; eoie's crate exports. Proposal: one lib `export` wrapper (named, typed), then a mechanical rewrite in eoie (eoie team). Global 107: core base `global (x : string)` exists, so rewrite to `global`, mechanical. StaticStringConcat 18: the `^^` operator form, decided by CARET-OPERATOR-ORIGIN's rule. Array intrinsics ArrayCreate 10, ArrayIndex 5, ArrayIndexSet 6, ArrayLength 6: core base has the prototype instances create/index/set/length on `array`, so rewrite to those (this is also the prototype usage TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCE wants). Conv 5: core `conv`. StringLength 4: core `sm.length`. LTE 6, Sub 3, Add 2: the operators `<=`, `-`, `+`. Without a core alias (keep as primitives until core grows one): Indent 6, NominalCreate 4, UnionToRecord 4, SymbolToString 3, RecordTypeFoldBack 2, Unbox 2. Order: Global (107) and the arithmetic/array/string ones (about 47) are mechanical rewrites, then the Export wrapper with the eoie team, and the rest wait for core aliases (upstream fork, UPSTREAM-FORK-START).")
inl caret_operator_origin () = Task ("CARET-OPERATOR-ORIGIN", "Where did the ^^ operator come from, is there a more canonical one for that operation, and does it conflict with ^^^/other operators: trace its introduction and fix or document by rule (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", TaskKind, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "spiral,operators,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_caret_operator_origin () = Planning ("CARET-OPERATOR-ORIGIN", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_caret_operator_origin_1 () = Note ("CARET-OPERATOR-ORIGIN", 1u32, "2026-10-24T11:43Z", "main", "2026-10-24 07:43 main: origin traced. `^^` was added on 2026-10-14 by the formatter work (SPIRAL-FORMATTER notes 10-20, lane-a). It is a parser operator (ParsedStringConcat -> StaticStringConcat, annotated string since note 17), and the formatter emits it to split string literals longer than 120 chars, so the split compiles to the same residual as the joined literal. It takes the `^` precedence class (45, right associative). The lexer reads maximal operator tokens, so it does not clash with bitwise `^^^` in parsing, but the two read alike. Spread: 1,089 lines in spiral (all but 7 in apps/compiler/tmp samples, written by the formatter), eoie 4, tibia 12, polyglot 7. Canonical candidates: Spiral's own `++#` (lib string concat, used everywhere), OCaml's `^`, or implicit adjacent-literal concatenation (C, Python). Decision rule, written before measuring: if `\"a\" ++# \"b\"` folds to one literal on every backend (residual identical to the joined literal, in a C/Rust/Gleam/Lua probe), the formatter emits `++#`, every `^^` is rewritten to `++#`, and the parser operator is removed; otherwise `^^` stays and is documented next to `++#` as the compile-time form.")
inl rust_prefix_study () = Task ("RUST-PREFIX-STUDY", "The rust. prefixes added when rust_type/fsharp_global moved back into rust/rust.spi: keep them only if strictly necessary. Study the SOTA module/package resolution (does open rust expose rust.spi members bare? what do Rust/OCaml/F# module systems do for folder modules with a same-name file?) and remove the prefixes if a principled open makes them unneeded, with no resolver hack (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,modules,lib,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_prefix_study () = Planning ("RUST-PREFIX-STUDY", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_prefix_study_1 () = Note ("RUST-PREFIX-STUDY", 1u32, "2026-10-24T12:29Z", "main", "2026-10-24 08:29 main: settled by a probe on the installed compiler (scratch rust-open-probe, Rust backend, packageDir lib): `open rust` then bare `clone 41i32` fails with ETY-UnboundVariable: clone, and `rust.clone 41i32` with no open compiles (`41i32.clone()`). So `open rust` opens the rust/ FOLDER record (its children rust and rust_operators; that is why file_system.spi follows it with `open rust_operators`), never rust.spi's members, and the `rust.` prefix on rust_type/fsharp_global is strictly necessary under today's resolution. No resolver regressed. Qualified `rust.X` is also the user's own lib idiom (rust.ref 184x in the 10-02 lib), so the prefixes stay; no hack such as a double open is added. Not done here: the SOTA module design, the Rust 2018 model, where a folder `rust/` plus `rust.spi` is ONE module (the file is the module, the folder holds its children), so `open rust` would expose the members and the submodules alike. That is a compiler resolution change with a corpus-wide effect; it belongs to the package-manager design (PACKAGE-HEADER-REVISIT / PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN, note added there) and must be measured on the corpus before any prefix is dropped.")
