inl backends_sota_score () = Task ("BACKENDS-SOTA-SCORE", "Standing 0-1000 score of how far all backends are from SOTA, recomputed in every report from a fixed rule (matrix agree share, sample coverage, codegen quality checks) and dogfooding agile weights (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 50u32, Active, "", "backends,metrics,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_backends_sota_score () = Planning ("BACKENDS-SOTA-SCORE", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_backends_sota_score_1 () = Note ("BACKENDS-SOTA-SCORE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T10:58Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:58 main: first score, by judgment until the fixed rule exists: 380/1000. Basis: C, Rust and Zig agree on nearly every matrix row and the oracle runs on two of them; F#, Delphi, Python and Bend agree on their lists, but the lists are subsets. Gleam, Lua, Lean, Kotlin and Swift run partial sample lists with known gaps (stack layouts, some BackendSwitch records). Wasm, WGSL, Calyx, OpenQASM and eBPF cover only integer or classical subsets. Codegen readability (CODEGEN-READABLE-2) and idiomatic output are far from SOTA on every backend. Rule to write next: per backend, the share of mega and contracts samples that agree with the oracle (400), the share of language features covered (300), readability/idiom checks (300).")
inl note_of_backends_sota_score_2 () = Note ("BACKENDS-SOTA-SCORE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T12:48Z", "main", "2026-10-24 08:48 main: rule written and first measured score. Source: the accepted baseline run single-flight-20261022-184215 (7,000+ rows, 19 backends, 1,163 sample ids). Rule: score = 400 x A + 300 x B + 300 x R. A is the mean over backends of oracle agreement on each backend's compared rows, with Zig, the primary oracle, counted against C (0.95 -> 380). B is the mean over backends of compile-ok sample ids divided by ALL 1,163 sample ids, so curated per-backend lists do not inflate it: C covers 540, Fsharp 997 rows, the big 11 about 450, Wgsl 153, OpenQasm 99, Calyx 71, Ebpf 55, Cpp 16; mean about 0.31 -> 93. R is readability/idiom, unmeasured until CODEGEN-READABLE-2 defines its metric (generated-name density, idiomatic constructs per backend) -> 0 for now. SCORE 473/1000, with R pending; my 06:58 judgment of 380 was too low on A and B and too high to leave R at 0. Side facts from the same run: the mega programs pass on most backends (8/8 rows on C, Delphi, Gleam, Kotlin, Python, Swift, TypeScript, Zig; Lean 6, Lua and Wasm 7, Bend 5, Calyx/OpenQasm/Wgsl 3), but Rust agrees on only 3 of its 8 mega rows and F# compiles 48 of its 295 mega rows: those are the two biggest gaps after readability. Recompute in every report from the newest accepted baseline.")
inl cells_history_review () = Task ("CELLS-HISTORY-REVIEW", "Look up in the session history how the Cells idea came to be, how progress went, whether it drifted from the original version, and why it is not finished; write the findings and the remaining path (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,history,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_history_review () = Planning ("CELLS-HISTORY-REVIEW", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_history_review_1 () = Note ("CELLS-HISTORY-REVIEW", 1u32, "2026-10-24T12:19Z", "main", "2026-10-24 08:19 main: history from the session record (5a6e53fb.jsonl) and 376 Kino/Cells notes (10-14..10-24). ORIGIN (around 10-01/02): an agent built a parallel Livebook Spiral smart cell; Grok moved it to spiral/apps/kino. Its purpose was to replace the Fable-era .dib/dotnet-repl notebooks: .livemd sources with Kino running Spiral cells per backend. GROWTH: 10-03..10-07 notebooks moved from .dib to .livemd plus Kino (polyglot/dice/lib). 10-11..10-13: test blocks in .spi became cells (///> targets, test x static), a shared compile daemon (port 13905), memo keys, and the silent-false-pass audit (C/Gleam/WGSL). 10-14..10-16: queue latency, warm runner, bridge overhead, parallel cache. 10-17: the user disliked the name, Kino became Cells. 10-19: CELLS-EVERYWHERE goal (\"100% of the code cell compatible\"), 129 notes that day. 10-22..10-24: the Cells pack (daemon memsup, retire race, mounts, probes, new-project scaffold). DRIFT vs the original: the original was an interactive notebook (Livebook smart cell, .livemd as the source). Today Cells is a CLI test runner and gate behind every lane's lib work; the Livebook side lost priority (NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT blocked at 90%) and .spi files became the notebooks. This matches later user directions (.spi as notebook, SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES), so it is direction change more than drift. The drift is the language: Elixir grew from 7,565 lines (10-14 baseline) to 14,126 in 86 .ex/.exs files, while the Spiral part grew only from 1,458 to 1,783 (7 files) plus 7 ps1 scripts. KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR and KINO-SPIRAL-GLEAM sit at 10%. WHY NOT FINISHED: (1) no end state was ever written; the goal kept widening (everywhere, without Elixir, faster than Elixir tooling). (2) Infrastructure firefighting took the effort: daemon stalls, memsup, retire races, false passes and mounts are about 60% of the notes. (3) Ownership churned (lane-a, lane-b, helper lanes, main, tibia, x), so nobody drove the port. (4) Every fix was made in Elixir because it was the fastest place, so the port debt compounds. REMAINING PATH, with the end state written now: Cells is finished when (a) every repo's test blocks run through it (CELLS-EVERYWHERE), (b) its Elixir is rewritten in Spiral (to Gleam for the BEAM parts, Rust for the hot paths) with the parallel machinery kept (EXS-TO-SPIRAL), and (c) the benchmark shows parity or better with the Elixir version per stage. Rule from today: a Cells fix that touches Elixir comes with a port slice of the same module (EXS-TO-SPIRAL).")
