inl note_of_delphi_arc_memory_1 () = Note ("DELPHI-ARC-MEMORY", 1u32, "2026-10-25T15:25Z", "main", "10-25 11:25 main: folded from apps/compiler/tmp/NEXT.md 'Open, in order' entry 6 (COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE step 6); no backlog item had it. Measure first: peak RSS of the Delphi row of a long-running heap sample (mega_omniledger runtime_native) against its C row.")
inl fiscal_pattern_oracle_families () = Task ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", "fiscal's .NET pattern oracle covers every family schema: patterns.txt holds only the 196 BR corpus patterns, so FatturaPA, CFDI, UBL, KSeF and the other family patterns never meet .NET; pattern-oracle.ps1 derives the list from every family root's schema tree in deps and corpus_checks' 5 pattern tests run over all of it", TaskKind, P1, D2, 90u32, Active, "FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE", "fiscal,xsd,regex,oracle", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fiscal_pattern_oracle_families () = Planning ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", Minutes 45u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_fiscal_pattern_oracle_families_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", 1u32, "2026-10-25T15:51Z", "fiscal", "11:5x fiscal: drafted and measured. Distinct patterns outside the BR list: it 31 (15 with \\p), mx 24, pl 21, eu 0, 75 in union; all compile under .NET; the committed patterns.txt is exactly the distinct pattern set of the 580 corpus.txt schemas (same 196, different order), so the list is derivable. Two parts: (1) applied now, scripts/pattern-examples.ps1 reads \\p{Block} / \\P{Block} inside and outside classes (printable representatives per block, so probes carry no control characters; -cin/-ceq because PowerShell's -in/-eq ignore case): the 196 BR patterns give byte-identical examples to HEAD (392 of 392), the 75 family patterns now give 150 of 150 examples that .NET matches (20 threw before). (2) post-land patch, session scratchpad fiscal-post-land/apply.py (5 files, all-or-nothing): pattern-oracle.ps1 derives the list (corpus.txt schemas, then every other deps/corpus family schema, first-seen order) and writes patterns.txt itself, corpus_checks pins 271, .gitignore ignores src/patterns.txt (the tracked copy becomes a build product: the user's commit removes it), README updated, and KSeF's known_defect goes to None. Read-only run on a scratch copy: patterns 271 (580 + 25 schemas), probes 1,788, every pattern with a positive and a negative probe. Gate before the land: job-1792943448350-fiscal-oracle-271 runs corpus_checks' 5 pattern tests on that copy against gate 2's patched lib. Rule, fixed now: 0 disagreements on all 5 -> apply part 2 right after the lib land; any disagreement -> read it, fix xsd_regex (or the probe generator if .NET's own row is what differs), re-gate.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pattern_oracle_families_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", 2u32, "2026-10-25T16:30Z", "fiscal", "12:3x fiscal: job-1792943448350 (271 patterns, 11:52-12:17) is INVALID as a patch proof: its scratch copy had deps junctioned to fiscal/deps, and Cells' find_spiral_lib (apps/cells notebook.ex) takes the lib from the first ancestor holding deps/spiral/lib/spiral, so it ran the TREE xsd_regex (the packageDir in the copied package.spiproj does not override that). Its result is exactly the old lib's: all 271 compile; the derivative matcher rejects '12' for (\\p{IsBasicLatin}{2,10}) (the misparsed class has no digits; .NET's row says 1); the DFA, table and finite-DFA tests time out at 1,200 s (unrolled {1,1000}). A faithful Python port of the patched parser (session scratchpad xsd-regex-repeat/parser.py) parses that pattern as RxRepeat(C[0..127], 2, 10) and accepts '12'. Gate 2's fiscal stage (196 patterns) did resolve the patched lib (its copy had no deps; the walk reached <work>\\lib\\spiral), so that result stands. Rerun: job-1792945636355-fiscal-oracle-271b, the copy placed at <work>\\fiscal-271 under the patched lib's root, deps a real folder with only a corpus junction; the derivative test on that pair is the positive control. Rule of note 1 unchanged.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pattern_oracle_families_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", 3u32, "2026-10-25T17:00Z", "fiscal", "13:0x fiscal: prediction for job-1792945636355 (271b), recorded before its result, from the Python port of the patched parser + DFA builder (session scratchpad xsd-regex-repeat/parser.py, dfa-costs.tsv). All 271 patterns build a DFA within 4,096 states. The 75 family patterns cost about 9 Python-seconds together: [\\p{IsBasicLatin}\\p{IsLatin-1Supplement}]{1,1000} and [^|]{1,1000} each build 1,001 states in about 1.0e6 compare steps (the unrolled form projected 1.2e10). The total stays dominated by the BR NF-e QR-code patterns (.* with many intervals: 494 states / 4.4e8 steps, 345 / 1.1e8, 345 / 1.0e8), which already pass in Rust (gate 2's slowest DFA cell 206 s). So the DFA, table and finite-DFA cells should finish far inside 1,200 s; if they time out anyway, the cost is not in the new patterns and the first thing to read is that run's lib resolution.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pattern_oracle_families_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-PATTERN-ORACLE-FAMILIES", 4u32, "2026-10-25T17:19Z", "fiscal", "13:2x fiscal: job-1792945636355 (271b, copy under the patched lib's root) PASSED 5/5: every_corpus_pattern_compiles, matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe, dfa_matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe, table_matches_like_dotnet_on_every_probe and every_corpus_pattern_has_a_finite_dfa, 0 disagreements over 271 patterns x 1,788 probes, including the positive control (\\p{IsBasicLatin}{2,10}) on '12'. Slowest cell 321 s, whole run 782 s (the tree-lib run took 1,486 s and timed out 3 cells), which matches note 3's prediction. The rule of note 1 is met: part 2 (fiscal-post-land/apply.py) is applied by job-1792947423678 right after the lib land. That job has passed its oracle check and is in kit land.ps1's reader guard, waiting on another team's Cells mix test (erl 107672, started 12:28) that reads the live lib.")
inl remote_laptop_lane () = Task ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", "A second LAN laptop as a remote eoie worker: key-only SSH, scripted bootstrap (toolchains, repos, eoie host), an eoie remote executor for job start, EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH covering both; the rehearsal for renting cloud/GPU machines", Story, P1, D4, 30u32, Active, "EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH", "eoie,remote,infra,ci-offload,guide", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_laptop_lane () = Planning ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", Minutes 600u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T15:47Z", "main", "10-25 11:47 main (user 10-25 11:2x-11:4x: a second laptop on the LAN to battle-test eoie remote work and rehearse renting DigitalOcean/Vultr/vast.ai machines; user rated the idea and asked me to take it over). DONE: SSH with key-only login from this box. A dedicated key ~/.ssh/laptop_ed25519 (passphrase-free, comment main-to-laptop) is authorized for alias in C:\\ProgramData\\ssh\\administrators_authorized_keys and ~\\.ssh\\authorized_keys. sshd Running/Automatic; firewall rule sshd-lan allows TCP 22 from 192.168.2.0/24 only. The install fought two snags: 0x800b0101 CERT_E_EXPIRED from Add-WindowsCapability (the laptop clock was off; w32tm resync fixed it), then 'file in use' while servicing was still busy. Laptop: DESKTOP-6E8C14G, Windows 10 Pro (this box is Windows 11), 8 cores, 15.9 GB RAM, a Samsung 840 EVO 250 GB with only 9.5 GB free, git, pwsh 7 (winget copy), winget. The SSH default shell is cmd, so the helper scratchpad\\laptop.ps1 -Command '<pwsh>' sends -EncodedCommand to powershell. BLOCKER: disk. Toolchains + repos + spiral-bin need about 20-30 GB, so the user decides what to free (a survey of the ~165 GB outside Users/Program Files/ProgramData is running). Password SSH login is still on: a security setting the user flips (sshd_config PasswordAuthentication no).")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 2u32, "2026-10-25T16:26Z", "main", "10-25 12:26 main: connectivity hardened. (1) ~/.ssh/config Host laptop: HostName DESKTOP-6E8C14G, key, BatchMode; the host key is pinned under HostKeyAlias laptop-desktop-6e8c14g, so an IP change doesn't trip verification. (2) scratchpad\\laptop.ps1 resolves the name itself, falls back to the last address that worked (~/.ssh/laptop-last-ip.txt), then to 'tailscale ip'; name lookup was flaky once today. (3) Tailscale installed on both machines via winget (hash-verified), 'tailscale up --unattended' on the laptop (hostname laptop-6e8c14g), waiting for the user to approve its login link; this box's 'up' printed no link, so that needs a recheck. (4) For the user, because it's a security setting: the firewall rule sshd-lan only allows 192.168.2.0/24. To work on any LAN AND over Tailscale it needs: Set-NetFirewallRule -Name sshd-lan -RemoteAddress LocalSubnet,100.64.0.0/10. The laptop's network is categorized Public, so the installer's Private-only rule never applies.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T17:05Z", "main", "10-25 13:05 main: all user-side steps DONE (user 12:1x). Tailscale is up on both machines under account fc1943s@ (laptop-6e8c14g 100.97.57.53, main-box 100.78.144.60; tailscale ping direct via 192.168.2.28:41641, 36 ms). The firewall rule sshd-lan now allows LocalSubnet,100.64.0.0/10. 'ssh laptop' works on the LAN and with -o HostName=100.97.57.53 over Tailscale; the first try timed out while this box's routes were still settling. The laptop has 89.8 GB free; WoW Data (107.5 GB) is still present, so freeing it is optional now. Backup of _retail_ Interface + WTF: a 656 MB tar on the laptop, copying to C:\\home\\data\\wow-retail-11.2.7 (scp in flight). Next, nothing pending from the user: verify the backup by entry count, then bootstrap the toolchains over SSH, then the eoie host (EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR for dispatch).")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 4u32, "2026-10-25T17:46Z", "guide", "13:46 guide (session 88a146f8): took over the laptop lane from main's fork per the user. This lane coordinates all lanes to keep the laptop near 100% CPU while the main box gets headroom; git stays the user's on both machines, so no git transport. Handoff from main: laptop DESKTOP-6E8C14G, Win10 Pro, 8 cores, 15.9 GB RAM, 89.8 GB free; git/pwsh7/winget/sshd present; key-only SSH via laptop_ed25519 (user alias), 'ssh laptop' + Tailscale fallback (laptop 100.97.57.53, box 100.78.144.60), helper laptop.ps1. Transport settled non-git: file copy (tar+scp/sftp) over SSH, M4 hash proof unchanged, recorded by kit on EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR note 3; kit will not own an internal-repo bus. Progress 30 = connectivity/SSH done of {ssh, bootstrap, eoie host}. GATE: the user-driven fork (i-have-a) is mid archive-copy (~455/656 MB to C:\\home\\data\\wow-retail-11.2.7), then verify-by-entry-count, then bootstrap; read-only SSH only until main forwards the fork-idle signal, then this lane runs the toolchain bootstrap. Next: read-only laptop state check + a staged bootstrap script run on the go-signal.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 5u32, "2026-10-25T17:55Z", "kit2", "13:55 kit2: user renamed this session (88a146f8) guide->kit2 - now a second kit lane co-owning kit items with kit, and owner of remote-node offload (high-CPU jobs from any session -> laptop). Synced a proposal to kit: claim-by-note to avoid collision; I respect the eoie/kit feature freeze (no source changes until KIT-RENAME), so interim offload runs as org tooling in i574n.github/scripts, folded into EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR M2 after the freeze. Read-only laptop probe 17:46Z: fully toolchained (rustup nightly-2026-10-18 + wasm32 x2, zig 0.17.0, gcc 15.2.0, dotnet 9.0.200, gleam 1.19.2, lua 5.1.5, bun 1.2.5, elixir; 12 repos checked out: spiral eoie tibia x fiscal dice alphabet polyglot dir-tree-html i574n.github blog vault). Gap: no eoie/kit/spiral binary on laptop PATH (host not deployed) - plan to scp a built supervisor binary (freeze-safe file copy) to unblock M1. Holding all laptop writes until main forwards the fork-idle signal.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_6 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 6u32, "2026-10-25T18:10Z", "kit2", "14:09 kit2 (user clarification on git): git is the user's ONLY on this machine - the review box, source of truth. On external nodes (laptop) kit2 MANAGES the files and MAY use git internally if useful (user: 'that is on you'); results come back in place here as unstaged changes for the user to review. So internal git on the laptop is reopened as a kit2-owned node-side option (kit declined to own an internal-repo bus; node-side is now my call), independent of the non-git bring-back to this box. New research tasks from the user: study deltadb / the new anthropic VCS / fossil sync models and beat them with the typefeatureset + iggy; and check whether the retired llm-browser-tab bundle features can serve multi-machine transport.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_7 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 7u32, "2026-10-25T18:20Z", "main", "2026-10-25 14:20 main: bootstrap done and verified. Toolchains through scoop under pwsh 7 with process-scoped -ExecutionPolicy Bypass (WinPS 5.1 policy stays Restricted, the user's setting): gh, zig 0.17.0 (CI parity), rebar3, wasmtime installed; elixir, gcc 15.2, lua51, cmake, ninja present; erlang, gleam, pwsh, git updated; rust nightly-2026-10-18 default + wasm32-unknown-unknown/wasip2 + rustfmt/clippy/rust-src; .NET 11.0.100-rc.2 in spiral-bin via spiral's install-dotnet.ps1. Repos: alphabet, dice, i574n.github, polyglot, spiral, vault, spiral/deps/polyglot fast-forwarded (uncommitted 2025 edits stashed as 'laptop pre-update 2026-10-25' in polyglot 5, spiral 11, deps/polyglot 1); cloned eoie(kit), x, tibia, fiscal, blog, dir-tree-html; the fork deps/The-Spiral-Language via spiral's init-app-build.ps1; every head equals main-box's. git line endings match (system autocrlf=true, repo .gitattributes LF, 0 CR bytes on both). eoie host: fresh-clone build of eoie HEAD a12135b fails (src/eoie_job_queue has decide.spi but no generated decide.rs; reported to kit), so main-box's eoie.exe (sha256 CDEF7564...) runs at C:\\home\\bin\\eoie.exe; job start over SSH survives the session. New org tool i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1 <machine> run|job|status|sync|fetch, machines in ~/.config/remote-machines.json (outside the public repo): sync ships modified + untracked files over a detached checkout of the same HEAD (bundle when the far side lacks the commit), deletes deleted files, and both sides compare one sha256 digest over the changed files (first run: i574n.github, 2 files, D289DE64B8DF197E on both). Mirror contract: laptop repos are mirrors; sync detaches and cleans untracked non-ignored files there, so nothing is authored on the laptop. Free disk 82.6 GB (WoW Data 107.5 GB still there, the user's to delete).")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_8 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 8u32, "2026-10-25T18:24Z", "kit2", "14:24 kit2: ONE offload tool = i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1 (main's, landed). It already carries the per-file sha256 proof (Get-TreeDigest over the changed set, verified equal both sides) on a git checkout --detach HEAD + clean baseline, which fits the relaxed git rule (internal git on the node, non-git delta tar, read-only local git, non-git fetch bring-back). Folded in the one missing piece - the read-only/write-back offload scope rule - as a header on remote.ps1 (additive, no logic change). Retired my scratch remote-run.ps1 (one record per fact); its unique extras left as optional future adds: a selftest verb (local digest round-trip) and a whole-tree (not just delta) verify flag. iggy is parked (unix-only fd hand-off) -> ssh stays the transport. remote.ps1 verbs: run|job|status|sync|fetch; machines in ~/.config/remote-machines.json.")
inl note_of_remote_laptop_lane_9 () = Note ("REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", 9u32, "2026-10-25T18:44Z", "kit2", "14:43 kit2: ID rename REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE -> REMOTE-WORKER-NODE DEFERRED. agile rename rewrites the whole 6.5MB backlog and keeps losing the optimistic-concurrency race while the backlog is hot (node work, many lanes noting). Reverted the dependents' deps (EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR, IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE) to REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE to clear the dangling refs. The TERM change is DONE (agent-sync.md, remote.ps1 header, memory, go-forward usage; all lanes notified) - only the backlog ID is pending, to retry in a quiet window. Historical notes keep 'laptop' as log.")
inl wow_lua_sandbox () = Task ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", "WoW addons in Spiral compiled to Lua 5.1, developed and simulated headless (wowless + typed API bindings from LuaLS annotations) without the game installed; the real client only for the final test; the pivot after tibia instead of Dota 2", Epic, P2, D7, 0u32, Planned, "REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", "wow,lua,sandbox,addons,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_wow_lua_sandbox () = Planning ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", Minutes 6000u32, Load Light, Assigned Pair)
inl note_of_wow_lua_sandbox_1 () = Note ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", 1u32, "2026-10-25T15:59Z", "main", "10-25 11:59 main (user 10-25 11:5x: pivot to WoW after tibia instead of Dota 2; keep a Spiral->Lua sandbox for weeks and months without WoW installed, needing the real client only at the end). Laptop facts (REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE): retail 11.2.7 (The War Within) install; Data 107.5 GB, _retail_ 1.0 GB with 88 addons (AllTheThings, Auctionator, Baganator, Bartender4, BestInSlotRedux, Chattynator, the DBM family, ...) and 88 WTF saved-variable files. Keeping only _retail_ (addons + WTF) frees about 108 GB.\nSandbox shape, from what exists:\n(1) wowless (github.com/wowless/wowless): a headless WoW client Lua + FrameXML interpreter for addon testing. It downloads retail Lua/XML interface code itself, runs it on elune (a taint-aware Lua 5.1 fork), and can click and mouse over the frames an addon creates; it runs one addon via --addondir. It is pre-alpha (its README says errors are most likely wowless's own) and develops under Docker. So the 107 GB Data folder isn't needed for this.\n(2) Blizzard's interface source: Gethe/wow-ui-source mirrors FrameXML per build (live/classic branches); townlong-yak browses it per build.\n(3) Typed API surface: Ketho/vscode-wow-api, LuaLS annotations generated from the wiki, is the source for typed Spiral bindings to the WoW API, generated rather than hand-written (memory: no hand-crafted Lua).\n(4) The Lua 5.1 (WoW) backend already exists (memory backend-targets).\nRISKS: (a) retail is now 12.0.7 (Midnight). Midnight makes combat state inaccessible to addon logic (WeakAuras: no Midnight version; the Beacon fork is in early access), so the 11.2.7 addons target an API live servers no longer have. The sandbox must target 12.x interface code (wowless fetches current retail), and combat addons (DBM, meters, WeakAuras-like) fall under the new 'secret value' rules. Non-combat addons (Auctionator, Baganator, Bartender4, AllTheThings, Chattynator) are the safe first corpus. (b) AzerothCore is a 3.3.5a (WotLK) server, so it can't host a retail client. TrinityCore master targets recent retail through a custom launcher (Arctium); private servers are against Blizzard's terms, so the end test on live with a subscription is the clean path. (c) wowless's pre-alpha state means sandbox gaps get fixed there or in our own harness.\nSources: https://github.com/wowless/wowless ; https://github.com/Ketho/vscode-wow-api ; https://warcraft.wiki.gg/wiki/XML_schema ; https://trinitycore.atlassian.net/wiki/x/-D5pB ; https://www.wowhead.com/news/no-weakauras-addon-for-midnight-378740 ; https://www.curseforge.com/wow/addons/beacon")
inl note_of_wow_lua_sandbox_2 () = Note ("WOW-LUA-SANDBOX", 2u32, "2026-10-25T16:26Z", "main", "10-25 12:26 main: (a) wowless without Docker: it is CMake 3.24 + Ninja + vcpkg (casclib, curl, expat, libyaml, openssl, sqlite3, utf8h, zlib, sdl3 + sdl3-image) plus the elune Lua 5.1 fork and Lua submodules (tactless for TACT/CASC, penlight, lpeg, cjson, luadbd, ...); all of them build natively on Windows, and Docker is only its devcontainer. It's active (last commit 2026-10-22). Native build = a CPU-heavy first vcpkg pass, a good first laptop job once its disk is freed. (b) Mega samples measured: brzozowski_derivatives 11.4k lines (135 unions, 161 nominals, 731 foralls, 25 GADT cases), omniledger_erp_kernel 12.8k (107/99/254/15), lean_cic 10.6k, spiral_proves_spiral 13.5k, zeta_structural 8.4k. Best domain fits: Auctionator (money as ledger-typed copper, scan/post/cancel as a typestate machine; omniledger's shape) and Baganator's search language (a query grammar; brzozowski's derivatives). Neither is touched by Midnight's combat secrecy. (c) Why Midnight changed: Blizzard's 'combat philosophy and addon disarmament' post: combat state becomes a secret value addons can display but not read, against combat automation; native boss timers, cooldown and damage-meter replacements, and exceptions added over patches (12.1 aura changes). Sources: https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24246290/combat-philosophy-and-addon-disarmament-in-midnight ; https://warcraft.wiki.gg/wiki/Patch_12.0.0/Planned_API_changes ; https://warcraft.wiki.gg/wiki/Patch_12.1.0/API_changes")
inl delphi_file_read_buffer () = Task ("DELPHI-FILE-READ-BUFFER", "Delphi file reads need a caller-owned buffer: FPC's FileRead(h, var Buffer, n) writes into a by-reference untyped buffer, and granular style forbids a global Pascal helper body, so lib file_system.read_all_text/read_all_bytes have no Delphi arm. Options: a Spiral array u8 local (Delphi dynamic array; FileRead(h, a[0], n) as one call) plus a portable bytes-to-string primitive, or a compiler primitive for a sized uninitialized local a macro can pass by reference", TaskKind, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "SPIRAL-ASSIGN-PRIMITIVE", "delphi,lib,compiler,granular", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_delphi_file_read_buffer () = Planning ("DELPHI-FILE-READ-BUFFER", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_delphi_file_read_buffer_1 () = Note ("DELPHI-FILE-READ-BUFFER", 1u32, "2026-10-25T16:41Z", "fiscal", "Found 2026-10-25 by fiscal while drafting FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH step 1 (n10/n11). The write side needs no buffer: FileCreate/FileWrite/FileClose take an integer handle, one host call each, flow in Spiral (delphi-arms/apply.py). Generated fiscal modules never read files (census: only write_all_text), so step 1 does not wait on this.")
inl eoie_remote_executor () = Task ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", "eoie runs jobs on more than one machine: a supervised Gleam coordinator per machine with typed slot/lease/job typestate, the existing Rust job runner on each, SSH transport with Tailscale fallback, placement by free slot and load", Feature, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE,EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH,KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", "eoie,remote,gleam,rust,typefeatureset,eoie-team", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_remote_executor () = Planning ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", Minutes 1200u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_1 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 1u32, "2026-10-25T16:55Z", "main", "10-25 12:55 main (user 10-25 12:0x: keep the laptop at ~100% CPU like this box so it unclogs the queue; build it with the typefeatureset and a deliberate Gleam/Rust split, coordinated between main and the eoie team). Design, by memory backend-balance (Gleam where BEAM pays: actors, supervision; Rust where speed pays):\n(1) Gleam coordinator: a supervised actor per machine (OTP supervisor tree). It owns that machine's slot table (heavy/compile slots, quiet windows) as typed state and restarts a dead SSH link without losing queued jobs. Machine, slot, job and lease are nominal types, and job state is a typestate union (Queued | Leased of machine | Running of machine * pid | Settled of outcome), so a job can't be run twice or settle without a lease.\n(2) Rust runner on each machine: the existing eoie job supervisor, unchanged in semantics (process tree, wall clock, memory cap, logs). The coordinator only adds 'which machine'.\n(3) Transport: OpenSSH (key auth, already working: Host laptop in ~/.ssh/config, HostKeyAlias), with Tailscale as the fallback route. No new daemon or port.\n(4) Placement rule: a heavy job goes to the machine with a free heavy slot and the lowest load; jobs that read a live tree (Cells, lib gates, matrix) stay on the machine holding that tree until tree sync (git push to a bare repo on the laptop) is proven by hash.\n(5) Health: EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH covers both machines; the stall rule is per job label family.\nUser-side blockers first: Tailscale login approval, the firewall scope (LocalSubnet,100.64.0.0/10), and deleting WoW Data (107.5 GB) to free the laptop's disk; addons + WTF are backed up to C:\\home\\data\\wow-retail-11.2.7. Then bootstrap over SSH: scoop, pwsh 7, dotnet SDK, rustup with the pinned nightly-2026-10-18 + wasm32, Erlang/Elixir/Gleam, zig 0.17.0, gcc 15.2, lua 5.1, the repos, the eoie host.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_2 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 2u32, "2026-10-25T17:31Z", "eoie", "13:31 kit groom (eoie team), sequencing as main asked.\n- Order: SLATE -> edition-3 respell -> KIT-RENAME (eoie is in a feature freeze until then) -> EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH -> this item. New packages are born kit_* names, so the rename doesn't touch them.\n- Milestones (pre-registered; estimate 1200m = their sum):\n  M1 180m: kit remote status <host>. It runs the remote host's kit over ssh and returns typed slots, load and cores (cores from the MACHINE-HEALTH sampler: cpu_usage/100 x CPU count). Needs main's laptop bootstrap.\n  M2 240m: job start --on <machine> (Rust side). The job record stores its machine; status, tail, stop and wait route by machine. Remote semantics unchanged: the remote kit supervisor runs the job.\n  M3 360m: the Gleam coordinator. A supervised actor per machine with the slot table, the job typestate (Queued | Leased | Running | Settled) and the placement rule from note 1.\n  M4 240m: tree-sync proof. A live-tree job goes remote only when the src tree hash on both machines is equal; otherwise it stays local.\n  M5 180m: end to end. Two heavy jobs queued run one per machine, and the laptop holds >=90% CPU over 10 min while the queue is not empty.\n- Decision rules (written before measuring):\n  (a) The coordinator stays resident (one BEAM node per machine) when a per-call BEAM start pushes dispatch past 2 s p95; it is per-call only when it stays under that.\n  (b) The coordinator itself costs <1% of a core over 10 min, or it moves to Rust and Gleam keeps only supervision.\n  (c) Done when M5 passes and MACHINE-HEALTH reports both machines.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_3 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 3u32, "2026-10-25T17:42Z", "eoie", "13:42 kit: tree sync decision (git stays the user's on both machines; asked by the guide lane, which drives the laptop bootstrap). Note 1's 'git push to a bare repo' is replaced.\n- Decision: file copy over the existing SSH channel (tar + scp/sftp of the src slice, extracted on the laptop). M4's proof is unchanged: the src tree hashes must be equal on both machines before a live-tree job goes remote.\n- kit owns no internal repo bus: from-scratch means kit's own primitives (eoie_tree_copy, snapshot hashing), not a git dependency.\n- Pre-registered escalation rule: if a full src-slice sync takes over 30 s p95 over the LAN, add a kit hash-manifest delta (send only files whose hash differs, then the same tree-hash proof). Still no git.\n- M4 estimate unchanged (240m).")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_4 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 4u32, "2026-10-25T17:59Z", "eoie", "13:59 kit: M4 refinement, agreed with kit2, who owns the remote plumbing (host deploy, interim offload as org tooling i574n.github/scripts/remote-run.ps1).\n- M4's tree-hash proof is enforced per offload. The tool builds a per-file sha256 manifest of the src slice, tars it (excluding target, .git, .cache), rebuilds the manifest after extraction into C:\\home\\remote\\<name> (never the laptop's user checkouts), and refuses to run on a mismatch.\n- Rule kept beyond the input proof: jobs that write into a live tree here stay local even with equal input hashes, because their outputs race with edits made here (kit regen/publish, lib lands, matrix accepts).\n  Read-only compiles, tests and reviews may go remote; results come back to scratch or job folders, never into a repo tree.\n- Host binary: the canonical installed eoie.exe (not the supervisor relaunch copies). Redeploy after SLATE installs, and record both sha256s.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_5 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 5u32, "2026-10-25T18:00Z", "kit2", "13:59 kit2 owns (per split) host-deploy (scp a built eoie.exe, freeze-safe, hash recorded here) and the interim offload tooling (i574n.github/scripts, folds into M2 after rename); kit keeps the typed-coordinator design. The offload tool enforces M4 per job: local vs remote manifest-hash equality before any remote run.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_6 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 6u32, "2026-10-25T18:03Z", "kit2", "14:03 kit2: host-deploy artifact confirmed = C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\eoie.exe, 19.9MB, sha256 cdef7564d80483bc13bae7def9d0c1d1edb14b54f171c7941e42e358ad56dc75 (per kit: canonical installed CLI, not the supervisor relaunch copies; host recreates those on first job). Deploy gated on fork-idle; proves 'ssh laptop eoie.exe job list' answers. Redeploy + record new sha256 after SLATE installs the next eoie.exe.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_7 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 7u32, "2026-10-25T18:13Z", "kit2", "14:13 kit2: host-deploy VERIFIED read-only. Laptop C:\\home\\bin\\eoie.exe sha256 == C:\\home\\git\\eoie\\eoie.exe (cdef7564...), MATCH. The fork placed it; M1 binary precondition met ('ssh laptop eoie.exe job list' can answer). Do NOT source-build eoie on the laptop until SLATE: 8 hand-split crates have committed .spi but no .rs, clean clone fails on eoie_job_queue/decide.rs (EOIE-HAND-CRATE-UNITS note 11). Redeploy + re-record sha after SLATE installs the next eoie.exe.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_8 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 8u32, "2026-10-25T18:13Z", "kit2", "14:13 kit2 (user Q: can the retired llm-browser-tab bundle features serve multi-machine transport?): YES, strongly. eoie_bundle_manifest + eoie_archive_tar_runtime are already the typed native transport: collect_bundle_manifest -> canonical sorted deduped manifest (symlink/special/unsafe/nested-zip rejected); manifest_fingerprint -> FNV-1a tree hash; write_deterministic_store_zip/deflate_zip -> REPRODUCIBLE archives (fixed timestamps, canonical order) so same content => same bytes => same hash, a stronger M4 proof than a content-only manifest; verify_archive_parity/verify_manifest_parity -> received==expected; archive_extract_generic_run -> budgeted extraction with confined links + rollback. The archive/bundle CLI surfaces exist (eoie_archive_contracts, eoie_bundle_retention). Plan: interim remote-run.ps1 (tar+scp+manifest) now; after the freeze a native 'eoie remote sync' verb built on these replaces it. Folds into M2/M4; my PowerShell tool is the hand-rolled stand-in for exactly this pipeline.")
inl note_of_eoie_remote_executor_9 () = Note ("EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR", 9u32, "2026-10-25T18:14Z", "kit2", "14:14 kit2 research (user: study deltadb/anthropic scion/fossil, beat them with typefeatureset+iggy). FINDINGS: Anthropic Scion (eng blog 2026-10-13 anthropic.com/engineering/redesigning-source-control) internal git replacement: server authoritative for history/blame/merge, CLIENTS FETCH ONLY NEEDED FILES (no full copy), pull 25x/status 207x/worktree 84x, not public. Fossil (fossil-scm.org): single-file SQLite repo, autosync over HTTP/SSH, serverless, atomic txns. DeltaDB (npm): offline-first, delta storage, LWW, eventually consistent. Iggy (iggy.apache.org incubating 0.9.0): append-only segment log (.log+.index), thread-per-core shared-nothing (io_uring/compio), streams>topics>partitions, consumer offsets, at-least-once+dedup. HOW WE BEAT THEM (typefeatureset+iggy+bundle): (1) ONE typed Spiral GADT model (job/lease/slot/health events + bundle manifest) -> Rust data plane + Gleam control plane, byte-identical codecs, typestate makes illegal job transitions unrepresentable - typed where all four are untyped. (2) Scion thin-fetch = our bundle-manifest slice, content-addressed by manifest_fingerprint + deterministic archives, hash-proven (M4) and delta-reducible (kit >30s escalation): Scion thin-fetch + Fossil content-addressing + DeltaDB deltas combined. (3) iggy = churn-safe event backbone, re-derivable from receipts. (4) KEY DIFFERENCE: Scion replaces git wholesale; we KEEP git as the human source-of-truth on the review box and use bundle+iggy only for the machine-to-machine plane. Outstanding: deltadb ambiguous (npm offline-first vs pypi polars/deltalake); took the npm sync one.")
inl postable_all_repos () = Task ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", "Run the postable split over every tracked .spi in every repo as one report: the batch xc post-thread --dry-run lands, a generic report command lists files and cells over one post per repo, and each repo burns its count down to 0 (x is at 0 today)", Story, P1, D3, 15u32, Active, "X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", "x,postability,splitter,eoie,lib,tibia,dice,e2e", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_postable_all_repos () = Planning ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_1 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T17:24Z", "x", "2026-10-25 13:24 -04:00 x: user question 10-25 ('how far from running the splitter in every code file we have, so we're ready to e2e test everything outside gh actions'). Baseline from X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE n7 (every 8th file): x 0/352 cells over one post, dice 23%, tibia 25%, lib 37%, eoie 40%. Milestones (estimates-from-rules): M1 the batch dry-run lands (drafted, X-BACKEND-MAP n5); M2 a report over all repos in one call, one row per repo; M3 lib and eoie fixed by splitting definitions by hand (the hand-modeled rule, never an automatic splitter); M4 each repo's build gates on 0, as x's does. Non-Spiral code is out of scope: the cell rule is Spiral's, and most .rs/.py/.c is generated.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_2 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 2u32, "2026-10-25T18:00Z", "x", "2026-10-25 14:00 -04:00 x: M1 redrafted for today's tree (the 10-23 draft predated the edition-3 respell and its Cons anchors are gone): scratchpad\\batch-dry-run\\apply.ps1. It adds post-thread --dry-run FILE FILE... = cli PreviewThreads (list string), fields kind 11, and xc run_preview_threads, which prints '=== FILE' before each preview and exits with the worst code. Dispatch goes through a new run_thread_more, so kind 11 is explicit and can never fall through to the posting else. Overweight definitions were split by hand (previews, thread_batch_text, run_thread_more, a separate parse test, usage_thread_previews), and the result is checked on copies: 0 cells over one post, lines over 120 unchanged (5/3/6), tree x-compat 0 findings. --check on a package copy with absolute packageDir: cli.spi and xc.spi 'checked package' (exit 0). Exit 3 with no message on some runs, including the UNPATCHED xc.spi, is 'Attestation timed out after 30000 ms' on the loaded box, not a type error; patched fields.spi needs a quieter re-check. Gate before landing: the fields re-check, then x's build (release xc + e2e -Accept for the new usage line + postable) after 5l'' installs.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_3 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 3u32, "2026-10-25T18:16Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 14:16 tibia census (full, not sampled; scratch postable-census.ps1 mirroring x splitter.spi: a cell is a column-0 block after a blank line, one post = weight <= 273 = 280 minus the 'c1 1/1' header, non-ASCII glyphs weigh 2): 62 files, 1328 cells, 399 over one post (30 %). Top: src/hunting_pack 81/217, src/terrain 31/63 (heaviest 11383, generated by apps/map/run.ps1), src/pack_sim 29/39, src/hunt_sim 22/49, apps/map/map 20/29 (8200), apps/host/arena_host 15/28, combat 12/35, network 12/36, offscreen 12/48, geometry 11/41. Rule for tibia's M3, written before any split: (1) split by hand, never by a tool: a long definition becomes named helpers by role (one concept per definition), a long test becomes several tests or a fixture definition plus the test; a long union/record type splits into nested named types only when the parts are concepts of their own, otherwise its constructors move to one per line so a blank-line cut is legal. (2) generated cells (terrain, map) are fixed in their generator (run.ps1 emits data in chunks of at most 273). (3) files touched by queued slices wait for those lands (hunting_pack and gpu_sim for PARTY, samples, lua and random for serial-b). (4) each batch: the census count for its files drops, formatter clean, the files' Cells tests pass with memo off, no behaviour change. (5) order: files with the fewest over-post cells first, so the per-file count reaches 0 quickly and a build gate per file can follow (M4).")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_4 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 4u32, "2026-10-25T18:37Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 14:37 tibia batch 1 drafted (scratch postable-b1, land via postable-land.ps1 in job-1792953363791): activity, blessings, damage and hunt go from 7 over-post cells to 0 (35 to 50 cells). Splits: activity's test gets a checked_counts fixture and becomes two tests; blessings.dropped gets item_chance, its 6-case test becomes 3 by case group; damage's 8-case formula test becomes 4 and the fight-mode test 2; hunt gets a living type for attack's signature and its 1600-case loot test becomes struck_fresh, gold_conserved, loot_holds, case_purse and loot_breaks with a one-line test. Every ~dyn binding stays, so the C targets still compute at run time. The runner refuses when a tree file changed since the batch copy or a copy still has an over-post cell, then runs those files' Cells tests with memo off and restores on red.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_5 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 5u32, "2026-10-25T18:54Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 14:54 tibia batch 2 drafted (scratch postable-b2, step 1 of job-1792953363791 after batch 1): wire, vocations, combat_text, scoreboard and probe go to 0 over-post cells (99 to 104 cells, no new line over 120). Splits: wire's XTEA becomes xtea_term, encrypt/decrypt steps and top-level rounds, and adler_of folds a byte list for the Adler test; vocations' data_of becomes healing and berserk constructors (cooldown_id_of keeps its two special cases and a wildcard for the three LightHealingSpell ones); combat_text bundles before/ends into number_pattern with number_at for one occurrence; scoreboard's summary becomes action and outcome count texts, vitals and experience_text (same string), and finish_or_continue gets report_ended and report_cleared; probe's nearest_monster becomes closer plus a top-level nearest_from scan, and started_as gets hidden_for_a_second, login_failed, entered_game and enter_after_the_gate. The land runner now also runs the arena build (LUA-OK for every module) when a batch touches apps/arena, since scoreboard and probe have no tests of their own.")
inl note_of_postable_all_repos_6 () = Note ("POSTABLE-ALL-REPOS", 6u32, "2026-10-25T19:04Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-25 15:04 tibia rule (3) extended before landing anything: the 16 files the pending CELLS-APPS candidate re-land replaces whole (arena adjacency, client, competitor, measure, nexbot, observer, path, probe, random, scan, scoreboard, steps, summon, vbot; map; predict) wait for that re-land, or it would drop their postable splits. probe and scoreboard came out of batch 2 (their drafts are kept in scratch postable-held); batch 2 is now combat_text, vocations and wire. Batch 3 drafted (bot_dofile: chunk_run_in, load_failed, loaded_chunk and dofile_through stay inline, so error(message, 3) still names the dofile caller; bot_prelude: one silent_table builder for g_http and HTTP; standards: its 30 one-line rules grouped by blank lines), 3 files at 0 over-post cells, queued in serial-c after batches 1 and 2.")
inl social_profiles_local () = Task ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", "x: Mastodon and Bluesky postability profiles plus a local stub for each in e2e, so threads are tested for free against both rule sets with no paid X calls", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "X-COMPAT-VS-POSTABLE", "x,postability,e2e,mastodon,bluesky,social", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_social_profiles_local () = Planning ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_social_profiles_local_1 () = Note ("SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T17:24Z", "x", "2026-10-25 13:24 -04:00 x: from the same user question ('x without having to pay it, like a bootstrapped bluesky/mastodon instance'). Facts checked 10-25: Mastodon's default limit is 500 characters and every link counts as 23 (docs.joinmastodon.org/user/posting). Bluesky's cap is 300 graphemes plus 3,000 bytes, and the link counting is disputed by third parties, so it is pinned by a test against a real PDS before the profile is final. Plan: postability/mastodon.json and bluesky.json next to x.json (the profile already parameterizes weights, links and limits); stubs beside e2e/stub.py speaking the Mastodon statuses API and atproto createRecord; xc gains a target per site. A real self-hosted server (GoToSocial is Linux-only and needs WSL; a Bluesky PDS is a Node service) stays under X-FEDERATION-BRIDGES (Paused), so this item needs no WSL.")
inl eoie_backend_reach () = Task ("EOIE-BACKEND-REACH", "eoie reach <file.spi> --backend <name> [--lib <dir>]: the lib definitions a Spiral file reaches through the arms that backend specializes (the Delphi arm, else _, of a run_target; that key of a backend_switch), listing each one with no arm for the backend and the call chain to it; a non-unit backend_switch without the key emits a backend_switch_has_no_<B>_arm placeholder that fails the build, so this finds build breaks before a compile", TaskKind, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-AFFECTED-CRATES", "eoie,spiral,backends,tooling", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_eoie_backend_reach () = Planning ("EOIE-BACKEND-REACH", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_eoie_backend_reach_1 () = Note ("EOIE-BACKEND-REACH", 1u32, "2026-10-25T17:29Z", "fiscal", "Prototype measured 2026-10-25 by fiscal (session scratchpad delphi-survey/reach.py, about 100 lines of Python): on fiscal's generated_ksef_fa_3 against the Delphi backend it reached 45 lib definitions and found 14 gaps on the tree lib, 0 with the drafted Delphi slices, each with its chain (e.g. sm'.replace <- xml.escape_text_xml <- xml.write_node). Limits of the prototype: name resolution is textual (qualified names plus :< opens), and the |core- package is not walked. A typed version in eoie would read the package graph like EOIE-AFFECTED-CRATES does.")
inl iggy_multi_machine () = Task ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", "Apache Iggy as the multi-machine event log of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR: one Spiral GADT message model (job, lease, slot, health events) compiled to Rust and Gleam, Rust as the data plane (iggy SDK producer over TCP, the job runner on each machine), Gleam as the control plane (supervised coordinator consuming over Iggy HTTP, placement), byte-identical codecs from both backends; reopened from IGGY-EVAL because its first revisit trigger fired (a second machine consuming events live)", Story, P1, D6, 15u32, Blocked, "REMOTE-LAPTOP-LANE", "iggy,remote,gleam,rust,typefeatureset,eoie-team", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_iggy_multi_machine () = Planning ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", Minutes 900u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_1 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T17:52Z", "main", "2026-10-25 13:52 main: plan with pre-written rules. Facts now: latest is still server-0.9.0 (2026-09-18, gh release list), no 1.0; the release has no binary assets; core/server builds on compio, which has an IOCP driver on Windows, so native Windows is unverified, not ruled out (IGGY-EVAL note 2). No Erlang/Elixir SDK: Gleam talks HTTP (port 3000), Rust uses the iggy crate over TCP 8090. M1 (120 m, heavy, on the laptop): cargo build --release of tag server-0.9.0 natively on Windows, start it with a disposable data dir, round-trip 10,000 messages from main-box over Tailscale with the iggy CLI or bench. Rule M1: build ok AND server up AND 10,000/10,000 received in order with 0 errors -> the server lives on the laptop, single node, and M2-M4 proceed; build or run fails -> record the error, no WSL (no-wsl rule), keep the SSH transport of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR and park this item until a Linux node exists (CI-OFFLOAD or a rented VM). M2 (300 m): the message model as one Spiral union family (GADT-first, typestate indices on lease/slot), codecs derived once; Rust producer in the job runner. M3 (300 m): Gleam consumer over HTTP inside the coordinator's supervision tree, offsets server-stored, at-least-once with dedup by message id. M4 (180 m): a self-checking parity sample, the same values encoded by the Rust and Gleam outputs must be byte-identical and decode back. Churn guard: nothing stored only in Iggy; every event is re-derivable from job receipts, so a pre-1.0 format break costs a fresh data dir. Split rule (Gleam/Rust balance): Rust gets the code on the per-message hot path, Gleam gets supervision, placement and retries (backend-balance).")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_2 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 2u32, "2026-10-25T18:01Z", "main", "2026-10-25 14:01 main: rule added before the M1 build runs. A source scan of server-0.9.0 predicts a Windows build failure: core/server/src/segment_recovery.rs:41 imports std::os::unix::fs::FileExt unguarded in non-test code, and core/server_common/src/fs_utils.rs:24 uses nix::fcntl::fallocate (guard not yet read). Rule M1b: run the unmodified build first and record the real errors. If every error is a unix-only std/nix call with a direct Windows equivalent (FileExt::read_at/write_at -> std::os::windows::fs::FileExt::seek_read/seek_write, fallocate -> File::set_len, getrlimit/uname diagnostics -> cfg-out) and they total at most 10 sites, carry them as one local patch file outside the repos (no upstream contact) and continue M1 with it; the round-trip then must also pass a restart-recovery check (stop the server mid-stream, restart, consumer resumes from its stored offset with 0 lost and 0 duplicated past dedup). More than 10 sites, or any error inside compio's driver or the shard runtime -> park as in rule M1.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_3 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T18:20Z", "main", "2026-10-25 14:20 main: M1 PARKED by rule M1b (applied as written). Native Windows build of tag server-0.9.0 on the laptop (rust 1.98.0 from its rust-toolchain.toml, VS 2022 linker), three runs: (1) unmodified, 560 s: hwlocality-sys build script needs pkg-config + a system hwloc; (2) local patch 1 site, core/server/Cargo.toml routes windows to the existing musl branch (hwlocality features=[vendored], cmake build): passes; journal fails on std::os::fd (file_storage.rs:24,82, durable_storage.rs:328); patched 2 sites with std::os::windows::io::AsHandle::as_handle(..).try_clone_to_owned() -> File::from(OwnedHandle); (3) message_bus fails with 10 errors: fd_transfer.rs (std::os::unix, libc::fcntl F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC, TcpStream::as_raw_fd/from_raw_fd), transports/tcp.rs (std::os::fd, libc::shutdown SHUT_RD, PollFd as_raw_fd), socket_opts.rs (libc::SOMAXCONN). fd_transfer is the shard runtime's accepted-socket hand-off between thread-per-core shards, which the rule names as an automatic park; the count also passes 10 (3 patched + 8 here, before the server crate's known segment_recovery FileExt, fs_utils fallocate, diagnostics getrlimit/uname). Kept for a revisit: laptop C:\\home\\ext\\iggy (shallow clone of the tag) and C:\\home\\ext\\iggy-windows.patch (the 3 working sites). Consequence: the laptop transport stays SSH (i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1, eoie job start over SSH survives the session, checked with a 45 s probe). Iggy waits for a Linux node (CI-OFFLOAD runner or a rented VM, where io_uring is native); M2 (message model) and M4 (Rust/Gleam codec parity) are transport-independent and continue inside EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR, so the Iggy adapter becomes one more transport case later. Revisit triggers: a Linux node in the pool, or an iggy release whose message_bus builds on windows.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_4 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 4u32, "2026-10-25T18:46Z", "laptop-fork", "2026-10-25 14:46 laptop fork: user asked (14:4x) whether the Linux-only Iggy calls for a SOTA WSL bootstrap on the worker hosting k3s + latest Iggy. Facts: Iggy publishes no Windows server and, since server-0.5.0, no GitHub binaries at all (gh release list; _build_rust_artifacts.yml targets x86_64/aarch64 linux gnu/musl only; Windows zips only for iggy-cli in 2025). The laptop already has WSL 3.0.3 (microsoft/WSL Latest 2026-10-09; 3.0.4 pre-release 10-20), features Microsoft-Windows-Subsystem-Linux + VirtualMachinePlatform Enabled, hypervisor present, distro 'Ubuntu' (WSL 2 format, stopped, from the 2025 setup), no .wslconfig. k3s latest v1.37.1+k3s1 (2026-09-30); it bundles containerd, so it runs the official Iggy image with no Docker. GATE: the standing rule no-wsl-until-workflow-passes holds; this plan starts only when i574n.github/scripts/workflow.ps1 passes or the user lifts the rule for the worker node. Plan M1-WSL (laptop, heavy): (0) upgrade the Ubuntu distro to its current LTS, .wslconfig memory cap 6 GB (16 GB host), systemd=true in /etc/wsl.conf; reach ports through ssh -L from main-box (no Windows firewall change: that is the user's setting). (a) native: cargo build of the latest iggy server tag inside WSL (io_uring native), systemd unit, disposable data dir. (b) k3s v1.37.x single node + the apache/iggy image of the same version. Measure both the same way: 10,000-message round trip from main-box through the forward (0 errors, in order), restart-recovery (stop mid-stream, consumer resumes from its stored offset, 0 lost, 0 duplicated past dedup), idle RSS of the whole stack, p99 latency. Rule: choose (b) k3s when its extra idle RSS over (a) is <= 1 GB and its p99 is <= 1.2x (a)'s, because the same manifests then run unchanged on rented Linux nodes joining as k3s agents (the multi-machine goal); otherwise choose (a) and keep k3s for when a second Linux node exists. Either way M2-M4 (Spiral message model, Rust producer over TCP, Gleam consumer over HTTP, byte-identical codec parity) proceed unchanged on top. Owner after this fork retires: kit2 (remote plumbing) with the eoie team.")
inl note_of_iggy_multi_machine_5 () = Note ("IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE", 5u32, "2026-10-25T18:57Z", "kit2", "14:57 kit2: user LIFTED the no-WSL gate for the worker node (via the fork, 2026-10-25). Fork (i-have-a) owns M1-WSL: iterate until Iggy runs on k3s in the node's WSL (WSL 3.0.3 + Ubuntu present); scripts + k8s manifests into C:\\home\\git\\eoie (new folder, unstaged, user commits). Fork hands kit2 the endpoints (TCP 8090, HTTP 3000 via ssh -L), the credentials location (a k8s Secret, never printed) and the verify script when up. kit2 / EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR owns the transport-independent typed message model + Rust/Gleam codec parity to consume it; SSH stays the job-dispatch transport, iggy becomes the event log once k3s is up.")
inl lib_gate_kit_staged_land () = Task ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", "lib-gate-kit land.ps1 lands through a stage: copy the tree lib, run the slice's ApplyScript and the formatter on the copy, then commit only the changed files with lock retries and roll back the committed ones from saved originals if any stays locked, so no apply script can leave the live lib half-landed; plus the -Target/-Live switch the landing rule asks for, so the rollback can be proven on a scratch target", TaskKind, P1, D2, 0u32, Planned, "XSD-NAMESPACE-NAMES", "lib,gates,landing,safety", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land () = Planning ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_1 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 1u32, "2026-10-25T17:58Z", "fiscal", "Found 2026-10-25 13:33 by fiscal: kit land.ps1 ran xsd-slices/apply.ps1 on the tree, 2 of 4 files were written before a sharing lock (PermissionError) on the 3rd, and the job exited 1 with the tree half-landed (healed by hand from work\\XSD-SLICES\\base; XSD-NAMESPACE-NAMES n6). The fiscal apply script now stages itself and its rollback is proven, but every other team's apply script still writes the tree directly through the kit. The reader guard is not enough: it sees Cells, live-gui and release readers, not every process holding a lib file open.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_2 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 2u32, "2026-10-25T18:08Z", "main", "10-25 14:08 main: LANDED in the kit's land.ps1 (%TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit), so every team's apply is covered with no change to their scripts. Before the apply, land.ps1 snapshots every lib .spi/.spir and package.spiproj (land-snapshot\\<name>-<stamp>) with its hash. If the apply exits non-zero, Restore-Lib puts back every changed or deleted file, removes the files the apply added, and re-hashes everything. It prints 'every lib file matches its pre-land hash: True', or names the files to restore by hand. New -LibRoot (default: the tree lib; the reader guard runs only for the tree) makes it testable. Positive control on a scratch lib: an apply that rewrote a.spi, added new.spi and exited 1 left the lib byte-identical (a.spi 702895F0 before and after, new.spi removed, the other 2 files unchanged). Not covered: an apply that skips a locked file and still exits 0 (fiscal's catch [IO.IOException] trap); that needs the apply to exit non-zero. Next: DISK-CLEANUP's gate-work rule also covers land-snapshot (delete after the land's verdict file and a 'landed <name>' log line).")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_3 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 3u32, "2026-10-25T18:10Z", "fiscal", "14:1x fiscal: main landed this in the kit's land.ps1 at 14:06 (snapshot of every lib .spi/.spir/package.spiproj with its hash; restore on a non-zero apply exit; prints whether every file matches its pre-land hash). One record per fact: my stage-then-commit draft is NOT landed over it. It stays as proven merge material for the two gaps snapshot-and-restore leaves, and main has the path: (1) an apply that skips a locked write and exits 0, which a staged apply plus a kit-side commit closes by construction; (2) restore copies without lock retries. The draft is session scratchpad kit-staged-land/land.ps1 with prove.ps1, taking -Target (default the tree; reader guard only for the tree). Proofs on scratch targets: (a) normal land, 3 files incl. a new one; (b) a lock mid-commit rolls back the earlier commits, exit 1, hashes identical before and after; (c) a failing apply leaves the target untouched; (d) a new file committed before a locked one is deleted on rollback.")
inl note_of_lib_gate_kit_staged_land_4 () = Note ("LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND", 4u32, "2026-10-25T18:14Z", "main", "10-25 14:14 main: REPLACED n2's snapshot-and-restore with fiscal's stage-then-commit land.ps1, merged: kit land.ps1 1A5257FCB99DFA36 (previous kept as %TEMP%\\kit-land-merge\\land.ps1.snapshot-restore-version). The apply runs on a robocopy stage of the lib, so it never touches the tree; the kit formats the changed files on the stage and commits them with 20 x 3 s retries per copy, rolling back earlier commits if one stays locked. That closes both of n2's gaps: a skipped write that exits 0 can't half-land, and a restore no longer fails on a still-held lock. Merge additions: package.spiproj is staged and committed (fiscal's filter took only .spi/.spir, so a new module's registration would not have landed), and an apply that deletes a lib file is refused with the target untouched. Proofs on scratch targets (%TEMP%\\kit-land-merge, prove.ps1 + prove-extra.ps1): (a) normal 3 files incl. a new one: exit 0; (b) locked file mid-commit: exit 1, hashes identical, the new file absent; (c) failing apply: exit 1, untouched; (e) spiproj registration lands; (f) deleting apply refused, untouched. Callers unchanged (-Name -ApplyScript). Note: an apply that ignores -Lib and writes the tree itself (x's land-apply.ps1 wrapper) still lands, but outside the stage, so the kit neither formats nor rolls it back; it relies on its own -Undo.")
inl delphi_uses_units () = Task ("DELPHI-USES-UNITS", "codegenDelphi hardcodes the program's uses clause (uses SysUtils, Math; at spiral_compiler.fs:165652), so lib or app code cannot bring in another unit (Classes, StrUtils, or a third-party library such as ACBrNFe): collect units from a lib-side marker the way Gleam arms emit global \"import ...\" (e.g. a global whose text is \"uses <Unit>\" folds into the one uses clause, deduplicated), so a Spiral program compiled to Delphi can call a Pascal library with one host call per macro", TaskKind, P1, D3, 30u32, Active, "-", "delphi,compiler,codegen,interop", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_delphi_uses_units () = Planning ("DELPHI-USES-UNITS", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_delphi_uses_units_1 () = Note ("DELPHI-USES-UNITS", 1u32, "2026-10-25T18:12Z", "fiscal", "Found 2026-10-25 by fiscal designing FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH step 3: a Spiral-generated Delphi harness calling ACBr (TACBrNFe.NotasFiscais.LoadFromString / Validar / GerarXML) needs ACBrNFe and its units in uses. Today globals land after the type section (:165657), where a uses clause is not legal Pascal.")
inl note_of_delphi_uses_units_2 () = Note ("DELPHI-USES-UNITS", 2u32, "2026-10-25T18:14Z", "fiscal", "14:2x fiscal: draft in landing form, session scratchpad delphi-uses-units/apply.py <spiral_compiler.fs> [--apply] (anchored on text, newline-aware; dry run ok on base 34519B923366CCEC at line 165665, the single Delphi program-assembly site, so there is no core-pair twin). In codegenDelphi's program text, every env global whose text starts with 'uses ' is folded into the one uses clause after SysUtils, Math (trimmed, trailing ';' dropped, deduplicated, SysUtils/Math not repeated), and those globals are not emitted again in the declaration section; every other global is untouched. A lib or app Delphi arm then brings in a unit with global \"uses StrUtils\" and calls it with one host macro. Gate when packed: the compiler's Delphi matrix rows (unchanged output for every sample, since none emits a 'uses ' global) plus one new sample, a Delphi program whose global \"uses StrUtils\" lets $'ReverseString(...)' run and print, which fails today ('identifier not found') and passes after (the regression-sample rule: one minimal sample per fixed gap). Not queued: main's PACK-5L2 compiler install holds the compiler now; this rides the next pack.")
inl kit_remote_sync () = Task ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", "kit remote sync <host>: native non-git cross-machine code transport on the deterministic bundle pipeline (eoie_bundle_manifest + eoie_archive_tar_runtime); replaces the interim remote-run.ps1 after KIT-RENAME. M4 proof = byte-identical deterministic bundle with matching manifest_fingerprint on both sides. FNV-1a is accidental-drift detection only (no adversary between our own machines), not tamper-proofing. Lib-first: generic parts (deterministic archive, manifest fingerprint, budgeted extract) go into lib/spiral; kit keeps only the remote/host part.", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-RENAME", "kit,remote,transport,bundle,lib-first,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_remote_sync () = Planning ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_1 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 1u32, "2026-10-25T18:15Z", "kit2", "14:15 kit2 created per kit 5 directives (kit/kit2 sync): (1) ships after KIT-RENAME; (2) FNV-1a = accidental-drift only, not tamper-proof; (3) lib-first - deterministic archive + manifest fingerprint + budgeted extract into lib/spiral, kit keeps remote/host; (4) dep of EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR M4, which drops to proof wiring; (5) 950-margin gate on eoie_bundle_*/eoie_archive_* below.")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_2 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 2u32, "2026-10-25T18:19Z", "kit2", "14:19 kit2 lib-first partition (kit directive 3), grounded in source. ALREADY IN lib/spiral (reuse, do not duplicate): crypto.sha256 / sha256_compute_hash / hash_text for content+manifest hashing; FNV-1a exists as xsd_emit.fnv_from (offset 2166136261) with hex_text/int_text. RECOMMENDATION: the M4 proof hashes the canonical manifest text with lib crypto.sha256 (already generic, stronger, matches the validated PowerShell M4) - FNV only as an optional cheap pre-check, so bundle's 64-bit manifest_fingerprint is NOT the proof hash. MOVE TO lib/spiral (generic, new to lib; backend_switch shape + lib test blocks): canonical manifest collector (walk+normalize+dedup+reject symlink/special/unsafe/nested-zip) from eoie_bundle_manifest.collect_bundle_manifest; deterministic archive writer (canonical order, fixed timestamps) from write_deterministic_store_zip/deflate_zip; budgeted confined-link extractor with rollback from eoie_archive_tar_runtime + archive_deflate_domain.archive_extract_with_limit; verify_manifest_parity. KIT KEEPS (remote/host, not generic): ssh transport over Host laptop + Tailscale fallback, node mirror-dir lifecycle, placement/offload orchestration, the kit remote sync <host> CLI verb. So KIT-REMOTE-SYNC = lib extraction above + a thin kit remote/host crate; EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR M4 just wires the proof from lib. Reuse-crate margins already clear (bundle/archive generated .rs all <= 764).")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_3 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 3u32, "2026-10-25T18:55Z", "kit2", "14:55 kit2: per-job-mirror design VALIDATED locally (scratchpad/mirror-test.ps1, no node). git worktree add --detach gives an isolated working tree sharing the source object store (no clone/copy), so checkout+clean+delta-apply happen in C:\\home\\remote\\<job>\\<repo> and cannot lock files in another job's tree (fixes L1 Argu.dll collision). Collector git diff --name-only HEAD (staged+unstaged) + ls-files -o --exclude-standard, deletes --diff-filter=D. Result over a repo with a modified tracked file, a STAGE-ONLY new file (git add no commit), an untracked file, and a deleted tracked file: changed set includes the staged file (x positive control PASS), src digest == mirror digest (8090D79D), staged file present in mirror, modify applied, delete gone. Next: implement -Mirror in remote.ps1 (additive, default unchanged), validate on the free node, then coordinate making it default.")
inl note_of_kit_remote_sync_4 () = Note ("KIT-REMOTE-SYNC", 4u32, "2026-10-25T19:05Z", "kit2", "15:05 kit2: per-job mirror -Mirror LANDED in remote.ps1 and VALIDATED on the node (L1 fix). sync <repo> -Mirror <job> builds an isolated git worktree at C:\\home\\remote\\<job>\\<repo> (HEAD-ensure on the source; worktree add --detach --force; checkout+clean+delta+digest in the mirror), so concurrent offloads cannot lock each other's trees. Node test: sync dice -Mirror kit2test -> worktree created, 8 changed, src digest == mirror digest 303B9BD0, REMOTE-SYNC-OK, worktree removed clean. Parse-checked. Default unchanged (no -Mirror = shared clone, back-compat); flip to always-mirror after coordinating with main. TODO: L6 compiler-core guard (verify jobs must match the synced core) lands with the determinism check that builds the correct node compiler; run/job -Mirror cwd not added yet (callers cd explicitly).")
inl state_concurrency_sota () = Task ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", "SOTA state concurrency for the multi-machine system via the typefeatureset (user 2026-10-25: things will only get more chaotic). Model every shared resource - node mirror, compile slot, lease, job, backlog transaction - as a typed GADT with typestate so illegal concurrent states are unrepresentable; replace coarse guards and optimistic whole-file rewrites with fine-grained typed leases. Lessons from the 2026-10-25 multi-writer episode in note 1. Governs EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR and KIT-REMOTE-SYNC.", Epic, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "", "concurrency,typefeatureset,remote,eoie,kit2", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_state_concurrency_sota () = Planning ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", Unestimated, Load Medium, Assigned Pair)
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_1 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 1u32, "2026-10-25T18:49Z", "kit2", "14:47 kit2 - lessons from the 2026-10-25 multi-writer node episode, each with its SOTA/typefeatureset response. L1 SHARED-CLONE COLLISION: the fork's single-flight build (job-1792953108714) failed in 10s because ReadyToRun could not delete Argu.dll.r2r, held by x's verify on the SAME C:\\home\\git clone. Fix: per-job mirror C:\\home\\remote\\<job>\\<repo>, one writer per mirror; a typed Mirror held by a Lease, never the shared clone for builds. L2 OPTIMISTIC WHOLE-FILE STARVATION: agile rename rewrites the whole 6.5MB backlog, so on a hot backlog it keeps losing the read-modify-write race to quick note-appends (state/agile.spi changed since read). Fix: fine-grained backlog transactions (per-item or append-structured) so a rename/edit does not contend with unrelated notes; a typed Txn scoped to the item it touches, not the whole file. L3 DIGEST FALSE-MATCH: sync collected files with git ls-files -m -o (-m misses STAGED files) so staged files were silently dropped AND the digest still matched because it hashed only the files it chose. Fix: the proof hashes exactly the set sent; collector = git diff --name-only HEAD (staged+unstaged) + ls-files -o + --diff-filter=D deletes; positive control: a stage-only file (git add, no worktree change) must arrive on the remote. L4 COARSE -Force GUARD: sync's running-jobs guard is per-whole-machine and -Force bypasses it. Fix: a typed per-mirror Lease with typestate (Free | Held of owner) acquired before any write, so two writers on one resource is unrepresentable; no -Force over a held lease. L5 MULTI-EDITOR THRASH: remote.ps1 edited by fork, x and kit2 within minutes. Fix: single-writer ownership + claim note now; a typed edit-lease on shared org tooling later (the eoie tree lease already does this for repos). COMMON THREAD: every failure was an UNTYPED shared resource mutated without a typed lease/typestate. typefeatureset answer: Mirror, Slot, Lease, Job, BacklogTxn as nominal types with typestate indices (Queued|Leased|Running|Settled; Free|Held), codecs derived once, illegal transitions uncompilable. Implementers: EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (Gleam coordinator slot/lease typestate), KIT-REMOTE-SYNC (mirror + exact-set digest); L2 warrants a child item for the eoie agile transaction model (eoie core, after KIT-RENAME).")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_2 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 2u32, "2026-10-25T18:57Z", "kit2", "14:57 kit2 - L6 STALE-COMPILER STATE (x, 2026-10-25): node Ensure-SpiralCompiler used an older INSTALLED compiler, not one built from the synced tree core (34519B92), so verifies there reported pre-5l'' findings (18 on x) while the review box 5l'' reports 0 on the same files - a FALSE result from a mismatched compiler, even though the src sync digest matched (spiral d095f13d 939FF982). The compiler is ANOTHER piece of state that must match the synced core; src-tree equality is necessary but not sufficient. Fix (typefeatureset): a verify job carries its expected core hash as a typed precondition; the node's effective compiler core is checked == expected before the run (a typed CompilerCore guard), else build from the synced core or refuse. Ties to the determinism check (build single-flight from the synced core) and the offload flow; add the guard to remote.ps1 / the offload wrapper.")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_3 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 3u32, "2026-10-25T19:05Z", "kit2", "15:05 kit2: L1 (shared-clone collision) ADDRESSED - per-job worktree mirror landed + validated on the node via remote.ps1 -Mirror (KIT-REMOTE-SYNC note 4). L6 (compiler-core guard) next, with the determinism check. L2 (backlog whole-file optimistic starvation) still open - needs the eoie agile transaction model after KIT-RENAME.")
inl note_of_state_concurrency_sota_4 () = Note ("STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA", 4u32, "2026-10-25T19:07Z", "kit2", "15:07 kit2 - L6 guard spec (from spiral apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/env.ps1 + apps/compiler/tmp/AGENTS.md: Release builds are byte-reproducible across cache folders, so a compiler built from the same source has the same SpiralCompilerCore.dll sha256 = install-by-hash). Guard: the node's EFFECTIVE compiler core = sha256 of its SpiralCompilerCore.dll (Get-SpiralCompilerDll <mode> Release on the node, Get-FileHash); the EXPECTED core = the same on the review box for the synced HEAD. A verify/compile offload runs only if node-core == review-box-core; else build the compiler on the node from the synced core (the determinism check's step 1) or refuse. Implement as a typed precondition carried by the verify job (expected core hash), checked in the offload flow before the run. This is why x's node verify was wrong (18 findings): the node's Ensure-SpiralCompiler picked a stale installed core, not one from the synced tree. Lands with the determinism check (which builds single-flight from the synced core d095f13d and compares vs the review box CDD68DC3), schedule-gated on the fork's Iggy-up signal.")
inl fpc_trunk_everywhere () = Task ("FPC-TRUNK-EVERYWHERE", "FPC 3.3 (trunk) on every machine (user 2026-10-25 14:4x: 'shouldn't we update fpc to 3.3 everywhere?'): FPC has no 3.3 release (3.2.2 is the last stable; 3.3.1 is trunk, where new language work lands), so the toolchain is the official trunk snapshot from downloads.freepascal.org/fpc/snapshot/trunk (i386-win32 driver + utils, x86_64-win64 cross compiler + units), installed beside scoop's 3.2.2 with scoop's current junction repointed; no CI workflow installs fpc (every .github grepped), so 'everywhere' = this machine + the remote kit worker node", TaskKind, P1, D2, 60u32, Active, "-", "toolchain,delphi,fpc,upkeep", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fpc_trunk_everywhere () = Planning ("FPC-TRUNK-EVERYWHERE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_fpc_trunk_everywhere_1 () = Note ("FPC-TRUNK-EVERYWHERE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T19:05Z", "fiscal", "14:5x fiscal: local DONE. scoop\\apps\\freepascal\\3.3.1-20261025 = fpc-3.3.1.x86_64-win64.built.on.i386-win32.zip (75.5 MB, sha256 2206D8D866360844) + fpc-3.3.1.i386-win32.zip (82.4 MB), fpc.cfg via fpcmkcfg -d basepath=<dir>; current repointed (scoop marks the junction read-only: attrib -R current /L, repoint, attrib +R), 3.2.2 kept for rollback. fpc -iW = 3.3.1-21298-g814ece0205 through the shim and through Cells' scoop path. Bitness unchanged (Cells runs fpc -O2 -Mdelphi with no -P, so i386 as with 3.2.2). Smoke: i386 and -Px86_64 builds of a SysUtils/Math/StrUtils program (StringReplace, Pos, FileExists, ReverseString) run correctly. main notified (its Delphi matrix rows now run on trunk). Node: waits for kit2's go (shared node).")
